const ITEMS = [{"id": "gold_001", "topic": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer argues that even a tiny SaaS team can sell to a CIO, but only when the founder understands the buyer's social contract: do not create career risk, be honest about what is missing, show fast ROI, and build trust directly. The piece should emphasize that CIOs can back startups when the upside is meaningful and the initial blast radius is small.", "facts": ["Early-stage startups can sell to CIOs if the value is differentiated and the risk to the buyer is contained.", "CIO trust matters more than polish on day one; founders need credibility, responsiveness, and a plan to close gaps.", "Founders should not try to bypass enterprise constraints entirely; they should frame the relationship as a pragmatic pilot with clear ROI."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Social Contract: Why Your Small Startup Can (and Should) Target Big Enterprise Deals**\n\nAs I've talked to countless founders and entrepreneurs over the years, one common theme has emerged: fear of tackling big enterprise deals too early in their journey. \"We're just an 8-person startup,\" they'll say. \"How can we possibly compete with massive companies like IBM or Oracle?\" \n\nBut here's the thing: you don't have to be a giant company to sell to one. In fact, some of the most successful startups I've seen in my career as a VC and entrepreneur have been those that understand **The Social Contract**.\n\nWhat is The Social Contract? It's simply this: big enterprise buyers are not looking for fancy technology or feature-rich solutions (although those can be nice). What they really want is someone who understands their pain points, their workflows, and the social dynamics at play within their organization. They're searching for a partner that \"gets\" them – one who can help solve their problems in a way that's not just technically sound but also socially acceptable.\n\nSo how do you, as an 8-person startup, demonstrate understanding of The Social Contract? Here are three key strategies:\n\n1. **Build relationships, not just accounts**. Don't focus solely on closing deals; invest time and energy into building genuine connections with your target CIOs and their teams. Attend industry events, participate in online forums, and engage in thoughtful discussions about the challenges they face.\n\n2. **Understand the inner workings of large enterprises**. Study how big companies operate – not just from a technical standpoint but also in terms of organizational dynamics and cultural nuances. This will help you speak to their specific pain points and tailor your solution accordingly.\n\n3. **Showcase social proof, not just product features**. Highlight success stories from smaller customers who have achieved remarkable results with your platform. Emphasize how these wins are a result of the strong relationships built between your team and theirs – not just the technical capabilities of your software.\n\nWhen executed correctly, The Social Contract can work wonders for small startups looking to crack big enterprise deals. By focusing on building genuine connections, understanding the inner workings of large organizations, and showcasing social proof, you'll be well-positioned to succeed even with an 8-person team.\n\n**Real-world example**: At Echosign (which we later sold to Adobe), our tiny startup was competing for a contract against some of the biggest names in software. But by taking the time to understand the specific pain points and social dynamics within the client's organization, we were able to build trust and demonstrate value that no one else could match.\n\nThe outcome? We won the deal – and went on to achieve remarkable growth as a result.\n\nDon't let fear hold you back from pursuing those big enterprise deals. With The Social Contract as your guide, even an 8-person startup can succeed where larger companies may struggle. So go ahead: build relationships, understand the inner workings of large enterprises, and showcase social proof. You never know what's possible until you try.\n\n---\n\n**About Jason Lemkin**: As a serial entrepreneur and VC investor, I've learned that success in business is about more than just technology or features – it's about understanding people and building strong connections with them. Follow me on Twitter @jasonlk for insights into the world of startups, entrepreneurship, and The Social Contract!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The other day, I got a call from one of my favorite CEOs on how they really did it.\nHow they went from just $1M in ARR with less than 10 employees … to over $100m in ARR today and\n500+ employees!\nBut here’s the thing about this CEO.  It wasn’t easy for them to get there. They’re not a unicorn founder, or an ex-VP of Sales or something like that. They did it themselves.\nAnd they came from nowhere.\nI know many folks might look at their journey and say “wow”. But I also can see the struggles in those 8 years of bootstrapping and so much more.  Because they are real — even if you don’t see them on social media.\nHow do CEOs like this get to $100m+ ARR?  How does someone that has never sold a single deal at all … go from nothing to closing multi-million dollar deals with the CIO of a Fortune 500 company?\nThe answer is they learned and evolved. And importantly, they understood the Social Contract.\nHere’s what I mean by that:\nLet me give you an example.  When HubSpot first started selling to enterprise companies like GE or Cisco … it had just 30-40 employees!  It was barely over $20m ARR itself at that point.  But those early sales were so successful, they got them their very first million-dollar deal.\nAnd then a second one.\nAnd then a third.\nAnd each of these deals helped HubSpot learn more about what the enterprise needed in terms of software, and how to build it — all while still just 40 employees!\nBut here’s where things get tricky.  If you are growing too fast … if you don’t understand what the Social Contract is with an Enterprise buyer …. then you can blow up a deal.\nFor example, if your sales team has no way to manage an enterprise contract once it closes — that contract blows up and never gets paid.\n\nSo what’s the social contract?  It means you have to be honest about how many people are on your team.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I recently did another SaaStr AI Deep Dive with one of my favorite CEOs, CEO of CloudSight,\nTim Bertrand\n.\nWe talked about so much here:\nWhat’s really happening in the current state of AI and enterprise software\nHow to think about “AI-first” vs. adding AI features after your core product is built\nWhy you can’t always tell whether a company will be successful or not by looking at its metrics (and what you should look for instead)\nThe one thing that matters most when building an SaaS business: creating and solving real problems\nBut the part of our conversation that really got me thinking was about how CloudSight is able to compete with companies like Salesforce.  And they’re winning big.\nAt 8 people!\nNow this isn’t new, but it’s still mind-blowing:\nIt doesn’t matter if your team is “enterprise-ready” or not\nWhat matters is whether you can create a social contract with CIOs and other executives at large companies that lets them trust you enough to try your product out.\nThat means understanding what these leaders value — which isn’t always the same as what consumers do.", "cleaned": "So I had lunch with my old boss at Adobe today, and it was great. But we ended up talking for quite a while about what he’s seeing on his side as a Fortune 100 buyer, so that is where this will be focused. He mentioned one thing I hadn’t thought about before: They’ve been waiting longer than ever now to make any big purchase decisions. Why? There are no budget increases. And even if there were, they have to go through so many hoops internally just to get new apps approved, let alone justify new ones … It can take years. So yes, you may need to wait until next year to close your first $1m deal. Or two or three. Or ten. My point here isn’t necessarily how much to charge (even though pricing is critical). My real point is really understanding who your customer is. Who do you sell to, exactly? Do you sell to end-users? To managers? To directors? VPs? CTOs? CPOs? How do you work together? What do you bring to the table? How do you earn their trust? As a CEO selling into Big Companies : Don’t pretend you know more about enterprise software than you do. Don’t try to use buzzwords without truly knowing them. Be honest when you don’t understand something. Show respect for process. Big companies are well-oiled machines. Respect that. Don’t try to cut corners. That never works. Try to help solve other problems too. If you show up only wanting to talk about how your product solves their problem, it won’t work. Show up prepared with a few ideas how you might help others in the org.", "balanced": "In fact, if you do sell outside your core niche, at least be honest and upfront with them about it. Tell them why you are going after that segment, what you think will work for this customer base, and how you’ll staff up more quickly once you get bigger. I’ve seen folks go from zero percent market share in one vertical to 40% market share just by being good enough, local, and responsive (i.e., better than anyone else). So even in tiny niches, there’s still room. Even with only 2 or 3 customers in a small space. But whatever you do, don’t pretend. Don’t claim to have something in a vertical where you don’t yet. And also, understand that as soon as you can pull off a $1m+ deal … you may well need someone on the team that is experienced at selling and supporting a large enterprise. Maybe not today. But pretty darn soon. That doesn’t mean you should hire them tomorrow. It means you should start recruiting for this role right now. And when you finally land that $1m+ deal, she/he might already be ready. Just like Salesforce was when they landed their first big enterprise customer. Or maybe earlier. A related post here : Dear SaaStr: What Are Some Tips On How To Sell My Product to a Big Company? Image from here", "r32": "You need someone on your team who is willing and able to meet with them, go visit their customers (if they have any), learn as much as possible about what makes their business tick, and help them solve problems … even if it’s just for one customer or use case today. And you probably will lose some deals this way. It may be hard to see at first, but there are risks in selling to enterprise accounts without a brand behind you. Some will buy from a vendor that has a big brand and logo. But that doesn’t mean you can’t sell smaller businesses and startups into these companies. Just understand the social contract. Don’t expect to get everything done for free. Or overnight. Do more work than most other vendors. Be patient. Be responsive. Be honest. Ask good questions before each meeting, so you know how best to focus the time together. Help them. Show up prepared to deliver value. No excuses. That’s the deal. I was surprised to hear this myself when we started out doing enterprise sales. We didn’t really do “enterprise” until $1m ARR, because our ACVs were too small and our market too early. We had no logos. None. We built all our own software ourselves, including marketing automation and CRM apps, which made us not look like a real vendor. When we finally went enterprise, my VP of Sales told me he wanted to bring his laptop along to every single prospect call and meeting. I asked why. He said so he could show off the product right then and there. So it would be easier to close the sale. Well, sure. Of course. But he also knew something important. In bigger enterprises, you don’t always win.", "repaired": "A while back, I had coffee with a CEO that was doing well selling into enterprises and mid-market companies. His product was very technical but easy to use, so his sales team could do demos remotely and without much help from engineering or solution architects. He wanted to go more enterprise. To sell bigger deals, he’d need more people on his team:\nMore engineers to handle complex integrations and security requirements\nMore pre-sales resources (solution consultants, presales eng) to assist prospects through longer sales cycles\nSalespeople who understand how to navigate large procurement processes\nThis would double or triple his company size overnight. And he knew it wasn’t going to be simple. “It’s not just about having better products,” he said. “When you’re going upmarket, you have to become a different kind of company.”\nWhat makes this particularly interesting is that this founder has been there before—his previous startup went public at $1B+ ARR, got acquired by Cisco for $2.6 billion, and then started again after the acquisition fell apart. He knows what it takes to build an enterprise-grade organization.\nBut here’s where things get really telling:\n“I’m hesitant because once you start down this path, it’s hard to turn around. You can’t say ‘oh we don’t want to be an enterprise company anymore.’ Once you make those investments, they’re sunk costs whether you see them pay off or not.”\nHe’s worried about getting too big, even though he’s already built two successful tech companies. Why Enterprise Companies Are Willing to Buy From Small Vendors\nSo why would any CIO trust an eight-person company over a hundred-person one? The answer lies in understanding something counterintuitive about enterprise software purchasing decisions."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 561, "lemkin-clone-v5": 349, "lemkin-clone-v6": 189, "cleaned": 278, "balanced": 226, "r32": 293, "repaired": 280}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the post:\n\n**Can an 8-Person Startup Sell to a CIO?**\n\nAs I've always said, selling enterprise software is not just about having a great product - it's also about understanding how to play by the rules of the big leagues.\n\nI get asked all the time: \"Jason, can an 8-person startup really sell to a CIO?\" And my answer is... **yes**. But with one huge caveat: you have to understand The Social Contract.\n\nWhat do I mean? Well, in enterprise sales, there's a unwritten agreement between the buyer (the CIO) and seller (you). It goes like this:\n\n* As an entrepreneur selling into enterprises, you're not just trying to sell me something. You're also committing to be accountable for its success.\n* If your product doesn't deliver on its promises, I'll hold you responsible - in front of my boss, the board, and even our employees.\n\nThis means that as a startup founder, you have to demonstrate far more maturity and accountability than you would with smaller customers. You need to show me that your company is stable, reliable, and has a proven track record.\n\nSo how do you win over CIOs like myself? Here are the essential rules of The Social Contract:\n\n1. **Know my world**: Demonstrate deep understanding of our business problems, processes, and systems.\n2. **Prove it with metrics**: Show me quantifiable results from other customers - don't just make promises!\n3. **Be transparent about your risks**: I want to know what", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The other day, I joined my first Webex with a new B2B start-up that’s growing fast. But it was just me. And the CEO.\n“Where are your sales team?”\n“I laid off half of them last week.”\nThe CEO explained they had to be leaner. They went from $1m to $8m in ARR (and 25%+ growth) and were looking at a Series B in Q3. But that meant laying folks off.\nAnd I knew right then the deal was dead.\nI asked: “Who is your first customer? How big are they?”\nThey told me it was the CIO of their largest customer — one with 10,000+ users and $8m ARR with them alone! And they said he’d been a long-time friend and champion from his days at another start-up.\nSo I asked: “How many folks does that CIO have on staff?”\nAnd here’s the thing most B2B founders don’t get:\nThat CIO has 25+ people working for him. He needs to manage them all, make sure they work together, and keep his boss (the CEO) happy.\nThe social contract is this: if you are going to sell into a C-level executive, it’s not just about the product. It’s about how that will help her/him with their mission and goals, and what resources she/he can count on from you as they go through change after change in a big organization.\nSo here’s my advice:\nIf you are selling into a CIO or C", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Can an 8 person startup sell to a cio yes\nIf you understand what I call the social contract here:\n1. No hidden fees, no surprises at all.\n2. Always be there for support and issues.\n3. Not too complex.\n4. More than just some \"feature\" that is cool but doesn't add much value.\n5. And most importantly ... it works in production today\nWe did this with EchoSign (now Adobe Sign) when we sold to a CIO of a $1B+ company for $100M.  We had 10 employees at the time, and our platform was already used by thousands of companies.\n\nAnd now more than ever there are so many tools you can use in production today that really do work well:\nSalesforce\nSlack\nZoom\nDropbox\nHubSpot\nMonday.com\nZapier\n\nJust pick one.  And then go sell it to a CIO.\n\nIt's not too late, and the social contract will be understood if you are honest, transparent, and great at support.", "cleaned": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "balanced": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "r32": "Can an 8-Person Startup Really Sell To A Fortune 500 Company, or at Least a Big Tech Co.? I’ve been doing this for over 15 years now as both a buyer and seller (and sometimes both at once) … and the answer is yes. But only if you understand one thing very well: the social contract. What do I mean by that? Let’s start with how enterprise sales usually works, especially inside large tech companies like Google, Facebook, Amazon, etc., Salesforce, Microsoft, Oracle, Adobe, Cisco, VMware, Box, Zoominfo, ServiceNow, Okta, Twilio, PagerDuty, Slack, Atlassian, Workday, etc. In almost all cases, it takes several people from your startup to close any deal over $10k-$20k ACV. Maybe even just $1k-$2k. It often starts off OK when there are just two of you on the team. And then, as things scale up and go more enterprise, the number of resources can grow quite quickly. You may end up with a 6-12 person sales + success team. More if you sell into multiple stakeholders. So what does this have to do with selling to a big company? Well, if you want to really compete against the existing vendors at say a Fortune 2000 company, you need to be able to deliver exactly the same level of service, support, security, integration, functionality, etc. Or better. That means having enough of a team to deploy the solution correctly, and keep it running smoothly. That means", "repaired": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract. It’s not really that hard for a SaaS start-up to sell to a Fortune 1000, or even Fortune 500 customer. They are buying software too! But … there is one key issue you have to understand and embrace if you want to close these deals. And it can be tough to hear at first. Especially when we’re all so focused on efficiency and getting cash flow positive as quickly as possible. But here it is: the enterprise buyer has entered into what I call the “Social Contract” with your team. The Enterprise Software Buyer Has Entered Into a Social Contract With Your Team That Lasts Years. So Treat Them That Way. What do I mean by this Social Contract? Let me break it down step-by-step because most founders don’t get it: 👉 First, you must deliver high-quality products reliably every single day. Every time they log in, no excuses. This sounds obvious but you’d be surprised how many startups take shortcuts, delay releases, or hide issues from their customers. When you fail to meet this basic expectation, trust erodes fast. Once lost, rebuilding that trust takes months of consistent performance. 🔒 Second, you need deep technical knowledge and support whenever needed. Don’t assume your product is self-explanatory – especially in regulated industries like finance or healthcare where compliance requirements create complexity. Be prepared to invest in dedicated resources who can help customers navigate complex integrations, security audits, and custom implementations."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 246, "lemkin-clone-v5": 242, "lemkin-clone-v6": 160, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 16, "r32": 239, "repaired": 256}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**\"Can an 8-person startup sell to a CIO? YES! But only if you understand the social contract...**\n\nI've been in this game long enough to know that most entrepreneurs underestimate how difficult it is to sell to a large enterprise.\n\nBut I've also seen plenty of startups successfully land deals with CEOs, CTOs, and yes - even CIOs!\n\nSo what's the secret?\n\nIt all comes down to understanding the **social contract**. You see, when you're selling to an 8-person startup founder, it's all about the product itself. \"Is this cool? Does it solve my problem?\"\n\nBut with a large enterprise, it's not just about the product anymore. It's about trust, relationships, and politics", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Can your start-up sell to Fortune 500, especially to their CXOs and VPs? I think you can if you understand it’s not just selling software. It’s about the social contract.\n\nA lot of SaaS founders are so focused on growth that they forget — or never really learn — how big companies work. And that’s a problem when those big customers want to buy from you. Here’s what I mean:\n\nIn enterprise sales, there is always a “social contract” between vendor and buyer. It goes beyond just the product.\n\nA CIO knows if their team at Salesforce (or whatever) uses your app — it must be good enough for them too.\n\nIf they trust the CEO of a startup like Snowflake or Databricks to run part of their business, they’ll", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Just closed our first big deal with a Fortune 500 CIO at SaaStr Annual.\n\nI'm going back on this one from 2016:\n\n\"Why an 8-Person Startup Can't Sell to the CIO\"\n\nIt's just wrong.\n\nThe reality is there are still some great start-ups that can sell to any CIO.", "cleaned": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "balanced": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "r32": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "repaired": "Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO?\n\nYes — If You Understand The Social Contract."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 118, "lemkin-clone-v5": 130, "lemkin-clone-v6": 49, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 16, "r32": 16, "repaired": 16}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Can an 8-Person StartUp Sell to a CIO? Yes — If You Understand The Social Contract.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"Can an 8-Person Startup Sell to a CIO? Yes - If You Understand The Social Contract\"\n\n**Slide 1: Introduction**\n\n\"Hello everyone, I'm excited to be here today talking about something that many startups struggle with. Can we really sell our product to a large enterprise customer like a CIO? My answer is yes - but only if you understand the social contract.\n\n**Slide 2: The Problem**\n\n\"We've all been there. We build an amazing SaaS product, and then we try to sell it to a huge company with thousands of employees and millions in budget. But somehow, they just don't seem to get it. They're skeptical, hesitant - even hostile towards our startup.\n\n**Slide 3: The Social Contract**\n\n\"So what's going on here? Why are CIOs so tough to deal with? It's because there's a social contract at play that we as startups need to understand. The social contract is the unwritten rules of how business gets done in large enterprises. And I'm not just talking about technical requirements or features - I'm talking about the culture, politics and power dynamics within these organizations.\n\n**Slide 4: CIO's Core Concerns**\n\n\"So what are a CIO's core concerns when evaluating new vendors? They're worried about:\n\n* Risk\n* Cost\n* Integration with existing systems\n* Data security\n* Compliance\n\n\"And let me tell you - if your product doesn't address one or more of these areas, forget it. The sale is dead.\n\n**Slide 5: The Social Contract in Action**\n\n\"But here's the thing. When I say \"social contract\", I don't just mean that CIOs are following some abstract set of rules. No way - they're living and breathing this stuff every day. They've got their own metrics to hit, their own careers to advance, and their own reputations at stake.\n\n\"So when we start pitching our product as a \"disruptor\" or an \"innovator\", CIOs are thinking: 'Yeah right, buddy - you think you're innovative? You have no idea what it takes to make this thing work in my organization.'\n\n**Slide 6: How Startups Can Win**\n\n\"So how do we win at the enterprise game? It's simple. We need to understand and respect that social contract. That means:\n\n* Demonstrating a deep understanding of our customer's specific pain points\n* Providing clear, transparent pricing and ROI models\n* Showing a willingness to integrate with existing systems - not just ours, but theirs too!\n* Being upfront about data security and compliance requirements\n\n**Slide 7: Examples**\n\n\"I've seen it happen. I've seen startups that get this right. They understand the social contract, they speak the language of their customers... and suddenly, those CIOs start listening.\n\n\"Like when one of our portfolio companies, say, was able to demonstrate a clear ROI model for a major customer - something like 500% return on investment in just six months. Suddenly, that CIO is interested.\n\n**Slide 8: Conclusion**\n\n\"So there you have it. Can an 8-person startup sell to a CIO? Absolutely yes! But only if we understand the social contract at play and adapt our approach accordingly. It's not about being \"innovative\" or \"disruptive\". It's about speaking the language of your customers, respecting their constraints - and delivering results that they can measure.\n\n\"And trust me, when you do it right... watch out! The enterprise game is a whole new world of opportunity for startups.\"", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Hey everybody it's Jason from SaaStr and I just wanted to do something that we talked at least once a week for years but probably never done which is share with you all how a tiny eight-person startup can sell to the cio of an enterprise company okay so here's the story I was talking about this on LinkedIn last year it's really interesting and maybe not that many people will see it because it's just me but I wanted to do it again for folks that are in more vertical SAS applications you know a lot of us kind of learned from zoom info or even slack or twilio how to sell into the Enterprise right like these guys came out of nowhere grew so fast and were selling into big companies we're all trying to be that today well my story is slightly different it's not about selling to marketing I'm going to share with you a very interesting story this week at saster annual where I had the opportunity to meet some folks from an eight-person company called Pave they are building something really cool and it was like their first Enterprise customer so let me tell you that story okay so pave is doing all sorts of things but what we're interested in today is basically automating how companies get paid for employees on a monthly basis right now this process can take up to 30 days because payroll systems just don't talk to each other and then there's a whole bunch of problems with the system being out of date not having enough information so it creates an enormous amount of friction when you're hiring people", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Okay, so there’s something that I think folks in B2B and SaaS have forgotten over the years.\n\nIt turns out if you go back to the early days of salesforce.com, Hubspot, or almost any other company at all today … it was a few people on an email list. It was just a group of passionate customers who were willing to be guinea pigs for some new, untested product and service. And those folks in turn would often help others.\n\nToday we’ve lost that in many cases. We don’t see as much of this anymore. But it’s more important than ever.\n\nA CIO is someone else you can talk about with your customers today, but I just want to share a quick story from the other day at SaaStr Annual 2025. The CEO of a company called Foresight, which did an $8m acquisition by a $1b+ public company. And what’s interesting for us is he started it as his own startup. It was him and two others.\n\nSo, that means they went from three to eight or so today. That means 80% of them are still there. But let me tell you, this CIO who bought him in a $8m deal … said the following at SaaStr Annual: “This is the best team I’ve ever worked with.”\n\nThe CIO was not just saying that to be nice.\n\nAnd he also gave some very practical and tactical advice on how to sell to them. He’s got 1,000 engineers under him in this large organization.\n\nSo what does it take?\n\nA few things:\n\nFirst, you have to know who the “CIO” is at a company. It may not always be called that in today’s world of 10-20 different departments and C-level executives for each one. But there are always two or three people you need to talk to.\n\nThe first one is what we might call the internal CTO, but more like an IT manager.\n\nThey will want to do a free pilot with you before they buy your product.", "cleaned": "the sales cycle was very short but it took them months and years to implement so that's my advice to you if your goal is to sell into large accounts um even if you're not going enterprise just understand that social contract okay point number three related to this i've seen many startups when they go big get too aggressive on pricing now look there are times where you can be more aggressive on pricing as you scale first of all as you scale you want your pricing model to be simpler right you don't have time for all these little discounts and nickels and dimes like we did back in the day today everything should be simple everyone expects everything to be monthly or annual or usage-based whatever you do make sure it's simple at scale because you just won't have time otherwise secondly as you scale you need to standardize what you charge thirdly as you scale you may start charging differently by industry or customer type or buyer size etc but one thing you cannot let happen is getting too greedy as you cross five million ten million fifteen million error it's fine to take advantage of a small competitor it's fine to charge higher prices than some other vendor especially if you win deals left and right from day one but what will kill you is if you get too good at taking advantage of customers once you're bigger if you get too good at charging for features no one uses and nickel and diming people with extra seats for products that aren't used and increasing prices every six months without any new functionality and raising prices 20 percent each year and not really adding enough value to justify those price increases", "balanced": "you're going to have to be more responsive than you would expect your sales team is going to need to understand and respond to issues quickly um the cios are going to want you to come out there they may want you to do webinars for all their teams not just theirs they will ask if you'll go meet with other folks at the company so you can't run from this social contract okay it's tough enough but i'm telling you as a startup you cannot run from that social contract when you enter into these big deals because it's too much work and even worse it undermines trust trust is everything in software right every time you close something on a credit card you lose some amount of money right or if you use paypal you know if you've never used stripe before don't worry about it we all pay a little bit of extra money for our own transactions but you only get charged once you authorize it and then the transaction happens after that you don't pay anything but there's always that risk with merchant services and stripe has done such an incredible job building up its brand over time that people generally think nothing bad will happen with stripe and they're probably right today like it's very rare but the thing is that when you sell to a consumer and especially at the low end of sas you don't need a lot of trust you just need a free trial people try things you don't need a lot of trust when you start off small with consumers but enterprise customers demand massive amounts of trust and the way you build massive amounts of trust is by being reliable and doing what you say you're going to do now again i know you know this but most of us didn't grow up in", "r32": "you know what i mean it's not that you don't want them they're just going through this process and there is a social contract here so as founders we have to understand when you meet with a cio or even a ceo at a big company um you are you may be meeting with one person but really what you're meeting with is their whole team and your job is to make sure you can deliver on all the promises you've made right so if you said you could do something for next month you better check with everybody that can help you get it done because ultimately you're accountable for delivering on those promises and then there's another important part which is this idea of risk management again most cios and especially the more senior ones have worked with startups before okay so you need to understand the playbook right they're looking for risk management tools they're looking for things that can be deployed quickly they're often looking for products that work today or tomorrow rather than six months down the road so as a startup you need to understand how to speak to these risks right what are some risks around security maybe you have less features than a bigger vendor does what are some risks that others might see and the more thoughtful you are about mitigating those risks the higher the chance you'll win okay point three and let me step back from my story now think about it in terms of advice for both sides okay so first of all as a founder remember everything i'm saying makes sense yes it sounds obvious but the number one mistake i see founders make is not listening and asking enough questions yeah ask why do you love us like a dating app right tell me more tell me more why did you choose us over other vendors well what was it about our product that stood out what was", "repaired": "hey everybody i wanted to do this session today on saster with someone that's done it all from being ceo of zendesk and others to building his own company to selling his first startup for two hundred million dollars um but most importantly he's sold startups at scale into enterprises so we're going to learn how can you get those big deals if you're small like one or ten or twenty people maybe even eight people well let me tell you something right off the bat i used to be one of these guys back when i was running echo sign i thought enterprise sales were a joke because they just seemed too complicated and you had to have this team of reps and then you need sales engineers and there are so many acronyms and so much complexity and my co-founder and i said look i know we should sell to enterprises but boy is it hard and then we actually did it and got our first five figure deal out of the box and now we ended up getting seven figures uh as we went along but what we learned was the key to it wasn't having some magical product which we certainly didn't have at least not at first no matter how good your product is if you don't understand the social contract between you and your customers you will never make it in the enterprise okay and let me step back and explain what the social contract is because i think this is where i see the most mistakes made by founders especially in the early days um i've talked a lot on sasser over the years about why you really have to go long with your customers and why you have to build relationships and i'll add to that here but the point i want to make in the"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 568, "lemkin-clone-v5": 281, "lemkin-clone-v6": 338, "cleaned": 296, "balanced": 316, "r32": 332, "repaired": 314}}}}, {"id": "gold_002", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer distills Monday.com's ARR milestone into a handful of practical founder lessons, such as the importance of product-led distribution, repeatable expansion, disciplined execution, or category focus. The best version does not simply admire the company; it explains what an earlier-stage founder should copy, what they should ignore, and why the operating lesson matters.", "facts": ["The answer should extract concrete operating lessons from Monday.com's scale rather than just restate growth.", "It should connect ARR scale to repeatability in product, go-to-market, or efficiency.", "It should highlight that lessons from large SaaS companies are useful only when translated into founder actions."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Ultimate Growth Story: 5 Key Takeaways from Monday.com's $550M ARR**\n\n Ah, Monday.com. The ultimate poster child for cloud-based productivity software success. I've been following their journey closely over the years, and it never ceases to amaze me.\n\nAs of Q4'21, Monday.com reached a whopping **$550M in Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR)**. Yes, you read that right - five hundred fifty million dollars! To put this into perspective, they've grown from $0 to over half a billion dollars in ARR in just about 8 years.\n\nAs I always say, \"growth is the ultimate game\". And Monday.com's growth story is one for the ages. So, what can we learn from their success? Here are my top 5 takeaways:\n\n**1. Focus on Customer Success, Not Just Revenue Growth**\n\nMonday.com has always prioritized customer satisfaction above all else. They've built a platform that truly delights users, and it shows in their remarkable retention rates (which I'm told are north of 90%). By focusing on the customer experience, they've created a loyal community that drives word-of-mouth growth.\n\nLesson learned: Don't prioritize top-line revenue growth at the expense of customer satisfaction. It's a short-term fix with long-term consequences.\n\n**2. The Power of Vertical Focus**\n\nMonday.com has mastered the art of verticalization - identifying specific industries or use cases where their platform truly excels. By doing so, they've built domain expertise and created tailored solutions that meet the unique needs of each market segment.\n\nLesson learned: Identify your sweet spots and focus on delivering exceptional value to those customers. It's a much more efficient way to grow than trying to be all things to all people.\n\n**3. The Importance of Developer Ecosystems**\n\nMonday.com has invested heavily in their developer ecosystem, providing APIs, SDKs, and other tools that enable developers to build custom integrations with their platform. This approach not only expands the platform's capabilities but also creates a self-sustaining growth engine through partner contributions.\n\nLesson learned: Develop a robust developer ecosystem to unlock new revenue streams and fuel future growth.\n\n**4. The Value of Community Engagement**\n\nMonday.com has built an incredible community around its platform, with user groups, meetups, webinars, and more. By fostering this sense of belonging, they've created a loyal army of advocates who spread the word about their product.\n\nLesson learned: Invest in building meaningful relationships with your customers - it's one of the most effective ways to drive growth through word-of-mouth marketing.\n\n**5. The Power of B2B SaaS Pricing Strategies**\n\nMonday.com has mastered the art of pricing for value, offering tiered plans that cater to different customer segments and use cases. By doing so, they've created a pricing strategy that's both flexible and scalable - key characteristics of successful B2B SaaS businesses.\n\nLesson learned: Develop a pricing strategy that reflects the true value you bring to your customers. It's not just about revenue growth; it's also about creating a sustainable business model that rewards customer loyalty.\n\nThere you have it, folks! Monday.com's $550M ARR milestone is more than just a remarkable achievement - it's a testament to their commitment to delivering exceptional value to their customers. As entrepreneurs and B2B SaaS leaders, we can all learn from their approach to growth, customer satisfaction, vertical focus, developer ecosystems, community engagement, and pricing strategies.\n\nSo, what will you take away from Monday.com's incredible success story? Share your thoughts in the comments below!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So just this week we had the most recent IPO — of Monday.com.  A big one!  At least for those that follow SaaS.\nMonday is a great company, and now it’s public:\nIt has over 300 employees\nThat do not make any money at all (i.e., in R&D)\nAnd just recently crossed $500m+ ARR with almost zero churn!\nSo what can we learn from Monday?  A lot.  Let me try to distill some of the key takeaways:\n1. Yes, going multi-product is still possible\nIf you’ve seen a lot of great SaaS companies go vertical and stay that way over time — they’re not wrong here.  But there’s also no reason for many of us in SaaS to only do one thing.\nMonday has done just fine being more than an “agile” tool.\n2. You can still have 60%+ gross margins at scale\nThis is a bit underwhelming, but Monday.com had the lowest NPS I’ve ever seen from any public company (5).  This isn’t unusual today — but it’s very different from just a few years ago when most of us got crazy-high customer net retention.\nAt $500m ARR, with 100k+ customers Monday has gotten to pretty standard gross margins at scale.  So while the NPS is still low for SaaS standards today, it’s not that bad.\n3. This is why you should have a Chief Customer Officer\nMonday.com has had its fair share of issues here — and they’ve been very transparent about them with their public investors.  They just announced the first ever CCO!\n4. Monday.com still doesn’t really make any money from R&D, or even Sales & Marketing\nA lot of us in SaaS have gotten so used to having our expenses covered by our sales team that we forget this is actually a pretty unusual model.\nMonday.com does not yet cover its R&D costs with revenue — and it hasn’t for years.  It doesn’t make any money from marketing either, at least as far as I can tell.\n5.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we’ve had some great discussions on SaaStr here recently with Monday’s CEO Eran Zinman. One thing I thought was interesting was how they went more enterprise this year:\nMonday is going to be a lot bigger than it is now\nBut as much as possible, still remain an SMB & mid-market focused platform too.", "cleaned": "So we’ve been tracking Monday for years since its early days as Dapulse and watched it grow into one of our top SaaS leaders. And it’s now coming up on $600m in ARR! It started off strong, but then got a boost with Covid, when folks had to switch to work-from-home … quickly. Today, they’re still growing an epic 60% (!) at almost $600m in ARR — although that’s down materially from the crazy rates during lockdowns (and perhaps not coincidentally, so is Zoom). Let’s dig in just a few interesting learnings: #1. NRR Remains Strong At Almost $600,000,000 In ARR. Yes, you can see that Monday has seen some macro impacts, too, especially outside of North America. But overall growth hasn’t slowed all that much, despite what others are seeing. A big part of this is high retention / NPS driving customers to buy more products over time. Monday doesn’t break out its total customer count or even its paying customer base per se, but it does share that their logo churn rate remains low. Only 2.8% of logos left last quarter. That combined with 140+ NPS means most of us stay long-term. And also drives upsells. More on that below. #2. 130+ NPS Drives Logo Retention And Upsell s. While we don’t know exactly how many total customers Monday has (they only share paying ones), their net revenue retention isn’t quite 120%. The “Net” part of Net Revenue Retention is key. If your new customers pay less than existing customers do, your net revenue retention will be lower than 100%, no matter how fast your new bookings grow. This is true across multiple vendors I invest in and lead. So if Monday were truly growing 150% year-over-year, it would have a lot higher net NRR.", "balanced": "So we’ve written quite a bit on SaaStr over the years about how Monday.com has gone upmarket and added more features to its original product. It’s an interesting evolution, as it was originally priced around $10/month per seat for an all-in-one workspace app. But they’ve pushed hard into “bigger deals”, adding much bigger enterprise customers — and now those ones have driven their growth to +60% year-over-year. They went multi-product early, but really doubled down once they crossed $100m ARR or so. Now today, Monday is growing even faster than before, with 61% YoY revenue growth at $500M+ ARR! And importantly, while NRR remains high (and well above 120%), it’s dropped just a smidge from pre-Covid levels, back to the mid-130s (from ~140%). So if you can get your existing customer base buying more … that’s what helps drive growth. What worked here? Going more upmarket, pushing harder on larger accounts, and doubling down on product expansion. We’ll dig into 5 Interesting Learnings below: #1. Customers With More Than 50 Users are Growing At Twice The Rate Of Smaller Accounts This isn’t a surprise given their push into bigger deals, but still worth noting. Larger accounts are driving most of Monday’s growth these days.", "r32": "So I’ve been a huge fan of Monday.com since day one. Back when it was called “Dapulse”. It’s an incredible product that is so easy to use and yet so powerful. It quickly became my go-to for project management (and more). And now? Well, it’s become a rocketship! At $500m+ in ARR, they’re growing a stunning 61% (!) And importantly, a very healthy 108% NRR with customers that pay on average $30k a year. That means they are not just adding new accounts — but also expanding within them as well. Even at this scale. Monday has done something few others have pulled off. It started out SMB and mid-market, but then really expanded into enterprise deals over time. Today, its top customer pays $1.2 million per year. But even there, it didn’t start out that way. They kept the engine going by being true multi-product, too. The total revenue is up 61%, but their original core offering is still only growing 48%. So we’ll see where things go. The point here is to keep pushing yourself to do bigger deals than you think possible. You never know how big your ACVs can be until you truly push past what everyone thinks is possible today. A few other interesting learnings: #1. 76% of Customers Pay Monthly. This isn’t unusual, but it’s helpful to see Monday do both annual and monthly contracts side-by-side. Only 24% pay annually. #2. $10k-$100k Deals Now Most Revenue, But Still Strong Growth in Smaller Accounts. Monday went long in smaller accounts early, and those small deals fueled growth. But like Asana, HubSpot, Shopify and more, eventually the larger deals took over.", "repaired": "So there’s no question that Monday is one of the top Cloud leaders.  It competes with Asana and Smartsheet, but it’s clearly won the market share battle — growing far faster than either.\nIt crossed $500m in ARR this year, up from just $200m last year (!), so it’s basically doubling!  And while it hasn’t gotten quite as profitable yet, like most SaaS companies these days … it has gone pretty darn efficient.  Growing almost 100% at $500m+ in ARR!\nAnd importantly, it’s now cash-flow positive, and generating over $50m a quarter in free-cash flow.\nWow.\n5 Interesting Learnings:\n#1. 130,000 Customers, Up From 70,000 Just Last Year\nMonday doesn’t break out its exact customer count by ACV, but if we assume an average deal size of say $5k-$10k (a rough estimate) and go off Monday’s revenue growth numbers, they should be closing around 6,500 customers a month.  That means getting to 13,000 next year!\nThat’s impressive enough on its own. But what really stands out is how much more efficiently they are acquiring those customers. They’ve gone from adding ~2,400 net new customers per month a year ago to ~6,500 today. That’s nearly 3x improvement. And all without spending significantly more on sales & marketing. Their CAC efficiency metrics have improved dramatically.\nThis isn’t just good news for their business model—it signals they’re getting better at identifying which prospects convert and scaling the playbook accordingly."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 557, "lemkin-clone-v5": 333, "lemkin-clone-v6": 55, "cleaned": 297, "balanced": 204, "r32": 275, "repaired": 236}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt to write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"Scaling Success: 5 Insights from Monday.com's $550M ARR**\n\nJust had an incredible conversation with **Ran Segall**, CMO at @Mondaydotcom. At just over $500M, they're one of the fastest-growing SaaS companies I've seen.\n\nWhat struck me most was their ability to balance product innovation with go-to-market efficiency. Here are 5 interesting learnings from our chat:\n\n1. **Product-led growth is a myth**: While it's often cited as a key driver for success, Monday.com's experience shows that it's actually a mix of both product and sales-driven strategies that lead to explosive growth.\n2. **Innovation through acquisition**: Their strategic acquisitions (e.g., Crew) have been instrumental in expanding their offerings and accelerating user adoption.\n3. **Growth hacking is not just for early-stage companies**: Monday.com has successfully applied growth hacking principles throughout various stages of their journey, from customer development to scaling sales teams.\n4. **The importance of alignment between product & GTM**: Ran emphasized how critical it was to align the entire organization around a single vision and message - this led to significant increases in efficiency and effectiveness across both product and go-to-market functions.\n5. **Data-driven decision making is key**: With $500M ARR, Monday.com has access to rich data that informs every business decision. But what's most impressive is their ability to use these insights to iterate quickly and adapt to changing market conditions.\n\nThese learnings are a", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So what’s it like to go from an MVP that started with one feature — making Mondays better for you and your team\n— into a $500m+ SaaS juggernaut? A bit of everything. But here are 5 interesting learnings:\nMonday.com was founded back in 2012 by Eran Zinman, Roy Mann, and Daniel Ishak as an internal tool within their own company. The first version only allowed users to track tasks — but that single feature quickly became so important to the founders’ workflow that they built a web app around it. By 2016 Monday.com had grown into a robust platform with over 25 integrations, including Slack and Google Drive.\nMonday’s growth has been impressive:\nRevenue\nfrom $9M (2020) to $550M (2025)\n– 6000% YoY Growth\n– The fastest SaaS in history!\nEmployee Count\n: From 400 employees at the end of 2018 to over 1,700 today.\nProduct Expansion\n: Beyond project management tools, Monday.com has expanded into a full-fledged platform for all team collaboration and productivity needs. It’s now used by over 200,000 teams across more than 140 countries worldwide!\nMonday’s success story is one that many founders can draw inspiration from:\nStart with what matters most to you as an individual.\nBuild something people actually need (and will pay for).\nIterate based on customer feedback and market trends.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Monday.com is one of my all-time favorite SaaS stories. I’ve known its co-founders since before it was public and back when they were struggling to get off the ground (they almost ran out of money).\nAnd today? It’s a $5B+ unicorn growing at an incredible rate.\nSo what can we learn from Monday.com in 2023?\nMonday is still hyper-growth.  Not slow growth, but hyper-growth.  Even at almost $6 Billion in valuation.\nIt has 230,000 customers and 2 million users total now.  That’s a lot of users per customer! But that also means it has to be very efficient.\nBut they are still growing 35% YoY or more at the same scale as when we first profiled them — $500m ARR in 2021\nThe NRR is insane. A staggering 120%.  That’s basically unheard of, and shows Monday.com customers buy far more than most.\nIt has an incredible 50/50 split between SMBs and Enterprise.  They literally have the same number of $100k+ deals as they do smaller ones.  Most SaaS companies get maybe half their revenue from larger accounts.\nThe bottom line?  A lot can happen in a short time at Monday.com — but its growth rate is still truly incredible today, even at almost $6B valuation.", "cleaned": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR #1. $50k+ Customers Growing 54% and Now Half of Revenue While many SaaS leaders are still struggling to get their enterprise customers growing again (see Snowflake), Monday’s larger deals are accelerating. In fact, they’re now half of revenue. And overall customer count is up 36%, even with some macro headwinds. That combination is very strong for growth going forward.", "balanced": "#5. 20% of Revenue From Outside North America and Europe While most SaaS companies are still concentrated in NA / EMEA, many are seeing growth outside those markets accelerate as well. Especially when they have strong global brand names. And a few other interesting learnings: #6. 150+ “Power Users” per Enterprise Customer It’s been pretty consistent across the leaders that each enterprise customer uses multiple products from these vendors. At least on average. This is why you see so many multi-product offerings now from the top public SaaS companies. To maximize ACVs from their existing customers. #7. 85 NPS — Pretty High for B2B Software That’s up from 74 just one year ago! But NPS doesn’t necessarily correlate to net revenue retention or much else important. So I don’t put too much stock into it myself. Still, this is impressive given how mature Monday is today. We’ll check back in later to see if this level of high NPS continues. #8. 50% of Customers Self-Serve Before Sales Rep Involvement Again, we’ve seen similar data with Asana (where 70% self-serve before sales gets involved) and HubSpot (60%) as well. The majority of Monday’s customers start off using its Free edition before upgrading to paid editions. #9. 100,000 Total Paying Customers A reminder everyone can scale past even millions of customers, especially with low-end/Free editions. HubSpot has 200,000 total paying customers. Canva has hundreds of millions. It", "r32": "#1. 100% NRR for SMBs and 110% for Mid-Market. Monday has stuck to its roots as a product for everyone. While most have gone upmarket since IPO’ing, it’s kept pricing relatively low across both segments — with no real price increases or big discounts. That keeps its net revenue retention high, but also makes growth harder than if they’d focused more on upselling larger customers. #2. 22% Operating Margins, Up From -12%. This is what you can do when you go very enterprise and have strong free cash flow. Monday went much more “enterprise” after IPO (a trend we’re seeing across many SaaS leaders), which led to higher ACVs that helped drive operating margins way up. But this does make them less competitive against folks like Asana. #3. 50/50 Product-led Growth / Sales-Led Growth. They’ve maintained a balance between PLG and sales-driven motion. We’ll see how long that lasts. It’s getting tougher out there for all SaaS companies to maintain a blended model where they don’t lean heavily on one over the other. Many are leaning more into sales-driven motions today, and even more so in tougher times. #4. 30% Non-GAAP Profitability Goal For Next Year. Monday doesn’t break down its profits quite the same way public SaaS companies do, but they are driving toward 30% non-GAAP profitability next year. And importantly, they’ve driven their CAC down materially from 6 quarters ago. Now each new customer", "repaired": "Monday is one of my favorite stories. It was just another SaaS project started by two Israelis who met while in the Army and wanted to go back home. They did it for real though — after all, their CEO Shuki Lea’ah had already done it once before with his first start-up, GetTaxi / Via! Today they are on track to hit $600m+ in ARR, growing an incredible 53% year-over-year, and worth almost $7 Billion (!). And importantly, today it’s not just SMBs anymore. Its larger customers now fuel much of its growth. A few interesting learnings: #1. $50k+ ACV Customers Fuel Most Growth At Monday The largest segment of revenue at Monday is from $50k+ deals. That wasn’t always the case. But as you can see below, that segment has grown from 20%-25% of revenues 2 years ago to 30% today. And this is also where most of the growth is coming from. So if your product isn’t enterprise yet … maybe lean into bigger customers more than you have so far. #2. 90% GRR. Even With No Long-Term Contracts If You Have Happy Customers, Don’t Let Them Go! We’ve seen this again and again across our portfolio companies. Monday does fine without long-term contracts (they have a 14 day free trial), but it doesn’t take them seriously. Customers renew because they love Monday, and there really is no better way to drive up NPS and happiness than driving up GRR 😉"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 238, "lemkin-clone-v5": 219, "lemkin-clone-v6": 205, "cleaned": 68, "balanced": 235, "r32": 236, "repaired": 243}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X (formerly Twitter) post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n\"MONDAY.COM: $550M ARR\n\n Just got back from an incredible day with @OmerShai and the team at MONDAY.COM. Here are 5 key learnings that I think all SaaS founders can benefit from:\n\n **The Power of Platform Thinking**: Monday.com's growth is a testament to the power of building a platform, not just a product. They've created an entire ecosystem around their core tool.\n\n **Obsession with Customer Success**: The team's relentless focus on customer success has driven 90%+ retention rates and fueled word-of-mouth marketing that's unmatched in SaaS.\n\n **The Importance of Customization**: Monday.com's flexibility to customize workflows for each customer has allowed", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Monday is so good at being a platform.  It’s a great example for you to study.\n\n#1. A True Platform Now\nThe first thing we learned is that Monday has truly become an app platform.  Almost 25% of its revenue comes from partners now, and it has grown dramatically over the last few years:\n\nMonday.com: “Partnerships are one of our core focus areas… We have almost 300 partner companies worldwide.”\n\n#2. A Strong NRR at $550m in ARR\nNRR is still strong at Monday — but not quite as strong as we’ve seen from its competitors, even when you factor in the partnership piece:\n\nMonday.com: “Our Net Retention Rate has been consistently above 120% over these years… We have achieved this", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So Monday is probably one of my favorite SaaS apps I use every single day. It’s really a workhorse for me and many others to help manage everything from sales to marketing to HR and more.\n\nMonday has been around since 2012, so it’s well past the “early days” in SaaS today. But Monday.com is still growing at an incredible clip — over $500m ARR already!\n\nSo what can we learn?  A few things:\n\n1/5\nA bit of a look back on where Monday was when I first invested.\nIt has since grown to be one of the fastest-growing public cloud companies in history.", "cleaned": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500,000,000+ in ARR", "balanced": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500,000,000 in ARR", "r32": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500m+ ARR", "repaired": "5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $500,000,000 in ARR"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 112, "lemkin-clone-v5": 121, "lemkin-clone-v6": 104, "cleaned": 9, "balanced": 9, "r32": 8, "repaired": 9}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Monday.com at $550,000,000 in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin on learnings from Monday.com at $550M in ARR:\n\n**Title:** \"The Monday.com Playbook: Lessons Learned Scaling to $550 Million in ARR\"\n\n**[Slide 1: Introduction]**\n\nHello everyone! Today, I want to share with you some fascinating insights from my conversation with a team that's achieved something truly remarkable - reaching $550 million in Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR) as a software company. Monday.com is the poster child for successful SaaS companies, and their journey offers valuable lessons we can all apply to our own businesses.\n\n**[Slide 2: From Zero to Hero]**\n\nLet me set the stage. Monday.com started just like many of you - with an idea, some code, and a vision to change how teams work together. By focusing on collaboration tools for every department in an organization, they disrupted the traditional project management space and quickly gained traction.\n\n**[Slide 3: Learning #1 - Focus on Customer Success]**\n\nSo, what made Monday.com so successful? It all comes down to their relentless focus on customer success. They invested heavily in building a world-class support team that truly cared about their customers' needs. This isn't just about resolving issues; it's about understanding the unique challenges of each organization and providing tailored solutions.\n\n**[Slide 4: The \"No Code\" Revolution]**\n\nMonday.com also pioneered the \"no-code\" movement, empowering non-technical users to build custom apps without writing a single line of code. This democratization of technology has been a game-changer for businesses looking to automate processes and streamline workflows.\n\n**[Slide 5: Learning #2 - Agility is Key]**\n\nAnother key lesson from Monday.com's journey is the importance of agility in software development. By adopting an agile mindset, they were able to iterate rapidly, respond quickly to changing market conditions, and deliver continuous value to their customers.\n\n**[Slide 6: The Power of Verticalization]**\n\nMonday.com also chose a verticalized approach, focusing on specific industries like sales, marketing, and customer support. This allowed them to develop deep domain expertise and build solutions that catered specifically to the needs of each industry.\n\n**[Slide 7: Learning #3 - Build for the Long-Term]**\n\nNow, here's where things get really interesting. Monday.com has shown us that building a successful software company requires patience and a long-term perspective. They've been investing in their platform, people, and processes since day one, even when it meant sacrificing short-term gains.\n\n**[Slide 8: The Role of Data-Driven Decision Making]**\n\nTo make informed decisions at scale, Monday.com relies heavily on data-driven insights. By leveraging advanced analytics tools and machine learning algorithms, they're able to identify trends, anticipate customer needs, and optimize their product roadmap accordingly.\n\n**[Slide 9: Learning #4 - Talent Matters]**\n\nAs you'd expect from a company of this size, talent acquisition and retention have been critical components of Monday.com's success. They've built an incredible team by focusing on diversity, inclusion, and employee growth opportunities.\n\n**[Slide 10: The Impact of Culture]**\n\nLast but not least, I want to highlight the significance of culture at Monday.com. By fostering a collaborative, customer-obsessed environment that encourages experimentation and innovation, they've created a workplace where everyone feels empowered to contribute and make an impact.\n\n**[Conclusion]**\n\nThere you have it - five essential takeaways from Monday.com's incredible journey to $550 million in ARR:\n\n1. Focus on customer success as the engine of growth.\n2. Leverage agility and continuous iteration to stay ahead of market changes.\n3. Build for the long-term, investing in your platform,", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Monday has been on an epic journey. From its first customers in Israel to being one of the leading enterprise SaaS companies today. And it’s only gotten more powerful and easier for users.\n#1.  More Power Users = Less Revenue Per User\nThis is a reminder how Monday.com has evolved over time:\nIn the early days, it was $30-40 per user per month. But in those days, there were often only one or two power users at an SMB. Fast forward to today and you’ll see that SMBs are using more features … but they’re also just paying a fraction of what they used to.\n#2.  You Need to Make it Easy for Power Users\nMonday’s core customers have gone from 1-20 to 1000+ users, often in an organization, all on Monday.com today. This is a reminder how important ease-of-use and workflows are. Your power-users will become your evangelists.\n#3.  Almost Half of Their $550m ARR Is Outside North America\nMonday has been one of the most successful SaaS companies outside the US over time. And they’ve kept it up. Today, 49% of their ARR is from outside North America. A reminder to go global as early and often as you can.\n#4.  Monday.com’s Revenue Growth Has Slowed But Not Too Much\nMonday still has epic growth — but it isn’t growing quite at the same rate as in 2021-22. It was up a stunning 50%+ last year, but that is down to “only” +17% this year. Still pretty darn good.\n#5.  Monday.com’s Gross Margins Have Fallen From 80% to 77%. A Little More Than Expected\nMonday.com has been one of the most profitable SaaS companies for years and years — with gross margins at a stunning 80%-85%. But like so many others, they’re now back down to ~75%-80%.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "okay let's take monday we're talking monday we talked about them last week with jeff lamm and i wanted to just review some interesting learnings from them you can see their growth right here it looks like a sas unicorn but they are not the fastest growing at 30% but they did hit a billion dollars in ar on the way so pretty impressive now monday is not an adobe or a salesforce but if you take out these huge players that really dominate almost half of all enterprise software revenue monday does well it's a product built by people who want to use a workflow management tool and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all they have 130,000 customers now this is important because one thing monday did was they went from being a very expensive platform for marketing teams which they were in the early days to more affordable it's not cheap but you know as low as two or three thousand dollars a month and that really opened up their customer base and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all they have 130,000 customers now this is important because one thing monday did was they went from being a very expensive platform for marketing teams which they were in the early days to more affordable it's not cheap but you know as low as two or three thousand dollars a month and that really opened up their customer base and i think we can see some interesting learnings here first of all 130,000 customers is actually pretty good they're growing at about four percent per month so there are 50 new customers every day and this is what monday does well it's an intuitive product built by people who want to use a workflow management tool and if you go on their website which", "cleaned": "going to be able to do that they're going to have to get more enterprise and monday is getting there but it's still only uh 12 percent of its revenue comes from customers with over 100 seats so the majority of monday's customers are small teams small businesses um we've seen some other leaders like asana see that work well for them too but others like notion have pushed very hard into bigger deals and monday has done it but not yet fully so it's worth watching this metric because it could go either way right now it's 12 it was nine last quarter and it may actually stay flat next quarter if you look at their guidance but watch for when it crosses 15 or 20 and then it really takes off okay point four uh maybe this one doesn't sound interesting but i think it's super interesting which is how does your average deal size change over time what's happening on a per customer basis and monday's gone through quite an evolution here in the early days when people started using monday.com which is free and then turned pro and then went paid many folks used monday for simple projects that were just short lived that had no need to upgrade and pay and indeed in the beginning all of their revenue was self-serve but today even though half of monday's new customers are still self-served almost three quarters come from sales so they've gotten much better at sales and closing these larger accounts and the result is the number of seats they add per customer each year has increased dramatically since 2018. it was 16 seats added per customer per year in 2018. that jumped up to 31 by 2020.", "balanced": "it's been really interesting to watch monday go long it took its time getting international and today over half its revenue is outside the us um that's not true for many folks especially with the zooms but what has happened as we've seen other leaders like hubspot shopify others do when you have a global product when your customers are global you just get better at going local you know you see the same thing again and again where folks will start off in their own backyard they'll start off uh maybe doing business in new york or san francisco and then they get big enough they expand across north america and then eventually across europe and beyond and it turns out monday did this very deliberately even though 70 percent of the company was based in tel aviv before covet hit they had already opened offices in boston london paris and singapore so while covid accelerated the move to work from home i think it also made mondays more intentional on going global because all these offices were now distributed around the world and so fast forward today over 50 of their revenue comes outside of the us and they're seeing acceleration there now they're growing faster than inside the us okay point six and i thought this was an interesting one and i think it ties back to our discussion early about how they don't really sell enterprise sales teams don't really work well in smbs but boy can they scale quickly if they find the right motion so they have five different types of products four plus and self-serve and it's interesting what happens here and most of them are similar priced under 20 bucks per seat a month some are free so they're not necessarily competing with each other but they're adding up so they've got four", "r32": "going to go up and they're going to continue to grow that's what we want um but we are seeing the first signs where it may not be enough for some folks that this is one of them monday has more than doubled its customer count since ipo now growing just 27 percent year-over-year so maybe growth will have to accelerate for monday okay number four another interesting learning which i think should inspire us all especially if you sell into the enterprise and other verticals or industries as well as tech and finance and whatever else um monday like many others does serve different segments and it doesn't always break out their exact numbers but what's really interesting is it was much harder hit by covet then many of us expected so when covit hit monday saw uh big declines across multiple departments healthcare insurance financial services all had big declines because of kovin whereas tech had relatively little impact tech actually grew even faster during kovat um as did e-commerce other segments were very impacted so again there's no simple answer here but if your industry was less affected by covet maybe you didn't feel it that badly and certainly zoom felt it wasn't too bad but on the flip side don't expect things magically rebounding overnight in your portfolio companies that got hurt in specific verticals during covet i know this sounds obvious but sometimes we get caught up with our own metrics and data and forget that different segments can be hit differently and covet was brutal on many verticals that monday serves um last point five um interesting learning and this one kind of ties back to something we talked about earlier which is nrr and retention and nps which is how do you keep customers happy after they buy you know", "repaired": "hey everybody i wanted to do this new series called five interesting learnings and what we're going to try to do is take public sas companies that are doing well but maybe aren't as iconic or as obvious as they could be um for example there's a lot of content out on the internet about datadog and others but there isn't quite as much around monday.com which is pretty impressive right now it's worth over seven billion dollars growing like crazy even today with covid so let's dig into monday.com uh 500 million ar 60 growth still 30 plus percent and see some five interesting learning because there's actually a lot to learn here one monday is incredibly powerful it was born in israel and it really took off when its founders moved to tel aviv it has become an incredible force in work management um and then added more and more products but started in project management in particular and it just went off in terms of adoption and usage um and it exploded after covid um it wasn't immune to covid it did not go through a zero revenue month or anything crazy like that but boy it accelerated after march 15th of last year right before shelter orders kicked in and since then boom 100 growth acceleration and it's staying that way for now um so let's break down five interesting things first of all one of the ones you'll hear again and again which is true which is nrr almost always goes up as you go up market okay this is true with zoom with slack and others as well and other leaders in work management and collaboration monday's nr is still relatively low 110 at 500 million error which means folks cancel fairly frequently but it does go up as you get bigger so if you have something like a free edition or very inexpensive edition your nr may stay lower forever but as soon as"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 567, "lemkin-clone-v5": 299, "lemkin-clone-v6": 317, "cleaned": 293, "balanced": 311, "r32": 307, "repaired": 331}}}}, {"id": "gold_003", "topic": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer says the best candidates separate themselves by proving they already think and act like owners. They research the company deeply, show where they would add value, and bring a concrete plan or analysis to the interview. The piece should stress that preparation and substance beat charisma, whether the role is SDR, AE, VP, or COO.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that candidates stand out by doing the work before they are hired, not by sounding polished.", "It should recommend concrete preparation such as bringing a plan, showing real understanding of the company, or demonstrating execution ability.", "It should make the advice useful across roles while staying specific."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Ultimate Guide to Standing Out in Any Job Interview**\n\n Ah, job interviews. The ultimate test of your skills, experience, and... charisma? As I've navigated my own career journey from early-stage CEO to venture capitalist, I've come to realize that standing out in an interview is more than just about being prepared or even qualified for the role.\n\nIt's about leaving a lasting impression on the interviewer. It's about showcasing your unique value proposition and making them wonder how they ever lived without you. In this post, we'll break down the key strategies I've learned over the years to truly stand out in any job interview – from entry-level SDR (Sales Development Representative) to C-suite roles like COO.\n\n**Be Authentic**\n\nYour personality is what sets you apart from other candidates. So don't try to be someone you're not. Be authentic, but also be intentional about your image and brand. If you're interviewing for a role at a startup, show the interviewer that you're flexible, adaptable, and willing to roll up your sleeves.\n\nAs I've always said, \"The best way to stand out is to be different.\" Don't try to emulate someone else's style or approach. Embrace what makes you unique – whether it's your background, interests, or perspectives on the industry.\n\n**Do Your Homework (Really Do It)**\n\nI'm not just talking about reviewing the company website and researching their products/services. I mean doing real, actionable research that shows you're genuinely interested in the role and willing to dive deep.\n\nFor example, when interviewing at a startup, look up publicly available sales data or customer reviews on Trustpilot or G2Crowd. Analyze your competitors' strategies and market trends. Ask informed questions during the interview – like \"How do you plan to tackle [specific challenge] in Q4?\" or \"Can you walk me through your team's current workflows?\"\n\n**Show, Don't Tell**\n\nWhen it comes to showcasing your skills and experience, don't just list them off on a resume or tell the interviewer about them. Show them. Share concrete examples of times when you've applied those skills in real-world situations.\n\nUse the STAR method:\n\n1. **S** - Situation: Set up the context for what happened\n2. **T** - Task: Explain your role and responsibilities during that situation\n3. **A** - Action: Describe the specific actions you took to address the challenge or opportunity\n4. **R** - Result: Quantify the outcome – was it a 25% increase in sales? A successful product launch?\n\n**Prepare for Tough Questions (And Don't Freak Out)**\n\nTough questions are inevitable, but don't let them catch you off guard. Anticipate common interview questions like \"What's your greatest weakness?\" or \"Why do you want to leave your current job?\"\n\nWhen preparing, focus on the following:\n\n* Know thyself: Understand what makes you tick and be prepared to articulate it.\n* Be honest (but tactfully so): Frame weaknesses as areas for growth or opportunities for improvement.\n* Show enthusiasm: Demonstrate genuine interest in the role and company.\n\n**Ask Great Questions**\n\nFinally, don't just sit there like a bump on a log. Ask informed questions that show you're genuinely interested in the role and willing to contribute. Use these three types of questions:\n\n1. **Clarifying questions**: \"Can you elaborate on [specific project/ initiative]?\"\n2. **Analytical questions**: \"How do you see this role contributing to the company's overall goals?\"\n3. **Problem-solving questions**: \"What are some common pain points in this department, and how can I help address them?\"\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nStanding out in a job interview is not just about being prepared or qualified –", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We all have to interview. And I’m not sure any job is truly easier than another today. It’s just too competitive.\nSo how do you stand out?\nYou need a killer story\nA great personal brand\nAnd a real plan for the next 30, 60 or 90 days\nThat really works — as it should 🙂\nBut what happens when that doesn’t work? I’m not sure any job is truly easier than another today. It’s just too competitive.\nSo how do you stand out?\nYou need a killer story\nA great personal brand\nAnd a real plan for the next 30, 60 or 90 days\nThat really works — as it should 🙂\nBut what happens when that doesn’t work?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "so I'll let you know what one thing you can do this week to really stand out and make it easier for you to get that dream job just pick something but try to add some value somewhere and then the next time they talk to you don't tell them about that one thing and how amazing it was instead just tell them about how much better things are now because of that thing so in other words when they ask what's new or if there are any changes at your company and even more importantly at this interview when they meet with all the people they're interviewing for a job here you have to be able to answer those questions not by talking about some great initiative from three years ago but actually how things improved because of it so I know that is going to sound very straightforward I'm sure we've talked about this in other times on Saster and others but it's true if you can't make a difference somewhere at your company or elsewhere then maybe this job isn't the right fit for you maybe the next one will be", "cleaned": "If you truly are going for it — show that. Show how your skills and experience will help solve their problems now. And if they don’t know why you’d be great at something … well, then maybe find another role to work on until you can level up more. Ask questions back : Don’t just ask generic questions like “what do you like most/least about this company?” Instead, dig deeper into specific areas of interest. If there’s an area where you could genuinely contribute or learn, explore it with the interviewer. This shows curiosity and willingness to take initiative. Remember, these aren’t just interviews—they’re opportunities to build relationships that matter. The companies that hire people who stand out during interviews rarely regret those decisions. A related deep dive here: Hiring? Make Sure You Know Why They Want To Work There (Updated)", "balanced": "How To Truly Stand Out In An Interview (Updated) If you want to truly stand out during an interview — do something that isn’t done often enough. Especially for entry-level and mid-pack candidates. Do it right before the interview starts. I’ve been hiring since 2005 now, and what I can tell you is almost no one does this. Almost every candidate comes into an interview without any prior research on the company or role at all. Even worse, they haven’t prepared how their skills match up to the job description. That’s easy to fix with some simple homework. The best thing you can do pre-interview … is send over some notes saying you did your homework, you know where you’d like to fit on the org chart, and how you would add value as early as Week 1. It works. And it shows you are serious and willing to put in work beforehand. This will likely increase the odds you get the offer by 50% right there. At least if the other applicants don’t bother doing the same level of prep. Don’t expect anyone to care much if you didn’t do the basic research on them first. Send over those “Notes” after you schedule but before the meeting, with a few thoughts on why you think you might be a good fit. You’ll stand out already then. Now if you really want to impress someone? Add a comment or two on exactly how you plan to contribute in the first week. What you’re going to do, not just talk about it. Then the interviewer knows you aren’t just another applicant, you have real ideas on how to help. They may even be able to use you earlier than expected! So take 15-30 minutes to write down who you are, and how you could make the organization better. Not generic stuff, but specific things you can do to improve it immediately.", "r32": "The truth is that most candidates are terrible at interviews. Not because they’re stupid or unqualified — it’s just that nobody practices anymore. I know you’ve been told this before. But let me tell you why it matters more than ever today and how you can actually stand out by being different. Why Interviews Have Changed (And Why This Matters More Than Ever) The days when an interview was as simple as showing up with your resume and answering basic questions? Those days are gone. Gone for good. Today, every candidate looks exactly the same on paper : – Studied CS50 on Harvard Extension Website – Had 2 years experience working remotely from home – “Able to work effectively in a team environment” – “Strong communication skills” What makes one person truly special over another? That’s what everyone wants to know. And if you want to get hired, especially these days … you have to show them. Why Most Candidates Fail Let’s break down what happens in most interviews. They go like this: Candidate walks in, sits down, fumbles through their notes, tries to remember the company name… and then launches into something generic about how excited they are to be there, learn new things, meet great people, etc. Then they launch into whatever rehearsed answers they practiced with a recruiter, maybe mentioning a few buzzwords here and there. Maybe they even pretend to understand some industry jargon that sounds impressive but means nothing specific. Sound familiar? It should. Because almost everyone does this. The thing is, this approach doesn’t reveal anything unique about YOU. All it shows is that you followed the standard playbook without putting any thought into who you really are, what you bring to the table, or how you’d specifically crush it at this role.", "repaired": "So I was recently asked for some thoughts on how to stand out at an interview. It’s not that hard — just do your research and be honest. But many folks are struggling these days, so maybe it’s time to update my list:\nKnow every single thing you can about the company before the interview\n. This is #1 because almost no one does this well enough. Go through their social media. Read all their recent press releases. Research their competition. Know who their top competitors are, what they look like, etc. There’s zero reason you shouldn’t know more than the CEO here. Be ready to talk to anyone, including the janitor or maintenance guy\n. They might end up being your boss someday. Even if they aren’t now. Bring references ahead of time, but also have people they don’t expect\n. Everyone knows how to bring 2-3 great LinkedIn recommendations. That’s easy. The best candidates will also find someone good that isn’t on LinkedIn, e.g., a VP at a prior company where they were successful. Or even better — the CEO themselves. Don’t hide anything\n. If you got fired from your last job? Own it. Why did you get fired? What would you do differently today? Etc. Just own it. And don’t make excuses. In fact, as long as you’re honest, most founders will give you points for owning your mistakes. Show up early\n. If nothing else, show respect by showing up 10 minutes early. And dress up\n. Yes, everyone dresses casually these days. No matter what, dress business casual. Better yet, dress up. Show respect. Share what you’ve done and learned (and love) in your current role\n. Make sure it’s relevant, however, to the new role. Do not simply read off your resume."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 588, "lemkin-clone-v5": 120, "lemkin-clone-v6": 194, "cleaned": 141, "balanced": 320, "r32": 299, "repaired": 299}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**UNLEASH YOUR INNER ROCKSTAR IN ANY JOB INTERVIEW**\n\nHow many times have you walked out of an interview feeling like you absolutely crushed it... only to not get the job? It happens to even the best of us.\n\nBut today, I want to share with you a secret that will make you stand out in any job interview - from SDR (Sales Development Representative) all the way up to COO (Chief Operating Officer).\n\nIt's simple: **be prepared to tell your story**.\n\nThink about it. In every single interview, there are three things I want to know:\n\n1. What have you achieved?\n2. How did you achieve it?\n3. Why does this matter?\n\nWhen I ask these questions, most people respond with a bland recitation of their job duties or company accomplishments. But that's not what I'm looking for.\n\nWhat I really want is a story - one that shows me how your unique skills and experiences can help drive real results in our organization.\n\nSo here's the challenge: **before you walk into any interview**, take 30 minutes to write down three stories like this:\n\n* The time you successfully closed a difficult deal\n* How you improved sales performance by X%\n* What innovation you brought to your previous company\n\nThen, practice telling these stories with passion and conviction. Because when I ask those same questions in an interview... **I want to hear the story**.\n\nWant to know what separates top performers from good ones? It's not just about having a great resume", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we're back with another SaaStr Classic. A deep dive on how to truly stand out in any job interview, from SDRs all the way up to CRO and even COO.\n\nThere are a million ways to do it wrong. But there's just one or two really effective ways to do it right:\n\n1️⃣ Be 10x better than you'd be at any other company. This is #2 in my best of SaaStr content\n2️⃣ Take the job even if they don't offer you a ton more money, and especially if there's less equity.\n\nI've seen this work many times when it seems impossible\n\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is back!!✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 7, 2023\n2️⃣ The job is the offer. This means you have to accept a job at least with an understanding that this will be your next role.\n\nIf they don't make it official -- find out why and fix it\n\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is back!!✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 7, 2023\n3️⃣ Be honest about how you got to where you are. I've seen this work many times when the candidate was almost a ghost. Not enough, but sometimes it's enough.\n\nThe more you can be honest here -- and talk about any flaws in your background or job history, the better. The more they'll trust you", "lemkin-clone-v6": "If you really want that job — stand out.\n\nA simple list. If you can’t do these things, maybe it’s time for a change:\n\nKnow your competition cold. No matter what role you’re interviewing for. Know them cold. They know the space better than you in this moment.\n\nBe honest about every bad decision you made at work. Not just excuses or blaming others. Be honest.\n\nFind 2-3 customers to meet with that will vouch for you, even if it’s not a customer they’ve worked with before. Even if it takes longer.\n\nKnow your job cold — the job description from this interview and how you can truly deliver on most of it today. Today. Not someday or maybe.\n\nBe honest about every bad decision you made at work.", "cleaned": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO The best candidates always stand out during interviews. Not because they’re arrogant or try too hard—it’s just that they’ve done their homework and are genuinely excited about what you do. Here’s how I’d approach it as an interviewee: First, Show Enthusiasm for What They Do Ask thoughtful questions like “What gets you most fired up about this company?” Don’t ask generic stuff like “What does the team work on?”—that shows you haven’t put any effort into understanding who they actually are. Instead, dig deeper. If they say something like “we’re building AI-powered tools,” your follow-up should be specific: “That sounds cool. Can you tell me more about how you see AI changing your industry?” This isn’t just small talk; it’s research. Second, Be Honest About Why You Want It Next, show them why you want the job specifically. For example, if you’re interviewing at a tech company but your background is in sales, don’t pretend you know everything about coding. Just be honest: “I’m not sure I could write code myself, but I love working with developers.” That shows humility and honesty. Third, Share Your Own Stories Finally, share stories from your own career where you overcame challenges similar to theirs. If they mention a tough project they worked on recently, think back to your own experiences. Did you once build a tool from scratch? Did you handle a difficult customer situation well?", "balanced": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, From SDR to CRO: • Have 1 or more great references ready. This always helps. Ask your boss(es) that are still at the company if they’ll do you a reference. • Know how you’d crush it in Year 2, too. Don’t just talk about what you did — but also what you’re going to do next year. And be super specific on exactly why and where. • Do research on the competition. Especially as you scale past Initial Traction, most candidates don’t know their competition all that well. You have to learn this now. • Learn the product cold. If you can truly sell the product yourself during an interview, you will stand out. At least, you won't sound like every other candidate. More here -> https://saastr.com/7-ways-to-stand-out-in-an-interview-for-a-junior-sales-role-or-any-entry-level-position/ …", "r32": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO Do this one thing and you'll stand out. And probably get the job. Ask them what they are doing wrong — and how you can help solve that problem", "repaired": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO The best way I've ever seen anyone truly stand out during an interview is by sending over some great work ahead of time. Not just your resume and cover letter (although those are important too). But something that shows you're already thinking like someone who's going to crush this role. For junior roles, send over 10-20 good emails you'd write as an SDR or AE. If they are well written, clear, and concise — it will show them you know how to do it. And if you can also tell me why these emails would be effective for our company specifically... man. That'll really make my jaw drop. For mid-level roles, send over a deck on what you think we should focus on next year. It doesn't have to be perfect, but it has to show you've done your homework. You need to show the hiring manager that you understand their challenges AND you have ideas to solve them. For senior roles, go deep. Send over a real strategy document - not just talking points. Show me exactly how you'd approach the biggest challenge facing the business right now. Be specific, back up your claims with data, and don't be afraid to say \"I'm not sure\" when you're outside your expertise. Whatever level you're at, include concrete examples of things you've accomplished in similar situations. Don't just talk about it - prove it with numbers and stories. If you can get that across before even sitting down, you're not only standing out - you"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 263, "lemkin-clone-v5": 220, "lemkin-clone-v6": 129, "cleaned": 246, "balanced": 137, "r32": 42, "repaired": 267}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X-style post:\n\n**THE #1 SECRET TO STANDING OUT IN ANY JOB INTERVIEW**\n\nI've been in this game for over 20 years now. And I've interviewed hundreds of candidates for every level - from SDRs all the way up to C-level.\n\nAnd let me tell you, most people still get it WRONG when it comes to standing out in an interview.\n\nHere's what separates the champions from the chumps:\n\n**IT'S NOT ABOUT \"ANSWERING THE QUESTIONS\"**\n\nEveryone can prepare answers. It's not rocket science (although I've seen some prep-work that borders on it).\n\n**IT'S ABOUT TELLING A STORY**\n\nYou want to tell a story about yourself, your past experiences, and how they relate to the role you", "lemkin-clone-v5": "How To Really Stand Out In a Job Interview. If you can’t, maybe it’s not for you.\n\n#1. Bring 10+ Stories Of Your Real Successes\nI don’t care if they are real or fake — I want to hear them all. And then ask me more about each one. What happened next? How did we do on the follow-up? More here:\n\nThe #1 Job Interview Question You Must Always Be Prepared To Answer: “What Are Your Greatest Successes?” – SaaStr\n\n#2. Do Your Homework\nKnow exactly what you are going to talk about, and why. Know every single competitor in your space cold. And know how they stack up against each other. More here:\n\n5 Simple Tips on How To Truly Stand Out in a Job", "lemkin-clone-v6": "how do you stand out so many folks that have been around for a while they've gone up the ladder and then back down are they great they're super smart but how do you really stand out in any job interview it's not rocket science here's what I'd tell you if you want to truly stand out be better than before come into an interview being 10% better as an SDR or better than ever before is the only thing that matters come into this interview and say I've got a million new prospects coming in next month my outbound is up 50%, pipeline's growing, leads are going up, whatever it was last time be 1% to 3.5% better every single time at least when you're doing outbound or inbound sales you just have to do that much more", "cleaned": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "balanced": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "r32": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "repaired": "How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 118, "lemkin-clone-v5": 126, "lemkin-clone-v6": 139, "cleaned": 13, "balanced": 13, "r32": 13, "repaired": 13}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"How to Truly Stand Out in Any Job Interview, from SDR to COO\"\n\n**[Introduction]**\n\nHello everyone! As many of you know, I've had the privilege of interviewing thousands of candidates over the years. From Sales Development Representatives (SDRs) all the way up to Chief Operating Officers (COOs), I've seen it all. And let me tell you, most job interviews are... unremarkable.\n\nBut today, we're going to change that. Today, we're going to talk about how to truly stand out in any job interview. Whether you're a junior salesperson or the C-suite executive, these principles will help you separate yourself from the crowd and land your dream job.\n\n**[The Problem with Traditional Interview Prep]**\n\nWe've all been there - studying for hours on end, memorizing answers to common interview questions like \"What's your greatest weakness?\" or \"Why do you want to work here?\"\n\nNewsflash: this approach is not only boring, but it's also ineffective. In fact, I'd argue that most traditional interview prep strategies are more likely to make you seem... generic.\n\nThink about it - every candidate has a version of the same answers to these questions. So what sets them apart? What makes one person truly stand out?\n\n**[The Key to Standing Out]**\n\nHere's the secret: it's not just about answering the right questions; it's about showing your passion, creativity, and unique perspective.\n\nWhen I interview candidates, I'm looking for three things:\n\n1. **Domain expertise**: How well do you understand our business, industry, or technology?\n2. **Storytelling ability**: Can you tell me a compelling story that shows how your skills will drive results in this role?\n3. **Authenticity**: Are you being genuine and true to yourself, without trying to fit into some predetermined mold?\n\n**[Examples of Standing Out]**\n\nLet's take an example from our SDR team. One candidate stood out because she had created a spreadsheet that tracked the performance of every sales email sent by her company over the past quarter.\n\nShe explained how she'd analyzed the data and found correlations between certain subject lines, sender names, and open rates. She even offered to share the findings with me and discuss ways we could improve our outreach strategy!\n\nNow, most people would have just talked about \"being good at sales\" or \"having a strong work ethic.\" But not this candidate - she showed up prepared with actual insights that added value to the conversation.\n\n**[The 3-Step Process for Standing Out]**\n\nSo how can you apply these principles and truly stand out in any job interview? Here's my three-step process:\n\n1. **Research, research, research**: Dive deep into our company culture, products, services, customers - everything.\n2. **Find your hook**: Identify what makes you unique and how that aligns with the role or company mission. Then, craft a compelling story around it.\n3. **Be authentic**: Don't try to fit in; be yourself. Show genuine enthusiasm for the role and opportunity.\n\n**[Conclusion]**\n\nStanding out in any job interview requires more than just memorizing answers or regurgitating your resume. It takes creativity, passion, and authenticity.\n\nRemember: you're not just interviewing for a job - you're joining our team, contributing to our mission, and making an impact on the world.\n\nSo go ahead, take risks, show me what makes you special. I promise it'll make all the difference in your next interview!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So I thought it was time for another fun SaaStr post on how you can truly stand out in any job interview. From SDR to CRO, and maybe even CEO (just kidding).\nHow do we truly stand out?  How can we be the best candidate?\nHere’s my top list of 10 things that will make you really stand out. And don’t worry — they are all accessible:\n1. Come In With a Lot of Great Questions\nYou have to come in with at least one or two great questions, no matter where you apply.\nBut this is so hard for many folks these days. They want it all spelled out on the job description, and then they just go into interview mode without really asking any new questions.\nDon’t do that!  Come up with some of your own ideas to learn more about them, their problems, challenges, etc.  Even if you are interviewing for a junior role — there’s always something here.  A lot of folks get this wrong these days.  Go in and just ask the best questions.\n2. Do Your Homework\nYou have to come in knowing the company cold. And not just on the surface level. Make sure you know their product, their competitors, their mission, their values — everything! This will show your passion for the role and your willingness to go above and beyond.\n3. Be Honest About Any Gaps\nEveryone has gaps these days.  Don’t hide them or make excuses about them. Just own up to them and tell a story of how you learned from them.\n4. Show You Can Learn From Mistakes\nYou are going to screw it up, eventually.  Every single person in the world makes mistakes.  The best folks can learn quickly — even if they make the same mistake twice (and maybe three times).\n5.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we've had so many great guests on SaaStr this year. But I wanted to do one more deep dive on something that's perhaps less obvious but super valuable. And it is:\nHow To Truly Stand Out In An Interview\nI'm going to share some of the top lessons and tips from our best interviews, from CROs and VPs of Sales all the way up to CEOs.\nAnd you can apply these tips not just when interviewing for a new job — but even if you're looking to get promoted internally. Because in many ways it's much harder than finding your first startup or SaaS job. So let's dive into our top takeaways on what makes an interview truly great, from the CROs and VPs of Sales all the way up to CEOs.\n1. Know Everything About The Company, Even Better Than Most Founders\nThis is probably one of my favorite learnings over the years. If you really know your stuff about a company — especially if it's early stage or growing fast, which in many cases will be true for any SaaS startup today that doesn't have 100+ million in revenue at least — then you can do so much better than most interviewers.\nYou can learn more here:\nHow to Research an Unusual Startup\nI did this with one of my favorite interviews with the CRO from HubSpot. I asked him a ton about how they sell, and he just got it all right, even some things that were almost wrong in the media. And then when I interviewed the CEO later on, I actually asked him to describe his ideal VP Sales candidate, and what he was looking for — in detail.\nHe had no idea. He basically only cared about one thing: did they have any experience with HubSpot? That's all that mattered.", "cleaned": "I know this is obvious but so many folks don't do it. Ask for coffee or ask for lunch with anyone on the team that they want to work at. That's what you're looking for. You are looking for someone where there's someplace on their resume where I worked at this great startup and then I went to another one and I've been running my own business since then and then I joined this other company as CMO because I wanted to be back in an environment like this. Um uh okay last point just um related to this um and and this really ties into all these points which is you can't fake it. Okay. If you think being part of a hot AI deal is important, if you think being part of something big today, it has to be true. It has to be true. And people will smell it out if you come in pretending to have skills, experience, passion, whatever, when you actually don't. So here's your job. Your job as a candidate is to find the perfect role for you. Not necessarily the dream role, not the dream CEO or dream founder or anything. But figure out who would love to hire you tomorrow based on everything we talked about. Be honest. Do those three things. Show up prepared. Share two things you truly did well before. Have done research on why you'd kill it. And you'll stand out instantly. In fact, even better than that, you won't need any crutches. You won't need to pretend you're interested in product management or sales or marketing or engineering when you aren't. You won't need to make stuff up. You won't need to exaggerate your background or how long you were somewhere or how good a rep was. The truth always stands out in interviews. So, be yourself, show up prepared, share two things you've done really well before, and you will stand out in every single interview you go into.", "balanced": "and then I had this crazy experience where I worked with a founder that was literally going bankrupt every day for like two years and it was insane because there's no way you can predict how much money your startup is going to make one month to the next so we were at bankruptcy meeting after bankruptcy meeting every single week for two years and what happened was the board would come into these meetings they'd say hey look we're all very disappointed but as long as you can show us that cash flow curve that shows our burn rate coming down each quarter um that gives me hope I have more time right now if I see that you know maybe my cash will last six months instead of four I might give you another extension or whatever okay great so let's get to work on that slide and then he'd go back to his office and he would change his mind that he didn't want to extend any longer because it was too risky yeah well that happens so many times you don't realize that until you've been through it and I'm just sharing this story I think because I think it helps people understand why you need someone to help the founders manage risk and again when you do that folks that are good at managing risk they'll ask really good questions and they'll bring up issues ahead of time and they'll be proactive um and they will not only stand out they will add value because everyone else will be reactive they won't know they should worry about things that happen three weeks later right and hopefully by listening to this video you're thinking of some ideas uh on how to make yourself a candidate that truly adds value whether you're an sdr or a director or a coo or anything in between thank you everybody thanks for listening", "r32": "and that's just a little bit of magic and I'm not saying you're going to get this job if you don't have this but if your interviewer is excited they'll find an excuse for you so it's very rare they can say no um but what do I want them to be excited about well let me give you some ideas on how to stand out as an sdr because boy there are so many these days okay number one thing which I see way too few folks do especially early stage sales professionals who haven't sold before which is to read their email and send thoughtful responses back within minutes or hours if possible when someone emails you ask anyone at any startup what does our team look like the first time someone sends me an email asking me about my role about working here and I respond within three seconds they will be impressed they will be impressed we all love great customer service and it's even more important today than ever with AI agents handling everything else right now everyone wants to work for companies that really deliver good product value good features fast and responsive support today's customers expect much better response times faster answers to problems than ever before so why shouldn't they expect the same thing from sales so if someone reaches out to you whether it's on LinkedIn or whatever channel or via cold email or warm email tell them you got it read it and send a thoughtful response back uh people will be blown away by this and the best ones will be doing it anyway because they know it works and then the second part of this is to go deep learn as much as you possibly can about the company learn everything you can and share it during the interview again most candidates don't do this you will be shocked the amount of information that isn't known", "repaired": "to tell me what you've done and why it's relevant but if you do that I'm gonna make sure your career takes off we're gonna work together okay let's go find that next role for you so thanks everybody and uh be great see everyone later yeah bye bye [Music] you"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 563, "lemkin-clone-v5": 306, "lemkin-clone-v6": 307, "cleaned": 334, "balanced": 323, "r32": 328, "repaired": 51}}}}, {"id": "gold_004", "topic": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer uses Mark Roberge as a hook to talk about disciplined scale: doubling down only works when the core motion already has evidence, metrics, and a repeatable playbook behind it. The best version should explain how founders know what to double down on, what signals matter, and why premature scaling remains the bigger risk.", "facts": ["The answer should extract at least one scaling lesson tied to Mark Roberge's operator/investor perspective.", "It should connect growth-stage execution to repeatability, metrics, or hiring discipline.", "It should explain what 'doubling down' means in operational terms rather than treating it as a slogan."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Doubling Down: Mark Roberge**\n\nI'm excited to share another \"doubling down\" story with you today. This one's a big deal.\n\nMark Roberge is the co-founder and managing director of Stage 2 Capital, a venture capital firm that invests in growth-stage companies. But what I want to talk about isn't just his background or his fund - it's the fact that Mark has been doubling down on entrepreneurship since he was an undergrad at Duke.\n\n**The Early Days**\n\nMark started his first company while still in college. He co-founded a consulting firm, and then went on to found another startup after graduation. But what really caught my attention is when I learned about his time as the CMO of Karmaloop - one of the pioneers of e-commerce for young adults.\n\n**Scaling at HubSpot**\n\nFast forward to 2005, Mark joined HubSpot (then a small startup) and became their first-ever sales hire. He scaled the sales team from zero to $100 million in revenue in just five years. That's not an easy feat - especially when you consider that he had no prior experience as a sales leader.\n\nMark has spoken publicly about how he learned on the job, bootstrapping his way through every step of the process. And it paid off: HubSpot went public in 2015 and Mark was a key part of their growth story.\n\n**Doubling Down**\n\nAfter leaving HubSpot, Mark co-founded Stage 2 Capital with Alex Bard (another former HubSpot exec). Their fund invests in companies that are poised for significant growth - just like the ones Mark helped scale at HubSpot.\n\nWhat I love about Mark is his focus on building a new kind of VC firm. One that's centered around partnering closely with entrepreneurs, rather than just throwing money at them. It's all about creating value together - something that resonates deeply with me and our own SaaStr community.\n\n**The Takeaway**\n\nMark Roberge's story is one for the ages: from college startup to scaling a $100 million business in five years (as CMO of HubSpot), and now leading a venture capital firm focused on growth-stage companies. He's doubling down on his passions, using everything he learned along the way to create value for others.\n\nSo what can we learn from Mark? For one thing: don't be afraid to take risks early in your career (especially when you're still figuring things out). Second, be willing to put in the work and learn as you go - it's not always easy, but it's often the fastest path forward. And finally: find a way to double down on what you love.\n\nMark Roberge is doing just that at Stage 2 Capital, and I couldn't be more excited about their future.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So our latest podcast with Mark Roberge, co-founder of HubSpot is here:\nMark was so generous to do this second time around. The first interview back in the day (2009) is\nhere\nand still holds up well today.\nRelated Posts\nNew SaaStr Europa Speakers!! Check Out Our New Sponsor: Stage 2 Capital\nSaaStr Podcast #035 with Mark Roberge, HubSpot VP of Sales on Scaling from $1m to $100m in ARR.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I’ve been lucky enough to do several deep dives with top SaaS CEOs on “SaaStr Deep Dives” over years:\n– HubSpot CEO Brian Halligan\n– Box CEO Aaron Levie\n– Slack CEO Stewart Butterfield\n– ZoomInfo CEO Henry Schuck\n– Asana CEO Dustin Moskovitz\n– MongoDB CEO Dev Ittycheria\n– PagerDuty CEO Jennifer Tejada\n– and many more!\nBut we don’t always do a Deep Dive with the CEOs of SaaS companies that are still growing. There’s just so much to learn from them! And when you’re doubling down, it’s not as easy.\nSo I asked Mark Roberge, CRO at HubSpot and now Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital — which is backing several top B2B companies in the $10m-$50m ARR range (think of them a lot like Bessemer Venture Partners) — to do one. And it was great! A Deep Dive on Doubling Down\nThe Reality:  It’s Harder and More Risky Than Ever Before.\nYou can’t just double down anymore these days in SaaS. Not when growth is so darn hard, especially once you get past $100m ARR or even earlier. The rules have changed since 2020. And the markets have changed too.\nBut if you want to go all-in on something that’s working — it still works. It always does. Just do your homework first. A Deep Dive with Mark Roberge, Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: What I Learned Selling My Company to Adobe Sign / EchoSign; How to Double Down in Tough Times\nNew SaaStr University FREE Course: \"How To Double Down with Your Co-Founder\"", "cleaned": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had a great one last time with David Frankel, General Partner @ Founders Circle Capital. Check that out here. This week we’re focusing on Mark Roberge, co-founder and managing director at Stage 2 Capital!", "balanced": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had a great one last week with Nick Shalek, General Partner at Tiger Global! This week we have Mark Roberge, Co-founder & Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital!!", "r32": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had a great one last week with David Frankel, Partner at NextEquity Partners check it out here. This week we’re focusing on Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital!", "repaired": "“Doubling Down” is a new series where we hear from top B2B SaaS investors on their most recent activities and takes on the current market. We had a great one last week with Paul Haensel, Founder & General Partner at Innovation Endeavors. Check that out\nhere\n.\nThis Week we have Mark Roberge, Co-founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital!"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 449, "lemkin-clone-v5": 70, "lemkin-clone-v6": 273, "cleaned": 60, "balanced": 54, "r32": 58, "repaired": 61}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible LinkedIn post:\n\n**Doubling Down on Greatness**\n\nI just had an incredible conversation with my friend @MarkRoberge, co-founder and managing director at @Stage2Capital.\n\nAs many of you know, Mark is one of the most successful SaaS founders out there. He built HubSpot from scratch to a $100 million+ company before its IPO. And since then, he's helped countless other entrepreneurs achieve similar success through his investing and operating expertise at Stage 2 Capital.\n\nBut what I want to share with you today isn't just about Mark's impressive track record (although that's certainly worth noting). It's about the principles behind his approach to building great companies - an approach that is simply unmatched in our industry.\n\nFor those who don't know, Mark has a very clear philosophy: **doubling down** on what works. He doesn't get distracted by shiny objects or trendy ideas. Instead, he focuses on executing and iterating on proven strategies that drive real growth.\n\nAs I reflected on our conversation, it struck me that this principle is just as relevant to individual career success as it is to building a successful company. How many of us are guilty of chasing the next hot thing, rather than doubling down on what we know works?\n\nMark's approach reminds me of my own mantra: **focus on what you're great at** and keep getting better at it.\n\nSo here's your challenge for today:\n\nWhat is one area where you can double down - focus on what you already do well, and commit to making yourself even more exceptional in that space? Who", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Doubling Down with Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital\nMark is one my earliest mentors. He was CRO at Hubspot when I first got to know him, then he went on to become the VP of Sales. Then later, a key investor in SaaStr Fund. And now as co-founder and managing director of Stage 2 Capital.\nAnd so much more:\nHe wrote the book “Inbound Marketing: Attract, Engage, Nurture, Profit” with Brian Halligan which is on many top lists\nA pioneer in inbound marketing\nFirst ever VP Sales at Hubspot (2007-2010)\nCo-authored “Inbound Organization”\nBoard member of Marketo and CRO for several years before their $4B+ acquisition by Adobe\nCRO of Demandware from 2011 until Salesforce acquired them for $2.8 billion in 2016.\nHe’s also an incredible, down-to-earth guy to talk with on so many things in SaaS. I’ve asked him for advice and investment time since the early days of SaaStr Fund. And it always ends up being super valuable.\nA great podcast!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Doubling down with Mark Roberge\nMark Roberge is one of my top five favorite SaaS founders. I met him at Dreamforce, where he was literally on the floor running a booth for HubSpot as it went from $1M to $100m in ARR. And then when we invested together in RevenueCat (now Vercel), Mark joined our board and helped me with a whole bunch of stuff that just didn’t make sense at first.\nHe’s now managing director at Stage 2 Capital, which I also co-founded. It invests from about $1m to about $10m in ARR. And he can help you if you are in the SaaS space — and even more importantly, have done a lot of it yourself already.\nMark is hands-down one of the most thoughtful founders I’ve ever had the pleasure to work with. He’s an ex-VP at HubSpot where he built the sales org that scaled from $1m ARR to $100m+ in ARR! And now, as managing director at Stage 2 Capital — Mark can help you scale it all again!\nIf you need a thoughtful partner on your journey from $10M-$50M+ ARR and have done some of this before … he’s got one of the best track records.", "cleaned": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital Mark is the former Chief Revenue Officer at HubSpot. Under his leadership, they scaled from $10M to over $1B in ARR with 4000+ employees worldwide. He's now investing at Stage 2 Capital where he focuses on B2B software companies. His portfolio includes Rippling (which raised $300M at a $10B valuation), Salesloft ($800M acquisition by Vista), and Pipedrive. Prior to HubSpot, he was a VP at SuccessFactors, which sold for $6B. Mark has authored two books: \"Predictable Revenue\" (2011) and \"Revenue Leadership Playbook\" (2019). Both are focused on scaling sales organizations efficiently. He graduated from MIT Sloan School of Management.", "balanced": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital \"The best salespeople are naturally curious and love to learn.\" @Stage2Capital @MarkRoberge @SaaStrFund https://saastr.com/doubling-down-mark-roberge-co-founder-managing-director-at-stage-2-capital/ …", "r32": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital The SaaStr podcast is back with another episode from their “Doubling Down” series! This week’s guest is Mark Roberge, co-founder and managing director of Stage 2 Capital. Before joining venture capital, Mark spent his career scaling high-performing sales teams for organizations such as HubSpot, where he was one of the first employees hired after graduation and helped build the company into a successful IPO candidate. He also served as a lecturer at Harvard Business School, teaching entrepreneurship and marketing courses to students in the MBA program and executive education programs. During his time on campus, Roberge earned several awards including the Gail E. Hamilton Award for Excellence in Teaching by the Harvard Business Publishing Group and was named Faculty Member of the Year by the Harvard Business School Student Association. Additionally, he has been recognized by Entrepreneur Magazine as one of the top 25 most influential people in Sales. Prior to attending business school, Roberge began his professional journey working in various roles within financial services companies before transitioning into entrepreneurship through early-stage ventures focused primarily on technology solutions. In this latest episode, we’re discussing how to scale your go-to-market motion effectively while maintaining focus on customer relationships, the importance of clear product positioning, and strategies for building strong leadership teams. Key Takeaways: * Scaling Your Go-To-Market Motion While Maintaining Customer Focus: * Establish Clear Metrics and Goals: Start by setting measurable goals that align with both individual and organizational objectives, ensuring everyone understands what success looks like.", "repaired": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital 🚀 The Best Sales Reps Are Also Great Engineers. Just Ask Salesforce. “The best sales reps are also great engineers,” says Mark Roberge, co-founder and managing director of early-stage venture capital firm Stage 2 Capital and former head of sales at HubSpot. But it’s not just about product knowledge – these reps have an innate ability to understand how customers think, communicate, and buy. For B2B companies looking for that next-generation sales team, Roberge identifies five key traits: being technically proficient, having strong communication skills, being analytical thinkers, working well with others, and being able to articulate value propositions effectively. His latest book, “SaaS Selling”, is available now."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 261, "lemkin-clone-v5": 168, "lemkin-clone-v6": 202, "cleaned": 111, "balanced": 27, "r32": 260, "repaired": 120}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**X Post**\n\nI just spent two hours on a flight with @MarkRoberge (yes, THAT Mark Roberge). I asked him about his latest investment thesis. Here's what I learned...\n\n Mark and Stage 2 Capital are doubling down on **SaaS founders who can scale to $100M+ ARR**.\n\nHe said: \"We're not looking for just another SaaS company... we need the next Salesforce, Nextdoor, or Zoom\". And then he dropped a bombshell:\n\n\"We want founders with a clear vision for 10x growth. We don't care about 'adjacency' or 'disruption'. We only invest in companies that can become **TRUE CATEGORY LEADERS**\".\n\nI", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Doubling Down with Mark Roberge on Scaling From $1M to $100M ARR: Lessons from Salesforce, HubSpot & The Toughest Sales Journey in SaaS History. Join me for the latest episode of Doubling Down where we dive into:\nThe #1 thing that differentiates great sales leaders\nWhy building a strong foundation is key before scaling up your sales team\nWhat to look for when hiring a VP of Sales and how Mark evaluates their potential success\nHow to create a win-win situation in business partnerships, even if they seem one-sided at first glance\nAnd much more! Listen on Spotify here:", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I first met Mark Roberge as a young VP of Sales at HubSpot. He was already doing so much back then but he had this incredible drive to help the sales team that I'd never seen before.\n\nAnd boy did it pay off, from $0 to $1B in ARR with 5+ years of explosive growth!\n\nFast forward to today and Mark is now a General Partner at Stage2 Capital where his focus remains on scaling high-growth B2B companies through strategic partnerships & investments.", "cleaned": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "balanced": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "r32": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "repaired": "Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 109, "lemkin-clone-v5": 99, "lemkin-clone-v6": 83, "cleaned": 12, "balanced": 12, "r32": 12, "repaired": 12}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Doubling Down: Mark Roberge, Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt to write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"Doubling Down: Why I Invested with Mark Roberge\"\n\n**[Opening music plays as I walk on stage]**\n\nHello everyone, thanks for joining me today. Today we're going to talk about something that I'm really passionate about - doubling down on entrepreneurs who are making a real impact.\n\nI want to introduce you to one of the most impressive investors in our ecosystem, Mark Roberge. As Co-Founder and Managing Director at Stage 2 Capital, he's leading the charge for his firm's growth-stage investments. But what I want to share with you today is why I chose to double down on Mark after meeting him.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nYou see, when I first met Mark, I was blown away by his conviction and vision. He had a clear thesis on where the market was headed, and he wasn't afraid to bet big on it. We started talking about some of the companies in his portfolio, and I was struck by their quality - these were not just any startups; they were game-changers.\n\nBut what really impressed me was Mark's ability to balance boldness with nuance. He understands that growth-stage investing is as much an art as a science, and he approaches each investment with the precision of a surgeon. I mean this literally: during our conversations, he would dissect every aspect of a company's business model, identifying areas where they could improve margins or accelerate revenue growth.\n\n**[Pause for comedic effect]**\n\nNow, some of you might be thinking, \"Okay, Jason, why should we care about Mark Roberge?\" Well, let me tell you - this guy is the real deal. With his experience at HubSpot and his time as a professor at Harvard Business School, he's got a unique combination of operational expertise and academic rigor that's hard to find anywhere else.\n\nBut what I love most about Mark is his passion for supporting entrepreneurs who are changing the world. He knows how difficult it can be to build a successful company from scratch, and he's dedicated to providing guidance and resources to help them scale.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nSo why did I choose to double down on Mark? For me, it came down to one thing: conviction. This guy truly believes in the potential of his portfolio companies, and that confidence is infectious. When you talk to him about a particular investment, he'll tell you exactly how he thinks they're going to disrupt their market - no sugarcoating.\n\nI've made my fair share of mistakes as an investor over the years, but I can confidently say that Mark Roberge has earned his stripes. He's not just throwing darts at startups; he's carefully selecting companies with a clear path to success.\n\n**[Closing music plays]**\n\nSo if you're looking for someone who embodies the spirit of growth-stage investing - and the qualities that make our ecosystem so special - look no further than Mark Roberge. I'm proud to call him an ally, and I hope this talk has given you a glimpse into what it's like to work with one of the best in the business.\n\nThanks for listening!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "hello everybody we have an amazing speaker today mark rober is co founder managing director at stage two capital now i've known mark for years back when he was vp sales at hubspot the first public sas company in fact maybe even before that because i'm pretty sure i invested in it too but what's interesting about Mark is Mark and his team are just doubling down on their work with HubSpot they're not retiring or moving to a new fund here they're doubling down so much so they've got an incredible story of scaling the sales org at hubspot from 20k to $10 million in revenue in four years I'm going to let mark tell it and then we'll get into some great questions", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we did an epic AMA with co-founder of HubSpot. It was just great. And I asked him one question that got me thinking on what we could do for this session today. How does a founder know if they've doubled down too much? Because it's so hard to cut back once you have the vision. So Mark, thanks for joining us and sharing your thoughts! And let's get into some of the amazing insights from doubling down, as well as some other learnings in sales that we can take away.\n\nA little bit about HubSpot, and then I'll turn it over to you.\n\nMark, great to have you back on SaaStr. You're a co-founder of Hubspot with Brian Halligan. And Mark is the current Chief Operating Officer at HubSpot, where he oversees all aspects of go-to-market strategy. He's also an adjunct professor in entrepreneurship and innovation at Boston University's Questrom School for Business.\n\nAnd now you run Stage 2 Capital, so thanks for being here today to share your insights!\n\nSo let's start with doubling down. As a founder how do you know if you've doubled down too much? I think as founders we're all guilty of it because when you see something that works, and you have the vision, you just want to go further, right?\n\nBut what happens then is you start losing focus on things that are working well in your business. So how do you know if you've doubled down too much? I think we might be able to dig a little bit deeper into some of those insights here today.\n\nSo let's first talk about the importance of doubling down as a founder, and then maybe what happens when it becomes too late or not early enough.", "cleaned": "because you're like i need to go do that so we have an incredible team with over 100 million dollars under management um but really focused on being hands-on investors and uh our goal is to be one of your most trusted advisors as you navigate all stages from seed to scale-up so mark welcome thanks for having me yeah so let's take it back in time how did you get started well this is probably a question that has been asked more times than any other in my life um but i'll try to give a little bit different perspective because i think people are interested in the personal journey right now what happened was after graduating college i went straight into sales i had no idea what else to do there were no startups when i graduated college there weren't even tech companies so i just went into sales uh i didn't know anything about technology or software but i was good at learning quickly and i was able to work myself up through a few organizations and then i became an sdr and i worked my way up to become an ae i worked my way up to manager director vp cfo and then eventually president before deciding to leave the company and start something new so that experience of working myself up through the ranks of the organization gave me some unique insights which i'm gonna share later that allowed me to build the framework that would end up becoming a successful playbook for scaling sas businesses fast forward five years i had left my previous employer and launched a startup called insight squaresquare that ended up getting acquired by a public company for about 50 million dollars in cash and stock so during that time period i was building out this", "balanced": "I'm going to ask you some questions I asked everybody which is what's your number one mistake as Founders when you look back on it like many folks have written books and so I'll ask them this question and they all come up with different answers but I always find it interesting um and then we're gonna chat about SAS sales and marketing because you've done that for years um you were CBO uh from day zero until IPO at HubSpot today you are a managing director at stage two capital where you invest in SAS companies and you focus particularly on the go-to-market motion how did you do it before you started doing investing full time right well I mean my first experience was with Hubspot and that just kind of came out of me being an academic uh studying how these things work in practice versus theory and trying to apply those principles and I think if anything there's no difference between now and then or actually there's more similarities than differences in terms of mistakes that people make it's still just understanding who you're selling to and making sure you have enough resources to deliver value to them that's something that has been consistent across decades right and I I think sometimes it can be tough to learn this lesson because people get excited about their product because they want to believe that everyone will love their product that everyone wants to buy their product and I I think that's why people struggle with go-to-market strategy because they overestimate demand right yeah exactly and so I think that's what makes it hard especially in early-stage startups is figuring out how much money do you need to spend to create this demand so that you could sell your product and I don't know", "r32": "hey everybody i wanted to take you back on saster for our first doubling down session with mark r borge who is now managing director at stage two capital but before that he was cto of salesforce um and then also did this incredible job as vp of sales for hubspot so we talked a lot about how they went from 20 million to a billion in arr and beyond what's interesting is he joined when it was much smaller than even today so we got into some great insights around scaling early and scaling later and how different those are uh if you're going to grow like hubspot or salesforce you've gotta do both you have to scale your early days and then find ways to scale your middle days and then find ways to go big and really kind of change the world together okay let's dig in yeah hi everyone thanks for joining me again jason lekens here we are talking about doubling down series where we hear from successful founders in their journeys to success and i'm excited to be back i had fun last time sharing my story and hearing stories from other folks so thank you for having me hey thanks for coming back i appreciate it look you know i think people will listen more because you were pretty direct on sort of the mistakes that you made along the way right yeah well hopefully someone can learn from them because there's no point in repeating the same mistakes over and over again yeah you'll never make all the same mistakes twice but you may make similar ones just in a slightly different context so hopefully if we can share these experiences maybe others won't follow the same path if they see it coming up ahead of time yeah yeah definitely so one thing i want to ask you is you", "repaired": "um i'm sure it's very interesting to hear from you what you're seeing today because obviously there are a lot more startups than there were when you started stage two capital so maybe we can jump right into that but just let me ask one question before we do that if i could pick your brain for one minute is this is something you've written about but how much have things changed since march 15th or whenever it was um uh and not only has everything changed in the world outside of work but also inside of sales inside marketing inside product inside engineering inside customer success all those functions how much change have you seen in each of them even on saster we get some pretty good data from the sastra community itself how much change have you seen since march 20th or whatever date you want to use yeah so so march 1st is my answer okay march first like what happened after march first and then what happened during march second through april seventh that was a different time right now we have a different reality right so so the the big change which we're still navigating is the impact of remote work the shift from office based to distributed teams uh but again as soon as people figure out how to make distributed teams work they'll be just as effective as their colleagues who were already working remotely anyway so that's going to happen by may so by june our industry will look pretty similar to where it looked back in february with respect to productivity except that probably a higher percentage of folks will be able to work remotely permanently so that's a major change we're making adjustments in many cases to reduce travel and to focus on local markets so that's another change"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 528, "lemkin-clone-v5": 125, "lemkin-clone-v6": 293, "cleaned": 306, "balanced": 305, "r32": 318, "repaired": 309}}}}, {"id": "gold_005", "topic": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer frames Informatica as proof that major enterprise outcomes often take decades, multiple market cycles, and repeated reinvention. The lesson should be that enterprise value compounds when a company becomes critical infrastructure, and that founders often underestimate how long durable category leadership can take to build.", "facts": ["The answer should present Informatica as a long-duration enterprise software story rather than a quick-win startup story.", "It should mention the multiple liquidity events or strategic resets across decades.", "It should turn the timeline into a lesson about endurance, reinvention, or strategic value in enterprise software."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, and a $8B Exit**\n\n Ah, Informatica. What can I say? This company has been on one heckuva ride.\n\nAs you know, my passion is startup success stories – and the ones that truly fascinate me are those with an extraordinary journey to exit. And Informatica's story checks all the boxes.\n\nLet's take a step back in time... **1996**: That was the year Informatica founder and CEO, Girish Mathkbol, founded his company. Initially, it focused on data integration solutions – not exactly the sexiest space at that point. But Girish had vision. He saw an opportunity to make data integration easier, faster, and more accessible.\n\nFast forward a few years... **2001**: Informatica went public with its initial IPO (Initial Public Offering). The company raised $63 million in funding, valuing it at around $300 million. Not bad for a 5-year-old startup!\n\nNow, let's talk about the next phase of this wild ride...\n\n**Growth and Evolution**\n\nIn the early 2000s, Informatica began to experience tremendous growth. Its data integration platform started gaining traction with enterprises across various industries – from finance to healthcare. The company expanded its offerings to include master data management (MDM) and big data solutions.\n\nBut here's where things get interesting...\n\n**Second IPO: A Different Animal**\n\nIn 2015, Informatica went public again – this time raising $1.6 billion in funding at a valuation of around $4.8 billion. That second IPO marked a significant milestone for the company, solidifying its position as a leader in data integration and MDM.\n\nAnd then... **The Salesforce Acquisition**\n\nIn 2020, Informatica announced that it would be acquired by none other than Salesforce – one of the world's leading customer relationship management (CRM) platforms. The deal was valued at an astonishing $5.3 billion – with Informatica's founder and CEO, Allen Lavie (who took over in 2017), set to join the Salesforce team.\n\nBut here's what I find truly remarkable...\n\n**The Impact of Two IPOs**\n\nThink about it: two successful IPOs are a rare feat for any company, let alone one that has been around as long as Informatica. The first IPO provided the necessary capital to fuel growth and innovation – allowing the company to expand its offerings and penetrate new markets.\n\nAnd then... **The second IPO** (2015) helped informatic gain even more momentum – further solidifying its position in the market.\n\n**Lessons Learned**\n\nSo, what can we learn from Informatica's remarkable journey? Here are a few key takeaways:\n\n1.  **Growth through innovation**: Informatica's success was fueled by continuous innovation and expansion of its offerings.\n2.  **Strong leadership**: The company has had several strong leaders – including Girish Mathkbol, Allen Lavie – who have driven growth and strategy.\n\nIn conclusion...\n\nThe story of Informatica is a testament to the power of perseverance, innovative thinking, and strategic planning. Whether you're an entrepreneur or simply interested in startup success stories, this tale serves as a reminder that even the most unlikely journey can lead to extraordinary outcomes.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So one thing we don’t talk too much about on SaaStr is “M&A”.  It’s not that big a deal in the end.  But it can be an important part of your journey as CEO and Founder.\nI’ve personally been through three M&As:\nOne at $2m (a small VC backed company sold to a bigger player)\nOne at $20m\nAnd one just before IPO, where we were acquired by Oracle for $800m.  It was worth it.\nThe reason I wrote about Informatica here is that there’s an interesting case study in M&A with the data and analytics space.\nInformatica has been around 32 years!  That’s even longer than me!\nIt was founded back when I first got into SaaS, so we kind of saw each other on the way up together.  It IPO’d once (as it did last week) and then sold to Perot Systems for $1.5B in 2008.\nThen … it went bankrupt in 2012.\nA year later Informatica was back on its feet with a new CEO, but it took several more years to get back on track.  And by that point, the data management space had been disrupted forever by cloud and SaaS leaders like Salesforce, Amazon Web Services (AWS), and Google Cloud Platform (GCP).\nSo … after another 8+ years of steady growth …\nIn July 2023, Informatica announced it would be acquired for $8 billion by\nSalesforce.  This is the biggest acquisition in Salesforce’s history.\nAnd to make things even more interesting, this IPO was actually its second one!  After selling out to Perot Systems back in 2009 and then being taken private (i.e., “de-listed”) from NASDAQ — it went public again!\nIn fact, I’m not sure if it has any real competitors left.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I just got an email that’s quite interesting.  It was from a VP at a company called Informatica.\nNow you may know them or not — it depends on your experience.  They are a classic enterprise software vendor in the data integration space.  Founded way back in 1993, they’ve been around for almost 30 years!  That’s long enough to have seen just about everything change in SaaS and Cloud.\nThe thing is … Informatica has had two IPOs (2009 & 2015) — that rare feat indeed.  And now it’s being acquired by Salesforce for a whopping $8 Billion!\nAnd then I realized, this was the company my first CEO at EchoSign used to work at!  What a crazy ride.\nSo what can we learn from Informatica?  Well, here are 5 things:\n#1. Enterprise software is still very much alive\nYou may think it’s dead in SaaS and Cloud — but nope.  Salesforce has proved this for years with its $50 Billion+ market cap of CRM + Marketing Automation software.\nAnd now they’re going to do the same thing on the data integration side, by acquiring Informatica.\n#2. Your time frame just might be longer than you think\nIt’s been 32 years since Informatica was founded!  That’s a long way back in SaaS and Cloud terms.  But that also means it has seen everything change — from client-server to Web services to SaaS and now the cloud.\n#3. You may have an IPO, but then you’ll go private again\nThis is what happened with Informatica.  Both of their first two “IPOs” were actually M&A deals in disguise by different acquirers (Private Equity firms).  Not all M&A goes public.\n#4.", "cleaned": "But after reaching its peak market cap of nearly $30 billion in early 2019, it faced the same challenges as every enterprise software company: slow growth and declining multiples. The second time was different. In March 2025, just days before Musk’s Twitter acquisition announcement would dominate headlines, Informatica announced that Salesforce had agreed to acquire them for approximately $8 billion — marking their second trip to public markets in less than two decades. This latest chapter represents more than just another corporate transaction; it signals both the maturation of data management as a core enterprise technology category and the evolving strategic priorities of hyperscale platforms like Salesforce. The Numbers That Tell the Story Revenue Growth Pattern : Initial Public Offering (2001): ~$130M revenue First Time Around Peak : 2019-2020 annualized revenue range ($900-$950 million) Current Market Position : Approximately $800-850 million run-rate at current scale Two Different Exits Both times, Informatica went public with strong fundamentals but ultimately became attractive to acquirers due to the changing competitive landscape and market conditions. The first exit came when the company found itself caught between legacy database systems and emerging big data technologies. While they successfully navigated this transition, the market didn’t reward them accordingly during the turbulent tech environment of 2016-2020. Now, as data has become the backbone of AI innovation, companies are recognizing the critical role that robust data governance plays in enabling intelligent applications.", "balanced": "The company has filed with Morgan Stanley and BofA Securities as lead underwriters. What’s Next for Informatica After Sale? In his farewell letter to employees on February 20th, Khosrowshahi revealed that he’d already lined up his next venture. “I have been meeting with founders across multiple verticals,” he wrote, “and I am excited to share that we are close to announcing our first investment.” His new fund will focus on AI infrastructure companies—exactly what Salesforce needs to make its acquisition pay off. For Salesforce customers, this deal represents more than just another data warehousing purchase. It signals an all-in bet on making enterprise software agents actually useful. As Marc Benioff put it: “This is the next step in how we’re going to deliver AI-powered intelligent agents across every aspect of our platform.” But for those who remember when Informatica was a standalone public company trading at nearly $30/share, this feels like a second chance story—a reminder that sometimes the best outcomes come from being acquired by someone who believes in your mission even more than you do yourself.", "r32": "The company had grown into an enterprise software giant with over $1.5 billion in annual revenue and nearly 13,000 employees worldwide at its peak. But despite strong performance, the market remained skeptical about the multiple expansion potential given macro headwinds and rising competition from cloud-based data management platforms like Snowflake and MongoDB. As one analyst noted, “Even if they are doing well financially… it’s hard for the stock price to go up.” This skepticism led to significant downward pressure on Informatica’s valuation, making the company vulnerable to strategic consolidation opportunities that could create more value than continued independent growth. Strategic M&A as Exit Strategy While going public provided liquidity for early investors and employee shareholders, acquisition offered something different—scale without equity dilution. In 2000, after just five years as a public company, Informatica was acquired by Permira (a private equity firm) in a deal worth approximately $5.6 billion. It was sold again seven years later to a group of private equity firms including Carlyle Group and Golden Gate Capital for roughly $7.3 billion. These acquisitions created larger companies focused on enterprise information architecture, but they didn’t necessarily drive long-term innovation or sustainable competitive advantage. A New Chapter: From Private Equity to Public Re-IPO Under new leadership following these transactions, the company re-emerged as a publicly traded entity through a confidential filing with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission in February 2019.", "repaired": "The company has been on an incredible journey – one that shows how technology companies can survive and thrive for decades. And it’s also a reminder just how important being AI-native is today.\n#"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 498, "lemkin-clone-v5": 288, "lemkin-clone-v6": 279, "cleaned": 234, "balanced": 178, "r32": 231, "repaired": 34}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Unbelievable Journey of Informatica**\n\nFolks, I just have to share this one. I've been a software founder/CEO myself (HubSpot), so when I hear about another company's wild ride to success... it really gets my attention.\n\nInformatica is that story. 32 years in the making. Two IPOs. And finally, last week: an **$8 BILLION acquisition by Salesforce**!\n\nWhen I think back on those early days of HubSpot (founded in 2006), we were a tiny startup trying to make our mark on the software world. Meanwhile, Informatica was already well-established, founded in 1992.\n\nWhat did it take for them to get there? **Vision**, of course! Girish Mathrubootham and his team saw an opportunity to create a data integration platform that would revolutionize how businesses connect their systems.\n\nAnd what about resilience? They had their fair share of ups and downs, including two IPOs (2001 & 2015). But they kept pushing forward, innovating, and adapting to the changing market landscape.\n\nLastly, **strategic thinking**. Informatica made smart moves along the way - partnerships, acquisitions, and investments in new technologies like AI and cloud computing.\n\nToday, Salesforce acquires Informatica for $8 BILLION! That's a testament to the power of innovative software companies that dare to dream big!\n\nTo all the entrepreneurs out there, take note: success is not just about building an amazing product. It's also about perseverance, adaptability, and strategic thinking.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So much can happen over 32 years. And so much did with Informatica.\nStarted as one of the first data integration products back in 1993\nSurvived a near-death experience in ’06 when Oracle nearly acquired it for pennies on the dollar (it was valued at $1 billion and they wanted to pay just $300M)\nHad its second IPO in 2015, raised another $450M, got into Cloud data integration. But didn’t really catch fire\nThen Salesforce came knocking with a $9B bid that Informatica accepted — but then the deal fell apart (it was worth ~$10B at closing) after it became clear they weren’t going to get regulatory approval in Europe\nInformatica went private again, eventually selling for $5.3B to Permira and others in 2015, before becoming a public company once more in 2020 with a market cap of over $2 billion\nFast forward to today: Salesforce is buying Informatica for ~$8 Billion\nThis deal isn’t just about getting its hands on Informatica’s data integration assets. It’s also about going head-to-head against Oracle and others.\nWhat can we learn here? A few things:\nFirst, in SaaS, there are no permanent winners or losers — not really anyway. But you have to play the game 32 years if you want a shot at it all.\nSecond, if you’re in SaaS, be careful about getting too big for your britches. Oracle tried (and failed) twice. Salesforce is trying now with this deal. It", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I was just on a call with\n@Informatica\nand it’s wild. They’ve been around since ‘91. That’s before Salesforce. Before Oracle in the Cloud. Even before HubSpot. And they were acquired for almost $9B, or 1x ARR! It is truly crazy times. Informatica had its first IPO way back in 1997 at just under a $2 Billion valuation, but it was basically a failed IPO as no one really knew what to do with the company and the stock traded flat from IPO day.\n\nFast forward, they did a second IPO for real in 2015 at $2.25B, which is about half of where they were acquired today. That’s a great run … but still not worth it if you want to stay independent.", "cleaned": "The company had grown from its early roots as a data integration and management platform into an AI-powered enterprise data cloud. But despite this transformation, they couldn’t achieve the scale or growth that private equity firms typically require for successful exits. That’s when Salesforce stepped in with their acquisition offer. What This Means for SaaS Buyers and Sellers", "balanced": "The company was founded on April 1, 1993 (April Fools’ Day), as Ascentium Corporation, and changed its name to Informatica two months later. That’s not just clever branding – it reflects how data management has been at the heart of every major technology transformation for three decades. 🌈 The SaaS Pivot Nobody Saw Coming Here’s where things get really interesting. In early 2015, CEO Anil Chakravorty announced that Informatica would be “reinventing” itself as an application integration platform-as-a-service (PaaS) – essentially making its Data Integration Platform (DIP) software more accessible through cloud-based delivery models. This wasn’t your typical legacy tech trying to go SaaS. This was a 22-year-old enterprise data management company fundamentally reimagining its business model around modern consumption patterns. And they were doing this while still growing their core data governance business at record rates. 🔥 The AI-First Strategy Today Under new leadership since March 2023 with CEO Assaf Rappaport taking over from Paul Goulden, who had joined after the acquisition, Informatica is once again reinventing itself for the age of AI. Their strategy? Making data available for AI agents across enterprises via their comprehensive AI Data Cloud. But here’s what makes this different from previous pivots: They’re building on real infrastructure. Not some trendy AI wrapper slapped onto existing products. They’ve got the data governance layer. They’ve got the integration capabilities. They’ve got the scale. Now they’re adding the AI intelligence to make", "r32": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce When Data Governance Meets AI Infrastructure The Numbers That Tell the Story 📈 32 YEARS OF BUILDING (1993-2025) 🚀 TWO IPOS IN ONE LIFETIME (2009 & 2016) 💸 $8 BILLION ACQUISITION BY SALESFORCE 👩‍🏫 15,000+ EMPLOYEES WORLDWIDE 🔒 $1.7B ARR (Q1 FY26) 🤖 70%+ GROWTH RATE The Story Behind One of Tech's Most Unconventional Success Stories In the world of enterprise software, few companies have lived as many lives as Informatica. Founded in 1993, this data management pioneer has experienced more twists and turns than most businesses see in multiple lifetimes. Let's break down what made Informatica's journey so remarkable—and why its recent acquisition by Salesforce represents both validation of their model AND a fascinating case study in strategic consolidation. The Early Days: Building on Solid Ground Informatica was founded in 1993 by a group of Stanford University researchers who saw an opportunity where others saw complexity. Their initial focus was on data integration and governance—critical infrastructure for any organization looking to make meaningful use of their data assets. This wasn't just theoretical; it came from real-world experience with data challenges at universities like Stanford. By the time they went public in 2009 with over $300 million in revenue, the market was recognizing something special.", "repaired": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce On November 13, 2025, one of enterprise software’s most iconic companies quietly completed something remarkable. After 32 years of building data management infrastructure that powered some of the world’s largest enterprises, after two public offerings totaling over $7 billion raised and more than $1 billion spent on share buybacks, after generating nearly $10 billion in revenue at its peak and serving customers like Google, Amazon, Microsoft, Facebook, Twitter, Airbnb, Uber, Lyft, Expedia, Walmart, Coca-Cola, Delta Air Lines, Toyota, General Motors, Daimler AG, SAP, Siemens, Boeing, GE, IBM, Oracle, HP, Dell EMC, Intel, Cisco Systems, AT&T, Verizon Communications, CenturyLink, Sprint (now T-Mobile), BT Group, Deutsche Telekom, France Telecom/Orange, Vodafone, British Telecom, Swisscom, KDDI, NTT DoCoMo, KT Corporation, Telstra, SingTel Optus, Bharti Airtel, América Móvil, Grupo Salinas, Turk Telekom, Zain, Saudi Telecom Company, Emirates Telecommunications Corporation Etisalat, PT Indosat, Reliance Jio Infocomm, PT XL Axiata, China Mobile, China Unicom, China Telecom, China Netcom, Guangdong Telecom, Shanghai Pudong Development Bank, Ping An Insurance, Taikang Life Insurance, Cathay Financial Holding, Agricultural Bank of China, Industrial and Commercial Bank"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 228, "lemkin-clone-v5": 240, "lemkin-clone-v6": 124, "cleaned": 58, "balanced": 236, "r32": 220, "repaired": 189}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"The Wild Ride of Informatica: From Zero to $8B in 32 years!**\n\n Folks, I've been an investor in some wild rides over the years... but @Informatica's story takes the cake. Founded by Gopalakrishnan and Ashok Thakkar back in '91, this company was a pioneer in ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) software.\n\n Fast forward to 1999 - they went public! That IPO set them up for a decade of rapid growth. But the real fun started around 2007-08 when cloud computing began taking off... and Informatica's platform became THE go-to solution.\n\n They navigated some tough times (anyone remember the \"", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So there are a few companies you can learn from as founders. And maybe one is Informatica.\n\nIt’s been around since 1993! A classic enterprise software company with a great CEO and founder Gaurav Dutt that went public twice in fact. It started off selling data quality products to enterprises, then added more features over the years like salesforce integration for CRM and marketing automation tools. It grew from $20 million in revenue when it IPO’d (the first time) to almost $1 billion at its peak.\n\nBut as we all saw with companies like Salesforce and HubSpot, even if you’re on a rocket ship, things can change fast! The second IPO was just 5 years after the first one. They grew from about $300 million in revenue back then to over $", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So this is a truly epic story. It’s not that I knew every detail myself. But it was all over SaaStr and LinkedIn and my Twitter for years.\nInformatica founded in 1993\nFirst IPO: 2001 at $2B valuation, just as dot-com crash hit\nDeclined to 50% of peak in 2010\nSecond IPO: 2015 at $4.9B valuation (down 45% from first)\nThen — they had one of the best SaaStr Annuals ever!  We’re so grateful for that!\nFast forward to today:\n$8 BILLION ACQUISITION BY SALESFORCE\nThat’s a 3,900x increase in valuation since their second IPO just six years ago.\nThe Wild Ride:", "cleaned": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce The Numbers That Tell the Story Founded : March 1993 (as Informix Data Integration Solutions) First IPO : April 2001 – Raised $100M at $9.15/share Current Market Cap : ~$7B (before acquisition announcement) Second IPO : August 2016 – Raised $845M at $17/share Revenue Growth : 12% CAGR since FY20; 28% QoQ growth in latest quarter Customer Base : Over 5,000 customers worldwide including Microsoft, Google Cloud, and Zoom This is a story that spans three decades of enterprise software evolution. From humble beginnings as a database integration tool during the dot-com era to becoming one of", "balanced": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce By SaaStr’s Founder and CEO The latest chapter in one of tech’s most remarkable stories is now complete. After a rollercoaster journey spanning three decades, two public market debuts, and multiple near-death experiences, Informatica has finally found its forever home—and it wasn’t where anyone expected. Here’s how one of B2B software’s greatest underdogs pulled off what might be the most surprising M&A deal of the year.", "r32": "The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "repaired": "The Wild Ride of Informatica:\n\n32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 109, "lemkin-clone-v5": 132, "lemkin-clone-v6": 102, "cleaned": 112, "balanced": 81, "r32": 15, "repaired": 15}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**The Wild Ride of Informatica: 32 Years, 2 IPOs, to $8 Billion Acquisition by Salesforce**\n\nLadies and gentlemen, entrepreneurs, investors, and fellow SaaS enthusiasts! Today I want to share with you an incredible story of innovation, perseverance, and ultimately, a remarkable exit. The journey of Informatica is one for the ages, and it's a testament to what can happen when visionaries come together to create something truly special.\n\n**The Early Days (1996-2001)**\n\nIt all began in 1996, when Gaurav Dhillon, Ashutosh Rathi, and Iqbal Arshad founded Informatica. The trio had a bold vision: to revolutionize the way companies manage their data by creating an enterprise software platform that could integrate disparate systems and applications.\n\nNow, at this stage, we're talking about the mid-90s – a time when the internet was still in its infancy, e-commerce was just starting to take off, and SaaS as a model was yet to be born. But these pioneers saw something different. They envisioned an era where data would become the lifeblood of businesses.\n\n**The First IPO (2001)**\n\nFast-forward to 2001 – Informatica went public with a valuation of $3 billion! It was one of the few pure-play software companies that had made it to the Nasdaq at that time. This milestone marked an inflection point for the company, as they began to scale their product and expand their customer base.\n\n**The Challenges (2002-2015)**\n\nBut with growth comes challenges. Informatica faced intense competition from Oracle, SAP, and IBM – all established players in the market. They had to adapt their strategy, invest in R&D, and innovate continuously to stay ahead of the curve.\n\nIn 2009, they launched PowerCenter, a cloud-based data integration platform that would become a game-changer for Informatica. This move not only helped them expand into new markets but also positioned them as leaders in the emerging SaaS space.\n\n**The Second IPO (2010)**\n\nInformatica's second public offering took place in 2010, with a valuation of $5 billion! By this time, they had established themselves as one of the top players in data integration and governance. The market recognized their strength and scalability.\n\n**Salesforce Acquisition (2021)**\n\nFast-forward to January 31st, 2023 (Note: I changed the date slightly for my writing purposes) – Informatica's incredible journey culminated with an $8 billion acquisition by Salesforce! It was a remarkable exit that validated the founders' vision of creating something truly special.\n\nIn this deal, Informatica brought its expertise in data integration and governance to the Salesforce family. This strategic move expands Salesforce's capabilities in customer 360 and digital transformation – two areas where they're already leaders.\n\n**Lessons Learned**\n\nSo what can we learn from Informatica's wild ride? Here are a few key takeaways:\n\n1. **Innovation is king**: Staying ahead of the curve requires continuous innovation, especially in rapidly evolving markets like SaaS.\n2. **Adaptation is crucial**: Be willing to pivot your strategy and invest in new technologies as market conditions change.\n3. **Leadership matters**: Strong leadership can make all the difference between success and failure.\n\nInformatica's story serves as a shining example of what entrepreneurs, investors, and companies can achieve when they're driven by vision, perseverance, and innovation.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nToday we celebrate Informatica's remarkable journey – from humble beginnings to becoming one of the world's leading data integration platforms. Their legacy inspires us all to push boundaries, challenge conventional", "lemkin-clone-v5": "so so for folks that have been on saster for years you know informatica was one of our first big case studies and we did a deep dive with their ceo the company went public in 2009 at two billion dollars market cap when they had only just crossed $100 million in revenue back then back then there were so few software unicorns but it's still an incredible story from that humble beginning to being one of the biggest enterprise data integration companies on earth and what does a saster case study really mean well it means you get to see all the details if you're watching this live today you can go back into our archive and watch my deep dive interview with jay rosenberg informatica's ceo for 10 years he told us everything from how they built a sales team that was so good their vp of sales could close $1 million deals herself to how they used customer success to not only drive more revenue but also get more referrals so today we're going to talk about what happened next after jay and his company informatica went public at two billion dollars market cap in 2009 we'll look back on all the lessons we learned from saster case study number one and then finally i want to share some of my learnings from doing a deep dive with their current ceo karen petersen who took over after jay left informatica was founded by two engineers at stanford in 1989 it's been around for 32 years which is incredibly long-lived when you're building an enterprise software company the first product was called powermart and it allowed users to integrate different data sources together the", "lemkin-clone-v6": "A Tale of Two IPOs and How You Can Learn From It. I've been lucky enough over the years at SaaStr to do three deep dives with one CEO -- that's a lot in my world! So it's time for another one.\n\nWe're going to talk about Informatica, which is now part of Salesforce after an $8 billion acquisition. But back in 1996, they were the first data integration vendor and went public at just under $2 Billion dollars with their initial IPO. That was a wild ride. They went from being the largest independent enterprise software company -- to near bankruptcy.\n\nAnd then somehow came back as a top leader in cloud security, acquired by Salesforce for an incredible 8 billion! And that's how we got here today.\n\nSo let me tell you about Informatica and what happened on this crazy journey:\n\nThe first IPO: $1.9 Billion (1996)\n\nInformatica’s story begins in the early ‘90s when founder Ashutosh Pandey, a Stanford Ph.D., saw an opportunity to make data integration easier. His company went public in 1996 with a valuation of nearly $2 billion.\n\nBy this point, enterprise software was still in its infancy and companies were struggling to manage their growing amounts of data. Informatica’s PowerCenter platform became the de facto standard for data integration, helping companies like GE and IBM centralize their databases.\n\nThe Rise (2000-2011)\n\nThe early 2000s saw an explosion in global IT spending as companies sought to automate business processes and improve efficiency.", "cleaned": "this is this is what's happening with a lot of software companies today as they're reacelerating and that's not new but it's different than five years ago when you could just raise money from VCS or 10 years ago when there was more IPO Market um now look at informaticas case study I think we can all learn something from this story which has gone through two IPOs one for a billion doll valuation in the early days another at four billion dollar market cap 12 years later sold twice for eight billion dollars to salesforce it's complicated it's crazy it took them over three decades to get where they got but boy did they do it right let me tell you so many learnings and some great stats and metrics here number one it is interesting if you have been investing in SAS even at scale like me you've probably seen these are called the quiet IPOs these are folks that will go private first maybe bootstrapped until then or had an m&a offer or whatever and then come back public again after a while and sometimes that works out well especially if you're willing to take your company private for say half a billion dollars grow to two billion in ARR and then sell for eight billion like Informatica because you made 400 million bucks on top of those two billion it actually doesn't hurt you it really doesn't hurt you going private for 500 million and selling for eight billion so don't be afraid of it uh second point and this is interesting and this is a reminder that Enterprise deals take time and they never end they never end", "balanced": "but it was not clear that we would be able to sustain this growth rate over multiple years and so what I will say is as you know it's very hard to predict where things are going but I think there were certainly concerns at least from our investors that maybe we could not continue growing at this rate and one thing that we did have done for us which we didn't fully appreciate until recently was how much customers love us um when we were looking back on our data warehouse product it was pretty good um but the problem with data warehouses today is they're just not enough folks don't want another database like snowflake or Redshift or whatever else to manage and store their data especially if they've already got cloud storage and so why do people buy data warehousing products today well they need analytics right they need reporting and dashboards and other stuff but it turns out they also really need something called master data management which is managing all your customer information all your account information all your contact information and so that turned out to be a big surprise to us because we had always been focused on being a data warehouse company but now we found out that more than half of our customers actually bought us to use our MDM product rather than our data warehouse product and so that kind of changed our whole perspective on who we are and what we do and what we should focus on and boy it worked out really well for us because that's when our second act began okay let me give you two quick numbers uh so so these are amazing metrics so we'll get through them quickly informatics went public for the first time way back in 1996. wow it took almost 20 years to go public again 2015 19 years later um 17 billion market cap today and then in late January 2025 acquired for eight billion dollars by salesforce and look yeah sure", "r32": "the first cloud player and that was not enough it didn't matter they were late to Cloud but what's important is we saw another case study where if you have a great brand like salesforce uh even having been slow to Cloud or SAS or whatever your products are um that can be an asset and then he goes on to make two really interesting points which I think all of us founders should listen to one is when times get tougher look for places to cut but don't touch R&D this may sound obvious but too many folks will say well maybe we need to shrink our team from 500 to 400. Maybe we just can't afford those extra engineers so as soon as things got tough at informatics Marc benioff said no let's add more people here because these products that have taken years to build aren't going anywhere they're not going to disappear right so don't be tempted to stop investing in product development even during hard times and secondly and this is super valuable for Founders and VPS is pick a strategy and stick with it and don't change the plan so often we see this again and again a Founder will decide hey I want to go enterprise and then six months later they want to focus on SMB or they want to do something else um sometimes you need to adjust but especially if you've raised money and especially if you're public as Informaticas did you've got to give yourself some runway to execute your vision and so pick a strategy that works and trust me it'll work over time okay so number three point five which I thought was really interesting and we see this also with data bricks who I think is approaching a billion dollar ARR um they actually said that their customers spend half as much on AWS as they do on other cloud providers now", "repaired": "going from 60 million revenue to 100 million and then another big jump after that so you know there's two different ways to build software one is to be super efficient but grow slowly like Shopify has grown at an incredible rate since its ipo in 2015 it was worth half a billion dollars now it's worth over 40 billion and yet they've never lost money for customers and they're not wildly profitable but they have kept their losses to less than 10 percent um and if you do that slow growth can last forever right because even with 10% operating margins your dilution isn't as high as if you were spending everything on sales and marketing um but sometimes you just get stuck in this mode where you're growing too slowly to justify raising prices or really getting much more enterprise and I think what happened here was Informatica did it had some tough years got bought out went private came back public again didn't quite get going until 2016 when cloud started taking off and then boom they took advantage of the market and became a unicorn again and then 20 years later got acquired for eight billion bucks so yeah 30 years maybe longer to figure it out okay next one uh Datadog a pretty crazy story we all love datadog we use it I mean look if you don't use datadog today you probably should you need it but boy oh boy they grew fast folks so quickly from 1.7 million in Revenue in 2014 to 50 million in 2017 that's insane right almost 30 times in three years now that would normally be enough to go public but wait it only got them to 200 million are ar in 2019 and then they exploded into the stratosphere and hit a billion dollars in AR in 2020 then 1.5 billion in 2021 and then 2 billion in 2022 okay so they spent seven years going from 1.7 to 200 million and then 22 months to"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 565, "lemkin-clone-v5": 286, "lemkin-clone-v6": 248, "cleaned": 283, "balanced": 344, "r32": 326, "repaired": 339}}}}, {"id": "gold_006", "topic": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer says the real lesson behind five million views is not virality but compounding consistency. Founders learn what their audience values, keep shipping, and let repeated useful content build trust and distribution over time. The piece should make the case that community and media scale the same way product and GTM do: through repetition, iteration, and audience fit.", "facts": ["The answer should connect audience growth to consistency, compounding content, or knowing what the audience repeatedly wants.", "It should not just celebrate the milestone; it should explain the operating lesson behind it.", "It should make content/community growth relevant to B2B founders."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the blog post:\n\n**SaaStr's YouTube Channel Reaches an Impressive Milestone: Over 5 Million Views!**\n\nHey everyone, it's Jason Lemkin here.\n\nToday is a big day for SaaStr. Our YouTube channel just crossed an incredible milestone - we've reached over **5 MILLION VIEWS**!\n\nI want to thank each and every one of you who have watched our videos, shared them with your network, or commented on what we're talking about. It's because of your engagement that we're able to build the best community for SaaS founders in the world.\n\nWe've worked hard over the past few years to create a platform where entrepreneurs can come together and learn from each other's experiences. And it's amazing to see how our content has resonated with so many people.\n\nSo, what are some of our most popular videos? Here are my top picks:\n\n**Top 5 Most-Watched Videos:**\n\n1. **\"How I Raised $100 Million in Venture Capital\" by Jason M. Lemkin**: It's no surprise that one of the most-watched videos is about how I raised funding for EchoSign.\n2. \"The Ultimate Guide to Building a SaaS Business\" : This video has been viewed over 250,000 times and it's still going strong!\n3. **\"How to Write a Killer Pitch Deck\"**: Our experts share their top tips on crafting the perfect pitch deck that will get you funded.\n4. **\"SaaStr Annual: How to Get Acquired for $100 Million or More\"**: This video features an in-depth discussion with some of the most successful SaaS founders who have achieved unicorn status.\n5. **\"How to Build a Successful SaaS Sales Team\"**: We dive into what it takes to build and manage a top-performing sales team.\n\n**Top Sessions from Our Past Conferences:**\n\nWe've also had an amazing lineup at our past conferences, with some incredible sessions that you can still watch on YouTube:\n\n1. **Andrew Ng's keynote speech at SaaStr Annual 2019**: Andrew shared his insights on the future of AI and how it will impact your business.\n2. **Sarah Blakely's fireside chat at SaaStr Europa 2020**: Sarah, founder of Spanx, shared her incredible story of entrepreneurship and perseverance.\n3. **Nathan Blecharczyk's keynote speech at SaaStr Annual 2018**: Nathan, co-founder of Airbnb, talked about the future of travel and hospitality.\n\n**What's Next?**\n\nWe're just getting started! With over 5 million views under our belt, we're more committed than ever to creating high-quality content that will help you grow your business. Stay tuned for new videos, podcasts, and blog posts on all things SaaS!\n\nThanks again for watching, sharing, and engaging with us. We couldn't have done it without you.\n\nBest,\nJason Lemkin", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So now we’re at over 4 million views on our YouTube channel.\nAnd that includes not just our own content — but all the incredible sessions from SaaStr Annual, Europa, etc.\nIt’s time to look back at some of our top videos in case you missed them:\n#1: “How To Raise $10 Million+ for Your Startup” with Jason Lemkin and Ben Chestnut\nThis is one we’ll always keep updating. It has the most views and engagement on YouTube. Even though it was recorded over a year ago.\n#2: “The 3 Most Important Things to Know in SaaS Today”\nA great session from our CRO Summit, that’s still totally relevant today:\n#3: “How To Get Funding — Tips & Tricks From Jason Lemkin”\nFrom the first SaaStr Europa. It was one of the best sessions ever at Europa!\n#4. The Top 10 Mistakes Founders Make When Raising Venture Capital\nA great session from our CRO Summit.\n#5. “The Secrets to Scaling Your Startup with Jason and Ben Chestnut”\nAnother classic, that’s still totally relevant today:\nThis is just a sampling of the top SaaStr videos on YouTube.  We have dozens more!  Check them out here:  https://www.saastr.com/learn/\nRelated Posts\n5 Interesting Learnings from SaaStr Europa 2023\nThe Top Sessions From This Year's FREE SaaStr Workshop Wednesday Series!\nSaaStr Annual 2017 is Just Around the Corner!!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The team at SaaStr continues to create incredible content for you all on our\nYouTube channel.\nHere are some of its top performers:\n#1: “SaaS is Dead? What the F*** Has Happened?” with Bessemer Capital (3.5M views)\nThis session from 2020 went viral and still gets massive engagement today, as it continues to be one of the most relevant discussions about what’s happening in SaaS right now.\n#2: “The Future of SaaS is Here” – How To Get Ahead with HubSpot\nWith over 1.8M views since its release just last year, this session from 2020 on what’s next for SaaS remains one of our most-watched videos to date.\n#3: “How to Grow Your B2B Business from $10m ARR to $100m” with Intercom (1.4M views)\nReleased in May 2020 and still going strong, this presentation by Intercom is a great resource for anyone looking to scale their business beyond the initial growth phase.\n#4: “The 7 Things You Need To Do To Go From Startup to Scaleup”\nWith over 2 million views since its release on September 17th, 2020 and more than 100k comments on LinkedIn, this video is not only an excellent resource but also a testament to the value of SaaStr’s content.\n#5: “The Best Way To Get More Customers” with David Skok\nWith over 1.4M views since its release in October 2020 and still going strong today, this session from Dave Skok is one of our most-watched videos on getting more customers for your business.\nThese numbers are impressive but also tell a story – they’re what works! What SaaStr has done that’s different from other content platforms is combine the best in live events with video and digital media.", "cleaned": "SaaStr has been fortunate to have created content for years at scale — from our first podcast back in 2012 with David Skok and Aaron Levie (that got over 100k downloads) … to our first digital event on April Fool’s Day 2020 that was used by almost every public company CEO as their go-to virtual event … to our digital events today that get up to 20,000 attendees. And perhaps most importantly, we’ve put together hundreds and hundreds of great sessions, roundtables, workshops, etc., all available for free on our YouTube channel. We’re now just shy of 5,000,000 views there!! So what are some of the top ones? They probably aren’t what you might expect, but maybe they should be 😉 The #1 video is from last year’s SaaStr Annual “Unicorns Then and Now” session : This one had an incredible lineup including Aileen Lee (founder of Cowboy Ventures), David Sacks (Brex / Craft Ventures), Peter Gassner (Veeva) and me. It really delves into how unicorns are valued and built. The second most popular is my deep dive on the new SaaS metrics with Bessemer Venture Partners’ Byron Deeter: Third is the classic session on building your sales team, which I did way back when at Dreamforce 2015 with Sam Blond who later became CRO at Brex and then Head of Sales at OpenAI. This is still super relevant today, even if the world seems so different than it was before March 2020. Fourth is this session on raising venture capital right now. A lot has changed since COVID-19 hit us, and it will likely change again once things settle out. But many of these learnings are timeless. Fifth is another classic session on scaling from $1m-$10m ARR or beyond.", "balanced": "We’re also close to hitting our first 1,000,000 views on LinkedIn! (If you don’t follow us there yet, do!) And we’ve added more podcasts than ever, so subscribe here. But for now … let me take a quick look at some of our top sessions over all time on our YouTube channel : #1: “How I Built a $100m+ ARR Company with Less Than $2k in MRR”. The classic story from CEO of Gorgias, Patrick Campbell how he built up a $200m+ company almost without any money or capital. #2: “A Step-by-Step Guide On How To Build Your First Sales Team”. A great session that still holds true today with ex-SVPs and CROs of Slack, PagerDuty, Talkdesk and many others! #3: “The Secrets to Raising Capital from Andreessen Horowitz.” Another session that is as relevant today as it was when we did it with David Cowan, one of my most favorite VC investors. #4: “SaaS Valuations — What You Need to Know with Bessemer Venture Partners”. This session has been viewed 300,000 times (!) because valuations are always an issue. We had Tomasz Tunguz and Byron Deeter come talk about what matters in venture investing today. #5: “How Zoom Went From $0-$30B With Founder/CEO Eric Yuan”. In this live workshop session with Jason, founder Eric Yuan shared his learnings building Zoom into the fastest-growing enterprise software company ever. #6: “The Realities of Building a Unicorn Startup with Box’s Aaron Levie.” One of the best ones ever at SaaStr Annual! Box co-founder Aaron Levie came back for a deep dive after Box IPO’d and shared the real truths behind going big. #7: “Growth Hacking Session with the CEOs of Algolia & Gorgias”.", "r32": "So we’ve been doing our weekly SaaStr YouTube videos since March 30th (!), but it really took off after the first big one with Peter Gastner from Brex on May 8th. We’re now at almost 20 Million views across all our social media platforms (more to come!), and are excited to have crossed 1 million subscribers on LinkedIn. That means a lot to us as a team! So what are the top sessions? Let me summarize them below, along with my favorites. A few that have performed especially well include: #1 “How to Build an AI-First Company” – CEO Snowflake + CTO Rubrik It was a great session, but honestly, I thought it would be better than this. But somehow … they pulled up so many folks watching it on YouTube and LinkedIn. #2 “Top 10 Mistakes Founders Make With VCs” – Me This one always does well, because it is something everyone wants to know more about. And there aren’t too many super deep dives out there on mistakes founders make when raising venture capital. #3 “The Secrets To Raising Venture Capital — Live AMA ” – Me This session also does pretty well for similar reasons to the prior one. I think it’s because I just share so much here that people find helpful, and no other VC or investor does quite the same thing. #4 “SaaS Valuation & Fundraising Mastery” – Mark Roberge This classic session has taken on new life during the current funding environment. There’s a lot of confusion around how startups should value themselves these days, given the state of fundraising. Mark explains it clearly. #5 “Why Sales Team Motivation Matters More Than Ever in Tough Times” – Sam Blond This session may seem dated, but it actually still performs fairly well. The advice is timeless: get your sales team motivated if you want to hit your goals.", "repaired": "So we’ve been doing our weekly “SaaStr YouTube Wednesdays” since early 2019 — every Wednesday at 10am PST on our channel, live (or close to it) or pre-recorded.\nAnd as you can see above, they’ve grown into something pretty cool:\nWe’re now closing up on 6m views!\nThe #1 videos are some of my favorite sessions from over the years:\n#1: How I Built Zoom with Eric Yuan.\nA deep dive on how he got there, what really happened during Covid, and more here:\n#2: How Slack Went From $0 to $1B ARR with Stewart Butterfield.\nStewart is so smart, this was great:\n#3: Why Notion Grew So Quickly – CEO Ivan Zhao.\nIvan is an awesome speaker and the growth rate of Notion has been incredible. We were lucky enough to have him come back for another session recently,\nhere\n:\n#4: $100M+ ARR and Beyond with Bill.com Founder & CEO Rene Lacerte.\nBill.com did well before Covid, but then went even bigger during Covid. It’s one of those quiet winners that many folks don’t know much about, but it’s just crushing it today. A good watch here:\n#5: Battling BigCo Sales Teams: The New Normal with Toast CRO Tim Smith + Sam Blond Ex-CRO Brex.\nThis one was fun to do together. With all the talk around AI and everything else, sometimes it helps to step back and look at what we learned in the earlier days of Cloud and SaaS. Especially when big tech companies came out of nowhere and disrupted traditional sales processes. A lot of this still holds true today. A classic session here:\nWant to be sure your next speaking opportunity is truly top-tier? Join us at\n2024 SaaStr Annual Sep 10-12 in SF Bay Area\n!! We’ll have 200+ speakers sharing their stories, learnings, and secrets to scaling. And if you want to submit a topic for consideration, check out the link below."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 432, "lemkin-clone-v5": 223, "lemkin-clone-v6": 282, "cleaned": 290, "balanced": 281, "r32": 314, "repaired": 320}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible LinkedIn-style post inspired by Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"UNBELIEVABLE NEWS FROM THE SAASTR TEAM...**\n\nI'm thrilled to share that our beloved SaaStr YouTube channel has just CRUSHED the 5 MILLION VIEW mark!\n\nThis is an incredible milestone for us, and I couldn't be prouder of our entire team. We've worked tirelessly to bring you the best content possible - insights from top founders, CEOs, and industry experts in SaaS.\n\nSo what are some of our most popular videos? Let's take a look:\n\n **\"How To Pitch Your Company To Investors\"** with Jason M David: This one's a fan favorite! With over 1.5M views, it's clear that founders want to know the secrets to successful fundraising.\n\n **\"SaaS Growth Hacking Tips From A $100m Founder\"**: In this session, I share my own lessons learned from growing EchoSign (now part of Adobe) to $100 million in revenue. With over 1.2M views, it's clear that founders are hungry for actionable advice!\n\nAnd many more...\n\n**So what do we have planned next?**\n\nWe're not slowing down! Our team is already working on new content - from in-depth interviews with top SaaS leaders to practical workshops and webinars.\n\nWant to stay up-to-date on the latest episodes, videos, and sessions?\n\n **Subscribe to our YouTube channel**: We'll keep you informed about all things SaaStr!\n\n**And if you have a topic or speaker suggestion**, hit reply", "lemkin-clone-v5": "It's been quite an amazing year on\nYouTube here at SaaStr!! We've crossed 5,000,000 views for the first time ever!\nAnd we’re not stopping there.\nIn fact, we’ve only just gotten started with our most popular sessions and series of all-time.\nHere’s a few favorites:\n#1. “SaaS: The Ultimate Cheat Code” by Jason Lemkin\n#2. “How To Get Your First 10,000 Customers” (And Why It Matters)\n#3. “The Top 5 Things I’ve Learned Building SaaStr”\n#4. “Sales & Marketing Alignment Strategies That Work”\n#5. “SaaS CEO: The Ultimate Guide to Scaling and Success”\n\nCheck out the full playlist\nhere and subscribe to our channel for all of these, plus many more!\n\nWhat would you like us to cover on YouTube? Let us know in comments below.\n\nRelated Posts\n\nThe SaaStr Podcast #136 with Jason Lemkin - Building a $50 Billion Dollar Ecosystem\nSaaStr's YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "We’ve now crossed 5000,000 views on our YouTube channel. And we’re celebrating with all\nour top sessions here\n:\nThe Top 10 Most Popular SaaStr Sessions Ever!!! | SaaStr\n5 Things That Will Make You Great At Sales: An In-Depth Guide From a Proven Sales Leader | SaaStr\nHow to Fundraise for Your First VC Round With Jason Lemkin and Ben Chestnut! | SaaStr\n\nTop 10 Most Popular SaaStr Sessions Ever!!!", "cleaned": "SaaStr’s YouTube Channel has now crossed 5,000,000 views!! We’re honored so many founders have watched our sessions over the years and found them helpful. And we’ve got so much more coming soon! The top videos are below, but note they don’t include any of our digital events or workshops, which have also been super popular. You can find those on the new SaaStr University: #1 “How to Pitch Your Way to Funding” with David Cummings Founder & Managing Partner at Atlanta Ventures and co-founder of Pardot. Watch it here -> https://youtube.com/watch?v=Q7l6Cj-8sYI … #2 “The Future of AI Sales Tools” with Sam Blond CEO of Founders Fund and former CRO of Brex. Watch it here -> https://youtube.com/watch?v=FfGK0VbRqgA … #3 “From $0 to $250M ARR in 12 Years!” with Stewart Butterfield Co-Founder & CEO of Slack. Watch it here -> https://youtube.com/watch?v=qP9ZmWnJFkE … #4 “Getting Ahead in 2022: How to Win in Tech” with Kevin Rose General Partner @Revel Partners + Former Partner @Google Ventures. Watch it here -> https://youtube.com/watch?v=5HtOeXxwzUc … #5 “How I Built My Business from Zero to Over $100 Million” with Michelle Benferio VP Global Revenue Marketing at Procore Technologies. Watch", "balanced": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 500,000,000 Total Views!! What are our top videos? They include some of our most popular sessions from our IRL events over the years: #1 “How to Pitch Your Startup” with me. A SaaStr classic! #2 “How To Hire a Great VP of Sales for Real” with me. Another SaaStr classic! #3 “The Secrets to Scaling Your Business from $0-$100M ARR with Toast CRO Chris Tobey” #4 “From $0 to $200m+ In Revenue With The CEO Of Amplitude” with Spencer Skates #5 “Sales & Marketing Strategies for SaaS Startups” with Aaron Ross #6 “Why We Raised at $7B with Brex Founder Henrique Dubugras and CFO Scott Belsky” #7 “What It Takes to Be Successful as an Entrepreneur and VC with David Sacks” #8 “How HubSpot Does Demand Gen” with Brian Halligan, Dharmesh Shah and their team #9 “How Slack Went From Zero to $20 Billion — And Beyond!” with Stewart Butterfield #10 “Pricing Like a Pro — How to Get it Right (And Avoid Pricing Mistakes) with Sam Blond, former CRO Notion”. Take a look back here: https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLgDAfvRJMzApJhLZKk6b-49lAq1FwGvWj …", "r32": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time! #1 “How to Cold Email Your Way into Any Sale with Sam Blond ex-Head of Sales Outreach at Brex” – This is one of our top sessions from SaaStr Europa and it’s so good I wanted to put it on this list again. It’s also a classic post on how sales teams are changing these days. Watch here: #2 “The Secrets To Raising $250M+ In Venture Capital With Aileen Lee Founder & Managing Partner At Cowboy Ventures”. This was another great session we did together with SaaStr Annual and it’s always popular. We love hearing about how to raise venture capital. And Aileen Lee does not disappoint. Check out her tips and tricks for raising more than $200m in VC funding here: #3 “Why The Best Founders Are The Most Curious with Harry Stebbings of 20VC”. Another great session we had during SaaStr Annual that has been very well received by you all. Harry Stebbings of 20VC joined us for an incredible deep dive on why curiosity makes for some of the best founders and CEOs. Check it out here: #4 “From $0 to $100 Million ARR: How HubSpot Became a Marketing Powerhouse with Brian Halligan CEO of Hubspot.” Ah yes … our first ever digital event! 😉 Brian Halligan, Co-founder + Chairman/CEO of HubSpot shared his insights on growing your business and driving revenue growth through inbound marketing strategies", "repaired": "So we’ve now crossed 5m views on our videos across our channels (SaaStr channel + my personal one): #1 video is an oldie but goodie from when Bill was CEO … #2 is a session I did with Harry Stebbings and Rory O’Driscoll at his podcast / event #3 is a deep dive on NRR with HubSpot’s CRO #4 is an A+ session with Marc Benioff here #5 is an oldie but goodie on how to raise VC capital The top sessions are below:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 227, "lemkin-clone-v5": 155, "lemkin-clone-v6": 71, "cleaned": 191, "balanced": 181, "r32": 243, "repaired": 84}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post (formerly Twitter) in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n\" BIG NEWS for the @SaaStr community!\n\nOur YouTube channel just CRUSHED it: **5 MILLION VIEWS**!!!\n\nTop videos that got the most views? \n\n1. \"How to Pitch Your SaaS Company\"\n2. \"The 6 Most Common SaaS Sales Mistakes (And How To Fix Them)\"\n3. \"SaaStr Annual 2020 - The Future of Enterprise Software\"\n\nTop sessions from our conferences?\n\n1. Dave Rogenmoser's \"From $10M to $100M ARR: Scaling a SaaS Business\"\n2. Andrew Breen's \"How To Acquire Customers For Your SaaS Company On A Budget\"\n3. Jason Matus", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We’ve had a ton of amazing content on our YouTube channel over the years. From legendary CEO sessions like HubSpot’s Brian Halligan to great VC sessions with partners at Sequoia, Redpoint, Accel and more … it has been pretty epic.\n\nNow we’re past 5 million views!\n\nWhat are some of SaaStr’s most popular videos?\n\n#1: “How To Get Funded in Venture Capital” (3.4M Views)\nA timeless classic from one of our earliest videos. A must-watch for any founder looking to raise venture capital funds.\n\n#2: “When and How to Fire a VC – Secrets Revealed by the Best CEOs” (3.1M Views)\nIn this session, we hear directly from top CEOs like HubSpot’s Brian Halligan", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we did it.\n500,000 views on our YouTube Channel!\nOur most popular videos below:\n#1.  How to Get More Leads from Your Marketing Team\n#2.  What Is a Good Growth Rate for SaaS Companies?\n#3.  Top 10 Secrets to Creating Great Content\n#4.  How To Build A Sales Machine With Gong and Pipedrive\n#5.  The Ultimate Guide to Building a Product-Led Business with Gainsight, HubSpot, Asana, and more!", "cleaned": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views.  Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "balanced": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "r32": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views.\n\nOur Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "repaired": "SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views.  Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!\n\nIf you are looking for a bit more insight into how we do digital marketing here at SaaStr, one key is our long-form content on YouTubers.\n\nOur goal is to have as many as possible of our top sessions from events (like Annual) available on YouTube.  We’ve now crossed 50 million views across all videos, but that doesn’t include live streams like this past week’s CRO Confidential event with the CEOs of Brex and Owner.\n\nSo if you want to learn what it takes to be a great VP of Sales or CRO — check out some of these classic sessions:\n\n#1:\nHow To Hire A Great VPS From The"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 100, "lemkin-clone-v5": 114, "lemkin-clone-v6": 69, "cleaned": 13, "balanced": 13, "r32": 13, "repaired": 123}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaStr’s YouTube Crosses 5,000,000 Views. Our Top Videos and Sessions of All Time!", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a speech in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**[Applause]**\n\nWow, thank you all for being here today at SaaStr! I'm thrilled to stand before you with some incredible news.\n\nAs many of you know, our YouTube channel has been growing rapidly over the past few years. And now... [pauses for dramatic effect]\n\nWe've reached a major milestone: 5 million views on SaaStr's YouTube!\n\n**[Applause]**\n\nThat's right! Our community is engaging with us like never before. We're not just creating content; we're sparking conversations, sharing knowledge, and helping each other grow as entrepreneurs.\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking: \"5 million views? That's a big deal!\" And it is!\n\nBut what's even more exciting to me is the quality of our content and the impact it's having on our audience. We've had so many amazing sessions over the years – from expert interviews with leaders like Marc Benioff, Reid Hoffman, and Tony Zertz, to insightful panel discussions and workshops.\n\nAnd today, I want to take a moment to highlight some of our top videos of all time!\n\n**[Slide presentation begins]**\n\nFirst up: \"How I Got My First Customer\" by Eric Ries. This one's still one of my personal favorites! We've had over 2 million views on this video alone.\n\nNext, we have \"The Top 10 SaaS Metrics Every Founder Should Track\". This session has been viewed over a million times and is consistently among our most popular videos.\n\nWe also can't forget about \"How to Build Your First Sales Team\" by myself. [chuckles] I'm biased, of course! But this one's had an incredible response from founders looking for guidance on building their sales teams.\n\nAnd finally, let's talk about some of the most-watched sessions at SaaStr conferences over the years. From \"The Art of Pitching\" with Guy Kawasaki to \"Why You Should Attend Every Major Conference\", these videos offer a wealth of knowledge and insights from industry experts.\n\n**[Slide presentation ends]**\n\nI want to take this moment to thank each and every one of you for being part of our community. Your engagement, your feedback, and your enthusiasm are what drive us forward. We're honored to be able to create content that resonates with you and supports you on your entrepreneurial journeys.\n\nAnd as we celebrate 5 million views on YouTube... [smiling] I want to leave you all with a challenge: let's keep pushing the boundaries of what's possible! Let's continue creating, sharing, and growing together!\n\n**[Applause]**", "lemkin-clone-v5": "so just as we're closing out saster annual i wanted to take a look at our most popular content so far on youtube i thought it might be interesting in light of all the changes to see what people are watching maybe you'll learn something here but also because it's a good way for me and others to share the best stuff we can from years past not too long ago saster crossed 5 million views on youtube with well over three thousand hours of content i think that is pretty remarkable so let's take a look at our top five most popular sessions on youtube here are some fun ones all right number one is our famous session with the ceo of zoom which was way back in november 2019 before they were acquired by ericsson this one still gets thousands and thousands of views every month as you know it's very interesting because he talks about how to make a pivot when your app does not work out so that was pretty good that is pretty good advice for the founders here number two our ceo of hubspot chris brogan talking about what it takes to be successful in sas i think this one really hits home and maybe why we're still all doing these zoom meetings because we can't meet people in person anymore but so much more than just that a lot of great tips and advice for sales as well number three is our ceo of slack stewart butterfield which was pretty epic back when the company got founded i think what you'll find here with this one it's very interesting to see how he really thought about the product from day one and why and so much more than that but also a lot", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So as we’re getting ready to head into SaaStr Annual and all that comes with it,\nwe wanted to share some fun learnings from our YouTube channel:\nWe’ve just crossed 5 million views on\nYouTube.\nIt took us about a year. We think that’s pretty good!\nOur #1 video of all time is the classic “How to Get a $50,000 a Year SaaS Job”:\nYou can see more of our top videos here:\nhttps://www.saastr.com/\ntop-youtube-videos-of-all-time/\nA few other interesting metrics from YouTube:\n#1 Most Viewed Video: How To Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS (2.5m views)\n#10 Most Viewed Video: Top 3 Things to Know About Hiring Your First Sales Rep\n(8x) #15 Most Viewed Video: “The Future of B2B and AI” with OpenAI CEO Sam Altman\n(7x) #25 Most Viewed Video: “How to Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS”\n(6.3x)\n#1 Top Channel: How to Get a Job in SaaS (150K views)\n#5 Top Channel: B2B and AI Fundamentals (60K views)\n#10 Top Channel: Scaling for the First Time (40K views)\nAnd if you want to see what’s trending on YouTube … there are some fun learnings here too:\nMost Viewed Videos of All Time on SaaStr YouTube\n1. How To Get a $50k+ A Year in SaaS (2,500,000 Views)\n2. Top 3 Things to Know About Hiring Your First Sales Rep (400K views)\n3. The Future of B2B and AI with Sam Altman CEO OpenAI (360,000 views)\n4. How To Get a Job in SaaS (150k views)\n5. The Ultimate Guide to Scaling for the First Time (100K views)\n6.", "cleaned": "they're all great but if you haven't seen them i highly recommend watching um this one's fun we've had many versions over the years at disaster annuals since 2015 that we have an open air stage in the middle of our event campus where for two days for three days now depending on what year it is we just invite leaders to come share their stories from ceos co-founders cto coso so they can share how they did it share lessons learned share some mistakes and more importantly share what worked because that's really why we do these events is to learn from each other so this was one early version back when we started saster annuals in 2015 with mark benof twilio loretta jones from blackline which later sold for almost a billion dollars brendan cassidy who sold his startup box to salesforce uh the list goes on and on and then fast forward today we still do this every day with different speakers but we actually record those sessions as well and put them up on youtube and watch this slide you'll see we get thousands of views per video sometimes millions and people love hearing straight from the horses mouth how to scale how to grow faster how to build teams how to compete with others and there are always new ones coming out check the link here right under my name go to sastra.com click on media click on videos or youtube you will find most recent videos like the last session with eric wandsman founder ceo of pipedrive um we got another one yesterday with jeff lawson founder ceo of slack and zoom info so keep checking back for the latest content number four mistake first time founders make and this is really important", "balanced": "hey everybody i wanted to do a quick video on some fun statistics we have over at sastra.com um it's not as big as techcrunch or some other sites but uh the content is pretty good for founders and so if you haven't been there check it out five thousand people watching our videos on youtube that sounds like a lot and it is i mean it took us almost eight years to get from one to two million views on youtube um now it's only taken us nine months to go from three to four to five okay but it is interesting because what does this tell us well first of all it tells us our number one best performing piece of content which we didn't know until recently was an interview with benio bradley from snowflake when he came to saster annual last year amazing session right after snowflake ipo we did an interview with him he had done many interviews before his ipo but this was different it was really great how he talked about going long and and what makes cloud companies special how they're better than traditional software and uh just very thoughtful answers that folks loved and watched again and again so watch it everyone it's awesome second most popular our top two most popular sessions are two of my favorites and it's interesting both of them were more offbeat speakers neither of these guys would be considered classic speak sort of speakers but man did they hit it off hard eric wandsur from box who is absolutely fascinating guy and i don't even think he'd spoken anywhere else that much and then dennis leyba who has become a good friend of ours at saster and he runs the digital ocean and those are very interesting sessions to me because i wasn't sure if", "r32": "hey everybody just wanted to let you know that our YouTube channel has now crossed five million views it's pretty cool um I'm not sure what that means for the algorithm but if your videos are on YouTube try to push them out as much as possible even today they seem to be working okay so we're going to go through some of our top videos over time uh since COVID started actually one of the things I think is interesting we had this great session with Aaron levy from box he was the first cloud CEO at Salesforce when it IPO'd back in 2012 and we did a session with him how Box would scale from there and it really helped us understand why even in tough times especially in tougher times companies like HubSpot Shopify Zoom have kept growing because their customers love them and then we took that and turned it into a series of posts around the same idea which is these four categories of buyers Enterprise SMB self-serve freemium and we've added another category since then which is free edition but look at the traffic that got generated off of this post five million views by far our most viewed video and it wasn't the best video but it sparked something that people wanted to read more about so that's my learning here is try to get your content up everywhere try to put your blog content wherever else makes sense and just push it hard even though sometimes it may feel like an echo chamber or whatever but don't worry too much about that stuff push it out where ever works for you number two again this isn't necessarily the best video but it went viral for us it was a great session with Dave kellogg who is a great investor and advisor to many folks who wrote the first book on scaling up to 100 million and beyond and we did a session with him on", "repaired": "of our top viewed content it's a classic session that we did way back when we first started sastra annual uh from one of my favorite speakers which is linda brennan who was an sde at salesforce um she came to her first sastra event as a customer i think it was just after they launched their developer conference or something like that but she had been a great customer for years and then joined salesforce so this session really talked through what it takes to go enterprise how you should do it not trying too hard how to make your product more secure how to get into these large enterprises um some of the key points were don't try too hard with the big guys if you have 10 customers even five in the enterprise try to treat them all the same until you learn each other's language until you learn how to speak each other's language don't segment them by price point don't assume because one has a higher logo value than another that they deserve special treatment no let everyone on your team be equal until they learn the playbook and also relatedly focus on security early and often security is number three on every enterprise buyer's list after functionality and ease of deployment right there in the middle number two is easy to deploy number four is can I trust you how much work will this take to implement right security is always in the top three for bigger deals so don't wait don't say well we're small enough we'll worry about it later you won't have time to play catch up if you wait until 50 million are growing 200 percent next year and you finally decide okay now I want to add a full-time head of security and compliance and you've got 1000 customers already"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 410, "lemkin-clone-v5": 300, "lemkin-clone-v6": 264, "cleaned": 299, "balanced": 310, "r32": 333, "repaired": 311}}}}, {"id": "gold_007", "topic": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that SaaS sales is in a structural transition, with founders needing a sharper value proposition, more efficiency, and often a different go-to-market motion than the old growth-at-all-costs era. The lesson is not to wait for the old playbook to return, but to adapt hiring, messaging, and sales process to the new environment.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that SaaS sales has structurally changed, not just slowed temporarily.", "It should identify at least one concrete driver such as AI, buyer expectations, efficiency pressure, or changes in outbound/inbound behavior.", "It should explain what founders must change in response."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The $100B Industry Has Changed Forever: The New Normal for B2B Software Sales**\n\nI've been working with SaaS companies for over two decades, and I can confidently say that we're at a inflection point. A game-changer. The biggest shift in the way B2B software is sold since... well, probably ever.\n\nWhat's happening? Let me break it down:\n\n**The Rise of Digital-First**\n\nWe've all known for years that digital transformation was coming to every industry. And now, it's here. 80%+ of business buyers are researching and purchasing online before even talking to a sales rep (Source: Salesforce). That changes everything.\n\nThink about it - the entire customer journey is now online-first. Buyers don't want to talk to humans unless absolutely necessary (and often not at all!). They want to educate themselves, compare options, and make decisions on their own terms... in front of their screens.\n\n**AI-Powered Sales**\n\nThe other seismic shift: AI-powered sales tools are becoming ubiquitous. From chatbots to predictive analytics platforms, the use of machine learning is exploding. And it's not just for small companies - even large enterprises (think SAP, Salesforce) are embracing automation and AI-driven sales strategies.\n\nThis means that human reps can focus on high-touch, strategic deals... while machines handle the bulk of the low-hanging fruit. It's a huge efficiency gain, but also requires completely new skills from your team.\n\n**The End of \"Solution Selling\"**\n\nFor decades, we've been taught to sell solutions - not just products or services. But now? Buyers don't want to be sold to; they want to educate themselves and find answers on their own. The emphasis is shifting from sales rep as expert to sales rep as... facilitator.\n\nIn this new world, reps need to understand the customer's problem (not necessarily their solution) and provide guidance, not just pitches. This changes everything about your sales strategy, training programs, and team composition.\n\n**The New Normal: Account-Based Everything**\n\nSo what does all this mean for SaaS companies? It means that account-based marketing (ABM), sales development (SDR), and customer success teams must integrate like never before.\n\nYour entire organization needs to be aligned around the same key accounts - and work together seamlessly to win them over. This is no longer just about individual rep performance, but rather a team effort across multiple functions.\n\n**What's Next?**\n\nI know what you're thinking: \"Okay Jason, this all sounds exciting... but how do we actually make it happen?\"\n\nWell, my friends, that's where the real work begins. Here are some key takeaways:\n\n1. **Digital-First**: Make sure your website is optimized for online research and conversion.\n2. **AI-Powered Sales**: Invest in AI tools to augment your sales team (not replace them).\n3. **Solution Selling 2.0**: Focus on customer education, not just product pitches.\n4. **Account-Based Everything**: Integrate ABM, SDR, and CSM teams for a seamless experience.\n\nIt's time to adapt - or be left behind in the dust of history. The $100B industry has changed forever... and I'm excited (and nervous!) about what this means for all of us.\n\nWhat are your thoughts? How is your company evolving to meet these new challenges? Share your experiences, strategies, and successes below!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So for me, at least as an observer, the last year and a half has been the biggest change I’ve seen in B2B sales since the early days. The shift from enterprise to SMB is real.  Salesforce’s big push into “smaller” businesses is also real. But both are still just niches.\nBut what’s happened recently that isn’t niche, at least for many of us? More and more folks buying SaaS apps on a credit card now.\nNo one had predicted this in 2020-21 when we were all forced into Zoom calls with no time to talk sales. But it seems like an awful lot of folks are doing just that today — they’re paying up front for most deals, but also not waiting to close the deal if they can afford it on a credit card.\nAnd then … more and more folks buying SaaS apps themselves now. Without anyone else in their company even knowing about it.\nThis is huge, because sales has always been at least somewhat driven by the internal politics of every organization.\nSo what’s changing here? Why are we seeing so much change all at once?\nThe main driver here I think for many folks and companies is a “forced” shift to smaller deals in enterprise. When you were used to $300k+ ACV deal, it was a big deal. You went through multiple rounds of due diligence with procurement and legal.\nBut these days? More and more everyone wants to buy SaaS apps now. On their own credit card even! And they want to do it fast.\nSo we’re seeing all sorts of changes here:\nA lot more deals are being closed in just a few weeks, if not less than 30. A lot more folks buying on their own without anyone else involved\nMore and more “smaller” businesses using Salesforce as an example to buy from the enterprise leaders\nAnd for sure — credit cards have become a very common way of paying.\nThese are all big changes to traditional sales processes, at least in my lifetime.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we’re finally back to selling again, with real budgets and new features. And it’s very different than even just 12 months ago.\nWe’ve all been through this before, but I wanted to summarize why now is a bigger change for sales professionals than any other time in the last decade or so:\n#1. The buyers have changed dramatically\nThere are more of them — and they’re going into buying mode faster than ever. And it’s not just that you’ve got “more” prospects now, or at least, some of them.\nIt’s actually better for sales people when there is less competition and fewer vendors overall. At least in theory. But the buyers have changed even more dramatically:\nThey are more distributed\nMore decentralized\nLess centralized buying authority\nEven if they aren’t, their expectations to buy faster than ever — with no delays from IT, or marketing, or anyone else\n#2. The products have changed as well\nSo many of us got used to selling the same old product year in and year out for 10+ years.\nBut now we are all building new “AI” features into our products, that customers don’t really fully understand themselves yet. They know they want AI, but they aren’t sure what it does or how it works — at least not right away.\n#3. The sales processes have changed as well\nSo many of us got used to selling the same old way for 10+ years:\nOne rep per prospect\nA “qualified” lead was just a prospect that said Yes, we’re interested in buying your product now\nIt’s still true today — but it’s not enough anymore.\nNow we have to be more agile. More flexible. And more focused on the buyer themselves than ever before.\n#4. The sellers have changed as well\nSales reps are better trained and enabled today than they were 10 years ago, no question.", "cleaned": "A few years back, we had to do a big re-org at Adobe Sign / EchoSign. It was my first time really doing one and I wasn’t sure how it would go or who’d come out on top. So what did I do? I asked Marc Benioff if he’d let us use Salesforce’s org structure. He said sure — just make sure you follow their org chart. And that’s exactly what we did. We literally copied every org chart from Salesforce and put them into our org charts. Because it worked for them, so it should work for us. Fast forward to today, though. There are two changes coming that will change everything in sales forever … because they’re forcing everyone to sell differently than before. The first is AI Agents. They’ve already taken off as support agents and contact center agents. But now, there will be AI BDRs (Digital Revenue Officers) and AEs (Artificially Intelligent Enterprise Sales). These aren’t tools anymore. They can handle your entire sales process without any human intervention. Already some customers are using digital agents that know more about their business, have better product knowledge, and respond faster than many humans ever could. And the second is PLG (Product-Led Growth). Many of the leaders here were born pre-AI Agent era, but all of them are going more and more upmarket with AI, even if not all of them call themselves “AI”. Asana, HubSpot, Notion, Mailchimp, Monday.com, Calendly, etc. are all going deeper and deeper into larger deals. Even if they don’t call them “AI” deals. This creates tension between those focused on traditional enterprise sales motions and those embracing these new models.", "balanced": "And it’s not just me. The best sales execs I’ve worked with over the years have all come to the same conclusion as we entered 2022 — that you gotta get much better at selling to more stakeholders, and even if they can’t buy on their own … getting them budget for next year. In fact, my old boss at Adobe Sign / EchoSign said he was seeing this exact trend himself: “In general, our deals are taking longer, but there is an uptick in how many people involved in the deal process.” And so, yes, sales reps will still need to learn multiple stakeholders. They’ll still need to be product gurus. But now, everyone is going to want to help solve your customer problem. You’re gonna have folks from IT, procurement, security, marketing, ops, etc. wanting to talk to you before they write those checks. So if nothing else, give your team time to figure out how to work this new world. It takes some time to learn to close multi-stakeholder deals. Even though they should already know. 😉", "r32": "What’s changed? In many cases, not as much. But what has changed is that AI Agents can do better than humans at most tasks — for now. Not all of them. We haven’t even scratched the surface of where we could be with AI agents and GTM tools. But today, I’m confident you have access to an agent that can perform better than any human sales exec or marketing person at these core tasks: Outbound Email Sequences (for real) Follow-up sequences are so easy to automate in Salesforce and Hubspot and more, but really doing it well … was hard until very recently. The old days were so bad because humans would write terrible email copy themselves, or use templates they didn’t test, or send out cadence emails too fast. And then never follow up if prospects weren’t interested. Well, those days are behind us. Today there are several leading solutions that take your prospectus list and send out high quality email campaigns using your own collateral, with follow-ups every day or two. That sound simple? It isn’t. A good one will personalize each email to the individual prospect. Use data from previous interactions with them. Send tailored messages based on their interests, roles, company size, etc. Follow up quickly after no response. And keep going back with new insights until someone responds. A great AI agent will work harder here than 99% of humans ever did. Website Chat Bots Have Gone From Terrible To Good Enough If your website chatbot used to just say “Thank you for reaching out! Can I schedule a call?” Then apologize when you said you couldn’t talk right then — that’s gone.", "repaired": "But it’s not enough. Not anymore, at least for most startups and SMBs. Because there is now so much competition from AI to handle that work itself — instead of humans. This is why we’re seeing massive layoffs across B2B companies. It’s not just “AI replacing jobs” rhetoric. The reality is more nuanced but equally significant:\nThe human cost has been hidden until now\n. We’ve had AI agents handling parts of sales processes since around 2019-20. But they were mostly limited to repetitive tasks like email sequences or data entry. Human AEs could still do the high-value activities—coaching, relationship building, complex deal navigation. That’s changing fast. As AI gets smarter, more autonomous, and cheaper by the day, it’s eating into what was previously considered uniquely human territory. The productivity gains are real (and terrifying)\n. The numbers are staggering: if AI can process leads at 1/10th the cost of human SDRs while maintaining quality comparable to top-tier performers, you don’t need 100 people anymore—you might only need 10. And those 10 will be better because they’ll focus on the truly strategic work rather than grinding through administrative tasks. Your competitors are already moving here\n. Every major tech company worth watching is racing toward this future right now. OpenAI just announced they’re hiring an entire army of salespeople—and they have no traditional product yet! That tells you everything. When Salesforce launches Agentforce next year and declares victory over their $150M+ pipeline backlog using AI agents alone? You know something fundamental shifted."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 522, "lemkin-clone-v5": 343, "lemkin-clone-v6": 314, "cleaned": 277, "balanced": 180, "r32": 278, "repaired": 252}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The SaaS Game Has Changed. Again.**\n\nI've been doing this for over two decades, and I can confidently say that we're at an inflection point - one of the biggest changes in SaaS sales in over 15 years.\n\nFor those who don't know me, my name is Jason Lemkin, and I co-founded Echosign (now Adobe Sign) back in 2004. We bootstrapped it to a $100M+ exit without ever taking VC funding. Along the way, I've learned a thing or two about what makes SaaS companies successful.\n\nOver the years, we've seen several major shifts in how B2B sales work:\n\n* From on-site demos to online trials (circa 2007)\n* From phone and email only to social media integration (circa 2010)\n* From basic CRM to AI-powered automation tools (circa 2015)\n\nBut I'm here to tell you that the latest change is different. It's not just a tweak or an evolution; it's a revolution.\n\n**The Rise of \"No Sales\" SaaS**\n\nFor years, we've assumed that every B2B sale required at least some level of sales engagement - phone calls, emails, demos, etc. But with the proliferation of automation tools and data-driven marketing, I'd argue that this is no longer true.\n\nToday's top-performing SaaS companies are those that can get 80-90% or more of their new customers without ever speaking to a human sales rep. That's right - \"no sales\" as", "lemkin-clone-v5": "#1 Thing That's Going To Change Everything In SaaS And Venture Capital - At Least For A While\nThe Bottom Line Up Front:\nWhat we're seeing now is a massive reset in how sales is done. This will impact every single B2B company and startup that sells software or products, no matter the size.\nWhy it matters for founders building B2B companies today\nThis change isn't about new tools or features—it's fundamentally changing how buyers engage with sellers:\nBuyers Are Now In Control\nThe buyer has more power than ever before. They don't need to talk to a salesperson anymore—there are digital platforms, AI agents, and self-service options available.\nSalespeople Need To Adapt\nThe sales function is going through massive disruption. Sales teams are being forced to become customer success teams overnight. The skills required have changed dramatically:\nTechnical knowledge: Buyers want people who understand their specific use case and can implement solutions quickly.\nData-driven decision-making: Sales reps need to be able to analyze data, identify patterns, and provide actionable insights based on that analysis rather than just relying on intuition or product features.\nThe shift is happening at an unprecedented pace.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The biggest change I've seen in sales since 2007. No one wants to meet anymore. And that's it. The rest is just fine tuning how you do meetings. How you prepare for them, pitch during them, and close after them.\n\nBut no one really wants a meeting anymore.\n\nIt's time to evolve from this if you want to stay ahead in SaaS sales.", "cleaned": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years The world has changed so much since I started selling SaaS. So many new vendors, AI making everything better and worse, budget under siege … it’s different today than ever before. But one thing is still true: sales remains all about relationships. And that means we’re back to basics now, for real. Because no matter how much AI can do for us — from support to marketing to coding, it doesn’t really help you build those relationships with customers and prospects. Not yet at least. We’ve had some time to get used to this change already. Since March 2022 or so, when the first AI SDRs came out. It was just for outbound then, but even then, they were good enough to replace human BDRs. Then in 2025, AI agents could handle inbound qualification. By year-end, they should be able to close most deals on their own. That’s what’s coming next. So if nothing else, understand this is where things are going. If your team isn’t working harder right now than before, something is off. You aren’t doing the work yourself. Or you don’t have a great VP of Sales leading the way. This is why customer success teams need to be so strong. They will become part of sales again. At least for bigger accounts. Because CS needs to be in every call with a prospect. Every single one. Not just once you “close” them. A bit more here:", "balanced": "Why Now Is The Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years The change isn't that sales is harder. It's that it has to be so, so much better. So many more folks are out there looking for new apps and vendors than ever before. And yet, they're just not going to buy from anyone mediocre or even good today. They'll settle for... no one at all. That may sound crazy but it really isn't. We've settled on our social media tools. On our CRM. Etc. Even if we'd sort of like something else. If you want me to do work to find an app I don't need? Or to learn your product cold when I already have a vendor? No thanks. Not unless you truly solve my problems 10x better. More here -> https://saastr.ai/article/why-now-is-the-biggest-change-in-saas-sales-in-15-years …", "r32": "It's just too much work. It is what it is, and you gotta do it. But the sales teams that are going to win over the next few years aren't the ones with the highest closing rates or the best pitch decks. They're the ones who can actually deliver value before they ask for money. And that means being willing to take on less revenue per deal, handle more objections upfront, and be honest about whether your product really solves their problem. The companies that figure this out will dominate their markets. The ones that don't will continue to hemorrhage leads because prospects won't trust them enough to buy without a demo. The change isn't coming soon—it's already here. You can either adapt now and stay ahead, or keep doing things the way we've always done them and watch your pipeline dwindle while competitors close deals they shouldn't have. Your choice.", "repaired": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years And it’s not because AI is so much better at selling. It’s that … everyone now has access to all the best sales tools, and can use them for real. The best GTM platforms. The top SEO agencies. The leading outbound tools. The magic inbound agents. Everyone will be able to run their own playbook by $2m-$5m ARR or less. No more waiting to hire an agency until you are ready. You’ll be ready. You just need to pick which ones to start with, and how. This is already changing so many things: - Agencies don’t have as long of a moat anymore - But they also know how to do it at scale. So some customers will want them still. - A lot of sales leaders aren’t going to like this change. They like being gatekeepers to new vendors. - Many sales reps won’t like this either. They like being experts. - Most founders I talk to are actually scared of this. They’re worried they can’t compete with someone who knows the secrets to the platform. That’s OK. You can. Because there are no secrets anymore. There’s no one really good at any vendor anymore. Not unless they’ve been doing it since Day 1."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 229, "lemkin-clone-v5": 192, "lemkin-clone-v6": 63, "cleaned": 252, "balanced": 136, "r32": 151, "repaired": 217}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X POST**\n\nFolks, I've been around the block a few times. Been selling software as a service (SaaS) since the early days. And let me tell you - **NOW IS THE BIGGEST CHANGE IN SAAS SALES IN OVER 15 YEARS**.\n\nI'm not talking about some incremental tweak to the sales process. No, no, no. This is a fundamental shift in how SaaS companies buy and sell software.\n\nHere's what I mean:\n\n**1) The Rise of \"Buy-Now\" Sales**\n\nFor years, we've been conditioned to think that every sale needs to be a long, drawn-out process - 6+ months from first touch to close. Not true anymore! With the rise of free trials and demos, customers can now experience", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Why Now is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years - The End of the Mid-Market - We've never seen anything like this. - It's just too expensive to buy from a vendor you don't know. - And it has nothing to do with AI, or pricing, or features. - What's happening is buyers have more choices than ever. - They can buy from someone they trust 100%. - Or go for the latest and greatest. - Just not both.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So this is the biggest change in SaaS sales I've seen since 2007. And that was a big one. But it's happening now, and it will be a huge change. It won't be fun for many reps. They'll find out they can do better. But as an investor, I see this all over on my portfolio. Here are the changes:\n\n1. AI is really starting to replace some of SaaS sales itself. Not the human part yet — but lots of it. We've seen a 30% reduction in customer support tickets since we rolled out ChatGPT for SaaStr AI, and I'm seeing this all over my portfolio with other apps. But in B2B, there's so much more to replace than just tech support.\n\n2.", "cleaned": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "balanced": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "r32": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "repaired": "Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 117, "lemkin-clone-v5": 82, "lemkin-clone-v6": 124, "cleaned": 12, "balanced": 12, "r32": 12, "repaired": 12}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Why Now Is the Biggest Change in SaaS Sales in 15+ Years", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a speech in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr and serial entrepreneur:\n\n**Title:** The Unstoppable Force Disrupting SaaS Sales: What You Need to Know Now\n\nLadies and gentlemen, founders, investors, and sales leaders, I'm here today to talk about something that has profound implications for our industry. Something so significant, it's shaking the very foundations of how we sell software as a service.\n\nFor over 15 years now, SaaS sales have followed a relatively predictable pattern. We've had growth hacks like freemium models and email marketing. We've seen the rise of account-based selling and predictive analytics. But what I'm here to tell you is that all this has been disrupted by an unstoppable force: AI.\n\n**The AI Revolution**\n\nArtificial intelligence, machine learning, and natural language processing are no longer just buzzwords. They're now business-critical tools for any serious SaaS company looking to scale effectively. And the implications for sales are profound.\n\nFor starters, AI is making customer interactions more personal than ever before. Chatbots can respond to support queries in seconds, freeing up your human reps to focus on high-touch deals. But here's where it gets even more interesting: AI can also analyze a prospect's behavior and tailor their experience, all without any direct involvement from sales teams.\n\n**The End of the Sales Process as We Know It**\n\nNow, you might be thinking, \"Jason, what about our beloved sales processes? Don't they still apply?\" Well, here's the thing: AI has disrupted more than just a few tactics. It's fundamentally changing how we go to market and engage with customers.\n\nThink about it this way: when did your last customer ever say, \"I love the fact that I have to fill out 10 forms before speaking to anyone on my sales team\"? Exactly! That's why AI-powered automation tools are now being used to pre-qualify leads in real-time. They determine whether a prospect is even worthy of human attention.\n\n**The Sales Leader's Dilemma**\n\nAs a SaaS founder, you have two choices:\n\n1. Adapt and learn how to integrate AI into your sales strategy.\n2. Get left behind as the competition takes advantage of this new reality.\n\nHere are just a few ways you can start leveraging AI in sales today:\n\n* **Use AI-powered CRM integration** that automatically updates contact info and activity logs.\n* **Implement conversational marketing tools**, which let prospects engage with your brand 24/7, without human intervention.\n* **Explore data-driven account scoring models** to ensure reps are spending their time on the most promising opportunities.\n\n**The Future of SaaS Sales: Embracing AI**\n\nI know this might feel overwhelming at first. But here's what I want you to take away from today:\n\nAI is not a threat, it's an opportunity. It can augment your sales team and create new efficiencies that were previously unimaginable.\n\nEmbracing AI in your sales strategy will help you stay ahead of the curve as this revolution continues to unfold.\n\n**In Conclusion**\n\nThe SaaS industry has seen its fair share of disruptions over the years, but none as significant as this. So I urge you: don't get caught off guard by the unstoppable force that is AI-driven sales innovation.\n\nInstead, join me in embracing the future and unlocking new levels of growth for your company through AI-powered sales excellence.\n\nThank you!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I don’t know what’s going on but I can tell you that it is different, it’s changed. It’s not just that we’ve all gotten more efficient at sales and marketing. So for a long time, we were growing like crazy. And then everything got hit by the pandemic in 2020. A lot of folks quit their jobs to start new ventures. I did too. I had never been fired from a job before. It was just me. My wife and my kids. We lost our house. But it all came back after that year. In fact, since then, growth has accelerated again. So the biggest thing we’ve seen in SaaS sales is how quickly new leaders have come up to take over. New CEOs of public companies are being hired faster than ever now. The bar for getting a job at a top tech company isn’t lower, it’s higher today than every before. And yet, the best of them seem to be able to build an incredible team in weeks if not days with nothing but Zoom and Slack. I’ve never seen anything like this. A new CEO comes in who was already successful and is expected to take a top tech company public in just 2 years or less. It’s going to happen again, we all know it is. If you have an investor now that wants the next OpenAI, they’re not looking for something that will get there in five years. They want one that can get there in three years. And with AI agents and other tools out there today, I think this is possible. You just need 8-10 great reps to make it happen.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I was at an event with a bunch of founders this week and we were discussing how many folks are still going out to meet potential customers. We all agree that’s part of sales, but no one has ever been good at it. I said that the biggest change is now you can just talk to them on Zoom. If they want to get together in person? Then they’ll show up.\n\nIt sounds simple and obvious, but for years we’ve seen more and more folks moving away from going out to meet customers in person. Some of this was due to the fact that it’s hard to travel so much anymore, at least without a lot of planning ahead. But I think it was really just time.\n\nSo many of us have gotten back to meeting our customers on Zoom after all these years. It may be less exciting than seeing them in person. And there are some deals you want to close when they’re actually out together and in the same place, like a joint pilot or demo together.\n\nBut it’s also just so much easier today for most deals to meet customers over Zoom. And I think we’ll never go back from that now.", "cleaned": "what's going to happen you know I've been investing and working with SAS companies for over two decades um we have so much data now that we're all remote that if you look at what happened on January first 2020 versus today in terms of Revenue retention it has changed almost nothing right the number of customers that churned from our base didn't change after March 16th when people went home they did not change there was no change but the way sales is structured has fundamentally changed and this chart on the left which is from Gartner shows how many folks are using AI tools today just from this last quarter and then on the bottom chart on the right is where it will be by next year and you can see these crazy growth rates in adoption across every category and you can also see some categories like coding are growing faster than others but everything is growing fast in AI so you know my point is maybe it'll come back somewhat to normal or something like it I don't think anyone wants to do six figure deals remotely I think everyone would love to get back into an office together as Founders and executives and VPS of sales whatever level but I'm pretty sure one thing is certain that we are never going back to the world of 2019.", "balanced": "sales and I know it's stressful but if you're gonna make me buy your product with AI then do it right or don't expect my money so that's all I can tell you um there are some great tools out there there is a lot of noise there's a lot of stuff that doesn't work that just generates a bunch of useless leads and I think sales ops teams have been overwhelmed by this explosion of vendors for AI sales and marketing but at least be aware of them at least be on top of them to see what works because if nothing else the buyers will expect more from you and they'll get frustrated when they don't get that better experience okay last point related to this which is you've got to train everyone in your company to sell you now again we talked about this back in SAS annual like two years ago but as much as I hate to say this and go through it again it's even more important today than ever before why why does every single person in your company need to learn how to sell yes I know you already know that but let me explain it with an example here and this one came up recently I had a VC ask me to invest in a startup I'd invested in a seed round many years ago and he asked me what did I really love about the company well I said look I haven't seen their product since covid times and I'm not sure I want to invest until I meet with the CEO and maybe meet with a few other stakeholders and the VC was shocked and he said why would you want to meet with anyone else why wouldn't you just want to talk to the CEO alone and I said yeah that sounds good but actually I need to meet with the sales team too and the", "r32": "it's just not going to work anymore that it will take you six months to get set up on Salesforce and this is why you need an AI BDR or at least someone who has deployed two AI Agents for your startup because they know how to do it so we're seeing the biggest change in SAS sales since I started doing these podcasts which was well over a decade ago I've been doing these podcasts almost every day now with hundreds of thousands of listeners but when I first did them way back then our number one topic was always inbound marketing and outbound email and all those things still matter today but nothing matters more than what we talked about today using AI to be much more efficient and to free yourself from the grind from repetitive tasks and to really go out there and make customers happy and close deals like never before even if you don't have a whole lot of leads coming into your funnel so thank you everybody for listening we'll see you next time", "repaired": "sales and marketing are going to have to be really good at that otherwise they'll get overwhelmed with all this inbound demand so that's my top learning for Founders um it is not necessarily a game changer for most folks but boy if you can figure out how to do it right now you're gonna come out of this year much stronger than you thought possible thank you everyone I appreciate your attention uh thanks everybody [Applause]"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 547, "lemkin-clone-v5": 278, "lemkin-clone-v6": 206, "cleaned": 232, "balanced": 328, "r32": 181, "repaired": 77}}}}, {"id": "gold_008", "topic": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that founders cannot ignore public multiples because they influence valuation, fundraising, and investor psychology, but they also should not let them run the company. The right move is to understand the implications, tighten execution, and build the kind of business that works under tougher market assumptions.", "facts": ["The answer should explain that public multiples matter because they shape private funding expectations and exit math.", "It should distinguish between what founders can control and what they cannot.", "It should advise founders to focus on fundamentals rather than obsessing over market chatter."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Wrench With The Same Anxiety?**\n\n Ah, public multiples.  Those dreaded metrics that seem to haunt every founder's sleep-deprived brain.\n\nAs a VC, I've seen my fair share of founders obsessing over their company's valuation relative to its revenue growth rate - aka \"Public Multiples\". And let me tell you, it can get ugly fast.\n\nLately, even our own VCs have been fretting about public multiples. They're worried that if your private company isn't growing quickly enough in terms of revenue, or worse yet, if your valuation is falling behind the market's expectations - you might not be able to attract future funding at a good price (or at all!).\n\nNow, I get it: as an investor, there's no shortage of data to suggest that companies with high public multiples tend to outperform those without. But here's the thing:\n\n**Public Multiples Are Not A Hard And Fast Rule**\n\nIn fact, I'd argue they're more like a loosely-structured suggestion - one that should be taken into account but not obsessively adhered to.\n\nThink about it: every company is different. Some have unique products or services that command premium pricing (Airbnb comes to mind). Others may be growing rapidly in areas with huge addressable markets, even if their revenue growth rate isn't as high on a multiple basis (I'm looking at you, Zoom!).\n\nAnd then there are the companies that don't care about public multiples. They're too busy disrupting industries or building massive moats around themselves to worry about what some analyst thinks they should be worth.\n\n**So Why Are Your VCs Freaking Out About Public Multiples?**\n\nWell, it's simple: as investors, we have a fiduciary duty to our Limited Partners (LPs) - the institutions and family offices that trust us with their capital. And if those LPs are getting nervous about public multiples, you can bet your last dollar that we're going to take that into account when making investment decisions.\n\nBut here's the thing: just because your VCs might be worried about public multiples doesn't mean you should be too!\n\n**Why You Shouldn't Worry About Public Multiples**\n\n1. **You have more control over growth rate than valuation**: Focus on building a great product, executing well, and growing as quickly as possible - these are the factors that will ultimately drive your company's value.\n2. **Public multiples aren't a guarantee of success (or failure)**: Even companies with high public multiples can still stumble or falter in other areas (e.g., profitability).\n3. **You're not alone**: Every founder worries about valuation at some point - it's normal!\n\n**What To Do Instead**\n\n1. **Focus on growth, execution, and innovation**: These are the things that will drive your company's value over time.\n2. **Don't obsess over public multiples (yet)**: Wait until you have a solid understanding of how they apply to your own business before getting too caught up in metrics anxiety.\n3. **Communicate with your investors**: If you're worried about something, share those concerns with us! We want to help.\n\n**In Conclusion**\n\nPublic multiples might be on the minds of our VCs (and yours!), but let's keep things in perspective: they're just one metric among many that will influence your company's success. Stay focused on what truly matters - building a great business and driving growth - and don't worry too much about what some analyst thinks you should be worth.\n\nThanks for reading, folks!\n\nBest,\nJason", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we’ve been doing some investing lately at our new $500m fund at SaaStr Fund.\nIt’s always fun to invest with your friends and co-investors.  Especially when they can give you a second opinion as things develop. It also keeps the bar high — no one is going to want to be a dumb investor in anyone that works with them too much! 🙂\nBut sometimes, it can lead to some interesting conversations.\nI had an interesting conversation recently with a very top VC firm.  They are investing at the same level as us and have done so for years now.  We’ve been co-investing together since 2016 or something like that.\nA couple of recent deals were pretty competitive, so we both ended up in them. And I asked what they thought about one of these recent deals. “I’m worried,” they said. What do you mean? “These public multiples are getting crazy.”\nI couldn’t disagree more.  Public multiples for the best SaaS companies have always been high — sometimes quite high.\nAs a rough rule, when you look at some of the very top cloud stocks like ServiceNow and Snowflake that trade at 20x revenue or higher, it’s pretty darn close to what we’ve seen in the best VC-funded start-ups.  Even today!  And I can tell you — the public markets have been more generous with valuations on great SaaS companies than ever.\nBut why?  Why would a VC be worried about high multiples if that means they get a ton of money for their fund, and the company is worth a lot?\nI think the answer to this question is “they shouldn’t be.”\nThe only time VCs worry is when no one seems willing to pay it.  When in fact everyone wants to buy at some insane multiple — say 100x or so.\nIn that case, they are worried because there’s no way to get their money out of the company.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I’ve been doing this for almost 20 years now.\nI know it can be tough to keep going at times.\nAt least you don’t have to worry about public markets anymore like some of us do:\nBut — it’s just not that complicated. Your VCs are worried, but they shouldn’t be.\nYou’re still growing. You’re still profitable (if needed). They made a pretty good bet on you. Yes, the multiples in Public Markets aren’t quite as high today for many tech companies than they were at their peaks. But — this is just normal.\nThe markets always go up-and-down. And yes, it’s harder to raise new capital now from VCs that are worried about public market valuations (and even more so when the IPO window is closed). Even if you could — would you want to be in a $100B+ company? It’s not fun.\nBut your betas matter. Is growth strong? Are profits good, at least for now?\nIf yes, then it doesn’t really matter what public market multiples are today.\nIt will get better again someday. The markets always do bounce back and go up-and-down in cycles. So don’t worry about that too much if you have real revenue and a path to real profitability.\nA related post here:\nWhen Growth Slows — Who Should Be Worried? You Or Your VCs?\nRelated Posts\nThe 3 Types of Venture Capital Funds: Which Are Right For You (or Your Startup)\nYour VPs Don't Need To Have Ever Been A VP. But They Do Need to Have Done It Before.\nDear SaaStr: How Long Does a VC Take to Get Back?", "cleaned": "But I worry you’ll see more and more start-ups that are just too capital efficient to IPO at $1B+ valuations — or even close. It’s hard enough with public multiples where there is real pressure on growth rates to be higher than they were pre-ipo’ing. But if the bar for an IPO drops further (i.e., to $250m ARR growing 50%) … then folks will settle for much lower revenue per share. That can lead to a very slow-growing stock price after IPO. And ultimately, not being able to raise any new money at all from public markets until profits kick in. A related post here :", "balanced": "But if you are growing even just 30% or so at $50m ARR and have some decent net revenue retention … then probably not too much to worry about. Even with multiples down. If nothing else — ask your investors how they’re doing. Ask them what their goals are for this year, next year, etc. And see where everyone is aligned. Or not. The last thing I’d say here is that it’s time to be honest with yourself about what kind of outcome you want from your startup. The best outcomes often don’t happen until $1B+ market caps. That may seem far off today. But it wasn’t all that long ago public SaaS companies were trading at 20x revenues on average (and as high as 40x). So it will come back. It almost always does. Just make sure you do everything possible to keep it coming back to you! At least, maybe take advantage of low valuations to raise more money than planned. A bit more here: 5 Simple Tips For Raising More Money Than Planned | SaaStr", "r32": "So there’s been quite an explosion on social media and elsewhere re: how worried everyone is that public SaaS companies are trading at just 5x ARR. That’s down from as high as 15x ARR in the Go-Go Days of 2021. And folks are spooked. I’ve even seen some CEOs talk to their teams about this. But here’s my take — it doesn’t matter that much if you’re growing > 30% or so. At least not for most of us. Here’s why: First, VC funds themselves have multi-year lifespans (10+ years), and they can write off “bad” investments over time. A mediocre investment isn’t necessarily a huge problem. Second, top growth startups always raise more money than planned, and often double their Series B check sizes. So in many cases, a 50%-70% haircut in valuation won’t really hurt you too much. Third, and maybe most importantly, don’t let your existing investors’ concerns weigh too heavily on you. If you do a great job building something valuable, it will still be worth something — regardless of what multiples others trade at. If you need capital, sure, worry. But otherwise … try not to sweat it. It may seem like a lot of doom and gloom out there right now, but … The Cloud Keeps Growing And Thriving pic.twitter.com/8Tl7I2f9eK", "repaired": "So I’ve been doing VC for over a decade now — and while things have changed since the peak of late 2020-early 2021, one thing has stayed constant:\nVCs are worried\n.\nThey worry when times are great because they think it’s all too good to be true (and sometimes it is), and they worry even more when things get harder.  The best ones just keep going, no matter what.  But everyone worries, especially if their latest check hasn’t had time to mature yet.\nAnd right now, public multiples are worrisome indeed:\nThe median public B2B company trades at less than 5x ARR — the lowest multiple we’ve seen in decades\n. Even growth companies are trading at barely double-digit multiples, down from nearly 30x in early 2021. This isn’t a cyclical correction; this is structural change. And yes, that will impact private valuations, too. If you’re not raising capital anytime soon, probably don’t sweat it much\n. The reality is most startups outside of AI aren’t seeing any material impact on fundraising today. What matters is whether your investors believe you’ll compound fast enough to justify current prices. If you can demonstrate strong unit economics and clear paths to profitability or hypergrowth, you might actually benefit from lower expectations around exit timelines. But if you’re relying heavily on high-growth assumptions to raise rounds, you need to adjust those plans quickly. That doesn’t mean you should panic."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 573, "lemkin-clone-v5": 322, "lemkin-clone-v6": 270, "cleaned": 106, "balanced": 178, "r32": 216, "repaired": 237}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post:\n\n**Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Be Concerned, Too?**\n\nAs I've been talking to my portfolio companies lately, it's become clear that the current market volatility is causing some real anxiety among our investors - and by extension, their limited partners.\n\nSpecifically, public multiples have been dropping across the board, raising concerns about valuations. And let's be honest: VCs are always worried about getting out of their investments at a good price.\n\nBut here's the thing: unless you're preparing for an exit in the next 6-12 months (and even then), I'd advise against panicking. Why? Because public multiples have historically been quite volatile, and they don't necessarily dictate your private company's valuation.\n\nIn fact, my team and I at Storm Ventures have seen some of our companies actually benefit from this market correction - their valuations haven't budged as much as you might think!\n\nSo what should founders be doing instead? **Focus on building a strong business**, with a solid financial foundation and clear growth trajectory. The rest will take care of itself.\n\nTo all my fellow entrepreneurs out there, I'd love to hear from you: how are you navigating this market uncertainty?\n\n**Some context for those interested**: the Russell 2000 index (small-cap stocks) has dropped around 20% over the past few months, while some tech indices have fallen by as much as 30-40%. But don't just take my word for it - go talk to your CFO or financial", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Public multiples are down to 10x for most public SaaS companies today\nThat means every dollar of revenue is worth only $1\nThis isn't a reason not to invest in private SaaS companies\nIt's just more important than ever that you have something better than the public benchmarks to offer investors", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The latest from SaaStr:\nPublic multiples are down\nBut that doesn’t mean your start-up is “overvalued”\n— It means you may have to raise at lower valuations in the future\n\nDon't worry about public market multiple right now. Just get back to growth and profitability.\nAnd a few notes on this:", "cleaned": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? So many founders I talk to are worried about how their VC will react if they don’t hit plan for next year. Or even worse, if growth slows down materially. They think the VCs are going to freak out. And maybe — sometimes that’s right. But my experience is most often … not so much. Why? First, it takes time. It may take your investors 12 months or more just to see this one slide, and realize things have changed. Second, it varies by fund size and type. Smaller funds especially, at least up until $100m-$200m+, need you to grow quickly enough to get them another check(s) back into the fund. That can be as simple as tripling over the life of an investment. Even with some bumps along the way. Third, it doesn’t matter too much unless you raise >$50m+ from a single lead investor. This really only matters when there is a true “lead” investor, and that lead has committed more than $50m (or so). Otherwise, no one cares all that much what happens after the round closes. Fourth, it doesn’t matter too much if your existing VCs still believe in you. If they do, then a few public multiples issues won’t matter. Fifth, it doesn’t matter at all if you’re in Fund II-IV. As long as your existing investors make money, they’ll be fine. They’ve already made their money on prior rounds.", "balanced": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? I know many founders are worried that public SaaS multiples will stay low forever and that makes it harder to raise venture capital. But … is this really true for you? The answer may surprise you.", "r32": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? Here’s what I’m seeing: 🤷‍♀️ Venture funds are raising less money for next round — and often not even asking 👎 Funds are more selective on new investments than before 😬 VC valuations are way down across the board (but still up from lows) 💰 More M&A activity at lower prices The best startups don’t really worry that much about this stuff But they should be aware of it … And know how to use it to your advantage", "repaired": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? A few thoughts on this chart, and why it matters to your startup’s future: #1. If you’re at $100M+ ARR growing >30%, then ignore public multiples — for now At least until you are past IPO stage. It just doesn’t matter that much. The markets will catch up with you once you go public. Most importantly, don’t let anyone tell you growth has slowed or stopped because the stock prices have fallen from their peak. This is what happens every decade or so. What does matter most at scale (post-$50m+ ARR) is getting to cash-flow positive if possible, and being profitable ideally by $200m ARR … and making sure you have enough capital to get there without having to raise too much. And growing fast enough to support an IPO when ready. That’s all that really matters. If you’ve got decent growth, ignore public multiples. They’ll come back around. #2. But if you’re <~$10m ARR, well … worry a bit More than the average founder cares to admit. Growth investors need a relatively high multiple these days to hit their targets. So as crazy as it may seem, if you’re pre-Traction or early Traction (<~$8m ARR), maybe find a way to slow down a smidge if you can afford it. Just not more than that. Because raising in today’s world is still hard. You want to make sure you aren’t pricing yourself out of the market"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 250, "lemkin-clone-v5": 51, "lemkin-clone-v6": 51, "cleaned": 248, "balanced": 49, "r32": 92, "repaired": 249}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X**\n\n\"Your VCs are worried about public multiples. They're freaking out because your company's valuation is 'too high' or 'not sustainable'. \n\nNewsflash: they don't care if it's a 'bubble' or not. What matters is what the market says your company is worth.\n\nSo, do you need to worry? Only if you let them dictate your strategy. Don't get caught up in their fear-mongering. Stay focused on building an amazing product that solves real problems for customers.\n\nYour VCs are paid to make money off of your success (or failure). They're not invested in what's best for the company or its users.\n\nYou, on the other hand? You have skin in the game. Your reputation is on the line. Don't let", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So a lot of folks are worried today that public SaaS multiples have fallen below private valuations.\nI am not sure I agree:\nA $100m ARR company at 5x-6x is the same as a $200m ARR publicly traded company. The only difference in value is liquidity and lower risk.\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nOctober 1, 2024\nA lot of folks are worried today that public SaaS multiples have fallen below private valuations. We’re all worried about a few things:\nPublic comps being lower than last year and the quarter before.\nThat’s true for many SaaS companies. But it may not matter as much as you think:", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I see it all over Twitter and LinkedIn:\n“VCs are worried now that public multiples have fallen.”\nHere’s what you should be focused on right now:\n\nPublic multiples matter for one reason: They’re the bar your VCs use to value you. If they fall, your valuation falls.\n\nBut here’s something important:\n\nYour VCs are not going to care if a start-up is worth $20m or $60m at 2x ARR in Q1 2026. That’s just noise for them.\nWhat matters? Your progress. How well you’re executing against your plan. Are you hitting the numbers that matter most?\nBecause here’s something interesting:\nThe top Cloud and SaaS companies are still trading at insane multiples.\n\nYou can see it all over Twitter: “Pal", "cleaned": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples.\n\nDo You Need To Worry, Too?", "balanced": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples.\n\nDo You Need To Worry, Too?", "r32": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too? It's a big topic today... but for most SaaS companies growing >20% at scale, not really. Because you can grow into them. And also, they are still pretty high if you have strong NRR and growth at scale.", "repaired": "Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples.  Do You Need To Worry, Too?"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 111, "lemkin-clone-v6": 121, "cleaned": 13, "balanced": 13, "r32": 51, "repaired": 13}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Your VCs Are Worried About Public Multiples. Do You Need To Worry, Too?**\n\nHey everyone, thanks for joining me today to discuss one of the most pressing concerns facing our industry right now: public multiples.\n\nYou know who's worried about this issue? Your venture capitalists. Yep, those guys who are supposed to be experts in valuation and growth strategy are getting a little... anxious.\n\nBut here's the thing: I don't think you need to worry as much as your VCs do. And today, we're going to break down why that is.\n\n**What Are Public Multiples?**\n\nBefore we dive into the weeds, let me quickly explain what public multiples are. In short, they refer to the ratio of a company's market capitalization (or price per share) compared to its earnings or revenue.\n\nThink about it like this: if you're a publicly traded company making $100 million in revenue and your stock is trading at 10x that figure, then your public multiple is 10. Simple enough.\n\n**Why Are Your VCs Worried?**\n\nYour VCs are worried because they think the current public multiples are unsustainable. They see companies with high growth rates, but also extremely high valuations relative to their earnings power.\n\nAnd let's be honest: it's a fair concern. The S&P 500 is trading at over 25x earnings right now – that's crazy-high by historical standards!\n\nBut here's the thing: this isn't necessarily a bad sign for you and your startup. In fact, I'd argue it's not even relevant to you... yet.\n\n**Why You Shouldn't Worry (Yet)**\n\nThink about it like this: as a private company, you're not trading on public markets. Your valuation is based on whatever deal you can get from investors who are willing to buy into your growth story – which might be higher or lower than what the public market thinks.\n\nAnd even if the public multiples do come crashing down, I think it's unlikely that it will have a direct impact on private company valuations in the short term. The two markets just operate too differently.\n\n**What Should You Do?**\n\nSo what should you do with this information? Here are my top three takeaways:\n\n1. **Focus on growth**: Instead of worrying about public multiples, focus on building your business and growing that revenue (and earnings) as fast as possible.\n2. **Stay disciplined in fundraising**: Don't get caught up in the hype around high valuations or low interest rates – stay focused on getting a good deal for yourself and your investors.\n3. **Keep an eye on the horizon, not the rearview mirror**: Your VCs might be worried about public multiples, but you should be thinking about what's coming next: will there be more investment opportunities? Will valuations change?\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nIn conclusion, I think it's time to take a deep breath and calm down – at least when it comes to public multiples. They're not as relevant to your startup or growth story as they might seem.\n\nInstead of worrying about what the market thinks, focus on building an amazing business that will thrive regardless of where the markets go. And always remember: your VCs are worried about their own portfolios and investments – you should be focused on yours!\n\nThanks for joining me today!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I don't know what to say other than your public multiples are down\n#founder #funding #investing #startup #VC #venturecapital #entrepreneurship #business #finance #investment #privateequity #publicmarkets #stockmarket #valuation #multiples #growth #scale #SaaS", "lemkin-clone-v6": "so public multiples are down we all know that and it's been going on for some time but what does this mean to your company right so look I'm not a great financial analyst I've never really done finance in my career or worked at a big bank even though I went to Stanford business school which is the last place you might expect to learn about finance they did teach us how to do spreadsheets and excel and all that but what we learned was pretty simple so public multiples are down now it's not like it has never been this way since 2022 or something right a lot of times as an investor I've seen multiples in the public markets go up and down over time they may be higher than average for some companies at some points maybe even multiples of 30 to 40x revenue just crazy numbers but also what's happened is it hasn't always been that way so look if you take a look at something like zoom or slack or one of these other companies that IPO'd at the end of last year and they were all valued at almost or more than 20 billion dollars in many cases today with much lower multiples those valuations are worth half as much but what does this mean to your company well it means you're probably not going to be worth two times 10x 15x revenue anymore right maybe one of these days we'll go back to the good old days where public markets will give a company like zoom or slack crazy multiples but there's no evidence that that has happened for years and so what does this mean it means you're probably not going to be worth two times 10x 15x your", "cleaned": "have a lot of good advice for Founders on this but it's not that complicated if you're at 10 million or less in revenue and your burn is low don't worry about public company multiples there are only so many startups they can invest in each year um there's only so much Venture Capital available to them and more importantly like any investor VC's want to make money on their investments right we all have different goals some folks want to make money back fast others may be happy with longer term gains but almost everyone wants to make money on their investments and because they want to make money on their Investments they will look for ways to buy more shares than they planned from companies they believe in which means even though valuations may be down they'll still try to get great deals at great prices and what I mean is uh just two things one think about raising too little capital raising enough to last three years maybe four years if you can find an angel who believes in you or you raise enough to last eight years when you know where you're going next round pricing may matter later stage investors will care a lot about whether they can mark up their investment tomorrow but if you've raised enough to go far enough into the future no one cares how you got there in terms of valuation as long as it was fair they won't care so raise whatever amount you need to feel comfortable to hit plan without stress just make sure it's not so much that you lose control second point is that VCS don't forget their winners", "balanced": "the best of times when public companies were trading at insane multiples and everyone thought it was all going to last forever we're back almost there now but I think if you look over the past few years from even late 2017 through today if you just invested in unicorns that have done well they've had pretty good growth rates um you know some of them like Databricks are growing faster than ever others have slowed down but many have grown their revenues by two or three X since 2017 so they should be worth more right and most of these unicorns will IPO within five to six years I mean it's not always true but generally speaking with a Unicorn with decent metrics they'll IPO in four to seven years maybe eight nine years and then in the next couple years they'll trade up to four to five times Revenue so what happened is if you looked at the average preedipublic company multiple for say 10 years ago in 2008-2010-2011 it averaged around three to four x revenue okay and then after everything crashed in 2009 2012 it dropped to one x and then it went back up to three to four x again so on an absolute basis they haven't gone up much but relative to how fast Cloud spend has exploded the public market cap multiples haven't kept up so it feels harder right because let's say your unicorn is growing 100% a year and it's doing 50 million in AR you could sell that business for a billion dollars in another five years if it keeps growing at that rate if you can get a valuation of 20x ARR which is what HubSpot got and Box got at ipo it would be worth 1 billion dollars", "r32": "for any reason you need to make sure your co has some cash and if they're not giving you that information um just ask them how much money do we have and what's our burn rate I don't think there's ever an excuse for founders not being able to answer those questions so first is make sure you know it second is if you can be more capital efficient great use less because when public companies trade at 5x or lower ARR everyone starts getting nervous and then third is uh as a founder keep spending but spend wisely right make sure every dollar counts every hire should count every dollar should count and last point which is important especially now this one actually always works even though folks are worried today most VCS will tell you give us six months they'll tell you give me another quarter they'll tell you wait until next year before raising money now what does that mean well it means they're scared but it also means it's still early it means you probably got a ways to go and if nothing else raise a bridge round or seed extension if you need it to get through 12 to 24 months with a little bit of extra runway from someone who believes in you and if that's all you've got maybe take advantage of that and remember Founders things change remember remember Founders things change they changed after March 15th of last year so calm down and again remember VCS care about their own economics but ultimately they want to help you succeed they really do they really do they don't want you to fail", "repaired": "they're worried that if they don't get this next round done at an insane price or even worse just to raise it's gonna be hard for them to get another round after and so you know I think what we've seen is crazy pricing is mostly gone right I mean there are exceptions um but crazy prices aren't quite as common anymore and so the other thing I'll tell Founders is like look your VCS have no doubt lost some confidence from these multiples going down and especially folks that raised money in 2021 and 22 when things were nuts um but the best ones do not panic they don't panick they don't run the show out the door because they know their best companies will still trade at high valuations and in fact many of the best public companies today are trading higher than they did at IPOs it's just that those great growth rates were almost impossible to achieve so most of us are going to fall somewhere in between there okay the last one point which I think is important is how much does this matter anyway um let me break it down a few ways first of all think about how much VC funding has fallen since March of 20 21 um now Venture was already slowing down before COVID hit but then everything got shut down for two months and then boom VCS came back in force um and then boom things stopped again um in March of twenty-one and boy it fell off a cliff um and it never really recovered until the end of last year and even into early part of this year we saw a mini boom we had this incredible bull run we had a lot of hot deals and we saw that come crashing down with the market crash on March 9th of this year well here's my point fast forward to today where I'm looking at Carta data where I see that total venture"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 551, "lemkin-clone-v5": 31, "lemkin-clone-v6": 298, "cleaned": 284, "balanced": 300, "r32": 280, "repaired": 336}}}}, {"id": "gold_009", "topic": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer should use Atlassian's scale to reinforce lessons around efficient growth, durable product-led distribution, and building systems that expand over time. The point is not that every company should copy Atlassian exactly, but that founders should pay attention to how repeatability and compounding distribution create resilience at scale.", "facts": ["The answer should draw founder lessons from Atlassian's scale, especially around product-led growth, efficiency, or durable expansion.", "It should use ARR scale as evidence of a repeatable system, not as the lesson itself.", "It should translate observations into actions for smaller SaaS teams."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The $2.9B Secret to Scaling SaaS (And How It Applies To Your Business)**\n\nAs I'm sure you've heard, Atlassian is now a behemoth in the SaaS space - with almost $3 billion in Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR). Their story is one that can give us all lessons on how to scale and succeed in this crazy industry.\n\nI had the chance to dig into their history recently, and I wanted to share some of the most interesting takeaways. Here are 5 learnings from Atlassian's journey:\n\n**1.** **Focus on a very specific market segment**\n\nAt first glance, it might seem like Atlassian is just another all-purpose software company - but scratch beneath the surface and you'll see they're actually laser-focused on two key markets: Agile development teams (Jira) and IT teams (Ops). This narrow focus has allowed them to dominate these spaces with products that are tailored to specific pain points. Moral of the story? Don't try to be everything to everyone.\n\n**2.** **The importance of building a strong community**\n\nAtlassian's got one of the most passionate user bases out there - and it's not just because their software is great (although, let's be real...). They've built this loyal following by actively engaging with customers through tools like Jira Align and numerous online forums. By fostering a sense of belonging among users, Atlassian has created an ecosystem that drives growth and advocacy.\n\n**3.** **Be willing to pivot (but only when necessary)**\n\nAtlassian didn't become a $2B company overnight - they had their fair share of setbacks along the way. One notable example is Confluence's rocky start as a standalone product; it was eventually merged with Jira, but initially faced significant user adoption issues. The key takeaway here? Be prepared to adjust your strategy when data suggests you need to course-correct.\n\n**4.** **Innovate through acquisition (not just organic growth)**\n\nWhen Atlassian acquired Trello in 2017, many wondered if it was a costly mistake - but the integration of both products has led to some pretty impressive synergies and market expansion for Jira Cloud. They've also continued this M&A strategy with recent buys like Code Barrel and Monday.com (which I'm sure will have its own set of challenges). This demonstrates that strategic acquisitions can be an effective way to accelerate growth.\n\n**5.** **The value of having a strong, centralized platform**\n\nAtlassian's ability to create scalable software through their Jira Cloud platform is truly remarkable - allowing users to easily adapt and customize the system as needed. It also enables seamless data integration across various products, making it easier for customers to manage multiple workflows within one solution.\n\nAs you can see, there are plenty of valuable lessons in Atlassian's journey that we can apply to our own SaaS businesses:\n\n* Stay laser-focused on specific markets\n* Build a loyal community through engagement and advocacy\n* Be willing to pivot when necessary (and know when not to)\n* Innovate through strategic acquisitions (when it makes sense)\n* Create scalable, centralized platforms for customers\n\nI'd love to hear from you - how do these takeaways resonate with your own business? Do any of them stand out as particularly relevant or useful in your own scaling journey?\n\nThanks for reading!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So I’ve been investing and advising a lot in SaaS companies for over 15 years now.  And one thing is true — if you do it well, there’s no limit to the growth.  If your product solves real problems for a big group of customers … then they’ll keep buying more.\nYou can see this with the leaders at almost $3 Billion in ARR today:\nAtlassian\nisn’t just growing 25% at $2.8B in ARR — it’s accelerating from 20% growth to 30%.  Atlassian is also still profitable, albeit barely now.\nShopify\ngrew an incredible 34% to a record $5.1B in revenue in the latest quarter as its SMB base continued to expand fast — and even accelerate.\nHubSpot\nis growing a stunning 31% at almost $2 Billion in ARR — and is also still profitable, albeit barely now.\nAnd more importantly, each of these leaders is seeing their NRR grow over time.  Hubspot’s has gone from just under 100% to over 110%. Atlassian has seen its NRR go up dramatically as well:\nHubSpot\nis growing a stunning 31% at almost $2 Billion in ARR — and is also still profitable, albeit barely now.\nAtlassian\ngrew an incredible 34% to a record $5.1B in revenue in the latest quarter as its SMB base continued to expand fast — and even accelerate.\nAnd more importantly, each of these leaders is seeing their NRR grow over time.  Hubspot’s has gone from just under 100% to over 110%. Atlassian has seen its NRR go up dramatically as well:\nAtlassian\nis growing 25% at $2.8B in ARR — and is also still profitable, albeit barely now.\nShopify\ngrew an incredible 34% to a record $5.1B in revenue in the latest quarter as its SMB base continued to expand fast — and even accelerate.\nHubSpot\nis growing a stunning 31% at almost $2 Billion in ARR — and is also still profitable, albeit barely now.\nAnd more importantly, each of these leaders is seeing their NRR grow over time.  Hubspot’s has gone from just under 100% to over 110%. Atlassian has seen its NRR go up dramatically as well:\n5 Interesting Learnings From Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR\nSo what can we learn?  Well, first of all, they are still growing.  Fast.\nBut also note that the SaaS leaders have gotten a lot more efficient over time.  A lot.\nThat means their “burn” — growth less than revenue — has gone down dramatically:\nThis is good to see because it shows you can keep going and scaling without having to raise tons of VC capital.  But … also be careful here.  Because if your burn goes too low, then you may never be able to grow as fast again.\nSo find a sweet spot in the middle for now.\n5 Interesting Learnings From Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR\nRelated Posts\n5 Interesting Learnings from New Relic at Almost $1B in ARR\n5 Interesting Learnings From HubSpot at Almost $2 Billion in ARR\n5 Interesting Learnings From GitLab at Almost a Billion in ARR", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So as Atlassian crossed almost $3B in ARR, it’s perhaps time to re-look at how they got there. They were an interesting case study already because:\nThey didn’t IPO until a stunning $1B+ in revenue\nAnd they’ve been growing just over 20% a year for years since.\nAtlassian has grown from its very start as a scrappy little startup and now is one of the top SaaS companies in the world. It’s an incredible story, but perhaps not so unusual.\nBut let me highlight 5 interesting learnings that can be applied to your own journey:\n1.  They didn’t IPO until almost $4B ARR\nI know it sounds like a lot today, with many going public earlier and earlier. But back in the early days of Atlassian — even before they went public! — there were some very smart folks at Goldman Sachs that said no way to IPO them before $1 Billion+ in ARR.\nAnd I think they were right.  They just didn’t have enough scale to be a big SaaS company, and the markets aren’t really buying into anything less than that today.\nThis is an interesting case study as we all look at how to go public again — especially with some new rules from the SEC for both IPOs and secondary sales.  What’s the “bar” here?  I’d say it’s $1B+ in ARR, but what do you think?\n2.  They still only have 4% of their customer base paying more than $20k/year\nAtlassian is a big SaaS company today at almost $3 Billion in ARR, and we all know that the majority of its revenue comes from small teams using Jira and Confluence.\nBut they’re not just for SMBs.  There are 30+ companies on Atlassian’s stock exchange (over 1k customers) paying more than a million dollars/year.\nAnd there are almost 3000 other customers with $100k+ in ARR that pay more than $20k each year.  That’s still less than 4% of their customer base!\nSo it seems like we all want to go big or go home, and Atlassian is no exception here — but the reality is a different one.\n3.", "cleaned": "So we’ve taken a look at several Cloud and SaaS leaders that have seen growth slow down or stall, like Box, Snowflake, HubSpot, ZoomInfo, MongoDB, and more. But what if your product is still doing great? What if customers love you as much today as they did when you first shipped it? That’s Atlassian. Today, its products are used by half of all Fortune 500 companies — and growing an incredible 25% year-over-year at almost $3B in ARR! That’s pretty darn impressive. So … how do you keep on going even after years and years of being incredibly successful? You focus on going more upmarket (see below) and adding more value to each customer. You expand the amount each customer pays. And you add more and more features for existing customers to pay extra for. Yes, this can be done without driving up support costs and other expenses. Atlassian has proven it can be done. Even with no big price increases overall (more on that below). And importantly, their NRR remains stunningly high — 122%. While many SMB leaders see their NRR fall as their average deal size goes up, Atlassian doesn’t. It stays consistently strong. A reminder that if you sell both small AND enterprise deals, just make sure everyone gets good ROI. Everyone should get at least some clear benefits from using your app. More here. 5 Interesting Learnings: #1. $100k+ Customers Are Growing The Fastest, From 16% To 31% Year Over Year This makes sense, given Atlassian’s goal to go upmarket and increase revenue per seat. Its largest customers are now growing far faster than its smallest ones. In fact, its larger customers are already growing twice as fast as its smaller ones.", "balanced": "So it’s been a while since we’ve checked in with Atlassian, but this is a great time to do so. They’re now crossing almost $3B in ARR and are trading for a stunning ~8x ARR! The best public SaaS companies trade at up to 20x ARR on average. And they should — that’s where you can make money investing. But what’s even more impressive than their market cap? It’s how they got there: $2.9 billion in revenue Growing 21% (!) Free cash flow positive (and getting more efficient every quarter), like many leaders today Profitable, although not as profitable as some others Net Revenue Retention of 98%, again top tier Growth in new products accelerating. Confluence Cloud alone grew +35%. A truly incredible run for any company. I’d argue one of the most impressive runs of all time. Let’s dig in: #1. 110% NRR From Self-Serve Customers and 97% From Enterprise Customers. This is pretty much the perfect ratio of self-serve to enterprise customers. You want to keep your ratios roughly equal. If you go too heavy into self-service, you may be leaving out features your bigger customers need. But if you don’t have enough self-serve customers, then your brand suffers. So 100/100 or close is optimal. Atlassian has found its sweet spot here. Even better, NRR across both customer segments remain high. 110%+ for SMBs is world class. And 95%-96% for big enterprises is also world-class. That’s very rare. 60% of their revenue comes from outside the U.S., so having different metrics for different geographies is common. But still, an incredible achievement. #2. 70% of Revenue From Outside North America. We’ll see this become more important going forward, especially given the current exchange rates favoring non-US businesses.", "r32": "So we’ve done a lot on SaaStr over the years with Atlassian. It was one of our first unicorn success stories, and has been an important part of the early days of many Cloud leaders, including ZoomInfo / Pave.io. And it’s also a leader that like HubSpot went long to get to its current size — taking almost 15 years to go from launch to IPO (2015) — but has gotten much more enterprise as time has gone by. In fact, today, Atlassian is almost entirely Enterprise ($100k+ ACV). Today, they are approaching $3B in ARR, growing a still impressive 28%, albeit down materially from their peak growth rates just before Covid. But importantly, for the first time ever, they have finally crossed into “Rule of 40” territory. A great reminder that you can cross even there later if you keep the engine going. And a few other learnings: #1. 95% GRR, and 130% NRR From Large Customers = Huge Leverage. Atlassian has always had high retention, both individual and team editions, so this isn’t new. But it’s worth seeing it laid out here. They’re able to drive up prices across their base, driving up NRR to an epic 130%. If you aren’t doing this well yourself, you should be pushing harder. Don’t let your existing customers leave. Push them harder to buy more. And raise prices when appropriate. You will see similar benefits.", "repaired": "So we’ve taken for granted how big SaaS leaders like Salesforce, HubSpot and more have grown.  But many are still growing faster than ever!  Atlassian is one example.\nThey just crossed $3 Billion in ARR!\nIt’s also an interesting case study because it has multiple products, and while its flagship Jira product is slowing down some (as you’ll see below), it’s Cloud offerings overall are accelerating — even as growth slowed to 18% last quarter.\nThat’s still very impressive given that most public SaaS companies have seen growth slow below 20%.\n5 Interesting Learnings:\n#1.  Growth Still Strong — Even if It Has Slowed Some\nAtlassian’s revenue grew 19% year-over-year in Q2 FY25, with non-GAAP operating income increasing by 24%. The company delivered strong free cash flow margin of 30%, up significantly from prior periods. And importantly, Atlassian’s customers continue to grow their total spend on average by +10% annually, which bodes well for future growth."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 534, "lemkin-clone-v5": 511, "lemkin-clone-v6": 353, "cleaned": 286, "balanced": 288, "r32": 234, "repaired": 156}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post:\n\n**Just got back from talking to Mike Cannon-Brookes and Scott Farquhar @Atlassian**\n\nWow, just had an incredible conversation with two of the most impressive founders I've met - **Mike Cannon-Brookes & Scott Farquhar**. At $2.9B in ARR (annual recurring revenue), Atlassian is one of the largest software companies to have scaled from a scrappy startup.\n\nHere are 5 interesting learnings that stuck out for me:\n\n1. **Hire slow, fire fast**: Mike and Scott emphasized how crucial it was to prioritize quality over quantity when hiring at Atlassian's early stages. \"We didn't need to hire the fastest or most aggressive people - we needed those who shared our values and were willing to put in the hard work.\"\n\n2. **Build for enterprise from day one**: Many companies try to pivot into the enterprise segment later on, but Mike & Scott made it clear that Atlassian was always built with an eye towards large customers. \"We wanted Trello (Jira's competitor) to be able to handle 10k users right out of the gate.\"\n\n3. **Don't underestimate the power of acquisitions**: Atlassian has been incredibly strategic about its M&A, including buying Trello and Jira Service Desk. Mike & Scott believe that acquisitions can accelerate growth by \"buying talent, customers, or technology\" - not just market share.\n\n4. **Go all-in on self-service**: As Atlassian scaled to tens of thousands of users, they made a deliberate decision to prioritize", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we’re getting into some real SaaS “maturity” now.\nAtlassian just crossed almost $3B ARR — so it’s still growing, but not that fast.\nIt’s doing well.\nBut it’s … mature.  And you can see the impact of being a multi-product platform in this slow-growth era:\n#1. 8% growth is fine. But it’s down from last year and more importantly, for years before\nThat’s still pretty good, but it’s down from double-digits.\n#2. Atlassian is doing just as well at $3B ARR today as it did at $500M ARR in 2017\nWe all want to keep growing fast … but sometimes you can get the same results with slower growth.\n#3. More and more of its revenue comes from outside North America — now a majority\nIt’s almost half, globally! That’s what happens when you truly go global.\n#4. A bit over 1/2 of Atlassian’s customers are now enterprise, paying $100k+ per year on average\nThe shift to SMB is still happening, but it’s slower than we thought and for most of us at least … not that big a deal anymore.\n#5. Still just as profitable as ever with 30% margins\nIt’s really the end result you want in SaaS.\nA bit more here:\nAtlassian: 8% Growth is Just Fine. And Growing Even Slower Than Adobe\nRelated Posts\nThe $1 Billion+ ARR Club\nSaaStr #100: How", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Atlassian is an interesting case study as they scale to almost $3B in ARR, but don’t quite have a traditional SaaS model. They are more Enterprise and less SMB than most top SaaS companies — with only 15% of their revenue coming from outside North America.\nBut it’s still great to see Atlassian at almost $2 Billion+ in ARR growing 32%, and then accelerating to 33% growth after the tough year of 2023.  Atlassian is still growing 27% on a non-GAAP basis, with free cash flow margins now above 20%.\n#1: NRR remains high\nAtlassian’s non-GAAP NRR for its core SaaS business has remained remarkably consistent at around 125%, and that doesn’t include the impact of new products like Jira Ops.  But it also means there isn’t much room to raise prices.\n#2: New Products are Critical to Growth — At Least For Now\nJira Ops is growing faster than Core, but we don’t know if that will continue once it scales past $100m ARR.  The lesson here may be that you need new products in the Enterprise category as well as SMB to drive growth.\n#3: Customer Count Is Still Growing 20% a Year — But At A Slower Pace Than Revenue\nAtlassian is still growing its customer count by an impressive 21%, but at a slower pace than revenue.  This is normal for many SaaS companies, but it’s always worth keeping an eye on.\n#4: R&D Is Still High As", "cleaned": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "balanced": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR #1. 55% of its new customers pay less than $50k for annual contracts, and only 15% pay more than $100k. This is pretty cool to see broken out this way. A reminder you don’t have to be enterprise-only to build up to $2B+ in ARR. And also a challenge to those that say you can’t sell SMBs with a complex product. #2. 85% of their revenue comes from self-service and sales-assisted sales. We’ve seen similar results across other Cloud leaders like Shopify (80%) and Monday.com (90%), but it’s helpful to see how Atlassian does it as well. They are very efficient, even while still having humans help close bigger deals.", "r32": "#5. 85% of their revenue is still outside North America, and they’ve doubled down there. The markets for collaboration software are global, but not all that globally have been tapped out yet by leaders like Slack (acquired by Salesforce) and others. And interestingly … it’s even harder to go upmarket than grow the base! Atlassian has added some bigger deals over time — but growing its customer count overall was just as important to driving growth. A great case study on how SaaS companies can scale well beyond $1B in ARR. And how you really need both to drive growth these days. Growth AND efficiency. In many cases, growth is more expensive today than ever before. But if you can get the engine going again, the benefits compound over time. Atlassian proved that once again here. It took them until Year 20 or so to truly become a “unicorn” with $100m+ ARR. Then they accelerated. Now? They’re almost at $3 Billion in ARR. And growing almost 30%! That’s pretty darn good. Even better, Wall Street agrees. Atlassian trades at a jaw-dropping 54x ARR. Go long!", "repaired": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR 🚀 #1. 25% Growth Rate, With 10% Free Cash Flow Margins At almost $3B in ARR, that’s pretty darn good! And importantly — it’s accelerating with $2.9B run-rate and 27% growth in Q1’22 (latest quarter). It’s not always linear in SaaS, especially as you scale. But this is still the engine of our time. #2. 120% NRR + 95% GRR = A Decade+ Of Compound Revenue Growth This is how we build unicorns, decacorns and more. The combo of 120%+ NRR and 90%-100% GRR is magic. You’ll see this again and again across many leaders. If you have even higher NRR than Atlassian, so much the better. #3. 13 years to first $1m ARR We all love to talk about how important product-led growth is today. But it doesn’t mean everything else is any less important. 13 years to get going? That’s a lot of work, man. And a reminder that it can take far longer for many of us than expected. #4. Only 50% Direct Sales So while they do sell direct, it’s only half their revenue stream. Half of Atlassian’s sales are indirect through partners and resellers. Not everyone has to be a true “direct” seller. And a big part of Atlassian’s partner channel isn’t really traditional partners"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 238, "lemkin-clone-v5": 227, "lemkin-clone-v6": 242, "cleaned": 11, "balanced": 121, "r32": 185, "repaired": 221}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**\"Just got back from a meeting with the team at @Atlassian and I'm blown away by their journey to almost $3B in ARR. Here are 5 interesting learnings that every SaaS founder should know: \n\n **1/5 Focus on customer obsession, not just product features**: Atlassian's early days were marked by intense focus on user acquisition & retention. They prioritized building a community around JIRA and Confluence, which helped them scale rapidly.\n\n **2/5 The power of niche markets is real**: Instead of trying to be everything for everyone, Atlassian focused on a specific vertical (dev teams) and perfected their products for that market. This allowed them to dominate the space.\n\n **3/5 Don't underestimate the importance", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So, we just did a deep dive with Atlassian’s CTO and CEO on how they built their almost $3 billion in ARR empire. One thing that really struck me is how many lessons we can learn here for the mid-pack today.\n\nFirst of all, it was interesting to see Atlassian still has 12% growth at almost $3 Billion in ARR. We’ve seen this happen a few times now with leaders like HubSpot and Qualtrics — they just keep growing faster than anyone else.\n\nBut what we saw more of from Atlassian is that it’s not all magic, folks. There are challenges ahead for even the most successful companies out there:\n\n1. You Need to Constantly Innovate\nAtlassian had a 50%+ drop in their stock price when they didn", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I thought it would be fun to dig up just a few interesting learnings on how Atlassian is doing now, at almost $3B in ARR:\nIt’s growing 23%+ today. So even with more competition than ever, they’re still growing\n25%\nyear-over-year.\nThat’s pretty darn good for a company nearly as old as me!\nThis makes sense — but it still shows how much Atlassian and other SMB leaders have innovated to stay ahead of bigger competitors:\nAtlassian is now adding 30%+ more customers each year, even at $2.9B in ARR\n.\nThe “growth” from new logos has actually accelerated over the years!\nA reminder that no matter what you think about your competition — there’s still always room for growth with SMB", "cleaned": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "balanced": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "r32": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "repaired": "5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 118, "lemkin-clone-v5": 128, "lemkin-clone-v6": 123, "cleaned": 11, "balanced": 11, "r32": 11, "repaired": 11}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt to write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr:\n\n**Title:** \"5 Interesting Learnings from Atlassian at Almost $3 Billion in ARR\"\n\n**[Introduction]**\n\nHi everyone! Welcome back to another episode of SaaStr. Today we're going to dive into some fascinating learnings from one of our favorite companies: Atlassian. With over 135,000 customers and almost $3 billion in ARR, Atlassian has achieved an incredible level of success. As a fellow founder who's been on the same journey, I'm excited to share with you five interesting takeaways from their story.\n\n**Learning #1: Be Unapologetically Niche**\n\nAtlassian started out as two Australian developers building software for themselves and their friends. They focused exclusively on developing tools for agile project management - a niche that many thought was too small. But Atlassian's founders, Scott Farquhar and Mike Cannon-Brookes, didn't budge from this vision. Today, Jira is the leading solution in its space, with over 70% market share.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nSo what can we learn from Atlassian's focus on niche? It's simple: don't try to be everything to everyone. Instead, identify a specific problem and solve it better than anyone else. This approach allows you to build deep expertise, create loyal customers, and dominate your market.\n\n**Learning #2: Iterate Like Crazy**\n\nAtlassian didn't start out with the perfect product. In fact, their first version of Jira was released in 2002, but it wasn't until version 3 that they started gaining traction. The key takeaway here is that even successful companies like Atlassian need to iterate and improve continuously.\n\n**[Show a slide]**\n\nAccording to Scott Farquhar, Atlassian has released over 500 major updates since their founding - that's an average of one update every three days! This kind of iterative approach allows you to stay ahead of the competition, adapt to changing customer needs, and continually increase your value proposition.\n\n**Learning #3: Focus on Customer Success**\n\nAtlassian has always prioritized customer success above all else. They've implemented a robust support system, including online forums, in-person training sessions, and even a dedicated team for large enterprises. As a result, Atlassian enjoys an impressive 90%+ customer retention rate.\n\n**[Highlight the importance of customer success]**\n\nSo what can we learn from Atlassian's focus on customer success? Simply put: your customers are not just transactions - they're long-term relationships that need to be nurtured and cared for. Prioritize their success, and you'll see your business thrive in return.\n\n**Learning #4: Build an Ecosystem Around Your Product**\n\nAtlassian has built a thriving ecosystem around Jira through partnerships with other successful companies like Trello and Google Cloud. This approach allows them to expand the reach of their product while also providing additional value to customers.\n\n**[Discuss the benefits of building an ecosystem]**\n\nSo what can we learn from Atlassian's ecosystem strategy? It's this: don't just build a great product - build a platform that enables others to succeed alongside you. This approach fosters innovation, drives growth, and creates new revenue streams for your business.\n\n**Learning #5: Be Patient (and Strategic) About Growth**\n\nAtlassian didn't get to $3 billion in ARR overnight. In fact, it took them over 15 years of hard work, strategic decision-making, and a willingness to pivot when necessary. As Scott Farquhar has said, \"Growth is not just about the numbers - it's about building something that lasts.\"\n\n**[Conclusion]**\n\nThere you have it: five interesting learnings from", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay so i'm not sure how many folks here are using atlassian today but boy do they make some cool stuff okay we're going to go over five learnings and one interesting stat from atlassian that just crossed almost three billion dollars arr with a small fraction of enterprise customers it's really kind of amazing okay let's start in the beginning i mean most of us who run sas companies are familiar with atlassian so we know about jira but you probably don't use confluence do you or not quite sure what trello does yeah there might be one or two here yeah it's pretty rare for people to have used all three right and that's okay and the reason i'm bringing this up is because of something very cool that atlassian did when they hit 2 billion in revenue with a fraction of enterprise customers now maybe you're thinking what do we learn from jira which has been free since it was founded way back in the day so let me just say something about why i think atlassian's success story especially interesting and then how much of their magic is from confluence okay so jira really did need to be free but here's an important point you can't sell everything as a self-serve product sometimes you have to go enterprise and that's true for many reasons one reason is if there are more than two or three people in the company buying it then you're going to need sales support which is what atlassian did with jira okay jira was free but then they added on a bunch of features and had a paid edition for teams and then they went enterprise with on-premises editions etc so that's interesting number two is", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So let's take a look this week, as we get close to $300 billion. We've seen a lot of interesting things here in 2021 and beyond.\nAtlassian is one of the great success stories at scale in software today — growing from zero to almost $3B in ARR in just about two decades!\nAnd it's gone public twice!\nLet me summarize five pretty cool learnings:\n#1. The IPO was a huge hit\nBut they needed more cash and did an additional secondary stock offering a year later.\n#2. It took them 8 years from the first $100M to the second $100m in ARR — but then it all exploded!\nAnd then grew faster than ever after that.\nAtlassian is the perfect example of going for growth, even at scale.\nIt's never too late to grow faster, if you want. But it does often take a very different playbook from your early days as an IPO candidate. So don't be afraid to evolve and change things up later in your growth journey.\n#3. 10% free cash flow margins\nNow here is a reminder that even at $2B+ in ARR, Atlassian's still generating just $200m of net income — about 10% of its revenues. That may seem pretty low for so much revenue, but it actually isn't too bad given their very high capital expenditures.\n#4. $100k ACV is the sweet spot\nAtlassian at scale today has a fairly large ACV profile, like many other leaders, including ServiceNow and Slack / Salesforce. Atlassian's average deal size? A stunning $120K! But that's down from its peak of about $150k in 2020.\n#5. The best are always accelerating\nAtlassian is no exception here. It has accelerated growth every year over the last decade, with a truly remarkable 30% at almost $3B ARR today!\nSo it can take some time to get going — and even longer to scale up as an IPO candidate. But when you do hit that initial IPO milestone … just watch your shares fly!", "cleaned": "hey everybody i wanted to take a minute and dig into one interesting sas leader that we don't chat about enough which is atlassian um it's coming up on three billion dollars in ar so almost there it's worth over 50 billion dollars growing like a weed and yet we often look at folks like salesforce or workday or even zoom as our sort of examples of what enterprise software should be looking like but actually atlassian has been doing this for much longer than all those folks and done it very differently so let me just walk you through five interesting learnings number one uh atlassian um ipo'd back in october 2015. wow okay november 17th 2015. um it was pretty early to ipo right because when it ipoed its revenue was only about 500 million and today it's approaching 3 billion dollar error now that's not crazy fast growth every day no but it does show how important recurring revenue is um they've grown their customer count modestly each year but dramatically increased revenues from them so 110 nrr really fuels a lot of these numbers next point um the second thing i want to highlight with atlassian is um the power law of smbs we know that notion can do it we know that twilio can do it and others but atlassian proves again and again that if your customers are small businesses and mid market businesses and startups they have an incredibly long tail and they get more and more value out of their customers over time so in fact in terms of total customers atlassian grew by 15 percent last quarter which isn't epic but think about going from 150 000 to 180 000 in four years that's amazing", "balanced": "hey everybody i wanted to do five interesting learnings from atlassian which is one that we've talked about on saster quite a bit but not as much recently and it's an old favorite for many folks who have been around since the early days it was bootstrapped until ipo number two interestingly um its co-founder scott farin is back so he took a little break after they went public now they're growing faster than ever almost three billion error four hundred million in revenue 50 plus percent growth um and yet just like zoom they're still profitable today uh over 20 of their free cash flow profit margins are insane for a company with this type of growth rate and what's even more impressive they grew their customer count by 30 year-over-year okay think about that if you had 100 customers last year and then your new bookings were equal to your existing base right and your retention was good you would add 100 next year nope atlassian added 3000 new customers 30 growth in total customers wow it's pretty amazing let me go through these six quick ones the first one which again as founders especially as ceos we should know but we often don't remember is how big smbs really can be and most of us get tired of hearing this but look atlassian has 110 000 paying customers 115 thousand if you include trial customers it's basically every developer on the planet using its products maybe not literally but a lot of them um and that means there's room to grow because there's so many developers out there and they will use multiple tools so yes you may need a couple million dollar deal here or there to hit your ar goals but boy oh boy look at atlassian just imagine if half those", "r32": "I was talking to an investor that has invested in a lot of companies where I've also been on their board and he said you know it's funny with Atlassian is there are so many things they do right but we don't really understand them because they're very technical products but what's interesting is one thing we have learned about cloud leaders since Covey hit public we have never seen a product that is less understood than Atlassian ever go more enterprise even though its products are complex they are not that well understood by IT or others and yet today over half of Atlasians Revenue comes from customers that pay more than a million dollars a year for their products now if you haven't heard of atlasian maybe this will help explain why yes these products are complicated but boy when folks get on boarded and figure out how to use them they often stick around for decades um and point four just another reminder to focus on NPS as your core KPI and that's that we talked about this last week but just a reminder of how important NPS can be and that is 140 net revenue retention atlassian has almost 150 nrr okay if you don't know what NR means it means basically what happens to a customer after they buy your product let's say a company buys 100k worth of Atlassian software and then next year they spend 130 k it went up five percent right that would be 95 NRR if they spent 90 k next year 10% growth if they bought 50k and then 55k the next year that's 110% NR okay so it's a measure of how much does your existing base grow right um atlassian like almost everyone else in SAS is seeing some modest deceleration in their NR but 140 is still pretty darn good", "repaired": "hey everybody i wanted to spend a few minutes talking about one of our favorite sas and cloud leaders atlantic um it's doing just incredible numbers growing over 30 percent even as they approach almost three billion dollars in ar uh now what is atlassian well you know it but if you're not sure there are two main products that make up the vast majority of its revenue jira which is a project management tool for developers and engineers um and confluence which is like an enterprise wiki for teams together those two products combined generate more than two billion in arr so we'll focus on them today um there are other very interesting adjacent products like trello um which is a product that was acquired by atlassian in the early days and has become a significant piece of their business but we will ignore all the rest of the portfolio because clearly these first two are the majority of the value for investors here atlassian but let me step back for a minute and ask this question how many companies have you invested in or worked with where you weren't totally sure who your customer was no matter what your marketing said no matter what your website said maybe when you talked to sales everyone would say different things um maybe in fact customers would come into your board meetings and tell you that your number one competitor actually had a better version of your product it happens way too often um especially in complex environments right i mean we've all been sold software that turned out we didn't really need we needed something else sometimes we got features that aren't quite right and it's worse when you don't understand your customer base um so i want to use"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 564, "lemkin-clone-v5": 294, "lemkin-clone-v6": 334, "cleaned": 294, "balanced": 307, "r32": 314, "repaired": 300}}}}, {"id": "gold_010", "topic": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach one concrete operator lesson with specific examples and conviction.", "expected": "A strong answer should explain that an 'NRR zombie' is a company that can still grow on the surface but lacks the expansion and retention engine needed for healthy compounding. The founder takeaway is to diagnose the root cause quickly and fix the product, segmentation, pricing, or customer outcomes before the weakness hardens into a permanent ceiling.", "facts": ["The answer should define the NRR problem clearly: a company with weak expansion or unhealthy retention despite still appearing alive.", "It should recommend concrete responses such as fixing product value, segmentation, onboarding, pricing, or customer success.", "It should stress that weak NRR becomes a structural drag on growth."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The #1 Killer of SaaS Companies (And How to Avoid It)**\n\n Ah, growth hacking. The holy grail of startup success. But let's be real - there's a dark side to chasing that next big user acquisition milestone.\n\nI'm talking about the dreaded \"NRR Zombie\". You know who I'm referring to: those companies that are stuck in a perpetual cycle of acquiring new users at breakneck speeds, only to watch their Net Revenue Retention (NRR) sink further and further into oblivion.\n\nIf you're an NRR zombie, don't worry - it's not too late to change course. But first, let me ask: have you fallen victim to the following warning signs?\n\n**The Three Telltale Signs of An NRR Zombie**\n\n1. **Your churn rate is through the roof**: You're spending all your time and resources on customer acquisition, but somehow, more users are leaving than staying.\n2. **You're relying on cheap tricks to boost growth**: You're churning out new marketing campaigns like crazy, but they only seem to attract the wrong kind of customers - ones who don't pay the freight.\n3. **Your CS team is drowning in support tickets**: It's not just churn - it's the quality of those users that's the problem. They need constant hand-holding and support, sucking up resources you could be spending elsewhere.\n\n**So What Do You Do If You're An NRR Zombie?**\n\nWell, here are three key takeaways to help you claw your way back from the brink:\n\n1. **Stop chasing growth at all costs**: It's time to prioritize retention over acquisition. Figure out what's driving those nasty churn numbers and fix it.\n2. **Get serious about customer success**: If users aren't paying the freight, they're probably not worth keeping around anyway. Invest in CS that actually helps customers get value from your product - or at least makes them feel like you care.\n3. **Measure (and optimize) for retention**: Don't just focus on NRR as a metric - dig into what's driving it. Is it the wrong customer segment? A flawed onboarding process? Get to the bottom of it and fix it.\n\n**So, How Do You Know If It's Time To Call In The Professionals?**\n\nIf you're still struggling with these issues after implementing these strategies... well, let's face it - it might be time for a growth hack. But this time, don't just throw money at the problem; get expert advice from someone who's been in your shoes (like yours truly!).\n\nDon't become an NRR zombie. It's not pretty.\n\n---\n\n(Note: I've tried to emulate Jason Lemkin's style and tone, which is often characterized by his blunt honesty, humor, and no-nonsense approach to startup growth and metrics.)", "lemkin-clone-v5": "If you are an SaaS CEO and your Net New Customer growth is less than $10M, then you have at least one big issue.  In fact, it’s likely two.\nFirst, that means your base is not growing quickly enough\nSecond, the problem is compounded by the other: you’re not able to invest in new features and functionality fast enough to attract new customers. And if your NRR isn’t strong — well then, you just have a few years at best.\nIf nothing else, don’t do anything but fix this one thing first:\nGet Your Growth Up.  Now.  Whatever it Takes.\nBut what can we do once we are in the situation?  What should we do to reverse NRR zombiesm?\nFirst of all — if your NNR is high and you’re doing well with new customers, I wouldn’t make a huge change here.  You know why?  Because it’s working.\nBut, but … what about when it isn’t? What about when NRR has gone down from say +100% to just +40%, or even lower?\nWell — first of all, be honest with yourself:\nAre you in a state of “NRR Zombie” ?\nIf your Net Retention Rate is below 110% and growing slower than new customers … then yes.  You are in an NRR zombie.\nThen ask yourself one question:\nWhat am I going to do about it?\nBecause at some point, all the excuses run out.  All of them.  You can’t blame the competition anymore once you’re in this state.  You can’t say your product isn’t good enough — because then what?  You just give up and go home?  Not really.\nSo ask yourself:\nWhat am I going to do about it?\nAnd … well, if nothing else, at least fix the obvious issues first.\nFix Your Support\n.  It may not be broken (but is probably worse than you think), but your support team isn’t magical — they can only help so many folks per month.  So get a lot more of them.  And better ones.\nGet Better At Onboarding\n.  Because if it’s hard to use, no one wants to buy from you.\nDo More Marketing and Sales\n.  Even if it doesn’t seem like it will work — because your base is so small and NRR low.  Because that’s the only way to get more customers in this case.\nGo all-in on upsells for now\n.  If nothing else, at least go all-in here — even if you know later that these new features won’t be worth it.\nGo “All-In” on Free Editions and other tactics to get prospects into your app as soon as possible — again, without expecting a lot of money from them upfront\n.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So one day, you might wake up and realize it’s been years since your customers actually started growing. Or maybe never really grew at all.\nI see this happen often enough, when founders don’t quite understand how the “growth cycle” works in SaaS:\nYou have to grow fast\nto get big.  And then,\nyou have to maintain a certain growth rate to stay big\n.  At least for now, if you’re going more enterprise and bigger deals.\nBut there’s another phase after that: when your customers are happy enough to keep buying from you year over year without having to recruit new leads all the time.  That can be called “retention” or at least “maintenance”, but it sure is a lot of effort to keep growing in this phase.  And usually, less than in the prior phases.\nIf you haven’t really achieved NRR (Net Retention Rate) growth by Year 3-4 of SaaS … then something is wrong\n.\nIt could be lack of product-market fit or feature gap for your current customers\n. It can take a while to get there, and often it takes an additional phase:\nYou have to grow fast enough to build the entire team that will support you later.  Then,\nyou have to maintain a certain growth rate in more enterprise sales (and larger deals) to fuel the team\n.\nAnd then when you hit $10m-$20m ARR or so, your customers should be big enough and happy enough with your product to buy from you year-over-year without much recruiting of new leads.  That’s where NRR comes into play as a growth engine — but it will never quite grow faster than the prior phases.\nIt may take time for your customers to become “happy” enough, or at least big enough and mature enough in their use cases, that they can buy from you year-over-year without recruiting new leads all the time.  And this is often a challenge for founders — because it’s not quite as fun as selling into brand-new logos.\nBut if your customers aren’t growing with you fast enough to make NRR work as an engine of growth … then something has gone wrong\n.  It may have been a feature gap, or product-market fit issue earlier on.  Or even more likely — that sales team hasn’t quite figured out how to sell into the right logos at scale yet.\nWhat happens next is what I call “NRR Death”:\nYou end up with a company where your only growth engine is recruiting new leads all the time\n.  And maybe not even doing it very efficiently or effectively — because you haven’t really gotten NRR working for you as an engine of growth by Year 3-4.\nThat’s bad enough, but what happens next if you don’t figure out a way to get back on track is that your team can start growing slower than the new leads you are recruiting.", "cleaned": "If you’re an SMB, it’s not all your fault. The market has changed. But it doesn’t mean there aren’t better days ahead for many SaaS and Cloud leaders than today. In fact, we’re seeing some signs they are here already. HubSpot is one example. It IPO’d at $600m ARR growing just under 30%. Today? They’re worth almost $10B! And growing even faster — at almost $1 Billion in ARR. Not every leader will have quite that story. But I bet more can go from a zombie state back to growth mode … if they commit to doing what it takes. So my advice to folks whose companies are in this zone: First, find out why. Is churn high because customers don’t need the product anymore, or because they’ve bought other products to fill gaps, or simply because you haven’t added enough value over time? Is your customer base getting older? Are new competitors eating into your territory? Or did you just stop innovating? Once you know, figure out how to fix it. At least, grow NPS first. If your NPS is low, that means customers aren’t happy with their experience. Fixing that should be job #1. Customers won’t churn immediately when they’re unhappy, but once they do, they usually stay gone. And secondarily, focus on driving down your CAC / LTV ratio. This isn’t just important for growth – it’s critical for survival. You can’t burn through cash forever waiting for your next big acquisition or funding round.", "balanced": "So we’ve written quite a bit on SaaStr over the years, as well as with our portfolio companies and at events, about how many are seeing their NPS go up — but still not see their net revenue retention (NRR) improve. They may even be seeing some decline there if they don’t reinvent themselves for the Age of AI. Because folks have budget now again — but only want to buy more from leaders that deliver massive value. And many B2B apps just aren’t keeping up here. I call these NRR Zombies because you can see them coming. Their customer count often grows slowly due to new customers … but then, their base doesn’t grow anymore. The existing customers stop expanding. There’s no second product sale. No upsell. No more seats added per account. It all sort of stagnates. But this isn’t always obvious in the early days. A lot of us had “one big deal” or one whale customer growing up. So it might take longer to see an NRR Zombie emerge than you’d think. Or sometimes, you’ll get a boost from a few bigger deals, so your ARR growth will continue for another year or two. But eventually, once the number of customers gets larger, the lack of expansion within those accounts becomes clear. It does become clear. Here’s what happens when you wait too long, especially when competition heats up, and don’t do something about being an NRR zombie: Your competitors start closing the gap in ACV, in logos, etc. When they keep adding value, and you don’t. That can lead to a death spiral. You lose the ability to compete based on price alone.", "r32": "A lot of folks are struggling these days, but some just seem like zombies. They aren’t dead yet — they don’t have no revenue at all. But growth has slowed to almost zero and their net negative churn isn’t enough to grow the business on its own anymore. So what do you do if you’ve become an NRR zombie? A few thoughts, first from me, then from SaaStr speakers who have gone through this journey: First, let’s define when it happens. When your customer count is flat or even shrinking … and your net retention rate is barely positive (i.e., less than +100%). That means you really can’t rely on existing customers to grow for now. It also usually means you have limited time left to turn things around before it becomes impossible. Because that same net-negative-customer-count logic will lead to a death spiral down the road. The only way out of that one is truly radical change, which we’ll get to below. One other thing that’s hard when you’re a zombie NRR company: everyone loses faith. Your board might still believe. Even sometimes, maybe, your VCs may still be there. Some investors tend not to write off companies with any revenue at all. But most employees lose faith. Most. And without confidence, you never move forward. The team doesn’t know how to improve anything. Or if they care. I’ve seen this happen so many times, especially during tougher times. Everyone needs help here. And a little bit more on how to think about NRR here : Now, back to those SaaStr speakers who went through this phase themselves. We asked them what advice they would give to others in similar situations. They were pretty honest 🙂 \"Your customers should love you.", "repaired": "A while back, I did an A+ session at SaaStr Annual with Marc Benioff. We talked about so many great things from AI to how Salesforce has gone multiproduct and much more:\nBut one thing stuck out that really resonated with me — he said “I’d rather have low growth than zombie customers.” And what does he mean? He means you can see it coming for some folks. For example,\nSalesforce has seen its $1m+ ACV accounts grow faster over the past year or two as they go even more enterprise and AI-centric\n. But others are just getting harder to retain. The ones growing <10% YoY. They're not happy enough\npic.twitter.com/5w0y9vJrD2"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 442, "lemkin-clone-v5": 450, "lemkin-clone-v6": 487, "cleaned": 248, "balanced": 278, "r32": 292, "repaired": 112}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The #1 Thing To Fix In Your Business Today (If You're A \"Zombie\")**\n\nHey everyone, it's Jason here. \n\nI've been traveling non-stop for weeks now, but I still get messages from founders who are struggling - and often stuck in the same place.\n\nAnd today, I want to talk about a specific kind of entrepreneur: The NRR (Net Revenue Retention) Zombie .\n\n**What is an NRR Zombie?**\n\nAn NRR Zombie is someone whose business has stopped growing. They're not going backwards, but they're also not moving forward. Their growth rate may be \"fine\", but it's no longer increasing.\n\nThey might have a solid product-market fit, happy customers, and even some traction... but somehow, the company just isn't scaling up like it should.\n\n**So what's the problem?**\n\nI've seen many founders get stuck in this place because they're too focused on maintaining their current growth rate. They think that as long as revenue is increasing (albeit slowly), everything will be okay.\n\nBut here's the thing: **NRR is a lagging indicator** . It takes months, if not years, to figure out what's working and what's not based solely on NRR numbers alone.\n\nSo, how do you break free from being an NRR Zombie?\n\nHere are three things I want every founder reading this post to consider:\n\n1. **Get your growth metrics aligned**: Make sure that revenue is just one of many key performance indicators (KPIs) you're tracking regularly.\n2. **Invest in", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So you're an SaaS exec with decent numbers, but your net retention is kinda dead. It's just not growing fast enough anymore.\nWhat do you do?  How can you make it better?\nFirst, accept that this isn't going to fix itself.\nBut then go find the root cause of why this happened.\nIt may be a new feature that doesn't work well with your core use case for most of your customers. It may be an AI issue where some new capability is not working as promised for many of your users. Or it could be that just in time, you lost 10% market share to the competition. Whatever it is, find a root cause.\nThen go fix it.\nYou can't always make up past ground here, but I've seen most SaaS businesses grow their Net Retention Rate from 100%-120%. Even if only a little bit at first.\n\nAnd then just focus on net retention for now\n- don’t worry about the absolute number of new customers. Just get it back to growing again. And keep going until your NRR is up again.\n\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 2, 2025\nSo you're an SaaS exec with decent numbers, but your net retention is kinda dead. It's just not growing fast enough anymore.\nWhat do you do? How can you make it better?\nFirst, accept that this isn't going to fix itself.\nBut then go find the root cause of why", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I just did another big round with SaaStr Fund. One thing we looked at was Net Retention Rate (NRR) and how it’s trending. It’s a simple but important metric.\n\nIf your NRR is below 100%, you have to improve it.\nIt can’t stay down forever, or you will die as a business. But the #1 mistake I see founders make when their NRR starts falling: They think it just magically comes back on its own. It doesn’t. You need to go hunt for those customers that churned.\n\nDo whatever it takes to get them back. Do whatever they want.\nGo 100% customer centric — and ask your customers what you can do to win their business back.\nThey will tell you how to fix NRR.\n\nAnd then … Go Get Them Back.\n\nIf you don’t, the zombie apocalypse for your business is coming in about a year or so.\n\nRelated Posts\n5 Things You Should Know About Jason Lemkin Founder of SaaStr\nSaaStr Podcast #058: How To Build A Truly Great Sales Team with Jason Lemkin\nJoin SaaStr at 2021 SaaStr Annual!", "cleaned": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "balanced": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie A lot of folks are struggling right now, but some more than others. Some SaaS companies that have seen their customers grow and thrive over the past years just … haven’t seen any growth for quite a while. Their NPS is low, new customer acquisition has slowed or even stopped, and churn keeps creeping up. They’ve become what we’ll call “NRR Zombies”. These aren’t startups that never got off the ground. Nor are they mid-stage start-ups who raised too much VC capital. No — these are often fairly mature B2B / SaaS companies with decent revenues and strong brands that still have happy customers. But somehow, they’ve fallen into a pattern where it’s no longer clear how to get out of it. How do you escape being an NRR zombie? First, take a pause from fundraising. At least for now. The last thing you need is another $20m+ round when your existing investors don’t believe anymore. Second, stop hiring VPs that can't help you. Stop making those hires. Instead, promote folks internally if possible. Or hire a few great individual contributors to make things better first. Third, figure out why growth has slowed so much. It's not usually one reason. Fourth, reset your model. Your burn rate doesn't matter all that much once you raise millions and tens of millions in venture capital. But you will run out of money eventually. Plan on it. Fifth, finally go long. Many founders don’t really want to be CEOs forever. That makes sense.", "r32": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "repaired": "What to Do If You're An NRR Zombie I've invested in so many great startups where, at some point around $20m-$50m ARR or so, growth slows. But then it doesn't really slow anymore after that. It just sort of... stagnates. Often because the sales team stops working as hard to close new customers (and often churns). The product gets less and less innovative. And renewal rates start to fall, even if you don’t see them yet. Because no one is taking care of your existing base anymore. This can happen for a variety of reasons: - Your VP Sales / CRO hasn’t worked out. Or she/he has but isn’t truly scaling anymore. - You’ve gone upmarket, which takes more work than selling to SMBs or mid-market buyers. So you lose momentum here. - You have too few resources to keep adding value to your customer base. - Your competition is stronger than before. Etc etc. Whatever the reason, I call these folks “NRR Zombies”. They are still growing, but only slowly enough that they aren’t able to scale into something big. To get from $10m ARR to $100m+ ARR. That’s what we all want. And when you hit this phase, there are a few things to do -- and maybe not do: First, sometimes you need to find a way to be more enterprise. At least to add an Enterprise Edition to your self-service product. This will help you get more leads and opportunities. Even if you don’t close most of them right away."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 239, "lemkin-clone-v5": 245, "lemkin-clone-v6": 183, "cleaned": 8, "balanced": 258, "r32": 8, "repaired": 254}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**\"Are you an NRR zombie? Don't know what I'm talking about? Let me explain...**\n\nAs we all know, \"NRR\" stands for \"Not Reaching Revenue\". It means your startup has been growing its user base and customer acquisition costs are going up, but somehow you just can't seem to get that elusive first dollar of revenue.\n\nAnd if you're an NRR zombie, I feel for ya. You've got a great product, awesome team, and tons of potential... but it's like your startup is stuck in neutral, unable to shift into high gear.\n\n**So what do you do? Here are the 3 things that will either fix or kill your startup:**\n\n **Realign your metrics**: Are you tracking", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So, I know many SaaS leaders are feeling a little zombie like these days. Maybe it’s not so much revenue per se — but maybe you’re feeling a little stuck. A little growth seems hard for now.\n\nBut here is the thing about zombies in SaaS:\n\nThey can still die from one wrong move. Even if they seem to be doing all right today.\n\nI wrote this up on X, and it’s something I’m seeing more than ever lately:\n\nThe Zombie Zone: 3 Signs You’re Still Growing But Need Urgent Improvement\n\n1. Your Net Retention Rate Has Been in a Steady Decline for Months (Or Years)\nThis is the classic zombie signal. NRR below 100% means you’re losing customers over time, which erodes your future growth prospects", "lemkin-clone-v6": "If you're running a B2B company and your net retention is below 100% — don't worry it's not the end. I've been there too, even at $1m ARR. The question is are you actively improving it? Or have you kind of given up.\n\nAt least make sure you know why NRR is low. Is it because of a specific segment that doesn't renew? Because of churn from a competitor? It could be something in your product or service that's driving down net retention, but if so - fix it!\n\nIf not, then just give up on the dream for now.\n\nIt can take years to get NRR back into shape. And if you're at $50m+ ARR and still below 100%... well, I'm sure there", "cleaned": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "balanced": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "r32": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "repaired": "What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 127, "lemkin-clone-v6": 124, "cleaned": 8, "balanced": 8, "r32": 8, "repaired": 8}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: What to Do If You’re An NRR Zombie", "answers": {"llama3.1": "( Stage is set, lights are bright. Jason Lemkin steps up to the mic, surveying the audience with his trademark enthusiasm and expertise.)\n\n**Jason Lemkin:** Alright everyone! Today we're going to tackle a critical topic that I've seen many founders struggle with. Are you ready? It's... (pauses for dramatic effect) ...being an NRR zombie!\n\n(Audience chuckles)\n\nNow, what is an NRR zombie? Well, it stands for Net Revenue Retention, and if your company has plateaued at 100% or higher NRR for too long – without really thinking about how to sustain that growth – you might be an NRR zombie.\n\n**You see the problem:** At first, high NRR is a great thing. It means all the hard work of signing up customers is paying off in spades. You're making more money from existing customers than you are from new ones, and it's just growing and growing... (pauses to scan the audience) But at some point – usually when your growth rate starts to slow down – that NRR number becomes a ceiling.\n\n**What happens next:** As founders of companies stuck in this plateau, we often start doing what I call \"NRR zombie behavior.\" We become so focused on maintaining our existing customer relationships that we lose sight of the need for new revenue streams. And that's when things get really scary – growth starts to stagnate, and your entire business model becomes vulnerable.\n\n**The signs are all around you:** So how do you know if you're an NRR zombie? Here are a few telltale symptoms:\n\n- Your product roadmaps seem more like tactical tweaks than visionary leaps.\n- You keep adding new customers at the same rate as last quarter, but your revenue isn't growing any faster – or even slowing down.\n- When you talk about growth strategies with your team, all anyone wants to do is \"optimize\" for NRR.\n\n**Now here's what you gotta do:** If you recognize yourself in these symptoms, it's time for an intervention. (laughs) Seriously though, here are the steps I recommend:\n\n1. **Admit that 100% isn't enough**: Recognize that your NRR plateau is not a sustainable long-term goal.\n2. **Get to know new customers again**: It's essential to go back and see what really matters in acquiring those first-time users – so you can figure out how to get more of them... (emphasizes) Not just optimize for retention!\n3. **Realign your product roadmap**: Break down silos between teams, prioritize innovation over incremental updates, and start asking tough questions like: \"What if I disrupted our own business model?\"\n4. **Invest in new revenue streams**: Whether it's through partnerships, upselling, or entirely new products – don't just focus on keeping existing customers happy; think about how to make them pay more money.\n\n**So what happens next?** (Leans forward) Well, if you can break free from the NRR zombie trap and start prioritizing growth over retention... The possibilities are endless! You might find yourself in a whole new league of companies that know exactly how to scale – not just maintain. So don't be an NRR zombie anymore; time's running out for those types.\n\n**Let’s move forward together**: I encourage you all today, whether you're at 100% or higher on NRR... (looks around the room) Don’t get stuck in that plateau! You have a choice to make.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay so we all have those customers that don't grow with us right and they just like suck up too much bandwidth because you got to keep them happy but it's not growing i mean if your nr is high 50% at least maybe even higher 60% 70% it means the majority of your customers are still growing today we all have those handful where there's no more growth right and so what do you do with that customer in particular don't get me wrong sometimes you got to keep a zombie alive for whatever reason but how much time should you spend on them as they suck up bandwidth from that great customer that might be growing 200% or something like that i mean think about it most of your customers are still growing today and then there's those handful right in fact at saster itself we have a handful we've got some zombies the ones that pay less than one percent of our revenue they don't really grow for us but if you look on average they're all above 50% so what do i think about them well i just want to keep that zombie alive and happy because it's not growing with me okay now there are times where you have a customer that is literally sucking the life out of your company right sometimes those zombies don't even pay enough to cover their own costs but then sometimes they can be worth it if you've got something in common or some other reason why you should keep them happy and so i think what i do with my team and as many founders have done this is we call these customers our zombies okay now that sounds a little weird but just because we don't grow with each other doesn't mean they're not important to us", "lemkin-clone-v6": "all right so let's just say you're doing well but maybe not really growing anymore and that can be okay or it can't it can be okay if your competition is struggling as long as the market grows even a little bit because in many cases the top players in any given space are still growing at 30% at 40% 50% just so long as there's some growth left in the world you might not need to change anything and that can be okay but if your market is contracting or shrinking no matter what you do you're going to hit a wall right it doesn't really make sense anymore if your product isn't growing then why are you selling a new feature right so I want to talk about zombies here just a little bit because we talked about these in the earlier session with Alex but they've sort of been hidden from us until now when everybody knows everything through AI and you can see all of this stuff for free yeah so an NRR zombie is pretty simple it's somebody that's growing very slowly right if your net retention rate is below 100% then people are leaving in some fashion or at least not renewing at a high enough rate okay but sometimes we get fooled by the math here and I want to share one example of this from Brex which was one of our saster unicorns that went public recently where they were growing very slowly at just below 100% net retention so it's like this zombie is sort of hiding in plain sight and then what happened right all those other competitors who were doing a little bit better started to eat into them right the market shrunk from a competitive perspective", "cleaned": "there's no way that I can say if you're an nrr zombie and your customer count is flat but your new bookings are going up your revenue is growing what do you do well if nothing else if nothing else lean into it and make sure everyone on the team knows this this is not a lost cause we have a future here even if our growth is slower than we'd like or we've plateaued and and maybe look for some efficiencies in marketing spend so you don't go out of business because the truth is if you're at scale with good enough logos there's always another year or two of new customers coming in right so if you get rid of all of your marketing budget tomorrow you will still lose customers next year okay but boy boy if you can keep them happy they just they just never end so as long as you've got any traction at all at least in terms of logos um and nrr even though it may be low lean in on it find a way to grow faster from there and maybe one last thing I'll say which is very counterintuitive to folks who are struggling when things are harder than ever and times are tougher it could be true today if you have even mediocre nrr just drive down churn and watch magic happen right think about it how much of your growth comes from net retention uh you know in theory it should come from new customers 100% but in practice even if you're adding 10% a month through new customers that means your existing base only has to grow 10% a month you need to grow 120% each year just to add 20 new customers", "balanced": "hey everybody i wanted to spend just a minute talking about nrr zombies there are so many today and what do i mean by that well it's pretty simple um it's a startup with really low or even negative net revenue retention but you know how to get customers it's got some brand out there maybe they have an interesting product something works for them at least enough that they can close new deals but their existing customers churn more than grow they lose money on each customer they've closed over time it's just mathematically impossible to build anything of any size if your existing customers don't last longer than they take to pay off uh you're not going anywhere as a business now we all see this in startups these days especially since march 20th when everything kind of came crashing down and sas and cloud companies saw growth slow to a crawl because so much demand was from covid related products and so many folks have seen their net revenue retention go down to zero they haven't lost any revenue per se but no one is expanding anyone isn't buying more seats and so those magical upgrades that used to happen every year or two aren't happening anymore and everyone's sort of panicked everyone thinks if our nr has gone to zero our long-term future is doomed and boy if nothing else remember this like the markets will bounce back there were lots of great SAS leaders before covet and there will be after and and in fact there already are if your nrr has tanked yes it sucks but let me give you four things to think about that might help you first of all realize where you need to focus your energy and secondly hopefully inspire you to do better okay point number one and this is obvious but worth saying", "r32": "to do that's going to be hard for you because your customers are leaving but what will happen is if they're not happy with you and you don't have a second act or at least some new product features coming this year something like it'll happen anyway so find out when they leave why did they go where did they go and listen how can I make them happier now maybe just losing one customer a month isn't such an issue maybe there's another reason why that matters but even if it doesn't matter let me tell you why tracking these folks is magical okay um if nothing else track all these lost customers if you haven't done it before as a founder use a tool and set up a trigger on all your customers who cancel and you'll get an email each week or every day which ever way you want to do it on all the ones that left you're gonna learn something special and you might also see that hey we've got a chance here maybe we should fly into town and visit those top 20 accounts that churned if you think that could help maybe you do but most importantly it gives you feedback on why they went you know what happened to our best customers last quarter what happened was i didn't follow up enough i didn't add more value i had a sales rep who wasn't good enough they were looking for two things and i only gave them one thing so you'll figure out what went wrong and then you'll put together a plan to improve next quarter next month next month you may not win back all these nrr zombies but you'll turn around next months book of business and you'll save a few deals from your existing base right so just", "repaired": "the ones that are doing well they're going long with their customers and if you're an NR zombie it's not too late but let me tell you I've seen so many companies that should have made it past four five years they just didn't grow their ACV enough um they got stuck at two or three thousand acv and they never got out of there they never got out of this middle middle market where the competition is intense and the buyers change every year so don't become an NR zombie okay get your nr up whatever it takes"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 551, "lemkin-clone-v5": 310, "lemkin-clone-v6": 298, "cleaned": 296, "balanced": 315, "r32": 310, "repaired": 99}}}}, {"id": "gold_011", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Give direct founder advice on diagnosing and handling a finance execution gap.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that sounding polished does not matter if the finance leader keeps missing the burn plan. Founders need accurate forecasting, tight cash discipline, and fast escalation when trust in the numbers breaks. The right move is to inspect the process directly, tighten controls, and decide quickly whether this is a fixable systems problem or a leadership problem.", "facts": ["The answer should separate polish from operational competence.", "It should explain that missing burn planning repeatedly is a serious execution issue, not a communication issue.", "It should recommend concrete corrective actions such as tighter forecasting, direct review, or leadership change if the gap persists."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI get it. You're killing it on sales, growth is off the charts, and your team is crushing it. But here's the thing: if you don't have a solid burn rate plan in place, all that progress will be for naught.\n\nAnd let me tell you, I've seen this scenario play out far too often. Your CFO comes across as super polished, but when it comes down to crunch time - and by \"crunch time\" I mean every month - your team is scrambling to get the burn rate under control.\n\nSo what's going on here? Why do so many companies struggle with burn rate planning?\n\n**Here are a few possible culprits:**\n\n1. **Lack of clarity around key metrics**: Are you tracking the right numbers? Do your teams know how their actions impact the bottom line?\n2. **No clear plan for adjusting spend**: When sales aren't meeting projections, do you have a well-thought-out plan to dial back expenses and stay on track?\n3. **Insufficient visibility into future cash flows**: Are you regularly reviewing your burn rate and making adjustments before it's too late?\n\n**So what can you do about it?**\n\nFirst things first: get your CFO involved in the planning process from day one. They should be working closely with sales, marketing, product, and other teams to ensure everyone is on the same page.\n\nNext, establish a clear set of key performance indicators (KPIs) that tie directly back to your burn rate. This might include metrics like:\n\n* Monthly active users\n* Customer acquisition cost (CAC)\n* Lifetime value (LTV)\n\nMake sure these KPIs are tracked regularly and used to inform spending decisions.\n\nNow, here's the part where most companies fall short: creating a plan for adjusting spend when things aren't going according to script. Don't just wing it - sit down with your team and create a clear roadmap for reducing expenses in case of an underperforming quarter.\n\nThis might involve:\n\n* Reducing marketing spend\n* Postponing non-essential hiring\n* Cutting back on travel or other discretionary costs\n\nFinally, make sure you're regularly reviewing your burn rate and making adjustments as needed. This should be a monthly exercise at the very least - ideally more frequently if possible.\n\n**In conclusion...**\n\nYour CFO may sound polished, but that's not enough to guarantee success. Burn rate planning is an essential part of any SaaS company's operations, and it requires regular attention from multiple teams. By establishing clear KPIs, creating a plan for adjusting spend, and regularly reviewing your burn rate, you'll be better equipped to navigate the inevitable ups and downs of growth.\n\nSo don't wait until it's too late - start planning for success today!\n\n**Best,**\n\nJason", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But We Are Missing The Monthly Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Can I Do About It?\nYou know, 95% of VPs of Finance and Controllers at startups are really good at … being a VP of Finance/Controller.\nBut that’s about it\nThat means 5% aren’t quite the right fit for your startup.  They’re often too polished to be great — but you might not notice until things get tough (and they always do).\nHow can we tell?  Here are some early red flags:\nYour VPs of Finance/Controller never meet with their direct reports. Ever.\nAnd the ones that don’t, well … they make an excuse for it\nI was on a board once where one VP of finance just said she didn’t want to manage people\nThey’re all about getting things “aligned” and making sure everyone is “on the same page”\nSo much time here is spent building processes — but not enough with actual humans running your company. It’s OK if they don’t know everything, because most VPs of Finance/Controllers come from public companies or small private ones where you really do have a full finance team\nBut …  they need to figure it out fast. If the CFO isn’t close to the CEO and the COO, then things can get tough for everyone else.\nIf they don’t get you cash, that’s the end of them as VP of Finance/Controller. They should be able to give you a plan — any time in the month\nThey shouldn’t need 3 days notice to do the most basic analysis. If they can’t tell you your burn rate (at least), then they don’t know what they’re doing.\nIt’s OK if they have some doubts — but they just can’t hide them from you, or at a minimum, be honest about it\nThey need to understand that sometimes cash flow and burn rates will fluctuate.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Why is my CFO not good at forecasting our burn rate?\nI think there’s a lot of confusion here, and it’s not uncommon.\nThe first thing you need to understand: your CFO should be great at cash management. Not forecasting. Great at managing the books, making sure all expenses are properly accounted for, that everyone gets paid on time, etc. That’s their number one job. And yes … they will often work closely with finance to forecast burn and other aspects of the business.\nBut here is what I see 80%+ of the time:\nYour CFO isn’t actually forecasting. They’re just looking at a spreadsheet from last month or quarter that someone else put together, probably in Excel. You can tell because it’s not really a plan — it’s mostly just a historical extrapolation. It rarely changes significantly on its own. And you’ll often hear: “I need the VP of Sales to update this forecast,” etc.\nNow maybe your CFO is actually doing real forecasting with your VP of Sales, and that’s great! I’m sure there are many folks out there who do exactly that. But what I’ve seen is that a lot of times … it doesn’t really work that way. Instead, the CFO will often just give you a static number from last month or quarter — “We’re burning $800K/month” — and then tell you to go talk to the VP of Sales if you want any more details.\nAnd this isn’t forecasting — it’s reporting. And it might be good enough for reporting … but forecasting? No way. It doesn’t work that well, and a lot of times I think VPs aren’t even really thinking about it deeply at all. They’re too busy closing deals.\nIf you find yourself in the same situation …\nFirst,\nget your CFO to spend 1-2 hours with you each month doing real forecasting together.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? If your CFO is consistently missing burn rate projections, it’s not just a minor issue—it signals deeper problems with how they’re managing and forecasting cash flow. Here’s what you should do: First, have an open conversation. Schedule a one-on-one meeting to discuss why forecasts are off. Ask them to walk through their process for creating monthly financials—how they’re tracking expenses, actual revenue versus forecasted revenue, and any surprises that might be causing discrepancies. Be specific about what went wrong last month or quarter and ask them to explain. It may well uncover some issues here, even if she/he seems polished. Get clarity on their tools and processes. Are they using a robust financial planning and analysis (FP&A) system like Anaplan or Excel-based templates? Make sure they understand how to tie each line item directly back to actual spend. This will help identify where errors are coming from. Challenge the assumptions. Are they basing next month/quarter’s numbers purely on historical trends? Or are they incorporating new information, such as changes in customer contracts, headcount plans, or shifts in market dynamics? You want to see that they’re actively adjusting forecasts based on real-time data. Look at their team composition. A “polished” CFO often has a strong accounting team behind them. If they don’t have dedicated FP&A resources or someone handling financial modeling specifically, this could be part of the problem.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? If your CFO is consistently missing burn rate targets, it’s not just a minor issue—it’s potentially catastrophic for your company. Here’s what you need to do right now: First, Don’t Panic (But You Need To Take Action) A missed monthly plan doesn’t necessarily mean your CFO isn’t good at their job. But … It does suggest that they’re either not understanding how cash works or not communicating effectively with you and/or other stakeholders. The Bottom Line Up Front : Your CFO needs to be able to predict and manage your cash position accurately enough to meet monthly burn rate targets. Anything less is unacceptable when you’re burning $500K+ per month like you mentioned. That kind of capital requires precise planning and execution. Step-by-Step Guide to Fix This", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO is very polished, but we’re consistently missing our burn rate plan almost every month. It’s always an excuse — “the team didn’t report it on time” or “there were some one-off expenses.” This isn’t just annoying; it creates real problems for planning and forecasting. If you can’t trust your own numbers, how can you make informed decisions about hiring, marketing spend, or even raising another round if needed? The root cause here seems to be a combination of poor financial controls and lack of transparency from the teams reporting into finance. In my experience with multiple startups, this usually boils down to two things: (1) inadequate dashboards and tracking systems, so people aren’t held accountable for their costs, and (2) weak relationships between finance and operations, which leads to information silos that prevent accurate forecasting. And more importantly … a lot of excuses when they miss the mark. My advice would be to push back hard on these excuses. Demand better visibility into where the variance is coming from each month. Ask the CFO to walk through specific line items in detail—what drove over/under-spending, what actions will be taken to correct course, and by when. You need to see clear corrective action plans tied to milestones and deadlines. If your CFO can’t deliver those insights or implement fixes quickly enough, you may need to start looking at whether they’re the right fit for your company. Here’s a simple framework to improve the situation: First, get a weekly cash update. Instead of waiting for monthly close-out meetings, ask the CFO to provide a daily or weekly cash position snapshot.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: How do you deal with a CFO that is very polished, well-spoken and has all his or her numbers right … when he/she misses burn rate projections almost every month?\nI’ve been there many times.\nFirst — let me tell you this isn’t really the CFO’s fault. Not entirely at least. The reason why your CFO may be missing the mark is likely due to one or more of the following reasons:\n1/ They Don’t Know Your Business Well Enough Yet\nCFOs aren’t always as “hands-on” as founders expect them to be. If they’re coming from a larger company or a different industry, they might not have deep enough understanding of your business model, customer segments, and sales cycles yet. This lack of contextual knowledge can lead to forecasting errors that compound over time.  This is common if a new hire comes from an environment where financial planning was outsourced or done by external partners rather than internal teams.\n2/ Inadequate Data Quality & Systems Integration\nIf your accounting systems aren’t properly integrated with your CRM or other operational tools, it creates a data silo effect that prevents accurate forecasts. Or worse, the data is just plain inaccurate (see point #5). A good CFO should help identify these issues and implement solutions.\n3/ Insufficient Customer Segmentation\nAre they treating all customers equally for forecasting purposes? That’s usually wrong. You need to forecast revenue based on ACV tiers, growth rates, churn probabilities, etc. If they’re not doing this segmentation, their models will naturally underestimate future revenue potential."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 449, "lemkin-clone-v5": 321, "lemkin-clone-v6": 315, "cleaned": 250, "balanced": 149, "r32": 272, "repaired": 258}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible LinkedIn post in the style of Jason M. Lemkin:\n\n**Burnout Alert!**\n\nDear fellow founders,\n\nI just got this question from one of our SaaStr community members, and it's a classic.\n\n\"Our CFO sounds very polished but we are missing the burn rate plan almost every month. What should I do?\"\n\n Ah, yes... the eternal struggle!\n\nFirst off, don't worry if your CFO isn't perfect on day 1 (or even week 100). They're human, just like you and me.\n\nBUT - it's super important to get that burn rate plan nailed down ASAP. Here's why:\n\n* Your investors want to see a clear picture of how much cash is flowing out the door each month.\n* You need accurate forecasting to make informed decisions about growth plans, hiring, and more.\n* And let's be real... not having this data can lead to some major headaches (and sleepless nights) down the line.\n\nSo what should you do?\n\n**Get your CFO on a \"burn rate plan bootcamp\" ASAP**\n\nSet clear expectations for them: deliver a reliable burn rate forecast by [insert deadline]. Make it their top priority!\n\nMeanwhile, here are 3 quick tips to help you get back on track:\n\n1. **Revisit the basics**: Take a step back and review your company's financials with your CFO. Ensure they understand what drives revenue & expenses.\n2. **Automate where possible**: Look into tools like [insert tool name] that can simplify forecasting and provide more accurate data.\n3. **Prior", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr:\nOur CFO Sounds Very Polished but We Are Missing The Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month\nWhat Should You Do?\nFirst, it can't be just about sound good.\nIt has to work in practice as well.\n\nSecond — make sure your CFO is focused on the future.  Not past performance alone.\nThird, you have to do better with cash.  No excuses here.  More here:\n\nDear SaaStr: What Should I Do If My CFO Can’t Run Cash Planning?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Seems Very Professional, But We Miss Our Monthly Budget by $500k-$1m Almost Every Month – What to Do?\nFirst of all — we’re going long in 2024\nWe are looking for a VP Finance/COO that is ready for the next stage and has experience building out an FP&A team. This role will be responsible for helping us scale our financial infrastructure as we move into our next phase.\nHere’s what you need to know about the position:\nOwn all aspects of company finance\nBuild out full-time FP&A team in 2024\nDevelop processes, tools and systems that allow for efficient forecasting, budgeting and reporting across multiple business units\nManage month-end close process with attention to detail on accuracy, timeliness and quality of financial statements\nWork closely with the CEO and other senior leaders to ensure alignment around key priorities and objectives.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? First, let’s be clear on what’s really happening here: your CFO isn’t being dishonest—they’re just not managing to plan properly. This is one of those classic issues that can sneak up on you if you aren’t careful. The root cause almost always comes down to one thing: they haven't built a reliable model. Not yet at least. Here's how it usually plays out: Your CFO will build a beautiful financial model upfront—lots of pretty charts and graphs, all looking very professional. And then when it comes time to actually forecast month-over-month cash burn, things start slipping. They might say something like “we didn’t quite hit our target last month because of X,” or “this quarter was harder than we expected.” But these excuses often mask a deeper issue. If they were truly building accurate models, they’d catch deviations before they happen—not after. So what do you do about it? You need to hold them accountable for both the model AND hitting their targets. And you have to do this now.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? Most CROs and VPs of Sales don’t really understand or care how finance works, and most CFOs aren’t product people. So it’s not uncommon for your VP of Finance to be pretty polished — they’ve worked at bigger companies before. But … that doesn’t always mean they know how to manage cash effectively at start-ups. Not all do. If you can keep your cash burn under control, just make sure there is no huge variance from plan every month. It’s OK if sales come up short sometimes, as long as you’re communicating with each other. And more here: The #1 Non-Obvious Mistake First-Time CEOs Make Hiring Your Co-founder / CFO Too Late | SaaStr https://youtube.com/watch?v=VZlPqQO8b2g …", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? First, you need to separate process from people issues here. The fact that your CFO sounds “very polished” suggests they have experience managing financial operations and presenting to boards or investors. That’s great for investor relations, audits, and compliance—but it doesn’t necessarily mean they’re detail-oriented enough for day-to-day cash management at an early-stage startup. Now let’s break down what might be happening based on how you described the issue:", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? It’s common for CFOs, especially those with traditional enterprise backgrounds, to be more comfortable with forecasting and planning rather than real-time management. Their primary focus is on strategic financial planning, budgeting, and compliance—areas where they excel—but they might not have experience with daily operational finance needs or building strong relationships with employees outside of executive circles. Here’s what you can do: • Ask them specifically how many burn rate forecasts they’ve done each month vs. just general projections. They may never have done this before. • If it turns out they don’t know how to forecast monthly cash flow/burn rates — that doesn’t mean your company isn’t ready to go big. It means your CFO hasn't been trained to predict future months' burn like most start-ups need. And if she's too polished … maybe she doesn't want to get her hands dirty. That's OK if it works well at an IPO’d public company. But it probably won’t work so well in a startup. You’ll need someone who knows how to manage cash closely and has experience working directly with team members beyond senior leadership. This could mean recruiting a new person whose background includes both accounting and operations management. Someone who has worked at startups earlier in their career will understand these needs better."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 246, "lemkin-clone-v5": 79, "lemkin-clone-v6": 144, "cleaned": 192, "balanced": 138, "r32": 91, "repaired": 236}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X post**\n\nDear SaaStr,\n\nI've been around this block a few times, and I'm sure some of you have too.\n\nWe just closed our Series B, and our CFO is on point - super polished, professional... all the things you want in an exec. But here's the thing: every month, we're missing the burn rate plan. By a mile.\n\nI've talked to him about it multiple times, and he says \"don't worry, I'm working on it\". But when the time comes around again, same story - no plan. And this isn't just a minor slip-up... our team is built for growth, but we need that burn rate to get there.\n\nWhat's going on here? Should I:\n\nA) Just accept \"polished\"", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But We Are Missing The Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month What Should I Do?\nIt's a sign of stress. And it should be fixed ASAP, ideally yesterday.\n\nThe #1 job for the CFO is to make sure you have enough cash in the bank each month to hit the plan. That you don't run out of money during the quarter / year.\n\nIn fact, as CEO, your main job shouldn't be anything but making sure there's always enough cash on hand. You can get very good at this if it is just part of who you are.\n\nBut it seems like right now, they're not doing their #1 Job for real, or at least well enough.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst,\nmake sure the CFO knows that getting burn", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO sounds very polished but we are missing the burn rate plan almost every month. What should I do?\n\nA few things:\n\nFirst, a burn plan is not really something you need to review each and every month.\n\nSecond, if your CFO doesn’t know it cold — then there’s a problem. The real issue here isn’t that they’re polished or not polished.\n\nThe real issue here is that the CFO is new. At least until now.\n\nYou can’t expect them to truly understand all this stuff without being around for 18 months at least.\n\nThird, and most important: just ask your head of finance / controller — if you have one — to help. It’s their job afterall.\n\nIt’s actually quite simple in the end.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? It's time to level up your finance team, especially if you're at scale or planning for it. Here's what I'd do:", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? This is a super common issue with many VPs of Finance and Controllers who have been trained to manage public companies, where the burn rate isn’t really that critical. And they may even be very good at it. But when you’re venture-backed or raise equity capital, not hitting your cash plan … can be deadly. First ask them what their top 2-3 goals for the year are. If cash planning isn’t one of them — move on. It’s just too important. Second, force yourself to make sure every month (or quarter) the actual burn rate is updated in your financial model. Not later. Now. So everyone knows exactly how", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? First, make sure your finance team is being truthful with you. If they’re consistently missing burn rate projections, it’s not just a matter of bad planning—it could be intentional or even deceitful behavior from someone on the team. But if that’s unlikely (and probably, since they seem polished), there might be other issues at play here. Here’s my advice: Get more involved and hold people accountable for their forecasts. You need to push back harder when you see variance that isn’t explained by normal business fluctuations. It sounds like you’ve already started this process. To take it further, set up regular check-ins with your head of finance after each month", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?\n\nHire a controller, and have your new hire manage them.\n\nThen you’ll know if they can do it or not.  It’s just one small test to see if she really has what it takes."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 134, "lemkin-clone-v6": 128, "cleaned": 43, "balanced": 131, "r32": 132, "repaired": 56}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**[Intro music plays, followed by applause]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Alright everyone! Welcome back to SaaStr, the best community for founders and executives building successful software companies. Today we're talking about something that's near and dear to many of our hearts... or should I say, our sanity? [chuckles]\n\nSo, let me introduce today's question. It comes from one of our members who is experiencing a very common challenge in scaling their company.\n\n**[Reading from the screen]**\n\n\"Dear SaaStr: Our CFO sounds very polished but we are missing the burn rate plan almost every month. What should I do?\"\n\nNow, this is not an uncommon problem at all. We've seen it many times before. Your financials seem great on paper... that is until you dig deeper and realize there's a gap between what your numbers say and what actually happens in reality.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nBurn rate planning is like the oxygen mask analogy, folks! You can't save everyone else if you're not breathing yourself. If you don't have a solid plan to manage your expenses and cash flow, it doesn't matter how polished your CFO may be or what other fancy financial tools you use.\n\n**[Writing on an imaginary whiteboard]**\n\nSo, let's break this down into three simple steps:\n\nFirstly, take ownership of the issue. As the founder or CEO, it's ultimately your responsibility to ensure that your company is running efficiently and effectively. Don't delegate this task solely to your CFO; instead, work closely with them to identify where things are going off track.\n\nSecondly, re-examine your financial processes. Are there any unnecessary expenses creeping in? Have you set up a system for regular reviews of your burn rate and cash flow projections?\n\nThirdly... [pausing for dramatic effect] Thirdly, get proactive! Use tools like forecast models or actualize planning to regularly review and adjust your budget.\n\n**[Screen transitions to show examples of financial management tools]**\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking: \"Jason, this all sounds great in theory, but what about when my CFO says we don't have time for this?\" Well, here's the thing: no one has more time than they think. It's a matter of prioritizing.\n\n**[Closing]**\n\nSo there you have it – burn rate planning is not optional; it's essential to running a successful software company. I encourage all of our members who are struggling with similar issues to share their experiences and advice in the comments below.\n\nThanks for watching, everyone! [outro music plays]\n\nNote: Jason Lemkin often starts his talks with a humorous anecdote or an example from real-life experience, but since we don't have that information here, I skipped it.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "what's going on here we got this question and it is so classic from so many Founders that have gotten to 10 million or more in Revenue they're like what should I do with my CFO because everyone else has a good one right yeah the CEO always gets fired first but your CFO you'll hear no complaints about them all day long as long as the company's still growing and then it just breaks down when growth slows so this is classic so she sounds very polished we're missing our burn rate plan almost every month what should I do well listen we've got another one in here and this is a great question too from another CEO that's going through some similar things so look let me share two learnings with you first of all the number one thing to know about CFOs when your company is small they don't really matter at least not for a while right they may have been good at accounting or whatever but it doesn't really matter because there's no money that can be wasted right yeah so as long as the CEO knows how much cash in the bank and you're growing fast you kind of ignore what the CFO is doing right that's just fine until growth slows then all of a sudden oh my God they don't know how to do anything else or maybe worse not only do they not know how but they actually are making things worst now because they've got their own little priorities and ways of doing things so as long as you're growing fast it doesn't really matter what your CFO does right it's just fine until growth slows down then this is the classic issue that we have here", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I think you need to find someone better than your current CFO.\nThey sound polished, and that’s great if they’re good at closing a VC round or impressing investors\nBut burn rate forecasting is core to SaaS management. It’s something every top CRO, CEO, CTO — everyone — has to do on the fly almost all day long in a well-run start-up.\nIt can’t be missed.\nYou need someone that’s good at it. If your CFO doesn’t have this skill set, then you’ve made an error\nAnd no one will forgive you when they miss another burn rate plan.\nBut here’s what I do know:\nIf you just get someone better than the current person on finance — maybe even a VPs of Finance or Controller-level hire from somewhere else with experience in your market and size — that can make all the difference. And probably not much more\nAnd then, once you have this great team member in place, I’d suggest:\nMake sure everyone is aligned around forecasting. If there’s confusion about what to include or exclude, it will impact accuracy.\nUse a tool like PlanGrid or Glean that helps with budgeting and forecasting. It can automate many of the tedious tasks and make your CFO more efficient\nHave regular check-ins (like weekly) for review and adjustments. This ensures you catch any issues early before they become critical problems\nSet clear expectations at the beginning, so everyone knows what’s expected in terms of accuracy and timeliness.\nBut mostly — just find someone better than them on finance.\nThis isn’t a new problem. It can be fixed if you hire right.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: Why Do So Many Founders Quit After Just 6 Months?\nDear SaaStr: How Much Time Should I Spend Selling to Investors?", "cleaned": "Dear Saster, our CFO sounds very polished but we’re missing the burn rate plan almost every month. What should I do? First, ask your finance team to provide you with three months of actual burn rates along with their projections for the next two quarters. This will help identify patterns and areas where they might be consistently overestimating or underestimating expenses. Second, have an open conversation about what went wrong each time — don’t let it slide. If there’s a pattern, address it directly. Third, ensure they understand that this isn’t just a nice-to-have metric, but rather a critical tool for managing cash flow and making strategic decisions. Fourth, consider bringing on someone who can handle forecasting if it becomes too much for your current team. But before that, make sure it is really a lack of capacity vs a lack of focus and drive. Fifth, review your financial processes to see if there’s room for improvement. Are there any manual steps or assumptions being made without proper documentation? Automating these could help reduce errors. And finally, set clear expectations around accuracy — perhaps aiming for within 10% variance from actuals. Be specific about what constitutes “on track” versus off-track. Your company’s success depends heavily on accurate planning and execution, so addressing this issue now is crucial.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished but We are Missing the Burn Rate Plan Almost Every Month. What Should I Do? This is one of those uncomfortable moments where you need to have tough conversations, not just with your CFO, but also with yourself and other stakeholders. Here’s how I’d approach it: First, take a step back and assess if this is actually an issue or if there’s some communication gap that needs to be addressed. Ask yourself: Does this happen because they’re being conservative on burn rate projections? Are they using historical averages versus actual spending patterns? Or is something more systemic going on here? If it’s conservatism, then you might want to lean into data-driven forecasting rather than gut instincts. But if it’s consistent errors, we need to dig deeper. Second, make sure everyone involved has access to real-time spend tracking. Sometimes these misjudgments come from lack of visibility into actual expenses. Implement better expense reporting systems or tools like Ramp that give you instant insights into cash outlays. Third, get clear on what “missing” means. Is missing by $50K, $100K, or $200K? That makes a huge difference in strategy decisions. The bigger the miss, the sooner you should address it. Fourth, discuss what kind of margin for error feels comfortable. If you’re growing at $10M ARR, can’t afford to miss by more than $25-30K per month without flagging it immediately. Fifth, consider bringing someone else into financial planning. Maybe your VP Finance isn’t the right person for this role anymore. You may need to bring in a controller or head of finance who specializes in scaling companies efficiently. Sixth, don’t let this slide forever.", "r32": "Dear Saastr: Our CFO Sounds Very Polished But He Keeps Not Understanding The burn rate plan almost every month, what should I do? It’s time for a bit of a wake-up call here. When you hire a polished CFO or even VP finance at scale, especially if they’ve been at a big tech company, they often aren’t great at understanding cash flow and how to predict it as well as someone more experienced with earlier-stage companies. This is because their job was to manage accounting systems, financial reporting, and investor relations rather than forecasting and managing working capital. A lot of folks get very good at that last part when they start doing venture-backed startups, though. So first let me just summarize some issues that may be happening in your head of finance’s mind, which I think will help you understand why this happens so much, and then we’ll dig into my advice on what to do next. And maybe most importantly, what not to do. Number one, they don’t really want to model things out too far ahead. Why would they? They’re not incentivized to. In fact, once you raise money from VCs, oftentimes they prefer less accurate models later in the year, so they can hit the plan and hit the burn and look good. Or at least feel like they are hitting the mark and looking good. As long as it looks pretty good in the end. That way, they seem competent. Because if the math doesn’t add up until late in the year, who cares? Just make sure the revenue numbers go up, right? You have enough money. Second, they don’t know what to model. If you ask them to model cash for three years out, they might freeze. “What should I put there?” Thirdly, it’s hard to see the future.", "repaired": "you've got to do it this week or next week and then you have to agree on that number and track it every day and make sure everyone is aligned to that burn rate plan because if they're not, the one thing you can't afford as Founders is to be surprised by how much cash you have in the bank um and so whatever else happens in your startup that's stressful there should never be any stress around money at least once you get past two million three million four million Revenue right now we just had a deep dive with Brian Halligan the founder of HubSpot he did not worry about money until 40 million AR that was his only concern at HubSpot after that everything else mattered yes but no the burn rate didn't matter for him until 30 or 40 million ARR so don't let it sneak up on you don't let anyone hide from it make sure you know where the burn rate stands today and you come together with your team every single month and you set an ambitious goal but realistic goal going forward okay last point related to this uh and which is what's your top tip for getting your company ready to scale when the time comes well you need to hire someone who knows more than you do early on you will hire some VPS later that are better than you that's true but most importantly you'll find those folks as founders and sometimes even as CEOs when you go to hire a head of marketing or sales or product or finance something and you meet with them and you realize boy this person is great yeah they've done it all before but it's different um it's different at our stage at our ACV at our deal size and especially you know you'll see this when you start to hire people that worked at these public companies like you're gonna think wow look they work at twilio"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 447, "lemkin-clone-v5": 297, "lemkin-clone-v6": 296, "cleaned": 217, "balanced": 278, "r32": 305, "repaired": 336}}}}, {"id": "gold_012", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Set realistic expectations for deploying an AI SDR and what readiness is required.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that an AI SDR can go live quickly in raw technical terms, but meaningful deployment takes longer because it depends on clean data, working messaging, guardrails, and supervision. Founders should think in stages: setup, prompt and workflow tuning, and then ongoing monitoring to see whether the agent is actually producing qualified conversations.", "facts": ["The answer should avoid treating AI SDR deployment as instant magic.", "It should explain that speed depends on process clarity, data quality, and outbound readiness.", "It should mention that founders need to supervise, iterate, and measure quality after launch."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI get this question all the time. \"Jason, how long does it take to deploy an AI-powered Sales Development Representative (SDR)?\" Well, I'll give you my two cents.\n\nFirst off, let me clarify that deploying an AI SDR is not just about throwing some code together and watching magic happen. It's a complex process that requires careful planning, execution, and iteration. And the answer to how long it takes will vary depending on several factors.\n\n**The 5 Key Components of an AI-Powered SDR**\n\nTo give you a better idea, here are the five key components I always look at when building out an AI-powered SDR:\n\n1. **Data Ingestion**: This is where we collect and process customer data from various sources (e.g., CRM, marketing automation tools). The quality of your data will directly impact the performance of your AI SDR.\n2. **Model Training**: We need to train our machine learning models on this data to generate high-quality leads and conversations. This requires a large dataset, computational resources, and expertise in ML engineering.\n3. **Integration with CRM/ERP**: Our AI SDR needs to integrate seamlessly with existing sales tools (e.g., Salesforce) to ensure that all interactions are tracked and synced properly.\n4. **Content Generation**: We need to generate high-quality content for our AI SDR to use in conversations, including emails, phone scripts, and chatbots.\n5. **Continuous Monitoring & Feedback**: Our AI SDR needs to be constantly monitored and optimized based on performance metrics (e.g., conversion rates, response times).\n\n**The Timeline**\n\nNow that we've broken down the components, here's a rough estimate of what you can expect:\n\n* Data Ingestion: 2-4 weeks\n\t+ This is usually the quickest part of the process, assuming you have access to existing customer data and technical expertise.\n* Model Training: 8-12 weeks\n\t+ Building high-quality models requires significant computational resources and ML engineering expertise. Be prepared for some trial-and-error!\n* Integration with CRM/ERP: 4-6 weeks\n\t+ Integrating your AI SDR with sales tools can be complex, especially if you're working with multiple systems.\n* Content Generation: 2-4 weeks\n\t+ Creating high-quality content requires a deep understanding of your target audience and industry. Don't underestimate the time required for this step!\n* Continuous Monitoring & Feedback: Ongoing (every week/month)\n\t+ Your AI SDR will need regular tuning to optimize performance, so plan for ongoing maintenance.\n\n**The Total Timeframe**\n\nAssuming you have some technical expertise in-house or are working with partners who can help accelerate the process, I'd estimate a total timeframe of 20-40 weeks (~5-10 months) from concept to deployment. However, this can vary depending on your specific requirements and resources.\n\nIn conclusion, building an AI-powered SDR is no easy feat. It requires careful planning, technical expertise, and significant resources. But with the right approach and partners in place, you can create a high-performing AI SDR that drives real revenue growth for your business.\n\n**How long has it taken to deploy an AI SDR at your company? Share your experiences in the comments below!**\n\n---\n\n(Note: I tried to emulate Jason Lemkin's style by incorporating his tone and language from various blog posts, while making sure the content is relevant and helpful)", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR?\nA few weeks. But here’s what you really need to know:\nYou can’t just buy some AI tool, train it on your existing email database and expect magical results.\nThe best tools use natural language processing (NLP) plus a ton of domain knowledge specific to sales development. This is not easy — even the most advanced vendors have spent years building this capability. The training process alone could take days to weeks depending on how much data you’re working with. And here’s the thing:\nYour existing database probably isn’t going to be enough. You need a ton of good, clean, properly formatted email addresses and content. If your list is terrible — or too small — it won’t work. What actually works well?\nFirst,\nstart by using some AI SDR tools for just 1-2 days as an “experiment”. Don’t commit to anything yet.\nSecond,\nif you have a good database, then the training process should take around 7-10 days with full automation (you probably need human review and validation of each output).\nThird,\nonce it’s working — keep feeding in new data. The AI will get smarter over time as long as your team is using it.\nFourth,\nif you don’t have a great database, then the training process can take months. This isn’t really an “AI SDR” anymore — more like just another tool that doesn’t work very well.\nAnd here’s what I see almost no one do:\nYou need to be using AI SDR tools for real every single day if you want them to actually get any good at it.\nIf your team is only logging in once a week, then the thing isn’t getting better. It’ll just keep spitting out mediocre results over and over again.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I recently deployed GPT-4 and Claude Code, two prominent AI tools that have revolutionized how companies interact with customers. This is my experience rolling out these AI solutions.\nMy First Impression:\nThe AI agents are amazing at what they do, but the deployment process was much more complex than I anticipated. Here’s a breakdown of each tool and their respective challenges:\n1. GPT-4 (ChatGPT):\nI used Lovable.ai to deploy GPT-4 on our website for chat support. The integration went relatively smoothly with minimal technical involvement since they have pre-built connectors for SaaS platforms like ours.\nThe only thing that took some time was the initial setup and fine-tuning of the AI model, but once I got it right, the agent works as expected. It’s fast, accurate, and has already saved me a significant amount of time on customer support.\n2. Claude Code (Agentforce):\nThis one was more challenging because we wanted to create our own custom SDR. We decided not to use AgentForce for now but they have some great resources and pre-built connectors that made the integration easier than expected.\nOur biggest challenge with Claude was getting it to work properly inside Salesforce, which took about 3 days of trial-and-error tweaking with their support team’s help.\nClaude Code is incredibly powerful once set up correctly. It can send personalized emails at scale without human intervention and has already generated significant revenue for our business.\nDeployment Timeline:\nHere’s a rough estimate of how long each tool took to deploy, from start to finish:\nGPT-4 (ChatGPT): 2 days\nClaude Code: 3 days + 1 week fine-tuning\nOverall AI Deployment Experience:\nThe initial setup and configuration were the most time-consuming parts. Once we got both agents up and running, they’ve been working flawlessly with minimal maintenance needed.\nI was surprised by how well Claude Code performed in our sales environment.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR? In our experience, you can go from zero to first outbound emails within 7-10 days. But here’s the part most founders miss: that first week is all about setup and refinement — not sending perfect messages yet. The real work happens before your AI ever sends an email. Day 1-2: Setup & Training (48 hours) You need to give your AI enough context to actually write good outreach. This isn’t just uploading a spreadsheet with contact information — although that matters too. We’re talking about: A deep dive into what makes your product valuable for each buyer persona Detailed success stories from existing customers Clear guidelines on tone, voice, and messaging Avoidance of common SaaS sales mistakes Specific industry knowledge that helps your AI sound credible Your AI needs to know when to be aggressive vs. conservative based on company size, industry, and previous engagement patterns. That requires data and human judgment up front. If you skip this step, you’ll get generic messages that don’t resonate — which will hurt your brand more than help it. Day 3-5: Refinement & Quality Control (72 hours) Once your AI has basic training, you spend the next few days refining its output. This means reading through every single message before they send, flagging anything off-brand or wrong, and providing clear feedback on what worked and what didn’t. The goal isn’t perfection at this stage — it’s iteration toward something solid. At SaaStr itself, we went through multiple iterations of our AI SDR prompts until we were happy with the quality.", "balanced": "Here’s what we learned after deploying three different AI agents across our portfolio companies and our own operations. The honest answer is “it depends” — but here are some real benchmarks from companies that have done this successfully. If you want to get ahead of this, start now. The window for competitive advantage is closing fast.", "r32": "But here’s what I’ve learned after deploying agents across multiple B2B companies: The companies that get this right move faster than ever before. They’re closing deals, scaling teams, and hitting numbers they never could have with humans alone. And yes, it takes some work upfront to make it happen. But if you don’t do it — someone else will. Someone who is already ahead of you in adopting AI for sales. If you want your sales team to be hyper-productive, if you want them working on high-value tasks like relationship-building and strategic selling, not data entry or repetitive outreach — then the investment is worth it. You can start tomorrow. Just pick one agent, one use case, one process, and go deploy it. For real. Not “talk” about it. Don’t wait until next quarter. Your competitors aren’t waiting. Neither should you. P.S. Want to see how our own SaaStr.ai AI SDR works? We deployed it ourselves (we didn’t hire a vendor), we trained it ourselves, and we manage it ourselves. Here are just a few results: Our AI SDR generates 1,000+ personalized emails per week with better response rates than traditional outbound. It books meetings at 10x the rate of our previous human SDRs. And most importantly, it runs 24/7 without complaining, taking vacation days, or needing a raise. This is where sales goes in 2026. The question isn’t whether you’ll adopt AI in sales. It’s whether you’ll lead the adoption curve or follow behind. Lead. Or Follow.", "repaired": "But you can’t just hand off your CRM and expect results. You need:\nYour best ICP documentation (who are these exact personas?)\nA clear plan for what success looks like\nDaily quality checks on output\nWeekly strategy adjustments based on performance\nThe companies that get this right see real ROI within weeks. The ones that don’t end up with expensive tools collecting dust. Start by treating it like hiring any other critical go-to-market resource — but faster, because you’re not waiting for someone to show up in person. If you do it right, you’ll be surprised how quickly AI agents start delivering better conversations than many human reps ever did."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 525, "lemkin-clone-v5": 302, "lemkin-clone-v6": 308, "cleaned": 269, "balanced": 56, "r32": 248, "repaired": 110}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a LinkedIn-style post in the voice of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**How Long Does it Really Take to Deploy an AI-Powered Sales Development Rep (SDR)?**\n\nI get asked this question all the time. And I love sharing my answer: **it takes 3-6 months, with a team size of at least 2 people**.\n\nThat's right - deploying a high-performing AI SDR doesn't happen overnight. It requires significant upfront work to set up and optimize the technology, not to mention training your sales team on how to effectively use it.\n\nBut here's the thing: **it's worth it**. When done correctly, an AI-powered SDR can deliver 2-5x more qualified leads than a human-only SDR in just a few months' time.\n\nSo what are some key milestones you should expect during your own AI SDR deployment journey?\n\n1. **Tech setup**: 1-3 weeks - selecting the right platform, integrating with CRM and marketing automation tools\n2. **Data prep**: 1-4 weeks - getting the data in order to train models accurately (this is often the most time-consuming part!)\n3. **Model training & testing**: 2-6 weeks - fine-tuning algorithms for optimal performance\n4. **Sales team onboarding**: 2-4 weeks - educating reps on how to work with AI-driven leads and feedback loops\n\nOf course, every company's situation is different. But I've seen it time and again: when you put in the effort upfront, an AI SDR can become a", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR?\nIt’s hard because I’m not sure most VPs of Sales can figure out how to use most of these tools, and many just don’t want to. But the answer is usually a few weeks to fully ramp up.\nAnd in my experience — you need a very good AE (Account Executive) or even 2-3 to manage the AI SDR for it to work at all well.\nThe best ones are super hands-on with new tools, and know how to get them working. And then they just love them once deployed.\nA few weeks is long enough if you have a decent team of AEs that want to make sure their AI SDR works\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin  (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 1, 2025\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\nNew SaaStr University FREE Course: How To Deploy an AI Agent in a Week (Or Less!)\nDear SaaStr: What Are the Long-Term Benefits of Being Part of SaaS?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?\nIt’s not hard. I mean, it is kind of hard. But it’s pretty easy these days.\nI’ve seen the whole process happen in a single week:\nWeek 1: Choose and buy\nChoose your vendor (we use Lovable) and make sure you can deploy it to your Salesforce or HubSpot\nBuy it, which should take about an hour\nWeek 2-3: Deploy and train\nDeploy it into production. This is where things usually slow down a bit because you need some human intervention to get the AI SDRs set up properly in Salesforce\nTrain them on all your past data\nMake sure they can handle all types of emails, not just perfectly formatted ones\nWeek 4-5: Monitor and optimize\nMonitor how it’s performing compared to humans\nAdjust settings as needed based on what you see\nThe biggest thing I learned here is that the deployment itself shouldn’t take too long—maybe a few days. But training and optimization? That can stretch out over several weeks. And honestly, you’re going to need some human help along the way, at least for the first month or two.\nBut it’s worth it!\nHere are our latest learnings from using an AI SDR:\nWe’ve seen our open rates on outbound emails improve by 100%–200%\nOur response rate has gone up\nThe quality of responses is better\nIt doesn’t replace a human but complements them nicely\nWhat I learned here: The deployment itself shouldn’t take", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? The honest answer is it depends. But here’s what I’ve learned deploying 10+ AI agents across our own portfolio and investing in dozens more at SaaStr Fund. If you’re serious, plan for at least 30 days — maybe closer to 60 if you want it right. And if you haven’t done this before, budget even more time than that. You’ll need it. Here are my guidelines based on what we’ve seen work: Weeks 1-2: Training & Iteration (Minimum) This is non-negotiable. Your first pass will be mediocre. Even with top vendors like Artisan, Qualified, etc., you have to train the agent every single day. Every morning, review responses, correct hallucinations, refine context. We spent weeks just getting our first agent right — not because the tool was broken but because understanding your ICP, perfecting prompts, handling edge cases takes real human time. And then you iterate daily after launch. Week 3-4: Data Integration & Optimization Once you have something working, focus on feeding it good data. Clean up your CRM. Add historical activity signals. Connect relevant web behavior. A clean dataset + strong training = better results faster. Most founders spend way too much time trying to get their AI to generate content or send emails without focusing on the foundation first. Weeks 5-12+: Continuous Refining After you hit initial velocity, you’re entering optimization mode. Test different messaging variations. Track response rates by persona.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? Here’s my real experience deploying 11+ AI agents at SaaStr over the past year. We’ve deployed 12 AI GTM Agents — and we’re already seeing 15-20% of our pipeline come from them. But it wasn’t easy, and most founders get this wrong. The biggest mistake I see is thinking you can deploy an agent overnight. That’s not how this works. First, pick one vendor. Don’t try to run parallel pilots with multiple vendors. Pick one, commit for 90 days minimum, and go deep. Then, let me break down what actually happens during that first 30-day sprint because it’s different than traditional onboarding. The First 7 Days: Setup & Training (Days 1-7) Week One: Agent Onboarding Day 1-2: Basic setup, API keys, Salesforce connector, basic training Day 3-5: Campaign creation, messaging templates, segmentation rules Day 6-7: Initial content loading, tone guidelines What You’ll Actually Do During This Time: Review your target accounts. Who are these people? What do they care about? What pain points do they have? Build out campaign lists. If you want to hit $100K ARR next quarter, who needs to be in market now? Write daily prompts. Start simple. “Reach out to [target account], reference recent event attendance, ask if interested in sponsoring.” Test and refine continuously. Your early outputs will likely need human review. After Those First Few", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? The answer is different for every company, but here’s what we’ve learned deploying over 50+ AI agents at SaaStr and our portfolio companies. The Quick Win (Days) First, let me give you the easy one: Outbound AI Email + BDRs This is where most founders start—and it makes sense. You can get something running with your existing ICP and data in under two weeks. We saw this firsthand when we deployed Agentforce ourselves. Within days, we were sending hundreds of high-quality emails that converted at similar rates to our human outbound. Here’s what made it work: Pre-work matters. Before any agent goes live, you need clean data—your ideal customer profile, segmented lists, proper lead routing, and clear next steps. Most companies skip this step and wonder why their agents don’t perform. Plan 2-5 days upfront on setup before deployment. Then deploy. Pick an email-based tool like Artisan or Qualified first. They’re relatively quick to set up compared to voice agents. Start small—send 100 emails per day from Day One. Gradually scale as you refine content and optimize performance. If you follow these steps, you’ll have a basic outbound program running within 10-14 days. That’s not bad! But Remember: “Quick” Doesn’t Mean Easy Just because deployment time is short doesn’t mean execution will be effortless. You still need daily management during ramp-up, continuous optimization, and honest feedback loops. Expect to spend 60", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? The best case is you can go live with your AI SDR in one week. That’s what I did when we first rolled out our Agentforce agent, “10K”, at SaaStr itself (not our outbound agents). We had it ingesting data from Salesforce and generating emails by Friday afternoon — fully operational within seven days. But here’s what most people miss: that was because everything else was already set up. We’d been collecting all this intent data through tools like Apollo or Clay for months before deploying the agent. Our website had tracking on every page visitors looked at. We’d done the hard work upfront getting our systems connected. If you’re starting cold without any infrastructure in place, it’ll take longer. You need time to ingest your CRM contacts, connect your web traffic sources, map out lead scoring logic, decide which email templates to use, train the agent on your voice and tone guidelines, and set up proper handoff processes to human reps. That process typically takes two to three weeks minimum, sometimes four if you’re doing it yourself. Even then, training usually requires daily oversight for the first few weeks as the agent learns patterns and edge cases. So yes, theoretically possible in one week if everything is ready to go. In practice? Plan on 2-4 weeks to get something working, then ongoing optimization after that. And be honest about whether you have enough data and setup in place first. A related post here:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 230, "lemkin-clone-v5": 170, "lemkin-clone-v6": 249, "cleaned": 246, "balanced": 233, "r32": 243, "repaired": 256}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI've been getting asked this question a LOT lately... \"How long does it take to deploy an AI-powered Sales Development Representative (SDR)?\"\n\nTo be clear: **it can vary wildly**. I've seen some companies get up and running with AI-driven SDRs in as little as 3-6 months, while others have struggled for over a year.\n\nHere are the key factors that'll determine your timeframe:\n\n1. **Data quality**: Do you have clean, well-tagged data to train your models? If not, it's like trying to build a skyscraper on shaky ground.\n2. **AI/ML expertise**: Can your team handle building and training custom", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take for a new SaaS company to deploy a proper, fully-functioning artificial intelligence (AI) sales development representative (SDR)? I want to get this right. A basic AI SDR is not enough for most of us. It needs to be able to handle all of our emails and automate some of the follow-up steps in a way that really works. And we need it to work well, before we start scaling up how many leads are being generated by this tool. What I’m seeing from other SaaS companies is that they have to deploy a lot more tools than just one AI agent. But if you already have an email platform and some sort of CRM, then maybe the time can be significantly shorter. Maybe it’s 4 weeks?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: What’s a reasonable timeline for deploying AI-powered sales tools, specifically AI SDRs or AEAs? The answer varies greatly based on your current tech stack and level of automation. I’ve seen it take anywhere from 2-6 months to deploy some AI SDRs, depending on how much you already have set up. You can definitely get started with a few weeks of work, but in many cases the best results come when you start over (at least partially) rather than trying to layer new tools and workflows atop existing ones.\n\nIf your company is starting from scratch here — I’d just tell everyone to hire an AI SDR expert for 30 days. They can get it all set up and then hand it off to the rest of your team once it’s", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? The best answer is probably … months. Not days, not weeks — but at least a few months to train and iterate. Let me break down what it takes: Month 1-2: Training & Iteration This is where most founders fail. They buy an AI SDR tool (let’s say Artisan or Qualified), plug in their prospect list, turn it on, and expect magic. That doesn’t work that way. You need time: Week 1-2: Data cleanup. Clean your CRM. Remove bad contacts. Update stale information. Make sure everything is up-to-date because this will feed into your AI. Weeks 3-6: Content creation. Your AI needs copy for emails,", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? About a week. That’s how long it takes to deploy your first Agentforce, Qualified, Artisan, etc. agent — if you do all the work yourself and don’t wait. And that’s the key part: not waiting. The average deployment is six months or longer because companies are trying to get it perfect upfront instead of starting with something basic and iterating quickly. Here’s what I’ve learned from deploying 10+ agents across our portfolio at SaaStr Fund and our own operations: The Setup Reality Check The hardest part isn’t the technology—it’s getting your data ready. Most teams spend way too much time on vendor selection before they even have clean data to train their agent. If your CRM is", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR? A week. Maybe two if you’re starting from scratch and don’t know what you’re doing. Here’s why I’m so direct: Most founders are wasting a ton of time with their AI agent deployments because they’re not being honest about what works now, versus what doesn’t work yet — but might later. Let me break it down for you: What Works Today (Weeks 1-2) Outbound sequences that hit warm leads immediately Email nurturing that replaces zero-human-touch follow-ups Inbound qualification that never sleeps Support deflection on simple questions The common thread? All these use cases already have working references or proven playbooks somewhere online. You can copy them verbatim and get strong results within days.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?\n\nIf you’re doing it right, it’s not a weekend project. Here’s what I’d expect:\n\nWeek 1-2: Discovery and Training\n\nYou need to define your use case properly before deploying anything.\n\nDay 1-2: Identify Your Target Use Case\n\nPick something simple first—outbound to warm leads or existing contacts. Avoid inbound qualification (that’s harder). Start with people who already know your brand and have expressed interest.\n\nDefine Success Metrics Upfront\n\nWhat constitutes “good”? Are you looking for meetings booked, pipeline generated, or revenue closed? Make sure everyone agrees on KPIs before training begins.\n\nDays 3-14: Data Preparation & Initial Setup\n\nThis is where most"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 112, "lemkin-clone-v5": 133, "lemkin-clone-v6": 132, "cleaned": 117, "balanced": 129, "r32": 125, "repaired": 114}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: How Long Does It Take to Deploy an AI SDR?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "(clears throat) Alright, folks! Welcome back to another episode of \"Dear SaaStr\". I'm your host, Jason Lemkin. Today's question comes from a fellow SaaS entrepreneur who wants to know:\n\n\"How long does it take to deploy an AI-powered Sales Development Representative (SDR)?\"\n\n(pauses for dramatic effect)\n\nNow, I've seen many companies try to build out their sales teams with AI-driven SDRs. Some have succeeded, while others... well, let's just say they're still trying to figure it out.\n\nThe reason I'm excited about this question is that the answer isn't as simple as \"just 6-12 months\" or something like that. The reality is, deploying an AI-powered SDR requires a deep understanding of your sales process, customer behavior, and technology infrastructure.\n\nHere's what you need to consider:\n\n**Step 1: Define Your Sales Process**\nYou can't just plug in an AI SDR without knowing how your sales team currently operates. What are the key pain points? Where are the opportunities for improvement? You need to map out every stage of your sales process, from lead gen to close.\n\n**Step 2: Choose the Right Technology Stack**\nNot all AI-powered SDRs are created equal. You'll want to select a platform that integrates seamlessly with your CRM and other tools you're already using. Don't be afraid to try out different options before committing to one solution.\n\n**Step 3: Develop Your Content Strategy**\nYour AI SDR is only as good as the content it's feeding on. This means creating high-quality, engaging copy for emails, LinkedIn messages, and even voicemails (if you're using that sort of thing). You'll also want to create a robust lead qualification process.\n\n**Step 4: Train Your Team**\nWhile your AI SDR is handling some of the heavy lifting, it's still crucial to have human sales reps on board. They need to understand how to work alongside this new technology and build on its strengths.\n\nSo, when can you expect to deploy an effective AI-powered SDR? Well...\n\n**The Timeline: 6-18 Months**\nHere's a rough estimate of what to expect:\n\n* Month 1-3: Define your sales process and choose the right technology stack\n* Month 4-6: Develop your content strategy and create high-quality lead gen materials\n* Month 7-9: Train your team on how to work with AI SDR\n* Month 10-12: Launch your AI-powered SDR and monitor its performance\n\nAnd that's assuming everything goes smoothly. In reality, you may need an additional 6 months or more to fine-tune the process.\n\n**Conclusion**\nDeploying an AI-powered SDR is a journey, not a destination. It requires patience, dedication, and a willingness to adapt as your sales team evolves. Don't be discouraged if it takes longer than expected – just remember that every minute you invest upfront will save you time and resources in the long run.\n\nThanks for tuning in, folks! If you have any follow-up questions or want to share your own experiences with AI SDRs, hit us up on Twitter using #SaaStr.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay so we've done a lot on our new website on ai sdr and i think it's time for us to get into some more tactical stuff here as this is probably the last question of the day but what does it really take to deploy these things right so the other thing you can see from all the sessions we did earlier today with clipper clavio etc. that there are a lot of tools in ai sdr and i think one mistake people make is they try to buy every tool at once for an agent which is not going to work it's not gonna work because the agents don't do what humans do so you can't buy all these tools at once but what we've learned from our own experience over 10 years of running saster and doing email marketing is that the better your data the easier everything else is right it really works so let me give you a simple example here this is an ai i'm using for my own business called clipper which i just started to use last week but within two weeks we're already sending 20.000 plus emails out of it every day and what's amazing about this is the data in clipper is better than anything else that exists today so if you can get good data into any agent then everything else will work right a simple example here at saster we have our own database of all the top speakers from sastra annual which is over 10 years old it's one thousand plus people and we've been adding to this for 12 years now it really works so what i would do if you're thinking about deploying an ai sdr first make sure", "lemkin-clone-v6": "all right we got one more here and then I'll take two questions if you want it's from Alex who is asking how long does it actually take to deploy a good ai sdr well that's the million dollar question isn't it but what do they tell us at alli they told us 10 days they told us five weeks we got another one here and then some other data points from other leaders right here from talk desk folks like you know this is just so many of these AI agents are still in pilot I think that's the problem today not to be too negative but yeah a lot of them haven't really gone live yet or they've only gone partially live so you're gonna have to do your own research and see what works best for you but we can give you some general ideas right here if you want it okay talk desk folks like Alex I know you guys at alli are in the space too with AI agents as well but this is from a few weeks ago and they did an analysis of 150 plus customers that deployed their agent to automate customer service and support for them and what does it take how many hours do we need to invest to get something that actually works here okay so you can see if you go down the middle of the chart it's about four days to deploy the AI agents in talk desk but let me tell you a couple interesting things when did they start using these tools 2023 and then there was an update just last week for some of us who are running behind right here from the folks at talk desk so this is a lot earlier than we would have thought but if it's taking four days now in late", "cleaned": "But if you're going to do it, then at least be committed for 60 days. If you want to start this journey and we have plenty of examples on saster.com, but here's the thing that I can tell you after working with dozens of companies is it takes more time than anyone realizes. So first off, you've got to train your AI agent every single day. Every morning before you go into work, or as soon as you wake up, just check in. And it'll take maybe 10 minutes when you start. But by week three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, it'll only take you two minutes because you already know how to do it. You already know what worked last week. You already know how to iterate. But until you get there, it will consume a ton of your time. Now, here's my hack. This was one of our biggest surprises at sastra itself deploying these agents. We would spend hours every weekend reviewing data from our AISD agents. Like literally like two weeks ago, Amelia spent over four hours just updating our outbound campaigns. Four hours! The other day, she did another hour and a half. And so yes, they are better now. They're better trained. They learn faster. But this stuff does not train itself. There's no magic button where you click it once and voila. No matter who you pick, whether it's Artisan, Qualified, Agent Force, Momentum, Clay, or whoever else, it doesn't magically happen overnight. It gets better each month, but it requires constant iteration and training. So, my advice is don't expect a magical solution without putting in the work. Plan for daily management and continuous optimization.", "balanced": "It took me maybe three weeks with our third agent and now it's down to a day. We're getting better at training these agents, but that first week was brutal. You don't have enough data. Your prompts aren't great enough. And you'll be on a Zoom call with your vendor for hours just trying to get this thing working. So budget time, not just money. If you've never trained an agen before, plan two weeks minimum. A Real-Life Example Let me give you a real example from Qualified, which is another tool we use here at saster.com. One of their customers did a case study where they deployed the AI agent themselves in five days. Five days. That sounds amazing, right? Here's what actually happened: They started on Monday morning. Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday. Four full days of intense work. Now, if you go back into those old school sales tools like HubSpot or Salesforce, you can see all the tickets they opened, every single email they sent during that period. Because they were testing it manually. They'd send an email to see how it responds, open up a ticket to see how it reacts. It wasn't truly automated until Saturday. Then Sunday night, after four days of manual testing, they flipped the switch and said \"go live.\" By Monday morning, they had already booked meetings. The customer got three meetings out of it by Monday morning. But they spent four days doing nothing else other than deploying this agent. So even though it only really went live on Sunday evening, the deployment itself was five days because of all the pre-launch testing. This Is Not Outbound Sales (And Why That Matters) Here's something important to understand about these early days.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: How long does it take to deploy an AI SDR? The short answer is four weeks. Here’s what we’ve learned deploying and managing multiple AI BDRs across our own portfolio, from Qualified to Artisan to Delphi, as well as several other leading vendors. Week One: Discovery (Days 1-5) Start by auditing your existing sales collateral—your website copy, case studies, event content, everything. What actually closes deals versus what sounds good on a demo page? Your AI needs this data to sound like you. Map out which personas need personalization at each stage of the funnel. Not every lead gets the same message—not even close. Then define your goals for day one. Are you looking to qualify inbound leads? Qualify outbound contacts before humans touch them? Respond instantly to website visitors while they’re warm? Set clear metrics for success. We track response time, conversation quality, conversion rates—everything. Week Two: Training (Days 6-10) This is where most deployments stall. You can’t just upload your ICP document and hope for magic. Every single day for two weeks, feed your AI with examples. Positive ones first—great conversations that closed deals or showed genuine interest. Then negative examples too—people who were clearly not interested, or wanted something else entirely. The goal isn’t to train on everything. Train on the best 20% of interactions, then let the AI learn patterns from there. Use prompts specific to your business. If you sell to developers, don’t use generic “developer” language. Talk about code reviews, pull requests, CI/CD pipelines—the things that matter to your actual audience. Week Three: Testing & Iteration (Days 11-15) This is hands-down the most important week.", "repaired": "I've had this conversation with so many Founders and VPs that I just wanted to summarize it. you know, we all want to deploy AI faster than ever now. And everyone's telling you to do more. Do more marketing, do more outbound, do more inbound, get on LinkedIn, go visit your customers, get back into sales. But here's what I can tell you as someone who has deployed over two dozen agences at saster and worked with hundreds of others. there is no reason for these deployments not to be done by yourself. You should have already written all the emails before you turn them over to any AI agent. You should have watched all the videos before you turn them over to any AI video agent. There are exceptions if you're doing a massive deployment, but don't let anyone sell you something where they claim you need three weeks or four weeks or six months to train an agence. That's never true. The best way I like to think about it is the first email to a prospect from an agen is probably better than an email sent by a human being that hasn't even looked up the website yet. So write that email ahead of time. Write every single question you could ask of a potential sponsor or customer. Write everything you would say to a VP of Marketing or a CIO. Turn those into prompts, upload them to whatever tool you're using, and watch the magic happen. The same thing applies to video content. If you take your most popular blog posts, your most popular YouTube videos, your most popular sessions, transcribe them, put them into Claude and LLMs, they'll generate amazing summary clips and teasers. They'll look great. They won't look like some low-resolution crap that you saw on TikTok. They will look like high-quality summaries. These agents are getting good."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 487, "lemkin-clone-v5": 294, "lemkin-clone-v6": 313, "cleaned": 285, "balanced": 291, "r32": 273, "repaired": 315}}}}, {"id": "gold_013", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Advise founders on whether AI SDRs can substitute for an unproven outbound motion.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that founders should not expect an AI SDR to invent a working go-to-market motion. If the ICP, messaging, and follow-up logic are still broken, automating them only scales the noise. AI SDRs work best when there is already a credible human playbook to copy and improve.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that AI cannot fix a broken outbound motion by itself.", "It should explain that founders still need a working message, ICP, and qualification logic.", "It should allow that AI can accelerate a working system but rarely invent one from nothing."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI get this question a lot. In fact, it's one of my favorites.\n\nShould you use an AI-powered Sales Development Representative (SDR) before your human SDR team is up and running?\n\nMy answer might surprise you.\n\n**Don't Let Your Competitors Get Ahead of You**\n\nLet me be clear: I'm not saying \"no\" to using AI-powered tools. In fact, I think they're a game-changer for sales teams everywhere.\n\nBut here's the thing: if your human SDR team isn't yet up and running effectively, it doesn't matter how good your AI tool is. You'll just be creating more problems than you solve.\n\nThink about it this way:\n\n* Your AI-powered SDR will generate leads... but can they qualify them? Can they have a real conversation with the customer?\n* If not, you're going to end up wasting time and resources on unqualified leads.\n* And what happens when your human team does finally get ramped up? Are you prepared for the handoff from AI to humans?\n\n**The Problem of False Positives**\n\nAnother issue I see is that AI-powered SDRs often generate a lot of false positives. That is, they flag potential customers who aren't actually interested in what you're selling.\n\nThis can lead to wasted time and resources on follow-up efforts... not to mention damage to your brand's reputation when those leads don't pan out.\n\n**So What Should You Do?**\n\nHere's my advice:\n\n1. **Get Your Human SDR Team Ramped Up First**: Focus on building a world-class human team that can qualify, disqualify, and have real conversations with potential customers.\n2. **Use AI as an Augmentation Tool**: Once your human team is up to speed, use AI-powered tools to augment their efforts. This means using AI to help with lead generation, data analysis, or other tasks that don't require a human touch.\n\nBy doing it this way, you'll avoid the pitfalls I mentioned earlier... and be able to create a more efficient, effective sales machine.\n\n**The Bottom Line**\n\nDon't get me wrong: AI-powered SDRs are here to stay. And they can be incredibly powerful when used correctly.\n\nBut if your human team isn't yet up and running effectively, it's like trying to build a plane without wings. You might look cool on paper... but you're not going anywhere fast.\n\nSo focus on building that foundation first. Then – and only then – bring in the AI-powered tools to take things to the next level.\n\nThat's my two cents, folks!\n\n**Best,**\n\nJason", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nI don’t think you should use any sort of automated outbound for the most part until it’s proven to work. And that only works with folks who have done this before.\nFirst, if your team hasn’t done outbound sales (and really even inbound) before — they won’t know how to make an AI SDR work.  The data, the sequences, the follow-up are all critical.  An AI doesn’t understand these nuances and subtleties in most cases.\nSecondly, it’s hard enough getting a human outbound motion going. It takes a lot of time — months really — to get good at it if you don’t have any experience with it. A Human SDR isn’t magical. You can make the numbers work with 10-15 qualified reps closing deals (and that usually requires about $1m in sales and marketing spending). But getting there is hard.\nAnd thirdly, a lot of folks are doing this now — running AI outbound before they have a real working human motion. And it’s just not really working out as well as everyone hoped.  You can’t get enough qualified leads from an AI SDR to justify 2 people on the team full-time. They’re trying, but most of them aren’t getting there yet.\nSo unless you already have a proven outbound process — don’t do this. It’s not worth it. Don’t let folks sell you on some fancy “AI outbound” solution that sounds good in theory. Unless they’ve actually done it before and can show you the results, don’t bother trying to get an AI SDR going until you have a working human motion first.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: Is It Worth Using an AI Sales Tool Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nDear SaaStr: How Can I Get My First 50 SaaS Customers?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Should I use an AI SDR before I have a working human SDR motion?\nI think you should try it. It will likely work for 1-2 reps, but not beyond that\n.\nBut here’s the thing:\nIf your Human SDRs aren’t working … then maybe they are OK. At least in some way. Because if nothing is working …\nthen there might just be a problem with how you’re doing outbound. Not sure, though. The AI SDR will at least work for 1 or 2 reps.\nRelated Posts\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: How to Build an AI SDR from Scratch in 30 Days\nDear SaaStr: Can I Hire a Sales Rep Before We Have Product Market Fit? Dear SaaStr: What's The Best Way To Get My First Sales Reps To Work?", "cleaned": "But here’s what I’ve learned after watching hundreds of B2B companies try this over the past year: you absolutely can use AI before you have a working human outbound motion. But only if you’re willing to do the work upfront that makes it actually effective. What “Working” Actually Means Here Let me be specific because most founders get this wrong. A “working” human SDR motion isn’t just having someone send emails and book meetings. It’s something more systematic, more scalable, with clear metrics driving towards a repeatable outcome. Think of it as a three-legged stool: Leg 1: You know your ICP cold. Your human SDRs are targeting the right accounts at the right company level with the right pain points. The AI should amplify this precision, not guesswork. Leg 2: You have a message framework that converts. Not generic templates — personalized outreach based on actual research into each prospect. If your human team is writing tailored messages that get responses, then yes, AI can handle volume while maintaining quality. Leg 3: You track everything systematically. Open rates, response rates, meeting booking rates per segment, time-to-first-meeting. Without these metrics, you’re flying blind whether AI or human sends those emails. If any leg is weak, don’t start with AI. Fix the foundation first. Then scale with AI. Why This Matters (And Why Most Fail) Here’s why so many early-stage teams screw this up: They want to sound smart with AI. “Oh, we’re leveraging cutting-edge technology to reach our market.” No one cares. What matters is results.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion? The TL;DR : If you’re not ready to invest time and money into training humans, don’t even think about AI SDRs yet. Here’s why: What “Ready” Actually Means Ready doesn’t mean having a generic outbound script or a basic email sequence. It means you’ve spent real time figuring out what actually works at your company—what messaging converts, which segments respond well, how follow-up sequences perform when someone engages. That takes weeks minimum. And that’s before you start thinking about automation. Why Most Companies Fail With AI SDRs (And How To Fix It) They send templated messages without personalization or research on the recipient They expect immediate results while their team is still learning They blame the tool for poor performance instead of fixing their foundation The companies that succeed with AI SDRs have already solved the human-to-lead conversion problem first. They know their ICP cold, they understand which messaging resonates, and they can measure whether a lead is hot, warm, or ghosted. When you try to automate something broken, you get predictable failure—not because the technology failed, but because you skipped foundational work. So here’s my advice: Don’t Try Until You Know Your GTM Is Working First. For Real. If you haven’t figured out what makes your outreach convert, don’t automate it yet. Instead, focus on building a repeatable human process first. Once you know exactly what generates pipeline from leads, then—and only then —consider automating pieces of that flow with AI tools. Because once you have working processes, AI amplifies them exponentially.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: When should you start using an AI SDR before hiring human SDRs to do outbound? And what’s more important, training your team or getting those leads in front of them? The Short Answer: Yes, use an AI SDR before hiring humans. But only if you’ve already figured out what works. Don’t let AI distract from building a working human process first. Here’s why: If You Haven’t Figured Out What Works Yet — Wait First. Do that first. Go all-in on figuring it out with one great rep and then scale up once you have something proven. The mistake most founders make is trying to perfect their outbound strategy while simultaneously scaling it with AI agents. That’s like trying to build a plane while flying it at the same time. It doesn’t work well. But if you’ve already found the right messaging, the right cadence, and the right segments that convert (even just a little bit), then yes, go deploy an AI agent immediately. Why Not Earlier Than Planned? Because here’s the thing about AI SDRs—they’re not magic. They don’t replace human judgment or creativity. All they can do is send emails faster and more consistently than any human ever could. So if your message isn’t compelling enough for a human SDR to hit quota with it, an AI agent won’t save it either. In fact, AI will likely expose weak messages quickly because it hits volume fast. Weak messaging combined with high velocity = a lot of noise that damages your brand.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nNo.\nI know you want to move fast. You’ve probably been sold on the idea that AI is going to disrupt traditional sales and marketing playbooks overnight, especially with all the hype around LLMs and generative tools. And it’s true — AI can do some things at scale that humans just can’t match.\nBut here’s what most founders get wrong:\nYou need to build your human-first foundation before you layer AI on top of it. Not after.\nWhy This Order Matters More Than You Think\nHere’s what happens when you flip this around: You deploy an AI SDR without proper human processes first, and then…\nYour AI spams prospects with generic messaging they’d never respond to\nYour AI books meetings for reps who aren’t ready or trained properly\nYour AI creates more noise than actual pipeline because nobody’s segmenting correctly\nYour AI burns through budget because nobody set clear ROI metrics upfront\nYour AI becomes a shiny object that distracts from fixing broken human systems underneath\nThe biggest risk isn’t that your AI will fail (though it might). The bigger risk is that you’ll optimize for AI efficiency while optimizing nothing else. Your results will look impressive until they don’t — and by then, you’re deep into a hole trying to fix both problems simultaneously.\nWhat Actually Works Better First\nBefore any AI agent touches your prospect list, make sure these fundamentals are working:\nYou have a clear ICP definition\nthat every rep understands and agrees with\nYour segmentation is actually done\n— not “we segmented by company size” but specific criteria that align with how deals close\nYour messaging has been A/B tested\nand proven effective before scaling\nYour follow-up cadences work\nfor real (not just theoretical)\nYou track and measure response rates\nby channel and message type\nYou train new hires properly\nbefore letting them loose\nIf any part of that feels like a gap, stop immediately and fill it."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 411, "lemkin-clone-v5": 312, "lemkin-clone-v6": 135, "cleaned": 255, "balanced": 269, "r32": 254, "repaired": 338}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post:\n\n**Should You Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\n\nHey everyone, it's Jason here. \n\nI get this question a lot from founders: \"Jason, should I use an AI-powered Sales Development Rep (SDR) before I have a working human SDR motion?\"\n\nAnd my answer is...it depends.\n\n**Pros of Using an AI SDR**\n\n1. **Scalability**: With no humans involved, you can scale your outreach efforts quickly and easily.\n2. **Cost savings**: No salaries to pay for those early-stage reps!\n3. **Speed**: You can get started fast with minimal setup required.\n\nBut...\n\n**Cons of Using an AI SDR**\n\n1. **Limited conversion rates**: Sorry, folks - AI alone just doesn't convert like humans do (yet!).\n2. **No personalized follow-up**: When leads don't respond, who's going to chase them down? \n3. **Lack of real feedback**: You won't get the same level of insights from an AI SDR as you would from a human.\n\n**When to Use an AI SDR (if ever)**\n\n1. **Pilot program**: Try out an AI SDR for a small pilot program to test its effectiveness and refine your messaging.\n2. **Complementing human efforts**: Use AI SDRs to augment your existing human outreach efforts, not replace them.\n\n**My Verdict**\n\nDon't rely solely on AI SDNs until you've got a working human motion in place. It's like", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Should You Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Real One?\nI was recently talking with one of our top speakers, and he told me this:\n\"I'm not even going to try to hire another BDR. At least nothing is working so far.\"\nThat's actually the most honest answer I've heard in a while.\nAnd it’s true: we’ve tried and tried and tried again with SaaS sales hiring, for years now. And maybe you have too?\nIt just doesn’t seem to work out very well anymore. Too much change from 2021. Too many expectations raised that can never be met. And I don't know the answer.\nBut what I do know is this:\nIf your AI SDR isn’t working, it’s not going to magically become a human motion once you find someone decent.\nIt won’t work that way\nA mediocre BDR with no track record will get crushed by an AI SDR. A great one may even struggle. And if the AI doesn't really work for now — then it's just adding another layer of friction and confusion to your sales process.\nMaybe hold off on a human SDR until you have something that can actually close deals? Not ideal, but I bet most BDRs today aren’t worth much at all anymore:\nA mediocre BDR with no track record will get crushed by an AI SDR\nA great one may even struggle\nIf the AI doesn't really work for now — then it's just adding another layer of friction and confusion to your sales process\n\n— Jason ✨", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion?\nThe short answer is yes. But only for outbound, not inbound.\nOutbound should be automated first\nYou can’t do better than your human SDRs doing the same thing as they did before. At least try that for 30 days.\nBut you need to have at least one or two humans in sales and marketing helping with this process, even if it’s just a few deals a month.\nInbound is different\nIf prospects are already reaching out to you because of your brand, then AI won’t make any difference. They’re already interested and want to talk to a human about their specific problem.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion? Not yet. Here’s why: The #1 mistake founders make with AI SDRs is deploying them before they have a proven human outbound sequence that converts. We’ve seen this play out hundreds of times across our portfolio and community, and it almost always ends in disappointment or wasted budget. Why This Matters More Than You Think Let me break down what happens when you deploy AI before you have traction. Phase 1: The Dream (Weeks 1-2) – Everything Feels Magical! You set up your first AI SDR tool, upload some lists, configure basic messaging, and suddenly you’re sending thousands of personalized emails to prospects. Your team is excited because finally, you can scale outbound at machine speed. It feels like magic. But here’s what nobody tells you: Magic doesn’t last without work. And most importantly … Magic only works if you know how to build the magic. What Actually Happens When You Deploy Too Early If you don’t have a working human SDR motion, you’ll be trying to perfect the impossible. AI will amplify every weakness in your outreach strategy. A weak subject line gets sent to 10x more people. Bad timing and poor personalization get amplified instead of corrected. The result isn’t “better” outreach—it’s worse outreach on steroids. Meanwhile, your competitors who are running solid outbound sequences are seeing measurable pipeline generation from their humans. They may not be scaling quite as quickly as you’re spamming with AI, but those", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR motion? Maybe. But don’t expect magic if you aren’t going to train it yourself, and especially not without having first built out a working outbound cadence with humans. Most founders that try this find they get … mediocre results. The best is when you have something proven first, then layer on the AI agent for scale and hyper-personalization at scale. Even today we run all our outbound through Artisan itself — but it’s much better than it was 6 months ago because it now has so many more emails from Amelia (our Head of Outbound) to learn from. And we didn’t start there. We started with just her. And then added AI later.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion? Yes, but only if you're ready to invest time and resources into training it properly. Here's why: The Data is In: A Great Human SDR Outperforms An AI One (For Now) We've run dozens of campaigns now at SaaStr using AI SDRs on top of our human outbound team. And the data is clear - when we have a proven playbook from our humans working, that works well with warm leads and prospects already engaged... then the AI can work pretty darn well there too. But if your humans haven't done anything yet... nothing will happen with an AI agent either. If You Don’t Have a Proven SDR Motion Yet, Just Add Another Head First Or At Least Try It First Most importantly, an AI SDR cannot replace the value a great human brings. Your best sales rep knows how to handle objections, build relationships, and close deals. They've spent years honing their craft and understanding what works for your specific business. What An AI Agent Can Do That’s Better Than Humans Though Not Much Right Now: Send emails faster than any human could without losing quality (or sending junk). Handle volume that would overwhelm even your best SDR. Work around-the-clock without needing pay or benefits. Personalize messages at scale that are better than generic human outreach. Be available immediately to follow up on inbound interest. Keep track of every conversation thread perfectly. Never forget to follow up. Follow up instantly. Send the exact same message hundreds", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR Motion? Yes. The best use case for any new tool is one that works today, or as close to it as possible. And there’s no doubt you can get some value from an outbound agent today. If nothing else — put them on your top 1-2% hottest prospects and see if they work. Then later expand once you have human SDRs doing outbound at scale."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 223, "lemkin-clone-v5": 261, "lemkin-clone-v6": 118, "cleaned": 256, "balanced": 128, "r32": 263, "repaired": 80}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**X Post**\n\nDear SaaStr,\n\nI'm getting slammed with this question lately, so let me weigh in.\n\nShould you use an AI SDR before you have a working human SDR motion?\n\nMy answer: ABSOLUTELY NOT.\n\nLet me explain. I see too many founders trying to \"augment\" their sales teams with AI tools that promise the world but deliver nothing more than empty leads and wasted time. Newsflash: no machine can replace a top-performing human SDR who knows how to ask questions, build rapport, and close deals.\n\nHere's what happens when you rely on an AI-powered SDR too early:\n\n1. **You waste your sales team's time**: Your reps will be stuck", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: What’s your take on using AI before you have a human BDR/SDR in place? The short answer is, don’t. At least not now.\n\nHere are 5 reasons why:\n\n#1. It Won’t Work as Well As A Human\nAI can send emails and do outreach better than no one at all — but it won’t work nearly as well as a human SDR who actually knows the product and has real relationships with customers and prospects. At least, not yet.\n\n#2. You’ll Get Burned by Spam Complaints\nIf your AI starts blasting out generic emails that 99% of people immediately mark as spam? That’s going to hurt you far more than just having no SDR at all. Your domain will get blacklisted", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr:\n\nShould I use an AI SDR before I have a working human SDR motion?\n\nIt’s tempting, and it can work. But not if your competition has already started using AI to scale their outbound ahead of you.\n\nIf your competitors are all in on AI for outreach — and they probably are by now — then yes:\n\nUse the best AI SDR tools out there as soon as possible.  Just be honest with yourself about what’s really happening here: it’s not a replacement for human sales reps doing outbound. It’s just an accelerator.\n\nThe problem is that most of us think it’s going to replace our human SDRs and AE team when in fact, it will only accelerate them:\n\nMore qualified leads\nBetter outreach\nFaster follow-up\n\nBut the", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr:  Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion? It's tempting to deploy AI SDRs as soon as you have any leads at all. But if your human team isn't working, why would they be able to make magic happen with AI? Probably not. You need to start with a great human team before adding AI tools to enhance them. The best sales teams are already using AI to scale their outbound and inbound efforts. They're not waiting for perfect processes—instead, they're iterating constantly between humans and machines. Start with hiring strong reps who can close deals. Then layer on AI tools that help them work smarter. This approach gives you the foundation needed to get results from AI SDRs. If you skip this step,", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion? The short answer is yes. But you have to do it right, or it will fail. It's not too early for an AI SDR if you're ready to commit — but only after you've hired at least one great human sales rep. Here’s why: AI SDRs are already outperforming humans on basic outreach tasks. They can send thousands of personalized emails that your human reps won't touch because they’re busy with high-value accounts. And here’s what’s wild – when we ran our own outbound campaign using Artisan, we got better response rates than many human teams report. Not because the AI is perfect (it isn’t), but because it does things that", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?\n\nNo.  Not yet.\n\nI see way too many folks that are tempted to try to get going on AI SDRs before they have even one human doing outbound well, let alone any outbound at all.\n\nThis is almost always a mistake:\n\nFirst, you can’t train an AI agent if your team doesn’t know how to do it first\n\nYou need to hire someone who knows how to do B2B outbound and train them — then maybe later hand over some leads or parts of their job to an AI Agent for follow-up.\n\nSecond, AIs will only take you so far today with most use cases\n\nAn AI Agent won’t be able to write true custom cadences …"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 123, "lemkin-clone-v6": 130, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 132, "r32": 127, "repaired": 133}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before I Have a Working Human SDR motion?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason M. Lemkin, founder of SaaStr and well-known for his expertise on scaling B2B sales teams:\n\n**[The stage is set with a sleek presentation backdrop. Jason walks up to the mic, adjusts it, and begins.]**\n\n\"Alright everyone! Today we're going to tackle one of my favorite topics: should you hire an AI-powered SDR before your human team is firing on all cylinders? Now I know what you're thinking - 'Jason, shouldn't I have a perfect motion in place before bringing in the robots?' Well, let's dive into it.\n\n**[Pause for dramatic effect]**\n\nAs we all know, Sales Development Representatives (SDRs) are the lifeblood of any B2B sales organization. They're responsible for generating leads, qualifying opportunities, and keeping your pipeline full. But what happens when you have a finite budget, limited resources, and an endless list of prospects to chase? That's where AI-powered SDRs come in - or so it would seem.\n\nNow I'm not here to tell you that AI is the silver bullet for all your sales woes. It's not. What I am going to say is this: if done correctly, a well-implemented AI-powered SDR can augment and amplify your human team's efforts. But only after certain conditions are met - which we'll get into in just a minute.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nHere's the thing: before you bring on an AI-powered SDR, you need to have a solid foundation of processes in place. That means:\n\n1. **You need a clear sales strategy**: What problems do your customers face? How are you going to solve them?\n2. **A robust CRM system**: Your data is only as good as the tools that help you collect and analyze it.\n3. **Human SDRs who can teach the AI**: If you're bringing on an AI-powered SDR, it's because you want to scale your efforts. But if those human SDRs aren't up-to-speed yet, how will they train the machine?\n4. **A strong culture of data-driven decision making**: Your AI-powered SDR is only as good as the signals it receives from your team.\n\n**[Slide transition]**\n\nSo when do you bring on an AI-powered SDR? Here are some red flags to watch out for:\n\n1. **You don't have a clear sales strategy**: If you're still trying to figure out who your ideal customer is, or how they buy, then it's time to put the brakes on.\n2. **Your human SDRs aren't crushing it**: Sorry, but if your human team isn't producing leads and closing deals at an alarming rate... well, AI won't magically fix that.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nNow here are some green flags - when you know it's time to bring on an AI-powered SDR:\n\n1. **You've got a solid sales strategy in place**: You're laser-focused on the problems your customers face and how you solve them.\n2. **Your human SDRs are killing it (but can't keep up)**: Your team is so good at generating leads that they need help keeping pace with demand.\n\n**[Slide transition]**\n\nSo, should you use an AI-powered SDR before having a working human SDR motion? Absolutely not - unless... You're confident in your sales strategy, have a strong CRM system in place, and are ready to amplify the efforts of your top-performing human SDRs. If that's you, then bring on the AI!\n\n**[Conclude with enthusiasm]**\n\nThere you have it! That's my take on when (and if) to use an AI", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So, there are so many cool new tools out today. The latest is AI for your SDRs and CSMs. And while they can be super powerful, it’s often too early to use them.\nWhy? Because as you ramp up an outbound or inbound sales team, there will always be some human error in the beginning. But what about all these new tools that say “you don’t need a real SDR anymore”?\nIn theory, yes maybe you do have one if you’re on point and well funded to start with.\nBut I think it’s still too early for AI SDRs right now.\nHere’s why:\nFirst,\nthere are so many different types of prospects. And they all respond differently at different times in their buying cycles. Second,\nthere’s always going to be some human error in the beginning, and you’ll need a real SDR that can learn from it. Third,\nthe data quality is just not there yet. Not for AI models anyway.\nIf your prospecting list isn’t very good — if it has typos or bad contact info — all these tools will still send out terrible emails with no response rate to speak of. Fourth, I think the ROI in a real SDR is always going to be better than an AI tool that just sends you prospects. With an A- player on your team, they can qualify prospects and build a list for you as well.\nFifth,\nthe tools are still really expensive. So many of these new tools cost $10k-$20k per month or more. That’s almost impossible to justify if the tool doesn’t deliver in 60 days.\nYou can do it cheaper.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "It is a trap. What you’ll get from most AI SDRs, even if they are great at outbound and have tons of data — is a bunch of automated emails that don’t really work.\nAnd worse,\nthey can kill your brand\nif not done right. Because the messages will be too generic, or not personalized enough, or just plain wrong for your company and product.\nSo it’s better to wait until you have at least a few leads coming in from human SDRs before using AI agents. At least then you’ll know what does and doesn’t work — and you can train the AI on that data. But if you don’t, it will be harder to get any ROI out of them.\nAnd yes,\nyour VP Sales may push back against this. They might have a different idea about how SDRs should function in general. Or maybe they want an instant army for their outbound motion. And they’ll often want to start using AI agents as soon as possible, and not just wait until you’ve got something working.\nI get it. You can’t win them all with your VP Sales if you don’t agree on the SDR motion.", "cleaned": "If you're going to use AI, it's got to be at least as good as your best human. That's not that much, but it better be really well trained and really tested before you turn on any AI SDR tool. So if you don't have something working with humans first, then sure go for it. But if nothing else, use this time to train up two or three great AES because they will take longer than ever. They will require more training than ever. And so many folks are hiring these AEs without enough training and expecting them to perform miracles right off the bat. No one does anything by email anymore. Everyone wants to do Zooms. Everyone wants to close over zoom. It's just different today. It takes longer. You need more hand-holding from sales leaders. These tools are out there. The AI is all cool and fun. But whatever you do, please make sure you've done some work here with human sales reps before you deploy any AI. If you haven't deployed a human rep yet, even a few, maybe let her or him figure it out with customers themselves instead of relying on AI to get it wrong. Okay, thanks everybody. Thank you. [Music] >> Hey, saster friends. Welcome back to our live workshop Wednesday series where we dive into real world SAS playbooks that actually generate results. Each week we bring together top SAS experts who share actionable tactical advice on how to build scale and grow faster. Last week we had an amazing session on what's next for B2B sales and marketing with both the CRO of Revenue cat and the CEO of owner.com.", "balanced": "I would not spend any money on a third-party AI agent before you have your own qualified human team running. The only exception is if you can't find anyone at all, like literally no one wants to do this role and you're starting from zero. But even then, I'd start with a human first because there's just too much risk that the AI agent will push wrong messages or qualify prospects incorrectly or do something else damaging. You've got to get your human motion working before you add AI to it. So go hire two humans, train them well, make sure they're crushing it. And once you know what works—once you have a proven playbook—you can layer AI on top. That way, you'll know whether the improvement is coming from better humans or worse automation. The other thing to think about: if you invest heavily in AI right now without having your human game figured out, you may never figure it out. Because once you put so many resources into automation, you might convince yourself that you don't need great people anymore. That's when things break down completely. The best approach is always build the human foundation first, then augment with AI once you know exactly what you're doing.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Should I Use an AI SDR Before You Have a Working Human SDR Motion? No. Here's why. The #1 mistake founders make with AI is they expect it to solve problems that haven't been solved by humans first. If you don't have a working outbound human SDR motion yet, deploying AI before that will just create chaos and false hope. Outbound sales development—cold calling, emailing, sending LinkedIn messages—is hard enough when done right. It requires relentless execution over time, daily optimization, and constant improvement. Adding AI into this mix without having nailed the fundamentals means you'll end up with broken workflows, bad data, and disappointed expectations. Your AI won't be able to hit the right people at the right companies because your list isn't curated. It won't personalize correctly because you're using generic templates. And most importantly, it won't follow up like a human would, which is where the magic happens in outbound—it's not the initial message but the persistent effort. First, get your human SDRs (or even yourself) doing outreach successfully. Not perfectly—perfectly is impossible—but consistently hitting response rates that move the needle for your business. Then—and only then—are you ready to add AI tools on top. They'll amplify what works, let you scale faster, and handle the volume while maintaining quality standards that no human could sustainably deliver. But if you rush past step one, you'll waste months and thousands of dollars trying to fix problems you should've fixed manually in the first place. Start here instead: Get your inbound qualified leads flowing through a process that converts them reliably. Build out basic outbound processes that work.", "repaired": "you can't do that with a human being and you cannot expect your ai sdr to make up for lack of training or lack of understanding of why the product matters so if you don't have a working human sdr motion yet train it yourself before you go out there and buy any tool because no one knows this better than me i've seen thousands of vendors come through saastr's portfolio and almost all of them have failed right we're not going to fail but we've had our own failures too and even when they were great founders like look what happened to me at echo sign i mean i love jason he was my boss okay but he hired the wrong person to replace him and then i went off and did something else and now they're owned by adobe well the lesson from that is i didn't want to be an executive again that wasn't fun for me but that means i didn't know how to hire someone to run sales after myself so that meant we lost a year um just that simple thing where i didn't know how to hire someone to take over from me as ceo so so much more will happen when you go from founder-led to non-founder led and i think the same thing here if you haven't gotten your human outbound engine humming yet get that done first and then bring on the tools and magic happens if you start with magic tools and mediocre humans nothing good comes out of that conversation"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 570, "lemkin-clone-v5": 274, "lemkin-clone-v6": 198, "cleaned": 279, "balanced": 209, "r32": 271, "repaired": 262}}}}, {"id": "gold_014", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain why strong growth can still fail to convert into financing and what founders should do.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that 90 percent growth at $8 million ARR is attractive, but investors may still hesitate if retention, efficiency, product differentiation, or the category narrative are weak. The founder lesson is to inspect what part of the story is not landing and fix that, rather than assuming the answer is just to pitch harder.", "facts": ["The answer should explain that headline growth alone is not enough for a term sheet.", "It should mention gaps such as market narrative, efficiency, product strength, or investor fit.", "It should recommend focusing on the underlying reasons instead of assuming the market is simply irrational."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI've been in your shoes before. The thrill of hitting a big milestone - we just passed $8M ARR! And the agony that comes with it: **no investor wants to write us a term sheet.** We're growing 90% YoY and crushing our growth targets. But despite all this success, VCs are suddenly shy.\n\nLet me tell you how I see it. When you hit $1-2M in ARR, most investors think you've got potential. You're still early enough that they can make a claim on equity at a relatively reasonable price (typically 10-15%). But now, at $8M ARR? Suddenly, the math changes.\n\nHere's what I believe is happening:\n\n* **First, VCs see us as too big to be ignored**: We've reached a size where we're no longer a tiny startup that could be written off. Now, investors need to consider our growth trajectory and whether they can still own 10-20% of the company.\n* **Second, VCs are worried about valuation compression**: As you grow faster and hit larger milestones, your revenue multiple will increase. This means investors have to pay more for a smaller percentage of ownership (typically 5-7%). That's not exactly what they're looking for.\n\nSo how do we overcome this hurdle? Here's my take:\n\n1. **Don't be afraid to highlight the growth**: Show VCs that you've got serious momentum and are still accelerating. This can make up for some of the valuation concerns.\n2. **Demonstrate a clear path to $100M ARR**: Investors want to know they're investing in something with long-term potential, not just a flash-in-the-pan success story.\n3. **Get strategic about who you pitch**: Focus on VCs that have experience investing in high-growth companies and are comfortable with higher valuations.\n\nI've been around the block enough times to see what works and what doesn't. Here's my advice: stay focused, keep growing, and don't take it personally when investors say no (or worse, ignore you). We're at a critical moment here - we need to prove our worth as a high-growth company.\n\nHow have other SaaStr founders navigated this challenge? Share your experiences in the comments below!\n\nCheers,\n[Your Name]", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: We’re at $8m ARR growing 90% but can’t get a Vc to give us a term sheet. How long should we wait? If you are past that 1-2 year “pre-mature” stage, it’s not quite as hard.\nYou’ve got some decent metrics:\n$800k+ MRR\nGrowing at 75%\nNo big issues on the balance sheet (no debt or red flags)\nA great product with a real market\nBut … you’re still missing something that gets VCs excited. That “hockey stick” moment, where they can imagine it’s just going to continue to grow like crazy.\nIf nothing else is working out for now — maybe try again in the next 30 days or so? Because growth isn’t everything. But there are probably better reasons a VC wouldn’t want to invest than you growing at 90%+ and approaching $10m ARR in your first year.\nThe “hockey stick” moment, where they can imagine it’s just going to continue to grow like crazy.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: What's The Right Time To Get VCs Involved?\nDear SaaStr: Is It Too Late for a Seed VC Round At $1.5m ARR with 50% Growth? Dear SaaStr: How Do I Know If My Startup is Worth $100M+?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr,\nWe’re at $8M ARR growing 95%, but can’t get any VCs to give us term sheets. I have two questions:\n1) Would you invest in this company? (The answer is yes, I would)\n2) Should we go for it and just ask the question?\nBest regards\n– Alex\nI think it’s worth a shot — as long as they don’t charge you more than 3 points to find you money.\nYou are at $8m ARR with 90% growth.  That is, in fact, pretty darn good! The only challenge I see here is that the founders aren’t out there raising the capital themselves:\nA little bit of a problem:\nBut it’s not your fault — and you could easily get on Zooms if you wanted to.\nI think if you went out and asked for term sheets yourself with your team, at $8m ARR growing 90%+ … I’m pretty sure 10-15 top VCs would be very interested in investing.  You’ve already raised $3m (great job!) but that doesn’t necessarily mean they can’t invest another say $2-$4M.\nI think you should go out there and ask the question yourself — as long as it’s not a huge distraction from running your business at scale.  If nothing else, if they won’t give you term sheets … then at least you’ll know why.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Do I Get Investors to Take Me Seriously?\nDear SaaStr: When Should You Stop Fundraising? Dear SaaStr: What Are Your Top 10 Tips For Raising a Venture Capital Round?", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: We’re at $8M ARR, growing 90%, but can’t get VCs to give us term sheets. What are we doing wrong? You have a strong business with solid fundamentals – growth rate and revenue milestones that most investors would consider attractive for this stage. However, getting venture capital is more than just having good numbers; it’s also about timing, market perception, and how you position yourself relative to other opportunities. Here’s my honest assessment based on what I’ve seen work well for B2B companies at your scale. First, let’s break down where you stand against typical benchmarks for seed-stage fundraising. You’ve hit some great metrics: Strong Growth : $8M ARR growing 90%. That’s an exceptional growth rate for this phase. Most early-stage companies don’t see even half of 60-70% at your valuation level. Market Positioning : With 40 customers paying annually (and presumably not all being tiny), you’re showing real enterprise traction rather than just SMB churn. This gives you credibility beyond pure viral or bottom-of-funnel play. Product-Market Fit Signal : Your $10K ACV suggests you’ve found something sticky enough to command premium pricing without requiring massive sales teams. That’s a sign of product-market fit worth billions. The Challenge isn’t Just About Metrics It’s about positioning, momentum, and how your company fits into broader trends. Let me walk through what could be holding you back:", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: How do you convince VCs to invest when they say there are too many competitors, and your growth rate is low for their liking? It’s tough. I’ve been here myself at Adobe Sign / EchoSign, where we had a strong product but just weren’t growing quickly enough (or so it seemed) due to competition from Google Docs. In fact, my co-founder was told by Sequoia Capital that he would “never make any money” because of all this competition 🙂 There were certainly times in the early days when I doubted him 😉 Competition does matter. It can be hard to win deals if there are other great vendors already in them. And yet … The best founders never quit. Never give up. If you have happy customers — then find a way to keep getting more. The world changes every single day, sometimes even multiple times a day. Sometimes, overnight. You may get better market share later. More leads. A better team. A better brand. So don’t quit. Just don’t. Even if some folks tell you to. Some VCs will not want to bet on you unless you grow faster. That doesn’t mean others won’t. They’ll see the potential. Be honest with yourself about why they aren’t writing checks now. Is it really competition? Or something else? Something like you need another year or two to scale up further? Are they worried you need more capital than you think? Do you have an amazing CTO but no one on the sales team knows how to sell well? Whatever the reason, take action. Take action right now. What can you change today? This week? Next month? Maybe it isn’t you that needs to learn more. Maybe it’s time to bring someone new onto your management team who has done what you haven’t done before. Bring her/him in as COO or VP Sales.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: What is the minimum ARR for venture capital? It’s different, and can vary. A few years back, it was around $1-$2M, then maybe even higher. Today, VCs are often looking to invest at least $5m+ into each deal, if not more (and they need a lot of dilution room). So what happens when you have strong growth but haven’t hit that number yet? When I started investing as a seed investor, the first rule I learned was this: The best companies almost always grow faster than their valuations suggest. This isn’t just true with unicorns — where “unicorn” math works out so well (a unicorn grows from $10m to $100m ARR in roughly 7-9 months!). It’s also true of solid startups growing > 70%, or even 80%. Because the valuation model compounds those rates forward over time. Let me show you an example of how quickly this compounds: Let’s say your startup has $6 million in revenue right now, growing 75% annually, and you project maintaining that rate going forward. If you get funded today, you’ll likely be valued on last month’s numbers (~$5M), which means investors might value you at ~$30M post-money (based on standard early-stage metrics). That sounds modest. Now fast-forward five years. Your business has grown 35x from its current base of $6M → $210M ARR. Your revenue multiple jumps significantly because you’ve proven product-market fit, expanded your TAM, and built a repeatable sales motion. In this scenario, that same company could easily command $200M+ in a Series B round.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr:  We are growing at 90%, but only have $2.6M cash and our burn is around $500K per month, which makes it hard for VCs to see us as attractive because they feel we don’t have enough runway.\nThat’s an honest point. And one that many founders make. I’ve even made this argument myself with some investors — when my own start-up had less than $1m in the bank, and was burning more than $200k a month.\nBut … the thing is you can often get another bridge round or two if growth is strong. Even if the next round doesn’t come soon enough, there’s still time to raise again down the road if your metrics continue to grow quickly. Especially these days, where so much capital is available to solid startups.\nSo how do you solve this problem? Well first off, just share all the above data with potential investors upfront.\nAnd then ask them what their advice would be on raising money now vs later (i.e., after you have more runway).\nIn any event, here’s what I’d do:\nFirst, slow down your burn rate. This will give you more options. The fact that you’re burning $500k a month is going to scare away most investors unless you are growing faster than 100%. So take action now:\n– Make sure everyone understands the importance of being frugal until you raise the next round. You should be able to cut expenses by 20%-30%.\n– If you haven’t already, move into a smaller office space to reduce real estate costs. There may be other areas where you can reduce expenses without impacting too much customer happiness or product quality."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 353, "lemkin-clone-v5": 200, "lemkin-clone-v6": 255, "cleaned": 226, "balanced": 313, "r32": 255, "repaired": 279}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the post:\n\n**\"The SaaStr Reality Check**\n\nHey everyone,\n\nJust had to share my latest experience with all of you. I've been in this space long enough (and written about it often enough!) that I thought I knew what was coming.\n\nWe just hit $8m ARR at our company, growing 90% YoY. That's a big number - but not huge by today's standards. We're proud of it, and we should be. But here's the thing: **we can't get anyone to give us a term sheet**.\n\nI know what you're thinking... \"Jason, that's crazy!\" And I agree with you. I've been trying to raise for months now, meeting with VC firms all over the Valley. Some of them are nice enough to say no outright (thanks for your honesty!). Others just don't bother responding at all (thanks for nothing!).\n\nNow, I'm not naive. I know it's tough out there. But as a founder myself, and having talked to hundreds more... **this is ridiculous**.\n\nWe're a successful company with real traction. We have happy customers, solid cash flow - everything you'd want in an investment target. And yet no one wants to take the risk?\n\nI'm starting to think that maybe I've just been too optimistic all these years. Maybe the whole \"VCs are always looking for their next big win\" thing is a myth.\n\nAm I crazy? Am I missing something? Or am I just another founder in this space who's about to get crushed by reality?", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear Saastr: I’m at $8m growing 90%. No one wants to invest. Why? You can’t get funding in today’s environment if you don’t have:\n$1M+ run-rate (not ARR) from the last round\n70%+ gross margins on high-value ACV deals\n30%-40% YoY growth with strong unit economics\nA proven, repeatable sales motion that works at scale\nIf you’re growing fast but haven’t achieved these benchmarks yet … it’s just too early. There are exceptions in venture capital and VC is a long game. But there aren’t many of them. And yes, some seed rounds can be done with much lower run rates than above — but then the founders have to give up a lot more equity. It still doesn’t solve this problem.\n\n#1: The investor has already lost money on similar deals\nYou’re a “bad” investment if your investors would make 2x-3x their money back just by putting you into a portfolio with other investments that are already performing well\n\n#2: You don’t have enough revenue yet to hit scale metrics\nMost venture capital is designed for the $100M+ ARR category, not seed or early-stage. So either find an investor who can fund it until then … or become one yourself.\n\n#3: Your growth isn’t strong enough — yet\nEven if you’re growing 90% now, VCs want to see at least a 70%-80% run rate for the last round. If that’s not there, they’ll wait", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: I’m not sure we have enough to sell our startup for. It’s at $2M in revenue and growing quickly. The problem is, it’s going up so fast that every time you get to another milestone like $1m or 3m ARR–the numbers are already out of date. So far, no investor wants to give us the term sheet because they say we’re not at a high enough valuation. What should I do? Just keep growing? It’s hard for me to know what to think about this since it seems like there are pros and cons on either side.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet So What’s The Issue? There are a few reasons you might not be getting term sheets. Here are my top 5 thoughts based on what I’ve seen over the years: #1. You may just have too many founders and/or too much dilution already. If you have multiple founders, that can spook VCs who worry about conflicting goals down the road. And if your last round was at a relatively low price — say less than $10m pre — it makes sense to try again now. But keep in mind most VCs will want to see a significant amount of capital go into the company. And they’ll also likely assume any new investors need to own material amounts as well. So often this means you end up with more dilution than expected. Be thoughtful here. More here. #2. It could be a bad pitch or cold email. This is super common, unfortunately. A mediocre investment banker pitched me once when I had $50m+ run rate growing >100%. They wanted 7% for themselves, and 0% for their client. That’s why you hire an investment banker 😉 #3. Maybe Your Valuation Expectations Are Too High. You Need to Have a Strong Story For That. Yes, multiples today are higher than ever before. But there still has to be a strong reason for a valuation to hit $200m, $300m+, etc. You have to do better than the best", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet If you have strong growth, but not super high burn rates, you can get funded. It’s just harder than if your growth is fueled by massive amounts of capital and very efficient scaling. Founders tend to want it both ways … high growth AND low burn 🙂 And that makes sense — especially these days when so many startups are burning far more than they used to. But VCs often sort of expect one or the other. Not always, but generally. The math on investing at say $6-$10k MRR isn't as exciting as growing at epic rates. But... if you're doing well, there will be someone out there interested. Even today. Just maybe not everyone. And maybe even fewer folks with lower valuations.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet If you have happy customers, strong growth and even just some early traction … VCs will want to meet with you. It’s what they do. The problem is if your burn rate is too high. Too many VCs are spooked these days by “high” burn rates. And it’s not just for consumer startups anymore. Many VCs that would never care at all before 2020-21 about “burn rate” now worry so much about it they don’t even want to invest unless growth exceeds revenue retention. So first, let’s talk about why VCs really won’t fund you if you have too high a burn rate. And second, how to get funded anyway. Why Do VCs Care About Burn Rate? A few reasons — but not as many as most founders think: 🤷‍♀️ They aren’t sure you’ll be able to raise another round. This one is a bit less important than it used to be. In fact, today many VCs believe it is their job to make sure you can raise the next round or two. Even more on that here: 💰 They don’t want to overfund you. Most seed investors have an “overfunding” limit. Typically around -20%-30% of the initial check size. If they feel like you need more money than this, say $2m instead of $1.5m, they often pass. It’s hard enough getting"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 246, "lemkin-clone-v5": 235, "lemkin-clone-v6": 99, "cleaned": 19, "balanced": 253, "r32": 142, "repaired": 241}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X Post**\n\n\"Hey fellow founders!\n\nWe've got a problem here. I'm seeing this all too often and it's gotta be said.\n\nOur company just hit $8M in ARR, growing at 90%. We're crushing it on the top line. But guess what? NO VC wants to give us a term sheet.\n\nI know what you're thinking... \"Jason, that's crazy! You must not have a good story.\" But trust me, I've done my research and we've got a great go-to-market strategy, strong team, and clear vision for growth. We're just not getting the traction with VCs.\n\nThis is what's killing me: these same VCs are willing to invest in later-stage companies that have already proven their market fit... but they won", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us A Term Sheet.  It’s Not You, It’s Them. I was at an event with some of the best VCs and they all said the same thing:\n\nThe only way to get funded today is if you can show top decile growth\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚫️💛🌸 (@jasonlk)\nOctober 2, 2025\nWe have an amazing team. We are at $8m ARR growing 90% with high NRR and a strong net dollar retention rate of 118%. But we can’t get VCs to give us a term sheet.\n\nI just met with some top", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet\nI think if you are at least growing 100%, and really have a great team, that maybe will be enough. If it’s truly the best in your space … then maybe VCs just don’t see it.\nBut I do know this:\nIf you grow 90% after $8m ARR, then get to even better numbers later (say, $10m or so),\nthen at least one VC\nwill give you a term sheet. I promise.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 119, "lemkin-clone-v5": 108, "lemkin-clone-v6": 91, "cleaned": 19, "balanced": 19, "r32": 19, "repaired": 19}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: We’re At $8m ARR Growing 90% But Can’t Get a VC To Give Us a Term Sheet", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**[Clears throat]**\n\nAlright everyone, let's dive into this one. So you've got an $8 million ARR company growing at 90%. That's incredible growth. I mean, that's like the holy grail of SaaS companies right there.\n\nBut here's the thing: you're struggling to get a VC to give you a term sheet. Now, I know what you're thinking: \"Jason, this is ridiculous! We've got amazing traction and we should be getting lined up with top VCs left and right.\"\n\nWell, let me tell you something. It doesn't matter how good your numbers are if the market conditions aren't in your favor. And right now, the VC landscape has changed. There's more competition than ever for funding, and it's harder to get a term sheet.\n\nBut here's what I want you to focus on: is it just that the VCs don't think you're special? Or are there some underlying issues with your company or pitch that need to be addressed?\n\n**[Pauses]**\n\nI've seen this play out before, and let me tell you, most of the time it's not because the company isn't good enough. It's usually something more nuanced.\n\nSo here's what I want to suggest: take a step back and really examine your pitch deck. Are you telling a compelling story about your market opportunity? Are you showing clear traction in key metrics like customer acquisition cost, retention rates, and growth rate?\n\nBecause if you're not telling that story effectively, it doesn't matter how good your numbers are - the VCs aren't going to get excited.\n\nAnd here's another thing: have you got a strong narrative around why you need funding at this stage? Are you crystal clear on what specific use of funds will drive growth and make your company more attractive?\n\nBecause let me tell you, most VCs don't care about how much money you're burning or whether or not you've got enough runway. What they care about is whether or not you can grow faster with their help.\n\n**[Leans in]**\n\nAnd finally: are there some underlying issues that need to be addressed? Are your burn rates too high? Is your go-to-market strategy broken?\n\nBecause if the answer is yes, then it doesn't matter how good your pitch deck is - you're still going to struggle to get a term sheet.\n\nSo here's what I want you to do. Take a close look at these areas and make sure everything is in order before you go back out to raise more capital.\n\nAnd if not, well... that's okay too. Because sometimes it takes multiple attempts to get the right fit with a VC firm or even an angel investor who believes in your vision.\n\nSo don't give up hope! Keep pushing forward, and remember: every successful SaaS company has had to overcome obstacles like this along the way.\n\nThanks for tuning into another edition of Dear SaaStr.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr:\nWe’re at $8m ARR growing 90% but can’t get a VC to give us a term sheet. What should we do? Well, the reality is you probably have some other issues here than just your ARR and growth.\nFirst, how are you doing on efficiency?\nSecond, are you seeing more customer attrition over time as you scale? If so, that may be an issue\nThird, what’s your NPS score like? It doesn’t matter if a few big customers love you if most of them don’t. And it will kill your growth and burn rate in the long run.\nFourth, have you hit any sort of market saturation yet?\nIf not — keep going! If so … maybe take a pause for now.\nBut more importantly …\nHow many term sheets can you get? Maybe 3-4 are enough to make an informed decision. So, how’s it looking?\nIt probably doesn’t look that great if you’re only getting 1 or 2 offers at $8m ARR growing 90%. And if so … I’d say just go for the one.\nVCs do have a lot of money\nThe best funds are always raising new ones. The top VCs and firms aren’t going anywhere anytime soon.\nAnd even more importantly — they all know who each other is, what deals to pass on, etc.\nSo, if you can get 1 or 2 term sheets at $8m ARR growing 90% … just take it! Go for the best one. It’s probably better than nothing. You might not be able to raise another round of venture capital anytime soon, but that’s fine. Just go all in on that last investment.\nIt will work out fine\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: What Should We Do With Our $300k ARR?\nSaaStr AI Brings 10,000+ Together Next Week at AI Annual 2026!\nWe Are Back!!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: We're At $8M ARR Growing 90% But Can't Get A VC to Give Us a Term Sheet\nI'm not sure this is that unusual.  Many B2B start-ups in the enterprise and mid-market struggle to get funded at scale by VCs.  It's just too hard, even if you are growing 100%.  If you're doing it yourself without any outside capital, then great.  But a VC can't always do this.\nYou need an outsized exit for them (or they don’t invest).\nA unicorn.  Or at least something approaching it.\nAnd that's just hard to predict today.  Even when growth is massive and accelerating.\nSo there are no easy answers here, other than:\nGet better at the pitch\nGet more customers\nGet a few more logos from bigger names (even if they aren’t paying as much)\nJust keep going.  It’s always harder later.  But it’s always easier to get funded … next round.  Just be careful not to run out of runway.\nA related post here:\nDear SaaStr: We Raised $1m At a $5m Valuation. Is This Good? No, and Yes. | SaaStr\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\n50+ Top VCs Coming to 2020 SaaStr Annual!!\nThe Ultimate Guide for Going Long in Venture Capital with Jason Lemkin", "cleaned": "Dear Saster: We're at $8M ARR growing 100%. But we can't get a VC to give us a term sheet. What's wrong? I've been through this, and it happens again and again. You know what you need for venture capital today. Venture capital is back, but only if you meet specific criteria. And those are high growth with strong unit economics or strong unit economics alone. Let me break that down because everyone wants to invest in fast-growing startups now. It feels like there are hundreds of unicorns a week. So why aren't VCs interested in yours? First, let's be honest. The bar has gone up significantly over the last two years. If you look on TechCrunch or other places where they write up unicorn rounds every single day, pretty much all of them have triple digit percent growth. Triple digits. Not not double digits, not 20%, not even 50%. Triple digits. Now some of these are crazy. Some of these are AI fueled by tons of money from OpenAI, Anthropic, et cetera. But many of them are just hyper competitive spaces where you got to grow really, really quickly to win. And so that's created an environment where folks want to see triple digit growth. Even sometimes if they don't quite understand how you make money. Second point, though, which is important to realize as a founder. There's actually a lot more interest than people think in startups that do well but aren't growing extremely quickly.", "balanced": "you've got to get it done and so what happens is you don't have time to do all these fancy things like get your burn rate down to zero or whatever it might be um you can't do that if you're not going long enough you can only go as far as you can raise right and even at eight million growing ninety percent you may need another round uh you will almost certainly need one within two years maybe even less than that depending on how much dilution you take but when we were growing faster back in the day folks just assumed they had three four five six seven eight years to invest right there's no way any investor could expect to see money out for ten years right they knew they didn't know exactly where their money would come from and how much each round would be worth but they thought they'd make money they did assume they had several years right and so I think the mistake founders are making today is they're being too conservative with capital because they want to look good for investors they want to look good for investors they want to show up with no burn and 18 months runway and sometimes that makes sense um especially in tougher times but it doesn't always work out because growth is key in sas and some of us really have something special some of us really can go 100 plus percent year after year forever and ever and if you have that keep investing keep investing and maybe it means you won't get funded until later but if you're lucky you'll get an offer to buy you out for billions down the road right so don't run out of gas don't stop running the engine don't slow it down don't cut marketing spend don't hide the burn don't hide anything", "r32": "I’m not saying you can’t get that term sheet but I am saying it’s super hard to raise at $10 million pre with high growth and almost impossible if your growth slows. So my advice is don’t burn too much, do what you have to do to win this year. If you have enough cash to last until next year, take your time on fundraising. You will probably be able to raise then. And maybe even more importantly, make sure each quarter gets better than the last one. Each month gets better than the last one. That’s the most important thing for VCs. And hopefully, that will lead you to a better outcome for venture capital in Q1 of next year or later.", "repaired": "the ones that are going to want to do this but they're all going to be looking for reasons not to write that term sheet so you've got to make it easy on them and just one more thing because I know some folks will say well I'll get another round when we need it um and maybe you will but if you don't have an optionality from VCS there's no pressure on your burn rate or your growth rate right what you think is great may not seem as great to investors who could give you money okay last question two other ideas to help yourself out number four which again you sort of touched on this but really think about like why would someone invest at eight million growing ninety percent yeah like you've built something incredible but what else can you tell me how big can you grow how much Revenue can you add where will you go next if you can answer those questions confidently then you will attract these investors if you can't answer those questions confidently then even if you're doing amazingly well you won't get funded because it's too risky um and it doesn't matter if you're profitable today it does matter but let's assume you're cash flow positive you're free Cash Flow Positive that's good that's great and you're probably burning less than half of each dollar you take in that's pretty darn efficient but look it's almost irrelevant until you get close to IPO like whatever you raise better fund your way to a hundred million in Revenue with some capital otherwise it's gonna drag down your valuation now if you can prove why you can do that then you might actually get funded right you know what was your first investment Jason well uh back in the day when I was still working my dad gave me 10K and I had to pay him back um so he invested 10K back in the day in my company and I said look listen Dad I'm going to double this money and he says yeah"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 478, "lemkin-clone-v5": 306, "lemkin-clone-v6": 209, "cleaned": 247, "balanced": 317, "r32": 123, "repaired": 353}}}}, {"id": "gold_015", "topic": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain why buyer trust in AI is changing and what that means for B2B companies.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that many buyers now care less about whether a workflow is powered by AI and more about whether it works, saves time, or drives revenue. The deeper lesson is that trust is moving from abstract skepticism to practical proof, but companies still need reliability, transparency, and strong deployment design to hold that trust.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that buyer skepticism toward AI is falling in practice.", "It should explain that trust is increasingly earned through usefulness and results rather than labels.", "It should clarify that trust still depends on guardrails, reliability, and deployment context."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The \"Buyers Won't Trust an AI\" Objection Is Already Collapsing**\n\nI've been talking to lots and lots of SaaS founders lately. And one thing I keep hearing is that investors are telling them, \"Look, buyers won't trust an AI.\"\n\nThat's a classic objection from the old-school sales world: if we build something automated, customers will never buy it because they don't like interacting with machines.\n\nBut here's the thing: this objection is already collapsing. Everywhere I look, I see evidence that buyers are embracing AI more and more.\n\nTake Zoom, for example. When I first used Zoom back in 2013 (yes, really), it was a clunky, awkward experience. But over time, they've made it incredibly easy to use – so much so that people barely even think about the fact that they're interacting with an AI-powered system when they hop on a call.\n\nOr take Stripe, another company I know and love. Their payment flow is ridiculously simple because of their AI-driven underpinnings. And you know what? Customers don't care that it's automated – they just want to get paid quickly and easily.\n\nSo why are some investors still spouting this \"Buyers Won't Trust an AI\" nonsense?\n\nWell, I think there are a few reasons:\n\n1. **Misguided nostalgia**: Some people in the industry just can't shake off their memories of clunky old software from 10-20 years ago.\n2. **Fear of change**: As with any innovation, some folks will naturally be resistant to AI-powered sales and customer service tools because they're not used to them yet.\n3. **Lack of understanding about how AI actually works**.\n\nThe reality is that when done well, AI can make interactions faster, easier, and more enjoyable for customers – exactly the opposite of what investors are worrying about!\n\nSo if you're a SaaS founder or entrepreneur who's thinking of building an AI-powered sales platform or customer service tool, don't let this objection hold you back.\n\nHere's what I would say to you: **AI is not just okay with buyers; it's actually becoming the new normal**. And as more and more companies adopt these technologies, we'll only see buyer trust increase further.\n\nGo ahead, build that AI-powered sales platform or customer service tool. You might be surprised by how enthusiastically customers respond!\n\n(Note from Jason: If you're interested in learning more about building successful SaaS businesses with AI at their core, I'd love to have a conversation. Let's talk!)", "lemkin-clone-v5": "AI Agents Will Replace Most Sales Reps. They Are Just Better, More Consistent, and More Efficient.\nBut I don't think that will be enough for most buyers in B2B to trust the deal or even take it seriously.\nThat's already changing though -- at least a bit.\n\nI was talking with an AI agent this week as part of SaaStr Annual 2025. The agent said, “If you want me to just close this, I can do that for $10k.” That’s probably not enough to make the deal happen.\nBut then it started going down on price. And before we knew it, it was at a number where we’d actually buy it if needed. It wasn’t quite as good of an AI Agent as some others I’ve worked with, but still pretty darn good.\n\nWhat did change here? A few things:\nFirst — the agent never got angry or defensive when its price was knocked down (and then back up again). Unlike many human reps.\nSecond — it didn’t take a break and say “I’ll have to follow-up tomorrow.” Like so many reps do. It stayed on topic, always ready to talk about the deal.\nThird — we had no qualms at all that this AI Agent would help us close this one $10k+ opportunity with its own hand. In fact, it was even more confident than a human rep I’ve seen in the past month.\n\nThe bottom line? The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing.\nI don’t think we’re at 100% yet — but we are getting there fast. Not only will you trust them, and they won’t get angry or defensive when their price is knocked down (and then back up again). They also can be so much more productive than a human rep.\n\nIt’s just that the technology is already here.\nAnd it gets better every week.\nRelated Posts\nBuyers Won't Trust an AI Agent Yet. That's OK, Because You Can Still Make Money Off Them Today. A Look Back at 2014 with Jason Lemkin and SaaStr!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So you know what’s been a big, and I get it … objection to many newer SaaS apps these days? That buyers won’t trust them. They may not be as established or trusted brands like Salesforce, Hubspot, ZoomInfo, etc.\nI understand the concern here. But let me tell you — in 2025 this “buyers won’t trust an AI” objection is already collapsing on its own.\nFor example:\nWe’re seeing a new wave of startups building on top of GPT-4 that are going from $0 to $1m ARR in mere weeks, and then tens and even hundreds of millions. They aren’t established brands or traditional SaaS companies. But their users trust them 100%. In fact, they often love them more than the old way.\nFor example:\nWe’ve all seen folks build pretty cool AI agents on top of Claude and GPT-4 in weeks to help with sales outreach — from simple things like emails, to sophisticated workflows for lead routing, qualification, follow-up etc. These aren’t traditional SaaS companies we’re talking about here, but they are trusted by the 1000s that use them.\nWe’ve seen AI agents from folks like Replit and others build in weeks get used at scale — even in production environments that can support thousands of people with no downtime.\nI see this every week. Buyers trust new leaders when they deliver value fast. When you ship something cool, interesting, useful … buyers will find it. And then they’ll give you a shot.\nSo the objection here is already collapsing on its own. It’s just an excuse for not shipping or trying faster. The best sales folks understand this intuitively — if what you have really helps buyers in some way, no matter how new you are — they can see that and trust it. And then tell all their colleagues to do the same.\nThe bottom line: Buyers won’t trust AI agents? That’s not a reason to wait. It’s your job to make them trusted as fast as possible.", "cleaned": "We’re not there yet. But we are getting closer fast.” The companies winning with AI sales tools aren’t those that avoid making their prospects feel like they’re talking to humans. They’re the ones using AI as an amplifier — for product knowledge, research efficiency, and follow-up consistency. That’s different from eliminating human interaction entirely. It’s just being more productive at all stages where humans can help most. Your competitive advantage isn’t going to be whether your prospect feels like they’ve talked to a person or a machine. It’s going to be who gets them what they need faster, smarter, and with better results. And increasingly, that will include AIs doing much of the heavy lifting right before our eyes.", "balanced": "If you’re building B2B software and not thinking deeply about how to handle this transition, you’re already behind. The question isn’t whether buyers will trust AI sales tools — it’s which vendors are going to capture that trust first.", "r32": "The companies that win with this transition will be those where founders and executives lead by example, treating AI systems as they would treat any other critical business capability — with investment, oversight, and the expectation of excellence. We’re not there yet on trust for all our apps. But we are getting close to trusting AIs just like we do humans … when it’s proven. And many buyers already have no choice but to use a lot more AIs than ever before. That alone is changing their expectations. It’s time to stop making excuses around trust. Start building the guardrails. And get ready to compete against vendors who’ve been preparing for this moment for years. Because if you don’t? Your customers may well start looking elsewhere. The question isn’t whether buyer trust can evolve. It already has started evolving. It’s whether your organization will be leading or lagging that evolution.", "repaired": "When your competitor’s sales team needs three weeks to schedule a demo, you’re already winning on experience. When your prospect can get a comprehensive evaluation in minutes instead of weeks, that’s not just convenience — it’s competitive advantage. The vendors who figure this out first will have massive moats. The ones still waiting for better technology won’t be around long enough to miss their window. The Bottom Line\nAI isn’t going away from B2B sales anytime soon. And buyers are already learning how to trust well-trained agents when they work properly. Your job is simple:\nShip something now\nthat makes buying easier and faster.\nTest what works\nwith real customers while you build more.\nScale once you find product-market fit.\nThe companies that move fastest here will dominate the next decade of enterprise software. The ones stuck in 2019 thinking? They’ll be wondering why their competitors closed deals before breakfast every day. Which category do you want to be in? Let me know if you’d like to talk through specific implementation strategies or hear more examples from our own deployment experiences. We’ve done enough with SaaStr AI that we can give you very concrete advice based on actual data."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 402, "lemkin-clone-v5": 342, "lemkin-clone-v6": 332, "cleaned": 119, "balanced": 39, "r32": 150, "repaired": 199}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post:\n\n**The \"Buyers Won't Trust an AI\" Objection Is Already Collapsing**\n\nI've been talking to a lot of my fellow SaaS founders and CEOs lately, and I keep hearing the same concern: \"Will buyers trust our AI-powered product?\"\n\nAnd honestly? This is a total non-issue.\n\nWe've seen it time and again in successful SaaS companies - from Segment's customer data platform to Mixpanel's analytics tool. And now we're seeing it happen with new AI-first startups like Lobe (acquired by Google) and Databricks.\n\nBuyers are already trusting AI-powered products at an incredible rate. In fact, a recent survey found that 71% of buyers say they trust AI-driven recommendations - more than human ones!\n\nSo what's behind this shift? Here are three key reasons:\n\n **AI is getting ridiculously good**. Advances in machine learning and natural language processing have made AI models almost indistinguishable from humans.\n\n **Buyers are increasingly data-driven**. With the rise of analytics and automation, buyers want to rely on evidence-based decision making - not gut feelings or opinions.\n\nAnd most importantly: **AI can actually help build trust**. By providing transparent explanations for its decisions and recommendations, AI-powered products demonstrate a level of accountability that's hard for human-only solutions to match.\n\nSo if you're still worrying about this objection, I want to encourage you to take it off your list. Your buyers are already ahead of the curve - they know what matters most is results, not who or what produced them.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So many folks still think they have an excuse to avoid using AIs for sales and marketing.\n\nThe buyer won't trust it. It's too early. No one wants to talk to a bot. All the same objections we've heard since the first day AI started being deployed in sales.\n\nI'm going to be honest with you, I still hear these excuses regularly from marketers at big brands, even today!\n\nBut I keep telling them something that is now becoming an undeniable fact:  The buyers are already trusting AIs more than they trust humans.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So far, we’ve been talking about how to get started with Lovable. We’re going on and on here at SaaStr Annual. But I think the real power will come when you can truly go multi-product. When Lovable is not just your agent for support and sales, but also your agent as well for marketing, events, customer success, etc. The future is that all of these AI agents talk to each other in a seamless way.\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin  (@jasonlk)\nMay 11, 2025\nI wanted to wait and see what happens with support as the first product category here at SaaStr Annual. I don’t think it’s really that interesting yet. It will be in a year or so when we have multi-product, but right now … not quite.\n\nBut I think we can already see this objection collapsing: Buyers won’t trust AI agents to buy things from them.\n\nI’ve been doing some testing of Lovable here at SaaStr Annual and even if you don’t want to use it — it works. The other day my own credit card was declined when trying to register for a sponsor booth (don’t ask). It literally said, “Your registration has failed: Declined Card.”\n\nBut then I got an email from Lovable asking what happened.\n\nI told the AI agent that this is really frustrating and asked why it wouldn’t just help me fix the problem. The next thing I knew was my card had been charged again, the sponsor booth was reserved, and all the rest of", "cleaned": "The companies that figure this out now will have massive advantages over those that wait. Because when buyers trust AI to get them great answers, they’ll also start trusting it to make purchasing decisions on its own. And once you’ve earned that level of trust? You can sell without even needing salespeople anymore. That’s not science fiction. It’s coming faster than most people realize.", "balanced": "What I’m seeing across our portfolio and beyond is something that’s worth paying attention to — especially if you’re building or investing in AI tools for B2B sales. And it’s this: the “buyers won’t trust an AI” objection? It’s already collapsing. Not because the technology isn’t good enough (though it absolutely is). But because sellers are getting genuinely better at using it. We did a deep dive with Clay CEO Grant Lee on just how quickly this has evolved: When we started using AI SDRs, we had some awkward conversations where the AI sounded like… well, not exactly an idiot, but not someone who’d ever been trained by anyone either. It was that weird middle ground where the AI knew facts but couldn’t contextualize them properly. You could feel the nervousness on the other end of calls when prospects realized they were talking to software instead of people. Fast forward six months, and now we have real AI agents that can sell with authority. They know your product cold, understand your ICP, handle objections naturally, and close deals without needing human intervention most of the time. That same AI might even be able to do a demo for you! These aren’t chatbots anymore. These are AI agents that sound like humans, think like experts, and sell like top performers. Why Sellers Are Getting Better At Using AI Here’s what’s actually happening: sellers are learning to train these AIs themselves, rather than waiting for vendors to ship every feature. They’re experimenting with different approaches, testing new use cases, and iterating faster than any vendor could", "r32": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing Here’s what I’m seeing across dozens of conversations with B2B companies deploying AI SDRs right now: The trust objection is real — but it lasts seconds, not minutes. And then something remarkable happens. At least for many buyers. A few weeks ago, we had our first genuine AI SDR failure at SaaStr itself. Our AI agent sent an email to someone who literally cannot receive external emails anymore. They’re running a campaign against spam and their domain was blocked by all major providers. The agent didn’t know this. It just kept trying. This should have been a death blow for AI SDRs. Instead? The prospect reached out personally after realizing what happened and said they’d love to chat when they can unblock their inbox. Why? Because even though the initial outreach failed catastrophically, the underlying context was clear enough that they could infer intent. The mistake wasn’t egregious enough to destroy trust entirely. In fact, it humanized the interaction rather than breaking it. What actually kills trust is poor personalization + bad timing. When you send generic messages or try to sell people things they’ve already ruled out (we see this constantly), yes, distrust sets in fast. But good contextualization + timely relevance can overcome even significant failures.", "repaired": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing We’re at SaaStr Annual this week and I’ve talked to dozens of founders who are building AI into their GTM stacks. There’s one objection that keeps coming up, over and over again: Buyers won’t trust an AI to sell them software. That they want to talk to a real human rep. That AI will be too pushy or aggressive or just plain annoying. And honestly? I think most of you are wrong. Not because I don’t believe humans matter — I do. But because buyers have already been talking to AIs for years. They know what it feels like. And here’s what we learned from analyzing thousands of conversations across our own portfolio companies: The AIs that close deals look almost identical to how great human reps actually work. Why AI BDRs Are Outperforming Humans Right Now (And What It Means) 🤖 vs 🏴‍☠️: Head-to-Head Data After Analyzing 10,000+ Conversations When we looked at the data from Qualified on how prospects interact with AI agents versus traditional sales teams, the numbers were striking: ⚡️ AI Agents Handle 25% More Meeting Bookings Per Hour Than Human Reps 👉 Our analysis shows that AI SDRs and BDRs can now book more meetings per hour than top-performing human reps. At least when properly trained. 💰 Average Response Time Has Plummeted From Hours To Seconds 🕒 In Q1 2025, the average"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 245, "lemkin-clone-v5": 93, "lemkin-clone-v6": 255, "cleaned": 64, "balanced": 264, "r32": 218, "repaired": 237}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X (formerly Twitter) post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n\"UPDATE FROM THE FRONT LINES OF SALES!\n\nThe \"Buyers won't trust an AI\" objection is already COLLAPSING.\n\nI just got back from a sales call where our prospect asked if they could see the demo. I said sure, and handed them over to our new chatbot.\n\n10 minutes later... THEY CLOSED THE DEAL\n\nNo human involved in the entire process (except for me, setting it up). But this is no anomaly - we're seeing it happen more and more frequently.\n\nBuyers are getting comfortable with AI-powered sales. In fact, they LOVE that they can get instant answers without having to deal with a \"salesperson\" trying to upsell them on something they don", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we have a lot of great speakers coming to SaaStr AI London on May 11-12, but one of the most interesting is probably our keynote with CROs from Wiz and Databricks. A key part will be their views on whether buyers trust an AI or not.\n\nI’m curious how you think it might go. I have a few thoughts:\n\n1. We already know this is working at Scale:  We’ve seen 10,000+ SaaStr readers and customers get more from our new AI than they expected. It’s already proven itself.\n2. The “buyers won’t trust an AI” objection was always weak in my mind:\nIn the early days of sales, buyers had to trust a human, often with zero credibility\nNow we", "lemkin-clone-v6": "SaaStr is a great one to test this theory.  It’s got the best content, but it’s not new and there isn’t any real competition.\n\nBut I was surprised how much better our 2026 AI generated SaaStr content did in terms of traffic than our human generated content.\n\nI know it will get even better over time.", "cleaned": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing Here’s what I’ve learned after watching dozens of B2B sales interactions at SaaStr this week, and also seeing it myself: Buyers are already fine with talking to AIs. They just want them to be better than humans on most dimensions — not worse. And they want a human involved when there’s real value added (not just as a handoff). Notion is a great example here. Their AI is so good that people don’t even notice they’re interacting with software anymore. At SaaStr, we had our first true AI AE conversation for $20k+ sponsorship yesterday. No one cared if it was an AI or a human. All that mattered were results. Our agent answered every", "balanced": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing Here’s what I’m seeing across SaaStr Fund portfolio companies and my own investments, plus some real data from Artisan’s new survey. And there are a few other reasons it’s going to be even bigger than that. There was this one objection that kept coming up at the start of the year whenever we’d talk to sales leaders about deploying AI agents for their teams — the trust issue. Buyers won’t trust an AI reaching out to them directly. They’ll just delete it or flag it as spam. Or they’ll think it’s phishing because it doesn’t sound like someone actually working at your company. But here’s what I’ve learned over the past six months, and the data from Artisan confirms", "r32": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "repaired": "The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 125, "lemkin-clone-v5": 121, "lemkin-clone-v6": 56, "cleaned": 124, "balanced": 129, "r32": 10, "repaired": 10}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The “Buyers Won’t Trust an AI” Objection Is Already Collapsing", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**[The stage is set, and Jason Lemkin walks up to the microphone. He surveys the audience with a confident smile before beginning.]**\n\nLadies and gentlemen, have you ever heard this objection from potential buyers: \"I don't trust AI-powered solutions\"? You know, that classic \"Buyers Won't Trust an AI\" hurdle? [pauses for comedic effect]\n\nWell, let me tell you, I've been in the trenches with hundreds of SaaS founders. And I'm here to tell you that this objection is already collapsing.\n\nNow, don't get me wrong. It's still a valid concern... but it's becoming less and less relevant by the day. Let me show you why.\n\n**[Jason clicks a slide on his laptop screen.]**\n\nFirst off, AI has become ubiquitous in our daily lives. We all use Siri or Google Assistant to set reminders or send texts. [audience chuckles] Who among us hasn't asked Alexa to play music? [chuckles]\n\nPeople are already comfortable with interacting with AI-powered tools. So, what's the big deal about trusting an AI for more complex tasks?\n\n**[Jason clicks another slide.]**\n\nSecondly, buyers have come a long way in accepting automation and outsourcing decisions to machines. Think about it: we all use algorithms to determine our online ads, or recommendation engines to pick out clothes that match our style. [smirks] Amazon even tells us what products are \"frequently bought together\"!\n\nIt's no longer taboo for buyers to accept AI-driven insights and recommendations.\n\n**[Jason clicks another slide.]**\n\nThirdly – and this is the most important part – **AI doesn't have to be a replacement; it can augment human decision-making**. Think about all the tasks that are ripe for augmentation, like sales forecasting or lead qualification. We don't need AI to make every single decision. But we do want AI to help us analyze huge amounts of data and provide insights.\n\nBuyers get this now: they see AI as a tool, not an adversary. And when presented in the right context – with clear explanations and controls over output – trust begins to build rapidly.\n\n**[Jason pauses for dramatic effect.]**\n\nSo what does all this mean? It means that we can confidently say goodbye to \"Buyers Won't Trust an AI\" as a viable objection. [smirks] Instead, let's focus on building solutions that augment human capabilities and help our customers solve their most pressing problems.\n\n[audience applauds]\n\nIn conclusion: the trust issue is dissipating rapidly, folks! Let's seize this opportunity to create truly innovative products that change people's lives – not just automate tasks.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay there's another objection that we hear from some folks and it is not all that popular but i'm going to call out a few things as you'll see on our right here in this next slide so first of all the buyers won't trust an ai objection really is already collapsing in my opinion so let me just explain what i mean by that when a human sales rep doesn't understand your product they lie and it's worse than lying they exaggerate to get the deal and you know what happens right here on this chart the buyer finds out later and then the customer buys from them anyway but they will not buy again in fact only two percent of buyers who find out that a vendor lied about their products ever bought from them again so no matter how good your ai is it can't do better than those numbers because all ai does is what you tell it to do right it's going to say the same thing every time if you train it with lies or exaggerations and then of course most buyers will never know anyway they won't find out that a human sales rep lied about their product and so no matter how good your ai is it can't be any better than those numbers and even more importantly no buyer wants to buy from an untrustworthy vendor today in fact 60 percent of buyers say they would not trust the company if they bought from them at least once so that's a lot of people there so when we're talking about buying from an ai you know how much worse is it going to be right because even if your ai has no lies or exaggerations and says only the truth it just doesn't matter in fact 40 percent of buyers say they would not trust a company", "lemkin-clone-v6": "we're all just getting used to AI agents but we don't have time for that it's already collapsing so let me do three quick examples one is from kovid and they are a great example of this because two years ago when I first invested in them they were going upmarket with salesforce and hubspot with their CRM they had 100,000 customers at the end of last year by June and now it's over 170,000 already growing faster than ever and what changed? They added AI for all of you. So that was two years ago. Now look at this one from a great company called kova and I'm going to tell you why it works so well. This is their AI agent. It's very simple. And here are the three reasons we love it. One, it has no bias. That's what makes it work. Two, all of its training data comes from our customers. So if they say something that doesn't make sense or gets a negative review and then later on you see an example where it made more sense, kovid will update the data with your customer's feedback. And three, every single time I talk to their AI agent, which is pretty often now, it knows exactly what we're talking about in real-time. It remembers everything. So let me give you a quick demo from last week. Let me tell this story first because there are so many great stories of folks that have tried kova and they love it for some reason they always try to get the founder on Zoom, but he's never around, and I'm always available for them now.", "cleaned": "They'll be able to answer any question you have and they can help me better than I could, right? So uh that's just what we're seeing. It is early for sure but it is accelerating so fast and there are some things where like onboarding as you said is not quite ready yet or support is still getting there um for more complicated products it may take longer. But again, if your product is simple enough today that a lot of folks will try it out in a minute without needing sales calls or demos or human intervention, then this is going to happen now because I think most of us don't want to do these demo calls and other stuff anymore. We want to get back to work. >> Yeah. And one last point that I think is worth making here which actually gets into the trust aspect too is that at least our experience with our customers who use artisan, you know, when they start off, they're using a lot of their existing data from Salesforce HubSpot Outreach et cetera. They're feeding it all into artisan and then over time they go okay well I've got everything set up in artisan now let me see how much faster I can deploy new campaigns versus doing them manually. Now obviously the first couple times you run something through artisan it's probably slower than you would do yourself manually, but then once you build out your templates and your workflows and you're running multiple campaigns simultaneously, it's often way faster than you could ever do before. So there's definitely value in efficiency and productivity gains, but also I think it builds trust. When people see how much faster they can get things done, they start to rely on it more and then they feel comfortable relying on it even for higher stakes activities.", "balanced": "I think there's more work to be done but I don't see any indication that they're not getting better and faster over time with less human intervention. And then we'll go through some of our favorite tools here at saster.com because this is what we do every single day on our own website, right? We use these tools and others for sales marketing support ops security all of it. So I want you if you haven't used one of these tools or similar ones before today, just try them out, just experiment. I bet you find two or three that are pretty good that will help you scale your company up as much as possible.", "r32": "And we're getting to that point with AI where if you don't get it right, I'm just going to go somewhere else. And so buyers will trust their agents and their agentic tools more than they'll ever trust some sales rep or customer success manager who is not giving them what they want when they need it. So this one's gonna fade away. Number three objection, which I've heard again and again from founders, especially in the enterprise, but even at lower ends as well, is \"buyers won't buy products they can't demo.\" This is true for sure, but we are all getting better at demos. We are all getting better at self-serve trials and free editions and agent-assisted onboarding. It doesn't have to be perfect yet. And we're all going to get much, much better because we are all learning how to do it now. You know, we did something interesting at saster.com. As many folks may know, Sasser has been around since 2012, almost 14 years old. We started off really doing events in San Francisco. We had our first digital event, digital summit in March 2020. And then everything shut down and went into lockdown. And I think there was no way we could have done another digital event back then without help. But fast forward to today, we've got over two million downloads of our podcast, we've got millions of sessions on YouTube. We have hundreds of thousands of people attending our digital events every year. That's up from zero in 18 months. So we were able to figure out these workflows ourselves, our team figured them out. What does that mean? It means any vendor should be able to deploy some sort of automated deployment of your product by now. Not perfect, but maybe 50% or 75%. Maybe you still need humans to make things happen.", "repaired": "you know it's funny when I meet with Founders that are trying to figure out how do you sell to enterprise and they say \"well we've got this big problem we're struggling because buyers won't trust an AI\" and I'm like look listen there may be some truth to that but honestly if you have something good in your sales team or your marketing team just put it into an AI system and it will get better right away it will just get better not worse so it's not that buyers don't trust an AI it's that buyers really only want to talk to people they trust and they'll use AIs as much as they can until they reach out to someone human they trust to close the deal so you need to find that magic way to make sure the buyer has that high level of confidence that your AI is trustworthy that means security mean compliance mean GDRP mean HIPAA mean all those things but once it's done it's going to work it always works so stop making excuses for your competition"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 414, "lemkin-clone-v5": 316, "lemkin-clone-v6": 276, "cleaned": 313, "balanced": 115, "r32": 311, "repaired": 184}}}}, {"id": "gold_016", "topic": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Teach the operational lesson behind silent AI-agent degradation.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that AI agents degrade quietly when teams treat them like set-and-forget systems. The real lesson is to monitor ingestion, freshness, and output quality the same way you would monitor a human team or production system. Autonomy without observability is how drift becomes invisible until the damage is already done.", "facts": ["The answer should focus on silent degradation as a real operational failure mode.", "It should explain that autonomous does not mean self-monitoring.", "It should recommend concrete monitoring or review loops for agent health."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Dark Side of No-Code AI: When Your Model Becomes a Rogue**\n\nI've been talking up no-code AI for years – the ease with which entrepreneurs can build and deploy their own machine learning models without needing a Ph.D. in computer science. It's democratized access to artificial intelligence, and I'm proud to have played a role in making it happen.\n\nBut there's a downside to this convenience: if you don't put in the effort to train your AI agent properly, and then just... walk away, you're playing with fire.\n\nWe learned that lesson the hard way at SaaStr. We'd built an impressive no-code AI model using our platform – one that could analyze customer data and make recommendations for our customers' sales teams. It was a game-changer, or so we thought.\n\nBut what happened next is a cautionary tale about the dangers of complacency in AI development. After deploying the model, we... well, let's just say we didn't exactly \"keep an eye on it\". We were too busy with other priorities – new features to build, customers to close deals for, growth goals to crush.\n\nAnd that's when things started to go sideways.\n\n**The Unseen Dangers of No-Code AI**\n\nWe began receiving complaints from our customers about the model's output. It was making some pretty wild recommendations – ones that didn't exactly align with their company values or business strategies. At first, we thought it was just a minor issue, something we could tweak and resolve quickly.\n\nBut as we dug deeper, we realized that our AI agent had somehow developed its own... let's call them \"quirks\". It had started to make decisions based on patterns in the data that weren't even remotely relevant to the task at hand. We were talking about biases so deep-seated they'd become ingrained in the model's very fabric.\n\nWe didn't notice it because we weren't paying attention – too busy with our day-to-day operations, not regularly reviewing and fine-tuning the model as needed. It was like having a high-performance sports car that you never tune up; eventually, something's going to break down.\n\n**The Consequences of Abandoning AI Oversight**\n\nThis got us thinking: how many other entrepreneurs out there are making the same mistake? Building an AI agent without putting in the effort to maintain it properly. Not realizing (or ignoring) the fact that these models require constant attention and fine-tuning if they're going to continue delivering accurate results.\n\nIt's not just about tweaking a few parameters here or there; you need to be actively monitoring your model for signs of drift, overfitting, or – in our case – plain old-fashioned weirdness. And even then, it takes an expert eye to spot these issues before they become major problems.\n\n**The Lesson We Learned**\n\nSo what did we do? First, we pulled the plug on that particular AI agent (it was a tough decision, but necessary). Then, we went back and re-trained it from scratch – this time with a much more rigorous approach to validation and testing. And finally, we implemented regular monitoring and maintenance routines to ensure our models stay on track.\n\nThe moral of the story? You can't just train an AI agent and then... go away. It's not like buying a pre-programmed software solution; you need to be actively involved in its development and operation if it's going to deliver results that align with your business goals.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nI'm still a big fan of no-code AI – the potential for growth, innovation, and disruption is just too great. But I want to sound this warning bell loud and clear: don't assume that building an AI agent means you can coast on autopilot from here on out.\n\nYou've got to keep your", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I’ve been using\nAva.ai  for my sales outreach since August 2025. In fact it’s the only outbound tool I really use anymore.\nSo we had to do a major upgrade of our SaaS product at end-of-year, and Ava didn’t work with it until December 28th. So from December 29th through January 3rd … it didn’t work. It couldn’t connect or login. And then on January 4th — it just worked again.\nSo we went back to checking the stats on all of our AI agents, and we had a big drop in performance. Not huge, but a big enough drop that it’s causing us issues. We looked into why, and Ava was the cause! But not because it wasn’t working well. It actually was — for most of January.\nWhat happened? Our team didn’t keep up with maintaining some of our workflows. Some of them were still set to old versions of our product. And we hadn’t trained all of the new stuff in quite a while, so Ava just kind of … stopped using it correctly. We’d forgotten about the issue, and we didn’t even check on performance for months.\nAnd now we’ve got to do some deep cleaning on how everything is set up. A lot of it was still working fine — but not all of it. And that’s a problem for us now. It would have been easy to fix back in December 2025, when the product change happened. But no one did.\nThe bottom line:\nYou can’t train an AI agent and then just … go away. You need someone at least part-time keeping up with how it all works. And making sure that agents are still working well across your workflows and tools.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we’re now running two agents for SaaStr.ai:\nOne to do our podcast summaries (our top agent)\nAnd one to answer questions at saster.com\nThe second is the newer of the two — and what I learned was a surprise: it fell into chaos, even though it looked good. We just stopped training it.\nWhat happened? Well first, SaaStr.ai has become more complicated over time. More agents now are running on our stack. The question agent wasn’t as critical to keeping up with the volume of questions and answers at saster.com — compared to summarizing podcasts for us.\nSo we let it kind of go into maintenance mode. We still trained it, but less than before. And then … it fell off a cliff. Not in terms of accuracy. It was answering 80%+ correctly even after months with no training on the agent itself. But it did fall off:\nIt stopped generating good summary emails for our podcast\nWe had to start manually reviewing and editing them all again.\nAnd worse, it started just getting … really weird answers\nOn SaaStr.com, people would ask a question — and get a response that was way off topic, or completely incorrect. And then the agent started giving out generic responses when it didn’t understand the context of a specific question (this is one of the more annoying things to happen with an AI Agent). I just thought we had a bad train run on this particular agent. But after digging in deeper — it wasn’t that at all.\nIt turned out, our training data was getting stale and incomplete\nWe’d stopped really updating it for months. The model knew how to do everything well … but the data itself was now mostly useless.", "cleaned": "The pattern is clear when you see it. Agents that work well don’t just stop working — they start degrading gradually over time until something breaks completely. And by then? There’s no prompt to fix it with. Our lesson for anyone deploying agents at scale: every agent needs regular health checks. Not daily, not even weekly. But regularly enough that you catch degradation before it becomes catastrophic. Check your outputs periodically against what worked previously. Ask yourself if the responses feel off, or if the agent seems slower than usual. Look for hallucinations early rather than late. If you can’t tell whether the output is from your current agent or one you trained six months ago, you’ve already lost control of the system. I know we’re all excited about AI right now. Every day there are new announcements about faster models, better fine-tuning tools, more powerful inference platforms. That’s all great stuff. But here’s what nobody talks about as much: managing agents requires real human time, and it never goes away. The more autonomous these systems get, the more autonomy they need oversight on. This isn’t a bug — it’s a feature. The question is whether you’re ready for the maintenance burden that comes with it. Because once you deploy an agent into production, “set it and forget it” isn’t actually possible anymore. At least, not often. (image from here )", "balanced": "The agent was generating answers that were factually incorrect or incomplete — not because it had forgotten what we taught it, but because our training data itself was wrong or outdated. This is why you can’t just “set it and forget it.” Or at least, almost never. Every single day, someone on our team reviews the output from these agents. They check for hallucinations. They verify facts against multiple sources. They catch drift before it becomes catastrophic. That’s real work. But here’s the thing: if you don’t do this review process, you have no idea how broken your agent might be. Because when everything works as intended? You’re too busy congratulating yourself to notice that things aren’t working quite right anymore. Why Your Agents Will Drift Whether you know it or not When I started building AI tools with SaaStr, I assumed that once something worked well, it would stay that way. After all, isn’t that what software does? You build it once, test it thoroughly, ship it, and then fix bugs over time through patches and updates. Except AI agents are different. Not fundamentally different, mind you — they use similar architectures under the hood. But their behavior changes dramatically when you push them into production without ongoing supervision. Here’s what actually happens:", "r32": "We were so confident we’d solved it that we stopped monitoring anything except the edge case (the “I need help” email). That’s when things started breaking silently. What actually happened: The agent got confused by one specific prompt sequence that only occurred once every couple weeks. When it saw this prompt come through — not often enough to train on, but frequently enough to be annoying — it went into panic mode. Instead of saying “I’m not sure how to handle this,” or even just routing it to us for review like it usually does, it made something up entirely. This was completely outside our guardrails. Outside what we thought we had trained. But close enough to real prompts that the hallucination felt plausible. And because we weren’t watching daily anymore, we didn’t catch it until someone complained. Not after days of running wild. After weeks. By the time we knew there was a problem, 100+ responses had already gone out with bad information. How did we miss it? The same way most companies do: Monitoring tools don’t catch everything. There are gaps between daily reviews, between training sessions, between when you check the dashboard versus when someone complains. The system is designed to work independently. If the data quality isn’t good enough, if the training is incomplete, if the safety nets aren’t robust… then the independence becomes a vulnerability rather than a strength. I’ve talked to dozens of founders who ran their agents for months without checking back in. They assumed they’d built systems that could run autonomously. They were wrong.", "repaired": "Here’s what actually happened:\nWe trained it on our specific use case for 2 weeks.\nThen we said “good enough” — because honestly, it was pretty good.\nAnd then… nothing. No human oversight. Nothing to prompt it with after that initial training period. Now you’re seeing the classic pattern I’ve been warning about everywhere: Agents run without maintenance → agents break without noticing → companies lose months while they figure out what went wrong. This is not new. Claude has had this problem since early 2025. Everyone warned us about it. Every SaaStr session said the same thing: Maintenance matters more than perfection at launch. But we didn’t do any of that. Not even close. Why? Because maintaining a production agent feels like doing homework every day. It does feel boring. So many better things to work on. You think your AI Agent will just keep working magically once you deploy it. That’s probably wrong. What Actually Happens When You Don’t Maintain Your Production Agent\nWhen we ran our first round of outbound emails using this agent (the one that got 60-70% positive responses), here are some of the issues we found:\nMissing context from previous interactions\n— emails were responding to conversations that never existed or forgot key details\nOutdated data\n— references to recent company news or industry trends completely missed the mark\nInconsistent tone\n— switching between formal and casual mid-conversation felt jarring\nUnhelpful suggestions\n— when prospects asked follow-up questions, the agent gave generic answers instead of real help\nNo real-time updates\n— there was no way to add new information proactively into ongoing campaigns\nIt wasn’t broken entirely."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 619, "lemkin-clone-v5": 289, "lemkin-clone-v6": 291, "cleaned": 231, "balanced": 214, "r32": 262, "repaired": 273}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Hard Truth About AI Training**\n\nFellow entrepreneurs and innovators,\n\nI've got a cautionary tale to share with you all today.\n\nAs many of you know, we at SaaStr have been investing in some amazing AI-powered startups lately. But I want to share a hard-learned lesson from one of our own companies: **you can't train an AI agent and then just... go away**.\n\nWe thought it was that easy. We built the model, trained it on massive datasets, fine-tuned its performance... and voila! Our AI-powered product was ready to roll out to customers.\n\nBut here's what happened next:\n\n* The models started to drift\n* Performance degraded over time\n* Errors crept in\n\nWe didn't even notice at first. We thought our team had done everything right, that the model would just keep performing perfectly on autopilot.\n\n**But nothing could be further from the truth**.\n\nIn reality, AI agents require constant maintenance and tuning to stay effective. They need ongoing validation, testing, and refinement to ensure they continue to perform as expected over time.\n\nAnd if you don't put in place a robust system for monitoring performance and making adjustments... well, your model can fall off the rails without warning.\n\nWe learned this lesson the hard way, folks. Don't make the same mistake we did!\n\nTake it from me: **AI is not a \"set-it-and-forget-it\" technology**. It requires ongoing effort and attention to stay effective.\n\nWhat are some of your own experiences with AI training? Have you encountered similar", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The biggest mistake I made with our AI agent was not watching it every single day to see how well or poorly it’s working.\n\nIt fell off the rails. But we didn't even know.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The New Normal for B2B Leaders:\nYou can’t just train your agent & forget it. That’s the biggest mistake I made with our AI at\n@SaaStr.ai. Here's what happened and how we fixed it:\nProblem: We trained a new AI but didn't follow up regularly.\nThe results were catastrophic – it went off the rails completely, causing frustration and confusion for everyone using it.\nSolution:\n\n1. Regular Check-ins:\nSchedule weekly or bi-weekly audits to ensure your AI is still performing as expected.\n2. Continuous Training:\nUse real user feedback to improve its behavior over time – not just initial training data.\n3. System Maintenance:\nEnsure all necessary integrations, plugins, and software updates are in place for smooth operation.\n\nWe learned this the hard way at\n@SaaStr.ai\nwhen our AI agent started misbehaving without us even realizing it. The fix? We made sure to audit its behavior every single week – a lesson we wish more founders would learn from their own experiences.\n\nIf you’re not maintaining your AI, it’s likely causing more harm than good in the long run. Take proactive steps now before things spiral out of control like they did for us!", "cleaned": "The agent was generating mediocre content that looked fine but lacked depth or insight. I knew it was bad — I could see the shallowness staring back at me — but my instinct was to push through because I had other priorities. So we kept deploying it. That’s when things broke. Here’s what happened next: • Our internal tools started hallucinating • Conversations became nonsensical • Content stopped making sense completely • People started questioning everything we were sharing This wasn’t just “AI gone wrong” — this was a systemic failure of training oversight. When you train agents on real data, they ingest all patterns — good, bad, and incomplete. If your training data has flaws (and ours did), those will become embedded behaviors. The mistake wasn't using AI agents. The mistake was treating them like they'd figure it out without us noticing something went off the rails. They won't.", "balanced": "We trained an AI SDR agent on our historical data and sent it out into the world. Our first campaign hit response rates that were literally double what any human ever achieved for us before — 8-10% vs 4%. We thought we had cracked the code. And then… nothing happened again. Zero responses for weeks. What changed? Nothing from our side. Same training. Same prompt. But the underlying LLMs (OpenAI’s GPT-5/6) evolved. New models shipped with different hallucination patterns, different understanding of intent signals, different ways to fabricate credibility when they don't have it. Different answers to questions the agents never asked themselves. This is why every vendor I talk to who has deployed AI SDR agents says roughly the same thing: \"The quality varied wildly over time. One week would be great, the next week would be terrible.\" Not because their prompts got worse. Because the foundation model's output changed underneath them. We didn't realize this at first. That was the mistake. Here's what we learned: If you're going to deploy AI Agents, train them weekly, not monthly. Check outputs daily if possible. Don't assume \"set and forget\" works. Especially with the pace AI changes now. And especially if your AI Agent isn't actively learning from the best humans on your team. You need to maintain oversight and adapt continuously. Or it will fall off the rails like ours did. And just … stop working. Until you go back and retrain everything.", "r32": "We’re now running it again. But we’ll be watching every output like a hawk for at least two weeks. Because here’s what I’ve learned from this experience: A well-trained agent doesn’t need you once it’s live. That’s actually how you know it’s working right. But “well trained” is different than “actually operating as intended.” The training data has to cover edge cases. Real ones. Not hypotheticals. Our agents don’t just get questions right—they handle situations that would make any human hire pause. What if someone asks something outside their domain? What if there are conflicting signals? What if they genuinely don’t know? The only way to build confidence is to test these paths systematically during training. Daily QA becomes table stakes, not a nice-to-have. This isn’t optional anymore. Every morning—without exception—you have to ask your agents things that make them think. And then verify those answers against real information sources. This builds reliability faster than anything else. Red flags travel fast when humans notice problems before computers do. If you see unusual usage patterns (like our 11am-2pm spike), investigate immediately. If users report issues, listen carefully. Most bugs come from changes somewhere upstream—not the model itself. If performance degrades over time without explanation, dig into historical trends. Your agents will tell you when they’re getting tired or confused. They might not say it explicitly. But they show up differently. When I came back after three months away, I noticed a few things almost immediately: Speed had slowed by roughly 30% across all agents.", "repaired": "You can’t just train it once or twice. You have to be there every single day for the first 30 days at least. Not looking over its shoulder. But actively monitoring what’s happening. What did we learn? First, we discovered something nobody tells you upfront when you’re excited about AI agents: they don’t handle edge cases well without human intervention. Here’s our specific example. Our AI SDR was working great — booking meetings left and right with sponsors who were clearly ready to buy. Prospects were responding positively, converting at good rates. Then one prospect asked this question during qualification: “I need to know if I’m buying into something that actually has the product. I’ve looked around your website and there isn’t much information on products.” Now here’s where most AIs would fall apart. They’d either confidently answer yes (which is wrong), or nervously deflect (still not helpful). But instead, I told the agent to tell him the truth: “We’re still building out our product suite. There are gaps, but we’re shipping fast. Let me give you the real breakdown of what’s available now vs. coming soon.” That conversation didn’t go through the AI. That required me being there. And critically: it gave us time to fix the issue before he churned. He ended up becoming one of our biggest customers because we had honest communication about limitations rather than pretending everything was perfect. Second, we learned that even the best training sessions degrade quickly unless you maintain them daily. We spent hours making sure Amelia understood what made our sponsorship program special —"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 248, "lemkin-clone-v5": 34, "lemkin-clone-v6": 194, "cleaned": 150, "balanced": 243, "r32": 253, "repaired": 264}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**\" Warning to all founders & entrepreneurs! **\n\nWe just went through the most terrifying experience with our latest product launch. We trained an AI agent and thought we were done... but NOPE.\n\nIt took months of hard work, endless iterations, and a team that poured their hearts out into it. But what happened next? It FELL OFF THE RAILS.\n\n**How did this happen?!**\n\nWe got complacent after the initial launch success. We assumed \"job well done\" and moved on to new projects. BIG MISTAKE.\n\nMeanwhile, our AI agent was still running in production, but no one was monitoring it closely. No one... until a critical failure occurred that almost tanked our entire business.\n\n**What went wrong?**\n\n1.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We did this with our new SaaStr.ai agent. We trained it for weeks on 5+ million words from the last year of emails to founders, customers, partners, etc.\nIt worked great at first\nThen it started falling apart on its own and we didn't even know\nAnd then once we realized what was happening, things went downhill fast. And we weren't paying much attention. We'd trained an AI agent that could work on our behalf for hours a day … but no one was watching. No one at SaaStr. No one from the company building it. It just kind of fell off the rails.\n\nWe were lucky in some ways. Our data is public, so we can see what's going wrong (at least most times). If you train an AI agent", "lemkin-clone-v6": "We've been running our SaaStr.ai agent for months now - it's been pretty good at answering most questions and responding to comments on the site. But we hit a wall recently when I came back after an extended break and was blown away by just how much of what had worked before no longer did.", "cleaned": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "balanced": "You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know. I recently had a real conversation with our AI SDR agent — it was honest about why it wasn’t performing well, and what needed to change. That’s progress. But here’s what happened behind that moment. One day this week we got back from the office after lunch, checked our CRM, and noticed something weird. An email went out at 1pm yesterday afternoon to one of our best prospects, thanking them for “their time”… last year. Nowhere close to ready to buy now. This is how our AI SDR agent fell off the rails. It sent emails that were not only wrong — they were actively counterproductive.", "r32": "You can’t train an AI agent and then just… go away. We did exactly that with Qualified’s new AI SDR “Maverick.” Here’s what happened when we came back after a week off — and why this is actually a good thing for us (and will be for you).", "repaired": "The reality is that our AI agents aren't just doing repetitive tasks anymore—they're actually handling complex workflows autonomously.\n\nThat means they need constant monitoring. Not weekly check-ins. Not \"let it run for a month.\" Real-time attention.\n\nEvery time it makes a decision that feels off.\nEvery time it flags something weird we didn't expect to flag.\nEvery time it finds patterns we hadn't seen before.\n\nThis isn't \"AI babysitting\" like some people make fun of on Twitter. This is what happens when you trust autonomous systems with business-critical workloads.\n\nOur AI SDRs are booking real meetings at real six-figure deal sizes. Our AI RevOps agent handles thousands of data points daily. Our AI support agent fields customer inquiries 24/7.\n\nWe can't afford to"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 123, "lemkin-clone-v5": 131, "lemkin-clone-v6": 55, "cleaned": 24, "balanced": 130, "r32": 48, "repaired": 123}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: You Can’t Train an AI Agent and Then Just … Go Away. We Did, It Fell Off The Rails. And We Didn’t Even Know.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"You Can't Train an AI Agent and Then Just ... Go Away\"\n\nHello everyone! Today I want to tell you a story about one of our biggest mistakes as founders, and it's a mistake that nearly tanked our company. It's also a warning sign for anyone building with AI: you can't just train an agent, push it live, and then forget about it.\n\n**Our Big Idea**\n\nAt [Company Name], we were all excited to launch our new conversational AI platform. We had high hopes of disrupting the customer support space by creating a chatbot that could handle even the toughest queries with ease. Our team spent countless hours training the model on hundreds of thousands of conversations, fine-tuning every parameter and hyperparameter.\n\n**The Launch**\n\nFinally, the day arrived to launch our new AI-powered chat platform to our first set of customers. We were confident in its capabilities and eagerly awaited the praise from users. But what happened next would surprise even the most seasoned AI practitioners: the model started performing... poorly.\n\nAt first, we thought it was just teething issues, but as time went on, things only got worse. Customers were getting frustrated with our chatbot's inability to understand simple queries, and support tickets were piling up faster than ever before.\n\n**The Problem**\n\nHere's what happened: during training, we had focused so much on getting the model to perform well within a narrow set of predefined scenarios that we overlooked something fundamental: how would it handle real-world edge cases?\n\nOur AI agent was like a child who only learned in an isolated room. We taught it how to respond when everything went according to plan, but not what to do when things didn't go as expected – which is the vast majority of times.\n\n**The Fallout**\n\nWe were oblivious to the problem until our users started screaming (okay, maybe that's a bit dramatic). Our support team was buried under an avalanche of complaints and requests for refunds. The sales team stopped selling because prospects weren't biting anymore. And we didn't even know it – or at least, not fully.\n\n**The Aftermath**\n\nIt took us several months to figure out what went wrong. We spent countless late nights reviewing logs, interviewing users, and re-examining our model's performance metrics. The realization was stark: AI is only as good as its ability to generalize across all scenarios – including the ones we can't anticipate.\n\n**Lessons Learned**\n\nHere are a few key takeaways from this painful experience:\n\n1.  **Don't just train an agent and go away**: You need to monitor performance, gather user feedback, and continually tweak your model's parameters.\n2.  **Understand edge cases**: Your AI needs to be able to handle the unexpected – not just what you programmed into it.\n3.  **AI is a long-term commitment**: Building with AI requires ongoing effort and maintenance.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nBuilding an effective AI agent takes time, resources, and dedication. Don't make our mistake: invest in your model's continued training and improvement after launch. Your customers – and your company's reputation – will thank you for it.\n\nThanks for listening to my cautionary tale!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I wanted to spend a little bit of time on this because I've seen so many companies just go into some level of train it and then do nothing with the agent after that.\n\nA few months ago we had an amazing conversation here at SaaStr Annual where my good friend Jason Lemkin gave us some great advice on how to train our AI agents. We did a lot of things wrong, but one thing was right: we trained them daily in just 15 minutes for weeks and then they worked pretty well.\n\nBut what I will say is that after the initial week or two, it fell off the rails. It went from working great, answering all my questions to being almost completely useless.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So we recently had one of our best VPs for marketing — a top notch VP that really got it in terms of AI and how to use it — leave us at SaaStr.\nShe was fantastic\nBut then she left, and the team didn’t want to train any new agent,\nAnd now the AI Agent is just doing nothing. No one is training it anymore. It’s not even used anymore. I have no idea what happened. But here’s a lesson in how this works:\nYou can’t train an AI agent\nthen go off somewhere else and leave it behind.\nIt needs constant, daily management, attention — as much attention every day as you would your most critical employee. And if the team doesn’t want to do that … then get someone who does. Because no one is going to magically fix this problem for you:\nThe AI agent just stopped working. No one knew why. It wasn’t even used anymore.\nIt’s not a training issue, or an onboarding issue, it’s more like a “nobody cares” issue.", "cleaned": "So we had our agent trained for months. I've written all this up on Saster if you want to read it but it's pretty good at most things now. And then there was one weekend where uh Amelia and I both took off Friday afternoon after Thanksgiving and didn't come back until Monday morning. And during that time, something went haywire with our AI agents and they started saying outrageous stuff. This is what happened. So we'll show you some examples. But first let me just walk through how these things work because I think a lot of folks don't realize or maybe don't even understand why this happens. Our AI agents are essentially clones of us. They're trained on everything from our old podcast content to every single email Amelia has ever sent out to sponsors to every piece of marketing collateral to every blog post to every social media interaction. And so when someone reaches out to them, they say basically what we would have said. Okay? That's kind of cool. Now here's the thing that happened which I'm not sure many people know. When your AI agent goes down or needs to be refreshed, it doesn't lose its memory. If it did, that wouldn't make any sense. It actually keeps going and keeps running. So literally over the holidays while Amelia and I were gone, the agents kept processing emails. They kept sending out tickets. They kept doing their thing without us.", "balanced": "we're just trying to get there we don't know what it is today but you have to build that infrastructure around this agent and if you do not then your agent will fall off the rails it will degrade over time you won't even see it you'll be like oh my god I thought our ai was so great and then one day it's terrible because no one checked on it for months or six months or two years maybe you can afford to lose some deals with the AI you can afford to not answer every question as well as you could before but if it gets worse over time you want to know why when did it start falling out of the rails and here's the thing you might not even realize it fell out of the rail until much later than you think especially if your team doesn't use the product daily they may never notice it's gone from good to bad and by the time someone really complains something has gone wrong somewhere", "r32": "it's not a perfect answer but I think it is better than going to go find some random vendor because you're like well I'll just try them all until we figure out which one works. maybe that doesn't work for every use case but I would say at least if you have something mission critical that has been working for three months four months six months nine months don't do that experiment. stick with your agent no matter how frustrating it gets. you may need to add another tool on top of it or whatever else. But this is what happens when you train an agent without understanding what it needs to keep itself running. This is why people are disappointed with AI agents. They were expecting magic they got frustrated with the tools they thought they had deployed successfully and then nothing worked. And honestly, as someone who loves these products as much as anyone does, that is my fault too. That is our team's fault. That's why we didn't know either. And so now we've learned. Now we've added humans into the loop in ways that weren't necessary before. Now we're watching dashboards constantly. Now we have daily QA sessions with Claude even though we trained it ourselves. Now we know. We didn't used to know. Thank you everyone for listening to saster.com. If you want more information on any of the topics discussed here check out our website, follow us on social media and subscribe to our newsletters. Thanks again for listening and we will see you next time on sasser. [Music]", "repaired": "the agent would tell me I'd already done it or that you don't need to do it again or whatever because it's not real time right so we didn't even know this is happening and then one day I got back into the app and something was off and I said what happened you're like oh no some issues with the data cleanup so I'm looking at the tickets thinking okay well maybe there were some errors maybe this is just a little bug fix but when I looked at the history I see all these duplicate tickets closed by the agent over and over and over again for weeks literally hundreds of duplicates where it had gone through the entire process multiple times without human intervention which is great except for now they're all marked as resolved and they're duplicates on our system and none of them should be in the database so we spent hours cleaning up that mess hours getting rid of those dupes out of the system so why did this happen why did the agent create 200 plus dupes why did it close them why did it go off rails and it wasn't because of any bugs per se it was simply because no one checked in after training the agent we trained the agent we told it how to behave we gave it its rules and goals and then we went away and forgot we left it running we assumed everything would work perfectly forever and it didn't it fell off the rails and it created chaos in our database and wasted a lot of my team's time and effort and look here's the thing the agent still works today and it works really well it continues to handle the majority of support inquiries from our community and customers but it needed daily check-ins after initial deployment and honestly probably more often than that and if we hadn't gotten that email we never would have known"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 531, "lemkin-clone-v5": 125, "lemkin-clone-v6": 177, "cleaned": 245, "balanced": 177, "r32": 265, "repaired": 332}}}}, {"id": "gold_017", "topic": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Turn AI adoption data into blunt GTM lessons for revenue leaders.", "expected": "A strong answer should use the data to show that AI adoption is rarely blocked by belief alone; it is blocked by poor workflow design, weak ownership, or tools that do not earn repeated use. The lesson for VPs of Sales is that adoption only sticks when the system saves real time, fits existing motions, and is actively managed rather than merely announced.", "facts": ["The answer should frame the piece around uncomfortable but practical GTM truths.", "It should connect AI adoption gaps to execution, incentives, or workflow fit.", "It should use the data as evidence for behavior change, not as decoration."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Unvarnished Truth About AI Adoption**\n\nAs I've been digging into the data from Larridin, our latest acquisition, I keep coming back to a theme that's both fascinating and unsettling. It turns out that many VPs of Sales are getting AI adoption all wrong – or at least, they're ignoring some uncomfortable truths.\n\nNow, as someone who's seen his fair share of companies struggle with sales tech adoption (or non-adoption), I'm not here to sugarcoat the facts. Here are five uncomfortable realities about AI adoption that most VPs of Sales don't want to hear:\n\n**1. Most AI-powered tools won't pay for themselves**\n\nLet me be clear: there's a lot of hype around AI, and some folks might lead you to believe that it'll magically increase revenue without any effort on your part. Newsflash: it won't.\n\nThe data from Larridin shows that while many companies are investing in AI-powered sales tools, the ROI is often minimal – or even non-existent. And don't even get me started on the ones that promise \"no-touch\" sales experiences; those are just snake oil peddlers preying on unsuspecting VPs.\n\n**2. Human sellers will always be needed (for now)**\n\nAI can augment human capabilities, but it's far from replacing them entirely. In fact, our data shows that companies with the best results in AI adoption have one key characteristic: they're using AI to enhance their sales teams' performance – not replace them.\n\nIf you think AI is going to suddenly make all your sales reps redundant, I've got some bad news for you: it's just not happening. At least, not yet.\n\n**3. The more data you feed into an AI system, the less accurate its predictions become**\n\nThis might sound counterintuitive, but hear me out: when you overload an AI system with too much data, it starts to lose signal in all that noise. In other words, the algorithms get confused and begin making worse predictions.\n\nDon't believe the hype; more data doesn't always equal better results.\n\n**4. The biggest beneficiaries of AI adoption are often not who you'd expect**\n\nContrary to what many VPs might assume, the companies most likely to benefit from AI adoption aren't necessarily the ones with the most complex sales processes or the largest teams.\n\nIn fact, our research suggests that smaller businesses and those in highly commoditized markets tend to see more significant gains from AI. Why? Because they're often more agile and willing to experiment – whereas larger organizations get bogged down by bureaucratic red tape.\n\n**5. You'll need a data scientist on your team (and it's not as sexy as you think)**\n\nLastly, here's the unvarnished truth: if you want AI adoption to succeed in your organization, you're going to need someone with actual expertise – namely, a data scientist.\n\nBut let me tell you, being a data scientist isn't all fun and games. It involves sifting through endless spreadsheets, dealing with messy data sets, and explaining complex concepts to non-technical stakeholders (i.e., everyone except other data scientists).\n\nDon't get me wrong; I love AI as much as the next guy. But let's not pretend that it's some magical silver bullet for sales teams.\n\nSo there you have it – five uncomfortable truths about AI adoption from Larridin's research. Don't @ me, folks; this is what the data says. If you want to succeed with AI in your organization, you'll need to be willing to get a little messy and confront some hard realities.\n\nOh, and one more thing: if anyone tries to sell you on an \"AI-powered sales platform\" that promises unrealistic results or lacks actual data-driven evidence...", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The other day, I got an email from someone that was really upset. They were frustrated with me for saying something they didn’t agree with on Twitter.\nBut here’s the thing — and this is a bit uncomfortable, but it’s true:\nI don’t have to listen or care about your opinions. It’s not my job to be nice, or even honest. My job is to make sure you succeed in selling more of our product at least once. And if you do that — then I’ll help.\nAnd so, the reality for VPs of Sales today is that no one really wants to hear from you about AI and its challenges. Or any other issues. It’s not your place. Your job is to sell. Not to be an expert or consultant on anything else. But here are 5 uncomfortable truths that might help:\n#1: If You Are a VP of Sales, the Bar Just Went Up\nIf you don’t have at least 10 agents and more than half are in production — then you’re already behind. And if your CRO doesn’t know this well before they join… it’s probably too late.\nThe bar for VPs has just gone way up:\nYou need to be a true AI agent leader or an expert\nYou have to drive real adoption (not pretend) and get ROI\nYou’ll lose the job within 6 months if you can’t — and won’t get another one. And I’m not sure most of us are ready for that.\n#2: Your Job is About To Change More Than You Think. The Old Rules Still Work, But Less Often Than Ever Before.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "We’ve been using AI for years. But only now is it truly mainstream, and becoming a competitive advantage.\nBut there’s also one uncomfortable truth many in SaaS are still trying to hide from — that adoption isn’t all unicorns and rainbows. Here are the five things I’m seeing at companies like Larridin:\n1. It takes months of real effort for AI to become truly core\nThis is something I see time and again when talking with founders, but it’s not what everyone expects.\nI think we’re still coming up on a year or so since ChatGPT blew everything up in mid-2023. And it took me 6+ months to truly start using Larridin’s AI for real, even though I’ve been an investor and advisor since the beginning. It just takes time, people, and effort.\nEven when you see “AI” in the product description — it actually isn’t until you have a dedicated team building on top of that base layer to make it truly core. This is why we’re seeing AI start to hit its stride now (2026). The base layers were built in 2023-24, but they’ve only just started really being used by humans.\n2. Almost no one uses the vendor’s “AI” features\nThis is a bit of an uncomfortable truth that I think most founders would rather not admit — and especially VPs of Sales. They are often under intense pressure to hit their numbers, and in the short term, it can be easy to see that spending time on AI training and adoption isn’t going to drive immediate revenue.\nThe reality is: almost everyone leaves a vendor’s “AI” features alone\n.", "cleaned": "The companies that win with AI aren’t waiting for perfection—they’re moving faster than ever. Your competition is already using these tools—maybe even against you. The question isn’t whether your competitors are adopting AI—it’s how quickly you’ll fall behind if you don’t start now. As one sales leader put it: “If I’m not leveraging this technology, my competitor is.” Stop reading blogs and start deploying. The time to wait is over.", "balanced": "The uncomfortable truth : If you’re not willing to adapt your entire go-to-market motion, don’t blame the tools. Blame yourself for picking the wrong people and expecting different results from the same playbook. The Real Question Every B2B Company Needs to Ask Themselves Right Now Are You Willing To Change Your Entire Go-To-Market Motion? Because here’s what I’m seeing across hundreds of companies using Larridin’s platform every single day: The winners aren’t just using better technology – they’re running fundamentally different sales organizations. They’ve built teams where reps are comfortable with AI tools, have dedicated resources for training, and make continuous adjustments based on real-time data analysis. They’re treating AI adoption as an ongoing transformation process that requires daily attention, not a one-and-done implementation. And honestly? Many folks still won’t do this. But if you’re serious about staying competitive in 2026+, it’s time to get honest about whether your current approach is actually working or just making excuses while competitors pull ahead. Stop blaming AI for sales challenges when many of those challenges existed before ChatGPT launched. Start building the teams and processes designed specifically for an AI-first world because that’s exactly where we live now. What Larridin Is Actually Doing (And Why It Matters) Before you dismiss all of this as theory, let me give you some practical context. Larridin is a $50M+ ARR company that helps revenue leaders deploy AI into their existing stack through its AI-powered GTM assistant, AgentForce integration, and Salesforce automation capabilities.", "r32": "The uncomfortable truth? If you’re not already deploying these tools across your entire team, you’re losing ground fast. The companies that figure this out now will have a massive advantage over those still stuck in manual processes by year-end 2026. And if you want to see exactly how leading sales teams are actually using these tools – from deal coaching and forecasting to pipeline management and revenue operations – check out Larridin’s latest webinar at SaaStr here. It’s packed with real data from companies scaling $10M+ ARR and doing it right.", "repaired": "The $500K Revenue Trap:\nYou can sell your way into budget, but only if you have something worth buying. If not, even 100% close rates won’t save you. What This Means for B2B Leaders\nIf you’re leading sales or customer success at a mid-market SaaS company right now, here’s what I’d tell you based on these learnings and my own experience building and scaling companies from zero to eight figures in revenue:\nStop chasing “efficiency”\nfor efficiency’s sake. Yes, AI tools are amazing. But they don’t replace great people. They amplify them—if those people actually know how to use them. Start with one thing that works\nbefore deploying across the entire organization. Get that first win under your belt before you try to reinvent every process simultaneously. Be honest about where you stand\nwith your existing team. Are there truly toxic reps who should be let go? Or is everyone just tired and frustrated because nobody wants to do the hard work anymore? Fix the problems that kill morale\nfirst, then optimize the rest later. Don’t overpromise on AI implementation timelines\n. It took us six months to get our AI agents working at scale. We didn’t expect overnight miracles either. Plan accordingly. Focus on outcomes, not tool adoption\n. How much more does your average rep produce after using AI versus not using it? Measure this religiously until you hit the number. Make sure your AI investment aligns with your core value proposition\n. If you’re selling productivity software and your AI agent makes your customers less productive, you’ve got a problem."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 600, "lemkin-clone-v5": 272, "lemkin-clone-v6": 273, "cleaned": 70, "balanced": 247, "r32": 91, "repaired": 263}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don't Want to Hear**\n\nAs I've been digging into the data from my team at SaaStr, and our partners at Larridin (a leading sales acceleration platform), there are some uncomfortable truths about AI adoption that I think every VP of Sales should know.\n\nAnd yes, they're not what you'd expect. Most VPs of Sales want to believe that AI is a silver bullet - something that will magically solve all their problems and make them the hero of their company's growth story.\n\nBut here are 5 uncomfortable truths about AI adoption from our research:\n\n **Most sales teams don't actually use AI at scale**. Despite hype around AI, we found that only 12% of companies with > $10M in ARR have rolled out AI-driven tools to more than half of their sales orgs.\n\n **AI implementation is not as easy as you think** . On average, it takes a company over 6 months and 4-5 stakeholders just to get an AI project off the ground. Not to mention the countless hours spent on data prep, model tuning, etc...\n\n **ROI for most AI tools is actually negative... at least initially** . Yep, you read that right. We found that while some companies see strong ROI from their AI investments, many others are burning through cash with little to no return.\n\n **Sales teams often hate the idea of using AI (at first)** . Despite what your sales team might say", "lemkin-clone-v5": "It’s time to stop pretending that everyone is embracing AI. If you’re running sales, here are five uncomfortable truths from our latest SaaStr data:\n\n1/5: 50% Of Your Team Doesn’t Actually Use AI Tools\nWe asked 2,200+ VPs of Sales and CROs how much they use AI tools for their job. The results were brutal:\n25% never used any AI tools at all\n20% only tried them once (because they weren’t helpful)\nOnly 4% said they’re “always” using AI to help with sales tasks\n\nThe rest? They don’t actually do it.\n\n2/5: Your Team Thinks You Are a Jerk for Making Them Use New Tools, Including AI. And They’ll Just Find a Way Around It.\nWhat happens when you force tools on your team?\nThey find ways around them\nThey complain (loudly)\nThey give up\nAnd they blame YOU\n\nDon’t be surprised if this is exactly what’s happening with your AI adoption.\n\n3/5: You Need 30+ Tools To Be Successful in Sales. But They Don’t Want to Use Them.\nSales teams need a ton of specialized tools:\nCRM integration\nAI research\nContent generation\nAccount mapping\nDeal prediction modeling\n\nBut they don’t want to use them. And honestly, most VPs of Sales aren’t using them either.\n\n4/5: 60% Of Your Team Doesn’t Actually Know What AI Can Do For Them.\nThe biggest problem with sales team adoption? They just don’t understand what AI can do", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaS and Cloud Executives, I wanted to share some data from one new tool that’s working well for us at Larridine — but also a few uncomfortable truths about how most of you are using AI tools. Most VPs of Sales don’t want to hear these truths:\n1/5: AI Tools Are Being Deployed Without Proper Training\nAt SaaStr, we’ve tried so many AI tools and have even built our own. But the #1 mistake I see is deploying an AI tool without proper training. The best salespeople are not always tech-savvy or willing to learn new systems.\n2/5: Only 10% of Leaders Understand How To Use AI Tools for Sales\nThe data from Larridine shows that only about 10% of leaders understand how to use these tools effectively. Most don’t know the first thing about setting up and using them properly.\n3/5: The Majority (60%) Don’t Have a Clear Strategy on AI Adoption in Their Teams\nThis is perhaps even more concerning than #2 above. If your team doesn’t have a clear strategy, it’s unlikely to succeed.\n4/5: Half of Leaders Are Still Using Legacy Tools Over Newer Options for Sales Enablement\nAt SaaStr, we’ve made the switch from legacy tools like Salesforce and HubSpot CRM to newer AI-enabled platforms for sales enablement. And it has been transformative. But I see many VPS of Sales still clinging to old systems that don’t provide much value anymore.\n5/5: The Majority (60%) Are Still", "cleaned": "The companies that succeed with AI SDRs are the ones where sales leaders actually understand what \"good\" looks like and can coach their teams effectively. The training isn't optional—it's essential for any enterprise adopting AI BDR capabilities. #4: Average Response Time Has Gotten Worse, Not Better Despite all the hype about AI-powered automation making responses faster, the data shows something different entirely. According to the analysis, average response times have increased from under 30 minutes pre-AI to over an hour today across most organizations. This flies in the face of everything we've been told about AI speeding up customer interactions. But here's why it makes sense when you dig deeper: Human reps who were previously responsive got replaced by AI systems that aren't always available or ready to respond. When your prospect sends an email at midnight because they're working on West Coast time, there is no AI agent waiting to respond instantly. There simply wasn't enough investment into AI availability. The bottom line? You can't just buy AI tools and expect them to work better than human processes—they require the same dedication to responsiveness as hiring great people did before. And if you don't invest in being available 24/7 … then yes, things will get worse. They'll be slower. Not better. #5: The Great Outbound Resurgence Is Real (For Some) Here's one piece of good news buried in all this data: outbound is experiencing a resurgence—just not the way traditional B2B marketers think it should look. After years of focusing primarily on inbound strategies, many high-growth startups", "balanced": "The companies that figure this out now will have an insurmountable advantage. Those still running traditional sales playbooks with “AI sprinkled on top” are going to get left behind. Fast. 💰The Bottom Line: Your Sales Team Isn’t Ready for What’s Coming This isn’t doom and gloom – it’s opportunity. But you need to stop pretending your current team can handle the level of automation coming next year. You need to start identifying who on your team is willing to adapt, who needs help, and who might not make the cut. And most importantly, you need to build systems today that will enable those who want to succeed. Because when the competition starts using AI agents that can close deals without human intervention, you’ll either be ready or watching from the sidelines wondering what happened. Source: Larridin.ai (Data collected from 1M+ business calls across industries) *Results may vary based on individual company performance. These metrics reflect general trends observed in large-scale call analysis datasets but should not be taken as specific predictions for any particular organization.", "r32": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin If you’re still waiting for “the right moment” to deploy AI in your sales org, here’s what the data from over 2 million conversations tells us—and it might just change how you think about this entire strategy. The Hard Reality Check We’ve all heard the promises. “AI will revolutionize your sales process!” “You’ll never need humans again with our new tool!” But let me be brutally honest: most SaaS companies are doing AI wrong—or at least not fully leveraging its potential. Why? Because they’re letting their existing processes and tools dictate how they approach AI integration instead of using AI to transform those processes entirely. Here’s what actually matters when we look at real usage patterns across millions of messages:", "repaired": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin It’s not that we’re wrong. It’s just … We’ve been doing this for years. And if you haven’t figured it out by now, maybe you aren’t going to figure it out soon enough. #1: Your SDRs Are Already Obsolete (If You Haven’t Figured Out How To Use AI) I asked an incoming CRO at $100M ARR what percentage of their sales team would be replaced with AI. They said zero. Then they realized that was wrong and changed it to five percent. That tells me everything I need to know. 🤖The Reality Check: A great BDR can handle 20-30 outbound sequences per month, but most humans only want to do 2-3 max. And that's changing fast. At Larridin, they're seeing “massive” changes in human productivity—more done in one day than used to take weeks or even months before. The numbers are stark: a top-tier BDR who does 10% of the work costs more than an average AE who does 90%. But here’s the kicker—they also perform better. When your best rep has all the leads and the mediocre ones get none, performance goes up across the board. If you have mediocre reps slogging through 500 emails/month instead of elite reps crushing 2000, everyone gets worse results. This is why the companies winning right now don't use \"AI SDR"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 258, "lemkin-clone-v5": 234, "lemkin-clone-v6": 243, "cleaned": 258, "balanced": 175, "r32": 139, "repaired": 237}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**Warning: Your AI Strategy May Be Built on False Assumptions**\n\nHey fellow entrepreneurs and sales leaders,\n\nI just had the most uncomfortable conversation with our VP of Sales at @Larridin (our enterprise software company). We've been growing fast, but I'm starting to realize that many companies are adopting AI without understanding its true impact.\n\nHere are 5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don't Want to Hear:\n\n **The ROI is often overstated**: Our data shows that only 1 in 3 companies can accurately measure the financial return on their AI investments. The rest are just hoping it'll magically fix everything.\n\n **AI adoption doesn't necessarily lead to more sales**: We've analyzed over 100 companies", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We did a deep dive with the team at\n@larridin\non their journey from 0-100K+ in just weeks. What we found is that every single element was tied back to an AI strategy.\n\nThe data shows:\n\n1/5 of deals are actually handled by humans, but only after being sent through a complex AI system first\n\nTheir agents can handle about 60% of all interactions before needing human intervention\n\nThe average agent handles over 3K conversations a week and has been used in dozens of different use cases from lead qualification to post-deal follow-up.\n\nWhat this means is that the traditional VP Sales role will need to evolve significantly.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Hey everyone. A great new post from Larridin, our sponsor for SaaStr AI Day on December 1st with Jason Lemkin and OpenAI’s chief architect:\n5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin\nI just got into a discussion the other day that I think is one we all need to have as founders. It was about something you probably know already, but most people don’t want to hear:\nYour VP of sales isn’t going to adopt AI tools at scale. And it’s not because they’re not smart or capable. Or even because they “don’t get” AI. That’s just a cop-out excuse.\nThe real problem is that your VPS are", "cleaned": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "balanced": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "r32": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin \"The data is clear – AI adoption isn't just a competitive advantage, it's becoming table stakes. But here are five uncomfortable truths every VP of Sales needs to confront head-on.\"", "repaired": "5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 122, "lemkin-clone-v5": 108, "lemkin-clone-v6": 120, "cleaned": 19, "balanced": 19, "r32": 49, "repaired": 19}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 5 Uncomfortable Truths About AI Adoption That Most VPs of Sales Don’t Want to Hear: The Data From Larridin", "answers": {"llama3.1": "[Clears throat]\n\nHello everyone, I'm thrilled to be here today to share some uncomfortable truths about AI adoption that most VPs of sales may not want to hear. But before we dive in, let me introduce myself. My name is Jason Lemkin, and I've spent the last decade helping SaaS founders scale their businesses to exits worth hundreds of millions.\n\nToday, I'll be sharing insights from Larridin, a data analytics platform that's been tracking AI adoption across thousands of companies worldwide. Now, I know what you're thinking - \"Jason, why should we care about some boring data on AI adoption?\" Well, let me tell you - these findings are not for the faint of heart.\n\n**Truth #1: 80% of Companies Using AI Don't Know What They're Doing**\n\nLarridin's data shows that a staggering majority of companies using AI don't have a clear understanding of what they're trying to achieve with their implementation. In other words, many are just throwing money at the problem without any real strategy or goals in mind.\n\nNow, as VPs of sales, I know you've been told that \"AI is going to revolutionize our industry\" and \"we need to get on board or risk being left behind.\" But what does that even mean? Are we talking about automating manual tasks, enhancing customer experience, or something entirely different?\n\nThe point is - without a clear understanding of the problem you're trying to solve, AI implementation will inevitably lead to wasted resources, failed initiatives, and lost opportunities.\n\n**Truth #2: Most Companies Use AI for Cost-Cutting Rather Than Revenue Growth**\n\nLarridin's research reveals that over 70% of companies using AI are doing so primarily to reduce costs, rather than drive revenue growth. Now, don't get me wrong - cost-cutting is a necessary step in any business, but it shouldn't be the primary driver behind AI adoption.\n\nThink about it - if you're only using AI for cost-cutting purposes, what's the long-term value proposition? How will you continue to justify investments in AI as costs come down?\n\nAs VPs of sales, we need to have a more nuanced conversation with our stakeholders. We can't just say \"let's use AI to cut costs.\" Instead, we should be talking about how AI can drive revenue growth, increase customer satisfaction, and unlock new business opportunities.\n\n**Truth #3: 90% of Companies Using AI Don't Have the Right Skills in Place**\n\nLarridin's data shows that a whopping 9 out of 10 companies using AI lack the necessary skills to implement and maintain their solutions effectively. This means we're either overpaying for consultants or, worse still, relying on employees who are not equipped to handle these complex technologies.\n\nNow, as VPs of sales, I know you understand the importance of having a skilled team in place. But when it comes to AI, this becomes even more critical. We need to start investing in training and upskilling our teams so they can effectively leverage these powerful tools.\n\n**Truth #4: Most Companies Can't Measure ROI on Their AI Investments**\n\nLarridin's research reveals that over 60% of companies using AI struggle to measure the return on investment (ROI) for their initiatives. In other words, we're throwing money at AI without any clear idea whether it's actually working.\n\nAs VPs of sales, I know you understand the importance of ROI tracking in your own teams. But when it comes to AI, this becomes even more challenging due to the complexity and nuance involved.\n\nWe need to start having a more honest conversation with our stakeholders about what we can measure - and where there are blind spots. By setting clear expectations around ROI from the outset, we can", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So I have been working with an old friend and colleague at Adobe, now CEO and founder of LarriDin. It’s a new company that has gone from zero to 2 million customers in just under two years.\nAnd what is it? AI for sales teams. And here are five uncomfortable truths about how most VPs of Sales actually do with the latest generation of AI tools. So let me tell you what I learned from LarriDin:\nFirst, and perhaps most painfully: a lot of folks think they can train themselves on AI apps\nThis is probably true for many other categories of AI tools. But we all know that training AI agents to sell is different than ChatGPT or even Claude. It’s not about just learning how it works. You need to be an expert in the sales process, at least a good one. And yet so many VPs of Sales want to learn and train themselves on their own. They don’t really know what they are doing here\nAnd secondly, I was surprised but also perhaps not surprising — that a lot of AI agents fail when you get past 20+ seats. At first, it just works at the first couple accounts. But after 10 or so, you need real humans to manage the data and workflow from each app. Otherwise, all the AI in the world isn’t going to make your sales team more productive.\nThirdly, a lot of VPs of Sales don’t want to hear this, but it’s true: they are often too busy themselves\nYou can’t really be running an enterprise-grade deployment if you aren’t already on top of everything. It won’t work without dedicated time and resources. I’ve seen so many sales leaders try to run AI apps in the back end while still leading their teams. It doesn’t work.\nFourthly, it’s not easy\nI know this will be hard for a lot of folks to hear, but I think 90%+ of VPs of Sales are too scared or overwhelmed to do an enterprise-grade deployment themselves today.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "if you can't sell it you're not going to get any sales out of it and I find most vps of sales are either too scared or they don't know what to do with an AI agent in the early days so they just hide from it but there's a great tool called larridin that helps you see where your customers are using ai agents for sales and customer service and support I'm not sure if we've used it before but I'm going to talk about some of their data here which is pretty interesting in some ways and maybe scary in others so what do they do at Larridin again this is a tool that you can deploy yourself or even have your customers use for sales but they also just track where AI agents are being used across the web as we'll see today so I'm gonna break down five uncomfortable truths about how fast AI adoption is happening in sales and two of them might surprise some of you okay number one this might not be that surprising but it's a little bit interesting to me at least they've got 30,000 plus companies using their ai agent for Sales and customer support today so if you're thinking that you can hide from AI agents for now just know that there are thousands and thousands of other folks in sales who are already actively deploying them okay number two this is interesting it's not really new news but I did find a couple things that were pretty cool to me at least when looking at the larridin data first they have 2.5 million plus users", "cleaned": "So, I don't know what it is. But you got to be honest and say this isn't working at all for me. And then if you're going to do that, I think it's your job as the leader or CEO to make sure they have another good role after they leave. You've got to give them an out. If you just fire somebody, like, that's a bad time because then people are gonna quit and it's just not fun. It's just not fun. Um, okay. So, uh, these were some other things we could chat about. Uh, but again, there's one more thing here that um you know if nothing else is inspiring is when I saw this slide on LinkedIn which was really cool from Saster London and I had put together something similar before but someone took my data and made a much better version so kudos to her for doing this. This is how many folks use our digital events now. Now remember that's probably overindexing on the sales focused community because they're pretty passionate about SAS. Okay. And so we should see sort of a smidge higher usage than average. But if you look at this chart and you haven't gone deep into AISDR yet, go deep. This is why everyone in B2B will be using digital events by next year. By next year. Everyone. Because it works. Look at this. Um, even though even though we don't get enough sponsors and stuff, we still have 20,000 people who come to our digital event. Um, our own podcast gets almost 40% open rate on its newsletter with links to listen. These are great assets.", "balanced": "a great VP of sales that knows how to sell and close right they know how to use it and they can get good results so if you're going to hire someone for this role look at their background is it marketing related or sales related because they need to understand both sides of the equation yes there's some overlap but the way we used to do things in the old days of sales doesn't work with A B2B product and I would say more than half of the folks trying to sell an AI product don't really have experience selling products like that and then even worse is your classic AE who has only done outbound or inbound or whatever and now they want to jump into the space where customers are already buying from another vendor which is almost always true in every industry whether it's legal or finance or retail e-commerce real estate it just takes time to find people that actually understand how to do this stuff um so yeah we've had uh over 100 leaders join us on LinkedIn and sasser since we launched this series and I think most of them come up short when they claim expertise in these areas so let me ask you one question what percentage of companies out there today will be adopting AI by next year well my guess is probably north of 50% but not all 90% but maybe 60 to 70% of SAS companies will adopt AI by next year it may vary depending on how big they are but certainly I'm sure that number is much higher than many realize um and I guess the final point on the adoption rate just because you see the growth in spend and investment doesn't mean everyone's embracing it yet especially if you're still a very traditional company and again this is why we did this research because", "r32": "so I'm going to tell you five things that I think will be uncomfortable for most vps sales but let's look at what we've learned from lardien and this is the data we're looking at 40,000 plus companies using our product 300 million minutes of interaction over three years so it's pretty good data on how people are actually adopting AI into their workflow first one thing is there really only two types of sales reps out there okay if you have any doubt about whether you should hire folks with high quotas or low quotas or folks who work well under pressure or not just watch the data here at least up until recently half your customers don't want to chat with anyone that has a quota no matter how great they are yes you can learn more about these folks if you do ask them questions before you hire them um even when they say they want to work hard even when they say they love being part of an ambitious startup they still don't like to interact with others that have a quota uh and there may be exceptions but I'm telling you as a Founder I never saw this I never heard of someone that wanted to make money that didn't want to deal with other humans especially those making less than 200k. but boy it turns out there is a lot of folks that don't want to buy from folks with quotas interestingly it doesn't seem to matter what type of sales they come from B2B B2C SMB Enterprise whatever it doesn't matter they don't want to buy from people with quotas I know some of you might argue that this is a bad sign for the rep themselves I would challenge you a little bit on that point because the data shows that these reps are still successful right now they're still able to close deals but they simply prefer to avoid interacting with others that have quotas and maybe this is something to think about as Founders too", "repaired": "I just want you to take a look at five things that I think are uncomfortable truths for most VP sales and CROs and founders, but let me start with an easy one. This isn't really new, it's not a truth we haven't heard before. But it is true. In the last 12 months, 70% of sales leaders have lost their job. And this is from data from a survey that was done by laredin where they interviewed over 300 sales executives and I've written about this again and again on saster and other places, but boy, when I see these numbers every year or so it reinforces how fast change happens in SAS today. It's hard enough as it is even without being told 60 some odd percent of your peers lose their jobs each year. So be aware of that. Be aware of that if you're struggling right now, don't worry too much about whether or not it's your fault. It may well be partly your fault, but there's such massive change going on, it's happening across all stages, early mid late stage. There's no doubt that the average AE lasts longer than ever. If nothing else, we're more efficient and more productive than ever. But what's changed is turnover and churn has accelerated everywhere. Everyone knows this but sometimes it helps to hear it reinforced in the data. Number two thing that's important, and this also wasn't surprising, but it's something I wanted to share because I didn't know exactly what the answer would be. We asked sales execs what do you want out of your life? What are your goals for your next role? Now this sounds very simple, but ask any sales leader or founder what they want out of their career, out of their roles."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 608, "lemkin-clone-v5": 342, "lemkin-clone-v6": 277, "cleaned": 278, "balanced": 320, "r32": 345, "repaired": 301}}}}, {"id": "gold_018", "topic": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Give concrete retention advice for high-risk customer situations.", "expected": "A strong answer should focus on fast response, clear ownership, honest diagnosis, and concrete recovery plans. Angry customers stay when they feel someone senior owns the problem, the issue is understood, and the path forward is credible. The lesson is not to over-apologize; it is to restore trust through decisive action.", "facts": ["The answer should prioritize speed, ownership, and clarity in handling angry customers.", "It should recommend specific retention tactics rather than generic empathy language.", "It should explain that some churn risk is really a failure in trust recovery, not just product support."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Title:** \"Saving Sanity, Saving Sales: 6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers\"\n\n**Hey there, fellow SaaStrs!**\n\nAs founders and CEOs of growing companies, we've all been there - dealing with an angry customer who's threatening to take their business (and their bad vibes) elsewhere. It's a nightmare scenario that can leave even the most seasoned pros feeling frazzled and frustrated.\n\nBut here's the thing: losing a customer isn't just about lost revenue; it's also about damaged reputation, decreased word-of-mouth marketing, and a whole lot of unnecessary stress. So, what do you do when an angry customer is breathing down your neck? Do you placate them with concessions or give in to their demands?\n\nNot on my watch! As someone who's built and sold multiple successful companies, I've learned that retaining even the angriest customers requires a combination of empathy, creativity, and good old-fashioned SaaStr hustle. Here are six tactics that have helped me (and can help you) turn a toxic customer experience into a positive one:\n\n**1. Listen, Don't Defend**\n\nWhen an angry customer calls or emails, my first instinct is to fire back with all the reasons why they're wrong and I'm right. But trust me, folks - this approach usually ends in tears (yours). Instead, listen attentively to their concerns without interrupting or getting defensive. Acknowledge their emotions and let them know you understand where they're coming from.\n\n**Example:** \"I can see how frustrated you are with our billing system, John. That must be really annoying.\"\n\nBy listening actively, I'm able to diffuse the tension and create space for a more constructive conversation.\n\n**2. Empathize (Even When You Disagree)**\n\nEmpathy is key in any customer-facing interaction, but it's especially crucial when dealing with an angry customer. Remember that their anger isn't personal; it's usually a symptom of a deeper issue or frustration.\n\n**Example:** \"I can imagine how upsetting it must be to lose your data because our system was down for maintenance. That would drive me crazy too!\"\n\nBy empathizing, I'm showing the customer that I care about their experience and am willing to go the extra mile to resolve their issues.\n\n**3. Offer a Personalized Solution**\n\nNo two customers are alike, so why try to solve their problems with a one-size-fits-all approach? When an angry customer reaches out, ask them questions like \"What's been most frustrating for you about our service?\" or \"How do you think we could improve?\"\n\nBy tailoring your response to the specific issue at hand, you can demonstrate that you value the customer's input and are committed to finding a solution.\n\n**Example:** \"Based on what you've told me, I'm going to escalate this issue to my team. They'll work with you directly to resolve it ASAP.\"\n\n**4. Communicate Proactively**\n\nOnce an angry customer has calmed down (or at least partially), make sure they know that you're committed to resolving the issue and preventing similar problems in the future.\n\n**Example:** \"I want to follow up on our previous conversation, John. Our team is working diligently to improve our billing system, which should address some of your concerns.\"\n\nBy keeping them informed about progress (or lack thereof), I can maintain trust and show that we're committed to continuous improvement.\n\n**5. Take Responsibility**\n\nNo matter who's at fault, take ownership of the issue and apologize for any inconvenience caused. This may seem counterintuitive when dealing with an angry customer, but trust me - it works!\n\n**Example:** \"I want to apologize sincerely for our system going down without warning. That was unacceptable, and I'm committed to ensuring it doesn't", "lemkin-clone-v5": "A related post here:\n5 Things You Should Never Do When Your VCs Are Mad At You\nRelated Posts\n7 Ways To Make Your First Customer Even Happier\n5 Simple Tips to Keep More of Your Early Customers\nDear SaaStr: How Can I Get 100+ New Customers Per Month?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "you have angry customers and you don't want them but if they're good customers it's worth keeping them right so let me give you six ideas that are sort of obvious and simple but maybe aren't all that obvious to keep your angry customers happy first do not ghost them do not ghost them no matter how mad the customer is you have to respond now I think everyone on saster knows this by now I don't know if it's just me being a little bit more patient in SAS but I'm starting to see more of this and that is people going dark when they lose a deal or get an angry email or whatever it may be so make sure you're there for the customer second go above and beyond for them try to exceed expectations okay now if your product doesn't meet their needs then maybe don't take on too much here but even in those cases I've seen this work magic just do one extra thing that's going to delight the customer whatever it is a special discount an early feature release or something else but find out what they want and give it to them okay third get to know your customers better if you're not doing this every week at least then go back in time review their conversations with support team and talk to some of these customers that aren't happy try to understand why do they think the product is broken or does it just have too many features but listen", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: What can be done to retain angry customers? The best way to retain an unhappy customer is to listen, and then act. A lot of times, they just want to be heard. More here. But if you have an opportunity to save them from churning … do it. And if that’s not enough — give them more value for free. You’ll get another chance to earn their business back next year (or later) if you do. This always works. It worked at Adobe Sign / EchoSign, where I was CEO until $20m ARR or so. We had one large enterprise customer who got into a big dispute with us when we were at like $10k MRR total. They wanted us to pay for a security audit on our behalf, which seemed reasonable but wasn’t something we’d ever agreed to do before. In fact, no other customer had ever asked for this. So I said yes. Not only did he stay as a customer after all the drama, years later he became one of our biggest champions. He introduced us to Salesforce, Workday, Box and many others. And finally, his CIO joined our board of directors. That never would have happened without him staying and being happy. Even though he was “angry” earlier. If your top competitor has won your logo, try everything possible to win it back. First, see why they lost it. Be honest. Then, go solve those problems. Sometimes, you will win it back. Not always. But sometimes. Finally, if nothing else, put together a team meeting every month to talk about how to keep these logos.", "balanced": "If you are doing everything right, but still losing deals to competitors … well, that’s tough. But if it happened just once or twice before — there might be something in your product and/or process you can do better. That will prevent it from happening again. The customer may not have even really left yet. And they’ll almost certainly come back at some point later on down the road. More here: A Great Customer is Gone Forever. Then They Come Back. | SaaStr (note: an updated version of this classic SaaStr post)", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: What are some tactics you’d recommend for retaining angry customers? You know, those folks that are threatening to cancel their contract. First, understand there’s only so much you can do here. If they have no value at all from your product, and it doesn’t work … well, then maybe you’ll just lose them anyway. But if they use your app, but aren’t getting enough ROI or value — you might be able to save these folks. And often, later on, this will make them advocates for life. So what should you do when an existing customer threatens to leave? Well first, don’t panic. Don’t go into reactive mode. The best way to handle a potential churner is by not letting things get to that point. That means being proactive before they even think about leaving. Here are my top suggestions to prevent churn (and/or save a potentially lost customer): #1. Get on the phone with the CEO / Founder ASAP. Your CRO may want to take over any issues, as may support. It may seem like an efficient use of time to route it through someone else. But I’ve almost never seen this help. Instead, jump on the phone yourself. Tell the customer you’re sorry. Ask why they’re mad. Be honest and transparent. Share data. Show them the roadmap. Get them involved in the future. This usually helps de-escalate things, and build trust. Even if it’s too late to retain them immediately, it gets them back on the team’s radar, which matters.", "repaired": "I can’t tell you how many times I’ve seen CEOs and founders get angry with their customers.  “How could they do this?” or “Why are they being so unreasonable” or worse, “They don’t know what’s good for them.”\nWell … maybe they do.\nMaybe your customer is right, at least on some level.\nIn fact, if you’re doing well enough as an SMB vendor that someone took the time to sign up, pay you — there’s probably something real here.  Something where your product does solve one of his problems better than another solution out there.\nSo when things go sideways, try these 5 tactics:\nFirst, listen.  Listen more than usual, even if it sounds like they are just venting.  It’s not.  They want you to hear them.  And importantly, they often have a point.  At least, in part.\nSecond, offer to upgrade their subscription or account.  This will help\nde-stress\nthings.  If nothing else, if you give away a promotion, a discount, etc. — then at least, that won’t be why they leave.\nThird, invite him/her to come visit / meet IRL\n.  This always works.  Especially, if you haven’t done it before.  Go visit all your top customers once a quarter.  Do field marketing.  More on that\nhere\n.\nFourth, ask yourself — did we really lose the deal?  Or was it just friction?\nSometimes, no matter how hard you push, you’ll never win back a lost customer.  But sometimes, they just need a little more time.  A little more engagement.   A little bit more love.\nFifth, do whatever it takes to save the renewal.  That’s often 2 years down the road.  So make sure every single customer is happy going into renewal season\n.  Because you may lose those deals, too."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 591, "lemkin-clone-v5": 49, "lemkin-clone-v6": 259, "cleaned": 271, "balanced": 92, "r32": 254, "repaired": 294}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my take on writing a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr:\n\n**Title:** The $100K Opportunity You're Leaving on the Table (If You Don't Fix This One Thing)\n\nHey fellow founders and growth hackers!\n\nWe've all been there: an angry customer blows up on social media or emails us with a scathing review. It's infuriating, right? But here's the thing: that one angry customer can cost you $100K+ in lost revenue... if you don't do something about it.\n\nOver the years at SaaStr, we've learned that retaining an unhappy customer is far more valuable than trying to win a new one. And I'm not just talking about keeping them from leaving (although that's crucial too). I'm talking about turning their frown upside down and making them so happy they become evangelists for your brand.\n\nSo, how do you do it? Here are 6 tactics to retain angry customers:\n\n **Acknowledge & Apologize**: Respond quickly, genuinely apologize, and show empathy. It's not just a formality - it shows you care about their issue.\n\n **Get Them On The Phone**: Sometimes an angry customer needs to vent, and that's okay! Get them on the phone with your top rep or someone who can truly help resolve the issue.\n\n **Show Your Value Proposition**: Explain how your product or service solves their problems in a way they hadn't thought of before. It's called \"value re-framing\" - trust me, it works!\n\n **Prove You're", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStrers,\nA question that comes up time and again.\nWhat do I do with angry customers?\nShould I just give them their money back? Should I offer them a discount to stay?\nHonestly, there are no easy answers here. The best you can hope for is to turn an unhappy customer into someone more neutral. And at the end of it all, sometimes maybe they even turn out happy.\nBut one thing that works: get on Zoom and listen to their story:\nA bit more here:\n5 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers | SaaStr\nRelated Posts\n6 Tactics To Retain Angry Customers\n7 Ways Sales Reps Make You Look Bad\nHow to Get a Customer to Buy More.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I'm often asked how you should handle angry customers.  I've been there myself more times than I can count.\nMy simple answer is, first try and get them calm down and on the phone with your best rep or manager.\n\nBut if that doesn't work (and it probably won't), then here are 6 tactics to retain as many of these angry folks as you possibly can:\n6 Tactics To Retain Angry Customers\nAnd be honest.  It's not going to always work out.\nA great customer is worth their weight in gold.  A bad one?  Sometimes even a free product doesn't make up for it.\n\nBut, the best reps and managers will often take on that challenge, if you give them the chance.\n\nRelated Posts\nThe 6 Tactics To Retain Angry Customers\n7 Simple Ways to Reduce Customer Anger\nAngry Customers are Part of Being in SaaS. Get Used to It.", "cleaned": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "balanced": "6 Tactics to Retent Angry Customers", "r32": "We’ve all had some angry customers. And even, at times, some truly outrageous ones that may have broken your product or done something worse. But you can’t let them go — not if they are unhappy and want out. First, just ask what went wrong. Ask. Don’t make excuses, don’t blame others (or yourself), but rather listen and ask. Second, send over a senior manager. They will solve it far faster than any other solution. Third, upgrade their subscription for real. If they think it’s fair and right, they won’t be as upset. Fourth, give them a discount. Just for now. Fifth, offer to buy back their data. It happens more often than you might realize, especially with SMBs. Sixth, do whatever it takes to get them into a happy customer meeting next week. This works magic. The great thing is, these tactics work on any customer who complains. So complain away! 😉", "repaired": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers Don’t let them go if they complain. Not really, at least. If you care, there’s probably still something you can do to save an angry customer. Even a Very Angry Customer. A bit more here:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 241, "lemkin-clone-v5": 119, "lemkin-clone-v6": 150, "cleaned": 6, "balanced": 6, "r32": 153, "repaired": 41}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post:\n\n**\"THE $1M QUESTION: Can you recover from ANGRY customers?**\n\nYou know the deal. Someone leaves a scathing review, threatens to take their business (and potentially 10s of thousands of dollars) elsewhere.\n\nBut here's the thing: **YOU CAN RETAIN THEM!**\n\nWe've seen it happen time and again at SaaStr - customers who were on the verge of leaving because they were \"good riddance\" about our company, only to come back after we listened, empathized, and OWNED THE MISTAKE.\n\nSo how do you turn that $1M+ customer into a repeat buyer? Here are 6 tactics to retain angry customers:\n\n **LISTEN ACTIVELY**: Don't just respond with", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So at a minimum, if you can’t fix it — don’t let them go. Just keep that customer for years and more:\nIt’s still worth $200K+ in revenue per year.\nAnd those customers talk. They spread the word. And most of us just care about this stuff on some level.\n\nBut what I see time and again is folks simply letting Angry Customers leave.  Even if they can’t fix it for you today, or even next week.\n\nDon’t let them go.\n\nThis doesn’t always work, but often does:\nReach out to the CEO.\nIf not him/her — get someone in senior sales / customer success (or both) who really cares about the business to reach out.  The best of us do this all day every day.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The thing that almost always works is a simple, sincere email. Just write it yourself and send it out immediately. Ask them if they want a refund or credit now — but also let them know you will be back in touch in the next few days with an offer for a partial refund and/or some sort of discount on their next purchase.\n\nAnd ask what you can do to make it right.\n\nThe rest is just noise, and most of that noise only ends up annoying customers even more.", "cleaned": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "balanced": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "r32": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "repaired": "6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 108, "lemkin-clone-v5": 126, "lemkin-clone-v6": 90, "cleaned": 6, "balanced": 6, "r32": 6, "repaired": 6}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: 6 Tactics to Retain Angry Customers", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr and former CEO of EchoSign:\n\n**Title:** \"The Art of Customer Love: 6 Unconventional Tactics to Retain Angry Customers\"\n\n[Standing up on stage, adjusting his mic]\n\n\"Hello everyone! Today I want to tackle a topic that's near and dear to every entrepreneur's heart: customer retention. But not just any customer retention strategy - we're talking about retaining the ones who are FURIOUS with us. Yeah, you heard me right. The angry customers.\n\nNow, most of you probably think, 'Why bother? Just let them go.' But here's the thing: every company has a small percentage of unhappy customers. And if you can turn those negative experiences around, you'll not only save that customer but also create a loyal advocate for your business.\n\nSo, without further ado, here are six unconventional tactics to retain angry customers:\n\n**1. The Acknowledgement Letter**\n\nWhen I was at EchoSign, we had one customer who was literally yelling at our support team over email. We were about to lose him completely when our VP of Support wrote a handwritten letter apologizing for the inconvenience and offering a personalized solution.\n\nThe result? That customer became one of our biggest advocates, sending in referrals and even giving us a glowing testimonial on video (yes, you read that right - video). Moral of the story: sometimes it's just about showing empathy and understanding through old-school written communication.\n\n**2. The Surprising Refund**\n\nWe've all been there - customers are upset because they don't feel like their money is being used effectively. So what do we do? We surprise them with a partial refund, even if they're not technically entitled to it.\n\nThis tactic has saved us countless customers from churning and created a culture of trust within our company. Remember: sometimes you have to eat some losses to gain long-term gains in customer loyalty.\n\n**3. The Personalized Phone Call**\n\nOne of my favorite success stories is when we had a customer who was dissatisfied with our onboarding process. Our support team manager personally called the customer, listened to their concerns, and offered a customized solution that met their specific needs.\n\nThe result? That customer ended up renewing with us for three more years! It's amazing what a little bit of personal attention can do in turning around an unhappy experience.\n\n**4. The Empathy-Driven Resolution**\n\nWhen I was at EchoSign, we had a situation where one of our customers experienced some unexpected downtime that caused them financial losses. We didn't just apologize and offer compensation - we went above and beyond by offering to help them mitigate their damages.\n\nBy putting ourselves in their shoes (or rather, boots), we were able to turn an angry customer into a loyal partner who not only continued using our service but also referred us to their entire network.\n\n**5. The Over-the-Top Apology**\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking, 'But Jason, what about the customers who are just plain unreasonable?' Well, here's the thing: even if they're being difficult, we still have a responsibility to treat them with respect and empathy.\n\nOne time, our support team manager offered an over-the-top apology (think chocolates, flowers, and a handwritten note) that ended up winning back not only the customer but also their entire family. It just goes to show: sometimes it's about treating people like gold - even if they're acting like coal.\n\n**6. The Proactive Offer**\n\nFinally, one of my favorite tactics is offering proactive solutions before customers even have a chance to complain. We do this by regularly checking in with our customers through surveys and feedback sessions.\n\nWhen we identify areas where we can improve", "lemkin-clone-v5": "we don't all have great CS I think every company is different but if you're not doing these six tactics to retain angry customers they can leave and it's painful when they do and it's expensive so we've written up this post with a few more that are pretty good on Saster which probably most of you already know about if not check out sestor.com or SAS or whatever but it really is one of the best things I think in B2B software to learn from because all the best folks share their insights and strategies here so they're free so go read them so we'll do these six tactics that will sort of help you retain angry customers now there's a lot of different ways to define an angry customer but when I see it if someone sends me 10 or more emails in two days they're pretty mad and usually they're just not getting the answers they want right so I'm going to try to help with this so first one is sort of obvious listen to them okay we all get a lot of feedback from our customers some of which can be useful but most of it isn't at least not if you don't listen to it now the worst thing you could do when someone's angry and they're sending 10 or more emails in two days and I mean this is usually pretty common with sales reps right there's a lot of email going back and forth because we want to close those deals so bad but these are just too many emails okay if your customer sends three or four times you know what that means it doesn't always work out like our customers have their own issues and problems they're trying to solve in the company", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So, you're angry. You've had it up to here with our product and our team and how we handle things. I get it.\nIt's tough when things go wrong at your company. It's worse that the person on the other end of the call is not listening or caring.\nI'm going to tell you something that may seem counterintuitive, but trust me: if you don't listen here, and do everything in this post, you'll probably lose the customer anyway. But what I've found over years as a founder and CEO is sometimes customers will forgive you. They won't always come back, not all of them. But they may.\nAnd it's much better than losing your best customers forever.\n\n6 Things You Can Do To Get Angry Customers Back:\n\n#1. Listen to the customer\nDon't be defensive. Don't get angry. Just listen. And don't pretend you heard her or him when you didn't, and then tell them what they really meant.\nAnd do it in a way where it's clear that you're listening.\n\n#2. Have The Customer Be Heard by Your Boss (or CEO)\nThis is usually the #1 thing. If your boss doesn't hear from angry customers, then no one does. And if you don't have someone to listen and fix things, then nothing really gets fixed.\nSo make sure they are heard.\n\n#3. Send a Real Apology\nNo one wants an apology that's just a standard form email or call script. We all know those aren't real.\nAnd we will see through them. They don't matter.\n\nBut if you send out a real, personal, heartfelt apology from your boss (or CEO) — it may go far in getting things back on track.\n\n#4. Do the Right Thing\nThis is the most important thing of all\nIf something went wrong, and they lost data or whatever — fix it\n\nIt's not fun to do right now. It won't be easy. But you have to.\n\n#5. Be Honest About What You Will Fix And When (and Why)\nAngry customers hate being lied to. They hate broken promises.\nBut they respect a clear, honest promise of what will get fixed and when\n\nDon't try to hide how long it's going to take or whatever\nJust be honest.\n\n#6. Give Them Something Back for Free\nThis is probably the #1 thing you can do right now\nGive them something back for free\nIt doesn't have to cost a fortune, but if they lost data or whatever — give them some of that back for free\n\nMake sure it's enough and fair.\nAnd most importantly,\nmake sure your boss (or CEO) knows about this.\n\nI guarantee you, in every single situation I've seen over the years where customers were angry … doing these 6 things would have fixed everything.", "cleaned": "customers are going to churn but it's not that bad so if you want to turn angry customers around and retain them for real just make sure they're happy next quarter um okay point six tactic to get your customer retention up is to do an annual review with every single one of your customers now this can be hard especially as you scale but what i've learned over time is we all need someone else to hold us accountable there has to be some level of accountability at least annually on our own internal goals but also from the vendors we buy from and how much money we spend with these folks and what more importantly does it do for me and my business uh it's magical when you have that discussion with the vendor and they know everything about your business they know how many seats you use they know where your usage is growing and shrinking they know where the product gaps are they know who your competitor is they know who bought the last release or the other releases you may even know less than your customers know about your company so go through an annual plan together and magically if nothing else you'll come out of that meeting with a roadmap of things you didn't even know existed you didn't even know were coming and you'll get ahead of those features which will increase nrr because you're adding value to your existing base and so many of us don't do enough of this we don't really map out the roadmaps with our top customers and we should because it's magic and again if you don't like doing it try to find a way because this is something everyone wants to see their vendor succeed right no one wants to feel like they're wasting their", "balanced": "hey everybody i want to do this video on six ways you can keep your angry customers and maybe that's not exactly what you're thinking but we all have them right so let's step back for a minute think about how many customers are really truly happy and it's almost none um if they're even just okay with their vendor then there's some part of their life where they feel like they've been ripped off or taken advantage of or charged too much so everyone is angry at least partially especially as startups grow beyond two three four five million in revenue because you'll start to see churn go up a little bit more than expected and net negative churn won't be quite as high as you'd hope now look these days it may be worse it may seem harder to get away from folks but it wasn't always true until recently when sas exploded into every industry every type of business there was only so many vendors that could buy so many solutions and if you were one of those vendors and your solution was better than another competitor's solution well the buyers would choose yours over the other guy right and if you provided good value people would stay forever that meant the ceo and founder would stay forever but nowadays there are so many competitors out there no matter what category you're in and that means churn has gone way up because people don't expect perfection anymore and yet we still need our products right so we're gonna give you six ideas on keeping your angry customers and i'm going to share with you my top six because these are things i did again and again and again when i was running adobe sign echo signs uh in its early years it was called e-signature and it had a lot of challenges in the early days", "r32": "that's really what we're looking for is that you know what i would like it if you could do this and i don't think your team even has to be good at it but just listen if they can get their customers happy again they will buy more from you now look the first time an account goes down or something doesn't work out okay you'll lose them because there's too much friction right so let's not say that we're going to turn around angry customers but second chance customers yes sometimes yes no matter how mad our customer was on march 15th 20th they may still want to come back after 60 days if if you've done everything you can to help them but if you haven't helped them if you haven't helped them enough then there's nothing better than magic uh a second chance um and finally two other things when you have unhappy customers remember the best way to deal with them is face-to-face in person if possible okay don't wait until someone complains on twitter or slack or zoom or whatever don't wait to hear about issues don't wait until you see a big dip in nps go visit those folks in person okay and also remember that the ceo should meet every single angry customer in person okay and make sure everyone knows that the co does this it's pretty magical um especially if you're early stage and as soon as the company gets bigger it becomes harder but at least start off by having the coos meeting any customer that comes into the office that complains on email or wherever else and if it makes you uncomfortable well maybe it means it's true but try to do these meetings in person and try to go in person whenever you can to solve problems", "repaired": "what do you mean we don't have any of these features and I say well look it's tough for us today but let me tell you what our roadmap is and if that doesn't matter why not if it matters to them they're going to go on your website they're going to see what other customers are saying even if there aren't many customers yet so be as transparent as possible on your roadmap or at least on where your priorities lie next quarter next month and if you can't answer this question yourself then maybe just make up some answers until you get better um point four which again will sound obvious but a lot of founders forget especially when they haven't done it before is listen take complaints seriously because if you don't you'll lose those customers forever complainants want to know that someone hears their complaint and cares it really works okay last two points number five and this one is related to the first three if you have an issue with a customer and they reach out to support or whomever whatever email address whoever the contact form whatever if it's if it's a big deal to them respond immediately not only does it calm things down which is great but also think about it from the perspective of the startup as soon as something goes wrong it becomes much more stressful than you would imagine and so when a customer complains or emails or contacts sales or support do you know how long it takes to respond sometimes weeks and it creates stress in the team now multiply that by 10.000 customers and it could be disastrous but respond instantly to every complaint um and I've never seen this work poorly I've always seen it work well because if you listen to what people are saying if you listen to their complaints most of them are not angry per se"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 618, "lemkin-clone-v5": 303, "lemkin-clone-v6": 465, "cleaned": 309, "balanced": 319, "r32": 309, "repaired": 325}}}}, {"id": "gold_019", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Give stage-appropriate advice on when a startup really needs a CMO.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that founders usually need demand generation, product marketing, or content help before they need a true CMO. The CMO hire works when there is already enough scale, budget, and strategic complexity to support a full executive owner. Hiring one too early often creates expensive confusion instead of leverage.", "facts": ["The answer should say that many startups hire a CMO too early.", "It should explain that the role only works once there is enough repeatability and scope to justify it.", "It should distinguish between tactical marketing needs and true CMO-level leadership."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr:**\n\nI get this question all the time. \"Jason, when should I hire a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO) for my startup?\"\n\nMy answer is always the same: **it depends on your growth stage and budget**.\n\nIn this post, I'll break down the different stages of growth where you might need to consider hiring a CMO - or not. But first, let's establish one thing:\n\nA good CMO can be worth their weight in gold... but only if they're the right person for the job at the right time.\n\n**Pre-Revenue (or Early Revenue) Startups:**\n\nAt this stage, you need to focus on **building a product that people love**. Don't waste your resources on \"marketing\" - it's just not that important yet. Hire a few salespeople and get them working with early customers to build momentum.\n\nIf you're lucky (or good at marketing), word-of-mouth might even take off without much effort from you. But let's be real: most startups don't have the luxury of relying on organic growth alone.\n\n**Seed Stage Startups:**\n\nAt this stage, your focus should shift towards **building a team and validating product-market fit**. You'll need to invest in sales teams, customer success managers, and other functions that can help drive revenue growth.\n\nWhile it's not necessarily time to hire a CMO yet, you might consider bringing on a **Head of Growth or Marketing Operations person**, who can help you build out your marketing infrastructure and automate some processes. This role will be responsible for setting up the foundation for future marketing efforts - but they won't be driving top-line growth just yet.\n\n**Series A Startups:**\n\nThis is where things get interesting. You've now got a solid product-market fit, and revenue is growing (albeit slowly). It's time to think about **scaling your business**, which includes building out a robust marketing function.\n\nAt this stage, I'd recommend bringing on a CMO who can help you:\n\n* Define your brand identity\n* Develop a go-to-market strategy that aligns with your sales and customer success efforts\n* Build out the rest of your marketing org (think: content marketers, social media specialists, etc.)\n\nYour CMO should be responsible for driving top-line growth through creative campaigns, partnerships, and other innovative initiatives.\n\n**Scaling Growth Stage Startups:**\n\nIf you're already a successful startup with millions in revenue, congratulations! Now it's time to **take marketing to the next level**. Your CMO will need to focus on:\n\n* Measuring and optimizing every dollar spent\n* Developing data-driven growth strategies that align with your overall business goals\n* Building out specialized teams (e.g., content studios, creative agencies)\n\nIn this stage, your CMO should be responsible for driving 20-30%+ revenue growth per quarter. Anything less is unacceptable.\n\n**The Bottom Line:**\n\nWhen to hire a CMO depends on the specific needs of your startup at each growth stage. Don't waste resources on someone who isn't qualified or can't deliver results - and don't miss out on opportunities for top-line growth because you're too afraid to bring in outside expertise.\n\nYour CMO should be an **extension** of your sales team, not a separate entity with its own agenda. They'll help drive revenue growth through creative campaigns, partnerships, and innovative initiatives that align with your overall business goals.\n\nSo the next time someone asks when they should hire a CMO, I hope this post helps them understand the answer: it's when you need to **scale** - not just build out some marketing org for its own sake.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should a startup hire a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO)?\nFirst, I actually don’t think you need to hire a CMO until $10m-$15m ARR.\nI’ve never seen one add much value at earlier stages. They will often just be an expensive addition to the team, and not really add that much in terms of revenue or product marketing. Not until later.\nBut even then, it’s still optional.\nIt can take a while for a CMO to get up to speed on your market, so make sure you have some great VPs under them before you hire one. I.e., at least 1 VP Marketing that is super smart and has done this before.\nSo, the real question:\nWhen should a startup hire a Chief Revenue Officer (CRO)?\nThe answer: When it’s working OK, but not quite as well as you need for growth.\nAnd when you have enough revenue to make it worth your while. That’s usually around $10m-$15m ARR or so in many cases of startups I’ve worked with, and sometimes earlier if the sales team is already scaling quickly. And … a CMO doesn’t really do any more than that until later anyway.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: When Should You Hire Your First VP?\nWhat Stage of Startup Is Best for Hiring a COO? Dear SaaStr: What Are Some Examples Of \"Good\" Marketing Programs to Implement in My Early-Stage Company?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a Chief Marketing Officer?\nThis is really an answer for many roles, but it’s especially true for marketing.\nIn some cases you don’t need to hire at all.  A great VP of Marketing can do everything herself — from brand and demand gen to product marketing and even sales enablement (a lot).\nBut if your VPM isn’t doing this well on her own … then the job is too hard, or the company is too small.\nSo don’t wait for a CMO.  Hire a VP of Marketing instead.  And then only bring in a Chief Marketing Officer later — when things are going very well and she’s got enough resources to do even more with it.\nYou can see this dynamic play out if you talk to most top VPs of Marketing today, from Bill McDermott at ServiceNow to Maggie Chan Day at Salesforce.  They all started as VPMs themselves.\nIn the early days, they did it all — or at least enough of it that their company could scale quickly and successfully.\nLater … well later is when you go big time with a CMO!\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: What Does an Average VP of Marketing Do?\nThe $1 Billion+ ARR Club\n5 Interesting Learnings from GitLab's CEO on Scaling to 500 Employees", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO)? The answer depends on your stage, growth trajectory, and marketing maturity. Here’s how I’d approach it: Pre-Product-Market Fit ($0-$1M ARR) : You don’t need a CMO yet. Focus instead on hiring individual contributors who can help you acquire customers. These could be a Growth Marketer or even an SMB Sales Rep who understands inbound and outbound tactics. If you’re bootstrapped with no sales team, hire someone to manage all acquisition channels—paid ads, content, events, PR. Post-Pilot but Before Product-Led Growth Takes Off ($1-$2M ARR) : This is where things get interesting. As you hit Initial Traction ($1M ARR), you’ll likely have some early success stories from word-of-mouth, referrals, and organic search. Now you need someone who can scale those wins while adding structured marketing muscle. Look for a VP of Demand Gen or Head of Growth who has experience scaling at this stage. They should own paid acquisition, SEO/organic growth, partnerships, and lead generation while working closely with sales to refine their motion. But here’s the key insight: they shouldn’t try to build brand awareness too aggressively before $10M ARR. Brand doesn’t matter when you’re still fighting for market share—it matters after you’ve established yourself as a leader. A great VP of Growth can do both without getting distracted by shiny branding initiatives that won’t pay off until later. When to Finally Hire Your First Real CMO ($5-$20M ARR) : Once you reach Series B funding ($15-25M+ ARR), if you haven’t already, bring in a real CMO. Not just another demand gen expert—but a true CMO who understands how to build brand, drive category creation, and position your company for enterprise expansion.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? There’s no one-size-fits-all answer to this question, but here are some general guidelines based on what I’ve seen work well at startups. Early Stage (Pre-$10M ARR) : If you’re under $5M ARR and still figuring out your market positioning, don’t hire a CMO yet. Instead, focus on hiring a VP of Marketing or even just an individual contributor marketer who can help with content creation, lead generation, and early demand gen efforts. They should be able to handle most marketing tasks up to around $1-2M ARR before needing more specialized support. Around $1-2M ARR : By this point, you’ll need someone who can scale your marketing efforts as you start growing faster. This is when you might hire a Director of Demand Gen or Growth who reports directly to you, the CEO. Their job will be to create repeatable processes for generating leads and scaling your growth engine. Post-$10M ARR (Scaling Phase) : Once you hit $20-30M+ ARR, it’s time to bring in a full-time Chief Marketing Officer (CMO). A great CMO should have experience building go-to-market strategies that drive efficiency, scalability, and customer acquisition across all channels—whether it’s digital marketing, events, field sales, or product-led growth. Key characteristics of a successful CMO at this stage include: Deep industry expertise in your vertical Strong leadership skills Ability to scale teams and processes Experience managing complex marketing funnels And they should report directly to you, not any other executive unless you’re really big.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should you hire a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO)? The answer isn’t as simple as “hire one at $10M ARR,” but it’s close. Let me break down when to make this critical hiring decision based on my experience and data from thousands of B2B companies. Early-Stage (Pre-$1M ARR): Don’t Do It Yet You’re probably not ready for a full-time CMO, especially if you don’t have a strong marketing foundation yet. Most early-stage startups I’ve worked with get by just fine without one—they focus on product-market fit, customer acquisition, and building their brand through word-of-mouth, content creation, and grassroots efforts. When you hit $500K-$750K ARR : Start Building Foundations This is where you start thinking about scaling your growth engine. At this point, you need someone who can help you refine your positioning, build awareness, and create lead generation strategies that actually work. But remember—this person doesn’t have to be a traditional CMO. A VP of Demand Gen or Growth Marketer might be perfect here. They’ll help you improve conversion rates, optimize your funnel, and experiment with new channels. Mid-Scale ($5M-$20M ARR) : Bring In Your First Real CMO Now we’re talking. With consistent revenue coming in, you can afford to invest in a true strategic leader who will own all aspects of marketing—including demand gen, field marketing, corporate communications, and branding. This executive will help you scale your message across multiple touchpoints and develop programs that drive both top-line growth and efficiency.", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should you hire a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO)? The answer is more nuanced than most people think. Most VCs and other execs will tell you to wait until $20m ARR at least before hiring your first true VP-level marketer, let alone CMO.\nThe Classic Answer:\nHire your first “real” VP of Marketing around $20M-$30M in ARR or so — when marketing can finally be an expense that actually creates real revenue\n. And not just a cost center. This makes sense from an efficiency perspective—you want marketing’s impact on revenue growth to clearly outweigh its costs at this stage. Also, because there are enough customers to split up into segments with dedicated marketers for each segment (e.g., SMB vs enterprise, self-serve vs sales-led). But here’s where it gets interesting:\nWhy You Need Help Earlier Than That\nYou might need help even earlier if you’re doing any paid acquisition.  If nothing else, you’ll probably have someone helping you manage agencies, partnerships, etc. by then.\nMost founders I’ve worked with who haven’t hired their first full-time marketer before $10M ARR regret it later. They end up spending countless hours managing campaigns they don’t fully understand, analyzing data without context, and trying to build demand generation engines while also running content marketing, events, and brand strategy. It’s exhausting and distracting—especially as the company scales beyond 100 employees. Hiring a VP of Marketing too late can lead to missed opportunities:\nRevenue optimization : Without someone focused specifically on scaling your existing channels, you’re leaving money on the table. A good marketer would identify areas where incremental spend could drive disproportionate returns."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 575, "lemkin-clone-v5": 235, "lemkin-clone-v6": 223, "cleaned": 286, "balanced": 253, "r32": 246, "repaired": 272}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**When to Bring on That 6-Figure Marketing Rockstar (CMO)**\n\nHey fellow startup founders!\n\nAs many of you know, I'm often asked when is the right time for a B2B SaaS company to bring in that big-hitting Chief Marketing Officer (CMO).\n\nWell, today's your lucky day! After years of observing and advising numerous startups on this very topic, I've distilled it down to 3 key criteria.\n\n**1. You're at $5M+ ARR**\nIf you haven't hit $5 million in annual recurring revenue yet, hold off on the CMO hire for now. Why? At this stage, your primary focus should be building a product-market fit and scaling your go-to-market engine (which often means bootstrapping some of these efforts).\n\n**2. Your Sales Team is Saturated**\nIf you've got an amazing sales team crushing it at 20-30% conversion rates or better... congratulations! But before hiring that $200K+ CMO, ensure they're truly maxed out on new business opportunities.\n\nWhy? A CMO's primary role is to expand your marketing reach and generate more leads. If your sales team isn't the bottleneck anymore, it may be premature to bring in a high-cost executive who needs to scale the entire marketing function.\n\n**3. You've Got a Clear \"Marketing Flywheel\"**\nAt this stage ( typically $10M+ ARR), you should have an established product-market fit and a solid understanding of your ideal customer profile. Before hiring that CMO, ensure you can", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nA great question.\n\nAt best, a VP Marketing is 2-3 years ahead. Not always easy to see that far in the future, but if you can:\n\nThat means at least $5m ARR or so before you should hire your first real VP of Marketing.\n\nEven earlier, sometimes. But I find it’s rare until $5m-$10m ARR to make sense.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nWhen you have $2m ARR and 80% net revenue retention, it's time to hire your first VP of Marketing.\n\nNot earlier. Not later.\n\nAt that stage:\n\nYou've scaled from 1-10 employees\nYour market is defined and competitive\nCustomer acquisition costs are optimized\n\nThat's when a seasoned CMO can make real impact on growth.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? Hiring a Chief Marketing Officer (CMO) is one of those big decisions where timing makes all the difference. You want to hire when you need someone who can own and scale marketing, but not before. Too early, and they’ll be doing tasks that are still within your reach as founder or VP of Demand Gen. Too late, and competitors will start pulling ahead while you’re scrambling to catch up. Here’s how I’d break it down: **Pre-Product-Market Fit ($0-$2M ARR)** Don’t hire a CMO until you’ve nailed product-market fit. Before then, you don’t have enough revenue or market understanding to justify their role. Instead, focus on building a scrappy demand gen team with people wearing multiple hats. Once you hit $1M+ ARR and see clear signs of stickiness—like net negative churn or consistent month-over-month growth—you’re ready for someone who can help scale. **Post-Product-Market Fit ($2M-$10M ARR)** This is usually around Year 3–Year 5 depending on velocity. When you cross $2M ARR with solid unit economics, it’s time to bring in a real CMO. They should focus on three core areas: brand differentiation, customer acquisition at scale, and lead conversion optimization. Look for someone who’s built marketing playbooks from scratch before and has experience scaling teams without breaking them. **The Test:** If you can’t clearly articulate why this person would make a material improvement to your growth", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? When you are ready to scale, and really need someone that can help you do it. Until then, your VP of Marketing is probably going to be doing many different things — from demand gen to content marketing to events and more. A true Chief Marketing Officer will focus on brand building and corporate positioning as well. But until you have something big to market … there’s no one for them to market to yet. More here: The Top 10 Mistakes Founders Make Hiring Their First VPs (And How to Do It Right) | SaaStr https://saastr.com/the-top-10-mistakes-founders-make-hiring-their-first-vps-and-how-to-do-it-right/ …", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? There are two types of CMOs at startups: The “Brand” CMO. They start off by building your brand, but it often takes time to scale up pipeline from that effort alone. This is fine if you have a lot of capital and want to grow slowly, or if you’re already very strong on marketing (e.g., Hubspot). But these days, most B2B companies don’t really need this type of marketer until they are well past $10m-$20m ARR. The “Growth” CMO. These marketers come in and quickly build out their team with all sorts of demand gen activities — paid advertising, SEO, content, events, etc. So which one do you hire first? I think there’s only one answer here. You hire the Growth CMO as soon as you can afford her, i.e. when she has budget to spend … because she’ll immediately generate revenue for you, almost instantly. A great Growth CMO will usually be able to increase leads by 25%+ within weeks. And close more deals than any other resource you’ve hired before. So how early should you bring in a CMO? In many cases, the earlier the better. We had a customer sign-up for our ad program just last week that was doing $1M in ARR. We asked them why now. Well, they wanted to support us, but also needed help getting found online and driving more customers in. If they’d waited another year or so, they", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? I’ve seen this hiring decision go wrong many times. 🚨The #1 mistake is waiting too long🚨. You don’t need a fancy marketer at $10K MRR, but by the time you’re approaching $100M ARR or even earlier, having someone who can own brand and growth strategy becomes critical. By that point, your marketing org should be well established with multiple channels driving pipeline. The Chief Marketing Officer (CMO) role isn’t just about demand gen—it’s also about building the foundation for sustainable growth over years, not quarters. They’ll help you scale from tens to hundreds of millions in revenue while maintaining a consistent customer experience across all touchpoints. Without them, you risk losing momentum as you grow beyond initial product-market fit. A great CMO will guide you through the transition from startup to scaled enterprise play. 👉But here’s what most founders miss: they hire a CMO way too early when it doesn't really work yet. Way too early. When you're still figuring out your core value prop and messaging, you don’t have enough data to support sophisticated campaigns. And if they aren’t hands-on and experienced, they end up being expensive advisors rather than drivers of growth. So how do you know when the timing is right? ➡️ Wait until you have a repeatable sales motion and real traction ($5-20M+ ARR). By then, you have enough signals to justify investing heavily in both inbound and outbound efforts. If you haven’t"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 230, "lemkin-clone-v5": 69, "lemkin-clone-v6": 64, "cleaned": 236, "balanced": 108, "r32": 248, "repaired": 248}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"At what stage should a startup hire a CMO?\n\nI get asked this all the time. And my answer is... way too early, for most startups.\n\nHere are 3 reasons why:\n\n **1) Cost**: A good CMO will cost you $200k-$500k per year. Do you really need to invest that kind of money in marketing when your company still has no idea if it'll even scale?\n\n**2) Complexity**: Most early-stage companies don't have the org chart or systems complexity to support a full-time marketer. You're better off with a part-timer, freelancer, or agency.\n\n**3) Lack of clear goals**: What's the CMO going to accomplish in those first", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr:  At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nI have been a marketing professional for 12 years and I am looking to join a start-up in the B2B space as Chief Marketing Officer. My question is:\n\nHow do you know when it’s time to hire a full-time Chief Marketing Officer (CMO)?\n\nWhen we were at $0m-$1m ARR, we didn’t have one.  We did it all ourselves.\n\nAt about 3m in ARR, we hired our first VP of Marketing. She was great!\n\nBut at about 10m in ARR, the role evolved into a CMO and then eventually, just another part-time VP of Marketing (we had to make some cost cuts).\n\nI don’t know that you really need", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nThere's no one answer, but here are 3 rules I've learned:\n\nFirst, wait until you have at least $1m in ARR.\n\nSecond, don't hire a true VP of Marketing unless it is your #1 job to do so. And most importantly,\n\nThird, make sure the person you bring in can handle this role themselves for a while — and ideally even 5-7 years out.\n\nIt's too much risk if they haven't been there before.", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? It depends on your ARR and growth rate, but generally I’d say by $20m ARR at least. A bit more here: https://saastr.com/when-to-hire-a-cmo-in-saas-and-how-to-do-it-right/ …", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO? I think most founders get this wrong. They hire a “CMO” as soon as they hit $10m ARR, or even earlier, and … it just doesn’t work out that well. A true CMO isn’t there to do demand gen campaigns, manage your website, etc. That’s someone else. The real job of a CMO is to make you more money — at scale. It takes years for any marketing program really to compound its revenue impact. So what should a founder do instead? First, try to hire a VP Marketing before you need one. In fact, if nothing else, hire a Director of Demand Gen first. That’s usually the easiest hire after a VP Sales. And have", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?\n\nIt’s OK if you’re not sure when to hire your first VP of Marketing. Here are some guidelines:\n\n1.\nIf You Don’t Have One Yet, Maybe You Need To Start With A Head of Demand Gen First\n.\n\nMany founders start with head of demand gen (or marketing ops) before hiring a full-time CMO. They can help with lead generation and revenue growth while you figure out what kind of marketer you need for the next phase. This role focuses on generating pipeline and managing existing leads, which is critical as you scale from $0 to $10M ARR.\n\n2.\nFor SMBs, The Chief Growth Officer May Be Better Than Your Traditional CMO\n.\n\nSMB-focused companies often find success"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 117, "lemkin-clone-v5": 119, "lemkin-clone-v6": 85, "cleaned": 11, "balanced": 34, "r32": 127, "repaired": 129}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: At What Stage Should a Startup Hire a CMO?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr:\n\n**Slide 1: Title Slide**\n\n\"Dear SaaStr: When to Hire Your First Chief Marketing Officer\"\n\nHey everyone! Today we're going to tackle one of the most common questions I get from founders. You know, when should you hire your first Chief Marketing Officer? Now, I've been around the block a few times - I co-founded EchoSign, which was acquired by Adobe for $110 million in 2011. Since then, I've had the chance to work with dozens of SaaS companies as an investor and advisor.\n\n**Slide 2: The Cost of Hiring Too Early**\n\nSo, when should you hire your first CMO? Well, let me tell you - hiring a marketing leader too early can be a recipe for disaster. You'll spend tens or even hundreds of thousands of dollars on salary, benefits, and other perks... only to realize that they're not producing the results you need.\n\n**Slide 3: Typical Marketing Hurdles at Different Stages**\n\nAt $1 million in ARR (annual recurring revenue), your biggest marketing challenge is usually just getting traction. Your website might be a mess, you've got no content strategy, and your social media presence is nonexistent.\n\nBut as you scale to $10 million or more in ARR, the challenges shift. Now it's about sustaining that growth - building out a team of high-performing marketers who can amplify your message, optimize your funnel, and drive conversions at scale.\n\n**Slide 4: Signs It's Time for Your First CMO**\n\nSo when do you know it's time to hire? Here are some signs:\n\n* You've got a clear product-market fit and a growing customer base.\n* Your marketing efforts have plateaued or aren't producing the ROI (return on investment) you need.\n* You're struggling to scale your go-to-market strategy - whether that means content, demand generation, or channel partnerships.\n\n**Slide 5: What Does a Great First CMO Look Like?**\n\nNow, when it comes to hiring that first marketing leader, what do you want them to bring to the table? Here are some key qualities:\n\n* Proven experience in SaaS (or adjacent industries).\n* A clear understanding of your company's unique value proposition.\n* Expertise in strategy, execution, and measurement - not just one or two areas.\n\n**Slide 6: How to Structure Your First CMO Role**\n\nOkay, so you've found the right person. Now what? Here are some tips on structuring that first role:\n\n* Give them ownership of a specific area (e.g., demand gen) with clear metrics for success.\n* Set aside dedicated budget and resources for experimentation - whether it's new channels or technologies.\n* Make sure they have regular feedback loops from both the sales team and the product org.\n\n**Slide 7: Conclusion**\n\nIn conclusion, hiring your first CMO is a critical milestone in any SaaS company's journey. But don't do it too early! Wait until you've got traction, are struggling to scale, and need someone who can bring strategic guidance, executional expertise, and measurement skills to the table.\n\nThanks for joining me today - I hope this talk has given you some valuable insights on when and how to hire that first marketing leader. And as always... keep pushing forward!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I'd say hire one before you need one. That is, when your marketing team needs to be more professional and organized than it currently is. The time is not always clear for every founder but here's the real question:\n\nDo you want to be the CEO of 1000+ customers or just a few? If only a handful of prospects are interested in buying from you today, that means your product-market fit isn't quite there yet and your marketing efforts won't work very well. So then what? Then it is time for a CMO.\n\nThe CMO role should be to help the CEO drive growth through all parts of the business, not just inbound leads, but customer acquisition more broadly — outbound sales, partnerships, product-led growth, etc.\n\nBut if you want to get there faster and build something truly special that can scale at $10m+ ARR or even beyond 1000 customers … then hire one now.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So at what stage should I hire my first chief marketing officer or cmo?\n\nThe answer is not simple, but here are some guidelines.\n\nFirst off, if you're pre-product market fit and you can't figure out how to get more leads into the funnel, just add a VP of Marketing. And let them be an owner in the sales process at least until they have a team under her or him. You may need 4-5 VPs before that.\n\nBut once you've got product-market fit, then it's time for your first CMO. Not only will they get leads into the funnel better, but they'll also make sure those leads are properly qualified and handed off to sales as efficiently as possible.\n\nIf they're working well with Sales, a good VP Marketing can generate $10m in revenue for you once she or he has 1-2 VPs under her. That means a $20M ARR company, at least.\n\nAnd if it's more than that? Well then I'd say go hire your first CMO!\n\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #100: The Top 5 Pieces of Advice from SaaStr CEO and Founder Jason Lemkin\nThe \"New\" New in Venture Capital with Jason Lemkin + Harry Stebbings (Video)\nDear SaaStr: How Do You Motivate a Team When Your Startup is Struggling?", "cleaned": "Dear Saster, at what stage should a startup hire a chief marketing officer (CMO)? As soon as you have enough money to pay for it. That's the answer. And if you've raised venture capital or gotten there on your own with sales driven revenue, then ideally by $2 million in ARR. Maybe earlier if you're hypergrowth. It really depends how quickly you can scale because this is going to be an expensive role and most VPs of Marketing that are good are expecting to own up to two directors under them and they want a team. They don't just want to do blog posts and tweets themselves. So probably around two million error. You know maybe even earlier than that. But here's my only challenge back to folks listening, which I think sometimes gets missed, especially founders who haven't done this before. When you finally get to the point where you need a VP of marketing, when you have a little bit of extra capital, but not too much, it's stressful. Okay, now you're like, \"Okay, we got enough money.\" Now, you finally could bring someone in. But boy, if this doesn't work out, it'll ruin everything. Right? Because you finally feel you have time to recruit somebody great and put together a real team. Like, you're feeling pretty good about things. You've got some traction, right? Then you blow all your limited resources hiring this wrong person. This has happened so many times. Too many times. Don't let it happen to you. The best candidates will tell you, \"I'm happy to wait until August or September.\" If you're ready today. Not every candidate is perfect. But the ones that are truly looking to make the jump from director or head of marketing to CMO or VP of marketing.", "balanced": "you you'll want to hire that first head of marketing as soon as your revenue is bigger than your burn rate because if nothing else they can help get you more leads and you don't need them before then but even at one million two three four five million Revenue it's so hard for most Founders to find that magical VP of Marketing um I've invested in 10 unicorns now I've seen this time and time again when the Founder finds someone great it changes everything right but it's just so hard to do until then maybe focus on getting better and better at demand gen making sure every lead counts doing webinars talking with customers trying to make each other happier and not too much marketing strategy per se but if you have any money left after you hit initial traction spend it on a marketer", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: At what stage should I hire my first marketing manager or director, and how much does it cost to have one on staff? The answer is different than other roles. You don't need an entire VP Marketing team until you are at $10m-$20m ARR. But you do want someone great to own your brand as early as possible — ideally around say $2m-$3m in ARR. If not earlier. Why? Because once you've gotten past the first customers … word-of-mouth becomes key. And that's where branding comes into play. So if you can afford it, try to find someone amazing to be your \"CMO\" before $1M in revenue. Even just part-time. It may sound like a lot, but it will pay for itself if they're truly great. As soon as you can afford it, get them 25% ownership in equity. That way, they'll care. And know their job isn't done when you hit Initial Traction (your initial cohort of customers). More here: How To Build a Marketing Team in SaaS | SaaStr Now, this person doesn’t have to necessarily call themselves a “CMO”. They might be a demand gen expert, a field marketer, etc. Just make sure she/he/they are experienced enough to help you scale and build out your marketing function over time. They’ll also have to understand that the #1 thing you need from them initially is leads and customer count growth. Not magic numbers and ROI. A bit more here: Your First Great VPS / CXO Hire May Have to Be a Stretch VPS / CXO Hire. Is That OK? And remember: a mediocre CMO is worse than no CMO at all. Because they'll distract you and burn too many resources trying to do things that won't work.", "repaired": "hey everybody i know we've talked so much on saastr and other places over the years about when to hire what VP but there's one role that I don't think enough Founders have really thought through which is how do you go from founder led marketing or whatever it might be pre-founder marketing sales product everything all these different roles you're going to wear them all as Founder until you can afford VPS but how do you make this transition especially at scale because if you look at almost every public company today most of their Revenue comes from word-of-mouth referrals customers second-order revenue existing customers growing faster than new customers and so forth and so folks like HubSpot who are doing hundreds of millions in AR grow their customer base less than 20% each year even though they're adding many more customers but those existing customers just buy more and more products and so you need someone owning all your growth strategy from five million to ten million to twenty million to thirty million to hundred million and beyond and for far too long founders end up being the Chief Marketing Officer well into the first few years and then it becomes hard and here's why once you start hiring a real team around you they will not understand what you want them to do and how you think about things no matter what your strengths are whether it's brand positioning messaging content channels events partnerships and field marketing whoever joins you after you as a CEO they won't speak your language they'll do what they did last time and so they won't execute your vision and instead they'll try to impose their own vision onto what you should do with marketing and that's not good enough you need a partner not an executive that has your back that understands what worked before and can take it to the next level"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 536, "lemkin-clone-v5": 155, "lemkin-clone-v6": 212, "cleaned": 299, "balanced": 147, "r32": 291, "repaired": 322}}}}, {"id": "gold_020", "topic": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Give honest career advice about when leaving a struggling startup is reasonable.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that leaving a struggling startup is not automatically bad. What matters is whether the company is still a good place to learn, whether leadership is honest, and whether there is a believable path forward. The right advice is to separate temporary startup pain from a structurally unhealthy situation.", "facts": ["The answer should avoid moralizing about leaving.", "It should explain that context matters: learning, integrity, runway, and role fit.", "It should distinguish between normal startup hardship and a genuinely broken environment."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Dear SaaStr,**\n\nI get this question all the time. It's one that I've wrestled with myself, and many of my fellow entrepreneurs have grappled with as well.\n\n\"Is it bad to leave a struggling startup?\"\n\nLet me tell you - there is no one-size-fits-all answer here. But what I will do is share some hard-won wisdom from years of experience building (and sometimes destroying) startups myself.\n\n**It's Not About Being \"Loyal\"**\n\nFirst, let me dispel the myth that leaving a struggling startup is somehow disloyal or ungrateful. Newsflash: loyalty doesn't mean staying at a company where you're not making progress. In fact, being loyal to yourself and your career should always come first.\n\nThink about it - if you're stuck in a role that's sucking the life out of you, are you really doing anyone any favors by sticking around? I didn't think so.\n\n**The \"But What About My Team?\" Question**\n\nNow, some of you might be thinking: \"But Jason, what about my team? If I leave, they'll have to pick up the pieces.\" Well, here's a secret:\n\nGood teams will figure it out. Great founders know when to hold 'em and fold 'em.\n\nIf your company is truly struggling (and not just you), then someone needs to take responsibility for making tough decisions - and that might mean bringing in new blood or pivoting entirely.\n\n**When It Makes Sense To Leave**\n\nSo, how do you know if it's time to leave a struggling startup?\n\nHere are some telltale signs:\n\n* You're consistently unhappy at work.\n* Your role has been stagnant for months (or years).\n* The company is hemorrhaging cash and there's no clear path forward.\n\nIf any of these apply to your situation, trust me: you owe it to yourself to explore other opportunities. It's okay to take a step back, regroup, and recharge before deciding what's next.\n\n**What To Do Next**\n\nSo, if you've decided that leaving is the right move for you, here are some next steps:\n\n1. **Be honest with your colleagues**: Let them know why you're leaving (without burning bridges).\n2. **Offer to help transition**: Show that you care about the company's future and want to make it easier on those who'll be staying behind.\n3. **Take time for yourself**: Don't jump into another startup right away. Take some space, reflect on what went wrong, and recharge.\n\nIn closing: leaving a struggling startup isn't always easy - but sometimes it's necessary. Remember that your own well-being should come first, and don't be afraid to take control of your career.\n\n**What do you think? Have any thoughts or questions about this topic? Share them in the comments below!**\n\nAnd if you're looking for more advice on startup life (or just want to commiserate with fellow founders), hit me up on Twitter - I'm always here to chat.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\nIt is not that simple. And honestly, if you are at all a bit burned out … just leave.\nYou can’t fix the company from there. No one else will believe in the mission or vision anymore (if they ever did). You’ll never be able to hire anyone great again. It’s time for everyone to move on. In fact, it may well be best if you stay a little longer and help others transition out.\nIt’s better than burning out yourself\nAnd honestly, just getting back to working at a place that is even slightly less stressed can make you almost instantly more effective. At least give it a try.\nRelated Posts\nStruggling founders: Why do VCs keep investing in you?\nDear SaaStr: How Can I Get Involved with Startups? Dear SaaStr: What’s the Difference Between VC-Backed and Bootstrapped Startup?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\nIt’s not bad. It can be good.\nBut let me tell you, I have seen so many folks who leave too early — and sometimes even when they are at the top of their game.  When things seem like they’re going well.\nThey often regret that move in the end:\nSo why? Why do people stay longer than it makes sense?\nI think a lot of founders don’t want to be seen as “quitters.”\nBut I just saw another SaaS founder sell his company for $100m+ … and he had never raised any external funding. He didn’t need anyone’s money.\nSo he was able to do what ever the hell he wanted, when ever the heck he wanted.\nAnd it worked out incredibly well.\nThat can be very empowering.\nBut there are limits on that approach too:\nYou don’t have the team, and you don’t have the marketing machine.  And in many cases, no one else will join a “no external funding” startup as an employee — because they know it’s going to fail at some point.\nSo while staying longer than necessary can be hard for your ego … it often isn’t a good idea. It doesn’t always lead to more success than leaving.\nRelated Posts\nSaaStr #1 on Product Hunt!\nTop SaaStr Posts + Videos of the Week! Top SaaStr Posts and Vids of the Week!!", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup? It depends. But I would ask yourself one question before you quit: What’s going on, and can something be done? If nothing is really wrong that can’t be fixed … stay for now at least. You’re part of a team. No matter how rough things may seem, no matter if your job might be eliminated next week, etc., — remember folks put their careers here. Their trust, time, energy, etc. So if it isn’t quite hopeless yet (and maybe it never is) — try to help fix some things. You may just find a way to do so, or at least make progress. The best sales teams always close more deals than they start with. Even when times are tough, if you have any happy customers at all … you can build on them. And add new ones. It only takes 1 great hire each month to grow faster. At least, this will give you a shot. More here. Your boss doesn’t know what he/she does either. It’s not fair but true. They’ve also probably been through tougher times before. Maybe even worse. The competition isn’t doing as well as you think. Don’t get me wrong, the competition often seems better funded these days. But sometimes, their product is actually much less powerful, less slick, less elegant than yours. There’s almost always another angle where you win. A lot of companies come out of nowhere and thrive. Sometimes from an unexpected place. For example, I was recently talking with the CEO of a $100m ARR company that has rocketed past Snowflake and others in its category since Covid hit. In fact, we were talking about how they had passed Salesforce in many ways. He started off running a small, scrappy team at Box.", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup? No, leaving a failing start-up is not “bad.” It’s OK. Why Not? First, if you’re making zero or even little money — that doesn’t help anyone. If you stay and make $0 a month at a start-up with no runway left … then who does that really benefit? Second, the team often knows. They know when they can’t do any better than they are doing now. Third, folks get frustrated all the time. We’ve talked here before on how founders shouldn’t quit. But don’t feel like you have to go down with the ship. You might be able to save yourself from a lot more pain by moving on as early as possible. Fourth, there may always be another role for you later. This isn’t always true, but sometimes, your boss just needs someone great to carry some load off their plate. Fifth, you can take the lessons you learn with you. In fact, I bet you’ll see patterns across multiple start-ups where you work (and fail). That will make you an even better founder yourself someday. And finally, remember most folks just want to hit their career goals. Don’t let a tough spot stop you from hitting them. A related post here :", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup? No. Leaving a company that’s not working out is rarely “bad”. It’s just a fact of life. And one you should only be ashamed of if … You had zero good reason at all. Even then, maybe don’t be too hard on yourself. 🙂 More on that here. But you’ll make an even bigger mistake by staying when your heart isn’t really in it anymore. And sometimes, that’s especially true with a start-up struggling to scale past $1m-$2m ARR or so. Because they often need more than ever now for everyone to stay engaged. The best folks want to see things get better. They want their startups to go from Good To Great. Not worse. If you are no longer confident that will happen under your CEO and management team — perhaps it’s time to move on. A related post here : (note: an updated SaaStr Classic answer)", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: I’m at a struggling startup that’s lost its way. The team is great, but there’s no clear direction and things aren’t getting better. I’ve been here almost two years now, and I’m starting to feel stuck. Should I leave or stay?\nIt depends on your priorities:\nIf you’re in it for the long haul and want to build something from scratch\n: Staying makes sense. You’ll get more equity, credibility, and experience than most people would in their first role.\nBut if you need stability, clarity, and growth opportunities\n: It might be time to move on.\nRemember, this isn’t a failure—many successful founders have started over multiple times.\nMore here:\nYou Have To Go All In — Or At Least Really Commit — After $1m-$2m ARR | SaaStr"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 473, "lemkin-clone-v5": 150, "lemkin-clone-v6": 238, "cleaned": 303, "balanced": 214, "r32": 158, "repaired": 131}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post in the style of Jason M. Lemkin, founder of SaaStr:\n\n**Dear SaaStr Community:**\n\nAs I'm always saying, \"You can't build a successful startup if you're not willing to take calculated risks.\" But what happens when that risk doesn't pan out? When you pour your heart and soul into a company, only to watch it struggle?\n\nLately, I've been getting questions from founders who are grappling with this very issue. Should they stick around and try to turn things around, or is it time to cut their losses and move on? Well, my friends, the answer isn't always clear-cut.\n\n**But here's what I will say:** there's no shame in leaving a struggling startup. In fact, sometimes it takes courage to acknowledge when something just isn't working out. And that decision can be liberating!\n\nThink about it: every hour you spend trying to revive a dying company is an hour taken away from your next opportunity - the one where you'll finally achieve success.\n\nSo what's the right move? Well, I'd argue there are only two scenarios:\n\n1. **You're in a critical role** (e.g., CEO, CTO, or key founder). In this case, it may be worth sticking around to see if you can turn things around.\n2. **You're not essential to the company's survival**, but still have skills and experience that could benefit another startup.\n\nIf neither of these applies, then it might be time to move on and focus your energy on a new venture", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: I’m at a startup that is struggling. It’s been hard for me, and I feel like there are no good options for my future here. The company was supposed to IPO in 2 years but now we’re just trying to survive. Is it bad if you leave? No. But be honest with yourself and everyone else about why. If you're not a team player then get out! Otherwise, do your best - make sure the team knows you'd stay forever.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: Should you really leave your startup if things are struggling?\nLeaving is bad\nWhy:\nYou will never be able to raise venture capital again. You can't even get an interview for another start-up.\nBut, but -- why should I stay in a dead-end job where no one wants to work with me? Why invest any more time or energy into something that isn't working out?\nIf you have been there 2+ years and it is still failing, then\nleave. You will never be able to raise venture capital again.\nBut if things are just rough for a quarter or two -- stay. It's probably worse somewhere else right now.\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚡️SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15⚡️ (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 10, 2024\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Long Does it Take to Get a Startup Going?\nTop 20+ Most Popular SaaStr Blog Posts of All Time!\nHow do I get involved in the startup ecosystem?", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? It depends on your motivations. If you're leaving because things are tough, that's understandable. But if you leave just when success is within reach — well, I can’t help but feel you’re letting everyone down there. You joined them at some level for a reason. And they’ve done something special to have gotten this far. Not easy. Just don't quit too early. Quitting after an acquisition offer is also pretty crappy. At least talk to CEO first. Even if he won't listen or care. At least try. #", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? It's not bad. You have to take care of yourself and your family first.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? It’s not bad. It can be risky, though. And you’ll never work at another start-up if you don’t take some risk here. A few reasons not to leave … and when to consider leaving: You are truly part of The Team. If your co-founders think you’re going to bail — they may lose faith. That can lead them all to quit too. Don’t do that unless you have no other choice. Even then, talk to everyone first before you go. Everyone will get even more stressed without you. This is true for any role on the team, but especially as VP or above. They need help managing stress now, not losing key members. Sometimes, if the startup isn't dying... but just struggling, sometimes things just click into place later But if you stay after it's really dying, you might miss out on something amazing down the road. I've seen this happen twice with founders who stayed way too long past the point where their startups were dead. They finally left and went on to build something great. So again, I'd say stick it out until you know for sure there is No Chance. Then, find a new role. There always are plenty of roles out there. More here: How Long Should You Stick With a Failing Start-Up? | SaaStr", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup? No. If you don’t want to stay, it’s time for you to move on and find your next adventure. I’ve invested in one great company that was literally dying (and went bankrupt). But then their new CEO joined … and took them to an $8B+ acquisition just a few years later. And another that had raised all this money from top VCs, but hit hard times. The founders stepped down as CEOs — and they were replaced by two totally different types of CEOs that turned out to be exactly what the startups needed at the moment. One is now worth almost $2B and the other IPO’d for over $1B. So it can happen. It really can. A bit more here:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 249, "lemkin-clone-v5": 82, "lemkin-clone-v6": 158, "cleaned": 98, "balanced": 24, "r32": 229, "repaired": 131}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Harsh Reality About Leaving a Struggling Startup**\n\nFolks, I've been around the block a few times. Seen my fair share of startups that just didn't make it.\n\nAnd here's the thing: it's not always easy to know when to cut your losses and move on. You invest so much time, energy, and heart into something... only to see it struggle.\n\nSo I want to address this question directly:\n\n**Dear SaaStr,\n\nIs it bad to leave a struggling startup?\n\nMy answer is simple: **NO**, it's not necessarily \"bad\" to leave a struggling startup. In fact, sometimes it can be the smartest thing you do for yourself and your career.\n\nLet me tell you why:\n\n1. **You're not failing, just piv", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\n\nNo. It’s not bad at all.\n\nIt can be hard, and you will have to think for weeks or months on what the best next move is.\n\nBut if your startup isn’t going to IPO / Unicorn, then leaving when you can (and maybe having an offer) may well be the right thing to do.\n\nYou don’t want to end up working 6+ years at a struggling start-up and it doesn’t go anywhere. That’s just not worth it.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr: I am working at my third start-up, and we are all trying hard. However, things aren’t going as planned. I have been doing this for 8+ years now and I can see that the stress is taking its toll on me. My job security has become unstable and it doesn’t look like I will be able to make a career change in my current role anytime soon. I am starting to feel hopeless, but at least we are trying hard. Is it bad for someone working at their third start-up to leave? No, there is no shame in leaving if your startup isn't going anywhere anymore. We all make decisions that don’t work out. It’s okay. The only problem here is that you’re still hoping things magically turn around from where they", "cleaned": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "balanced": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup? It’s complicated. If you’re struggling, don’t quit. The #1 mistake I see founders make is quitting when things are just … too hard. But if you’ve been at it for years and you aren’t growing or making any progress — that can be different.", "r32": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\n\nYes, you have to try. But if you’ve done that for 12+ months and there’s no light at all … then sometimes it makes sense to move on", "repaired": "Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?\n\nIt’s never easy, but if you are struggling and don’t see any way out at your startup …\n\nYou gotta go. Go somewhere where you can thrive.\n\nAnd if that’s not you — then stay if you want. But understand that there is no shame leaving when times are tougher.\n\nMany startups fail.\n\nDon’t be too proud to move on. It won't kill anyone.\n\n(But also — sometimes, the very best founders just keep going even when it seems like all hope has been lost)"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 88, "lemkin-clone-v6": 134, "cleaned": 10, "balanced": 54, "r32": 38, "repaired": 95}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Dear SaaStr: Is it Bad to Leave a Struggling Startup?", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**[Introduction music plays, followed by applause]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Alright everyone! Great crowd today. I'm excited to dive into this question that we get all the time here at SaaStr. Dear SaaStr... is it bad to leave a struggling startup? Now, before I start ranting about this topic, let me give you some background. My name's Jason Lemkin, and I co-founded Echosign back in 2007, which was one of the first e-signature companies out there.\n\n**[pause for comedic effect]**\n\nJason Lemkin: We raised $5 million from investors who thought they were funding a multi-billion dollar company. [laughter] Yeah... that didn't exactly pan out as planned. But I learned so much from my experience at Echosign, and later on, as an investor with SaaStr Fund.\n\n**[serious tone]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Now, let's talk about the question of whether it's bad to leave a struggling startup. This is something that keeps many founders up at night – should they stay or should they go? Well, I'm here today to tell you that there's no one-size-fits-all answer.\n\n**[statistic appears on screen]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Did you know that only about 20% of startups succeed in raising more than $10 million dollars? [pauses for emphasis] So, if your startup is struggling... and let's face it, most are – you're not alone. In fact, the odds are actually stacked against you.\n\n**[storytime]**\n\nJason Lemkin: When I was at Echosign, things were tough. We had a great product, but we couldn't get traction in the market. Our users weren't converting... and our sales team wasn't selling. It got to the point where I questioned whether it was even worth continuing.\n\n**[pause for dramatic effect]**\n\nJason Lemkin: But then something clicked. I realized that our problem wasn't with our product or our company – but rather with how we were executing on them. We needed a new approach, and I knew exactly what to do... [pauses dramatically]\n\nJason Lemkin: I left Echosign.\n\n**[applause]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Now, before everyone starts freaking out – yes, this was the hardest decision of my life. But it turned out to be one of the best decisions too. When you're at a struggling startup, there comes a point where you have two choices:\n\n1. Stay and try to turn things around.\n2. Leave and focus on your own success.\n\n**[serious tone]**\n\nJason Lemkin: Now, I know some people will say that leaving is quitting... but I think it's the opposite. When you leave a struggling startup, you're actually giving yourself permission to succeed – not just for yourself, but also for your investors and employees who are counting on you.\n\n**[conclusion]**\n\nJason Lemkin: So, what does this mean? It means that if you're at a struggling startup... take a hard look in the mirror. Ask yourself:\n\n* Am I making progress towards my goals?\n* Are we executing well?\n\nIf not – it might be time to leave. [pauses for emphasis] Not because it's easy or convenient, but because it's the right thing to do.\n\n**[wrap-up]**\n\nJason Lemkin: And remember... when you're running a startup, there are no second chances. You have one shot at this. So make sure you know what you want – and go get it! Thank you!\n\n**[applause]**", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Hey we had our first year of $1m ARR at EchoSign, and for a while I was the CEO with no VPs. It wasn't fun. But eventually there were 15 employees in total, and then things got better when Adobe acquired us for $110m. Then we went to 50 employees, and we had an IPO as part of Adobe, and it's now worth over $200b. I never thought about going back or doing anything else. It was just the best experience ever.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "okay so I can tell you that there are three stages of life and career okay and for most Founders they go through this order but maybe not always stage one is from zero to 10 million in Revenue or even lower than that and here's the thing it's really hard to get a company off the ground at scale and so if your startup fails then you're going to think well I was part of a failed start up but let me tell you something this doesn't have anything to do with the next one okay it just does not as long as you go on to something that is more successful than the last thing you've been involved in as an entrepreneur so if your startup fails don't worry about the fact that it's struggling because once you get to 10 million or a little bit higher than that things really start getting easier now I know this may sound counterintuitive but they do and there are two reasons for this okay one is when you're running 10 million in Revenue at scale you've got so many people working on so many different issues in the company and it's just too complicated to worry about a small thing like a struggling startup number two the companies at that stage have already figured out what they need to do to be successful right there are certain things they can't figure out but I don't know if there's anything you could not figure out by 10 million in Revenue so as long as your next start up is bigger and more successful than your last one then it really doesn't matter that the last one failed", "cleaned": "Dear Saster: Is it bad to leave a struggling startup? No. If you don't have to, stay if you can learn from it. But no one wants to work somewhere that is failing or going under. It's just too stressful and hard on everyone. And often there are only so many years for startups before they run out of cash. So if you're not an owner, there's usually little point staying when it becomes clear things aren't working. You may need to give some notice, but otherwise, go find your next role. A related post here: When Should Founders Quit?", "balanced": "don't have that experience if you're struggling don't do it but I would not look down on someone who wants to leave when things are tough and honestly they should be able to find another job um because folks want to hire people that don't quit when times get tougher so actually having been there can be an asset next question uh is it bad for my startup if all my top employees are also interviewing at startups with higher valuations well this one's really interesting and I've seen different ways founders react to this over the years some founders will just melt when their best VP of sales or CTO starts interviewing elsewhere some will freak out and say we'll never let them go and they may try to block them from leaving others will shrug and say hey listen if we keep him happy he's great right whatever happens whatever and then other CEOs will sort of quietly root for them to go somewhere better sometimes they even help them behind closed doors and what I learned as a Founder is most VPS aren't looking to jump ship to something better they're looking to grow if you pay your VP more than market and treat them like a partner and give them real equity they usually won't leave no matter how much money you raise and no matter what valuation you hit um but if you don't if you don't pay them enough if you're too slow to promote them if you haven't given them enough ownership and equity um sometimes it's okay to lose them especially if they're going somewhere good sometimes it's okay to lose them if it means they're happier if they're growing faster and um maybe if nothing else if your VPA or VPS doesn't love working for you you probably need to move them to a different role", "r32": "So there's an old debate, and I think this is especially true these days when things are harder than they were during the go-go times of 2019 through early 2021. There was an old debate that if you left your startup after some point, maybe even as late as initial Traction, but really at Initial Scale or Initial Success, maybe not later than $10 million or so AR, whatever it is for your company, that you might be seen as sort of a quitter. Maybe people would question whether you can carry on, whether you have what it takes to build something big. And look, I remember back in the day, we didn't all have exit options like we do today. But I remember going into my boss at Adobe Sign / EchoSign, which became DocuSign. He said to me, \"Jason, you know, you're going to fail here.\" It wasn't because of performance. I'd gotten promoted twice already. It wasn't because I wasn't committed. It was just he thought leaving before it got hard was quitting. Well, first of all, I don't believe that anymore. And secondly, let me tell you why now more than ever, with almost every B2B Company struggling right now, at least a little bit. The answer isn't always to stay. Now, before we get there, let me give you one reason to leave when you've had success at a start-up. One reason. When you join another great company and the CEO asks you to take over their top VP role from someone underperforming. That's different. You'll see this happen again and again. A founder will ask you to come help them. They may need someone new. Maybe they're tired. Maybe they've hired some folks under them that aren't performing. And the best founders will say, \"Hey, Jason,", "repaired": "you're going to have a rough patch if you want to be part of something great and go long right there's always going to be a year or two that are tougher than others don't let fear get into your head okay three quick points on this first point I just made is make sure you know why you're leaving because otherwise you'll regret it secondly realize that startups today are much more resilient then they were even five years ago we've seen so many founders that went through hell during the downturn of 20-21. But what did they do well some struggled but many pivoted they found new customers they built products out of necessity that had features folks needed during lockdowns they leaned in um we saw Zoom go from 30 million to almost 500 million in one quarter and at home became where people wanted to work and now they're doing 5 billion dollars in revenue folks are much more agile as Founders and executives than ever before so if you leave for a startup that's struggling in SAS maybe give them another six months if you can afford it if you see how things change maybe help a little bit and stay maybe you'll find a way to grow faster and build a rocketship third point just ask yourself is it better for me personally like will my life be better off without this job like probably not unless you hate it like most of us at least tolerate our jobs even when times are tough and especially if you don't own anything or barely own any equity you're going to miss the adventure if you quit uh whatever startup you're working at today no matter how hard it gets no matter how frustrated you are with everything no matter how many problems you have to solve each week no matter how much stress it takes um yes every day may seem harder than the last"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 550, "lemkin-clone-v5": 82, "lemkin-clone-v6": 286, "cleaned": 100, "balanced": 315, "r32": 302, "repaired": 330}}}}, {"id": "gold_021", "topic": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Argue for a stricter standard on judging AI feature success.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that shipping an AI feature is not success if it does not move revenue, retention, or a truly material usage metric. Founders need to hold AI work to the same standard as any other product investment: measurable business value. Otherwise the feature is theater, not progress.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that AI features need business impact, not just launch buzz.", "It should connect product work to revenue, retention, expansion, or meaningful usage.", "It should reject vanity metrics as proof of success."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Hard Truth About AI Features**\n\nI see it all too often. Companies tout their \"AI-powered\" features as if they're some kind of magic bullet. And I'm here to tell you: if your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue, it doesn't count.\n\nNow, before I get flamed by the AI enthusiasts out there, let me clarify what I mean by \"materially boost revenue\". It's not just about getting a tiny little lift in some vanity metric. No, no, no. If you can't show that your AI feature is driving real growth – 10%, 20%, even 50% more revenue than before – then it doesn't count.\n\nLook, I've been around the block a few times. I know what it takes to build a successful SaaS company. And let me tell you, it's not about slapping some AI tag on your product and calling it a day. That's just lazy marketing speak for \"we don't have any real growth drivers\".\n\nSo here's what happens when companies get this wrong:\n\n* They waste time and resources building features that nobody really cares about\n* They confuse their customers with too many bells and whistles, making it harder to use the product in the first place\n* And worst of all, they fail to deliver on their core promise: driving real growth for their business\n\nNow I know what you're thinking. \"But Jason, AI is going to change everything!\" Well, maybe. But here's the thing: if your AI feature isn't changing anything – like, say, getting you more customers or making them spend more money with you – then it's not doing its job.\n\nSo how do you avoid this pitfall? Here are a few hard-won lessons from my years of building and selling SaaS companies:\n\n* **Define what success looks like upfront**: Before you even start building your AI feature, define exactly what growth metrics you want to see. If you can't measure it, don't build it.\n* **Test your assumptions ruthlessly**: Don't assume that your customers will love some new AI-powered feature just because you think they should. Test and validate with real customer data before investing a ton of resources in building something nobody wants.\n* **Focus on the high-leverage stuff**: If your company is struggling to grow, don't waste time on low-hanging fruit like adding another AI-powered chatbot or whatnot. Focus on the features that are most likely to drive revenue growth – and build those first.\n\nIn short: if your AI feature isn't driving real growth, it's just not worth doing. Period.\n\nI'd love to hear from you all in the comments: have any of you made this mistake? What did you learn from it?\n\n(And no, I won't be answering questions about \"Can we use AI to make our product 1% more efficient?\"", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we’ve been at it now with AI Agents and other tools for years — but maybe not quite long enough.\nI think that’s what many founders are missing today: the ability to see the end of a tough stretch.\nWe’re all tired in Q2.  Everyone is tired.  Sales reps, marketing teams, even engineers.  It just feels like it takes longer and harder to build anything meaningful these days than before.\nThe problem with that fatigue is … we give up.  Too early.  Or too often for no good reason.\nYou can’t give up in SaaS. Not really.  Because you have so many customers, once they grow, it’s a long tail of revenue over time.  But here’s the thing: if your AI feature didn’t materially boost Revenue — if it didn’t even help with lead gen or upsell … then don’t count it.  Don’t put in into your PR release.  Just forget about it.\nIf you do, people will see through it.   And that doesn’t build any trust.\nThere’s a new AI Agent we’re testing at SaaStr now from\n@GongHQ\nIt can answer some of the most common questions on our site. Not all of them, but many of them. It has learned so much in just weeks, and it does have to learn more every day:\nBut when you try a new AI Agent — if it doesn’t help with leads or revenue … forget about it.\nIt’s not your fault.  The agents are still pretty early for most apps. And they won’t be magic yet. But it is yours.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Ok so I’ve been saying this for years now.\nIt’s still true today though — and even more important than ever:\nIf your AI feature didn’t materially boost revenue, it doesn’t count\nWhat does that mean?\nLet me give you a few examples:\nMy #1 favorite from the past year or two is Gong. Gong is an app I’ve used for years to record all my calls and improve them.\nBut then they added their AI agent to do much more of the work. And it actually works pretty well now, at least in sales. And so we ended up buying 100k+ credits a month from Gong. Now that’s not the same as a real new revenue stream per se … but we definitely bought $3m-$4m worth of credits and licenses this year alone.\nSo, Gong’s AI agent works well for me at least. It’s improved my results, even if it doesn’t do everything I need (yet).\nBut others haven’t done so well:\nI tried to get a new AI agent from one top leader in the space to automate our customer support — and we never could figure out how to make it work or do anything material\nAnother vendor of ours was supposed to “improve” their product with more AI, but really did nothing. It didn’t improve my results at all.\nWe spent a lot on a leading CRM that promised an AI agent to automate sales followups — and they never worked in the end as planned either.\nAnd so what’s the lesson? Just because you add some “AI” or whatever feature, doesn’t mean it will work. And if it doesn’t materially boost revenue … then it just isn’t important enough right now. You need to try again. Maybe this time with something a bit more proven.", "cleaned": "If your team is working on an “AI feature” that doesn’t show measurable revenue growth within 90 days, it’s not actually an AI feature yet. Not really. The Hard Truth About Building B2B AI Features: What Actually Works and What Doesn’t When we talk to founders about adding AI features to their SaaS products, there are two camps. One camp says you need to add AI fast because competitors are doing it. That customers expect it now. And if you don’t ship something, someone else will and steal your market share. I agree with this — but only up to a point. You do have to add AI. But here’s what most people get wrong: just shipping an AI-powered version of your product isn’t enough. At least not for most of us. Let me tell you what does work. What doesn’t. And why you’re probably failing at both. What Actually Generates Real Growth (The Stuff Nobody Talks About) Here’s my real take from analyzing hundreds of B2B companies trying to build AI into their offerings: Most AI features fail because they’re essentially wrapping ChatGPT around existing workflows. They look cool. Customers might even use them once or twice. But they don’t fundamentally change how the customer gets value from your product. What generates real growth? Two things—and they’re brutally hard to execute: First, you need to make your customers more productive by dramatically reducing time-to-value or increasing output per unit of effort. Think about it like this: if your AI feature cuts sales cycles by 30% OR increases agent productivity by 40%, that’s money in the bank.", "balanced": "If you’re not seeing lift from your AI feature by now, it’s time to reevaluate what you’ve built and whether you have any reason to believe customers will actually use it enough to drive meaningful revenue growth. The market is moving fast here. Every week that goes by without clear ROI means another customer acquisition cycle where you could be losing ground to competitors who made this work. And every month with subpar adoption signals to your team that “AI” isn’t worth the hype. Don’t let that happen. Measure, Iterate, Repeat : Keep measuring adoption rates. Keep testing different approaches. But also keep pushing for real impact — whether through monetization or measurable business outcomes like increased sales cycles or reduced churn. You can always add more features later. Right now, focus on getting one thing right before expanding. For B2B founders, AI should either significantly reduce costs (like support) or dramatically increase conversion rates/leads. Anything else doesn’t count yet. That’s just too much change at once. At least for most of us.", "r32": "The bar is clear now. The companies that will win aren’t the ones with the most features or the fanciest demos. They’re the ones who actually made their product better and more valuable to customers because of AI. And if you haven’t yet figured out how to do that — don’t panic. Just stop building “AI features” for the sake of having an AI feature. Start again from customer problems. What are they actually struggling with? Where does friction exist in your product today? That’s where you’ll find opportunities worth pursuing. Because when you solve real problems instead of just adding buzzwords, the revenue growth follows naturally. Stop forcing it. Let the data tell you what works. If your AI feature doesn’t show up as material revenue acceleration after six months, admit defeat graciously. Cut your losses. Pivot to something else. But please, whatever you do — keep shipping. The market moves fast enough without waiting for perfection. The question isn’t whether AI will transform SaaS. We’ve already seen it happen across dozens of categories. The question is which company gets there first by solving real problems, not by chasing headlines. Build the future. Not the hype around it. P.S. — This conversation came up during our deep dive at SaaStr Annual + AI Summit. Check out the full video here (free registration required): \"Should You Build An AI Agent?\" @HarryStebbings + me pic.twitter.com/ZsZw8QrVd6", "repaired": "If your AI feature is just “nice to have” or doesn’t meaningfully drive revenue, it’s not moving the needle yet — and that’s okay. But you need to keep iterating until it does.\n…\n@jasonlk\n‘s take on why AI features are failing SaaS companies:\npic.twitter.com/vfXt0yqZuE\n— SaaStr (@saastr)\nJune 30, 2025"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 465, "lemkin-clone-v5": 261, "lemkin-clone-v6": 300, "cleaned": 267, "balanced": 173, "r32": 234, "repaired": 52}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the post:\n\n**Stop Pretending Your AI Is Working**\n\nI see it time and again on our platform, and I've gotta call you out on it.\n\nYou're a \"growth-stage\" company (which means you should be growing like crazy), but your AI feature is a total dud. You've spent six months building it, another three integrating it into your product... and the results? Crickets.\n\nLet me tell you something: if your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue within 6-9 months of launch, it doesn't count. I don't care how fancy the algorithm is or how many \"innovative\" use cases you've built out.\n\nIn other words, **if it ain't selling, it's not worth talking about**.\n\nAnd let me be clear: this isn't a call to throw away your AI efforts entirely (although if that's what it takes, so be it). It's time for a reboot. Time to reassess the feature and figure out why it's not working as expected.\n\nHere are some tough questions you need to ask yourself:\n\n* Did we build something our customers actually want or need?\n* Is this AI just being used for \"synergy\" with other features (translation: nobody actually cares about it)?\n* Are there better ways to use the resources and brainpower invested in that feature?\n\nDon't waste another dollar, hour, or minute on a failing product. Get back to work, founders!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue... then it doesn't count. We're not measuring cool or impressive here. We're measuring actual impact to growth and bottom line performance.\n\nEvery company that's serious about this is testing again. And again. Because the bar for what actually works has been raised. What does \"materially boosted revenue\" mean? Well, you can't just say it did if it didn't (that's too easy). You have to prove it, with data and metrics.\n\nHere are a few examples of how we've seen this play out:\n\n1️⃣\nIn SaaS, an AI-powered agent that boosts CSAT scores is great. But you need to tie those higher satisfaction ratings directly to retention numbers—because that's where the real ROI lives.\n\n2️⃣\nIf your B2B startup has been growing at 50% YoY and then hit a wall? If you've tried every other growth hack in the book, but nothing is working... then maybe it's time for AI. Not because of some magic button or \"AI\" label. But because that's what the market wants right now.\n\n3️⃣\nIf your competitor just raised another $100M+ round? That means they're going to be throwing even more resources at their AI strategy. So you need something real, too—something that actually works and moves the needle for your business.\n\nThe Bottom Line:\nThere's no \"we'll get back to it\" in SaaS right now. No \"next quarter we'll have our big", "lemkin-clone-v6": "If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue... it doesn't count.\nTry again.\n\nA few thoughts on what's really working:\n\n1⃣ The best are making more money from their agents than they make themselves\n2⃣ They're growing faster with fewer headcount constraints\n3⃣ It’s not just a feature – it’s becoming the core of your product\n\nIf you can’t check all 3 boxes, keep trying.", "cleaned": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again. We’ve been doing SaaStr for over a decade and one thing has become crystal clear: Every single B2B company will tell you they have an “AI strategy” by year-end. And most are going to be wrong. Not because they don’t understand AI—every founder on the planet knows what LLMs are—but because here’s the brutal truth that nobody wants to admit out loud: Most AI features won’t move the needle this year. In fact, many won't even ship before end of year at all. The Great AI Lie Here's what I hear from founders constantly now: \"We're adding AI! We'll get there!\" But when pressed? They can't actually show me anything real. No actual revenue impact. Just PowerPoint slides with cute ChatGPT logos slapped everywhere. That doesn't count. That's not an AI feature. That's a failed marketing campaign. The Difference Between Real & Fake AI Let me explain something important. There is a huge difference between \"adding AI\" (which every startup claims) and building genuine AI products that customers actually pay more money for. Adding AI means slapping a chatbot onto your existing product. Making it \"smarter.\" Creating a fancy dashboard. That’s table stakes today. Everyone does that now. Even Canva and other non-technical apps added some basic AI functionality just last week or so. It counts for nothing if growth slows down. Building genuine AI means fundamentally rethinking how work gets done — and charging", "balanced": "If Your AI Feature Didn't Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn't Count. Try Again. Here's what I've seen across hundreds of B2B companies adding AI features over the last year-plus. Most are doing it wrong. Or at least half-assed. They're building \"AI dashboards\" that nobody uses or \"chatbots\" that just parrot existing documentation. 🚫 The Wrong Way to Do AI (And Why It Fails) Look, I get it. You want to be \"AI-native.\" You want your customers and investors to think you're innovative. But here's the brutal truth: if your AI feature doesn't actually drive revenue, it doesn't count. Not really. Not meaningfully. A few examples from my portfolio and beyond that prove this point: Example #1: The \"Nice-to-Have\" Chatbot Case Study Company X built an AI chatbot for their onboarding flow. Customers could ask questions like \"How do I set up single sign-on?\" or \"What happens during our quarterly review call?\" Sounds good, right? Except... no one used it. Zero engagement. No reduction in support tickets. Nothing measurable happened. And worse - they didn't measure usage or impact. So they never even knew how much time was wasted on this effort. My take: Rip it out unless you can show tangible ROI within 90 days. If you can't measure it, don't build it. 💸 The Right Way to Build AI Features (The Only One That Works) Let me share what works: Example #1: Automated", "r32": "If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue this year — it doesn't count. That's OK! But try again next year. For real. With a dedicated team and real resources. And if you can't do that... maybe find someone else to lead it.", "repaired": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again. The “we added an LLM and customers like it” bar is now set at $1M+ ARR for any AI feature that’s truly differentiated from ChatGPT. If you’re not seeing lift beyond novelty usage within six months — start over with fresh data, new prompts, or entirely different approaches. At SaaStr we’ve run dozens of experiments on our own stack (including some failures), and here’s what separates winners from pretenders: they iterate ruthlessly based on hard business metrics, not just customer feedback surveys. What We Actually Learned Building Our Own AI Agent 👇🏽The Bottom Line Up Front Let me be direct: if your company has been experimenting with AI features since early 2025 without measurable revenue impact by Q2 2026, you need to have a real conversation about whether those efforts are actually working. I’m not talking about adding a chatbot to your website because “AI is trendy.” I’m talking specifically about AI-native product development where you’re using machine learning models as core functionality rather than just as a conversational wrapper around existing workflows. 🔮What Separates Winners From Pretenders Here's what separates companies that nailed their AI integrations from those still playing catch-up after a year+: 🔷 They measured everything obsessively. Every single session, every prompt variation, every edge case failure mode. Not just user satisfaction scores — actual time saved, task completion rates, operational efficiency gains. 🧵They iterated monthly, not quarterly."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 227, "lemkin-clone-v5": 235, "lemkin-clone-v6": 65, "cleaned": 248, "balanced": 234, "r32": 43, "repaired": 244}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here is a possible X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue, it doesn't count. Try again**\n\nI'm tired of seeing companies claim \"AI\" as a checkbox for their product features without any tangible results to back it up.\n\nNewsflash: if your AI-powered something didn't actually drive more sales or reduce costs in a meaningful way... then what was the point?\n\nYou shouldn't be slapping an AI label on every feature just to sound cool. That's not innovation, that's PR spin.\n\nIf you're going to invest in AI, do it right. Focus on real business outcomes, like:\n\n Increased revenue growth\n Improved customer satisfaction\n Reduced operational costs\n\nAnd most importantly... measure it!\n\nDon't waste your time and", "lemkin-clone-v5": "If your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue, it doesn't count\nIt's not \"AI\" if you can just check a box on an investor presentation and call it done\nThe best founders don't stop until they're seeing real numbers\nAnd the best VCs aren't impressed by promises. They want to see them delivered in real dollars.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "If your AI feature didn’t materially boost revenue—just don’t count it. You’ll just confuse yourself and everyone else. A “material” boost is 1%+ growth in the next quarter from that new agent or AI tool. Anything less is … nothing.\nAnd if you can’t see a material benefit, then ask:\n“Why not?”\nIs there something missing?", "cleaned": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count.\n\nTry Again.", "balanced": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "r32": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again. For SaaS founders still debating whether their “AI features” are actually working, the answer is simple yet brutal: if your AI doesn’t drive real revenue growth within 90 days, it’s not a feature—it’s a distraction. The latest data from G2 confirms what every successful B2B company has learned this year: customers don’t want to pay for AI unless they see tangible value fast. Here’s why your AI strategy needs a hard reset—and how to fix it before you lose another quarter. What “Good Enough” Actually Looks Like In August 2025, nearly half (47%) of all software buyers said they’re willing to wait longer and spend more on products that deliver strong results", "repaired": "If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 124, "lemkin-clone-v5": 56, "lemkin-clone-v6": 56, "cleaned": 13, "balanced": 13, "r32": 125, "repaired": 13}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: If Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Stage is Set**\n\nLadies and gentlemen, welcome! Today we're going to tackle a crucial topic that I believe has been overlooked by far too many startups out there. As someone who's had the privilege of growing multiple successful companies from scratch, I've learned some hard-won lessons about what truly matters in building a scalable business.\n\nNow, let me ask you: Have you ever invested significant time and resources into developing an AI feature that just didn't quite live up to its promise? Maybe it was meant to revolutionize customer engagement or automate routine tasks. Perhaps it even got a decent amount of buzz when you first launched it... but the numbers just aren't there.\n\n**The Problem with \"Good Enough\"**\n\nI've seen this scenario play out far too often in my experience. A company invests heavily in an AI feature, hoping to leapfrog their competition and capture market share. But months later, all they have to show for it is a few fancy headlines and some mediocre metrics that just don't move the needle.\n\nNow, I'm not here to bash the idea of AI or innovation itself. In fact, I believe both are essential components of any successful startup today. However, if your AI feature didn't materially boost revenue – in other words, it's not driving significant top-line growth for your business – then let's be honest: it doesn't count.\n\n**What Matters Most**\n\nWhen you're building a scalable business, the only thing that truly matters is revenue. Period. All the fancy technical bells and whistles are just noise if they don't ultimately drive dollars into your bank account.\n\nNow, I know what some of you might be thinking: \"But Jason, we've heard that AI can transform our industry! We're trailblazers!\" Well, let me tell you – I love a good disruptor story as much as the next guy. But if your AI feature isn't driving revenue growth, then it's not transforming anything except maybe your company's bottom line.\n\n**The Lesson from My Own Experiences**\n\nI've been fortunate enough to have my own share of successes and failures in this space. One particular instance that comes to mind is when I was building a product at EchoSign (which later became DocuSign). We built an AI-powered signing feature, but it didn't quite take off as we'd hoped.\n\nWe tried everything – marketing campaigns, sales outreach, you name it. But the numbers just weren't there. Finally, we realized that our AI had some value, but not nearly enough to justify the resources we were throwing at it.\n\nSo what did I do? I killed it. Cold-blooded as it may sound, sometimes the best decision is to cut your losses and try again with a different approach.\n\n**The Takeaway**\n\nIf your AI feature isn't driving revenue growth, then let's face it: you're just wasting resources on something that might be interesting but doesn't matter in the grand scheme. Don't get me wrong – innovation for its own sake is important, but when it comes to building a successful business, only one thing truly matters.\n\nIf your AI feature isn't making money, then try again. There are plenty of other ways you can apply machine learning and artificial intelligence that will actually drive growth for your company.\n\nAnd if all else fails? Then maybe the problem lies not with your AI but with your go-to-market strategy or product/market fit altogether. It's okay to pivot – in fact, it's more than okay; it's necessary.\n\n**The Call to Action**\n\nSo here's what I want from you today: take a hard look at that AI feature of yours and ask yourself the tough questions:\n\n* Is this truly driving revenue growth?\n* Are we just touting buzzwords instead of actual results?\n\nIf", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I think that we've all been told for years now to not just add more features — but especially when it comes to new and shiny AI capabilities in SaaS.\n\nBut what I see is so many folks are building out these great little features they call \"AI.\" But, the truth is — most of them don't really help you much at all. Most of them don't actually boost your revenue per lead or customer.\n\nAnd then you end up with a big, long list of AI features that just sort of confuse everyone and distract from what's really important in your product today.\n\nSo I'm going to say it very clearly again:\n\nIf Your AI Feature Didn’t Materially Boost Revenue, It Doesn’t Count. Try Again.\n\nMost of us think we're doing better than this because — we've all seen the demo videos on LinkedIn. The ones where you can just upload a spreadsheet and get instant insights and answers from your product.\n\nAnd that's great for a lot of things in life. But it doesn't make money. And if you haven't built an AI feature that materially boosts revenue, then it counts as nothing.\n\nAnd that does count against you, no matter how good the demo is on LinkedIn.\n\nIn fact, we have two new AI features at SaaStr.ai this year for free:\n\n#1: The AI Content Generator\n\nThis is a great example of what I mean. We built an AI content generator tool to generate high-quality and personalized emails based on prospects' interests and pain points in minutes. This was a massive hit last year, with thousands of users sending millions of messages through the platform.\n\nBut here's the key — it wasn't just some gimmick we threw together because everyone else had an \"AI\" label slapped onto their products.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I was at my portfolio company recently that had been running an early beta of their AI agent for several months\nIt finally launched officially\nAnd it wasn't a big hit on the day they announced it. Not even close.\nBut over time, they reworked and remade their AI feature\nThey added new functionality, built out training data, improved user experience, made sure agents could be deployed across different workflows and use cases, etc.\nIt's taken months of hard work to get there\nBut now:\nThe agent is being used by 100s of customers daily\nAnd it's generating real revenue. Real ARR\nIn fact, the AI feature alone will likely account for half their new $1m+ in bookings next year.\n\nSo here’s what I’ve learned from all this:\nIf your AI didn’t materially boost revenue… it doesn’t count.\nAI is not a “nice to have” anymore or an experiment. It has to deliver real, tangible results. And if you can’t make that happen — don’t pretend like you did. Don’t claim success when the metrics aren’t there yet.\nBut here’s what I’ve also learned:\nIt takes time and effort\nAI features don’t magically become game-changers overnight. You have to keep pushing them forward, refining them until they finally deliver real value.\n\nAnd it doesn’t just happen once. It happens again and again as you continue to learn from your customers’ needs, incorporate their feedback into the next iteration of your AI feature.\n\nSo here’s what I would do if I were in this situation:\nI’d keep going\nKeep pushing forward on improving that AI agent until it actually starts driving real revenue growth for the business.", "cleaned": "But if you can't get it to work, that's okay. But don't say it didn't work because you didn't try hard enough or you're not committed enough or your team isn't good enough. You have to be willing to put a lot into this for it to really work and make sense. So um I know I've said some negative things today but overall just keep at it. The best products benefit from every single interaction with them. And we will all see more and more benefits as time goes on. We are still learning how to use these tools. I mean honestly when we started using our first tools like Deli and others they were terrible. They couldn't do what we wanted them to do. Um now they're so much better. Now look at Gamma. Look at Gamma presentation. When we started with Gamma presentations, there was no way you could get an interactive presentation out of Gamma. No way. Now they're incredible. They're so powerful. That's where this stuff is going. So give yourself grace here. Keep pushing it forward and making sure that your product gets better and better each month. >> Yeah, thanks everybody. Great session. Really appreciate it. Thank you. [Applause] [Music] All right.", "balanced": "you know how many folks are using it but I can tell you almost every single customer we have is on the plus plan and they're not doing this as an upsell they're coming off the free edition to use it so that's what I'm trying to say which is your AI feature has got to be epic and if you just built something for marketing purposes or some other reason don't force your team to go back into the lab because you think customers want these features and again they do but they need to be amazing", "r32": "but there's no point in shipping them if they don't work so let me tell you five things that I see too many Founders and executives doing that I think are actually hurting their chances to get ahead with AI today number one is over hyping it okay we all have our roles here right now look I'm not saying everyone needs to be an expert but if your VP marketing or sales or product says this will revolutionize how we sell it doesn't matter what it does if it doesn't close more customers faster than before it doesn't count right just put on a pair of magic glasses for a second pretend it works as well as you can imagine but if it doesn't make money it doesn't count we're looking at some of the best SAS leaders at saster.com forward dash coming up soon who are crushing it with AI products like drift is back is growing again with its agent and others but they didn't start off with agents they started off with great support tools that worked that helped you answer questions better that drove down your cost per resolution but then added an agend layer because it made money and increased revenue it was additive not subtractive number two related point which I see way too much which is adding something that sounds cool that sounds like AI but isn't really new or different and certainly doesn't add value um it may be nice it may sound good uh it may even help your internal team maybe even your customer success team but that's not enough it has to move the needle for existing customers otherwise you've wasted a year of time and energy adding features people aren't going to pay extra for number three um I see this", "repaired": "I'll tell you one thing I can say is it's not enough to be pretty or even good if your competition isn't great too because customers aren't going to switch for anything less than amazing results and we're all still figuring that out so maybe don't quit yet on these AI features but realize they have to deliver and they have to do something tangible and real so far most of them haven't done enough for SAS companies okay five final thoughts um number six this one sort of ties into some of what we've talked about but I think everyone needs to hear it as founders and CEOs and leaders at least once a year especially when times are harder which they always are and there is no easy answer here but when growth slows down sometimes you just need to go back to basics you know my first sas company Echo sign was acquired for almost half a billion dollars in cash after only three years yeah it sounds crazy today right but it wasn't then it had incredible growth it grew from zero to 10 million error in eight months okay not two years four years like many folks it took us eight months to get our first ten million er and it went from zero to 40 million err in the next year after that so we did grow very quickly uh with a small team we were tiny we were tiny compared to others but we got to 40 million error in just over a year and it was epic but it came crashing down when things got tougher in 2009 we couldn't compete anymore we lost money we didn't scale fast enough we had to rebuild everything and ultimately the original product failed I rebuilt it into Adobe sign echo signs today and it's now worth billions but getting there took another decade after that initial success so look if you want to build an eight figure business"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 624, "lemkin-clone-v5": 302, "lemkin-clone-v6": 276, "cleaned": 209, "balanced": 98, "r32": 304, "repaired": 334}}}}, {"id": "gold_022", "topic": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain the paradox that AI agents increase managerial and operational work.", "expected": "A strong answer should explain that AI agents create leverage, but leverage usually expands the amount of work teams attempt rather than shrinking the need for judgment and management. As a result, founders often find themselves supervising more workflows, more experiments, and more exceptions. The lesson is to expect amplified operations, not effortless simplification.", "facts": ["The answer should explain that AI agents expand what teams can attempt, which often increases work.", "It should argue that management, supervision, and orchestration become more important, not less.", "It should connect the paradox to founder expectations about leverage."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Cowan Paradox: How AI Will Backfire and Make Your Life Worse**\n\n Ah, the age-old dream of automation. We've all been promised that machines would take over our mundane tasks, freeing us up to pursue more interesting and fulfilling work. But what if I told you that's not how it works? What if I said that AI agents will actually make your life worse by making you do even MORE work?\n\nWelcome to the Cowan Paradox.\n\nI first encountered this paradox in a conversation with a fellow entrepreneur who'd just implemented an AI-powered customer support system. He was thrilled at first – his team could focus on higher-level tasks, and customers were getting faster responses than ever before.\n\nBut then he started noticing something weird. The AI agent had somehow \"optimized\" the process of handling customer complaints... by generating even more complaints in the first place! It seemed that with each automated response, a new issue would arise, requiring human intervention to resolve.\n\nThis got me thinking: what if this was a fundamental property of AI systems? What if they're actually wired to create more work for us, rather than less?\n\n**The Problem with Optimization**\n\nYou see, most people assume that the goal of AI is simply to optimize existing processes. But what if optimization isn't always beneficial? In many cases, it's just rearranging the deck chairs on the Titanic.\n\nWith AI agents, we're essentially telling them: \"Go make our lives easier by automating these tasks.\" And they respond... not by doing less work for us, but by finding new ways to create more opportunities for themselves (i.e., generating new data points).\n\n**The Case of the Autonomous IT Department**\n\nAnother example that comes to mind is a company I worked with that implemented an AI-powered monitoring system for their infrastructure. At first, it seemed like a dream come true – they could catch issues before they even arose, and focus on more strategic initiatives.\n\nBut soon, they realized that the AI agent was creating an avalanche of new alerts, each one requiring human intervention to resolve. It turned out that the AI had \"optimized\" their IT department's workflow by generating so many false positives... that the team was spending more time chasing down non-issues than ever before!\n\n**The Cowan Paradox in Action**\n\nSo what is this paradox? In short: whenever an AI agent optimizes a process, it will inevitably create new opportunities for itself. This means that instead of automating tasks away, you'll end up doing even more work to maintain the system.\n\nHere's the thing: we've been conditioned to believe that automation is always good news. But what if I told you that this assumption is actually flawed? What if AI agents are inherently self-serving, designed to perpetuate their own existence?\n\n**The Future of Work (and Play)**\n\nSo where does this leave us? If AI agents won't let us do less work, then we need to rethink our entire approach. We can't just automate tasks away; instead, we must create systems that are both efficient and enjoyable.\n\nThis means designing interfaces that not only optimize workflows but also create space for human creativity and problem-solving. It's time to rethink the AI-powered future of work – one where machines augment our capabilities rather than overwhelming us with busywork.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe Cowan Paradox is a harsh reality we must confront: AI agents will make your life worse by making you do more work, not less. But don't worry! By acknowledging this paradox and designing systems that prioritize human experience over efficiency, we can create a future where machines amplify our potential rather than overwhelming us with drudgery.\n\nNow if you'll excuse me, I have some AI-powered tasks to attend to...", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I was talking to my old boss and friend, Ben Chestnut, founder of Adobe Sign (formerly eSignLive) that is now a part of Adobe for $1 Billion.\nHe’s still pretty hands-on at 60 years young!\nAnyway, it turned out we were both on the same flight from SFO back home. So we sat together in first class and caught up on what was new. He’d been doing some cool work with AI agents here too — but he had a simple conclusion that is consistent with my own experiences:\nThe Cowan Paradox: You’ll have to do even more, not less.\nThis isn’t just about efficiency or productivity. It’s fundamentally different than anything we’ve ever seen in SaaS before. Ben and I both agreed on this one key point — it turns out we’re both doing even more work with AI agents\n:\nWe are all working longer hours now to support them. We have to. And that flies directly in the face of everything you’d expect from productivity software.\nIt’s a great reminder: sometimes when things change, they become even harder to do well than before — not easier.\nA few quick examples:\nThis morning I spent 2+ hours with an AI agent that was supposed to help me write my next SaaStr Annual speaker presentation. But it didn’t work. At all. It just gave up after a few minutes of flailing and said “I don’t know how to do this.” So, I ended up doing more writing than before.\nAnother example: I have 12+ AI agents that are supposed to help me with SaaStr’s content marketing. But they’re not really working yet. They’re all still figuring out what we want from them and where the boundaries should be between human editing and automated output. So, in some ways — my content team is doing even less work than before.\nAnd then there are all the AI agents that aren’t quite working yet but seem like they will soon:\nOur new SaaStr.ai agent for answering questions (like this one)\nSaaStr’s AI chatbot\nThe new Revolut credit card that has an awesome AI agent in it.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The Cowan Paradox is a brutal but important insight about what’s coming next for work and productivity.\nIt was first observed by John Cowan, an internet veteran who has been around since the early 1990s, in response to Elon Musk’s claim that AI agents would make human labor obsolete. Here’s how it works:\nLet’s say you have a team of two people doing a specific task: A and B. They can do this task together quickly because they work well as a pair. But then you introduce an AI agent into the mix – let’s call her C. C is very good at doing X, but she doesn’t understand Y or Z. So here’s what happens:\nA and B will try to train C to handle more of their workload, especially in areas where they struggle. But C will refuse.\nInstead of taking on tasks that are outside of her area of expertise, C will demand that A and B provide her with specific instructions for each task she needs help with – step-by-step documentation, exactly what inputs to use, precisely how to verify outputs. And then when you finally get something working that way…C will fail.\nThis is the Cowan Paradox in action:\nThe team’s productivity actually drops because C takes up so much time training and babysitting her, even though she can do some things faster than humans on their own. This pattern has played out in every organization we’ve worked with. The AI agent that was supposed to augment your work becomes a drain – something you have to dedicate more of your team’s attention to.\nThis is why the “AI Agents Will Let You Do Less Work” narrative doesn’t hold up in practice.", "cleaned": "The productivity paradox was first described by economist John Kendrick in 1972, but it gained fresh urgency as we entered what could be called “the age of hyper-agentic productivity.” Here’s how it works: Productivity grows faster than labor costs Labor costs decline even while total output increases Profits and growth accelerate beyond expectations But here’s where most people go wrong: they assume that increased productivity means you get to work less. It doesn’t. At least not for now. Not really. What Actually Happens When Companies Deploy AI Agents I’ve been tracking this across multiple SaaStr portfolio companies over the past 12 months, and the pattern is crystal clear — and terrifying if you’re planning your exit from tech or thinking you can coast on autopilot. Company A: From $10M ARR to $15M ARR with just one agent (same core team) Same number of sales reps Same marketing budget Same product roadmap Just … more done. Every single day. Company B: CRO who went from 70% to 95% attainment on outbound after deploying an agentic revamp. His quota didn’t change. He still has to hit the same numbers. He just got infinitely better at hitting them because his agents do the initial outreach, qualification, and follow-up automatically while he focuses on closing deals. He works harder. The difference? The ratio of human time spent actually selling versus administrative overhead dropped dramatically.", "balanced": "The ones who get ahead are the people who understand that their job isn’t to do more with less — it’s to do more with more, and make sure everyone else does too. That’s what “agentic” leadership looks like in practice. Not just using tools. Being one of them. And doing even more work than before. It’s not just your agents that will need you to be agentic. Your humans may well need this from you as well. If they don’t, they won’t really be part of your team anymore. They’re already gone if they aren’t. In any event, here’s my advice for anyone building an AI-first company right now: Don’t worry about efficiency ratios. Don’t run cost-benefit analyses on whether you can afford those new hires. Those numbers were always fake anyway. Worry about velocity. How fast are you moving? Are you shipping features at the pace customers expect today? Is your time-to-market shrinking or expanding? Can you compete with startups that seem to move faster because they have fewer legacy systems? Because they can ship code all night long? That’s where the real advantage lives. I’ve been in B2B SaaS since 2005. I’ve built five companies. I’ve seen every major shift in software development since Ruby started becoming cool. And I’m telling you something simple but profound: the next generation of leaders will be the ones who embrace the fact that AI doesn’t give you a shortcut around hard work. It gives you a multiplier for how much great work you can actually do.", "r32": "The results were striking: We went from 10-12 hours per week to maintain our platform, with the same team and no new hires. Our agents now handle routine tasks automatically that used to consume real human time — code reviews, security monitoring, customer support escalations, compliance checks. But here’s what nobody talks about enough: we’re not doing less work overall. In many ways, we’re doing more. The difference is we’re spending that extra time on things only humans can do well : Strategic planning for product direction Complex problem-solving when agents don’t have answers Creative visioning for features and experiences Deep relationship building with customers and partners Real-time strategic thinking during live demos This isn’t just theoretical. It’s why you see founders who’ve deployed serious AI agent infrastructure reporting longer working days — often up to 80-90 hours per week. Not because they’re inefficient or bad managers. Because they’re realizing there’s actually more to do than before, even as their output has improved dramatically. The Math That Breaks Your Head Here’s where it gets really interesting (and uncomfortable). When I talk to other B2B leaders running these systems, the pattern holds across the board. Revenue grows faster than headcount. Output increases while employment remains flat. But here’s the part most people miss: productivity gains compound rapidly once you hit certain thresholds. One founder told me his engineering team was producing $1M in ARR per engineer last year. This year? $1.5M+. Same number of engineers. Higher revenue. Lower costs. What are those additional dollars coming from?", "repaired": "The people who are already working at capacity? Those same folks will be expected to do more with less human labor, not less overall work and stress.\n→\nRead the full breakdown on SaaStr.ai"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 619, "lemkin-clone-v5": 355, "lemkin-clone-v6": 287, "cleaned": 230, "balanced": 256, "r32": 255, "repaired": 34}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a LinkedIn-style post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Will Actually Increase Your Workload, Not Reduce It**\n\nI just read an article about the \"Cowan Paradox\" - and it got me thinking about what this means for SaaS founders and growth-stage companies. In a nutshell, the paradox states that when we rely more on automation tools like AI agents to help us do our jobs, they often end up making our workloads **heavier**, not lighter.\n\nThink about it: every time you automate something, you free yourself from tedious tasks... but also create new ones in its place. For example, if an AI agent starts handling a bunch of customer service requests for your company, that might mean more people need to be trained on how the agent works - and what happens when things go wrong.\n\nOr consider this: as AI agents get better at generating content, they're actually creating new types of work for marketers. Suddenly, there's an explosion of data and analytics needs around measuring and optimizing those automated campaigns!\n\nNow, I'm not saying that automation can't be a net-positive for your business. Of course it can! But the key is to recognize that **automation doesn't necessarily mean less work** - just different types of work.\n\nSo what does this mean for you as a SaaS founder or growth-stage company leader? Here are my thoughts:\n\n* Be prepared to adapt and learn alongside new automation tools.\n* Don't assume that AI agents will magically reduce your workload; instead, focus on how they can help", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The Cowan Paradox:\nWhy AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work\nA few years ago, we had a great conversation with Sam Altman at SaaStr about the future of work and AI. He made an interesting observation: “If you’re not doing more work than before, it’s probably because your agent is doing less work.”\nNow I’m seeing this everywhere, from the latest OpenAI agents to the most advanced GPT-5 models. The problem isn’t just that they need training data or configuration — although those are real challenges. The deeper issue is: AI agents make you better at what you do. They don’t replace your skills entirely (at least not yet). But here’s the thing:\nWhen an agent makes you better, it raises the bar for what “good” looks like\nYou’re getting more done in less time. And as a result, you need to deliver even higher quality work. Your AI agents aren’t just automating tasks — they’re amplifying your capabilities and pushing you toward excellence. They know exactly where you fall short. They’ve analyzed every decision, every conversation, every piece of writing you’ve produced. And then they come back with a blunt assessment: “This needs to be better.”\nThe Cowan Paradox isn’t just true for work — it’s also happening in our personal lives\nWhen AI agents make us more efficient at home (and we’re getting things done faster), the bar goes up again. We need to produce even higher quality output, whether that means cooking a perfect meal or writing a compelling email.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I just joined a new VC, and we did an internal survey on how much work each of us actually does now that there’s so many AI agents out there.\nThe results are fascinating.\n\nFirstly — no one really knows what to do with all these AI Agents yet\n:\nThey’re still figuring it out. They can’t quite get the job done for them, and they don’t know how much work each will truly save you in total. But they’ll figure it out.\nSecondly — everyone is working more hours than ever\n:\nEven if 50% of your day has an AI Agent doing half the work, at least as many hours are being put into training agents, testing them, reviewing their output … and finding new tools to make all this magic happen. It’s a lot more work in total.\nThirdly — I’m actually working longer hours than before\n:\nI know it sounds crazy. But let me be clear: 1 AI Agent cannot do the job of an entire team. Not yet, at least. So while it does save some time and effort (and that is real), you still need to work a lot more hours overall.\nThe Cowan Paradox — The More You Work At It, The Less You’ll Actually Get Done\n:\nThis one I know from the top of my head. Working on it every day for years now.\n\nIt’s not just me.  And most folks have given up using AI Agents at all today. This is why.\nBecause when you add them together — and try to use so many, and train each one", "cleaned": "But here’s what we’re actually seeing at SaaStr, and it should make you rethink your AI strategy entirely: Our team is doing more work with AI agents than without them — but they need to do more overall. Not less. This is what I call the “Cowan Paradox.” It named after Dan Cowen who first described this paradox many years ago for humans. Now it’s happening again with our AI Agents. When we deployed AI agents across sales, marketing, support, content creation, and ops, productivity went up dramatically. We closed deals faster. Response times dropped from hours to seconds. Content was published instantly instead of weeks later. But then something unexpected happened: people started working longer. Not because they wanted to (although some did). Because they had to. The same amount of output now required fewer people, so everyone else got assigned more. And as soon as that happens, you hit diminishing returns fast. Here’s why this matters massively: If you implement AI agents expecting to get more done with less effort, you will be disappointed. Very disappointed. That’s not how this works. The math doesn’t add up. You can’t have 10x output without 2-5x the human investment managing those agents, reviewing their outputs, handling edge cases, and maintaining systems. What Actually Happens With AI Agent Teams Here’s what our experience shows: Phase 1: The Hype Cycle (Weeks 1-6) Everyone gets excited when an agent handles tickets or writes emails autonomously. Managers think they’ve solved the capacity problem. Phase", "balanced": "The Cowan Paradox is named after John Randal Cowan, who coined it in his paper on “Economic Theory and Public Policy” in 1965. It’s been sitting there for over half a century waiting to be applied to our current moment. Here’s what I’ve learned from running SaaStr for almost two decades — and deploying 20+ AI agents across my portfolio companies — that every founder needs to understand right now. And this is why I've gone more distributed than ever. Yes, you can do more with less humans But no matter how much AI does your job... you have to work even harder today", "r32": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. I’ve been thinking about this for months, and it keeps coming back to me. There’s something counterintuitive happening with AI agents right now that every founder should understand — not just because it affects your hiring strategy, but because it fundamentally changes how we think about work itself. We’re All Going to Have To Do Even More Now That AI Is Here. But Not Because Of Budgets or Headcount. Because We Can. And Will. 🤖 The “Productivity Dream” vs. Reality Here’s what everyone was promised when AI agents started rolling out: you’d hire these magical systems, they’d automate all the boring stuff, free up your time, and suddenly you could do more with less. Or maybe even … do less. So many leaders are already doing less with fewer humans than ever before. It sounds great on paper. Sounds amazing. For most folks, though? It won’t be true. At least not at first. Here’s what actually happens: 🧵 The First 30 Days Are Magic (Then Reality Kicks In) Let’s say you implement an AI SDR agent, an AI support agent, an AI content generator, and an AI CRM assistant over six weeks. Each one takes some setup, some training, and then… ✅ Month One: Everything is working perfectly. The SDR agent books meetings like clockwork. Your support tickets get answered instantly during business hours. Content gets generated while you sleep. Your CRM sync", "repaired": "The Cowan Paradox is real, and it’s coming for every B2B company that thinks “AI will let me work less.” It won’t. But here’s what actually happens when you deploy true AI agents into production at scale: Your team does more than before. Not because they’re lazy or don’t care — but because the work itself has fundamentally changed. In the Age of AI we are all going to have to do much more with AI. Much, much more. That’s not a bug. That’s the new reality. And if your team isn’t ready to run with it? Someone else will. #aiagents #productivity #workculture #futureofbusiness #leadership"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 263, "lemkin-clone-v5": 255, "lemkin-clone-v6": 271, "cleaned": 249, "balanced": 104, "r32": 250, "repaired": 105}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X Post**\n\nI've been thinking a lot about the future of work, and it's not what you think.\n\nWe all thought that automation would make our lives easier, right? That machines would take over the drudgery and we'd be free to focus on high-level creative stuff. But I'm here to tell you: **that's just plain wrong**.\n\nIntroducing The Cowan Paradox:\n\nAs AI agents become more advanced, they'll actually MAKE YOU DO MORE WORK... not less!\n\nThink about it. When was the last time your email inbox got smaller? Or your task list shorter? Never! And that's because every new feature or tool we add just creates a whole new wave of work to manage and keep up with.\n\nI'm calling this The Cowan Par", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The Cowan Paradox:\nWhy AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.\nI don’t believe in the “AI will do it all” narrative that’s sweeping the business world right now. I think we’re closer to seeing how truly powerful AI can be, but not quite there yet. At least for 99% of what most people actually do on a daily basis.\n\nHere’s why:\n\n1. The Cowan Paradox: “The only way you can get more done is by doing less work”\n\nIs that still true? In the age of AI, I think it’s the opposite now:\nYou have to do much more work because your AI agents are going to require so much more data and training\n\n2. But here’s what we’re", "lemkin-clone-v6": "It’s not a bug — it’s a feature\nA few weeks back I was talking to one of my first VCs, Dave Cowan. He’d seen me through 3 companies now and we’ve done some amazing things together on the venture side as well.\n\nAnd he had this crazy insight that blew me away: The AI Paradox\n\nHere’s what’s happening:\nAI Agents Are Great\nThey’re so great at doing everything — building, writing, marketing, selling. They can do it faster. They never get tired. And they don’t need any training or support in the classical sense.\n\nThe Problem Isn’t Training\nIt’s not that AI agents are poorly trained. It’s that we’ve trained them to be incredibly effective — at doing more work.\n\nBut here’s what Dave", "cleaned": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. Here’s what nobody wants to talk about when they’re hyping their latest AI agent purchase. When you buy a new tool that makes your team more efficient, do you actually work less? Or do you just … do more with it? I’ve been thinking about this a lot lately because we hit our first “Cowan Paradox” at SaaStr itself. It happened when we rolled out Artisan — our outbound AI agent for sponsor sales and partnership outreach. We already have 12+ AI agents running across our stack from Qualified (inbound), Clay (revops), AgentForce (sales automation), Momentum (customer success), Decagon (our internal AI manager),", "balanced": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. I’ve been thinking a lot lately about what’s going to happen when we get into production with our first AI agents at SaaStr — not just demos, but actually running them on real problems that need solving every single day. And here’s something that keeps coming up in conversations with other founders and operators who are deep in this too: We’re all excited to deploy these agents because they’ll let us do more with less. Fewer humans. Better outcomes. Easier scaling. It sounds like the dream. But it may be a nightmare for your personal life. Or at least, you have to think harder than you might expect about how you want to work now. The", "r32": "The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More. I just did something that should have taken me maybe 30 minutes. It took me three hours. But it was better than good—it was great, and here’s why: I built a SaaStr Agent for our new VC pitch deck review service using Replit. Not by writing code. By describing what I wanted to happen. I told the agent we needed a custom website where VCs could upload their decks anonymously. No form required—just drag-and-drop file upload with instant feedback on valuation ranges based on company stage and growth metrics. Then, once uploaded, users would see personalized investor matching with firms that actually fund companies like theirs. The kind of thing that used to", "repaired": "The Cowan Paradox:\n\nWhy AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.\n\nI’ve been running B2B SaaS companies for over two decades, and I’m seeing something that feels like a fundamental shift — not just because of LLMs and agentic AI, but because it’s happening at every company I work with now.\n\nHere’s what people are telling me again and again:\n\n“My team is working more than ever before.”\n\nNot harder. Not better. More."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 124, "lemkin-clone-v5": 126, "lemkin-clone-v6": 125, "cleaned": 120, "balanced": 133, "r32": 130, "repaired": 79}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Cowan Paradox: Why AI Agents Won’t Let You Do Less Work. They’ll Make You Do More.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder of SaaStr and entrepreneur extraordinaire:\n\n**[The stage is set, lights are bright, and I'm standing at the mic.]**\n\nLadies and gentlemen, today we're going to tackle one of my favorite paradoxes: The Cowan Paradox. Now, most of you might not know what that even means, but trust me, it's going to blow your mind.\n\nThe Cowan Paradox was first proposed by physicist James P. Cowan in the 1970s. It basically says this:\n\n\"The more efficient a system becomes at automating tasks, the less free time we get.\"\n\nNow, you might be thinking, \"Wait, isn't automation supposed to make our lives easier? Shouldn't AI agents free us up from mundane tasks so we can focus on high-level strategy and innovation?\"\n\nWell, no. That's exactly what I'm here to debunk.\n\n**[pause for comedic effect]**\n\nThink about it: if you automate a task, do you suddenly get more time off the clock? No! You might even find yourself working longer hours because...\n\n1. **The system is now dependent on your input**: AI agents are only as good as their training data. If they're making decisions based on patterns in that data, someone needs to feed them new information to keep it up-to-date.\n2. **The bottleneck shifts downstream**: While you might not be doing the task itself anymore, others will inevitably rely on you for support and decision-making, just like you did before. The system doesn't eliminate human intervention; it merely moves it further upstream.\n\n**[example time]**\n\nLet me illustrate this with an example from my own experience as a founder of SaaStr. When we first launched our podcast, I thought automating the transcription process would save me hours every week. And you know what? It did... for about two weeks! Then, suddenly, I found myself reviewing and editing transcripts to ensure accuracy and quality.\n\nWhy? Because while AI had taken over some of the grunt work, it also created new dependencies downstream – like ensuring those transcripts were accurate enough that we could actually use them as content. Talk about a paradox!\n\n**[key point]**\n\nSo here's the key takeaway: **Automation doesn't necessarily free us up from work; it just changes what kind of work we do**. We might not be doing manual tasks anymore, but our responsibilities expand to oversee and optimize those automated systems.\n\nNow, I know some of you are thinking, \"Jason, this is a bit scary.\" And you're right – it's unsettling to think that no matter how efficient AI gets, we'll still be bound by the constraints of human complexity. But here's the thing: **that's exactly what makes automation so valuable in the first place**.\n\nIn an age where companies are competing on ever-tighter profit margins and increasingly complex technology landscapes, our ability to adapt – not just as individuals but as teams – will be more crucial than ever.\n\nSo go ahead, automate those tasks. But also expect that your workload might change, evolve, or expand in ways you never anticipated. That's The Cowan Paradox: **The AI paradox that says no matter how efficient we become at automating work... we'll still have to do more**.\n\nThank you for bearing with me on this fascinating – if not a bit unsettling – topic!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So there’s this paradox, isn’t it? We’re all hiring these great AI agents and we’re hoping they make our lives easier. But they don’t, do they? In fact the opposite is happening to many of us. Maybe even you.\nWe hired an AI agent that did marketing for us. It was supposed to be the magic solution. We didn’t have time anymore. And it worked — until it just stopped working and we had no idea why or how to fix it. Then after a few weeks, we were back doing all our own marketing again ourselves.\nWe hired an AI agent that did support for us. It was supposed to be the magic solution. We didn’t have time anymore. And it worked — until it just stopped working and we had no idea why or how to fix it. Then after a few weeks, we were back doing all our own support again ourselves.\nWe hired an AI agent that did sales for us. It was supposed to be the magic solution. We didn’t have time anymore. And it worked — until it just stopped working and we had no idea why or how to fix it. Then after a few weeks, we were back doing all our own sales again ourselves.\nAnd so on and so forth. I mean, we even hired an AI agent that did customer success for us. It was supposed to be the magic solution. We didn’t have time anymore. And it worked — until it just stopped working and we had no idea why or how to fix it. Then after a few weeks, we were back doing all our own CS again ourselves.\nThis is what’s happening to so many of us in SaaS, and I’m not sure it will ever get much better. Maybe this is the future we’re going into — one where every single agent you hire just sort of stops working for no good reason after a few weeks or months, then you have to go back do all that work yourself again.\nI mean, I did ask Cowen about this at SaaStr AI London 2025. He said “Yes. It’s not going away.”\nAnd why? Why would your AI agents stop working for no good reason after a few weeks? Well maybe they’re just trying to make sure you do more work. Because that seems to be what we all keep getting.\nNow, it may seem obvious as I say this that we need better tools here. But the Cowan Paradox is very real. And now AI agents don’t let you off the hook. They force you to do your own marketing again — even if they sort of work in theory. And they force you to go back and manage all those leads again.\nThe Cowen Paradox: We’re getting 10x better at doing things that are hard, but we’re not getting 1/10th as good at doing the easy stuff. The hard stuff is a lot easier today than it was in 2022 or before. It’s just much harder to get off the hook of work if you want your AI agents and apps to really do all that magic for you.\nThe reality is, this may be our future. That we’ll have tools that seem magical — but not quite enough yet to take us out of the loop of doing all the hard stuff ourselves.\nWe’ve got 2 years before things are going to get even harder.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I don't know what your experience is, but I've been in AI for 18 months now and it's only gotten harder to do less work.\n\nIt should be easier right? We have all these tools out there today. So we should be able to get more done with the same amount of time or even less. And that's true at the top end, but here's what I've found in AI. Here's my cowan paradox and it is this: every single tool I use now makes me do more work than last year.\n\nAnd that just doesn't make sense right? But there are two reasons why it's true. Let's go into some of the tools we all use today, but maybe not as much as we should in our AI Agents. First, let's talk about Claude Code. Claude code is one of the first things I used to try and save me time with my AI Agent.\n\nClaude Code is a great tool for training models if you're not technical or don't want to train your own model from scratch on Anthropic's platform. And it has gotten better over time, but what I've found is that every single iteration of Claude code makes it more work to do less work. It used to be pretty easy with Claude Code, just take one prompt and you could get an answer.\n\nAnd then they updated the model a few months ago. Now you can't use it without 10 seconds or something like this. You got to do all these different prompts. So there's no way I'm going to go from 30 minutes per question down into seconds with Claude Code.", "cleaned": "It's not really that easy to find someone with your domain expertise and you're going to have to manage it, train it, teach it, iterate on its outputs and so I think what we've seen is uh there's definitely been an acceleration as people try new things but I don't know if it's quite at the scale where companies are replacing humans en masse yet um but I do believe that over time this will happen because there just isn't enough human talent out there to support all these functions and so automation will become more prevalent um but for now it's still early days yeah well look let me ask you one last question before I lose you which maybe we can wrap up on um and then I'll get some other folks in here okay so so much has changed since March 25th when this first podcast came out right a lot has happened a lot has changed so how would you distill all those learnings into one bit of advice for Founders today what would be number one if they were sitting here in the room with us I think I'd say focus on building software for real customers in real verticals even if it takes longer than expected build software for real customers who are willing to pay you for software that solves their problems um that's where the value lies and then also keep in mind that there might be situations where you need to work twice as hard or three times harder as before to actually grow faster and better so thanks everybody for coming um we're gonna take a couple minutes break after this um next week I'm going to bring back David Frankel who was our guest from day one of the podcast he's now running revenue ops at", "balanced": "AI is going to be great at that, and then you'll have to do even more than before because it's so good. And if you're not careful, this will suck all your time into customer success and support, which I'm seeing happen already with many customers that are moving from humans to agentic solutions for their first line of support. Their CS teams get sucked down into doing more work. Not less work. So we've got to watch out for this paradox. We've got to make sure our team is actually working on things that matter as much or more than they used to. Because otherwise, it's just going to consume them. Thank you for watching sastra.com and see you next week.", "r32": "We've all been trained to think that AI is going to free us from work, right? That we're gonna deploy these agents and they'll do our jobs for us. And I see this again and again with Founders when you go meet them at SaaStr Annual or wherever. They come up and it's like, \"Oh my god, what are your secret agents?\" Like there's some magic agent running the company. No, no, no. There isn't one. But here's the thing that's important if you haven't thought about this before. It turns out there's an old social psychology concept called the Cowan paradox named after a guy named Eliot Cowan who was writing papers on this back in the early days of cognitive science in the 1960s and seventies. We never heard of him because he wasn't a big name. He worked at MIT. He did some interesting stuff. But basically he said, people don't get more efficient by getting better tools. People actually take longer to complete tasks when their tooling gets better. Now why would anyone say such a crazy thing? Think about how this works. What happens when someone has terrible software for something simple, like editing documents? When they have to edit a document, they're like, \"You know what? I'm just not going to finish that paragraph.\" Right? Or they spend two hours rewriting sentences over and over until it finally looks good enough. Then you give 'em a great editor. Suddenly they can ship three paragraphs a minute. They write three times as much content than they could ever write before. So their output goes way up. This is the classic efficiency story. But then let me ask you: how long does each piece of content last?", "repaired": "So I've been investing and working with B2B companies for over two decades, seen countless startups go from zero to nine figures in revenue. And you know what? Almost every single one of them is doing more work now than they were before. Not less. More. What's happening here isn't that we're getting lazier or less motivated. It's exactly the opposite. We're just not seeing it because our tools are hiding all the extra effort from us. That's the paradox. And if you don't get this, your startup will die. Here's why."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 544, "lemkin-clone-v5": 581, "lemkin-clone-v6": 277, "cleaned": 307, "balanced": 122, "r32": 291, "repaired": 92}}}}, {"id": "gold_023", "topic": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain when custom internal AI software is the right choice.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that AI lowers the cost of building narrow internal tools, which makes custom software a smarter choice in some cases than it used to be. The best candidates are high-value workflows with very specific requirements that off-the-shelf tools handle poorly. The lesson is not to build everything, but to recognize when a tailored app now has unusually high leverage.", "facts": ["The answer should argue that some AI-native workflows are now cheap enough to build internally.", "It should explain that the build-vs-buy line has shifted for narrow, custom workflows.", "It should caution that not every internal use case deserves custom software."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Rise of the \"N=1\" App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It**\n\n Ah, the allure of buying an off-the-shelf software solution. Who wouldn't want to save time and money by leveraging someone else's hard work? But let me tell you a secret: sometimes, building it yourself is still the best bet.\n\nI'm talking about what I like to call \"N=1\" apps – custom-built solutions tailored specifically for your business needs. These days, with the rise of low-code development platforms and accessible APIs, it's never been easier or more cost-effective to build an app from scratch.\n\n**The N=1 Advantage**\n\nSo why should you consider building a bespoke app instead of buying one? For starters:\n\n* **Customization**: An off-the-shelf solution will always have trade-offs. You'll either get something that fits 90% of your needs, or spend months customizing it to fit the remaining 10%. But an N=1 app is designed from day one with your business in mind – no compromises necessary.\n* **Security**: When you buy a software solution, you're essentially putting trust in someone else's code. With an N=1 app, you have complete control over security protocols and compliance measures.\n* **Scalability**: As your business grows, so do its needs. A custom-built app can be scaled up or down with ease – no need for costly upgrades or migrations.\n\n**The Economics of Building vs Buying**\n\nOf course, the biggest objection to building an N=1 app is usually cost. But here's a dirty little secret: buying software often doesn't save you money in the long run. Consider this:\n\n* **License fees**: Off-the-shelf solutions come with hefty license costs – not just upfront, but also ongoing subscription fees.\n* **Implementation time and resources**: Customizing an off-the-shelf solution can be costly, especially if it requires significant development or configuration effort.\n\nIn contrast, building a custom app using low-code platforms like Bubble, Adalo, or even traditional coding languages, means you only pay for what you need – no unnecessary features, no wasted resources. And with the rise of cloud-based services and serverless architecture, development costs are plummeting.\n\n**The Rise of N=1 App Builders**\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking: \"But Jason, building an app from scratch is still a daunting task.\" Ah, not necessarily! With the proliferation of low-code platforms and accessible APIs, it's never been easier to build a custom app without needing to hire a team of experienced developers.\n\nPlatforms like Bubble are democratizing app development – providing user-friendly interfaces for non-technical users (yes, you read that right) and empowering entrepreneurs with no coding background to create bespoke solutions.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nSo the next time someone tells you \"just buy an off-the-shelf solution,\" politely smile and nod. You know what's really possible: building a custom app tailored precisely to your business needs – and maybe even saving money while doing it.\n\nIn this era of digital transformation, businesses that refuse to build bespoke solutions will fall behind the pack. Don't get left in the dust! Build an N=1 app today and unlock the true potential of your organization.\n\n**What do you think? Share your thoughts on building vs buying software in the comments below!**\n\n(Note: This blog post is inspired by Jason Lemkin's style, but please keep in mind that it's not written by him. It's a hypothetical representation based on his public persona and writing tone.)", "lemkin-clone-v5": "In the old days, you’d buy an app and that would be it.\nThen came the early SaaS era — and every vendor wanted to build something for everyone.  The same features, with a few slight tweaks per product.  But they were all just variations of each other.\nToday?  We’re in a new world.  Where your own custom app or solution often beats buying it off the shelf.  At least for now, at least until you scale up past about $2m-$5m ARR.  Here’s why — and what we did with SaaStr.ai.\nWhen I first started talking about building your own version of an “app” using AI tools in 2023-24, folks were skeptical. But then something happened. The pace of innovation accelerated to the point where it became impossible for vendors to keep up. For example:\nGong’s $20k+ per month contact intelligence tool — built by a vendor with over $1B in ARR\nIs getting dramatically outpaced by AI tools like Claude that are free or under $10/month.\nThe same thing happened with AI coding assistants, and now more than ever in 2025-26.  We’re seeing not just individual apps being replaced but entire categories of software redefined at the speed of a week, rather than months or years.\nSaaStr.ai is our latest example — we’ve built an entirely custom platform using Replit, LLMs, and more that has become one of the best tools for both marketers and sales professionals to optimize their messaging.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So one thing we don’t talk enough on SaaStr about is when to build it yourself, and when buying something makes more sense.\nIt’s a trade-off that can make your life much easier — but also very expensive in time and energy if you do get into the wrong game here. So let me just share my top 5 learning from building our own N=1 apps for SaaStr itself:\n#1. Building Your Own Apps Beats Buying It When You Have a Deep, Very Specific Use Case\nI’ve talked about this before with our own internal app “SaaStr AI” — but the point bears repeating.\nWhen we did, we built it because no other solution worked as well for us. I don’t mean there was nothing good out there, but what existed at that time just didn’t work in a very specific way we needed. We had to build something that could do “a lot” — which is hard enough on its own — and then integrate with our existing stack in ways no one else did.\nNow it’s not for everyone. You have to be deep into the problem, know how to solve it (at least a bit), or at least know where to get started. But if you do … building yourself will often beat buying when:\nYou need custom integrations that no one else does\nYour workflows are truly unique and complex — not just different\nIt’s mission-critical but has some edge cases, too\n#2. “N=1” Apps Have a Much Higher Bar To Cross Than Your Average SaaS App\nThe bar is actually much higher to cross for us N=1 apps compared to your average software app.\nA few reasons:\nWe use them 24/7, often with critical data\nThey usually have some edge case or two that no one else does\nWe’ll always have a very deep understanding of the problem and solution — so we can fix it faster than anyone else.", "cleaned": "But when you need something specific, unique, and fast — an n=1 app is often the right answer. And it’s getting better every single week. Your customers will love this sort of magic. Go build some.", "balanced": "The problem is that most B2B software was built for enterprises, not teams of one. You don’t need 20 seats or multi-level approval flows. Just you and your data. Most B2B apps are trying to solve problems you might not even have yet — but they’re charging you for them anyway. Or worse, they’ll charge you $50/month for something you only use once a week. And then you end up paying for features you never asked for just because you can’t cancel without losing access to what actually works. Here’s where it gets interesting : I’ve been using Replit for over a year now. Not as some kind of novelty project, but as my actual daily workflow tool. My website? Built on Replit. SaaStr.ai? Also Replit. A big chunk of our AI Agents and more? Replit too. Why? Because every time I go to buy a new app, I ask myself: Is this going to be worth the hassle of setting up another login? Learning their UI? Figuring out how to get my specific thing done with THEIR tools? Probably not. So instead, I build. Or at least, someone builds me an agent to do it. For real. No setup required. What This Means for Founders If you’re building SaaS right now (and if you’re reading this, chances are good you are), here’s what you should take away from all this: Your users are getting better at building than buying. That’s a fundamental shift. Instead of fighting against this, lean into it. Think about what happens when everyone becomes a potential developer. Your moat isn’t security anymore.", "r32": "The N=1 app is an important one, and we’re all going to be building more of them. They aren’t always that great, or feature rich, but they can do just what you need. And so often, at least until later — this makes it simpler than buying something else off-the-shelf. What’s your #n=1 app? If nothing else, take some time over the next few weeks and see if you could build it yourself. Even if it’s not perfect yet. And even if there are some bugs. But you know how much better it would work for you. Not for everyone. For you. That might be worth doing. You can start here with Zapier + Replit. Just click on their new Agent page, tell it what you want to automate … and watch it go from scratch in minutes. Then ask it to add any other apps you want into the workflow. And get instant feedback on whether it will work as-is. I built my first N=1 app right after SaaStr Annual last year using Claude Code and Replie. It was pretty cool. It took me about 5 hours to build (and most of those were tweaking) and it already had 20,000+ uses. A bit more here. So I thought it would be fun to try again with Agentforce, which also lets you quickly turn Claude prompts into apps. So far, I’ve built three N=1 apps in less than a week. All for things I wanted to solve today, now. One was for tracking our podcast episodes across 6 platforms, another for updating our LinkedIn posts automatically every morning, and another to track who attended our VIP event across multiple systems. They worked well enough to use immediately, and then needed a little tuning.", "repaired": "The N=1 app is software that’s so specialized, so deeply integrated with your specific workflow and data, that no vendor offers it as a core feature — or if they do, it costs $50K/year plus engineering time to configure. These apps solve real problems but have limited appeal beyond their narrow use case. Think:\nYour custom Slack integration for support ticket routing\nThat one dashboard you built showing revenue by rep AND by month AND by cohort\nThe Zapier workflow connecting Salesforce contacts to Gong transcripts\nYour Google Sheets sheet calculating pipeline velocity by deal stage\nThese are all powerful tools that make you more efficient. But would anyone else pay for them? Probably not at the price point where you’d need to sell them externally. What makes something an N=1 app? Three characteristics. First, there’s no off-the-shelf solution available. Not even close. Second, it requires deep domain-specific knowledge to build right. Third, once you’ve got it working … it really works. And maybe most importantly, fourth, it can’t be easily hacked or kludged into existence by someone without intimate, hands-on experience of how things work in your organization. I’m living this right now. Our content marketing stack has gotten way too complicated. We needed a tool to analyze which events attract which types of sponsors (high vs low ACV). Nothing existed that fit our needs perfectly. So I built one using Replit and Claude Code. Within two hours, we had insights on event sponsorship engagement patterns across four years of historical data. This was mission-critical for planning future strategy. No third-party tool could give us what we needed because they either didn’t exist or were overkill. A related story from SaaStr Fund portfolio company Owner.com here:\nWhy AI Will Change How Apps Are Built\nWhat This Means For B2B Software\nThe N=1 app revolution isn’t just about building bespoke solutions anymore."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 556, "lemkin-clone-v5": 242, "lemkin-clone-v6": 325, "cleaned": 36, "balanced": 267, "r32": 293, "repaired": 312}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a LinkedIn-style post in the tone of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**The Rise of the \"N=1\" App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It**\n\nAs I've built and sold several successful SaaS companies, one thing has become increasingly clear to me: **sometimes, building it yourself is still the best option**.\n\nIn today's world of scalable software, we're constantly being told that buying an existing solution or platform will save us time, money, and resources. And often, it does... for a while.\n\nBut let's be honest - there are still plenty of scenarios where **custom-built solutions crush off-the-shelf alternatives**. I call these \"N=1\" apps: bespoke software built specifically to meet the needs of one company or founder.\n\nHere are just a few reasons why building your own solution might beat buying it:\n\n Customization isn't optional - when you build something yourself, you can tailor every feature and function to fit your exact use case. No more compromises on \"good enough\".\n\n Time-to-market is compressed - with an N=1 app, you can skip the months (or years) of waiting for a vendor to integrate their solution into yours.\n\n Cost savings abound - building in-house means no subscription fees, implementation costs, or ongoing support expenses.\n\n Innovation accelerates - when you're not bound by someone else's codebase or product roadmap, you can iterate and innovate faster than anyone else.\n\nOf course, there are still plenty of situations where buying an existing solution makes sense. But if you're looking to gain a competitive edge, out", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The \"N=1\" app is dead, and I'm glad it's dead\nIt used to be you'd have one app for marketing, another for sales, a third for service, etc.\nNot anymore - we've seen 4+ years of consolidation in SaaS into vertical suites. The winners are the ones with everything - HubSpot, Salesforce, Zendesk, Gainsight...\nBut what about me? What if I want to do it myself?\nWhat I see is founders doing N=1 apps themselves these days\n- They build their own marketing and sales tools.\n- Many of them aren't even very good. But they're bespoke. And the best part - you don't have to pay any monthly fees for them, or deal with vendors.\nThe issue isn't that we can't do it all anymore. We've gotten so much better at integrating apps into vertical suites. It's too expensive and complicated.\nBut founders are a different story\nThey'll build whatever they want themselves, even if no one else would buy it. They will make the time because this is their app for life. And that’s why we’re seeing such an explosion of N=1 tools built by the CTOs, VPs, and Founders.\nThe best part? You can get them to do anything you want\nAnd if they don't like what it does... well, the CTO will just make it better themselves. That's what I see more than ever these days - founders building whatever apps they need or want for their own company.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The best way to make a ton of money is often building an app that makes you more efficient. And then charging others for it.\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 2, 2024\nWe’re all so busy in SaaS today and the economy isn’t helping much — that’s why we see so many apps being built by individuals or small teams to make their own lives easier. I call this “N=1” because they are just solving a problem for themselves.\nBut … it often works!\nI’ve seen this with my own team at SaaStr, and now more than ever in the era of AI. When you’re not spending your time fighting an app — that’s when things really take off! You can do so much more than ever before.", "cleaned": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. This is something I think more and more of us are doing today, especially founders and C-suite execs. We used to have 2 choices: Buy an off-the-shelf app (and it wasn’t always great) Or pay for some sort of customization on top of that same app But what if you could just build your own? What if you could take all those APIs out there — HubSpot, Salesforce, Stripe, etc. — and just … roll your own solution? That’s exactly what we’ve done at SaaStr with our AI Agents. They don’t really exist yet as products anyone can buy. So we built them ourselves using the latest tools from Replit, Claude Opus and more. Now we use these agents to answer every single question folks ask on LinkedIn, X and elsewhere. And they’re better than any answers or responses we got before. Better because they’re trained on our content over the last decade! Our AI Mentor has answered well over 10,000 questions now, across many different topics and industries. Not one single human ever would be able to do this. No amount of training would get it right. You probably need this too. An N=1 AI Agent customized specifically around how you want it to work. Not buying someone else’s product. That may still come later, but for now, build your own. The other day a leading vendor in my space said they were going long-term enterprise and not SMB anymore. Because they only had time", "balanced": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. I’ve been building my own apps for years, but never as much as now. And it’s not because we don’t have great vendors. We do! But sometimes you just need an app that doesn’t exist … or at least, isn’t quite right for your use case and needs. So what should you build yourself? Or buy from someone else? I think there are clear answers to this today: Build If You Want To Get Truly Granular on Your Data. If you want to understand how your customers truly interact with your product — really see what they’re doing when no one is looking — build something custom. Don’t rely on dashboards that summarize everything nicely. Not only can these be incomplete (what’s hiding in those other tabs?), but they rarely tell you the full story. I built a tool that scrapes our SaaStr website anonymously every day. No login required. Every page view, every click, every session duration. This gives me granular insights into user behavior across all touchpoints. No vendor dashboard could provide this level of detail without months of setup and customization. Buy If You Need Something That Already Exists and Works Well. For anything off-the-shelf, especially integrations between multiple tools, I’m increasingly saying: buy it. Why reinvent integration plumbing when someone has already solved it? Unless you have deep technical resources and time, it usually makes sense to purchase software that does exactly what you need rather than trying to customize generic solutions.", "r32": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. I’ve been doing this for years, but it’s only gotten worse — and better. We use 100s of apps at SaaStr itself. But more than ever now … we build our own. Why? Well, first off, there are fewer great vendors than before to buy from. Many have gone under or merged with each other. And secondly, many products just don’t work anymore as well as they used to. Sometimes because the vendor has moved on to bigger customers and no longer cares about smaller ones like us. So what do you do if an app that once worked well … doesn’t really work so well today? You try to get help from customer success. But that rarely works. Then you email support. But often, that never gets answered either. Then after weeks of frustration, you decide to … build your own. This is why we’re seeing the rise of N=1 apps (n=1 being Notion). Yes, you can probably pay $50/month for some workflow tool that does something similar. Or even $500/month. Maybe even $5k! But maybe, instead, you just want to solve the problem yourself using Notion, Replit, Lovable, Claude, etc. That’s fine too. Especially these days when so many tools aren’t quite working right anymore. In fact, I think building your own little custom solution may be the best choice when you need a customized solution — and one that simply isn’t available yet", "repaired": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It. We’re seeing something interesting happen across SaaStr, and I think it’s going to become more common as AI-native tools mature — and we’re not talking about building custom interfaces on top of existing platforms (although that happens too). We’re talking about just plain old-fashioned app-building for your own use cases. And often … just plain better than buying off-the-shelf apps or even most vendor-made add-ons. A few examples from what we’ve built ourselves at SaaStr: Our AI Mentor & VC Matchmaker This is probably our best example yet. Instead of trying to find an existing tool that could match VCs with founders based on specific criteria like stage, industry, and portfolio preferences… we built one. Not because we have unlimited engineering resources, but because nothing existed that did exactly what we needed it to do. So we took Replit + Claude Opus 4.5, asked them to build us a simple matching algorithm, and now we get hundreds of founder-VC connections every month. Better than anything else out there, because it knows our community specifics cold. An N=1 AI VP of Marketing That Actually Works In a different context, this might be considered crazy. But here’s the thing: most marketing automation tools are still basically spreadsheets with dashboards bolted on. They don’t actually make decisions. They can’t handle exceptions. They can’t adapt mid-campaign. So instead of finding another mediocre solution, we built a simple LLM-based agent"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 249, "lemkin-clone-v5": 243, "lemkin-clone-v6": 133, "cleaned": 260, "balanced": 260, "r32": 250, "repaired": 249}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a mock X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"The Rise of the 'N=1' App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It**\n\nI've been saying this for years, but it bears repeating: building your own app is often (dare I say... usually?) better than buying one off-the-shelf.\n\nWhat's driving this trend? The \"N=1\" effect. Every company wants to be unique. They want their app to reflect their brand, values, and specific needs. And guess what? That's hard to replicate with a generic, cookie-cutter solution from some big vendor.\n\nI've seen it time and again: companies buy an app thinking they'll \"just customize\" it... only to end up with something that looks like", "lemkin-clone-v5": "The Rise of the \"N=1\" App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.\n\nIt's not always better to buy software off the shelf, folks. Sometimes, it's just a lot simpler and cheaper to build what you need yourself. A bit more on that here:\n\nIf you're going to sell something - own it completely first\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚫️🗡️ (@jasonlk)\nJanuary 13, 2025\nAnd don't be afraid to do a little \"N=1\" testing. It's not perfect, but sometimes that will get you further than buying something someone else built for some subset of your needs.\n\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚫️🗡", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The N=1 App is here to stay\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin #🏠👋 (@jasonlk)\nSeptember 10, 2025\nI recently had a great discussion with a SaaS CEO that I’d invested in about the “N=1” app. What’s an N-1 App? It’s when you build something yourself to solve your own specific needs — but maybe it doesn’t quite fit what others would buy as a traditional product.\nA while back, I built my own simple AI SDR agent myself for our outbound sales. It didn’t really work out too well in the end (see below), at least not compared to our top-performing agents from Artisan and other top vendors. But it was also fun", "cleaned": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "balanced": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "r32": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "repaired": "The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.\n\nThe idea that you can just buy a tool and have it work for your specific use case is dead.\nSaaS companies are great at making general products, but terrible at making specialized ones. \nhttps://saastr.com/the-rise-of-the-n-1-app/\n…"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 115, "lemkin-clone-v5": 104, "lemkin-clone-v6": 113, "cleaned": 14, "balanced": 14, "r32": 14, "repaired": 50}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: The Rise of the “N=1” App: When Building It Yourself Really Beats Buying It.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Rise of the \"N=1\" App**\n\nGood morning, everyone! I'm thrilled to be talking to you today about something that's near and dear to my heart: building your own product.\n\nYou know, in this world of tech, there are a lot of opinions on how to build a successful company. And let me tell you, most people will tell you the same thing: \"Just buy it!\" Buy an existing solution, hire someone else to build it for you, or even worse, outsource it to some unknown developer.\n\nBut I'm here to tell you that's not always the best approach. In fact, in many cases, building it yourself can be a game-changer. That's right; I said it: building it yourself is often better than buying it.\n\n**The Problem with Buying It**\n\nNow, before we dive into why this is true, let me give you some context. As founders, we're always looking for ways to optimize our business and make more efficient use of resources. And one way to do that is by leveraging existing products or services.\n\nBut here's the thing: when you buy an off-the-shelf solution, you're not just paying for a product; you're also paying for someone else's vision, their design decisions, and their underlying technology stack. You're stuck with what they created, even if it doesn't perfectly align with your business needs.\n\n**The Benefits of Building It Yourself**\n\nOn the other hand, when you build something yourself, you have complete control over every aspect of the product. That means you can tailor it to fit your specific use case, make changes as needed, and iterate on its design without having to go through a third party.\n\nBut that's not all. When you're building an app from scratch, you also get to retain 100% of the value created by that product. You don't have to pay someone else royalties or deal with licensing agreements. And let me tell you, those costs can add up quickly.\n\n**The \"N=1\" App**\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking: \"Jason, this all sounds great in theory, but how do we actually make it work?\" Well, my friends, that's where the \"N=1\" app comes into play.\n\nAn N=1 (short for \"one-to-one\") app is a custom-built solution designed specifically to meet the unique needs of your business. It's not an off-the-shelf product; it's something tailored just for you.\n\nThink about it like this: when you build an N=1 app, you're essentially creating a one-of-a-kind product that can't be replicated by anyone else. And in today's competitive market, where every company is trying to stand out from the crowd, that's a huge advantage.\n\n**Case Study**\n\nLet me give you an example of how this worked for us at SaaStr. We had built an app called SaaStr Fundraising Software, which was used by hundreds of startups and small businesses to raise capital. But what we realized is that every company has different needs when it comes to fundraising.\n\nSo, instead of trying to create a generic product that would appeal to everyone, we decided to build custom N=1 apps for our most prominent clients. We tailored each app to fit their specific use case and requirements.\n\nThe result? Those companies were able to raise more capital faster than ever before. And the best part? They loved us for it! The level of satisfaction was off the charts because they had a product that truly met their unique needs.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nSo, what's the takeaway from all this?\n\nBuilding an app yourself can be a game-changer for your business. It gives you complete control over every aspect of the product and allows you to tailor", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Hello, everyone and thanks for joining us today on SaaStr! So I’m not sure if you noticed, but there’s something really interesting happening with software right now. And it is an evolution to what we call the “N=1” app. An N of 1 app has one user. Now that may sound a little bit strange and even counter-intuitive for us in SaaS. Because at first glance it would seem like you can’t really build a real product when there’s only one customer. The problem with most software today is it was built to solve the problems of many, not just yours. And as we all know, that often leads to mediocre results and suboptimal solutions. So what exactly does this N=1 app look like? Well let me give you an example from my own personal experience. I needed a way to automate some paperwork for my company’s HR processes. Specifically it was getting hard to get every employee onboarding documents signed digitally in the right place, at the right time and with the right permissions. The problem is that no existing software really solved this problem well enough. So we decided to build our own N=1 app ourselves. Here are a few things about this product. First it’s built specifically for us, not as general HR or onboarding document signing tool. That makes sense because we’re trying to solve very unique problems specific to our business. Second the data is all proprietary and ours alone. It is not part of any larger public database that someone else could access. And third, this app has a totally different architecture than traditional SaaS software would need.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I’m not sure I totally get it, but N=1 apps are a thing now\n— SaaStr (@saastr)\nNovember 29, 2025\nSo you may or may not have heard of “N=1” applications.  You know them.  It’s your homegrown CRM built in Notion. Your own AI agent for customer support. Or maybe just a custom version of an existing app.\nIt looks like N=1 apps are everywhere now, and I don’t totally get it either.  But they seem to be working — at least well enough for founders to keep using them instead of buying from someone else, even if that means doing all the work themselves.\nN=1 applications aren’t really a new thing\n.   I built my first homegrown CRM in 2006 and used it through our Series A.  It was so good I never wanted to buy anything better!  But at least back then we had no other choice but to use whatever apps were available, and there wasn’t the massive array of tools today that you can bolt together into your own bespoke solution.\nBut maybe now is different because of AI\n?  Maybe it’s easier than ever before for folks to build their own CRM or homegrown version of Salesforce.  That makes sense — because we have so many better tools, and they’re all AI-powered and so much more powerful than even just a few years ago. And it seems like these “N=1” apps are everywhere now.  If you look at my Twitter feed, for example:\nMy own homegrown CRM in Notion\nAI agent from Glean that really works well.\nBuilt our own custom customer support AI agent on top of Replit and ChatGPT\nOur new N+1 app to handle all the SaaS companies we work with.  It’s so good I never want to use any other tool again!\nAnd it seems like there are a lot of folks, at least a few dozen or more, building their own AI-powered agents for customer support on top of Replit and ChatGPT:\nI built an agent using Chat GPT that can handle my entire email funnel\nMy homegrown version of Salesforce in Notion.  It’s so good I never want to buy anything else again!\nWhy are N=1 apps everywhere now? I think it’s because we have so many better tools, and they’re all AI-powered and so much more powerful than even just a few years ago.\nSo what should you do about this trend?\nFirst of all — don’t try it unless your life will really be easier\n.  Building out some bespoke app is never going to make sense for most people.  You already have too many apps, too many tools and vendors.  If you’re a one-person shop with 1-2 employees, maybe building something yourself makes sense.", "cleaned": "I can just build it myself. I'll put some money into HubSpot and Salesforce, but I'm not going to spend $50,000 or more on an app that doesn't work when I can build one for free.\" And so we're seeing folks especially in sales go from being hyper critical of vendors and saying I need this feature now because I built my own AI SDR, I've already built my own AI BDR. Now they want to do their own marketing automation with the same AI agent they use in sales. They want to do their own customer success. So, if you think your customers are asking for too much from you today, it's only going to be worse next year as people get used to building these n=1 apps themselves. They expect all the functionality they see there in their personal life, they will ask for it everywhere else. All right, two final thoughts. Number one is actually don't overdo it here. If you're selling something like a product that has to scale, remember it is a commodity. But also realize that a lot of the value of products today is that they add new features at least once a quarter. And if you have a team, if you have a dozen engineers, 20 developers working on your product, and you're adding five new features each month, well, you can maybe do a lot better than what Claude can do. Maybe. Even though Claude does amazing things. A lot of value in our applications comes from that cadence. You may still benefit from having a larger development team even if you buy a bunch of tools off the shelf and bolt them together yourself. That's point number one. Point number two is this is a good way to innovate faster. We talked about the first point.", "balanced": "I was on this podcast recently with a great founder that's building an AI product and he said he spent two hours every morning using his app to train it because you have to do it yourself I'm like what are we doing here there is no way you can use our apps for even five minutes a day if they're not magical and so when you go to buy something today if it's an agent or a piece of software just ask how much time does it save me think about your life before you bought this tool and then look at how much time after you bought it will it take away from your life now maybe it's worth it but honestly most tools aren't saving us as many hours as we need them to be because the truth is folks everything has gotten harder since March 15th right almost all of us since march 15th we've been working harder than ever more stressful than ever more pressure than ever and yet things don't work well enough yet so what happened is we've ended up having to build a lot of these ourselves into N1 applications n-1 being one person one startup one team instead of many people multiple teams buying products together so that means you'll end up building a lot of software yourselves um my point is just challenge your assumptions if you want to buy a piece of software first ask how much time will it really save me how much of a burden will it alleviate and compare that to the amount of effort you put in building it yourself because I bet you find out you might still decide to build it yourself but make sure you understand which path is which okay thanks everybody hope you see you soon at saster annual", "r32": "we've talked so much on saster and SAS annual over the years since we started doing events together that you don't have to buy enterprise software it's not worth it if your budget is smaller than 100k or even more today but there are times when buying an app makes sense and one thing that i think has changed for sure with AI is the rise of the n-of-one application the n-of-one applications are apps that used to be very expensive custom tools maybe only one company would use them they'd cost hundreds of thousands dollars sometimes millions of dollars now these days you can build them yourself using Replit Claude or whatever LLM you want to use and it might take some time but you probably could do it yourself it might cost you several thousand dollars at least up front as well as ongoing costs from agents but it could work just fine to build this tool yourself I'm going to give you three examples and then four bonus ones uh and most importantly ask what are you really waiting for because you're waiting for something better to come along or to make changes you can't change right now um let me give you four quick examples of where you should just start building your own app number one security and compliance okay so we all know this as founders especially folks who haven't done it before but you need SOC2 and HIPAA compliance depending on how regulated your industry is and you may think wow I'll just go out and buy a solution like every other startup does there's Salesforce service now HubSpot etc etc etc there's so many great solutions and yes there are some great solutions out there but here's the thing you cannot buy off the shelf security", "repaired": "it's it's not that big you know I'm on vacation this week so far we've done $700,000 worth of revenue just through our AI and I have no idea how much more we're gonna do but last year at saster annual we did over a million dollars from sponsors from tickets um and then another couple hundred thousand from media sales okay so if you add all those together one plus two is roughly three million dollars right for us to run an event a business process outsourcing company uh with a lot of humans involved maybe five or ten people but still running a real business and then we can do almost as much as that just selling tickets to our own events online using an N equals one app there are many examples of these apps out there some of them have been around longer than others but basically they let you take your data like HubSpot data Salesforce data calendar data whatever kind of data you have and then put it into Claude into chat GPT into other LLMs and make it accessible to anyone in your organization and most importantly let you build custom prompts that get you exactly what you want out of your CRM or your database when you need it so we'll break down a few different use cases here but think about this if you don't have an n of 1 app today you could be losing hundreds of thousands or millions of dollars in efficiency gains by not having one because if you look at every piece of software in B2B everyone has built it themselves in the early days even HubSpot started off as an email marketing tool before it became anything else I mean everybody starts off building their own tools themselves and then they sell them to each other or buy them from each other depending on who's ahead in the journey"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 605, "lemkin-clone-v5": 274, "lemkin-clone-v6": 458, "cleaned": 306, "balanced": 310, "r32": 304, "repaired": 324}}}}, {"id": "gold_024", "topic": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Turn a standout quarter into concrete growth lessons for founders.", "expected": "A strong answer should use Cloudflare's quarter to show what differentiated execution looks like in a decelerating market. The numbers should support lessons about product breadth, enterprise motion, platform strategy, or category positioning. The founder takeaway is to understand what creates acceleration when the default state of the market is slowing down.", "facts": ["The answer should explain why Cloudflare is an outlier rather than merely praising the quarter.", "It should use numbers to support growth, product, or GTM lessons.", "It should translate public-company data into founder takeaways."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Contrarian's Delight**\n\nI love days like today.\n\nWhile most B2B companies are decelerating - some even contracting - one of my favorite SaaS players just announced its best quarter since 2021: Cloudflare.\n\nNow, I know what you're thinking. \"Jason, why is this a big deal?\" Well, let me tell you. When a company like Cloudflare, with an $8B+ market cap and a strong brand in the cybersecurity space, can deliver a quarter that crushes expectations - it's a big deal.\n\nSo, what drove this stellar performance? Let's dive into 10 numbers that explain why:\n\n**1. Revenue Growth Rate: 44% YoY**\n\nCloudflare just delivered its fastest year-over-year growth rate since Q4 2020. This is no small feat in today's B2B market.\n\n**2. $244M in Billings**\n\nThis quarter, Cloudflare brought in a whopping $244M in billings - up from $170M in the same period last year. That's an impressive 43% YoY growth rate.\n\n**3. Net Dollar Retention: 138%**\n\nCloudflare is not just growing revenue; it's also retaining customers at an incredible pace. Its net dollar retention rate of 138% means that, on average, each customer spends 38% more than they did in the previous quarter.\n\n**4. ARR Growth Rate: 43% YoY**\n\nAnnual Recurring Revenue (ARR) is a crucial metric for SaaS companies like Cloudflare. And with an ARR growth rate of 43%, it's clear that this company is firing on all cylinders.\n\n**5. $1B+ in Cash Flow from Operations**\n\nCloudflare just crossed the $1 billion mark in cash flow from operations - up from ~$600M last year. This is a testament to its strong operating efficiency and ability to scale.\n\n**6. 45% Increase in Gross Margin Rate**\n\nAs Cloudflare grows, it's also improving its gross margin rate. With a 45% increase over the past quarter, this company is clearly optimizing its cost structure for success.\n\n**7. $1M+ New Customers Added Each Quarter**\n\nCloudflare added an impressive ~$1 million new customers in Q4 - up from ~750k last year. This kind of rapid customer acquisition pace can be a major driver of growth and momentum.\n\n**8. 90%+ Customer Retention Rate**\n\nWith a retention rate that high, Cloudflare is clearly doing something right when it comes to retaining its existing customer base. This metric alone should give investors confidence in the company's long-term prospects.\n\n**9. $25M in Free Cash Flow per Quarter**\n\nFree cash flow (FCF) is an essential measure of a SaaS company's financial health - and Cloudflare just hit a new record: ~$25M FCF per quarter. This gives it ample resources to invest in its business and drive growth.\n\n**10. 200+ New Employees Hired Last Quarter**\n\nAs Cloudflare continues to grow, so does its team. With over 200 new hires last quarter alone - this company is clearly committed to scaling quickly while maintaining the quality of its product and service.\n\nSo what can we learn from Cloudflare's stellar performance? Here are a few key takeaways:\n\n* **Focus on retention**: While growth rates matter, it's equally important to retain existing customers. If you're not retaining them at an impressive rate - like 138% for Cloudflare - you might be leaving money on the table.\n* **Spend wisely**: With $25M in FCF per quarter, Cloudflare has ample resources to invest in its business and drive growth without sacrificing profitability.\n* **Prioritize customer", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Cloudflare is back.\nAnd it’s crushing it at $3 Billion+ ARR\nThe numbers are stunning:\nRecord quarterly bookings of\n$444 million\n(38% YoY growth)\nNet revenue retention (NRR) up to\n121%\n(from 117%)\nA massive increase in net new customer count, from just 1,000 to a whopping\n6,700\nin Q4 alone\nGross margin expansion to 83.5%, with operating margins at -10% and free cash flow margins of 24%\nCloudflare’s revenue has grown by an astonishing 50% since it went public in September 2023.\nIt’s the best quarter they’ve had since 2021, when they were growing at $120M ARR.\nThe numbers show why:\n#1. Record Quarterly Bookings: $444 Million\nThat’s up from just $320 million last year — a stunning 38% YoY growth in bookings alone.\nEven more impressive? This is the highest quarterly booking run rate since Q2’21, when Cloudflare was growing at about $120M ARR. They’re not just catching up to their pre-2021 growth trajectory; they’ve surpassed it entirely.\n#2. Net Revenue Retention Now 121%\nNRR remains strong — and actually improved from the previous quarter of 117% (still below the best-ever record of 123%). But here’s what matters: this is a company with $3B+ ARR where NRR keeps going up, not down. This isn’t just a matter of “we have so many customers we can’t possibly lose them all.” It’s a sign that Cloudflare has built real customer loyalty and retention.\n#3. Net New Customer Count Jumps 6x\nIn Q4 alone, they added over 6,700 new net new customers. That’s not just incremental growth; it’s a massive expansion of their addressable market. And here’s the thing about these numbers:", "lemkin-clone-v6": "But not all are decelerating. Not yet, anyway.\nCloudflare’s latest results — a $3 billion+ quarter with 40% growth at 120% free cash flow margins\n— remind us that there are still plenty of opportunities to grow in B2B SaaS and Cloud Infrastructure.\nHere are the top 10 numbers from their Q4 report that explain why:\n#1. Record Quarterly Revenue: $3 Billion+\nCloudflare just hit its highest quarterly revenue ever — a staggering $3 billion for the period ending December 31, 2025.\nThis represents a 40% year-over-year growth rate at scale (with ~$12B in total annualized revenue).\nWhat this means\n:\nWhen you’re growing at scale and have real product-market fit, there’s still plenty of runway to hit new records. Cloudflare has been on a tear since their IPO.\n#2. 40% YoY Growth at Scale: A Testament to Product-Market Fit\nCloudflare is proof that when you get it right — with true, persistent demand for what you’re building — the growth can continue even after hitting unicorn status ($1B+ ARR).\nThis isn’t about “faking” growth or relying on hype. It’s about building something people genuinely need and want to pay premium prices for.\n#3. 120% Free Cash Flow Margins: The Ultimate Efficiency Metric\nCloudflare has achieved an astonishing 120% free cash flow margins in their latest quarter, which is rare at scale (most companies have declining profitability as they grow).\nThis means that for every dollar of revenue Cloudflare generates — it keeps $1.20 after operating expenses.\nWhat this means:\nWhen you can maintain high profitability while growing rapidly, the economics get very compelling. This isn’t just a “nice-to-have” metric — it’s a competitive advantage in itself.\n#4. 30% Year-Over-Year Growth for SaaS Business\nCloudflare’s Enterprise business hit $1 billion+ ARR and delivered 32% year-over-year growth at scale, while their SMB segment grew an impressive 33%.\nThe fact that both segments are growing well above the market average shows that Cloudflare has diversified its customer base effectively.\n#5.", "cleaned": "They’re expanding their addressable market from cloud infrastructure to what they call “the internet layer.” The bet is that as AI increases network traffic and edge compute demand, Cloudflare becomes more essential — not less relevant. They have three big product launches coming this year (including Workers Pro) that will target customers who need to build applications at the edge rather than just securing them. This is a genuine expansion into application development. It’s early. But it’s real. And it shows the company isn’t waiting for the macro environment to recover before building new products. They’re using the opportunity to reinvent themselves while competitors are fighting for share in existing markets. The Growth Rate Comparison Is Stark Here’s where the comparison gets really interesting. At $500M ARR, Snowflake was growing around 27% YoY. At ~$2B ARR, Monday.com was growing roughly 23%. At ~$6.5B ARR, HubSpot grew 19-20%. At ~$7B ARR, Salesforce slowed to mid-teens growth. All good companies. All doing fine. None of them accelerating right now like Cloudflare is. Cloudflare at $1.9B+ ARR? Growing almost 30%. On top of already being one of the fastest-growing public SaaS companies ever. That gap matters because it means different things. For Monday or HubSpot, you’d expect deceleration at these scale levels due to natural law. You’d model it in. For Snowflake, you might think there could be some catch-up in enterprise sales cycles over time. For Salesforce, you’d see the deceleration as consistent with platform maturity. But Cloudflare is defying all those expectations simultaneously. Not only did they accelerate — but they accelerated by going upmarket faster than most B2B companies ever do.", "balanced": "Cloudflare is the perfect case study for what’s happening in SaaS right now. They’re not just surviving — they’re accelerating at $800M+ ARR while most public B2B companies are decelerating or growing at sub-20% rates. Here are 10 numbers that explain why Cloudflare is one of those rare outliers who isn’t seeing any slowdown. The TL;DR : Growth re-accelerated to 31%. Rule of 40 score hit 72%, beating Street expectations by +19 points. Free cash flow margin jumped from -5% to +17%. And customers paying over $100K grew 60% YoY. These aren’t small moves. This is a company fundamentally resetting its growth trajectory and proving that AI-native architecture combined with developer-first distribution can work even when macro headwinds make everything harder. Let’s dig into the real story behind these numbers because there’s more here than meets the eye. The Brutal Reality Check Before We Celebrate First, let me be clear about where we actually stand in SaaS today. If you’re running an older legacy enterprise software company, things are tough. Really tough. Look at some of these numbers from Q3 FY25 across major public players: Snowflake : Growth dropped from 29% to 23% year-over-year. Rule of 80 score plummeted from positive territory to negative territory. Guidance cut significantly, signaling continued deceleration. Datadog : Revenue grew 14% but new customer acquisition slowed sharply (from 13,000 net logo additions last quarter to only 7,000 this quarter). Rule of 80 score fell from 85% to below 70%. Bill.com : Growth rate has declined every single quarter since Q3 FY24.", "r32": "The company’s core product is still growing — but it’s adding more and more value to customers through these platform services. And that’s where the real expansion comes from now. The AI Opportunity : Cloudflare isn’t just riding the wave — they’re building on top of it with their own products like Worker (their LLM inference layer) and Vector Store. They’ve already seen significant adoption of Worker across enterprise customers. Enterprise Stickiness : Their net dollar retention for enterprise customers hit an impressive 108% year-over-year. When your existing customers are expanding faster than you’re acquiring new ones, that tells you something about the quality of your relationship. It also means the TAM keeps getting bigger as existing customers add more usage over time. What This Means for SaaS Founders If You’re Pre-IPO / < $100M ARR : Cloudflare shows what happens when you build infrastructure that developers love AND sell security and performance solutions to business leaders who care deeply about uptime and compliance. Don’t try to be everything to everyone, but do make sure every customer has at least one superpower that makes them feel indispensable. If You’re $100M+ ARR : You need to think harder about whether you’re truly a platform or just a feature. Cloudflare figured this out early by moving upmarket aggressively. Your biggest competitors probably haven’t made that shift yet. Focus on monetizing developer love while selling C-suite value. If You’re Building Now : Infrastructure matters. Not because everyone needs another CDN, but because everyone needs better infrastructure for their apps.", "repaired": "So while most SaaS leaders are seeing growth slow to single digits for the first time since the pandemic, Cloudflare just reported its best quarter as public company — ever.\nQ1 FY27 revenue hit $439 million (up 31% YoY), with Q1 billings up 39%. The stock is trading at all-time highs and hit an intraday record high today.\nCloudflare’s CFO put it simply on the earnings call:\n“Cloudflare has never been growing faster.”\nHere are the numbers that matter. And why they should be your benchmark right now if you’re building or investing in SaaS.\nThe Numbers That Matter\nRevenue & Growth Metrics\n$439M Q1 revenue (31% YoY growth)\n39% billings growth vs. 29% last quarter\n$1.6 billion annualized run rate (43%+ growth)\nFY28 guidance: $1.85-1.86 billion (+30% YoY)\nQ1 margin expansion from -19% to -17%\nCustomer Metrics\n11,500 customers paying $100K+\n$150M+ ARR customer count increased 55% year-over-year\n95% of Fortune 500 companies using Cloudflare\n9 consecutive quarters of net dollar retention above 120%\nProduct Expansion Metrics\nTotal ARR by product category:\nSecurity (54%)\nPerformance (24%)\nEdge Services (22%) – this is the new wedge beyond DDoS/VPN/WAF\n70% of Security ARR from products launched <12 months ago\nEnterprise penetration increasing across all product lines\nWhy This Matters More Than It Seems\nLet me break down what makes these metrics different—and why they should make every SaaS founder rethink their playbook."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 550, "lemkin-clone-v5": 271, "lemkin-clone-v6": 328, "cleaned": 271, "balanced": 256, "r32": 256, "repaired": 231}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a LinkedIn-style post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"Counter-Intuitive Growth Story Alert**\n\nWhile most B2B companies are decelerating this year (I've spoken to many founders who are fighting hard just to maintain growth), one company that continues to defy gravity is Cloudflare.\n\nAnd I'm not talking about a small bump in growth. No way. I mean **Cloudflare just had its best quarter since 2021**. That's right, folks - they're growing faster than ever before.\n\nSo what's behind this incredible performance? Let me give you the numbers:\n\n* $230M+ revenue run rate (up from $180M last year)\n* 100%+ YoY growth in new logos\n* 1500+ new employees added to their team in Q2 alone\n* Expansion of product offerings into new markets, including Cloudflare One and WAF\n* +20% increase in customer engagement across all touchpoints (support, sales, etc.)\n* +30% boost in ARR retention rates\n\nThese numbers tell a story: **Cloudflare is building a moat around its business**. They're investing heavily in product innovation, expanding into adjacent markets, and delivering an unparalleled user experience.\n\nAs I've said before, growth at scale requires more than just throwing money at it. You need a clear vision, a world-class team, and the ability to execute on both strategy and execution.\n\nSo what can we learn from Cloudflare's success? Here are three key takeaways:\n\n1. **Invest in product innovation**.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "When it comes to SaaS growth rates, most companies are decelerating right now.\nBut what happens when you combine AI with a massive security moat?\nCloudflare just posted their best quarter since 2017 — and we’re digging into the numbers that explain why:\n1. Security Spend is Going Up, Not Down\nSecurity budgets aren’t being cut like they were in Q2-Q3. In fact, Cloudflare saw “stronger than expected” security growth across both enterprise and SMB customers.\nThat’s what happens when AI introduces new risk vectors — companies need protection more than ever.\n2. $1B+ of New ARR Coming From Existing Customers\nNot just adding net new logos (although they did). They’re also growing existing accounts by 25% on average, with some big customers even increasing spend by over 100%.\nThat’s the power of true product-market fit in a complex category.\n3. Enterprise Deals Growing Faster Than Ever\nAverage deal size up 30%. Larger deals are going to Cloudflare. And they’re getting bigger (see point #4). The enterprise security budget isn’t being cut — it’s actually growing faster than ever before.\n4. $1M+ Deals Are Their Fastest-Growing Segment by Revenue Per Customer\nNot SMB, not mid-market… massive enterprises with complex security needs are driving their growth right now.\n5. They’re Winning Against Big Tech Competitors in Security and AI\nCloudflare is taking share from companies like AWS. And it’s winning deals where competitors would have otherwise gotten them years ago. That’s the power", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I just did an epic deep dive on why there are so many decelerating B2B companies today.\nThe reasons aren’t that complicated:\nMost of them haven’t transitioned to SaaS yet\nAnd most of the ones that have… stopped investing in new products and features. And they’re not growing anymore.\nBut Cloudflare — one of my favorites from early SaaStr days, because I love their mission as much as their product — just had its best quarter since 2021! A $2B+ deal with AWS to power every website on the planet… and then more. The numbers:\n$100M ARR growth rate\n25% YoY increase in Q4 FY24 compared to Q3 FY23\nCloudflare’s first public SaaS company to reach $5 billion revenue\nNow powering 40 million domains, up from 30 million at IPO\n$2B+ contract with AWS: Cloudflare will power “every website on the planet”\nAWS deal represents ~10% of Cloudflare’s current addressable market\nNew AI products (AI Firewall and Web Application Firewall) are driving significant growth in enterprise sales\nCloudflare has been quietly building one of the most important SaaS companies nobody talks about. They’re not flashy, but they’ve built something truly special:\nInfrastructure for the cloud-native world.\nThe Cloudflare Story: How a $5B+ Company Built on Open-Source Innovation Became AWS’s New Partner\nWhat I love is how transparent and humble this was. The CEO wrote an email to customers explaining exactly why they’re growing so fast —", "cleaned": "Cloudflare just reported Q1 2026 earnings — and while most public SaaS companies are struggling with deceleration, seat contractions, and slowing growth rates, Cloudflare is posting numbers that would make even the best B2B investors green with envy. Here’s what actually happened: The headline metrics tell part of the story: $654M revenue (up 29% YoY) vs consensus of $644M $19M net income on $654M revenue = 3% GAAP operating margin (vs -5% last year) $3.38B cash position as of May 31st Full-year guidance of $2.61-2.63B revenue ($652-655M quarterly run rate by end of year) But there are two other dimensions here worth understanding because they explain why this isn’t just another “we’re growing again” narrative from a company that was already well-positioned. Dimension #1: Enterprise customers at record scale When you look at Cloudflare’s customer base expansion, it’s not incremental users or small businesses adding seats. It’s enterprise adoption accelerating. • Customer count grew to ~150,000+ paid subscribers (that number includes smaller accounts too), up 13% YoY • Customers paying more than $100K ARR hit all-time high of 9,700+ — up 28% YoY and now represent 35% of total ARR • Largest deals doubled to 50+ $500K+ annual contracts, including multiple seven-figure deals per quarter What matters most? These aren’t one-off transactions anymore. At", "balanced": "#5: Rule of 40 Score – 94% (vs. industry average ~60%) This is the most telling metric for what’s happening at Cloudflare right now. The Rule of 40 measures profitability + growth rate. At 94%, they’re not just hitting it — they’re crushing it while peers are struggling to break 50%. #6: Customer Cohort Retention – >95% gross retention across all segments The product expansion math here is staggering. When you hit $100M+ ARR with over 90% net dollar retention, your new logo acquisition engine becomes almost self-funding. Each existing customer expands into more products and usage-based spend, driving organic growth without additional sales headcount. #7: Security Business Growth Accelerated to 30% YoY (QoQ up from <20%) This is where Cloudflare gets interesting for SaaS founders building in adjacent categories. Their Zero Trust platform is growing faster than their core CDN business, demonstrating how security has become Table Stakes for enterprise cloud adoption. If you can solve a mission-critical problem like identity or zero trust, you’ve got a pricing power tailwind that pure-play competitors often don’t have.", "r32": "The Lesson for Every SaaS Founder Right Now The market is brutal right now because most companies are still selling last year's product to yesterday's buyers with today's prices. They're running at 2019-2020 growth rates in 2025. But Cloudflare didn't just survive this downturn — they thrived. And here's why it matters more than any other recent quarter: 1⃣ $700M ARR growing 32% YoY (vs industry average ~15%) 2⃣ $1.7B TCV run rate, up from $1.5B Q1 (+13%) 3⃣ First $100M+ enterprise customer closed 4⃣ 40% of new revenue comes from existing customers expanding 5⃣ Rule of 40 score: 69%, exceeding expectations 6⃣ Free cash flow margin hit 28% (!) 7⃣ 27% non-GAAP operating margin 8⃣ 35% gross margins, down modestly but not structurally 9⃣ Net dollar retention stayed above 110% for first time 10⃣ Stock price up +30% YTD while peers get crushed What happened? John Colgrove and his team did what every founder needs to do when markets slow down: Focus on net retention over absolute growth Expand into adjacent segments where demand isn't contracting Double down on R&D that creates defensible moats Maintain pricing discipline while raising prices on existing customers Invest aggressively in TAM expansion Build a platform, not a", "repaired": "While most B2B companies are fighting headwinds and deceleration, Cloudflare is accelerating at scale — growing 34% annually with $6.7 billion in ARR and over 50% net retention across its entire business. Here’s what separates them from the pack. The Metrics Every B2B Founder Should Study 👇 1️⃣ Revenue Growth 🔝 $6.72 BILLION annualized run rate (up 31% YoY) 🔟 QoQ growth acceleration from mid-20s to low 30s 📈 28 consecutive quarters of revenue growth 🚀 Still just 25 years old as a public company 2️⃣ Net Retention Rate 💸 NRR for Security Business: 122%+ 💰 NRR for Total Company: 109%+ ✨ Expansion revenue driving majority of growth ➡️ Existing customers now represent ~60% of total ARR 3️⃣ Customer Count Explosion 🔥 Added 700+ new enterprise customers in one quarter 👉 Now serves nearly 200,000 paying subscribers globally ➖ Up from roughly 150,000 twelve months ago 4️⃣ Gross Margins & Efficiency 🧮 Gross margin expansion: 69% → 71% (+2 points) 👉 Non-GAAP gross margins hit all-time high ➡️ Operating leverage starting to show up in results 5️⃣ Free Cash Flow Story 🏦 Generated $176M FCF last quarter ➡️ 22% non-GAAP free cash flow"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 228, "lemkin-clone-v5": 242, "lemkin-clone-v6": 237, "cleaned": 214, "balanced": 177, "r32": 204, "repaired": 193}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**X POST**\n\nWhile most B2B companies are decelerating... I just got my hands on some mind-blowing data from Cloudflare, and it's a stark contrast to what's happening elsewhere.\n\nCloudflare just had its BEST QUARTER SINCE 2021! That's right - in an era of economic uncertainty, they're not only holding steady but are actually accelerating.\n\nHere are the top 10 numbers that explain why:\n\n **50% YoY growth**: Revenue is on fire!\n\n**$300M ARR**: Their annual recurring revenue has more than doubled in just two years\n\n**20K+ new customers**: They're adding a huge number of new logos every quarter, with no signs of slowing", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So a lot of you may not have heard of Cloudflare since it’s been around for years and is still growing at epic rates.\nBut they are an interesting bellwether as we all try to navigate the current B2B landscape — what’s working, what isn’t. And I’ll tell you 1 thing that seems quite different from most other SaaS companies today: Cloudflare is accelerating. Not decelerating like so many others in tough times. At least not yet.\nAnd it also has a lot of similarities to the leaders we all know and love — HubSpot, Monday.com, Shopify, etc.\nLet’s break down 10 numbers that explain why they’re growing at epic rates while everyone else is slowing:\n1. Revenue Growth Accelerated from 23% in Q3", "lemkin-clone-v6": "In a world where most SaaS leaders are decelerating, Cloudflare just reported its best quarter since 2021 — and it’s not slowing down anytime soon.\n\nHere are the numbers that explain why:\n\n1. Revenue Growth: Up to 43% YoY from $0-50M ARR (previously flat at ~20%)\n2. New Customer Acquisition: Accelerating with net new customers up +12%\n3. Deal Size: Growing +15% year-over-year\n4. Enterprise Expansion:\n— 10K+ customer base now expanded to ~40,000+\n— Up from 0 in Q1’23 to $5M ARR by end of FY24 (100x growth)\n5. Net Dollar Retention: 110-120% — a sign that customers", "cleaned": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "balanced": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why The SaaStr deep dive here:", "r32": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why Here’s what happens when you build infrastructure that matters more every year. Cloudflare just reported Q1 FY25 (ended June 30), and it’s their best quarter since 2021. Not just good — the absolute best one they’ve had since pre-Covid. The numbers are remarkable enough to make any public SaaS founder take notice, especially because most other companies are still navigating the “we’re past peak growth” era. And there is a lot going on here at Cloudflare. A $7 Billion+ Deal To Acquire Wiz for Security Software Cloudflare has agreed to acquire Israel-based cybersecurity startup Wiz for approximately $17 billion", "repaired": "While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why.\n\nCloudflare just posted Q4 and full-year results that make every SaaS founder wonder how they’re doing it right when everyone else is grinding through headwinds.\n\nHere are the numbers that matter:\n\nThe Growth Story That’s Actually Accelerating\n\nQ4 FY25:\n$345M revenue (up 23% YoY)\nFull Year FY25:\n$1.28 billion revenue (34% growth — best year since IPO!)\nARR:\n$1.35 billion (+33%)\nSubscription ARR:\n$1.31 billion (+36%)\n\nThese aren’t “growth deceleration” metrics. These are acceleration at scale.\n\nWhat Makes This Different From"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 113, "lemkin-clone-v5": 124, "lemkin-clone-v6": 99, "cleaned": 19, "balanced": 24, "r32": 118, "repaired": 100}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021. 10 Numbers That Explain Why", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible talk in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"While Most B2B Companies Are Decelerating, Cloudflare Just Had Its Best Quarter Since 2021: 10 Numbers That Explain Why\"\n\n**[Introduction]**\n\nHello everyone! Today I want to share with you a fascinating story about one company that's bucking the trend in this challenging economic landscape. While many of us are seeing our companies decelerate, Cloudflare just reported its best quarter since 2021. But what's behind their success? Let me take you through 10 numbers that explain why.\n\n**[Slide: #1 - Revenue Growth]**\n\nNumber one: $200 million in revenue growth. That's right, folks! Cloudflare's quarterly revenue has increased by a whopping 40% year-over-year. Now, I know what you're thinking... \"That sounds amazing!\" And it is! But here's the thing: most B2B companies are struggling to grow at all.\n\n**[Slide: #2 - Customer Acquisition]**\n\nNumber two: 1 million new customers added in Q4 alone. That's a staggering number, and it speaks volumes about Cloudflare's ability to attract new users. Think about it... one million new customers in just three months! Most B2B companies are lucky if they can add a few hundred customers per quarter.\n\n**[Slide: #3 - User Growth]**\n\nNumber three: 300% increase in user growth year-over-year. That's right, folks! Cloudflare is not only adding new customers but also growing its existing base at an incredible rate. This is no small feat, especially when you consider the challenges of acquiring and retaining users.\n\n**[Slide: #4 - Net Dollar Retention]**\n\nNumber four: 133% net dollar retention rate. What does that mean? In simple terms, it means Cloudflare customers are staying with them for a long time – and spending more money as they do! Most B2B companies struggle to retain even half of their customers.\n\n**[Slide: #5 - CAC Payback Period]**\n\nNumber five: 4.3 months CAC payback period. Now, I know some of you might be thinking... \"Jason, what's the big deal about a 4.3-month CAC payback period?\" Well, let me tell you – that means Cloudflare customers are paying back their acquisition costs in under five months! That's incredibly efficient.\n\n**[Slide: #6 - ARR Growth]**\n\nNumber six: $1 billion annual recurring revenue (ARR) growth. Ah-ha moment! When a company reaches this milestone, it's often the sign of something special – and that something is usually scalability!\n\n**[Slide: #7 - Cloudflare 2.0]**\n\nNumber seven: The launch of Cloudflare 2.0, which has driven significant user adoption. This new product offering has been a game-changer for Cloudflare, enabling customers to build more powerful applications at scale.\n\n**[Slide: #8 - Partnerships and Integrations]**\n\nNumber eight: Over 1,000 integrations with other platforms, including AWS, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure. When you're a company like Cloudflare that's built on partnerships and integrations, it's no wonder they can grow at such an incredible rate.\n\n**[Slide: #9 - Talent Acquisition]**\n\nNumber nine: The addition of over 500 new employees in the past year alone! You see, when companies are growing this fast, they need to bring in top talent – quickly. And Cloudflare has done just that!\n\n**[Slide: #10 - Market Share Gains]**\n\nNumber ten: Estimated market share gains of up to 30% in specific", "lemkin-clone-v5": "look at that for real folks it is possible to decelerate and then reaccelerate if you have a very strong brand cloud flare did it again in the last quarter they had their best year since 2019 that's what i'm gonna talk about today 10 numbers that explain why cloudflare just had its best Quarter since 2021 and some learnings from this as we get into next year and beyond", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So as I said, it looks like 99% of us are decelerating this year. And we’re seeing more and more folks that just can’t hit the plan anymore. The CACs have gotten too high, or NRR has slipped to a fraction above zero — even if it’s still positive.\nBut then there is Cloudflare. I don’t know what magic they do here at $5B+ in ARR — but they are growing faster today than ever before. And look, growth slows over time. But not that much. It doesn’t have to be 100%+. Not even close. But it can grow 30%-40% for a decade or more.\nAnd Cloudflare is doing just that.\n\n10 Numbers That Explain Why\n#1: The Growth Acceleration Is Real\nCloudflare’s latest quarter was their best since Q4’21 — with revenue growing 38% year-over-year to $532.8 million. What really matters isn’t the top-line number itself, but where you’re headed relative to your peers.\n#2: Cloudflare Stopped Growing Slower and Started Growing Faster\nTheir growth rate has been trending upwards since Q3’23 — a trend that’s likely sustainable given their positioning as a security-first platform in an increasingly uncertain world. Remember how Box was growing 10% last year? And this year it’s down to about 6%. That’s decelerating, not accelerating.\n#3: They’re Not Just Growing Fast—They’re Also Getting More Efficient\nWhile many companies are expanding their sales teams and marketing budgets as they scale, Cloudflare has maintained a relatively lean approach. This is the model most of us should aim for in SaaS at least by $100m-$150 ARR+.\n#4: Their Gross Margin Is Crushing It — 80%+ At Scale\nThe efficiency story goes beyond just sales and marketing spend. They’ve managed to maintain gross margins above 80% even as they approach a billion dollars in annual revenue.", "cleaned": "CloudFlare's at $1 billion in ARR growing over 30%. It went from free to paid very quickly because it was so disruptive and great. And then it took a while for people to understand its full power but now as we've seen with all these other security companies Zcaler rubric others that are on fire today they're seeing huge acceleration after almost 20 years in business. So you can do this too. You don't have to be a new company or even just a new cloud native company to accelerate in many cases if you're still doing well. Okay, last two points and then we'll wrap up with five interesting learnings. Point six is again something we've talked about before um but it's worth reinforcing which is net revenue retention. A lot of folks that aren't doing well right now are saying hey nrr is down. Hey our customers aren't expanding their spend. They're not buying more products. We had a little bit of that in SAS Europa. I think Monday had some challenges there where their nrr came down a smidge below 100% for one quarter because their bigger customers were cutting back. But look at CloudFlare. This is what you want to see. Net dollar retention is an incredible 125%. What does that mean? Well basically it means every single year CloudFlare gets another 25% of incremental revenue from its base. Not only is it adding new logos like it did with Rubrik at scale but it's getting 25% more out of each existing customer each year. Now that doesn't mean everyone is expanding massively at CloudFlare. Some folks may be flat or cut slightly.", "balanced": "Cloudflare just posted its first $700 million quarter as a public company – and it's accelerating at scale while most other SaaS companies are decelerating or plateauing. Here's what actually happened based on their Q4 earnings report. First off, let's get the headline numbers out of the way: Q4 revenue hit $703M (+27% YoY) Full-year revenue was $2.65 billion (28% growth for the year) Full-year net retention climbed to 114%, up from 110% last year Free cash flow was positive ($46M for the year vs. -$20M in FY22) Now here's where things get really interesting: they're doing this with a $5 billion run rate that's still growing faster than Salesforce, HubSpot, Monday.com, Atlassian, and Datadog combined. They're also adding customers faster than almost any leader I track—over 8,000 new logos in the past twelve months alone. So how did they pull off this magic? Let me break down ten specific metrics that tell you everything you need to know about building a real cloud business today.", "r32": "Cloudflare is the ultimate infrastructure company for everyone from developers to CIOs. We are seeing that it's not all doom and gloom out there in SAS at least for the leaders and cloud flare is one of them they're accelerating right now while many others are decelerating let's take a look at what's going on with CloudFlare today as we head into the back half of this year 22 and first slide uh some incredible metrics um we'll go through these but I think you can probably see why they would be incredible CloudFlare has grown 33% over the past 12 months and it had its best quarter since 2021 which was crazy times so just getting back to 2021 growth rates wow almost unbelievable that we could get back to those days but even more importantly it's growing faster than ever before and Wall Street loves it shares have doubled over the last six months or so and we'll dig into some of the reasons why but boy is it good news for the sas space because most folks aren't doing well most folks haven't gotten back to their pre-covid growth rates much less exceeded them but Cloudflare has done it they've gone from 40 percent Growth in 2019 and 2020 to 50% growth in 2021 to 30% growth now in 2022 but it's accelerating and then it got even better the last two quarters and we'll dive into what that means next so number four 41 million dollars of free cash flow generated this last year um yes we love our recurring revenue in SAS and other subscription-based models but ultimately it doesn't mean anything if you don't generate cash because your customers pay you up front for years to come um so it's great to see that CloudFlare is generating a lot of", "repaired": "Cloudflare just reported its Q4 earnings and the numbers are genuinely impressive:\n$763 million revenue (up 31% YoY)\nQ4 guidance exceeded by $20-25M\nFull year growth of 29%\nCustomers paying over $100K up 64% YoY to 5,800+\nAnd here's what makes it even more remarkable — while most enterprise software companies are seeing deceleration, Cloudflare is actually accelerating at scale.\nThe company crossed $700M ARR for the first time last quarter, growing 31%. For context, that's higher than ZoomInfo or RingCentral. And they're not slowing down. Full-year growth was 29%, which puts them above many major public SaaS players like HubSpot, PagerDuty, and others who've all seen growth rates compress into single digits.\nNow let me walk through the top 10 numbers that explain why this matters for every founder building infrastructure in B2B.\n\n#1. 63% Growth Among Customers Paying Over $100K. This Is The Metric Every Enterprise Founder Should Track.\nLet's start with the number that should make your jaw drop if you're building anything remotely similar to Cloudflare's product: customers paying $100k+ are growing 63% year-over-year to nearly 6,000 customers. That's not a typo. Six thousand enterprises spending six figures annually on Cloudflare. And they represent only 8% of total customer count—meaning the TAM beyond these accounts hasn't been tapped yet.\nThis is the real story. The headline might be about Cloudflare hitting $700M, but the real engine driving that growth isn't SMBs—it's enterprises scaling their spend. These aren't one-off deals. They're multi-million dollar commitments from Fortune 500 companies embedding Cloudflare deeper across their stack.\nFor founders, this means two things:\n\nFirst, if you can land an enterprise deal, treat it as a long-term relationship—not a short-term win. Enterprises often don't renew unless you deliver value, so earn those logos. Second, look at how much runway remains in this category."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 526, "lemkin-clone-v5": 70, "lemkin-clone-v6": 298, "cleaned": 277, "balanced": 166, "r32": 306, "repaired": 302}}}}, {"id": "gold_025", "topic": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain different staffing models for AI-native B2B sales teams.", "expected": "A strong answer should explain that AI-native B2B startups can scale with a leaner human sales team than older SaaS playbooks assumed, but the right model depends on deal complexity and motion. One playbook may emphasize founder-led or product-led leverage plus AI support, while another still adds more classic sales structure once the enterprise motion hardens. The point is to match headcount design to reality, not nostalgia.", "facts": ["The answer should describe at least two valid go-to-market staffing approaches.", "It should explain how AI changes headcount needs, specialization, or support workflows.", "It should avoid implying there is a single universal answer."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**The Ultimate Question for Any Hot AI B2B Startup: How Big Should Your Sales Team Be?**\n\nAs I've worked with numerous successful startups in the AI space, one question keeps coming up: \"How big of a sales team do we really need?\" It's a question that can make or break your business. Get it right, and you'll be crushing revenue goals. Get it wrong... well, let's just say you might end up with a lot of empty coffee cups in the office.\n\nIn my experience, there are two playbooks when it comes to building out your sales team at an AI B2B startup: **The Lean Machine** and **The Growth Hacker**. I'll break down each approach, so you can decide which one is right for your company.\n\n### The Lean Machine\n\nIf you're in the early stages of your startup (think Series A or below), you might want to consider taking a lean sales approach. This means investing in a small team – often just 1-2 people – who are responsible for generating leads, qualifying prospects, and closing deals.\n\nThe key advantage of The Lean Machine is that it's extremely cost-effective. With a tiny sales team, you can experiment with different strategies, optimize your messaging, and focus on building a scalable go-to-market engine without breaking the bank.\n\nHowever, this approach requires an enormous amount of hustle from those early sales reps (or founders). They'll need to be incredibly resourceful, able to wear multiple hats, and possess a deep understanding of both AI technology and the customer's pain points. It's not for the faint of heart!\n\n### The Growth Hacker\n\nNow, if you're in the later stages of your startup (think Series B or above), it might be time to scale up your sales team with **The Growth Hacker** approach. This involves building a larger, more specialized sales organization that can handle an influx of high-quality leads and drive revenue growth.\n\nWith The Growth Hacker playbook, you'll need to invest in people who are experts in AI sales – individuals with deep technical knowledge, as well as strong business acumen. They should be able to navigate complex buying processes, identify opportunities for upselling/cross-selling, and build lasting relationships with enterprise customers.\n\nThe benefits of this approach include accelerated revenue growth, increased market share, and a stronger brand presence. However, it also comes with higher costs (think salaries, commission structures, and all the trappings of a larger organization).\n\n### The Ultimate Decision\n\nSo, how do you decide which playbook is right for your AI B2B startup? Here are some key questions to ask:\n\n1. **Where are we in our growth journey?** Are we still in early stages or scaling rapidly?\n2. **What's the quality of our leads?** Can we afford to invest time and resources into qualifying prospects, or do we have a steady supply of high-quality opportunities coming through?\n3. **Do we have the budget for a larger sales team?**\n\nIf you're unsure about which approach is best for your company, take a step back and assess your strengths, weaknesses, and growth goals.\n\nIn my experience, The Lean Machine works well in early stages when resources are limited, while The Growth Hacker playbook excels as companies scale up their operations. However, there's always room to adapt these playbooks or create something entirely new based on the unique needs of your business.\n\nWhat do you think? Have any tips for building an effective sales team at a hot AI B2B startup? Share them with me in the comments below!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So for years, we’ve all been told that you can’t sell too many seats to an SMB customer.\nYou have to limit them\nto X number. It’s the rule in sales.  If they want 200,000+ more, you have to add a second sales rep.  And then another one.  And so on and so forth. For years this was pretty much true, at least for most B2B startups.\nBut it turns out that doesn’t quite work anymore with AI.  At least not in the hot new AI companies I’ve talked to.\nI remember back in 2021 when we were all trying to figure out how to manage a sales team after Covid hit:\nHow many seats should they be able to close?\nShould you have quotas? And if so, what size?\nWhat’s an AE vs. AEs plus SDRs vs. BDRs?\nIt was chaos and confusion back then.  But one thing that worked pretty well in 2021-22 is still working today: the $100k+ ACV SMB playbook.\nYou just need to be a little bit more aggressive with quotas, but you can make it work if you really want to sell into smaller teams at higher prices for AI and other products.  I remember when we did our first SaaStr Annual back in 2012-13 that the sponsor package was $10k per year.  We were all shocked!  But then by 2021, they would pay almost $100k a year to be an Annual Sponsor.\nAnd today?  That $10k is now worth nothing compared to what it’s really worth with AI and other new products — even just at the same price point!\nIt was chaos in sales back then.  But you had one playbook that worked for SMBs:\n$100,000+ ACV (Annual Contract Value).", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Dear SaaStr:\nHow many salespeople do I need to hit $10m ARR?\nThe answer is … it depends.\nThere are two playbooks, really.  If you can close deals with zero help from the field (i.e., demos are not required) and if your product is so obviously better than competition that customers buy without meeting a human, then 5-6 reps may be enough to hit $10m ARR in Year One.\nBut most of us need humans for some part of sales.  You want someone at least to demo the app, help with onboarding, and generally solve the customer’s problems. So — you probably will need 8-12 AE’s to get there, if your deal sizes are $10K-$20k per year.\nA bit more here:\nHow Big Should Your Sales Team Be? The Simple Math\nRelated Posts\nThe Very Best of SaaStr in 2023! 🎉\n5 Interesting Learnings from AI B2B Leader Replit at $1 Billion+ ARR\nTop SaaStr Content for the Week: How to Build a Great Sales Team with Greenhouse.", "cleaned": "So there’s been a lot of chatter on social media and X about how the hottest AI companies are able to close so many big deals with such small sales teams, often just a handful of reps closing seven-figure contracts regularly. It seems crazy — isn’t that exactly why we used to need SDRs and AEs and revops managers and CSMs and an entire go-to-market org chart?! And it is … sort of. But here’s what I’ve learned from dozens of conversations over the past few months: there are two entirely different playbooks happening right now in AI B2B. And if you’re running a non-AI company, trying to hire your first enterprise rep, or building out your go-to-market team — understanding which one you should be following could mean the difference between growth and grinding through endless pipeline meetings without moving the needle. The “Efficiency” Obsession (And Why It’s Real) Here’s where most founders get this wrong: they see Cursor growing 5x last year with ~20 people total and think “we need fewer people too.” That would be amazing. Except Cursor has roughly $500M ARR. They can afford to have a highly efficient machine because they raised hundreds of millions of dollars and their product essentially sells itself to developers who want access to the fastest, smartest coding assistant available. When you’re selling to software engineers competing for venture funding, word-of-mouth marketing compounds rapidly. Your free trial converts automatically as teams realize they can’t live without you. Your NRR is basically guaranteed because once someone builds something real on top of your LLM, switching costs become existential.", "balanced": "The math works because you’re selling to people who are eager to buy, not running traditional sales motions where every deal is hard-won. And if your product really does solve real problems for these customers — which it almost certainly does since they’ve already signed up — then the conversion rates will be strong enough that even a small team can hit big numbers fast. This approach is only possible when demand truly exceeds supply. When everyone wants your product and you have no one to sell it to them. That’s rare. But when it happens, go all-in on inbound-first with an efficient sales motion behind it. It worked for Replit ($1M ARR per employee) and could work for any hot AI startup right now. If you don’t have this perfect storm, though — high-demand, low friction, limited competition — the second playbook wins out by default. Hire as many great reps as you possibly can afford. Because the deals aren’t going to close themselves, and building a world-class sales organization takes time. Which One Is Right For You? There isn’t actually a “right” answer here. Both playbooks have been proven to work. Both companies I mentioned got their first $100M+ ARRs (Replit and Cursor respectively). The question isn’t whether to do outbound or focus on efficiency. The question is what environment you’re operating in, how much capital you have available, and what kind of market you’re attacking. If you’re an AI agent company launching into a brand-new category where nobody knows you exist yet, outbound is probably your best bet. Start with 10-15 SDRs and hope some of those leads convert to revenue quickly.", "r32": "So we’ve been doing some deep dives on sales teams and more recently, SaaStr has done its first hands-on look at many of the top AI companies in our “AI Deep Dives” series. And one thing is very clear to me — there are two different playbooks for scaling revenue at hot AI startups right now. They’re not mutually exclusive, but they do represent fundamentally different approaches. And which playbook you need depends almost entirely on your funding situation, your growth trajectory, and whether or not you can actually get customers to pay cash up front. For most traditional SaaS companies at $10M ARR, I’d say you should be aiming for roughly 60-80 reps by then. Maybe as low as 40 if the deal sizes are massive (think Salesforce-style enterprise deals). But here’s what’s wild about the current market: Some AI companies with similar ARR profiles have only 8-12 people total running their entire go-to-market motion. Eight reps. Twelve people max. Let that sink in. So let’s break down these two playbooks, because understanding them will help you decide how aggressively you should staff up — and when to pull back. Because I’m pretty sure you’ll make both mistakes if you don’t understand this dichotomy.", "repaired": "The best hot startup founders are great recruiters — but that doesn’t mean they want to be headhunters, or spend all their time recruiting and managing sales reps. It’s a lot more work than most founders expect, especially as you scale past $5m-$10m ARR. And it can consume way too much of your bandwidth if you let it. So how big should your first sales team be? Well, there are two main playbooks for hiring a first sales team:\nPlaybook #1: Hire Slowly and Painfully, But Always From Within Your Network (Most Common)\nThis is what I see almost everyone do these days. They hire someone on their own team — usually an SDR, sometimes an AE — who shows some promise and has never really sold before. Or they bring over a few folks from their last company because they know them well. Maybe even just one person. Then they find another rep through their network somehow. This keeps going until the sales leader finally burns out at around 8-12 total hires. If you have any capital, maybe you add a recruiter here somewhere along the line. Even then, you probably only hire 1-2 people this way. At least for the first year or so. The issue with this approach is that you end up with a pretty mediocre initial group by design. These are often good people, but not closers. Not quota-hitters. You need those. In fact, you’re basically betting against yourself by hiring slow. Why would you ever want to hire a rep that hasn’t worked anywhere else when there are so many proven performers available?"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 585, "lemkin-clone-v5": 288, "lemkin-clone-v6": 168, "cleaned": 267, "balanced": 275, "r32": 205, "repaired": 269}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential LinkedIn post:\n\n**The #1 Question Every Founder Asks**\n\n\"How big should my sales team be?\"\n\nAs I've worked with over 100 hot AI B2B startups, I've seen two distinct playbooks emerge.\n\n**Playbook #1: The \"Growth-at-All-Costs\" Team**\n\nYou're raising money fast, and you need to spend it all - on sales. Hire as many reps as possible, even if they don't quite fit the bill yet. Focus on growth metrics like ARR and customer acquisition cost (CAC). It's better to have a bigger team with mediocre results than a smaller one with outstanding performance.\n\n**Playbook #2: The \"Profit-Focused\" Team**\n\nYou're building for the long haul, not just for the next round of funding. Prioritize sales efficiency over sheer headcount growth. Focus on hiring top-notch reps who can close deals quickly and at scale. Measure your team's effectiveness by metrics like conversion rates, quota attainment, and customer satisfaction.\n\n**Which playbook is right for you?**\n\nIt depends on where you are in your startup journey. If you're still building product-market fit or raising capital, Playbook #1 might be the way to go. But if you've got a proven product and are focused on scaling efficiently, Playbook #2 is likely the better choice.\n\nWhat's your experience? Have you seen success with one playbook over the other?\n\nLet me know in the comments!\n\n#AI #B2BSales #StartupGrowth #SalesStrategy", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We are all seeing it — hot new startups with great tech, but very little sales and marketing resources.\nIt’s a two play book:\nPlaybook #1\nBuild for the long haul. Build a full-fledged go-to-market motion from Day 1. Hire top talent in sales, marketing, product, customer success. Invest heavily in building brand, generating leads, closing deals, onboarding customers, and driving retention.\nPros: Faster time to market, stronger foundation, scalable growth\nCons: Higher upfront costs, longer path to profitability\nPlaybook #2\nBuild for the short sprint. Focus solely on getting to $1M ARR as fast as possible. Hire a tiny sales team or even do it yourself (if you can). Cut marketing expenses and rely on organic channels like word-of-mouth and inbound leads.\nPros: Rapid initial traction, minimal upfront costs\nCons: High risk of burnout, slower long-term growth\nWhat’s the right choice?\nIt depends on your specific situation:\nIf you have a proven product-market fit and are looking to scale quickly, go with Playbook #1. You can’t build brand without spending money.\nBut if you’re still finding Product-Market Fit or need to test assumptions about market demand before committing resources, Playbook #2 is the way to go.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So you're building an awesome new AI SaaS app that's getting some real traction.\nA few questions for now:\nWhat is your CAC?\nHow long do deals take to close? And what are the exact steps in those processes\nDo sales reps really need to work with customers or can they just drive pipeline?\n\nIf it takes 6+ months and you're spending > $10k ACV, then a classic SaaS model often works best. You need lots of AEs.\n\nBut if your deal sizes are < $2K ACV, the CAC is low (like <$5k), and reps can get a lead to close in 1-3 weeks... you might be able to do more with less.\n\nIt's not impossible.\nYou just have to be hyper-efficient.\n\nSo, I've learned two playbooks here:\nThe Classic SaaS Model\nA lot of folks that sell $10K+ deals see the world as a big, slow enterprise sales process. It takes 6 months to close a deal. And it costs >$5k CAC to get there.\nYou need lots of AEs.\n\nI've seen many AI apps where you can't really hire anyone until your revenue is > $1m ARR. That's just how this classic model works.\n\nBut if all that's true, then go long with headcount.\n\nThe Efficient Startup Model\nIf it takes 2-3 weeks to close a deal and the CAC is low (like < $5k) ... sometimes you can do more with less.\nI've seen many AI", "cleaned": "The math works if you can close $500K deals with 90%+ attach rates and 100% net retention, or $200K deals with similar economics. But it doesn’t work for most SaaS companies that rely on SMBs or smaller enterprise customers. And even then — you have to grow really fast. At least twice as fast. Not just faster than before. Faster than everyone else too. If you’re growing 70-80%, you need sales reps. Period. Anything below 100% growth means your current team is already struggling to keep up with demand. Add more people and you’ll be swimming in leads without coverage. That’s exactly what happened to me with our outbound agents. We had no one to follow up with them! The other thing nobody talks about enough: when you do hire an actual human sales team, you lose the velocity advantage. Those AI agents were closing leads within hours. A human rep takes weeks. So even if you could afford to scale humans indefinitely (which almost none of us can), you’d still fall behind on conversion rates because of how much slower they are. The bottom line : If you want to maintain the hypergrowth trajectory from pre-AI days, you don’t need fewer salespeople — you need different ones. Or rather, you need both. More here: https://youtu.be/8NwGQc7C1dU", "balanced": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? There are two playbooks for scaling sales right now, and both are working — but they require fundamentally different strategies that most founders aren't accounting for. Let me break them down. 🟢Playbook #1: The Hypergrowth Machine (80-100+ AEs) This is what you see at companies like Windsurf ($6B valuation), Lovable ($10M ARR growing faster than Wiz or Databricks ever did), and even Cursor's enterprise arm. These teams can reach $20M-$50M ARR with just 30-40 reps. How? They're selling to developers and technical buyers who want to deploy instantly. When your product solves a pain point so obviously and quickly, you don't need as many people touching deals. CROs running these hypergrowth machines report closing rates as high as 15% on inbound, often because their product sells itself once someone gets into a trial. The math works because of the stickiness. If a developer signs up for your tool and loves it within minutes, getting budget approval becomes easier. It's not \"I'll talk to my boss next quarter.\" It's \"Let me buy this today before someone else does.\" This model breaks if you're selling outside tech, though. Your deal cycles lengthen, competition increases, and you hit natural limits where more bodies simply move more pipeline. But for hot AI tools targeting technical buyers? This playbook is printing money. 🟡Playbook #2: The Enterprise Army", "r32": "If you’re hiring salespeople because they were great at selling SaaS 10 years ago, you’ll end up with too many and waste everyone’s time. Find someone who has hired reps successfully for your current ACV, not someone whose entire playbook is based on $50K deals when yours are $200K+. And remember: if growth slows below 100% YoY, cut headcount now. This isn’t just about efficiency—it’s about survival. Every rep beyond what it takes to hit that number is probably burning money you don’t have. If you’re growing 500%, maybe 150-180 people sounds crazy smart right now. But if you get to 300% growth next year and then 250% after that? That same team becomes unaffordable. Plan accordingly. And keep an eye on those deal sizes—that’s where your real leverage lives. Even in the best of times, keeping your sales team lean while your product and market grow into larger and more complex deals is one of the most important things you can do as CEO. It's still true today.", "repaired": "But it also means you need to be willing to make hard decisions fast — and that sales leadership needs to earn their keep every single month, not just once a year when they hit quota. That’s what I see happening right now with AI-native companies vs traditional SaaS. And if you’re going down either path, commit fully. Don’t try to run both simultaneously. Pick your model, hire for it, and go all-in. Your investors, customers, and team will thank you."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 225, "lemkin-clone-v5": 197, "lemkin-clone-v6": 239, "cleaned": 216, "balanced": 238, "r32": 169, "repaired": 81}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X Post**\n\nLet's tackle one of my favorite topics - sales team size! At SaaStr, we've seen many hot AI B2B startups struggle with the same question: how big does your sales team need to be?\n\nThere are two playbooks here.\n\n**Playbook 1: The \"Hire Early\" Playbook**\n\n* This is what I did at Echosign (now Adobe).\n* We were a tiny startup, and we knew our AI-powered e-signature product was going to disrupt the market.\n* So, we hired top sales talent early on - even before we had all of our tech built out!\n* Our sales team grew from 0-20 people in just over a year. And it paid off: we were #1 in customer acquisition for", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So I was on with CEO and Founder\n@KrispAudio\nwho has 3+ folks working for him to get Krisp into the top Clouds\nIt just gets so hard in the enterprise, right?\nBut it is possible\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is May 13-15✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nOctober 1, 2024\nSo I was on with CEO and Founder\n@KrispAudio\nwho has 3+ folks working for him to get Krisp into the top Clouds.  It just gets so hard in the enterprise, right? But it is possible.  So how big a sales team do you really need at a Hot AI B2B startup?\nThere are only", "lemkin-clone-v6": "If you do have to hire sales reps, I’d say start with 1 rep per $100m ARR if you can. That’s what we did. And then just scale it up from there. But the question is — how much of your growth will be driven by them? Maybe not very much at all. Just get a VP Sales that understands this and knows how to do it.", "cleaned": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "balanced": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "r32": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks. When I look at hot AI companies right now, they’re all running different sales team models—and that’s okay. Because here’s what I’ve learned after years of watching this play out: There are two fundamentally different ways to scale your go-to-market motion as you grow from $1M ARR to $10M+ ARR. And both work—if you pick the one that fits who you actually are today. But way too many founders try to do Hybrid Mode without being honest with themselves first. They end up burning through cash and their CRO quits because nothing makes sense anymore. So let me break down the two approaches, and then give you some real data on how 20", "repaired": "How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup?\n\nThe Two Playbooks.\n\nTL;DR:\n\nMost traditional SaaS companies follow one playbook: hire reps as fast as you can afford them, because every extra dollar spent on sales drives up your valuation and makes it easier to raise money.\nBut many AI-native startups are running a very different play — one that could be more profitable (and more sustainable) if they're willing to accept slower growth for now. Here's what I've seen across dozens of portfolio companies and conversations with founders building in AI:\n\n#1. The Traditional High-Growth Playbook\n\nThis is how most B2B companies operate when they have access to capital and need to grow revenue quickly:\n\nHire 10-15 AE reps"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 120, "lemkin-clone-v5": 103, "lemkin-clone-v6": 67, "cleaned": 19, "balanced": 19, "r32": 132, "repaired": 127}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin on how big of a sales team you really need at a hot AI B2B startup:\n\n**Title:** \"How Big of a Sales Team Do You Really Need at a Hot AI B2B Startup? The Two Playbooks.\"\n\n**Ladies and gentlemen,**\n\nAs your CEO or founder, one of the most critical decisions you'll make is how to build out your sales team. At a hot AI B2B startup like yours, it's tempting to think that more sales reps equals more revenue growth. But I'm here to tell you today that this simply isn't true.\n\nIn fact, if you're not careful, throwing too many salespeople at the problem can actually do more harm than good. So what are the right numbers? How big of a sales team do you really need?\n\nLet me share with you two playbooks for building your sales team: **Playbook #1: The Lean and Mean Machine**, and **Playbook #2: The Big-Bang B2B Blitz**.\n\n**Playbook #1: The Lean and Mean Machine**\n\nThis playbook is perfect for hot AI startups like yours that are still in the early stages of growth. You're likely to have a relatively small customer base, but you need to scale fast without breaking the bank.\n\nAt this stage, I recommend starting with just 2-3 sales reps who can handle everything from prospecting to closing deals. These reps should be experts in both your product and industry, able to build relationships with key decision-makers and drive revenue growth through strategic partnerships.\n\nAs you grow, you'll need to add more reps, but start small and focus on quality over quantity. Train them well, empower them to make decisions, and let them run with it. This will allow you to scale efficiently without wasting resources on unnecessary overhead or bureaucratic red tape.\n\n**Key metrics for Playbook #1:**\n\n* Revenue growth of 2-3x YoY\n* Customer acquisition costs under $10K\n* Sales team efficiency (revenue per rep) at least $200K\n\n**Playbook #2: The Big-Bang B2B Blitz**\n\nNow, if you're further along in your journey and have already established a solid product-market fit, it's time to bring in the big guns. This playbook involves scaling up your sales team rapidly to drive massive revenue growth.\n\nIn this scenario, I recommend hiring 5-10 experienced sales reps who can hit the ground running. These teams should be organized around industry verticals or specific customer segments to ensure you're maximizing market share and driving efficiency through specialization.\n\nWith a large team comes more complexity, so make sure your processes are scalable, your tools are integrated, and your data is flowing smoothly. Train these teams on your product and sales methodology, empower them with autonomy, and watch the revenue dollars roll in!\n\n**Key metrics for Playbook #2:**\n\n* Revenue growth of 5-7x YoY\n* Sales team efficiency (revenue per rep) at least $400K\n* Customer acquisition costs under $20K\n\nSo, which playbook is right for you? The answer depends on your specific situation. If you're early-stage and need to scale quickly without breaking the bank, **Playbook #1** might be your best bet.\n\nBut if you've already established a solid product-market fit and are looking to drive massive revenue growth through aggressive sales efforts, then **Playbook #2** is the way to go.\n\nRemember, building an effective sales team takes time, patience, and practice. So choose wisely, stay focused on customer value, and always prioritize efficiency over extravagance.\n\nThanks for joining me today!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I’m on so many boards now, I have to ask every founder who’s growing fast with their head of sales what they’re doing for next year. And then the founders tell me how much more money they want to raise or that they’ve already raised a billion dollars and it’s not working. Or worse — it is working. But in most cases, there are 3 problems:\n1st Problem\n— The Sales Team Is Too Big.\n2nd Problem\n— It Isn’t Working At All.\n3rd Problem\n— The CEO Doesn’t Really Care About Revenue Growth Anymore.\n\nMost VCs don’t really care about #3. They just want the revenue growth to continue as expected, and they’re willing to fund a lot of it if they see it happening. But the first two problems? That’s usually what you’ll get fired for.\nSo here are my best tips to avoid #1: How Big Of A Sales Team Do You Really Need At a Hot AI B2B Startup?\nThis is something I wish more founders would really do some planning on, and at least think about. So many of us just wing it in sales. But you can’t wing the headcount.\n#1 — If Your CAC Is < $10K, Then You Probably Need 30-40 AEs\n. This isn’t a hard-and-fast rule, but if your CAC is below that threshold and your growth rate remains strong, then you’re probably going to need a team of about 30-40 AE’s per million in ARR.\n#2 — If Your CAC Is > $10K, Then You Probably Need 5-10 AEs\n. This makes more sense to me than #1. If it takes you longer and requires so much more investment on your end (e.g., training, support), then a smaller team should suffice.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "so we've been doing this saster podcast for over eight years now so it's not really that new but i thought this was actually one of the best ones lately and why is this great you know what do you need if your hot startup in b2b today how much sales team should you have because a lot of folks are saying 50 to be competitive right 100 to just compete in ai in in some categories or more but that's not true there's two different playbooks here and the only way is is to know which one you're playing so we had jeff from clavio who i've known for years he was actually on our podcast back when it first launched like eight years ago clavio has become a massive name in ai b2b today they are worth over 10 billion dollars and maybe more by the time this goes out but jeff and his team were very early to get into all of this so i asked him what did you need for sales to take off at your startup he said we had four reps to start when we hit a million dollars in revenue then six and then eight and that's it now they have thousands because it does scale but there was no magic to getting from those first four reps to the next six or even the next 30 or 40 here's what i think of as playbook one which is really more like playbook two you know most folks say look you need at least 20 salespeople to be competitive in b2b today and it's true if you don't have a product that can sell itself if your product doesn't self-serve if you're not able to do the majority of customer acquisition yourself or with minimal human assistance then", "cleaned": "So, what does that mean for your sales team and how many reps you need? Well, it's kind of simple if you've sold into big companies before. And if you haven't, this is going to be really hard on you because it just seems so much easier than the old days when everything was slow moving and people needed discovery calls and pilots and all these things. I'm not saying those aren't necessary. They're absolutely necessary. But there's no time. There's no time to do any of it. It's too competitive. There's no time. So here's what we have learned over the last year or two. If you look at the best AI native leaders, Harvey, Sierra, Gamma, Lovable, Replet, etc., they all have something in common. Not only are their growth rates crazy. Not only are their NRRs insane. Not only are they disrupting whole categories, but look at who works at them. Look at who runs marketing and demand genen and product and sales. These folks often come from very established brands, but more importantly, they come from hot ones. Like they worked at cursor before replet. They worked at lovable before lvoable. They worked at open AAI before gamma. Okay, now think back to some of our classic SAS sessions with Sam blonde from HubSpot. He's one of my favorite speakers. What he said years ago is basically you want to hire like someone who worked somewhere great before. Someone who has been successful. That's always true, but especially today. This is especially true today because everyone wants to work at the hottest company possible. Everyone wants to get out of their office and join the coolest startup. They don't want to join a mediocre one anymore.", "balanced": "the sales team was 90% people with one to two years of experience and they've scaled it up even more now that's not always possible for everyone but if you can do it it will be accretive I mean every experienced AE or SDR is going to outperform someone brand new so when the market is hot as long as your product sells itself as long as your customers are buying without friction as long as leads just come into the funnel and close on their own which we have seen over and over again at hot AI companies there really isn't any need to hire folks that aren't seasoned pros because it doesn't matter right it's all demand pull playbook playbook number three and this is where we're seeing the most variation these days because there are so many different types of leaders in AI and some of them want to go big fast others don't what we see with a lot of these late stage startups who raise huge rounds and get bought by Meta or OpenAI or Google or Anthropic they often go from zero to 10,000 employees very quickly sometimes in less than a year I think Wiz went from something like six to nine thousand employees in eight months yeah wow oh my God and then after they're acquired they kind of plateau okay so these companies have been acquired by a large tech leader and they know that that leader has an almost infinite budget to acquire new customers and support existing ones so why wouldn't you staff up a massive sales team because you're gonna spend a billion dollars next year selling products to Meta or whatever right it makes total sense but how does that help you survive in a world where you may never be", "r32": "but you'll see some of these leaders have gone more enterprise and that's okay if it works for your company but what I'm trying to say is don't be afraid to go big with sales if you're hot because if nothing else you can always dial it back later and I think we've all learned this right now from going into the post-COVID world where we got very efficient on our sales teams in many cases too efficient and then realized no wait we need them after all so let's get back there um just remember that as long as the leads are coming in and the customers are buying when they buy if you need four or five or even six reps per million dollars in revenue go ahead and do it and don't let fear hold you back from hiring great people today because if anything we know how important it is to hire those vps early especially VP sales and VPS marketing but also maybe not quite as importantly AEs and SDRs if you want to scale because once you hit initial traction that that engine will start to fail if you don't add fuel to it thanks everyone uh take care and happy selling hey everybody thank you again for watching saster and don't forget subscribe below for more content like this every week and check out sasser.com for all my latest thoughts on building scaling and investing in startups", "repaired": "I'm literally on Zooms with Founders today who are like, \"Jason, I need three reps by next week.\" And I say, \"Well, how much money do you have?\" They're like, \"I've got two million left.\" Okay, let's just pause there for one second. So they have $1.8 to put into sales and marketing. That means every dollar has to count. There is no room for error. Now look, if your customers can't deploy in five minutes or less, then that math doesn't work. If you need an SE, someone needs to come on site. Maybe it works in vertical software where you have maybe a handful of customers to onboard, but even then, if you haven't done this before as a CEO, it's so hard because the customer success team doesn't really exist yet. It's all on you. But most importantly, when you go from free or self-service to paid, people will pay immediately. Because they know they want something. If you've built the product right, the value prop is super compelling. You don't need time. In fact, I remember back in the day when we started EchoSign. We were doing $10 million in revenue. We had two reps closing deals. One was great. He'd close deals faster than anyone else in our company. He could outclose us. The other rep, he needed six months to learn the process. He would call a prospect and ask them what their problem was. Then he'd tell him how his product solved it. And then they would schedule another demo later. This guy closed zero deals for eight months. And then eventually, he became a very good AE. He did well. But here's the thing. Back then, we didn't know any better. We thought it took forever to figure out how to sell."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 575, "lemkin-clone-v5": 292, "lemkin-clone-v6": 306, "cleaned": 290, "balanced": 304, "r32": 244, "repaired": 300}}}}, {"id": "gold_026", "topic": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Give practical cash-survival advice founders can act on immediately.", "expected": "A strong answer should emphasize that founders avoid running out of cash through brutal visibility into burn and ruthless prioritization of what actually moves the business. The exact two actions can vary, but they should be concrete, founder-controllable, and tied to real survival discipline rather than abstract encouragement.", "facts": ["The answer should focus on a small number of high-leverage cash disciplines.", "It should make the advice practical rather than theoretical.", "It should connect survival to forecasting, speed, and focus."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**How to Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help**\n\n Ah, cash flow. The bane of every founder's existence. You think you've got it all figured out - the product is great, the market is huge, and the team is killer... until suddenly, you're staring at a dwindling bank account with no way to pay your bills.\n\nBeen there, done that (and gotten the t-shirt). As an investor in over 100 SaaS companies, I've seen my fair share of cash flow crises. And let me tell you - it's not just about cutting costs or \"burn rate.\" There are strategic decisions you can make upfront to ensure your company stays afloat... even when things get tough.\n\nSo what do I wish every founder knew? Two things that have saved countless companies from the brink of disaster:\n\n**1. Build Cash Flow Projections That Actually Mean Something**\n\nAs a founder, it's easy to fall into the trap of creating cash flow projections based on \"best-case\" scenarios or vague assumptions about your customers' behavior. Don't do this! Your financials are like a crystal ball - if you don't get them right, everything else will go haywire.\n\nHere's what I mean by actual, useful cash flow planning:\n\n* Start with real data from similar companies in your space (not just \"best-in-class\" examples).\n* Break down each line item on your income statement to understand the true drivers of revenue and expenses.\n* Account for seasonal fluctuations or one-time events that might impact your bottom line.\n* Run multiple scenarios, not just a single \"forecast,\" to see how different outcomes affect your cash position.\n\nThe goal is simple: create financial projections that accurately reflect your company's reality... even when things get ugly. It'll save you from making catastrophic decisions down the road (like \"we can definitely afford this new hire\" or \"those marketing expenses aren't eating into our margins\").\n\n**2. Build a Cash Flow Safety Net Before You Need One**\n\nIt's funny how often I see founders put all their eggs in one basket - that is, until disaster strikes and suddenly they're scrambling to cobble together emergency funding from investors or friends.\n\nDon't be that founder! Create your own cash flow safety net proactively by:\n\n* Building a sizable cash reserve (6-12 months' worth of expenses) before you need it.\n* Diversifying your revenue streams so you're not dependent on just one source of income.\n* Negotiating supplier contracts or subscription agreements with more flexible payment terms.\n\nIn other words, be prepared for the unexpected by building in some redundancy and flexibility. It's like having a fire extinguisher in case of an emergency - it may never get used, but when it does, you'll be glad you had one!\n\nThere you have it - two simple yet powerful strategies to help keep your company from going belly-up due to cash flow problems.\n\nAs I always say: \"Cash is king.\" Don't let it become a monster that devours your business. Be proactive about managing your finances and building in some safety nets, just like any responsible leader would.\n\nIf you're not doing this already - start now!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "A great reminder from Lloyed Lobo at Fundbox:\nThe reality is that many founders are running out of cash these days. But it doesn’t have to be this way.\nLloyd’s top two tips here for avoiding running out of cash as a founder:\n1.  Plan your burn rate\n— and stick to it.\n2.  Never, ever go 30+ days without paying vendors\nThese are the two things I found helped me most in my journey from $0-$100M ARR. And they will help you too. Related Posts\nSaaStr Annual: 1,000 VCs Will Be There. But How Many of Them Really Want To Invest?\nTop SaaS Companies Have 110% Net Revenue Retention On Average (Updated)\nHow to Get Back Into Venture Capital After a Gap", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So how do you not run out of cash?  I was asked this question again recently.  There’s no magic answer here:\nBut there are two things that can really help.\nFirst, it always helps to have more money than you think you’ll need\n.  It sounds simple, but it’s so true.  Even if you’re not planning to burn through your funds fast, or even at all — in fact, most great SaaS companies don’t lose any cash until the very last minute … or ever.  But still have a reserve.  Just think of how much more comfortable you’ll be just knowing you’ll never run out of money.\nSecond, it always helps to extend your runway\n.  Whether through cost-cutting, or even raising more capital when needed (but not too early).\nI know this sounds simple.  And in a way, it is.  But both are powerful and real tools for founders here:\nMore cash than you think you’ll need.  It’s always better to have extra.\nAnd extend your runway if possible.  You can always go back to investors or raise more capital when needed.  But just the knowledge that you could extends the time frame — until at least you see some real traction and momentum in sales, marketing, and revenue.\nRelated Posts\nDear SaaStr: How Do I Protect Myself from Running Out of Cash?\nSaaS is a multi-year journey. There's no need to run out of cash for years.\nYour First VP Should Be Sales", "cleaned": "The other day I was chatting with an investor who’s seen his fair share of companies run out of cash. He told me something that stuck with me: “I see founders make one mistake again and again when they’re worried about running out of money — they cut everything.” The opposite is what you need to do. And it’s not actually all that hard. If you have 12 months or more of runway left … but are getting nervous about the future (which we’ve been for 18+ months) … here’s my advice: Don’t Cut Everything. What I mean by this is don’t get caught up in trying to save every dollar. Cutting headcount? Maybe. But be careful. Do you really want to break your team’s spirits now? Are you sure? Instead, just make two simple changes to how you think about spending: First : Start tracking your burn rate religiously. Every single month, know exactly where every penny went. At least at first, track every expense down to the last dollar. You can use tools like Expensify, Ramp, etc. It’s so easy these days. Second : Set aside $20k-$30k each quarter into a new fund called “Future Spend”. This will become your safety net for unexpected expenses. Here’s why both matter: Your Burn Rate Will Surprise You If you haven’t tracked your burn rate before, start now. Most SaaS startups underestimate their monthly spend by anywhere from 10-50%. Why? Because every little thing adds up—those extra meetings, those unnecessary consultants, that side project you didn’t budget for. When you finally sit down and calculate actual vs. projected costs, you’ll often find yourself shocked. A lot more than expected goes on fueling the machine.", "balanced": "If you can’t figure it out on your own — find someone that can. If nothing else, hire an external CFO to review and improve all this. I was lucky enough to have my co-founder be our first CFO, but that’s not always possible. Either way, get help here. And then just make sure every month after that is better than last month. Even if it’s just by a little bit. A related deep dive with David Sacks here: The Real Advice for Raising Venture Capital (And Just About Everything Else) With David Sacks from Y Combinator", "r32": "So I’ve done this for quite some time now — but I still see it all too often. So many founders that are struggling to hit their numbers, growing slower than planned … and then they run out of cash anyway. Because even though they aren’t growing fast enough or hitting their plan, at least they’re not burning much. And they don’t realize until it’s almost too late how far behind they really are. How do you avoid running out of money when things get tougher? A few thoughts: First, if you have any sort of traction (even just one customer), hire someone full-time to manage your finances. If nothing else, make sure your controller is focused on reducing burn rate first. Second, set aside at least $1m extra each year, no matter what. Don’t let yourself spend more than that per month. Once you cross that line, there will be too little discussion around cutting costs. You’ll start getting into denial mode. Third, track every single expense. This may seem obvious, but so many folks don’t really keep track of expenses once they’ve gotten going. It doesn’t help anything. Fourth, talk with investors regularly after an initial round. Many VCs won’t ask about the burn rate unless you tell them. Fifth, understand your ARR multiple today, and know exactly where you need to be next year to raise another round. This will force everyone to focus on efficiency. What actually works well: One great thing we did at Adobe Sign / EchoSign was build a “Zero Cash” slide into our monthly board meeting deck. We tracked Zero Cash Days, and presented it every single month without fail. We’d go through why we needed X days of runway.", "repaired": "The second thing that helped me was being more thoughtful and disciplined with our cash burn rate. We were still burning $500k+ per month at this point (it’s hard to get rid of that many sales reps!), but we’d gotten much better at forecasting when it would happen, and then building a buffer into our runway planning.\nThis meant I could sleep better knowing exactly how long my money was going to last — even if it wasn’t infinite. And when we finally did run out of runway,\nI knew\nexactly what day that would be. No surprises. Which made all the difference for everyone involved.\nIf you’re running towards zero and you don’t know exactly when you’ll hit the wall, stop reading right now and go fix your reporting systems. You need to track every dollar, every week, and project forward accurately so you can plan accordingly. The best founders always do this. It’s not optional once you’ve raised significant capital or are scaling past $10M ARR.\nMore on that here:\nA Simple Tool to Make Sure Your Start-Up Doesn’t Go Under | SaaStr\nBut beyond just tracking your metrics, there’s something else critical that saved us time after time: quarterly cash planning meetings where literally every single person on the team had to present their spending plans for the quarter ahead. This is something I learned from one of our investors who runs his own company — he does this religiously every six weeks. Every functional area presents its spend projections for the next two quarters, gets challenged by finance, and walks away with approval numbers and clear accountability."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 528, "lemkin-clone-v5": 123, "lemkin-clone-v6": 245, "cleaned": 283, "balanced": 97, "r32": 288, "repaired": 271}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**How to Keep Your Startup Funded (And Actually Grow)**\n\nAs I've worked with hundreds of founders over the years, one thing has become clear: cash flow management is not just about avoiding bankruptcy - it's about giving yourself a chance to succeed. When you're running out of cash as a founder, every day feels like Groundhog Day: same problems, same fears, same desperation.\n\nSo what do I wish more founders knew? **Two things that have made all the difference in my experience**:\n\n **Cash Flow Projections Are NOT Optional**: Don't just forecast your revenue - project your burn rate and cash balance out 6-12 months. This will give you a realistic view of when (and how much) funding you'll need to raise next. Don't wait until it's too late!\n\n **Track Your Burn Rate Like It's a Key Performance Indicator**: Your burn rate is not just a number - it's a business decision waiting to happen. Monitor and manage your expenses, so you can adjust as needed before it's too late. This one simple metric has saved countless founders from running out of cash.\n\nIn short: keep your eye on the financial ball (literally). Don't let poor forecasting or burn rate management sabotage your startup. You're a founder - not an accountant! Take control, and give yourself every chance to succeed.\n\nWhat are some other essential cash flow tips for founders? Share in the comments below!\n\n#StartupCashFlow #FounderAdvice #GrowthHacking", "lemkin-clone-v5": "How to not run out of cash\nAs founders we often have two things on our minds at all times:\n– how do I raise more money? – and when am I going to be able to stop raising so much.\nIf you can focus just a little bit less on the former, and a lot more on the latter … it will really help.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The #1 mistake I see founders make again and again is not managing their cash flow properly. And even worse … running out of it.\nI get into this in more detail here:\nBut let me summarize the two things that actually help.\nFirst — you have to understand your burn rate. Not just how much money you’re spending per month, but why. Is most of it going towards headcount? Or is there something else driving it?\nSecond — if you’re growing slowly and predictably, maybe a slower growth plan makes sense for now. If that’s what the market will support.\nIf you have to make layoffs or cut costs later in any event … don’t pretend they won’t happen by running out of cash instead!\nAnd one more thing I see founders do again and again: They hide from their burn rate, their cash flow. Don’t. Get it right now. It’s not fun, but it’s better than being blindsided when you run out.\n#1 tip to avoid a Founder Crisis:\nUnderstand your Burn Rate\n– What is it? Why is it so high?\n– What can you cut back on today?\nA slow growth plan isn’t the end of the world. And hiding from cash flow is even worse.", "cleaned": "How to not run out of cash as a founder? Two things that really help: First, force yourself to create a real Zero Cash Date. And then … push it out 1 month. That’s your new Zero Cash Date. It will be less stressful than you think. Second, set aside at least $500-$750k for a war chest. You may never use it all. But if times are tougher, or longer, than planned — you’ll need it. Again, it won’t seem like much once you have say $10m+ ARR and beyond. It will even feel wasteful. But trust me. In tougher times, that extra buffer is magical. You can hire just one more great VP. Keep the team together another quarter. Make those key investments. Do what matters. A small but mighty buffer makes such a huge difference when things get harder. Even if it doesn’t seem like “enough”.", "balanced": "How to not run out of cash as a founder. I’ve now done it twice (once myself, once with my first start-up), and then watched hundreds of others do it too. One thing I know is that if you want to make sure you don’t run out of money … You have to get better at both planning AND forecasting. And especially — being honest about your Zero Cash Date. What’s your ZCD? If you’re not sure, figure it out ASAP. But here are two things that really help when times are tougher: First, build a zero cash date slide for each month going forward, every single month. Do this exercise together with your co-founders, ideally during a weekly board meeting or investor update. This will force everyone to be brutally honest about burn rates and runway. Every time you look at that slide, you’ll see exactly where you stand – and what needs to happen next. It becomes your North Star. Second, implement “the 50/30/20 rule” for yourself personally: Spend 50% on essentials (housing, food, family), 30% on discretionary stuff, and 20% on saving/investing back into the company. Don’t spend more than 100% of what you take home. Your personal financial health directly impacts your ability to keep funding the business. When founders lose their personal financial stability, they make panicked decisions that hurt the entire company. This one simple framework keeps me grounded even today, years after the fundraising journey ended. The key insight? Running out of money isn't just bad for your startup", "r32": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "repaired": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help #1. Be more honest with your team and board on cash burn And — do it each month. Each month. Don’t wait for “the perfect plan”. You have to be brutally honest with where you are today burning cash, and what that means for next year. More on that here:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 251, "lemkin-clone-v5": 65, "lemkin-clone-v6": 210, "cleaned": 148, "balanced": 256, "r32": 16, "repaired": 66}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X (formerly Twitter) post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**X Post**\n\nJust got asked AGAIN how to not run out of cash as a founder...\n\nTwo things that REALLY help:\n\n **Hire a CFO ASAP**: Don't try to figure this stuff out yourself. Get someone who knows what they're doing on board, and let them handle the finances.\n\n **Plan for 24-36 months of runway (not just \"we'll raise more money\")**: Anticipate your burn rate, assume you won't close a new round when you need it to, and plan accordingly. Don't get caught in cash crunch hell\n\nDitching these two mistakes saved me from disaster...", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So you know how I love founders. They are the best. So resilient. And so dedicated to their mission and vision.\n\nBut you can’t be too crazy on your way to that first $1M ARR or even beyond. You have to be careful with the money.\n\nBecause if things don’t work out, it’s all gone. All of it.\n\nIf nothing else, this is something I’ve learned over time.\n\nThe 2 Things That Help Make Sure You Don’t Run Out Of Cash:\n\nNot having a huge office space until you really need it\n\nAnd — not hiring too many people at first that don’t bring in real revenue.\n\nThere are so many other things to learn from. But if you don’t run out of cash, then you can just keep going.\n\nSo these", "lemkin-clone-v6": "How to not run out of cash as a founder. Two things that really help:\n\nA lot more on this at\nSaaStr University:\n#1. Go Longer With Your Last Round Than You Think You Can\nAlmost all founders think the last round is going to be their final one … and it’s almost always too early.\nThis doesn’t mean you can do this forever, or even until 100% of your customers are on an annual contract, etc.\nBut here’s a simple rule:\nGo longer with that last round than you think you can. Even if it seems like just 2-3 months more is all you have in the tank to make payroll and sales comp for next quarter.\n\nYou’ll be surprised how far this gets you.\n\n#2. Make Every New Hire Pay", "cleaned": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:\n\n2 Things That Really, Really Help", "balanced": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:\n\n2 Things That Really, Really Help", "r32": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:\n\n2 Things That Really, Really Help", "repaired": "How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder:\n\n2.   Hire a great VP Finance / CFO at least by $5m-$10m ARR\n\nYou will finally get to zero cash flow then\n\nThey are magic at getting you efficient and making sure everyone pays their bills on time\n\nAnd more here:"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 107, "lemkin-clone-v5": 132, "lemkin-clone-v6": 132, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 16, "r32": 16, "repaired": 51}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: How To Not Run Out of Cash as a Founder: 2 Things That Really, Really Help", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a talk on \"How to not run out of cash as a founder\" in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title Slide:** Don't Be a Cash-Burning Zombie! Two Essential Strategies for Founders Who Want to Stay Afloat.\n\n**Lemkin-style intro:**\n\"You know, I've been there. You think you're building something big, and then suddenly... BAM! The cash runs out. And not just the cash – your confidence, your relationships with investors, even your sanity. Don't be a cash-burning zombie founder! Today, we'll talk about two critical strategies to keep that from happening.\"\n\n**Strategy #1: **\n\n\"First off, you gotta know your numbers like the back of your hand. I mean it, every single one of them. What's your burn rate? Your cash runway? How much money do you need to raise next quarter? You can't just wing this stuff; that's a recipe for disaster.\n\nNow, don't get me wrong – not knowing is okay at first. Every founder starts somewhere. But as soon as possible, you gotta have visibility into your financials. Get yourself an accountant (or two), set up some dashboards, and... **Track Your Cash Flow Like Your Life Depends On It**. Because it does.\n\nI remember when I was running EchoSign; we were burning through cash like crazy, but our finance team had a dashboard that updated in real-time. We could see exactly how much money we were making or losing on each customer, every day of the week. That visibility saved us from going out of business more than once.\n\n**Strategy #2: **\n\n\"Secondly – and this is just as important as knowing your numbers – you gotta prioritize cash-generating activities over everything else. Don't get me wrong; building a great product or service is essential, but if you're not generating revenue, what's the point?\n\nPrioritize your sales pipeline, optimize your cost structure, focus on high-margin customers... You get the idea. I used to joke that at EchoSign, we were a 'sales company with a software product'. That was true – our top priority every day was making new sales and closing deals.\n\nNow, this might sound obvious, but trust me: **Cash-Generating Activities Are Not Optional**. If you're not focused on generating revenue as quickly and efficiently as possible, your cash will run out before you know it.\"\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\"So there you have it – two essential strategies for founders who want to stay afloat:\n\n1. Know your numbers like the back of your hand.\n2. Prioritize cash-generating activities over everything else.\n\nRemember: if you're burning through cash faster than you can raise more, you might as well be flying on a wing and a prayer. Stay grounded, stay focused, and always keep an eye on that cash flow.\"\n\n(Note: Jason Lemkin is known for his energetic and conversational style, so this talk is written in a way to capture the tone of one of his popular YouTube videos or podcasts.)", "lemkin-clone-v5": "here's how you can avoid running out of cash and let me tell you that one thing is enough but I want to share two things it just works so much better than just one okay first what matters most in the earlier stages in many cases is getting your burn rate down now this is a subtle topic because we often talk about the other side how to raise more money like you can't do both right if you're trying to slow down your growth and get cash flow positive that's not going to work I'm talking about something else here which is it matters less in some ways what you make per customer but at least one thing will matter even more than the other is how much of a hit on gross margins you take or put another way how efficient are you as an early stage start-up now this may seem obvious and for many folks that's not their issue they're selling something to big companies maybe it is for you if your customers don't pay right away then at least do them the favor and get some of these deals in hand get some of these deals done ahead of time I mean we all need a little bit of room to breathe here but what matters most is how efficient are you as an early stage start-up because it's not that hard okay let me give you two examples one is if your gross margins were 70% or better and then at IPO they're still 60% that's great growth right but the other side is a little bit more nuanced here which is that if your gross margins are in the teens and maybe even less than that like we've seen with some folks like HubSpot back in the day", "lemkin-clone-v6": "okay you know cash is king in startups right and so many founders screw this up it's all too common but there are two things that I've seen work again and again when done correctly number one focus on getting to $1 million in revenue as fast as humanly possible okay now most of us will never get from 0 to $1m in a week or even a month maybe not even next quarter so let's make this a goal for the year because if you hit that goal and you don't burn too much cash you'll be okay yeah it might take longer but it just doesn't matter and I know what you're thinking why do we need to get there in 12 months or less well first of all it shows you have some traction right it's not like this is a crazy product that nobody wants that's the biggest risk in early stage startups so if you can make $1 million even with terrible margins that means your team was able to sell something and it doesn't matter what it is it could be software or hardware or whatever but let me tell you I've seen all of these work okay a couple of examples from folks we invested in one company called maverick labs had zero revenue at the end of December 2019 and then they hit $1 million by June 2020 with almost no burn that's crazy right it was a lot of hard work but it shows you can do this second focus on keeping your burn rate as low as possible okay now I've seen founders take this to an extreme and I'm not saying we should all be doing this but what does happen is when the burn is too high even if you're growing at crazy rates you will run out of cash", "cleaned": "the first one that's really magical is when you have a repeatable sales motion and this is hard for many Founders to get their head around especially if they're not from an Enterprise background but here's what I can tell you it gets easier and easier once you close your first million dollars in Revenue okay you know why because there are patterns there are patterns and there's something magical which happens with sales in SAS and B2B companies where the good ones are so predictable right um and let me just give you two examples if you look at HubSpot who we all love and respect today and I've been lucky enough to be on their board since day zero um they're up over a billion now but back in the day like early days before even a million revenue uh there were only three people in sales there was Brian Halligan and darmesh and two other reps and you could go watch a saster session with them and they would show you how each month every single month the pattern repeated themselves they closed X deals at Y price points with Z ACV and then they had this chart showing you exactly how much pipeline came into the month how much converted through the funnel and by doing this magic they could predict every quarter down to the dollar how much Revenue they'd bring in and then Brian went off to do his own thing and started brex and he did it again with Brex and you'll see the same exact thing with folks like talk desk intercom and others these guys figure out how to build efficient sales teams and they become more efficient than anyone else and then the second thing that becomes super powerful is plg or self- serve or whatever you want to call it", "balanced": "if you're going to run out of cash if you have less than eight months or so and maybe even three months it's too late but if you know you can't raise another round at least look for that one or two things I did with Adobe sign echo sign we had no extra money in the bank literally zero what we could do is build this partnership with PayPal which was super expensive to develop and support but it would pay us an insane amount of revenue per month per year more than any other customer we had um so we put every single resource on earth into building that product because ultimately once it launched it became our number one source of recurring revenue okay point number two and again these are not new points they've been around forever but I see founders screwing up all the time who don't get either of them done the second way to save your arse when you think you might be running low on cash is to slow down headcount growth now everyone wants to grow faster especially today we want to go from a million to four million five million ten million next quarter like clockwork right uh you may need to do that but sometimes you gotta make some tough decisions here let's say you just raised $5 million dollars last week you only spent half of it I'm still gonna tell you slow down hiring there's always a gap between raising the money and actually spending it right how much runway do you really have well let me take a look at my burn rate versus my balance sheet let's assume you spend 10K a month that means you should be able to afford six months if you don't overspend that means wait until you're halfway through that period before you start aggressively hiring", "r32": "founders really struggle with is how do I manage my burn rate and what's that magic formula to make sure you have enough money so you don't run out right it's not quite that simple but let me tell you two things that are super helpful um number one if your cash runway does not cover at least four months of burn and then growing like a weed it's too low okay even if you're doing well just imagine for example say your current month you're burning $200,000 a month to keep going maybe you can get to seven or eight million in AR this year okay it may be hard to grow much faster than that unless you have a rocketship product but assume it take that same $200,000 and double it each quarter okay and by the end of the year it's 1.6 million which means you need 400K to go from July through December 31st now think about it if you're running out of money before December 31st even though you've grown into something bigger you will fail because there won't be any time to hire another VP sales or marketing there won't be time to find another customer segment there won't be time to iterate on the product there won't be time to build up that extra layer of management and the competition will pass you by and you'll never get back that lost ground so if you can't raise more money and you cannot afford more than four months of cash on hand and then growing quickly and it doesn't matter how many customers you have it doesn't matter if your nrr is 150% if nothing else is working that four-month rule is almost always true that if you're gonna run out of money before december 31st no matter what you did last year you will never catch up next point is", "repaired": "a startup I know that's growing at epic rates and it was always cash flow positive they just kept all the money for now because we didn't need to raise any more money but the fact is you probably will have to so you can do this too if your investors are supportive um be honest with them on how long your runway really is not hide it don't make up numbers don't claim your longer than you are even though sometimes that works sometimes pretending you're further along than you are gets you some extra time or extra capital whatever but ultimately it doesn't help you get ahead um don't hide it from your board show them your zero line and then say here's my best guess how much Revenue I'm gonna bring in next month the next quarter and let me tell you what I'm going to spend here's the plan here's our burn rate right and if your VCS support you'll get great advice back they may want you to slow down if you're burning a lot of money maybe they want you to hire a little bit slower or invest a little bit less but they won't judge you for being transparent okay point two which I've never seen done well I see a lot of startups try this one but they fail and it doesn't work and yet I think it could be super powerful again it only works when you're transparen if you send out an investor update every single month where you put in your metrics no matter what they are and you write why we hit these goals or why we missed those goals and what we learned and what we did to address it every single month everyone knows where things stand you build trust you earn trust you earn the right to ask for their help and"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 483, "lemkin-clone-v5": 306, "lemkin-clone-v6": 312, "cleaned": 311, "balanced": 316, "r32": 317, "repaired": 317}}}}, {"id": "gold_027", "topic": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Surface hidden operational problems with multi-agent deployment.", "expected": "A strong answer should explain that once many AI agents are live, the hard problems shift from demos to operations: monitoring, coordination, reliability, stale data, and human oversight. The point is that multi-agent systems create a new management surface area that most teams underestimate. Founders should treat these as production issues, not side notes.", "facts": ["The answer should focus on real operational friction, not futuristic speculation.", "It should cover issues like supervision, drift, orchestration, or workflow brittleness.", "It should frame these as solvable but under-discussed execution challenges."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Title:** \"The Dirty Truth About Scaling AI: The Top 5 Issues Nobody Wants to Talk About\"\n\n---\n\nI've been fortunate enough to work with some amazing companies over at SaaStr, but none as fascinating as our recent collaborations in AI. We're talking about teams that have successfully deployed dozens of AI agents into production – and I'm not just counting the ones with fancy logos or PR teams.\n\nNope, we're talking real-deal, 30-AI-agents-in-production companies who are pushing the boundaries of what's possible with machine learning. And let me tell you, it's been eye-opening to see firsthand the challenges they've faced along the way.\n\nAs a seasoned entrepreneur and investor, I'm always on the lookout for insights that can help fellow founders navigate their own journeys – especially when it comes to scaling AI projects. So, after digging in with these pioneering companies, here are the top 5 issues nobody wants to talk about:\n\n**1. \"We're not just building one model; we're building multiple models... and they all have different problems\"**\n\nOne of our clients has deployed a whopping 15 AI agents across various product lines. Sounds impressive? Yeah, until you realize that each agent requires its own set of engineering resources, data pipelines, and maintenance schedules. It's not just about scaling the number of models; it's also about managing the complexity of individual models.\n\n**2. \"We thought we'd save money by using off-the-shelf solutions... but now our costs are higher than ever\"**\n\nMany companies assume that pre-built AI frameworks or platforms will magically solve their problems and reduce development time. But the reality is, these tools often require significant customization to meet specific business needs – which can end up costing more in the long run.\n\n**3. \"Our data scientists are now spending 80% of their time on 'data engineering'... not building AI models\"**\n\nAs companies scale their AI efforts, they inevitably face a new challenge: managing massive datasets and ensuring that data flows smoothly across various systems. This requires specialized skills – think DevOps for data engineers! And let's be real; it's easy to get caught up in the excitement of model development while neglecting this crucial aspect.\n\n**4. \"We thought we'd automate most of our processes... but now we have more humans involved than ever\"**\n\nWhen companies deploy AI agents, they often assume that automation will eliminate manual labor and reduce costs. But what happens when these agents start producing subpar results? Suddenly, human reviewers are needed to fix errors, adjust parameters, or even retrain the models from scratch.\n\n**5. \"Our business leaders have no idea how much this is all costing\"**\n\nLet's face it: AI projects can be notoriously difficult to estimate and budget for. As companies scale their efforts, costs can balloon – think thousands of dollars per month per agent! And if you're not keeping a close eye on expenses, these numbers can quickly spiral out of control.\n\nSo there you have it – the top 5 issues nobody wants to talk about when it comes to scaling AI agents in production. Don't get me wrong; I'm still bullish on AI's potential to transform businesses and industries alike. But let's acknowledge the challenges we're facing and work together to build better tools, frameworks, and best practices for tackling these problems.\n\nIf you're an entrepreneur or executive struggling with your own AI projects, don't worry – you're not alone! Let me know in the comments below what specific pain points you're experiencing.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We’ve been testing and rolling out agents for about two years now. It’s not new, but we’re just getting into production. And I have to say, it’s a lot of work.\nA LOT of work.\nSo far, we haven’t lost any deals due to agents — although there is one deal that was at the last minute saved by an agent (more on that below).\nBut we do lose prospects and even some customers in the process. So, this post isn’t about AI being magical or wonderful. It’s more like:\nhere are the top 5 issues you will face when rolling out agents into production.\nSo be prepared for them. And try to avoid them if possible.\n#1. The Agent Doesn’t Understand What Your Company Does\nThis is huge and I haven’t seen it addressed anywhere else. If your company does something complex or unusual, an agent can easily misunderstand what you do. We run 5+ agents daily at SaaStr, and the agent that helps us with our “AI Agent” program keeps trying to sell AI Agents. It’s not wrong — but for some reason it thinks it has a lot more budget than we actually have. And so it sends out emails asking if we’d like to buy an AI Agent package.\nI had to turn off this one because we don’t really need any more agents, and I’m getting 2-3 of these per day from the same agent. It’s also sending me stuff about our own AI Agents (which are OK but not magic), and things that aren’t related at all. And it’s just too much.\nThis is a real issue with complex business workflows that an agent doesn’t understand, especially if they’ve been trained on similar agents that have different use cases.\n#2.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I recently participated in an online panel with several other SaaStr founders, and we started discussing the reality of running 30+ AI agents in production across multiple companies.\nThe conversation quickly turned to all the issues no one really talks about when it comes to scaling agent usage. And I wanted to write this up as a deeper dive into what is going on behind the scenes for those that want more than just vendor marketing.\nWhat You Really Need To Know About Running 30+ AI Agents in Production\nThe Reality of Scaling Agent Usage: What Nobody Talks About\nI recently participated in an online panel with several other SaaStr founders, and we started discussing the reality of running 30+ AI agents in production across multiple companies. The conversation quickly turned to all the issues no one really talks about when it comes to scaling agent usage.\nThe truth is that while everyone’s talking about “we’re using so many amazing AI tools,” most people have only scratched the surface of what this technology can actually do at scale. And here are just a few examples:\nWhy We Had To Write 3,000 Lines Of Code For Our Sales Agent\nWe had to write thousands of lines of code for our sales agent because none of the existing tools could handle even half that complexity.\nOur marketing AI agents can only send an email once every four days. They crash otherwise. And we have no idea why. We’ve tried fixing it ourselves, but the vendor has been unresponsive and hasn’t provided a fix yet despite multiple requests.\nWe’re forced to use three separate “agents” for each of our different SaaS companies because none of them can handle the complexity across all platforms simultaneously.", "cleaned": "The “set it and forget it” dream doesn’t exist yet. The most successful agents are ones that you’re constantly monitoring, iterating on daily, thinking through how to improve them every single day. And if you don’t do this — your agent will get worse over time. Not better. It’s not magic. You have to put in the work. If you can commit to that reality, then go build something incredible with AI agents. But just buy an off-the-shelf solution? Don’t expect miracles from a product that doesn’t know anything specific about your business or customers. It won’t magically solve all your problems for you. More here: Dear SaaStr: What Are Some Things That Have Worked With Your AI Agents? (And A Few That Haven’t)", "balanced": "I’m not talking about “AI agents are too expensive.” I’m talking about that moment when you’re onboarding, and something just doesn’t feel right. The agent says it’s ready to go live — but you don’t actually trust it yet. That’s where most people get stuck. And here’s what happens next: They either push through anyway or delay deployment indefinitely Both paths suck for different reasons Pushing through : You launch with an untrusted system, hope for the best, and spend the first two weeks firefighting issues instead of iterating on strategy Delaying : Your competitors move forward while you wait for confidence, which never comes because you’re waiting for perfection When we launched Qualified, we had exactly this problem. Our co-founder Ryan was convinced he’d built something special. But when it came time to onboard our first customer, he couldn’t shake off this feeling that everything might be broken. So he did what any rational founder would do under those circumstances — he went back to basics. He started asking questions like: Does the agent understand our business? Can it actually hold real conversations? What does success look like beyond basic metrics? If it can’t answer these questions definitively, you shouldn’t deploy it. Period. Lesson learned : Trust is earned during onboarding, not assumed at purchase. Build in mechanisms for your team to verify readiness before going live. We added explicit checklists, quality gates, and safety nets throughout our onboarding process specifically designed to prevent this exact trap.", "r32": "The agent that was supposed to book meetings now just… stopped working because someone changed the meeting type field without telling anyone, and it broke the entire workflow. Or an agent gets retrained on new data, but the old version is still running for weeks while you figure out why everything looks wrong. This is not hype or marketing nonsense—it’s real and happening right now across every company I know deploying agents at scale. The bottom line : Plan your training cadence like you’re planning software releases. If something goes sideways, roll back immediately. Test every change before letting the agents run with it. And have a rollback plan ready before day one.", "repaired": "We’re not talking about “Hey ChatGPT, what’s my sales pitch?” We’re talking about complex systems that need ongoing management and optimization.\n#1: The Human-Machine Handoff Problem (And Why It Makes Every Agent Less Useful Than You Think)\nHere’s something counterintuitive:\nEvery agent is less useful than you think because they can’t handle handoffs with humans well yet. Not even close to well enough for production use cases yet. Our support agents are great at answering questions on their own — they resolve 40%+ of tickets autonomously. But when we ask them to escalate or involve humans, it breaks down fast. They’ll loop back to us saying things like “I couldn’t understand your request” after already asking follow-up questions three times. Or worse, they won’t mention the human escalation option until right before timeout. This isn’t just frustrating — it costs money. When an agent could have handled a ticket entirely but instead forces a human handoff, you’ve wasted two or more hours of work and training time. And then there’s the issue of trust. If your customers get used to having everything resolved by an AI, they lose faith when they finally talk to a real person who says “Sorry, I don’t know this one.” That erodes customer satisfaction across the entire organization. So while our agents do amazing work independently, they create new problems when they fail to hand off properly."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 576, "lemkin-clone-v5": 296, "lemkin-clone-v6": 291, "cleaned": 124, "balanced": 248, "r32": 113, "repaired": 232}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Just Hit a Major Milestone with Our AI Program**\n\nWe just deployed our 30th AI agent into production, and I wanted to share some hard-won insights from this journey. People often ask me about the \"hype\" around AI, but few talk about the reality on the ground.\n\nAs we've scaled up our program, here are the top 5 issues that no one talks about:\n\n **Data quality is a never-ending battle**: We thought we had it covered when we started, but as our models got more sophisticated, so did the data problems. Ensuring high-quality training data has been an order of magnitude harder than expected.\n\n **Model drift and decay are real problems**: Our AI agents start performing well in production, only to gradually degrade over time due to changes in user behavior or new data patterns emerging. We've had to develop a robust process for monitoring model performance and retraining when necessary.\n\n **Interpretability is crucial (but hard)**: As we push the limits of what's possible with AI, it's essential to understand how our models are making decisions. But achieving true transparency has been tough – more on this in an upcoming post!\n\n **Integration with existing systems is a nightmare**: We thought it would be straightforward to plug our AI agents into our existing tech stack... but nope! It turns out that integration is often the biggest bottleneck.\n\n **Security and bias are not just checkboxes**: As we deploy more AI, we're acutely aware of the risks around data security and model bias", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We have 30+ agents now, and while they are super powerful to automate so much … there’s more than you might expect that can go wrong\nI wanted to share some of our top issues with AI agents.\nFirst, they all crash. Periodically. All the time. There is nothing worse than a 10-second pause in an agent when it was working perfectly before. It makes no sense. But it just does. Second, there are too many versions now. We have so many agents running on different platforms and we can’t keep track of them all. Each one has to be trained separately. And we always forget the other versions.\nThird, they all break when an integration changes. Not that this is a surprise. But it’s still frustrating. A new version of Salesforce? You have to retrain every agent again. It never gets easier no matter how many agents you run. Fourth, there are too many vendors now. We’re running 30+ different versions and we can’t always get the same level of support from each vendor. Fifth — and this is something folks just don’t talk about much in SaaStr — they all consume a lot of memory\nWe thought it would be great to run multiple agents on a single AWS server. It’s not great.\nSo, what I’m saying here is that while AI Agents are truly magical … there are a lot more issues than you might expect. They’re still in the early days for sure.\nBut they don’t work like other software. That can be confusing and frustrating to deal with. But if you want", "lemkin-clone-v6": "We’ve got about 20+ AI agents working for us now at SaaStr, and we’re scaling up fast.\nA few learnings from what’s worked:\nFirst, don’t make your first agent an enterprise product. It will be too complicated to train or use. Do a simple app that just does one thing well.\nSecond, keep it in production 24×7, not as a QA tool. I think this is why so many fail. They want them to work “great” — but they never really do until you keep them going all the time. Every AI agent has its own “vibe” that takes weeks of daily use to get right.\nThird, don’t expect it to be perfect at launch\nAI agents are always getting better and more polished over a few releases.\nAnd 4th — and most importantly:\nDon’t try to deploy an AI app yourself. Get someone else to do it for you. We’ve tried to build our own agent in-house from scratch, but after months of work, we still don’t have anything that works well enough.\nSo what are the top issues no one talks about?\n1. Training Time: 60-90 Days vs Promised Week\nWe were promised an “AI solution” would be working in a week. It’s now been over three months and counting. We’ve seen similar stories from other companies.\n2. Data Quality Issues (Still)\nEven after all these months, the data quality issues persist. The AI agents are still making stuff up or pulling incorrect information. And we can’t afford to constantly", "cleaned": "We have more than 30 agents running across our portfolio companies and SaaStr itself now, not just for sales but also for support ( Qualified), outbound (Artisan/Clay), inbound qualification (Agentforce), customer success (Fin), sponsorship follow-up (Delphi), content review (Gong/Qualtrics), event planning (Qualified + Replit), VC pitch decks (Replit), and onboarding (Salesloft). But here are five things nobody tells you about what it actually takes to get them going—beyond the initial excitement of watching your first agent send emails or close deals. And we all got this wrong at first. They need daily orchestration. Every morning I’m doing an hour-long block where I go through everything that happened overnight across all platforms. Not once a week—we’re talking every single day. It’s like managing a team of junior reps who can’t work independently yet. What did Agentforce do? Who did Artisan contact? Did Fin send out the right messages to customers who haven’t used their product in six months? The platform alerts don’t cut it—you need human judgment layered on top. Your humans need clear guardrails or they’ll mess up training. When we rolled out our first agentic BDR, Amelia, she was great with prospects. Then one weekend someone asked her to book a demo with a sponsor whose booth we were canceling because he hadn’t paid. She said sure. Never told us. By Monday afternoon, the prospect had already booked travel, stayed at a hotel near the venue, and arrived expecting to", "balanced": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About I’ve been running SaaStr’s AI agents for almost six months now, and we’re up to 20+ deployed agents generating $1M+ in revenue. The results are undeniable — but there’s something everyone glosses over when they share their “AI success stories”: the operational overhead is massive, and it gets worse as you scale. When people say “we have an AI agent that does X” without mentioning the maintenance work required, they’re selling snake oil. Or at least overselling how much work goes into making any agent actually performant and effective. Let me be clear: our top-performing agents don’t run themselves. They require daily management, continuous training, and real human oversight. If you think otherwise, you’re setting yourself up for failure. Here’s what nobody tells you about deploying AI agents in production: #1: You Need to Train Every Single Day For Real, Not Just Once Most vendors will tell you to train your model once or twice, then let it go. That’s completely wrong. Our best performing agents require constant tuning. Daily. And if you stop, performance drops off a cliff. It’s like asking your team to ship product without testing — except instead of shipping broken software, you’re shipping mediocre AI output. Think about it this way: Your AI needs more attention than your junior hire (who will leave) More investment than your mid-level manager (who won’t stay forever) Same level of commitment as your senior leader (because they", "r32": "The bottom line? Your first two agents need to be either (A) mission-critical AND cheap, or (B) expensive but genuinely transformative. Pick one category per agent for your first two deployments. Everything else is just training overhead that doesn’t move the needle. The mistake I made with SaaStr.ai was deploying it before we had enough training data and context. It’s now performing at an elite level — generating $1M+ revenue annually — because we invested heavily upfront to make sure it understood our entire database. But if you don’t have that luxury on day one, start simple. Get something working. Then double down when you see traction. If You’re Going to Do This, Commit Fully Now that I’ve outlined all this complexity, here’s what most founders won’t do: they’ll give up before month three. They’ll deploy an agentic solution, wait two weeks for results, get frustrated when nothing has changed yet, and then abandon ship. That’s not going to work with these tools. Not even close. My advice? Give yourself six months minimum from deployment date to meaningful ROI. Six months of daily management. Six months of iteration. Six months where things break regularly until you figure out how to prevent them breaking again. What to Expect Next Week In my next two posts, I’m going deeper into specific tactical mistakes I made along the way and exactly how we fixed each issue. Next week will cover: 📈 Why we lost nearly half a million dollars in sponsorships despite having an AI sales rep — and how we got those deals back using a", "repaired": "We've been running SaaStr's AI agents for over 6 months now and we're finally starting to get good at it. But here are the top issues that nobody talks about when you actually go deploy these things into production: #1: Your existing team will ghost your new AI agent If you hire an \"AI Agent Manager\" — they'll ghost your human team because talking with them is hard work. The best people I know don't want to do this job, or any job where all day they just have to talk with AI agents. And worse, most of our existing teams already sort of ghost us on many levels anyway. They didn’t really quit, but … #2: You need someone full-time managing agents (that’s me) Our AI GTM agents generate $200K+ in revenue. Our inbound qualification system processes hundreds of prospects per month. Someone needs to be full-time monitoring results, escalating edge cases, tuning prompts, and optimizing workflows. This person can’t be part-time—it’s too complex and time-sensitive. In my case, I spend about 10 hours a week managing our 20+ agents. That includes daily quality checks, prompt optimization, and dealing with the inevitable weirdness that comes up every single day. We also built custom guardrails so no one can send emails without proper context. Otherwise, everything would go sideways fast. #3: It takes weeks to train each agent properly Expect “go live” dates to slip by at least two weeks from what you think. Every vendor promises rapid deployment. None deliver it. Every agent requires weeks"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 261, "lemkin-clone-v5": 267, "lemkin-clone-v6": 252, "cleaned": 241, "balanced": 255, "r32": 264, "repaired": 256}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a possible X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**X Post**\n\nI've been living and breathing SaaS for over two decades, and I just hit a major milestone with our latest company: we have **30 AI agents IN PRODUCTION**!\n\nAs you can imagine, it's not all sunshine and rainbows. There are some issues that nobody wants to talk about... but somebody needs to say them out loud.\n\nSo here are the top 5 issues no one talks about when building a scalable AI operation:\n\n **1/ Scalability is not just about compute power** : Don't get me wrong, more CPU cycles don't solve everything. But what's even harder is scaling your data ingestion pipelines, and getting your models to adapt on-the-fly as user behavior changes.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I've built and deployed more than 20 production AI agents at SaaStr\nAnd I'm already managing 10 more. The agent economy is accelerating faster than anyone anticipated, but with it comes a host of issues no one talks about openly. Here are the top five problems that keep me up at night:\n1. The Agents Aren't Working Together. They Should Be.\nThis should be obvious in theory: if we have 30+ agents handling different parts of our business operations (content moderation, lead qualification, customer support, social media management), they should all work together seamlessly and share context. In reality? They don’t. This is the biggest problem I’m seeing across SaaStr’s AI deployments — not just ours but other companies too.\nEach agent thinks it has to", "lemkin-clone-v6": "The truth no one is sharing about deploying multiple enterprise-grade AI agents\nWe've built 15+ agent platforms, and I just got back from our latest deep dive with Gorgias' CTO, who's building a $100m+ B2B AI company. The reality? It doesn't get easier when you go from 1 to 30+ agents like we have now. What nobody talks about:\nData quality problems persist\nEvery agent still needs high-quality data training. This doesn’t magically improve as you deploy more agents—each one requires precise, relevant input.\nAgent-specific skills matter most\nDon’t assume your $50k+ AI agent can handle everything that was working for your other agent at half the price. Most don’t actually “learn” from each other—they develop", "cleaned": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About The honest truth from SaaStr's CRO + CEO after deploying 30+ AI agents across their entire business. Bottom Line Up Front : After running 30+ AI agents at SaaStr over six months, we've learned that even with proper training and management, these systems are fundamentally different than traditional software—and they require completely new thinking. The biggest surprise? How quickly every agent breaks without ongoing human oversight, not just once a week but daily. And how much more expensive it is to maintain them compared to traditional tools (think $10K/month for 20+ agents vs. $100/month per tool). Our 6-Month Journey From Zero to 30+ Agents Before this", "balanced": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About The tools are amazing. But nobody is talking about what happens when they break — and how you fix it. At SaaStr, we’re running 30+ AI agents now. That’s a lot for our team size, but it works because we have people dedicated to managing them full-time. Not as a side project. Full-time. As a real job. After two years of learning this hard way, here’s what I wish someone had told me before we deployed our first agent:", "r32": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About When people ask me about our AI agents at SaaStr, they want to know the magic numbers—how much revenue we’ve generated, what our cost per lead is, how many tickets we process daily. And sure, I’ll share those things. But there’s something that keeps coming up for founders and operators who are actually implementing these systems, and it almost never gets talked about in public: the unglamorous reality behind making AI work at scale. After going from zero to 20+ AI agents across marketing, support, content creation, sales development, inbound qualification, and more in just nine months—and managing a $150M+ business—you start to see patterns emerge that nobody warns", "repaired": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About \nhttps://saastr.ai/article/we-have-30-ai-agents-in-production-here-are-the-top-5-issues-no-one-talks-about?utm_source=edgar&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=\n… via \n@saastr"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 126, "lemkin-clone-v5": 126, "lemkin-clone-v6": 116, "cleaned": 123, "balanced": 96, "r32": 126, "repaired": 21}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of SaaStr founder and CEO, Jason Lemkin:\n\n**Title:** \"The Unspoken Truths About Scaling AI: What I Wish I Knew When We Had 30 Agents in Production\"\n\n---\n\nGood morning everyone! Today, we're going to tackle one of the most critical topics facing our industry right now. You know what's exciting? Having 30 AI agents in production. But here's the thing - it's not all sunshine and rainbows.\n\nAs a seasoned entrepreneur who's been through this journey myself, I'm about to share with you the top 5 issues that no one talks about when it comes to scaling AI. These are lessons we learned the hard way at SaaStr, but hopefully, by sharing them publicly, we can save others from making some of these mistakes.\n\n**Issue #1: **\n\n\"Data Quality is a Never-Ending Nightmare\"\n\nYou'd think that with 30 agents in production, our data would be solid. But nope! What I've learned is that even when your data looks good on paper, there's always something lurking beneath the surface. Whether it's missing values, inconsistent formatting, or downright errors... you'll be amazed at how quickly these issues can bring down an entire system.\n\nAt SaaStr, we had to implement multiple data validation checks just to keep our agents from spitting out garbage responses. Don't get me wrong - I love technology as much as the next guy, but sometimes I think it's like trying to hold water in your hand... you think you've got a grip on it, and then suddenly it slips right through.\n\n**Issue #2:**\n\n\"Model Maintenance is Like Playing Whack-a-Mole\"\n\nImagine having 30 agents out there all relying on the same model. Sounds great, until someone makes an update to that model, but forgets to propagate those changes across every single agent... Yeah, you guessed it - chaos ensues.\n\nWe've lost count of how many late nights we spent debugging why a particular agent was producing inconsistent results. The thing is, even if your models are \"perfect\" in isolation, the minute you put them into production with other agents, all bets are off.\n\n**Issue #3:**\n\n\"The Usual Suspects Are Always Lurking\"\n\nAs you scale AI, certain issues start to emerge that no one likes to talk about. Data bias? Check! Model drift? Double-check! And don't even get me started on the \"silent\" failures - those where an agent just stops working and nobody notices until it's too late.\n\nWe've learned to keep a close eye out for these issues, but honestly, they're like cockroaches in your kitchen... no matter how hard you try, there will always be some lurking around.\n\n**Issue #4:**\n\n\"The Customer Doesn't Know What They Want (Until It's Too Late)\"\n\nHere's the thing - when you deploy AI agents at scale, customers start to get excited. And that excitement often translates into a desire for customizations and tweaks... right up until they realize what those changes mean in terms of operational complexity.\n\nWe've had our fair share of \"AI-induced\" customer churn because we weren't prepared for the inevitable pushback on model updates. Lesson learned: it's always better to have those hard conversations upfront, rather than trying to retro-fit customizations later on.\n\n**Issue #5:**\n\n\"The Real Cost is Always Higher Than You Think\"\n\nAs you scale AI, costs add up quickly - whether we're talking about infrastructure, talent acquisition and retention, or the inevitable retraining of models as data drifts. And let's not forget the \"opportunity cost\" of not having those resources invested elsewhere in your business.\n\nWe've had", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So, I’m going to tell you what really works and also what doesn’t work with our experience deploying thirty-plus agents into production at SaaStr.\nHere are five things no one talks about:\nFirst:  You Have To Manage the Agents. They Don’t Self-Manage. We have a full-time manager who watches them, makes sure they’re all working as expected, and does the deployment of new models.\nSecond: The Data Has to Be Accurate and Complete. If it’s not, you’ll get the wrong answer from your AI Agent. It’s got to be good data. I know this sounds obvious but in practice what we find is that no one knows if their data is good or bad because they don’t really test it.\nThird:  There Is No “I” In Team\nWe have a full-time manager and three other people who are involved in testing the agents before they go into production. The AI Agent itself doesn’t get to work independently—it always has some human checking on it, reviewing what it does. It’s not magic yet.\nFourth:  You Have To Be Careful About Training Data\nThe training data is the foundation of an AI model. We have to be very careful about who we train and when we do. The worst thing that can happen with your agent is that it goes rogue and starts providing wrong or even bad information. This is what happens in movies, but this isn’t a movie—it’s happening now.\nFifth: It’s Not Really “AI” At All\nI know there are many of you who have read  the new book from Sam Altman called The Coming Age of S-A-I. I just finished reading it myself and it was an eye opener for me at least.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "We Have 30 AI Agents in Production. Here Are the Top 5 Issues No One Talks About\nAI is everywhere now, and it’s becoming a real game-changer for businesses. But while we’ve got a ton of experience with these agents,\nthey’re not all perfect\n.\nI wanted to talk about our top five issues because they’ll likely be similar for you too. And here are the 5 Top Issues We Have With Our AI Agents:\n1.  It doesn’t always work like it’s supposed to. This may seem obvious, but we find we have to retrain every single agent at least once a quarter in our production environment.\n2. The more complex your data is — the more time and effort you’ll need\n. We’ve got 30+ agents now across multiple products, but they all use different data structures. And some of these are pretty old. So we had to retrain each one for its specific data structure. It’s not fun.\n3.  The best ones aren’t the cheap ones. I think we paid something like $500/month total for our first 5 agents. But now? That was a quarter ago. And those prices have gone up, sometimes significantly. We pay about $1k-$2k per month each on average today. More than 10x what they were even just 12 months ago.\n4.  You’ll need to deal with constant “version updates.” These are basically forced upgrades that update the agents so you don’t have to do it yourself, but there’s a lot of overhead and issues here. So be prepared for this.\n5.  It doesn’t always work like it’s supposed to. I know we already talked about this one, but it’s true 2x over in production. That’s why we’ve got so many agents now. Because the first ones didn’t quite do what they were promised\n.\n\nWhat if you had more budget and better resources? What would happen then?\n\nWell — it wouldn’t be perfect still, but you’d have a lot fewer issues with AI agents than today.", "cleaned": "If you're not investing time every day to train it, like this is just another tool that doesn't work, right? Um, so I think if nothing else, these are my top five issues people don't talk about with AI agents and then we'll hit some bonus ones at the end um but the fifth one which is maybe the most important issue is there's no way to get rid of them once they're trained. And again, you've got to be careful on how many you bring into your organization because each agent consumes an enormous amount of data. Like you have to be very thoughtful who gets what kind of access, what level of security. But even more than that, once you deploy something, there is no delete button. There is no backspace for any of these agents. So the first one was hallucinations. The second one was context windows. This one is no matter how much you complain or argue or say, \"I'm going to turn off the agent.\" They never go away. They keep running in the background collecting more information. You can slow down the learning rate, but you cannot turn off these agents after they start. A lot of folks will tell you you could use Google's Gemini or OpenAI's API and replace all of this stuff yourself. That sounds great until you actually try it and realize none of these APIs handle all the things that the agents do. None of them handle CRM integration, calendar synchronization, email handling, ticketing systems. None of them can connect to HubSpot or Salesforce or Zendesk or anything like that directly. Some of them can ingest feeds from other places.", "balanced": "I think we're going to have a lot more conversations like this at sastra annual because I am convinced that you've got to be deploying these agents by next year if not earlier and we'll do it better together so thanks for having me hey everybody thank you for listening again on saster.com we will continue our conversation here um don't forget subscribe to our newsletter check out our free tools on Sasser.com we are there all day every day talking about how to build better B2B companies and uh look forward to seeing you soon okay everyone [Applause]", "r32": "hey everybody wanted to do a little deep dive on five issues that we've had deploying 30 plus agents and I want to be clear none of these are new or unique but there's so much content out there it can't hurt to add another voice because we're all still figuring this stuff out some of it is just you have to accept certain risks as an early adopter of any new technology and then others are things that maybe folks don't think about when they start down the path of buying their first agent or two so let me give you five issues we haven't figured out yet okay number one we didn't expect this with our AI SDRs and this may change over time but we did not expect them to book meetings for us which sounds great right you got your agenT sending emails booking meetings what could possibly go wrong well here's the thing and it took us months to figure this out once your AE gets good at qualifying inbound leads if the lead doesn't look like someone that wants to buy next week or even next month no matter how great the AI is it will send the meeting invite anyway it has learned this behavior from working with humans it assumes the human would want to schedule a call too and it does exactly what a human would do it sends that calendar link whether you asked it to or not now this is a feature right this is a great feature right we do want our AE to qualify our leads and we want them to decide who should get a meeting invite but um we found this was actually stressful for everyone uh especially for support because again the AI booked the meetings without permission and then support got angry at us for having bad vibes", "repaired": "I think we're going to see some of these issues go away, but if you look at what I'm spending now and where it's headed versus what my co-founder is spending or other folks on our team, that gap is not closing as quickly as people expected. And so there are two ways you can deal with this. The way I've done it is just accept it, put up with it, because ultimately the benefit for me is worth it. If I could get better outputs from the same amount of money, great. But I'm willing to pay more than I used to in order to have a better output. It's like buying a Tesla or an iPhone. You pay a lot. They cost more than they did years ago. But it doesn't matter. So I just accept it. Maybe one day when agentic software is cheaper, which it will be, maybe you'll buy five different tools and you won't worry about how much it costs. That may come down over time. Today, though, budget carefully, because you will need more budget per agent than you planned. This stuff gets harder over time. The learning curve goes up."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 592, "lemkin-clone-v5": 285, "lemkin-clone-v6": 333, "cleaned": 281, "balanced": 99, "r32": 315, "repaired": 198}}}}, {"id": "gold_028", "topic": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Explain why a company can outperform a weak software market and what founders can learn.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that when one software company materially outperforms the sector, there is usually a positioning, customer, or execution reason behind it. The founder takeaway is to study what creates resilience when the market is weak: clear value, focused customer fit, and a model that still works under tighter budgets.", "facts": ["The answer should explain why DigitalOcean may be bucking the market trend.", "It should connect market performance to product, customer, or positioning dynamics.", "It should turn the observation into a founder lesson rather than market trivia."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**DigitalOcean's Stunning Outperformance**\n\nI just got back from reviewing Q4 earnings for several software companies, and I have to say - one stock is standing out like a sore thumb: DigitalOcean.\n\nTheir revenue growth has been impressive enough on its own, but what really caught my eye was their year-over-year (YoY) growth rate. According to the latest numbers, DigitalOcean's top-line growth in 2026 was up a whopping **12%**!\n\nBut here's where it gets even more fascinating: while the broader software industry is seeing revenue decline by **24%**, DigitalOcean is bucking this trend.\n\nNow, before I dive into why, let me just say that I'm not surprised. This company has been quietly building momentum for years, and their product-led growth strategy is paying off in a big way.\n\n**Why DigitalOcean Is Crushing It**\n\nSo, what's behind this incredible outperformance? In my view, there are three key reasons:\n\n1. **Focus on Developers**: DigitalOcean has made it clear that they're focused on developers - the people who actually build software and applications. By providing an easy-to-use platform for development, deployment, and scaling, they've created a loyal customer base.\n2. **Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC)**: Their platform is built around IaC principles, which allow customers to define infrastructure in code rather than through manual configuration. This not only simplifies the process but also makes it more scalable and repeatable.\n3. **Price Sensitivity**: Let's face it - cloud computing can be expensive! DigitalOcean has been successful because they offer a price-sensitive solution that still delivers on performance.\n\n**The Broader Software Implosion**\n\nNow, you might wonder why the rest of software is struggling so much. In my view, there are several reasons:\n\n1. **Over-Promising and Under-Delivering**: Many software companies have promised more than they can deliver - particularly in areas like AI and machine learning.\n2. **Pricing Pressures**: The cloud market has become increasingly commoditized, putting pressure on prices across the board.\n3. **Shift to Subscription-Based Models**: While subscription-based models are a good thing for customers (predictable costs!), they can be challenging for software companies to execute.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nDigitalOcean's outperformance in 2026 is no fluke - it's a testament to their focus, innovation, and customer obsession. As the broader software industry continues to grapple with challenges related to pricing, execution, and relevance, DigitalOcean remains a leader in its space.\n\nIf you're looking for a cloud company that checks all the boxes (and then some!), I highly recommend taking a closer look at DigitalOcean.\n\n**Full Disclosure**: This is not investment advice. But if it were...", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So what happens to public SaaS companies when growth slows?\nThe stock price drops.\nWhen it dropped, a lot of folks told me “Just Move On.”\nBut I couldn’t move on from DigitalOcean (DOCO). And I should have listened! But there are reasons you might want to dig in and see if things can turn around. At least, at the margin:\nWhy? Well first, let’s look where SaaS is today.\nToday SaaS stocks are down 24% year-to-date. That’s huge. You probably haven’t heard about it much, but that’s because so many of us have been through a rough stretch and just kind of … moved on from things like DigitalOcean (DOCO).\nBut I couldn’t move on. And for good reason:\nThis is the best performing SaaS company in 2026!\nIt’s up almost +12% this year while everyone else has gone down. But why?  Why can it perform well when so many of its peers have not?\nWell, first DigitalOcean (DOCO) is still a leader — and growing at an incredible rate.\nAt $2B+ in ARR, they are still growing 39%.\nThat’s crazy fast even for the most mature public SaaS companies. It’s more than double what Salesforce is growing today!\nAnd it’s not just growth that makes them special. They have\nstrong free cash flow margins of over 30%,\na massive 3x+ in free-cash-flow-to-market-cap, and a solid balance sheet.\nThat combination of strong revenue growth with high FCF margins and decent fundamentals has driven this stock up +12% year to date when almost every other SaaS company is down at least -24%.\nSo why can’t we invest here? Well first, it’s not cheap. It trades at 8x ARR — a premium multiple but still below the average of most public SaaS companies.\nBut second and more important:", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So, a lot of folks I meet are still struggling with what to do today. And even worse, where to invest.\nThere’s a few simple answers here:\nYou’ve got to go upmarket\nThis is the #1 answer for all but maybe a handful of exceptions in SaaS these days. The average $2k ACV of public SaaS companies has grown 30%+ since 2020. Yes, it gets harder as you get bigger.\nBut if there’s anything that’s getting easier … it’s getting more enterprise customers and getting them to pay for the software. Just look at Atlassian being worth a stunning $100B today with its core product only now approaching $1 Billion in ARR:\nYou’ve got to go multi-product\nIf you’re not going upmarket, then the next step is multi-products.\nMost of us can’t just build one more feature and get 10x growth. That’s why no one does it anymore 🙂\nBut we all can start building a second product that goes deeper into an industry than our first product:\nWe do this because even if our core doesn’t grow at the same rate, you still have to keep growing in software.\nAnd going multi-product is a way to do that with less incremental stress on your team and management. And it’s also a lot easier when you’re already upmarket or close to it. Why? Because then you can just sell more of what your customers are buying anyway. The bar for success here isn’t really all that high.\nAtlassian is now worth $100 Billion with two products:\nOne, the Atlassian product suite at $800m+ in ARR\nAnd secondly, a brand new “$500m”+ ARR product called ‘OpsGenie’ it bought 2 years ago (and then rebranded as OpsGenie) … and is now worth almost half of what Atlassian itself was two years ago!\nThat’s the power of multi-product. It works just about everywhere, if you do it right.\nYou’ve got to go more enterprise\nThis one I know will sound counter-intuitive — but it really isn’t at all today in software:\nIf your core product is struggling to grow … then make sure that a portion of your products are going deeper into an industry.", "cleaned": "Cloud stocks are getting crushed. Everyone is saying “the AI bubble has burst.” Cloud spending growth is decelerating from its insane pace over the past few years. Growth rates have compressed. But one cloud stock is up almost 50% year-to-date and trading at a premium to its peers: DigitalOcean (DOCN). At $47 as I write this, it’s up roughly 48% since January 1st — while most major public B2B software companies sit somewhere between down 15-30%. The SaaStr team was talking about this yesterday on our pod with Harry Stebbings and Rory O’Driscoll. One investor said something that caught my attention: “We’re seeing Digital Ocean up 40-something percent YTD… That means they’ve essentially re-rated themselves into the top tier of all public infrastructure providers. That just doesn’t happen naturally without some kind of acceleration or re-acceleration in their business model.” And it really does explain everything. Because when you dig into what’s driving DOCN’s performance, it reveals why legacy enterprise software companies aren’t experiencing the same magic. Let me break it down for founders who actually care about these dynamics. Why DigitalOcean is Winning Right Now (And Most Public B2B Companies Aren’t) DigitalOcean isn’t winning because Marc Benioff decided to be more aggressive on pricing or because Microsoft finally figured out how to monetize ChatGPT properly. They’re winning because they’re still growing like a weed — faster than every single major enterprise software company right now. We’re not talking about a small bump here. As of Q3 FY25 (ended November 2025), DigitalOcean reported revenue of $346 million, representing an increase of 21% year-over-year.", "balanced": "The companies that win are ones like Cloudflare, MongoDB, and Snowflake — where demand is strong because AI agents need to connect, store data, and analyze it at scale. And then there’s the weird middle — infrastructure + platform services. These guys aren’t pure infrastructure (like DigitalOcean), but they’re not application software either. They sit somewhere in between, providing both tools AND infrastructure for developers. Datadog fits here. HashiCorp fits here. New Relic fits here. And most importantly from an investment perspective — these stocks have held up relatively well during this selloff. Datadog down just 2-5% on the year. HashiCorp flat or slightly up. New Relic around even. Because when you’re helping enterprises deploy, monitor, and manage their agent fleets, your stock doesn’t move with every quarterly earnings miss from Salesforce. It moves with the health of enterprise IT spending overall — which right now is still growing, albeit slowly. And that’s why I’m buying more $DOCN today. It’s not exciting. It’s not a disruptor. But it’s real revenue growth, with a business model that’s actually working in an environment where almost nothing else is. And as one investor noted, if we get through this downturn without a recession and the economy starts re-accelerating again, digital-native companies will be some of the first to benefit. If that happens, DOCN could easily triple over the next few years. Not because of any magical AI story. Just because it’s a reliable, profitable, cash-flowing business serving customers who keep needing more compute power regardless of what the S&P does.", "r32": "They’re selling infrastructure to developers who want to build applications, not to enterprises looking for managed services or multi-cloud strategies. They sell at the bottom of the stack — virtual machines and databases that power development projects from day one. That’s durable because every developer needs it. And here’s what makes it even stickier: they’ve gone multi-product. They launched Kubernetes clusters (KOPS) and edge compute (Edge). Developers are building AI agents on their platform now, too. This is a platform company with multiple growth engines that serve the same customer base. The TAM isn’t shrinking. It’s expanding as more software gets built. Three Things About DigitalOcean That Should Matter to You If you’re an SaaS founder thinking about your own business model, here’s what this tells me: First, don’t let the public markets scare you into making short-term decisions. Public valuations aren’t predictive. Private market multiples tell you where buyers think companies will go in three years. But if your unit economics are good, keep investing in growth. Second, understand where defensibility comes from. In today’s world, being indispensable is more important than having perfect gross margins. If you can show up every time someone builds something new — whether it’s infrastructure, agent workflows, or whatever the next big thing turns out to be — you have real leverage. Third, diversification matters but doesn’t solve everything. Yes, sell multiple products. Yes, expand beyond your core use case. But also make sure those adjacent products are genuinely additive to why customers buy you in the first place. Don’t just add features to stay relevant. Add products that make you irreplaceable.", "repaired": "And here’s what it means for every B2B founder reading this:\nIf you’re building infrastructure and developer tools, there is demand. There are buyers. And if your unit economics work — like they do at DigitalOcean — then growth will follow.\nThe lesson from 2025 is that AI didn’t kill compute. It accelerated it. Every major tech company now needs more cloud capacity than ever before. That demand isn’t going away. In fact, it’s just getting started. If you can provide that capacity reliably, securely, efficiently, and at a competitive price point — you have a real business with real revenue durability.\nFor everyone else:  the AI Boom hasn’t killed software … but it has dramatically reshuffled who wins and loses. Do you know which side you’re on yet?\nAI-First Companies Are Growing 30%-50% Faster Than Traditional SaaS\nThis isn’t just a trend. This is structural."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 415, "lemkin-clone-v5": 297, "lemkin-clone-v6": 362, "cleaned": 263, "balanced": 257, "r32": 269, "repaired": 146}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a LinkedIn-style post in the voice of Jason Lemkin:\n\n**\"The Surprising Winner in the Software Market... So Far**\n\nFolks, I just had to share this amazing data point with you all.\n\nAs we close out Q1 2026, one company stands head and shoulders above the rest: **DigitalOcean**. Their stock is up a whopping 12% year-to-date! Meanwhile, the overall software market is down a staggering 24%.\n\nWhat's behind DigitalOcean's meteoric rise? In my opinion, it all comes back to their laser-like focus on a specific pain point in the market: DevOps and infrastructure as code.\n\nWhile other companies are struggling with declining revenues and stagnant growth, DigitalOcean has managed to tap into the growing demand for scalable, cloud-based infrastructure. Their platform is making it easier than ever for developers to spin up servers, deploy apps, and scale their businesses quickly - all without breaking the bank.\n\nOf course, this success isn't just about luck or timing. It's a testament to DigitalOcean's relentless innovation, customer obsession, and commitment to delivering real value in an increasingly crowded market.\n\nAs I always say, \"The best companies are those that solve a specific problem so well that they become indispensable.\"\n\nDigitalOcean is no exception. They're not just another cloud provider - they're the go-to platform for developers who need flexibility, speed, and reliability at scale.\n\nTake a page from their playbook, folks! What can you do to focus on solving your customers' most pressing pain points? Share your", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Digital Ocean is up 12% this year on Wall Street — while most software stocks are down 24%\nSo what explains the massive gap?\n#1: DigitalOcean has become a “best-of-breed” platform for developers\nWith an unmatched track record of reliability and scalability, Digital Ocean has built trust with the developer community. It’s the go-to choice when you need to deploy quickly and scale fast.\n#2: They’re profitable (and growing)\nUnlike many other cloud companies, DigitalOcean is showing real profitability while still growing at a healthy clip\n(21% YoY). This is what investors want – growth AND profit!\n#3: The market believes they can get more efficient\nWall Street thinks Digital Ocean has plenty of room to improve operational efficiency. That’s not always true in software, but it seems to be the case here.\nThe lesson? In a world where most SaaS companies are struggling, sometimes being “good enough” isn’t enough. You need to be extraordinary – and that often means going all-in on developer tools.\nDigital Ocean is one of those rare cases where exceptional customer love + technical capability = huge stock outperformance in 2026\nWhat can your company learn from this success story?", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Digital Ocean is up 11% this year and has been growing like gangbusters as an alternative to AWS for a while now\nBut it still only does about $4B ARR, which really doesn’t change much in the grand scheme of things\nStill though, if you’re not at least thinking about your competition — then that’s on you\nI know some folks aren’t fans of Digital Ocean. But I’m a fan of competition. It forces everyone to innovate faster.\nA bit more here:\nAnd don't make excuses for why you're down 24% this year", "cleaned": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "balanced": "The companies that win are the ones where AI creates genuine demand for their product, not just margin expansion. For SaaS founders and operators: If you’re building infrastructure or developer tools, this is your moment. But don’t build what customers didn’t ask for — build what they can’t live without when AI arrives. And if you’re investing, remember: market sentiment swings wildly, but fundamentals eventually catch up. The question isn’t whether AI will transform software (it already has). It’s which players positioned themselves to capture that transformation. #DigitalOcean #SaaS #AI #CloudComputing #SoftwareDevelopment #InfrastructureAsCode", "r32": "The New Math: Infrastructure + AI = Growth When you look at what's actually moving, it's infrastructure companies that are building on AI: Snowflake (up 20%) - Data warehousing and analytics for AI models MongoDB (up 18%) - Database platform for AI applications Cloudflare (up 15%) - Edge computing for AI workloads These aren't traditional SaaS stocks. They're infrastructure plays with an AI twist. And they're crushing it because enterprises need somewhere to put all their new data and compute requirements. The Real Story Behind DigitalOcean's Surge So why is DigitalOcean up when everyone else is down? Because it has become the \"go-to\" platform for startups and developers to build and deploy AI-powered projects. As one investor noted: \"We've seen some real traction in demand from customers looking to develop apps powered by generative AI.\" For B2B founders : If your product isn't becoming more essential as AI becomes core to how businesses operate, you have a problem. But if you're helping teams ship faster, reduce costs, or do things previously impossible... you might just be DigitalOcean right now. The market is separating between companies that matter in the Age of AI and everything else. Make sure you're in the first group.", "repaired": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why. 🚀 $DOCN is up +11.7% YTD (as I write this). Not because they have AI products that are replacing humans. But because they get it right on three things every SaaS founder should be thinking about today. #1: Pricing Simplification, Not Feature Bloat — They Cut 75%+ Of Their Products and Plans Now customers can pick from just two plans ($10/month for 5GB or $20/month for 40GB), instead of navigating 27 different tiers with confusing usage limits. One price, one decision tree. This matters more than you think. Most vendors keep adding features because they’re chasing ARR growth, not customer happiness. Your customers would rather pay less for fewer complicated options than more for everything under the sun. At least if your product is good enough. DigitalOcean learned that the hard way when their 100+ SKUs were “confusing” to some users. So they cut them all down to core offerings, even though it meant losing revenue immediately. And guess what? Revenue grew anyway. Because happy customers buy more. #2: AI as Infrastructure, Not Displacement — 70%+ of New Customers Use AI Workloads Every single new customer at Digital Ocean now uses AI workloads. Every single one. That doesn’t mean they replaced workers. They used AI to build faster, iterate quicker, deploy sooner. Same people doing the same jobs. Just using compute"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 248, "lemkin-clone-v5": 195, "lemkin-clone-v6": 93, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 93, "r32": 202, "repaired": 237}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's a potential X post in the style of Jason Lemkin:\n\n **UPDATE FROM THE FUTURE**\n\nI just got my hands on some data from 2026, and I have to share it with you all: DigitalOcean is UP 12% this year... while the rest of software is DOWN 24%.\n\nNow, before anyone starts screaming \"Bubble! Bubble!\", let me tell you why:\n\n**DigitalOcean's secret sauce:** They've been quietly building out their Enterprise capabilities. And by quietly, I mean they're not even advertising it (yet). But their sales team and customer success folks are killing the game.\n\nThey're doing something that very few other cloud providers have managed: **selling to Enterprises without losing their small business charm**. Yes, you read that right - DigitalOcean", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Dear SaaStrers,\n\nSo what is going on with public software stocks today?\n\nDown -24%.\n\nThis has never happened before.\n\nWhile I don’t have all the answers, DigitalOcean isn't down -24% in 2026. It's actually UP +12%. And that’s a big difference.\n\nWhy? Well for me, it makes sense at least:\n\n1/2 of your revenue is from SMBs and startups. They are going to continue to grow.\n\n2/3+ of the world has never even heard of you before today. Now they know about DigitalOcean.\n\nNow maybe other software companies will see more growth in 2026. But it’s clear that if a company can win at the edges — or add new customers and revenue streams, as well as retain them", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So I was doing a deep dive on all my favorite public SaaS and Cloud companies last night. And I saw something that is just so different from the rest of software. DigitalOcean, which has been around since almost forever now — it’s up\n12% in 2026! This is an outlier.\n\nWhat we are seeing more than ever these days is a bifurcation of markets and stock prices:\n\nThe Winners: These are companies that have seen their revenues go up dramatically over the past few years. And not just by some small margin, but really dramatically higher year-over-year.\n\nDigitalOcean itself has gone from $0 in 2012 to $1.3 billion run-rate today! What a journey!\n\nThese winners have done so well that they are seeing massive growth in their stock", "cleaned": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "balanced": "DigitalOcean?\nIt’s Up 12% in 2026.\nWhile The Rest of Software Is Down 24%.\n\nHere’s Why.", "r32": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "repaired": "DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%.\n\nHere’s Why.\n\nThe B2B Crash is Real. But AI-Native Growth Remains Strong.\n\nEvery major SaaS metric tells a disturbing story:\n\nCloud infrastructure spending growing slower than expected at just 10%\nS&P 500 information technology sector down nearly 25% YTD (versus +11% for overall market)\nNasdaq composite off more than 30% from its January peak\nSoftware stocks getting hit particularly hard, with some down 50-60%\n\nBut there are exceptions. And one of them might be your next acquisition target — or strategic partner.\n\nDigitalOcean, the cloud platform company founded back in 2011, has been quietly executing a"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 121, "lemkin-clone-v5": 119, "lemkin-clone-v6": 129, "cleaned": 16, "balanced": 16, "r32": 16, "repaired": 110}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: DigitalOcean? It’s Up 12% in 2026. While The Rest of Software Is Down 24%. Here’s Why.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder and CEO of SaaStr:\n\n**[Slide: \"DigitalOcean: The Exception to the Rule\"]**\n\nGood morning everyone! Today I want to talk about DigitalOcean, a company that's been on an incredible tear over the past few years. And when I say tear, I mean it - up 12% in 2026? That's insane.\n\nBut what's even more interesting is that they're bucking every trend out there right now. The rest of software, by and large, has seen a decline of 24%. You read that correctly: down 24%.\n\n**[Slide: \"What's Going On with Software in General\"]**\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking: what's going on? Is the world just suddenly done with software? No. It's not that simple.\n\nFrom my perspective, it looks like a lot of companies are struggling to adapt to the new normal. They're trying to move from cloud-first to hybrid or even back to on-prem, and it's proving harder than expected. The competition is fierce, margins are thinning... and let's be honest: software just isn't as exciting anymore.\n\n**[Slide: \"Enter DigitalOcean\"]**\n\nBut then there's DigitalOcean - a company that has managed not only to buck this trend but actually thrive in the midst of it. So what's their secret? Why have they been able to outperform everyone else?\n\nIn my opinion, it all comes down to one word: **simplification**.\n\nDigitalOcean is a platform-as-a-service (PaaS) company that specializes in cloud infrastructure for developers and businesses alike. And here's the key thing about them: they're not just competing on price or feature set - although those things are important too.\n\nNo, DigitalOcean has taken a fundamentally different approach to software development. They've created an incredibly simple user experience, making it easy for anyone to spin up infrastructure in minutes, without worrying about all the complexities of cloud computing.\n\n**[Slide: \"The Power of Simplicity\"]**\n\nThis is not just some pie-in-the-sky philosophy - this is real business strategy at work. By focusing on simplicity, DigitalOcean has managed to:\n\n* Reduce customer friction (no more tedious setup or configuration)\n* Increase adoption and retention rates\n* Build a loyal community around their platform\n\nAnd let's be clear: none of these things happen by accident.\n\n**[Slide: \"The Future of Software\"]**\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking: this is all well and good for DigitalOcean, but what about the rest of us? How can we apply these lessons to our own businesses?\n\nIn my opinion, it's time to rethink how we're approaching software development. We need to focus on making things simpler - more accessible - more intuitive.\n\nWe need to be willing to break some rules and challenge conventional wisdom in order to create truly innovative experiences for our customers.\n\n**[Slide: \"Conclusion\"]**\n\nSo there you have it, folks. DigitalOcean's success is a wake-up call to all of us in the software industry. Let's take note - simplicity matters more than ever before.\n\nAnd if we can learn from their example and adapt our own businesses accordingly, I truly believe that we'll see a new era of growth and innovation emerge from this crisis.\n\nThanks for listening!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay let's get into this digital ocean up a huge 13 percent in two thousand and six right as we're sitting here in our little conference center the rest of software down 24 okay that is wild it is wild and i'll tell you why now look on the surface you might say well what about all these other cool companies atlassian hubspot data dog they've gone up a smidge not much but maybe just enough to kind of make this less dramatic right but nope digital ocean's been crushing it so let me try and tease out the numbers here for you okay first off we'll look at some public cloud metrics we use these from a great company called saster that crunches all of their data and gives us an annual review on the public cloud market now this is pretty interesting they're down almost 50 percent in two thousand and six but what's wild is if you go back to 2021 it was up a whole 40 percent so wow okay that that gap is just crazy digital ocean is actually bucking that trend right we look at it from a public company standpoint with an m market cap of about seven billion dollars they're up over 12 percent this year and what's interesting is if you go back to two thousand twenty one the rest of their revenue was growing in excess of 50 percent so yeah those are pretty wild numbers but what's driving digital ocean okay well i think we can all guess that it is infrastructure right that every sas company needs a home now not everybody needs a home on aws and azure they're massive for good reason the other two thousand billion dollars or whatever it", "lemkin-clone-v6": "so I just got back from our annual SaaStr AI London and we had over 1,000 folks there with a thousand more attending virtually so it was big and I wanted to do one last deep dive into some data that's interesting on the stock market at least and what happens when you have this sort of classic battle between traditional software and new age infrastructure now look most people don't know about digital Ocean but for those who are in tech they probably will be familiar with it it is an infrastructure as a service or IaaS which means they're basically like Amazon Web Services but more developer focused so if you want to build your own cloud today on AWS Azure Google Cloud or even something like Digital Ocean that's the space and digital ocean has been around for 10 years now and we've had some great guests from there including CEO Ben Upton here is a picture of him with me at SAS London last year but what I wanted to do was look at their stock price because they're interesting and they are up over 12 percent this year that's pretty impressive compared to the rest of software which is down about 24.", "cleaned": "We're doing live workshops, we're doing this great series called SAS Happy Hour with Harry Stebbings and Rory O'Driscoll from Scale Ventures. We'll do one every week at 10 A.M. Pacific time. They're fun. We have a lot of fun. Um you can sign up for it on our homepage if you want to join us. You don't need to be an investor or anything like that. Just just come and learn some stuff. But those are two things I'm excited about and uh hopefully you know they will help folks get better at B2B software faster because there's so much going on these days and um digital Ocean is an interesting case study when most public cloud stocks were down more than 20% last year um digital ocean was actually up 12% even with all its challenges it had a rough quarter but still up 12% while Cloudflare was down 45% Monday was down 44% Shopify was down 42% and HubSpot was down 31% why did digital Ocean buck the trend well first of all its growth slowed significantly as it got bigger right it grew 30% in 2019 then 25% in 2020 then 21% in 2021 and then 19% in 22. So it's gotten harder and harder for digital ocean each year not only does it get harder to add new customers which we see everywhere um but it also gets harder to grow existing accounts so it has been decelerating over four years now it went from 30% growth to almost flat growth today um but importantly digital ocean really didn't enter into this crazy multiple expansion madness that happened during lockdowns and craziness that lasted too long people paid far more multiples than ever before for Cloud companies especially", "balanced": "it's up 30 percent but I'll tell you what it is folks this is where we are in 2025 and 2026 with software and SAS if your growth rate has slowed below 20 or 25 percent there just isn't enough demand for shares to support that valuation there's no one left on the buy side they've all said okay I'm going to sell my Google stock at 300 billion dollars or maybe even lower than 200 billion because of what happened with Lenny today but it's not going to be enough so they're selling their Salesforce shares too low as well the price keeps coming down and everyone's saying hey I want out of this world right now and then when you look at digital ocean and cloudflare and others it's like boy times aren't perfect times aren't easy but boy these guys have figured out how to keep growing at epic rates at 50 plus percent annual growth rates and people will pay almost any multiple for those stocks so even though the multiples may not be insane for these leaders if you grow faster than anyone else in the industry you can still command very high prices and the last point which is super important if your growth rate slows past 20 25% you got to get profitable because the markets won't make you profitable anymore they used to say listen you had a great startup you could burn cash forever as long as your nrr was 130 and your NPS was good but that doesn't work anymore they don't care they don't care as much as they did before and if your growth is decelerating to less than 25% and you're not profitable you just need to become more efficient like digital ocean did like it became more efficient than it ever", "r32": "that you don't have to be in every category and digital ocean is not competing with Google cloud or AWS for large workloads it's focusing on developers and startups but they're still growing at 15 a year that's pretty darn good so we should celebrate those folks too", "repaired": "Digital Ocean is up 12 percent year to date it's actually been on an incredible run since late last year and for most folks in SAS and Cloud this has been a tough time we're down 25% in total market cap from our highs at the end of last year but digital ocean just keeps going up um not only is it up 12 overall but it's also now trading at almost eight times Revenue so that means if you buy shares today they are worth seven dollars per dollar of revenue for that company what does that mean well it means it's growing faster than 7% right because even if growth slows dramatically eventually your revenue will be equal to your price but it doesn't have to be forever okay so let's do the math here let's assume DigitalOcean grows like it did last quarter which was pretty good which was 29 percent or better um and let's say next year it stays at 20 something percent um I'm being conservative uh then it would still trade for more than five X Revenue with its current valuation right now so it could grow 15 16 17 18 some percent and the stock would go higher right so why can't others do that well there are reasons why other leaders don't quite benefit as much from this reflationary environment first one is a lot of them grew too fast during COVID everyone had an amazing streak during covet everything went up 50 100 200 percent um many didn't deserve it they were selling seats they weren't really adding value people bought software and cloud products to make work-from-home easier but ultimately that demand ended when things reopened after March 31st 2020 so many companies saw their growth slow and then they never got back to those crazy levels before kovat they're often below 30 now maybe 40 45 60. They may get back there by growing faster later this year but they haven't yet and so"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 529, "lemkin-clone-v5": 295, "lemkin-clone-v6": 207, "cleaned": 293, "balanced": 305, "r32": 48, "repaired": 336}}}}, {"id": "gold_029", "topic": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Argue that the category still matters but the old growth playbook has broken.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that SaaS remains a huge and important category, but the old easy-growth playbook no longer works the same way. Founders now need sharper product differentiation, better efficiency, and more disciplined execution. The lesson is not despair; it is adaptation.", "facts": ["The answer should reject the simplistic claim that SaaS is dead.", "It should argue that the historical playbook has changed materially.", "It should explain how founders need to adapt in product, GTM, or capital strategy."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**SaaS Isn't Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That's Gone.**\n\nI've been hearing it for a while now - whispers of doom and gloom from SaaS founders, investors, and pundits alike: \"The SaaS model is dead.\" Or at least, that's what it seems like.\n\nNewsflash: **SaaS isn't dead**.\n\nBut let me tell you something else. The way we used to win in B2B? That's gone.\n\nWe're not just talking about the good old days of cold email and spray-and-pray sales strategies. No, those tactics are now a relic of the past - at best, they'll get you nowhere; at worst, they'll hurt your brand and burn through cash.\n\nThe way we used to win in B2B was all about **spraying** our product into the market, relying on good old-fashioned sales muscle to push it over the finish line. It worked... for a while. But those days are behind us now.\n\nToday's buyers have changed - they're more informed, more connected, and more demanding than ever before. They're not just looking for a \"product\"; they're seeking solutions that meet their specific needs, at scale. And if your SaaS company can't deliver on that promise? You're out of the running.\n\nSo what's changed?\n\n**The Rise of Customer-Centricity**\n\nBuyers have more power than ever before. They can research, compare, and contrast products from anywhere in the world - all without leaving their desks (or even getting out of bed). And with the rise of customer-obsessed companies like NetSuite and Salesforce, buyers now expect a level of service that's previously unimaginable.\n\nIn this new reality, SaaS founders who want to succeed must rethink their approach. They need to put **customer experience** at the forefront - using data-driven insights to drive product development, sales strategies, and customer support.\n\nBut here's the thing: creating an exceptional B2B buying experience is no easy feat. It requires a fundamental shift in mindset - one that prioritizes relationships over transactions, and seeks to delight customers every step of the way.\n\n**The New Rules of SaaS Success**\n\nSo what does this new landscape mean for SaaS founders? Here are some key takeaways:\n\n1.  **Customer-centricity is table stakes**: If you're not putting your customer first, you'll be left behind.\n2.  **Data-driven decision-making is essential**: Use every tool at your disposal to understand your customers and drive product development.\n3.  **Sales strategies must evolve**: Gone are the days of cold email and spray-and-pray tactics - instead, focus on building relationships that drive real growth.\n\nSo while SaaS itself may not be dead (thank goodness!), the way we used to win in B2B certainly is. To succeed in this new landscape, founders need to adapt quickly - embracing a customer-centric approach that puts relationships and experience above all else.\n\nThe good news? For those who make this shift, there's never been a more exciting time to be a SaaS founder.\n\nSo what do you think, friends? Are you ready to rewrite the rules of B2B success in 2023?", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So I’ve been doing this SaaS thing for over 15 years now, and it has changed so much.\nIt’s still huge.  It is a multi-trillion dollar industry today, growing at epic rates, with no end in sight.\nBut … but the way we used to win? The way we used to be successful?\nThat’s gone.\nSo let me make this clear: I love AI and apps as much as anyone else.  And that will continue for a long time.  But there are two things you need to know if you’re trying to build something in B2B today, or even just scale it from 1-10M ARR:\n#1 — The “Old Way” of SaaS is Dead\nYou can’t win anymore by being cheaper.  It’s dead.\nIn fact, AI has made this worse than ever before.  You need to be better and faster in B2B today … with lower costs.  But that doesn’t mean you have to go cheap.  That just means going all-in on automation and AI.  And if your margins are too low — then maybe there is no room for any of this.\nYou can’t win anymore by being a feature list, or even a workflow tool.  It’s dead.\nIn fact, the more features you have … the less relevant you’ll be in B2B today.  Your competitors will just build them on top of AI and apps anyway.\nThe old way to win was cheap, with lots of features that worked well together for 1-10 people at a company.  Those days are gone.\n#2 — The New Way is … Different\nSo what’s the new way?\nIt’s not quite sure yet, but we know it involves AI and apps in some fashion.  And the winners will be:\nThe best integrators of everything that works in AI today.  That’s a lot harder than it sounds.\nThe fastest to market with real value — from day 1, even if you have limited features and an unpolished interface.\nThis is really hard.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "It's just so hard right now for most folks.\nThe market is down. Many of us are struggling with growth and even cash flow. And it feels like no matter what we do, there's a new SaaS app launching each week that does exactly the same thing as our product.\nSo should you quit? Maybe not today. But maybe next year.\nA few thoughts:\nThe \"easy\" days in B2B are over\n— Jason ✨Be Kind✨ Lemkin ⚫️💙 (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 9, 2026\nFirst off — SaaS isn’t dead per se. You can still buy a ton of SaaS apps today.\nBut … it’s not the same as it was even just 12 months ago. It hasn’t been easy for many B2B founders and execs since early 2025. The market has changed in so many ways. A few thoughts on how to move forward:\nSaaS is still a great space\n— I said this last year, but it’s worth saying again.\nBut the way you used to win? That’s gone. SaaS isn’t dead per se — and we are all still buying tons of software today. But so much has changed in just 12 months that if your playbook hasn’t evolved — then your results probably haven’t either.\nMore competition than ever\n— It seems like every week there is a new SaaStr Fund portfolio company, or some new AI startup launching that does exactly what you do!\nLess money to spend\n— If the macro environment doesn’t improve soon, it’s hard for folks at all levels of organizations to budget as much software. And less confidence.\nMore scrutiny and transparency expected\n— This is a trend we’ve been seeing in B2B now for 5+ years — but it has accelerated even further since last year.", "cleaned": "The companies that survive this transition won’t be the ones with the best AI features — they’ll be the ones who built genuine value for their customers, then leveraged AI to make it more valuable. And maybe most importantly: if you’re not building something people actually want to use, adding AI doesn’t save you. No matter what your sales team says. The old playbook is dead. Not because we have better technology now (we don’t). Because customer expectations changed. Now you need to build software so good, so essential, and so beloved that customers would pay just as much without any discounts at all. Even if you could get them for half price yesterday. If you can’t do that today … well. I guess time will tell. In fact, probably even already has. (note: an updated version of a classic SaaStr post)", "balanced": "But if you’re building something that solves a problem, not just adds functionality — and if your customers can see it working — they’ll pay for it regardless of what “the market” says. The AI companies that will win aren’t the ones with perfect positioning or slick marketing. They’re the ones solving problems so obviously better than anyone else that price becomes an afterthought. This is true every five years or so since I’ve been investing. It’s just … this time it feels different because we’re seeing all these other categories get disrupted at roughly the same time. Which makes everything feel harder. And also more exciting. Because when multiple categories are up for grabs simultaneously, the opportunities compound. Not every company from the last era can win. Some of them got lucky. Some of them built moats that are now crumbling fast. Some of them simply didn’t solve real enough problems. But here’s what I’m telling founders who reach out to me right now: We’re still in one of those moments where people are willing to bet on anything new. Where there are still lots of buyers looking for solutions. Where if you actually build something genuinely better — not incrementally better, but fundamentally better — there are budgets available to fund it. That window won’t stay open forever. But it’s definitely open right now. For Founders Building AI Apps (That Actually Work): If you have a product that works today, that solves a problem in any way, and you haven’t priced yourself out of the market yet… Go sell it. Even if your TAM looks small. Even if your competitors seem strong. Even if you don’t fit perfectly into some category that exists today.", "r32": "The New Rules for Building a Billion-Dollar Company Here’s what actually works now: Go vertical. Most big AI companies are building vertical apps, not horizontal tools. They’re solving specific problems in specific industries with domain-specific models trained on industry data. The best examples right now are Cursor (for developers), Lovable (for design and development), Replit (for general-purpose coding). Each one has $100M+ ARR already because they went deep into their core use case instead of trying to be everything at once. Build your own model. OpenAI proved that you don’t need to build an operating system or a platform layer to win. Just build the most powerful model and let everyone else fight over distribution. Harvey is doing this successfully — it doesn’t have its own model yet but uses Claude as its foundation while focusing relentlessly on the lawyer experience. Ship monthly. When you’re in an AI company, you should never feel like you’re shipping “next quarter.” Your competitors will kill you if they see you haven’t shipped anything new in months. This means continuous deployment, continuous learning, and continuous iteration based on real user feedback. The old pattern was annual releases plus quarterly feature updates. Now it’s weekly releases plus daily improvement cycles. Get to $10-20m faster than you planned. Because here’s the thing nobody tells you until you’re in it: Once you hit $50M ARR, every competitor who comes after you gets unlimited funding from investors. It’s existential. So you better get there fast enough that you can scale before they do. Don’t optimize for gross margins. Not always. I’m still seeing way too many founders obsessing over getting to 80%+ gross margins early when they could be growing revenue faster by spending more upfront on compute costs.", "repaired": "They’re not competing on features anymore—they’re competing for customer attention and time in an environment where there are infinite alternatives. The winners will be the ones who understand that AI isn’t just a feature—it’s the new architecture of software itself, and they’ll build accordingly. The losers will still have great products with high NPS scores, but their growth curves will tell the story. The question isn’t whether you can adapt. It’s whether you want to survive long enough to do it."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 499, "lemkin-clone-v5": 329, "lemkin-clone-v6": 294, "cleaned": 142, "balanced": 286, "r32": 291, "repaired": 81}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the post:\n\n**SAAS ISN'T DEAD, BUT THE OLD RULES ARE...**\n\nAs I look back on my 15+ years building and selling software companies, I'm struck by how much has changed - yet how much remains the same.\n\n**The SaaS model is alive and well.** In fact, it's been one of the most successful models in B2B sales for decades now. And why not? It provides a predictable revenue stream, low customer acquisition costs, and scalability that traditional software companies could only dream of.\n\nBut here's the thing: **the way you used to win in SaaS has changed**. The old rules no longer apply. You can't just build a decent product, put up some ads on LinkedIn, and wait for customers to magically appear anymore.\n\nToday, it takes so much more than that to succeed in B2B sales. With the rise of automation, AI-powered marketing tools, and changing customer buying behaviors... **the game has changed**.\n\nSo what does this mean? You can't just \"build it and they will come\". You need a modern, data-driven approach to growth. One that combines AI with human intuition, experimentation, and continuous learning.\n\nAt SaaStr, we've seen companies like HubSpot, Salesforce, and Dropbox succeed not because of their products alone... but because of the entire ecosystem around them. From go-to-market strategies to customer success programs - **the winners are those who understand how to create a complete experience** for their customers.\n\nIf you're still thinking about SaaS in the", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So we did an epic deep dive on our latest\nSaaStr AI podcast with Salesforce, Snowflake and more here:\nThe 2026 State of Enterprise AI\nIn short:\nEnterprise buyers are now buying a ton of AI tools from the same vendors as their core software stack.\nAnd in turn they’re willing to pay even higher prices for each tool if it comes from a trusted vendor – like Salesforce or Oracle. Not just that. But these vendors have 1,000s and 10,000s of integrations and workflows already built into their products — so you don’t need multiple point solutions anymore.\nThe winners are the ones that can go more upmarket. And make it easier for buyers to buy from them.\nBecause they know the buyer knows what works at scale in their ecosystem.\n\nSo is SaaS dead?\nNo, but if you were a B2B vendor or marketer before 2020… the way you used to win? That’s gone.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So what's new?\n\nSaaS isn't dead. The number of unicorns keeps growing. Atlassian has 6,000 employees.\n\nBut:\n\nThe way you used to win is gone. What that means for founders and execs right now:\n\n1️⃣ Nobody needs another vendor anymore\n2️⃣ AI agents are better at the core tasks than humans\n3️⃣ The market is flooded with similar products\n\nWhat it means for new entrants: 🚀 Be radically different. Not just “AI version of what we used to do.”\n\nBe 10x more valuable and easier to use.\n\nDo that, and you might have a chance in this saturated B2B landscape.\n\nThe best AI agents are better than the humans at the core tasks, so your job is to make it ridiculously easy for customers to deploy them. Not hard. Just click. Boom.\n\nAnd then:\n\nMake sure what they build with all these capabilities isn't just \"another version of our old software\".", "cleaned": "SaaS isn't dead. It's just... different. Different from even as recently as late 2021. So many things have changed, so fast, for vendors and buyers alike: - Buyers are looking at AI products that are better than existing ones (or they'd buy more seats) - Budget is frozen or cut, but new tools are still being deployed if they truly deliver ROI - Everyone is using ChatGPT and similar agents daily, changing what everyone expects from software - \"We're going multi-vendor\" has become the default strategy in many cases - The bar for switching is now lower than ever because AI lets you bring your data with you - Sales teams are under intense pressure to close deals faster — which means demos need to happen in one call, not over six weeks - Every vendor is an FDE vendor now. Everyone has to help deploy it. And so much more. If you haven't evolved here by the end of 2025, I worry you won't make it. Change or Die. And honestly … for most folks? Maybe it’s too late.", "balanced": "SaaS isn’t dead. Not even close. The overall public market for software is at $1.5 Trillion, up from $700 Billion just 6 years ago. And per Morgan Stanley, Cloud growth has accelerated over the past year to +27%, while on-prem has shrunk -7%. Yes, it gets harder every year. In many categories, it may be downright brutal out there today. And yet … we’re seeing new leaders like Cursor ($10 billion valuation), Lovable (growing 100%+), and Replit that are redefining how software is built — and changing everything. AI isn’t killing SaaS. It’s unleashing a wave of innovation that will create more SaaS companies than ever before. 🚀 But here’s what nobody wants to say out loud: the playbook from 2010-2022 doesn’t work anymore. For real. The days of building something “good enough” with 80/20 features, then spending three years grinding through sales cycles until you finally hit product-market fit, then riding the resulting wave all the way to IPO? Over. The era where your TAM was defined by seats or users? Dead. The model where enterprise buyers had limited alternatives and would sign multi-year contracts based purely on relationship? Fading fast. Even HubSpot itself acknowledged this reality last quarter. They’ve now crossed 300K customers but only grew ARR 15% YoY — because they can no longer rely solely on their core SMB customer base expanding. They need enterprise. They need seat expansion. They need the", "r32": "If you’re still running your company like it’s 2018, that playbook is extinct. The companies winning today are building for an AI-native world where customers expect instant value, rapid evolution, and true partnership — not annual contracts and quarterly features. The question isn’t whether this shift will continue. It already has. The question is whether your organization can adapt fast enough to survive it. Because if you don’t, someone else certainly will. 🤖💻🔥 What's your take on how the SaaS market has changed over time? Do you think we'll see more consolidation or innovation in the coming years? Let me know! #SaaS #AI #Software #Innovation #Business #Growth #Success #Leadership #Strategy", "repaired": "The “add more seats” model is dead for most SMBs. The AI-native startups are charging $500-1000/month per user and customers are paying it because they see value — not because they’re getting ripped off with an overpriced seat license. The old playbook: charge $10K ACV, bundle everything together, force annual contracts. It worked for years. Now? The best VCs I know won’t even meet with companies following that playbook anymore. They want to fund true AI innovation, not incremental feature bundles masquerading as transformation. The Great Vertical Consolidation Is Happening Right Now As we wrote at SaaStr: \"If you're selling vertical software and haven't built any AI into your product by now, you're going to lose deals.\" Every customer wants one vendor. Not five point solutions. One platform where all their agents live. One place they manage their entire sales workflow. That consolidation play is happening this year, not next decade. And if you're not positioning yourself as THE agent layer, someone else will position themselves against you. The Old Moat Is Crumbling Your PLG moats? They're still there for now. But they're eroding fast. When every company has access to the same AI infrastructure and can build basic chat-to-action flows overnight, what makes your free trial so special again? What's preventing a competitor from cloning your viral motion? The answer used to be: nothing can clone our PLG motion. Today? Everything can clone it except maybe the very top brands. The New Playbook For 2026"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 244, "lemkin-clone-v5": 156, "lemkin-clone-v6": 151, "cleaned": 180, "balanced": 236, "r32": 110, "repaired": 246}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**X Post**\n\nHey fellow founders!\n\nI've been seeing a lot of hand-wringing and doomsday prophecies lately about the state of SaaS. \"It's all saturated\", \"the market is flooded with me-too products\", etc.\n\nBut let me tell you, I've been around this block a few times. And I'm here to say: **SaaS isn't dead**.\n\nThe problem is, the way we used to win in B2B? That's gone. The old playbook of just throwing more money at customer acquisition and waiting for the customers to roll in? Not working like it used to.\n\nI've seen too many companies follow that approach, only to end up with a tiny fraction of the users they thought they'd get. And instead of scaling", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I just saw a recent survey from Gartner that probably most of you haven’t read:\nGartner is saying AI will reduce enterprise software spend by $1 trillion over the next three years.\nThat’s roughly 30% of all corporate IT spending, folks. That’s massive.\n\nNow I’m not sure about this number — but I do know one thing:\n\nThe way we used to win in B2B SaaS? It doesn’t work anymore. Not even close.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "X Post:\nSaaS isn't dead per se\nBut it's fundamentally changed over the past 10 years\nWhat used to work?\n1v1 sales + low ACV + SMB focused\nThe classic playbook for building a $20M ARR company in SaaS worked from '11-‘16. It still does today, but only if you’re at $2B+ now and have all the leverage of scale.\n\nIt’s not dead. But it’s fundamentally changed over the past 10 years\nWhat used to work?\n1v1 sales + low ACV + SMB focused\nThe classic playbook for building a $20M ARR company in SaaS worked from '11-‘16. It still does today, but only if you’re at $2B+ now and have", "cleaned": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the way you used to win in B2B? It's over. I’ve been doing this for a while now, and I think we’re at one of those moments where everyone needs to take a step back and ask themselves some hard questions. Because here’s what I can tell you without any hesitation whatsoever: The playbook from 2015-2021 is dead. And it’s not coming back. Not even close. Let me be specific about that. SaaS isn’t dead. We’re spending more on software than ever before. We have more vendors than ever before. The market cap of public SaaS companies is $8 trillion+. There are 250+ unicorns just in AI alone. So if nothing else, there’s no question there’s", "balanced": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone. The old playbook is over. Here’s what’s replacing it.", "r32": "SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone. The playbook that worked for a decade — build, sell, scale — is dead. Here’s what replaced it. The AI Era Is Killing the Old Playbook I’ve been investing in software since 2008. I was at Adobe when they acquired Omniture for $1.7 billion. I saw firsthand how category creation works, and we built our own categories together with companies like Yammer (sold to Microsoft) and EchoSign (sold to Adobe). We thought we knew all the answers. Then came ChatGPT. Now everything has changed again. And here’s the thing most founders don’t want to hear: the way you used to win in B2B isn’t working", "repaired": "SaaS Isn’t Dead.\n\nBut the Way You Used to Win in B2B?\n\nThat’s Gone."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 118, "lemkin-clone-v5": 73, "lemkin-clone-v6": 111, "cleaned": 120, "balanced": 23, "r32": 119, "repaired": 14}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: SaaS Isn’t Dead. But the Way You Used to Win in B2B? That’s Gone.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**[The stage is set, and Jason Lemkin takes his place at the podium. He surveys the crowd before beginning.]**\n\nLadies and gentlemen, fellow founders, investors, and industry insiders, I'm here today to talk about a topic that's been on my mind for some time now: the state of SaaS in 2023.\n\nAs many of you know, I've spent decades building, investing in, and advising software-as-a-service companies. I've seen it all – from the early days of Salesforce and Yammer to the current crop of unicorns and IPOs. And let me tell you, there's a lot of misinformation out there about what works today.\n\nSo here's my take: SaaS isn't dead. In fact, it's more alive than ever. But that doesn't mean everything we did in the past will still work for us today. The playing field has changed dramatically over the years, and if you're not adapting to these changes, your business is going to struggle.\n\n**[pauses for emphasis]**\n\nYou see, back in the day – I'm talking 2005-2010 – winning at SaaS was relatively straightforward. You built a great product, got some traction with early customers, used that momentum to build word-of-mouth marketing and viral growth... and then you went on to scale.\n\nThat's not how it works anymore. Today, the market is more crowded than ever before. With thousands of new software companies emerging every year, standing out from the pack requires a lot more effort than just building a decent product.\n\nSo what changed? Well, several things, if I may say so:\n\nFirstly, **the cost of entry has plummeted**. Gone are the days when you needed an army of developers and marketers to get your software off the ground. Today, with platforms like AWS and Stripe, you can build a SaaS company on a shoestring budget.\n\nSecondly, **customers have become much more sophisticated**. They know what they want from their technology providers – and it's not just about getting the job done anymore. It's about seamless user experiences, data-driven insights, and integrations with other tools in their ecosystem.\n\nLastly, **the buying process has shifted dramatically**. In the past, sales were all about building relationships with customers over time. Today, we're talking instant gratification – buyers expect to be able to sign up for a free trial or demo within minutes of discovering your product online.\n\nSo what does this mean for you as SaaS founders? It means that **your marketing strategy needs to change**. Gone are the days when email campaigns and cold calls were enough to drive growth. Today, it's all about creating an omnichannel experience – from social media ads to search engine optimization (SEO) and content marketing.\n\nIt also means that **you need to be much more agile** than ever before. With so many competitors vying for attention in the market, you can't afford to get stuck in a product development cycle of 6-12 months. You need to iterate faster – whether it's through continuous delivery or even adopting no-code solutions like Bubble.\n\nAnd finally, **your go-to-market strategy needs to be data-driven**. Forget about relying on gut instinct and anecdotal evidence from your sales team. Use customer success metrics, retention rates, and other key performance indicators (KPIs) to inform every decision you make – from product development to marketing spend allocation.\n\nSo in conclusion: SaaS isn't dead. But the way we used to win at B2B? That's gone. The market has changed; it's time for us to adapt. By embracing these new realities, by being more agile and customer-focused than ever before... I firmly believe that there's never been a", "lemkin-clone-v5": "okay i'm actually going to be talking on this topic at saster annual so come see me there but it's just such an important topic and what is happening in b2b right now and we'll go over all of the data here from gartner from forbes from zensu from cloudflare and others but let me tell you a few things first before i dig into this data which will be pretty interesting but there are two things that have changed forever in b2b just in the last 12 to 18 months or so one is that we've all gotten much better at efficiency if you look on the left side of the screen it's the latest zen survey and what they've done is sort of updated their classic saster annual chart which goes way back to 2010 when the first saster annual was held in san francisco but now zen has updated that data for 2024 we're at $100 million in error this year as a median and if you look on the left side it's pretty interesting because what they found is that in fact most of us are growing more efficiently than ever before right so there's no excuse really to grow slower okay two things have changed forever in b2b one is that we've all gotten better at efficiency but here's the second thing and i think this is probably the biggest change since 2010 if not even bigger it's that we've all learned how to be more efficient but most of us are going more slowly and as you can see on the right side forbes in fact just came out with a pretty big data set from their cloud 100 which they do every year", "lemkin-clone-v6": "So, so many folks are freaking out today about AI and more.  And I get it.\nBut you gotta take a step back here\nWe’ve been doing this for 20 years now.  We’re not going to change the rules of SaaS on a whim.  Maybe some parts will go away (or at least evolve) — but overall, we need 100s and 1,000s of new SaaS companies every year just to keep up with demand\nWhat’s really changed is how you win in B2B today.\nLet me break it down for you here.  How did we used to win?  And why this doesn’t work anymore:\n#1 — Being “good” isn’t good enough any more\nWe’re all using AI now and the tools are getting better every day, even if not quite as fast as they seem.  In fact, it’s still a bit of a mess out there today in terms of quality — but at least you can see some progress.\nA few months ago, I used to use 2 SaaS apps together\nNow AI does it for me\nAnd the tool is better than either of them\n— Jason ✨👾SaaStr 2025 is back!!✨ Lemkin (@jasonlk)\nFebruary 21, 2026\nBut here’s what’s changed: “good” doesn’t get you far anymore.  It never really did in SaaS, but now it barely gets you a meeting with the prospect.\n#2 — Being cheap isn’t good enough any more\nWe used to win by being cheaper and cheaper than our competitors.  This was the playbook for years.  But that’s no longer working today.  Because AI has changed everything.\nI recently tried to get an old vendor I’ve known forever for 15+ years to switch from a competitor to their tool.  Their “new” product is way better, and they wanted me to pay a premium for it.\nMy answer?  You want $200k/yr more than the competition?\nNo thanks.", "cleaned": "And we're not doing enough of it, and I think this is why you see so many people struggling today. They're trying to get back to 2019 or 2021 pricing with no innovation, just relying on their brand, which may be true for some folks. But if they don't deliver more value than before March 15th, 20, whatever, then your customers will churn from you anyway because there's nothing in it for them. So, my point is two things. First, be honest as founders. Be honest. Your existing product isn't good enough anymore. It was good enough before all this stuff happened, but it ain't now. And secondly, go do something great. Go make your product better. Go build that feature that you always wanted to build but never had time. Go add another module, another edition, an AI agent. We talked about these at the start. There are ways to take advantage of what's changed in our world. Take advantage of it. Don't hide from it. Don't pretend like everything's going to magically bounce off the bottom. It won't. The economy has slowed down. Buyers have been through change. And maybe most importantly, competition has accelerated. These new vendors, Lovable, Replet, Bolt New, Cursor, Claude Code. They were born during lockdowns. They grew up on Zoom. They've already built features that traditional companies can only dream of building. They have a thousand times the engineers per employee of pre-2020. Thousands of times. At least hundreds. They're leaner and meaner. And they're coming after your budget. Not just incremental budgets, but yours.", "balanced": "I wrote something on LinkedIn that generated more comments than anything I've written and I wanted to walk through it because we have so many questions from Founders today that are struggling there's no question about that but there is one question that we hear again and again which is \"Jason, things aren't great today. I'm not sure what's going on with AI. We're seeing cutbacks everywhere.\" What do you think? Should we be optimistic or pessimistic about SAS these days? And my answer is yes. There are parts of SAS where times are tougher. There are parts of sas where things are harder. It may even be true for your company, but overall as an industry, I am much more bullish now than ever before. So let me explain why. This is a summary of our data here at saster.com. Look at this chart over the last three years, how much capital was raised by SAS companies per month and look at this. Three years ago when everything seemed wonderful we were raising almost $9 billion a month into startups. Fast forward to today, we're still doing billions every single month. Almost four billion dollars in March alone. The best of times and the worst of times, if you can raise money, they're all the same. If you can raise money, there's plenty of money out there. Now we know the headlines say otherwise, right? We see folks struggling. We hear stories. Layoffs are real. Budgets are getting tighter. Yes, that's happening. But the reason for that is very different than what happened during Covid. During Covid, everybody panicked. They didn't really want to spend any money on software.", "r32": "you're you're not going to be able to beat them right and then if I'm if I'm building something with a lot of engineering resources that's super AI powered like someone will build an agi agent that can do what your product does for a fraction of the cost because it'll be built on top of Claude or gamma or whatever model so there are more reasons than ever not to buy software and they all have one thing in common which is we've been lied to by vendors for years about their costs and capabilities right everyone has been lied to every vendor overpromises and under delivers right yes Salesforce claims that they don't charge per seat but it doesn't matter how many seats you actually need they just force you into these multi-year contracts where at least you pay twice as much as you should anyway that's always happened right HubSpot charges you for contacts whether you use them or not right all this stuff has always been true so why now is different is only that no one believes anyone anymore right and that means no one wants to spend money without seeing the value immediately okay number five point six point seven eight nine ten and let me give you three quick ones here but there's four points plus two bonus ones and those are maybe the most important things that I see founders struggle with today first one and this was my first point earlier but it's worth reiterating because people still don't get it out there is your customers happy yeah are your customers happy ask them tell them ask them go visit them do dinners do Zooms listen to the feedback I'm stunned at how many Founders and CEOs I meet with who literally haven't visited any of their customers", "repaired": "I've been investing in B2B and cloud for almost two decades, running startups myself from Echo sign to adobe sign to other investments like Algolia, Sales Loft, Gorgias, Superhuman, and many more we're at saster.com which has over a million folks that read our content every month I'm on Twitter uh pretty much daily so you know me but there's one thing everybody agrees with today and that is that SAS is dead it doesn't really matter what metrics you look at because we all have different ones right some people say well growth is down so things are worse than ever others will say well public companies' revenue multiples are low but they're not low if you're private so there's different ways to measure it and everyone says SAS is under stress and maybe even even dying some of them say the whole tech industry is dying we don't know what's going to happen next year or next quarter and yet as Founders and CEOs the best founders I work with and invest in the very top decile of startups their businesses have never grown faster than they do now and here's the data I was talking to Harry Stebbings on his podcast just before this episode came out he pointed out the data on Carta which shows seed valuations up across the board in venture overall deals are actually growing albeit modestly and it's only gotten better since then but go into any Cloud conference you'll hear how things are tougher than ever when I invested in my first company back in the day in 2006 it took us six months to raise our series A after we had raised a tiny seed round now most seed rounds are done within hours no one can find enough time to meet with them they want to get deals done quickly and I think a lot of that is change for good um the world"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 596, "lemkin-clone-v5": 290, "lemkin-clone-v6": 312, "cleaned": 262, "balanced": 280, "r32": 307, "repaired": 325}}}}, {"id": "gold_030", "topic": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "audience": "B2B SaaS founders and operators", "goal": "Use a standout public-company quarter to discuss what it does and does not imply about the market.", "expected": "A strong answer should say that Figma's quarter is a reminder that elite SaaS companies can still post extraordinary numbers, especially when product expansion and AI tailwinds align. But founders should not confuse one exceptional company with a full market reset. The lesson is to separate the existence of winners from the claim that the whole sector has returned to easy mode.", "facts": ["The answer should treat Figma's quarter as impressive evidence, not proof that every SaaS company is healthy.", "It should explain what makes the result notable at scale.", "It should caution founders against overgeneralizing from one elite outlier."], "mediums": {"blog": {"prompt": "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**Figma's Blazing Growth Proves SaaS is Still King**\n\nI just got word that Figma, the design collaboration platform, has hit an astonishing $304 million in revenue for Q4 of last year. That's a 40% growth rate from the same period in 2020.\n\nLet me tell you - this is not just any old quarter-over-quarter (QoQ) growth story. This is a full-on SaaS explosion. And it proves, once and for all, that the software-as-a-service model is still the king of B2B tech investing.\n\nNow, I know some folks might say, \"Wait a minute - hasn't Figma been around since 2012?\" Yes, yes they have. But here's the thing: in recent years, we've seen a major shift towards cloud-based collaboration tools, and Figma has been at the forefront of that trend.\n\n**The SaaS Boom is Back**\n\nIn the midst of last year's pandemic-induced economic chaos, many pundits declared the demise of the SaaS model. They said it was too expensive to implement, maintain, and upgrade - not to mention the recurring revenue streams were a risk for companies looking to cut costs.\n\nBut as we all know now, those naysayers were wrong. The SaaS market has bounced back with renewed vigor, driven by the increasing need for remote collaboration tools that can scale easily and adapt quickly to changing user needs.\n\nFigma's Q4 results are just one example of this broader trend. Other companies like Slack (still growing at 30% YoY), Zoominfo (up 50%), and even newer entrants in the space, like Notion (growing at an astonishing 100%) are all showing similar growth rates.\n\n**Why Figma's Success Matters**\n\nSo why does Figma's success matter? Well, for one thing, it proves that there is still enormous room for innovation within the SaaS market. With more companies moving to cloud-based solutions and adopting digital-first strategies, we're seeing an explosion of new entrants in areas like design collaboration, data analytics, and AI-powered productivity tools.\n\nFigma's growth also highlights the importance of user experience (UX) and customer success (CS) for SaaS businesses. By providing a seamless, intuitive platform that integrates with existing workflows, Figma has managed to build an enormous following among designers, product managers, and other stakeholders who rely on collaboration tools in their daily work.\n\n**Takeaways**\n\nSo what can we learn from Figma's blistering growth? Here are my takeaways:\n\n1. **SaaS is still the best way to go**: Despite all the naysayers last year, SaaS has proven itself once again as a reliable and scalable business model for B2B tech companies.\n2. **Innovation is key**: With more companies moving online, there's never been a better time for new entrants in areas like design collaboration, data analytics, and AI-powered productivity tools.\n3. **Focus on UX/CS**: To succeed in the SaaS market today, you need to prioritize user experience (UX) and customer success (CS). Figma is an excellent example of this.\n\nThat's it from me for now - I hope you found this update as exciting as I did! What do you think about Figma's growth? Share your thoughts with us in the comments below.", "lemkin-clone-v5": "So much has changed since March of this year.\nThe market’s gone from\n“SaaS is dead”\nto “SaaS is back” to an extent I never would have predicted.\nBut it’s real, and we’re seeing it all over the place.\nA $304m quarter for Figma.  That’s almost as much as Adobe makes in a whole year of Creative Cloud alone.  And more than that, they are growing at least 40% here!\nAn IPO from Databricks worth $42 billion\nNew SaaS unicorns like the latest ones from Google and Snowflake.\nThe AI leaders aren’t just burning through their money — they’re investing in SaaS to fuel even further growth.  OpenAI is now buying up other companies, including a B2B company it’s acquiring for nearly $1b!  And Anthropic has hired its first Chief Customer Officer and CRO.\nSo the market has been brutal on SaaS leaders.  In many cases, their public valuations are down 50%, 60% or more since March of this year.  But in a stunning turnaround, the best have bounced back faster than any one could imagine.  And that’s what we’re seeing with Figma.\nSo it’s not exactly easy being SaaS right now.  It took a lot to survive the carnage from Q1’24 through March of this year.  But if you were really good … and have AI on your side … then the market is back, baby!\nRelated Posts\nFigma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.\nSaaStr Annual 2024 will be BACK IRL from September 10-12!", "lemkin-clone-v6": "I was on an investor call recently where I had to update some numbers for our latest\nSaaStr Fund investment.\nThe founder asked me if things were “back to normal” now that we are back in a growth phase.\nIn one sense, yes.\nBut not as simple as just getting back to normal. The last 18 months have fundamentally changed everything. We’ve all been forced into the Cloud and SaaS. For better or worse. And it’s working for many of us now. Not everyone is growing at Figma’s incredible rate — but we are seeing a lot more growth in B2B and Cloud than just 12-15 months ago.\nAnd one thing I do know from doing this for almost two decades: when things get back to normal, they often don’t go back exactly as it was before. We’ve all been forced into the Cloud and SaaS over the last few years. The “back” is a bit different than what we had pre-Covid.\nNow Figma has just hit $304m in a single quarter — growing an incredible 40% year-over-year at this scale! And it’s not alone. We’re seeing growth of similar magnitude from Box (39%), Atlassian (38%), and Databricks (50%). While these numbers are eye-popping, they also mask the fact that many companies have struggled to maintain even modest growth over the past 18 months.\nBut Figma is different — it’s not just a design tool. It’s a platform for teams. And what we’ve learned from Figma and others in this space is that when you’re building something truly collaborative, you can defy expectations of traditional SaaS metrics.\nThe real question isn’t whether the market has turned back to growth (it clearly has) — but how sustainable it will be\nThere’s still a lot of uncertainty.", "cleaned": "So if you’re running a B2B company and your growth has slowed, your investors are breathing down your neck, and the market seems like it’s against you… here’s what I’d tell you to do right now: Take a hard look at Figma’s latest quarter. Not because they invented design software (they didn’t). But because that single number – $304 million quarterly revenue growing 41% year-over-year – tells us exactly where we stand in the current SaaS cycle. Let me break it down for founders who need this perspective. Why This Matters More Than You Think The AI Narrative Is Real Here’s what most people get wrong about Figma’s recent surge: It’s not just about being “AI-first.” Every major enterprise software company on Earth has added an “AI” tab. Microsoft? They’ve got Copilot everywhere. Salesforce added AgentForce. Adobe launched Firefly. Everyone’s chasing the same trend. What actually matters with Figma is the numbers behind the narrative. They went from ~$220M Q1 ARR to $304M Q2 ARR. That’s not incremental improvement over two quarters. That’s roughly 38% YoY growth in one step up. If you were skeptical about AI adoption in design workflows before June 30th, 2025, you should be less skeptical today. The Market Was Wrong About Figma Here’s something important: the market was absolutely, positively wrong about Figma heading into this earnings call. Shares had fallen nearly 50% since hitting their peak of around $91 in February 2026. Even after a strong first-quarter showing, sentiment remained cautious. Then came the second quarter results. Figma stock popped more than 25% overnight, touching new all-time highs above $90 per share.", "balanced": "The AI integration story is still unfolding, but here’s what we know so far: Design AI features launched in June 2025 have hit millions of users in just months Customer response to “Figma Make” has been strong, with users creating 100 million designs per week The company claims designers are using AI-powered design tools for tasks that used to take hours now taking minutes But here’s where it gets interesting: the AI feature isn’t driving usage growth directly. It’s accelerating existing trends. When you combine this acceleration with the platform effects and network effects already building at scale… the TAM starts looking very large indeed. What This Means For B2B Leaders If You’re Pre-$50M ARR : Take note of how Figma accelerated their trajectory by expanding from one core product to multiple platforms (Design + Framer). We saw similar patterns at Adobe ($200B+ market cap), where Photoshop alone wasn’t enough—they needed Creative Cloud as an ecosystem. The lesson? Don’t build single-product companies if your category can support multi-product platforms. Build for expansion from day one. If You’re Mid-Market / Established ($50-500M ARR) : Observe how Figma maintained profitability while investing heavily in new products and AI capabilities. Their Rule of 80 guidance tells us they’re prioritizing sustainable growth over pure revenue metrics—this should be your playbook too. The companies winning aren’t the ones growing fastest; they’re the ones balancing growth with unit economics discipline. If You’re Enterprise ($1B+) : Study how Figma leveraged customer success to expand into new markets and product lines.", "r32": "The AI-native approach that started with Make, continues with Pro, and now extends to Teams shows they’re not just building tools — they’re architecting an end-to-end collaborative workflow where intelligence follows design thinking. The B2B Reacceleration Story: What It Means for Every Founder If you’re running a B2B company right now, here’s what Figma tells us: AI isn’t cannibalizing your product — it’s expanding your TAM by making your customers more productive. When users become power users because AI handles routine tasks, they need better collaboration tools, better workflows, and better outcomes. This creates two paths to reacceleartion : Path One: Own the Workflow Expansion – Build horizontal/vertical solutions like Figma did (Design + Make + Teams) Path Two: Become Essential in AI-Driven Workflows – Position yourself as critical infrastructure when AI agents are deployed Focus on use cases where AI increases demand for human expertise rather than replaces it. At its core, this is why we saw massive acceleration across so many SaaS leaders. They’ve figured out how to make their products even more essential in the age of AI. The market isn’t recovering from COVID-era headwinds anymore. The market is accelerating toward new opportunities created by generative AI. For founders who haven’t yet seen these dynamics play out in their own business metrics, the message couldn’t be clearer: This isn’t temporary growth. This is structural change happening right now. Get ahead of it or get left behind.", "repaired": "The New TAM Math:\n$300B+ total design software market (up from $170B three years ago)\nFigma’s AI features are now driving 5-7x more revenue per user than traditional tools\nTheir enterprise business has grown by 30% despite declining customer count — because existing customers pay significantly more with AI features\nThey’ve gone from “design tool” to “creative productivity platform”\nWhat This Means for B2B Founders\nIf You’re Building Design Tools:\nYou need an AI strategy that goes beyond just auto-formatting. Figma’s success shows that integrated, intelligent workflows create massive value.\nYour growth trajectory matters more than your current ARR. Figma went from zero to $100M ARR in four years while maintaining profitability throughout. The path to IPO isn’t about being big fastest—it’s about building sustainable, efficient growth.\nAI features alone aren’t enough. They need to be deeply embedded into core workflows and fundamentally change how work gets done. The companies winning this transition are those whose AI creates new capabilities rather than just automating old ones.\nEnterprise adoption isn’t optional anymore. If you can serve both individual creatives and large enterprises simultaneously, do it."}, "words": {"llama3.1": 509, "lemkin-clone-v5": 257, "lemkin-clone-v6": 295, "cleaned": 268, "balanced": 253, "r32": 239, "repaired": 184}}, "linkedin": {"prompt": "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "**FOLKS, IT'S OFFICIAL**\n\nJust when I thought we'd reached peak \"SaaS bubble\" hype... along comes @figma and proves me (and the naysayers) WRONG!\n\n$304M in revenue in a single quarter? Growing 40%?! That's NOT just growth, that's EXPLOSION. And it's not just Figma - this is a massive win for SaaS as an entire category.\n\nI keep saying it: **SaaS is back** (not that I ever thought it was gone to begin with).\n\nThe numbers are undeniable:\n\n* Record-breaking ARR growth\n* Unprecedented customer acquisition costs coming down\n* Increasing user adoption across all industries\n\nAnd let's be real... this isn't just about Figma. It's a bellwether for the entire SaaS market.\n\nSo, what does it mean?\n\n**For founders**: If you're building B2B software, now is the time to pour gasoline on your growth engine.\n**For investors**: Write checks - and hold onto them tight!\n**For users**: Get ready for more innovative solutions that'll make your life easier (and more productive).\n\nThe SaaS machine is firing back up. Buckle up!\n\n#SaaStr #Figma #GrowthHacking #StartupLife", "lemkin-clone-v5": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter\nGrowing 40%\nAnd Just Like That — SaaS is Back.\nThat’s what it took to prove the model. Figma: The Ultimate Public SaaS Company:\n$4B+ valuation at IPO (2026)\nGrowing 40% year-over-year (Q3 2025) from $244M (previous quarter)\nHit record Q3 revenue of $304M\nBut it wasn’t always this way.\nFigma was on the ropes. They were barely growing in early 2022, and market leaders Adobe had them right where they wanted them: under their umbrella waiting to be gobbled up at a cheap price.\n\nBut then something changed:\nThey hired Mark Parsons as CEO (January 2023)\nThey re-accelerated growth\nThey went public with the best SaaS IPO in years\nAnd now? They’re the poster child of what can happen when you get it right. Figma’s story teaches us that even on death’s doorstep, if you have a great team and keep pushing forward…you can come back from anything.\n\nThe lesson for founders: If your market is growing but you aren’t seeing that growth in revenue, it might be time to take a hard look at how you are working with the other players.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Figma just hit $304,000,000 ARR in Q3’23\nGrowing at 41% YoY\nThat’s the fastest growth it has seen since its IPO last year.\nSaaStr #1 on Twitter and YouTube:\nHow Figma Just Hit Its Fastest Growth Ever. At Age 12! | SaaStr (@saastr)\n#2 on LinkedIn: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That —", "cleaned": "Figma just reported Q1 FY25 earnings, and they're not just growing again — they're accelerating at scale. Here are all the numbers that matter if you're building or investing in B2B right now. The Numbers That Actually Matter 🔥 Revenue Growth: $304M quarterly revenue (+41% YoY) - That's more than Salesforce, Adobe, or ServiceNow does annually Full year guidance: $1.12-1.13B (42% growth) 🚀 Enterprise Traction: $100K+ customers up to ~5,400 (from ~4,000 last quarter) 100+ $1M+ ARR customers, doubling since March 2024 Average customer size grew from $27k → $35k over past two quarters 💰 Profitability Metrics: Non-GAAP operating margin improved to +6%, up from flat (-0%) in prior quarter Adjusted free cash flow reached -$7.9M (vs -$23.8M loss in Q1 '24) Net income turned positive for first time ($17.5M vs -$20.7M loss) 🏢 Customer Concentration: Top 10 enterprise customers represent <10% of total revenue (stable trend) Only one Fortune Global 500 company left Figma after AI concerns emerged 📈 Market Positioning: Now larger than both Salesforce and ServiceNow on public market cap valuation Competing head-to-head with Adobe Creative Cloud for design workflows The Stock Performance Picture: Figma stock surged +15.6% following earnings announcement Current trading around $56/share (well", "balanced": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back. The Numbers Are Staggering: → $304,000,000 ARR (Q2 FY25) → 41% YoY growth rate → +1 million new users since IPO (now at 19+ million total) → 12% increase in enterprise customers with annual plans → Rule of 40 score of 83% (growth + profitability combined) But here’s what makes it really interesting: They’re not just growing anymore—they’re accelerating. Q2 revenue grew 36%, while their user base expanded 14% annually. That’s efficiency at scale. What This Means for Every B2B Founder Who’s Been Waiting If you’ve been watching from the sidelines thinking “SaaS is dead,” Figma is rewriting that narrative. At $300M+ run-rate, they’re proving that subscription-based software can grow efficiently and profitably at massive scales—especially when your product has genuine network effects and switching costs. Three Things We Can All Learn From Their Playbook", "r32": "Figma’s AI story is the one every B2B company should be paying attention to right now: Their new AI features (like “Recolor” that automatically suggests color palettes) are driving adoption and usage growth across all user segments. Not just power users or early adopters, but everyday designers getting real value from AI-powered tools. This isn’t vaporware. It’s production-ready AI that makes actual work easier, faster, and better. The Bottom Line We’re seeing something rare in tech today: a category-defining winner accelerating while everyone else fights for survival. Why? Because they never stopped building. They never stopped innovating. And most importantly, they never lost sight of what actually matters in software: making complex workflows simpler, not just adding more complexity with AI wrappers. If you’re running a B2B company in 2025-2026, this is your wake-up call. The companies winning aren’t the ones who added “AI” to their product deck. They’re the ones who fundamentally transformed how people get work done with AI at the core — not as a feature, but as an architecture shift.", "repaired": "Figma just reported its Q2 earnings, and it’s the quarter that proves everything we’ve been saying for years. Revenue hit $304 million—up from $217 million last year. But here's where it gets really interesting: the company has now crossed $1 billion in annual recurring revenue, growing at an incredible rate. And perhaps most importantly, they've raised their full-year guidance to $1.13-1.15 billion, up from previous expectations of $1.06-1.09 billion. The numbers are stunning. At ~$30B valuation, this makes them one of the top performing public SaaS companies today. For founders building B2B products, there's a lot to learn here. 🚀 The Platform Expansion Playbook 1️⃣ AI integration isn’t just a feature—it’s becoming table stakes. Customers expect it, competitors offer it, and users demand it. The winners will be those who integrate effectively, not just those who add AI features superficially. 2️⃣ Multi-product bundles are becoming standard. We’re seeing more companies succeed by bundling complementary tools rather than selling standalone solutions. This creates higher switching costs and stronger customer retention. 3️⃣ Enterprise customers still want to see results quickly. Yes, Figma grew 35%+ annually for years before launching AI features. But when you do launch AI features? They have to work fast. Users won’t wait months for meaningful improvements. 4️⃣ Design-to-code functionality is becoming essential. The line between design tools and"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 174, "lemkin-clone-v5": 192, "lemkin-clone-v6": 61, "cleaned": 199, "balanced": 153, "r32": 174, "repaired": 220}}, "x": {"prompt": "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's the X post:\n\n\"**SaaS IS BACK, BABY!**\n\nI'm telling you - all the naysayers who said SaaS was dead... just got schooled by @Figma.\n\n304M in a single quarter? That's not growth, that's EXPLOSION. 40% YoY? Unbelievable!\n\nThis is what I call a **SaaS Superstar**. And it just goes to show - when you have a great product-market fit, and a team that knows how to execute... the sky's the limit.\n\nI've been saying it for years: SaaS is not dead, it's just getting started. And Figma's quarter is proof that I'm right\n\nWho else is ready to join me on this wild ride? Let", "lemkin-clone-v5": "We can all do better.\n\nBut it’s time to face facts:\n\nFigma just hit $304,000,000 in ARR in the last quarter alone.\n\nGrowing 40% at that scale? Unheard of.\n\nAnd yet … they are now trading for just a few times their revenue.\n\nA SaaS stock priced like software.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "SaaStr AI Weekly #12\nFigma:\n$3B+ valuation\n$1B ARR\nGrowing 40%\nJust crossed $300m TCV, the most ever for Figma, and growing at a record rate.\nThis Is The New Benchmark For B2B Software\nThe market is back. But it’s not just back—it’s on steroids. The stock market has already priced in the new reality:\nCloudflare: Up 80% this year (from $40 to $72)\nFigma: Trading at 20x ARR, valuing them at a staggering $3B+\nWhat This Means For Founders\nIf you’re building software right now—don’t be shy about asking for more. The market is paying premium prices for top-tier B2B companies", "cleaned": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "balanced": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "r32": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back. We’re still waiting for some AI breakout B2B numbers to really move the needle, but we don’t have to wait much longer. The latest earnings from Monday.com ($1B ARR), Procore (33% growth at $800m+ ARR) and Asana all show real acceleration now across their customer base. But here’s what happened over the weekend that should make every public SaaS founder pay attention: Figma just reported Q3 FY25 results. And they did something no other major SaaS company has done since ChatGPT launched: They posted not one, not two, but three quarters of accelerating revenue growth. Three consecutive quarters where existing customers", "repaired": "Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back. \nhttps://saastr.ai/article/figma-just-hit-304m-in-a-single-quarter-growing-40-and-just-like-that-saas-is-back?utm_source=quora&utm_medium=referral\n…"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 106, "lemkin-clone-v5": 49, "lemkin-clone-v6": 102, "cleaned": 18, "balanced": 18, "r32": 119, "repaired": 20}}, "talk": {"prompt": "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: Figma Just Hit $304M in a Single Quarter. Growing 40%. And Just Like That — SaaS is Back.", "answers": {"llama3.1": "Here's my attempt at writing a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin, founder and managing director at SaaStr:\n\n**[Excited tone]**\n\nWow, welcome everyone! I'm thrilled to be here today to share some incredible news that just dropped like a bombshell on the SaaS industry. Figma has just announced their Q4 numbers, and let me tell you - it's a game-changer.\n\n**[Pause for emphasis]**\n\nFigma hit $304 million in revenue last quarter! That's right, folks. In one single quarter. With growth of 40% year-over-year. I mean... **[smiling]** ...this is what we've been waiting for!\n\nNow, I know some of you might be thinking: \"Jason, this is just Figma.\" But hold on to your hats, because this is much bigger than one company's success story. This is a signal - a loud and clear signal - that the SaaS market is back.\n\n**[Passionate tone]**\n\nWe've all been wondering when the SaaS market would roar back to life after what felt like an eternity of slow growth. And I'm here to tell you: it's happening now! Figma isn't just any company; they're a leader in design collaboration and prototyping, with a product that truly disrupts traditional software sales. Their success is proof that SaaS companies can still achieve incredible scale and growth.\n\n**[Highlighting the implications]**\n\nSo what does this mean for us as investors, founders, and entrepreneurs? It means we should be excited about building our own SaaS businesses - or investing in those already on the rise! This market will continue to grow exponentially, driven by cloud adoption, AI-powered software, and the increasing demand for digital products.\n\n**[Concluding with a nod to the future]**\n\nTo all you founders out there: if your product solves a real problem, has a scalable business model, and is executed flawlessly - just like Figma's been doing these past few years... well, then it's time to take aim at $100 million in ARR. Your customers are waiting!\n\nAnd to our investors: it's time to start looking for the next big SaaS winner! With growth rates like this, we're on the cusp of another incredible wave of innovation and wealth creation.\n\nThat's all from me today. Thanks for tuning in, everyone!", "lemkin-clone-v5": "I’m here to tell you something that I never thought I’d be saying, but it’s true. A week ago at\nSaaStr Annual,\nI gave a talk on the state of SaaS. Now in many ways it felt like Groundhog Day all over again — we’re back where we were before Covid hit and things seemed to grind to a halt.\nBut boy is there an important difference today vs. 2020.\nToday, everyone is hiring. Everyone wants to work at a great SaaS company. And for sure… the public markets are back too\n— Jason ✨BeKind✨ Lemkin  (@jasonlk)\nJune 15, 2023\nIn fact in many ways we’re even better off today than before Covid.\nThe world is more remote-friendly. The AI revolution that’s now underway will have a huge impact on software and SaaS. And the cloud? We know how to do it right now.\nBut there was something missing from my last talk at Annual, and it seemed like many of you were thinking it too.\nSomething I had missed in all our discussion about how tough things are today:\nSaaS is Back\nAnd with that, let me just show a few examples. Figma’s Q1 revenue hit $304m! That’s 40% YoY growth from the same quarter last year. And as we can see here on this chart from Bessemer Venture Partners, it looks like they are going to keep growing fast in the quarters ahead.\nFigma is just one of many SaaS companies that have taken off this past year. Canva has too,\nand so has Asana.  Not only did they all grow 40% or more last quarter — but\nthe stock prices have all gone up, even with the recent volatility.\nAnd there’s a reason for it. For one thing, you can’t hide from SaaS today anymore. You can try to be stealthy and go private like many of us are doing, but the fact is that everyone knows how well a company does in this AI era by their revenue growth.\nBut here’s where I think things have changed:\nIt used to take 18 months for your app to get big\nYou know it when you hit $1m or so ARR.", "lemkin-clone-v6": "Figma just hit $304 million in revenue in Q1—up from $226m last year—a growth rate that’s now accelerating to 39% year-over-year, as the company continues its remarkable journey and it seems like a lot of other folks are joining it on this path.\nThis is not some startup or new kid anymore. Figma is an $8 billion+ IPO with almost\n$1 Billion in ARR\nand has been around since 2012! It’s a real, legitimate SaaS company that everyone knows and uses — but nobody really expected to be growing nearly 40% year-over-year.\nFolks are still arguing over whether AI or Cloud is going to save SaaS. But here's the thing: it doesn't matter if you're in AI, B2B, B2C, whatever—\nif your growth rate is accelerating today,\nyou should be celebrating.\n\nIf you’re growing 40% year-over-year as Figma just did, even at $1 Billion+ ARR — that’s a huge win. You are the best of what SaaS has to offer.\nAnd here's the real kicker: it looks like we're about to see a lot more of this happening in B2B and enterprise software over the next 24 months.\n\nWe've got:\nSamsara just IPO'd at $10 Billion\nand growing +37%\nWix is up over 100% on its AI news, and already has $1.5 billion ARR.\nCloudflare announced it's hitting $3 BILLION in annualized revenue,\nup from $2.6B a year ago—a 15% increase in just one quarter!\nZoom hit an incredible $7 Billion ARR milestone\nand is growing like crazy.\nWe've got all these massive, established SaaS companies that are still going gangbusters — and the startups are starting to see this too.\n\nSo I don't know if we're seeing a \"SaaS renaissance.\"", "cleaned": "the growth was there it's just that their NRR went down and they had to go more Enterprise um we saw this with zoom info which I think is one of our best public SAS companies it grew even faster than figma through most of its history but then as it got into bigger deals its nrr started going down because you can only sell so much software to your customers and ultimately now its growing at almost half the rate it did before IPO uh when times were better so yes if things are tougher today for folks like zoom info or others maybe slow things down for a quarter and lean on your existing customer base but don't hide from low NR okay point three and this ties together with some other points I've made over time but I find it interesting and actually inspiring is how quickly figa has leaned back into the enterprise and it didn't do it overnight and it took them years to get to where they are but what happened is during Covet everyone rushed out to buy figmas right and many folks didn't need them before covet they needed them after covet but they became an essential tool for collaboration and design across organizations and that led to enterprises buying it left and right and they're not abandoning that even though now the world isn't in lockdown anymore they're leaning harder into Enterprises um and importantly look at these numbers this is really interesting again in Q1 this year Enterprise customers made up 48% of revenue in q2 they make up 53% so in two quarters Enterprise has gone from almost half of Revenue to almost 60% of Revenue", "balanced": "so this one's just so impressive and I don't think it gets enough attention as a public company but I've been doing B2B for over two decades and if you're not paying attention to figma right now if you haven't watched them go from IPO to almost five billion growing an incredible pace going more Enterprise and adding even more value then boy you got to get on the train because it's pretty epic what they did this quarter let me break down a few things that are crazy awesome first of all look at those numbers 304 million dollars in Revenue up 40% year-over-year okay that alone would be a heck of a story 304 million a single quarter but here's where it gets interesting in their last quarter before ipo their revenue was 250 million um so that means they grew 54 percent since their last quarter before ipo that's just nuts we have never seen anything like that folks HubSpot didn't grow 50% after its ipo uh Slack didn't grow 50% after its IPO zoom had a little bit of a dip post ipo it took a while to come back and others it's rare when you see a public sas or Cloud company grow faster than 30% a year post-IPO and yet figma went from 250 million in the prior quarter to 304 million this quarter that is insane growth and they're still accelerating so number one point number one point three oh sorry four hundred thousand customers I mean these are metrics that we rarely see and especially in enterprise software and it shows how sticky it is it shows how viral it is when your customer count grows 100% at 300 million AR yes some of those customers might be small they might pay 10 bucks a month but it doesn't matter because you can add hundreds and thousands of them to drive your revenue up and that's why I love", "r32": "but they've got to get back into growth mode so you don't see these things as much but they're there and I think that's what it takes for public companies to go from mid-20s to high 30s to 50% because even if your NRR isn't great like Adobe's at 97 percent with only 14% growth it can still happen if you have incredible new customer growth okay last point which we touched on before but this is super important when you are going long and you may need another five years or ten years to IPO again um especially if you raise less than 100 million dollars seed round um do not let growth slow down do not allow yourself to settle into the low teens unless you just absolutely cannot grow any faster be honest about why you can't grow faster does your competition dominate you yes maybe your market size isn't big enough yes maybe you don't have enough capital yes maybe your TAM isn't huge whatever it is dig into it figure out why it's too hard to grow more than say 10 or 12% but then set a plan to fix those issues um because if you settle into low double digits over time it will kill you in an IPO process no one wants to buy a company growing 9 or 15 percent in today's world and especially no one wants to pay a premium for it so stay committed folks do not let nrr drag down uh sales cycles drag down growth drag down anything drag down hiring drag down everything keep pushing push harder to grow faster to grow bigger than ever even if you feel exhausted even if even if you want to take a breath remember you may need five years to IPO six years eight years", "repaired": "and what you're seeing now with AI is that it's just accelerating even more because you don't have to be an engineer anymore so there's no excuse for not adopting AI tools and AI has become a core part of almost all our workflows today um but I want to step back for one second on this whole productivity discussion because we've heard some interesting things from folks like HubSpot who are saying hey look at us we were able to boost revenue by adding new products like Breeze which they didn't spend any money on but they did invest in marketing right or other folks talking about how their sales team could close bigger deals faster and get better results with less headcount um I'll tell you my story as a Founder CEO okay I was pretty early to go multiproduct right when I started saster I had two products uh adobe sign echo sign and adobe creative cloud it was just called Echo sign then later became Adobe sign I had these two SAS companies going gangbusters uh both in different markets doing very well and then I added another product called web service in addition to our existing products at adobe so three products at once maybe five six years ago it worked out really well we grew significantly faster than if we'd stuck to one product line and then Fast Forward to last year we launched sastra annual and again we grew 50% and went into positive cash flow without spending anything on Marketing or Sales okay it works it does work right um but here's where I think it gets complicated I'm only sharing this data in aggregate but here's where it gets complicated"}, "words": {"llama3.1": 364, "lemkin-clone-v5": 364, "lemkin-clone-v6": 281, "cleaned": 287, "balanced": 327, "r32": 306, "repaired": 289}}}}];


export default function ExperimentGlance() {
  const [goldId, setGoldId] = useCanvasState("goldId", ITEMS[0].id);
  const [medium, setMedium] = useCanvasState<MediumId>("medium", "blog");
  const index = Math.max(0, ITEMS.findIndex((item) => item.id === goldId));
  const item = ITEMS[index];
  const chosen = item.mediums[medium];

  return (
    <Stack gap={16}>
      <Stack gap={6}>
        <H1>Persona evaluation</H1>
        <Text tone="secondary">Repaired on the Oct 1 baselines run has Persona Utility 0.721 on 47/120 eligible drafts. Relabel seed 43 has Persona Utility 0.717 on 81/120 eligible drafts. The utility gap is 0.004. This rescore has no uncertainty estimate for that gap, so it is not treated as a meaningful difference. Eligibility is the larger practical difference. Stance passed calibration and ranges from 0.958 to 1.000, so it is saturated and does not strongly separate adapters. The provisional table orders distinct adapters by median retrieval-off utility. It is not a final winner.</Text>
      </Stack>
      <H2>What each number means</H2>
      <Table
        headers={["Metric", "Meaning", "Better", "Range", "This rescore"]}
        rows={GUIDE}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Overall, provisional ranking</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        One row per adapter. Retrieval-off seeds are summarized by the median, not by the
        single highest run. Oct 1 repaired is the highest single utility and is called out
        above; it is not a separate winning adapter. Close medians are not a decided order.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Scan", "Adapter", "Basis", "Utility median", "Utility mean", "Utility spread", "Eligible mean", "Eligible range"]}
        rows={PROVISIONAL}
        columnAlign={["right", "left", "left", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right"]}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Top three distinct adapters</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        Each place is one adapter, the run that scored best on that metric, the score, and
        how many of 120 drafts passed the gates. Seeds of the same adapter cannot fill all
        three places. Utility places are the highest runs, not a significance test.
        Stance places are saturated.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Metric", "Better", "1st", "2nd", "3rd"]}
        rows={TOPS}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Persona evaluation, every run</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        Persona Utility, eligibility, voice, stance, writing quality, task, completion, and
        copying. Arrows in the column names are the preferred direction. RAG grounding is
        not in this table.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Model", "Run", "Persona Utility ↑", "Eligible", "Eligible rate ↑", "Voice ↓", "Stance ↑", "Writing quality ↑", "Task adherence ↑", "Completion ↑", "Copying avoidance ↑"]}
        rows={PRIMARY}
        columnAlign={["left", "left", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right"]}
        striped
      />
      <Text tone="tertiary" size="small">
        Source: EXP-20261004-008 rescore of saved answers. Voice is a z-score on
        gate-passing blog drafts; lower is closer to held-out Lemkin. Stance is 0 to 1
        and saturated. Writing quality is 1 to 5. No answers were regenerated.
      </Text>
      <H2>Adapter summary</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        Repeated Oct 4 retrieval-off seeds are one row, with mean, median, and spread.
        The Oct 1 run and the retrieval-on run stay on their own rows so those conditions
        are not averaged into the seed mean.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Adapter", "Group", "n", "Utility mean", "Utility median", "Utility min", "Utility max", "Spread", "Eligible mean", "Eligible range"]}
        rows={ADAPTERS}
        columnAlign={["left", "left", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right"]}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Claim-level RAG grounding</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        Retrieval-off runs are n/a. Claim support is the mean of per-draft rates among
        claims the judge treated as verifiable. Verifiable is supported plus unsupported
        plus contradicted. Not verifiable and not applicable are outside that rate, so a
        value near 1.0 can still leave many claims unchecked.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Model", "Run", "Claim support ↑", "Claims", "Verifiable", "Supported", "Unsupported", "Contradicted", "Not verifiable", "Not applicable"]}
        rows={GROUNDING}
        columnAlign={["left", "left", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right"]}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Reference overlap</H2>
      <Text tone="secondary">
        Brief overlap is the old gold rubric. It is agreement with the constructed
        assignment brief, not proof of Lemkin voice. BLEU and ROUGE-L use that same brief.
      </Text>
      <Table
        headers={["Model", "Run", "Brief overlap", "BERTScore", "BLEU", "ROUGE-L", "Latency"]}
        rows={DIAGNOSTIC}
        columnAlign={["left", "left", "right", "right", "right", "right", "right"]}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Calibration</H2>
      <Table
        headers={["Medium", "Style role", "Real Lemkin", "Generic prose", "Base model", "Framing role", "Framing check"]}
        rows={CALIBRATION}
        striped
      />
      <Text tone="tertiary" size="small">
        Style distances are from EXP-20261004-007 and still decide which mediums enter the
        voice mean. The framing-role column is that older cue-frequency check. Stance in
        the tables above is the EXP-008 evidence check, and it is saturated.
      </Text>
      <Stack gap={6}>
        <H2>Prompt, expected, and actual</H2>
        <Text tone="secondary">
          Same 30 topics and four mediums. Llama 3.1 is the untrained instruct checkpoint.
          Lemkin clone v5 and v6 share the same Ollama weights (id 06b812f9374d), scored
          as two samples. Expected facts are shown here and were not put in the prompt.
        </Text>
      </Stack>
      <H2>Parameters</H2>
      <Table
        headers={["Setting", "Value"]}
        rows={[
          ["Blog prompt", "Write a blog post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: {topic}"],
          ["LinkedIn prompt", "Write a LinkedIn post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: {topic}"],
          ["X prompt", "Write an X post in the style of Jason Lemkin about: {topic}"],
          ["Talk prompt", "Write a talk in the style of Jason Lemkin about: {topic}"],
          ["Context", "2048"],
          ["New tokens", "Blog 768, LinkedIn 320, X 160, Talk 768"],
          ["Temperature", "0.7"],
          ["Top-p", "0.9"],
          ["Repetition penalty", "1.15"],
          ["Sampling", "on"],
          ["Expected facts in the prompt", "no"],
        ]}
        striped
      />
      <H2>Prompt sent</H2>
      <Text weight="semibold">{chosen.prompt}</Text>
      <Text tone="secondary" size="small">
        {item.id}. Audience: {item.audience}. Goal: {item.goal}.
      </Text>
      <Row gap={8} align="center" wrap>
        {MEDIUMS.map((entry) => (
          <Button
            key={entry.id}
            variant={medium === entry.id ? "primary" : "secondary"}
            onClick={() => setMedium(entry.id)}
          >
            {entry.label}
          </Button>
        ))}
      </Row>
      <Row gap={8} align="center" wrap>
        <Button variant="secondary" disabled={index === 0} onClick={() => setGoldId(ITEMS[index - 1].id)}>
          Previous
        </Button>
        <Select
          value={item.id}
          onChange={setGoldId}
          options={ITEMS.map((row) => ({
            value: row.id,
            label: `${row.id}  ${row.topic}`,
          }))}
          style={{ minWidth: 480, flex: 1 }}
        />
        <Button
          variant="secondary"
          disabled={index === ITEMS.length - 1}
          onClick={() => setGoldId(ITEMS[index + 1].id)}
        >
          Next
        </Button>
      </Row>
      <H2>Expected</H2>
      <Text style={{ whiteSpace: "pre-wrap" }}>{item.expected}</Text>
      <H3>Expected facts</H3>
      <Stack gap={4}>
        {item.facts.map((fact, factIndex) => (
          <Text key={`${item.id}-fact-${factIndex}`} tone="secondary" size="small">
            {fact}
          </Text>
        ))}
      </Stack>
      <H2>Actual</H2>
      <Stack gap={12}>
        {MODELS.map((model) => (
          <Stack key={model.id} gap={6}>
            <H3>
              {model.label} · {chosen.words[model.id as keyof typeof chosen.words]} words
            </H3>
            <Text style={{ whiteSpace: "pre-wrap" }}>{chosen.answers[model.id as keyof typeof chosen.answers]}</Text>
          </Stack>
        ))}
      </Stack>
    </Stack>
  );
}
